#align(center)[

#gtable(columns: 5, [], [2. m.], [#he[כֶם];], [#he[־ְכֶם];], [#emph[your.];], [], [f.], [#he[כֶן];], [#he[־ְכֶן];], [], [], [3. m.], [#he[הֶם];], [#he[־ָם];], [#emph[eorum.];], [], [], [#he[מוֹ];], [(poet. #he[־ָ֫ מוֹ];)], [], [], [f.], [#he[הֶן]; (#he[הֵן];)], [#he[־ָן];], [#emph[earum];.]);

];

#ml("c") <p91c> Rem. 1. There is less variety of forms in these than in the verbal suffixes; the particular forms are used as follows:—

(#emph[a];) Those without a connecting vowel (on the derivation of these ‘connecting vowels’ from original stem\-vowels, see note on #xref(<p58f>)[§ 58#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];) are generally joined to nouns of a peculiar form (see § 96), the #emph[constr. st.]; of which ends in a vowel, as #he[אָבִ֫יךָ];, #he[אָבִ֫יהוּ]; and #he[אָבִיו];, #he[אָבִ֫יהָ];, #he[אָבִ֫ינוּ];, #he[אֲבִיכֶם];, #he[אֲבִיכֶן];, #he[אֲבִיהֶם];, #he[אֲבִיהֶן];, sometimes also to segholate forms ending in #emph[î]; from #he[ל״ה]; stems (see #xref(<p93x>)[§ 93#h(0.18em)#emph[x];];, y), e.g. #he[פְּרִיהֶם]; #emph[the fruit of them];, #bref("Amos 9:14"); (also #he[פִּרְיָם]; #bref("Isa 37:30");, etc.), #he[פִּרִיהֶן]; #bref("Jer 29:28"); (also #he[פִּרְיָן]; verse 5); cf., moreover, #he[חֶלְבְּהֶן]; #bref("Lev 8:16, 25"); and similar examples with #he[הֶן]; (#bref("Isa 3:17"); #he[הֵן];) #bref("Gen 21:28");, #bref("Ezek 13:17");, #bref("16:53");.#footnote[Also in #bref("Jer 15:10"); read (according to #xref(<p61h>)[§ 61#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];, end) #he[כֻּלְּהֶם קִלְּלוּנִי];; in #bref("Hos 7:6"); probably #he[אַפְּהֶם]; for #he[אֹֽפֵהֶם];.]; Also in #bref("Gen 1:21");, #bref("4:4");, #bref("Ezek 10:12");, #bref("Nah 2:8");, etc., the #emph[K#super[e];th.]; perhaps intends the singular, #he[לְמִֽינְהֶם];, etc., but the Masora requires the plural with defective #emph[ê];.

#ml("d") <p91d> (#emph[b];) The forms with connecting vowels (#xref(<p58f>)[§ 58#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];) are joined to nouns ending in a consonant. The connecting vowel is regularly #emph[a]; in the #emph[3rd sing. fem.]; #he[־ָהּ]; (for #emph[aha];) and #emph[3rd plur.]; #he[־ָם];, #he[־ָ֫ מוֹ];, #he[־ָן];, also in the #emph[3rd sing. masc.]; #he[(הֹ) וֹ];, since the #emph[ô]; is contracted from #emph[a\[h\]û];, and in the pausal form of the #emph[2nd masc.]; #he[־ֶ֫ ךָ]; (a modification of original #he[־ַ֫ ךָ];).

The forms with #emph[ē]; in the above\-mentioned persons are common only with nouns in #he[־ֶה]; (from stems #he[ל״ה];), #emph[constr. st.]; #he[־ֵה]; (cf. #xref(<p89f>)[§ 89#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];), e.g. #he[שָׂדֵ֫הוּ]; (from #emph[sadaihû];) #emph[his field];; #he[עָלֶ֫הָ]; #emph[its leaf];, #bref("Isa 1:30");; #he[מַרְאֶ֫הָ]; #emph[the appearance thereof];, #bref("Lev 13:4"); (from #emph[mar’aihā];; on the #emph[S#super[e];ghôl]; see k); but #he[שָׂדָהּ]; #emph[her field];. The orthographic retention of the #he[י];, e.g. #he[מַֽעֲשֶׂ֫יךָ];, #he[מַֽעֲשָׂיו];, gives to many forms the appearance of plurals; see the instances in #xref(<p93ss>)[§ 93#h(0.18em)#emph[ss];];.

Apart from these #he[ל״ה]; forms the connecting vowel #emph[ē]; in the 3rd pers. occurs only in isolated cases; #he[אוֹרֵ֫הוּ]; #emph[his light];, #bref("Job 25:3");; #he[לְמִינֵ֫הוּ]; #emph[after its kind];, #bref("Gen 1:12, 25"); \[\+ 12 times\]; #bref("Nah 1:13");; in #bref("Judg 19:24"); read #he[פִּֽילַגְשׁוֹ]; as in vv. 2, 25. On the other hand #he[־ֵךְ]; in the #emph[2nd sing. fem.]; and #he[־ֵ֫ נוּ]; in the #emph[1st plur.]; are by far the more common forms, while #he[־ָךְ];, #he[־ָ֫ נוּ]; are of rare occurrence; see e.—Instead of #he[־ְךָ]; (#he[־ְכָה]; in #bref("Gen 10:19");, #bref("Exod 13:16");, #bref("Jer 29:25");, etc., cf. #he[בְּכָה];, #he[לְכָה]; #xref(<p103g>)[§ 103#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];), #he[־ְכֶם];, #he[־ְכֶן]; (with #emph[Š#super[e];wâ mobile];), if the last consonant of the noun is a guttural, the forms are #he[־ֲךָ];, #he[־ֲכֶם];, #he[־ֲכֶן];, e.g. #he[רֽוּחֲךָ]; #emph[thy spirit];, #he[בֹּרַֽאֲךָ]; #emph[thy creator];, #bref("Isa 43:1");, #he[רֵיֽעֲכֶם]; #emph[your friend];, #bref("Job 6:27"); (on such cases as #he[בְּתוֹכְכֶם]; #bref("Hag 2:5");, see #xref(<p10g>)[§ 10#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];).—With #emph[Nun energicum]; (cf. #xref(<p58i>)[§ 58#h(0.18em)#emph[i];];, and on #he[עוֹנֶ֑ךָּ]; #bref("Job 5:1");, etc., cf. #xref(<p61h>)[§ 61#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];) #he[דַּיֶּ֑ךָּ]; occurs in #bref("Prov 25:16");, in principal pause.

2\. Rare or incorrect forms are—

#ml("e") <p91e> #emph[Sing.]; 1st pers. #he[־ֵ֫ נִי]; in #he[בְּשׁוּבֵ֫נִי]; #bref("Ezek 47:7"); (certainly only a scribal error, caused by #he[וַיְשִׁבֵ֫נִי]; in verse 6). #origpage("256") <orig-256>;2nd pers. #emph[m];. in pause #he[־ֶ֫ כָה];, e.g. #he[כַּפֶּֽכָה]; (#emph[thy hand];), #bref("Ps 139:5");, cf. #bref("Prov 24:10");; once #he[חֹנָ֑ךְ]; #bref("Ps 53:6"); (cf. the analogous cases in the verbal suffix #xref(<p75ll>)[§ 75#h(0.18em)#emph[ll];];); #emph[fem.]; #he[־ֵיךְ]; #bref("Ezek 5:12"); (in #bref("16:53"); also for #he[שְׁבִיתַ֫יִךְ]; probably #he[שְׁבִיתֵיךְ]; is intended), #he[־ֵ֫ כִי]; #bref("Jer 11:15");, #bref("Ps 103:3");, #bref("116:19");, #bref("135:9"); (corresponding to the Aramaic suffix of the #emph[2nd fem. sing.];; on the wholly abnormal #he[־ֵ֫ כֵה]; #bref("Nah 2:14");, cf. #emph[l];), #he[לֵכִי]; #emph[K#super[e];th.]; #bref("2 Kgs 4:2");, #bref("Song 2:13");. Also #he[־ָ֫ ךְ]; #bref("Isa 22:1");, #bref("Ezek 23:28");, #bref("25:4");.

3rd pers. #he[־ֹה]; (cf. #xref(<p7c>)[§ 7#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];), e.g. #he[אָֽהֳלֹה]; #bref("Gen 9:21");, #bref("12:8");, #bref("13:3");, #bref("35:21"); (always with #emph[Q#super[e];rê]; #he[אָֽהֳלוֹ];); #he[נֻחֹה]; #bref("Num 10:36");; #he[לֵחֹה]; #bref("Deut 34:7");; #he[בֻּלֹּה]; #bref("Jer 20:7");, #bref("Nah 2:1"); #emph[Q#super[e];rê];; #he[קִצֹּה]; #bref("2 Kgs 19:23"); #emph[K#super[e];th.];, for which #he[קִצּוֹ]; is read in #bref("Isa 37:24");; #he[עִירֹה]; and #he[סוּתֹה]; #bref("Gen 49:11");, cf. #bref("Exod 22:26"); (#emph[Q#super[e];rê]; #he[עִירוֹ];, #he[סוּתוֹ];); #he[סֻבֹּה]; #bref("Ps 10:9");, #bref("27:5"); #emph[K#super[e];th.];; #he[הֲמוֹנֹה]; #bref("Ezek 31:18");, etc., #emph[K#super[e];th.];; #he[תְּבוּאָתֹה]; #bref("Ezek 48:18"); \[altogether fourteen times in the Pentateuch, and some forty times in other books: see Driver, #emph[Samuel];, p. xxxv, and on #bref("2 Sam 2:9");, #bref("21:1");\].

#emph[3rd fem.]; #he[־ָה]; for #he[־ָהּ]; (with the softening of the #emph[Mappiq];, cf. #xref(<p23k>)[§ 23#h(0.18em)#emph[k];];, and the analogous cases in #xref(<p58g>)[§ 58#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];) occurs repeatedly before #emph[B#super[e];ghadhk#super[e];phath]; and other soft consonants, #bref("Exod 9:18"); (before #he[וְ];, if the text is right), #bref("Lev 13:4"); (before #he[ל];), #bref("Num 15:28, 31");, #bref("1 Sam 1:9"); (unless #he[אָכְלָה];, the infin. with fem. termination, is intended; #he[שָׁתֹה]; follows), #bref("Ezek 16:44");, #bref("24:6"); (before #he[ב];), #bref("1 Sam 20:20");, #bref("2 Kgs 8:6");, #bref("Prov 12:28"); (before #he[א];), #bref("Nah 3:9"); (before #he[וּ];), #bref("Ps 48:14"); (before #he[פ];), #bref("Ezek 47:10");, #bref("Job 31:22"); twice (before #he[ת];), #bref("Isa 21:2");, #bref("Jer 20:17"); (before #he[ה];), #bref("Num 32:42");, #bref("Amos 1:11"); (before #he[ן];), #bref("Lev 6:2"); (before #he[ע];); even in #emph[pause];, #bref("Lev 12:4");#super[ #emph[a];]; and #super[5 b];; #bref("Isa 23:17");, #bref("Prov 21:22");, also with Zaqeph, #bref("Isa 45:6");, #bref("Jer 6:6"); (probably), #bref("44:19");; on #he[הָשַּׁמָּה]; #bref("Lev 26:34");, etc., see #xref(<p67y>)[§ 67#h(0.18em)#emph[y];];. Cf. also #he[־ָא]; #bref("Ezek 36:5");.—Sometimes the Masora appears (but this is very doubtful) to regard the #he[־ָהּ]; with feminines as a shortening of #he[־ָתָהּ];, e.g. #he[נִצָּהּ]; #bref("Gen 40:10"); for #he[נִצָּ֫תָהּ];, #he[פִּנָּהּ]; #bref("Prov 7:8"); for #he[פִּנָּ֫תָהּ];; also #he[־ָם]; for #he[־ָתָם]; in #he[כִּתְבוּנָם]; #bref("Hos 13:2");, and #he[עָרְמָם]; #bref("Job 5:13");. The examples, however, are for the most part uncertain, e.g. in #bref("Isa 28:4"); the reading is simply to be emended to #he[בִּכּוּרָה];, and in #bref("Zech 4:2"); to #he[גֻּלָּה];, #bref("Job 11:9"); to #he[מִדָּה];, #bref("Neh 5:14"); to #he[פֶּחָה];. \[See also, after prepositions, #xref(<p103g>)[§ 103#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];.\]

#ml("f") <p91f> #emph[Plur.]; 1st pers. #he[־ָ֫ נוּ];, in #emph[pause]; #he[קִימָ֫נוּ]; #bref("Job 22:20"); (where, however, #he[קָמֵ֫נוּ]; is certainly to be read); cf. #bref("Ruth 3:2"); \[#bref("Isa 47:10");, cf. #xref(<p61c>)[§ 61#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];, h\], and so always #he[כֻּלָּ֫נוּ]; #emph[all of us];, #bref("Gen 42:11");, etc. \[cf. #he[בָּנוּ];, #he[לָנוּ];, #he[אִתָּנוּ];, #he[עִמָּנוּ];\].

2nd pers. #emph[fem.]; #he[כֶ֫נָה]; #bref("Ezek 23:48, 49");.

3rd pers. #emph[masc.]; #he[־ָ֫ מוֹ]; #bref("Ps 17:10"); (on #he[מוֹ]; in #he[פִּ֫ימוֹ]; in the same verse, and in #bref("Ps 58:7"); see l); #he[־ָ֑ הַם]; #bref("2 Sam 23:6");, according to Sievers probably to call attention to the reading #he[כלהם];. #emph[fem.]; #he[־ָ֫ הְנָה]; #bref("1 Kgs 7:37");, #bref("Ezek 16:53"); (in #emph[pause];); #he[־ֶ֫ נָה]; #bref("Gen 41:21");; #he[־ֶ֫ נָּה]; #bref("Gen 30:41");; #he[־ָ֫ נָה]; #bref("Ruth 1:19");; elsewhere generally in #emph[pause]; (#bref("Gen 21:29");, #bref("42:36");, #bref("Jer 8:7");, #bref("Prov 31:29");, #bref("Job 39:2");); finally #he[הֵן]; as suffix to a noun, only in #bref("Isa 3:17");.

For examples of singulars with plural suffixes see l.

#ml("g") <p91g> #strong[2.]; In the #emph[plural masc.]; and in the #emph[dual]; the suffixes are to be regarded primarily as affixed to the original ending of the #emph[construct state]; (#he[־ַ֫ י];, cf. #xref(<p89d>)[§ 89#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];). This ending, however, has been preserved unchanged only in the #emph[2nd fem.]; In most cases it is contracted to #he[־ֵי];, as in the #emph[constr. st.]; without suffixes (so throughout the plur. and in the poetical suffix #he[־ֵ֫ יהוּ]; of the #emph[3rd sing. masc.];); in the #emph[2nd masc.]; and #emph[3rd fem. sing.]; it is #he[־ֶי]; (cf. k). On the #emph[1st pers.]; and #emph[3rd masc. sing.]; see i.—Thus there arise the following #origpage("257") <orig-257>;

#ml("h") <p91h>

#align(center)[

#gtable(columns: 4, table.cell(colspan: 2)[#strong[#emph[Singular];.];], table.cell(colspan: 2)[#strong[#emph[Plural];.];], [1. c. #emph[my.];], [#he[־ַי];, #emph[pause]; #he[־ָי];], [1. c. #emph[our.];], [#he[־ֵ֫ ינוּ];], [2. m. #emph[thy.];], [#he[־ֶ֫ יךָ];], [2. m. #emph[your.];], [#he[־ֵיכֶם];], [f.], [#he[־ַ֫ יִךְ]; #emph[pause]; #he[־ָ֫ יִךְ];], [f.], [#he[־ֵיכֶן];], [3. m. #emph[his.];], [#he[־ָיו];, poet. #he[־ֵ֫ יהוּ];], [3. m. #emph[their.];], [#he[־ֵיהֶם];, poet. #he[־ֵ֫ ימוֹ];], [f. #emph[her.];], [#he[־ֶ֫ יהָ];], [f.], [#he[־ֵיהֶן];]);

];

#ml("i") <p91i> Thus the original #he[־ַי]; is (#emph[a];) contracted in the #emph[3rd sing. masc.]; #he[־ֵ֫ יהוּ]; and throughout the #emph[plural];, as #he[סוּסֵ֫יהוּ];, #he[סוּסֵ֫ינוּ];, etc.; (#emph[b];) retained unchanged in the #emph[1st sing.]; #he[סוּסַי];, the real suffix\-ending #he[י]; (see b) being united with the final #emph[Yôdh]; of the ending #he[־ַי];; and in the #emph[2nd fem. sing.]; #he[סוּסַ֫יִךְ];, with a #emph[helping];\-#emph[Ḥireq]; after the #emph[Yôdh];. On the other hand (#emph[c];) the #emph[Yôdh]; of #he[־ַי]; is lost in pronunciation and the #emph[ă]; lengthened to #emph[ā]; in the #emph[3rd masc. sing.]; #he[סוּסָיו];, i.e. #emph[sûsāw]; (pronounced #emph[susā\-u];).#footnote[In the papyrus of the decalogue from the Fayyûm, line 16, #he[ויקדשיו]; occurs for #he[ויקדשהו]; #bref("Exod 20:11");. Gall, #emph[ZAW.]; 1903, p. 349, takes this as an indication that the traditional forms of the noun\-suffix #he[יו]; or #he[ו]; represent #emph[aiŭ]; or #emph[eŭ];. P. Haupt aptly compares the Greek use of the #emph[iota subscript]; (ᾷ).]; The #emph[2nd masc. sing.]; #he[סוּסֶ֫יךָ]; and the #emph[3rd fem. sing.]; #he[סוּסֶ֫יהָ]; were formerly also explained here as having really lost the #he[י];, and modified the #emph[a]; of #emph[sûsakā, sûsahā]; to #emph[S#super[e];ghôl];; but cf. the view now given in #emph[g]; and #emph[k];.

#ml("k") <p91k> Rem. 1. As #he[סוּסֵ֫ינוּ]; represents #emph[sûsai\-nû];, so #he[סוּסֶ֫יךָ]; and #he[סוּסֶ֫יהָ]; represent #emph[sûsai\-kā, sûsai\-hā];, and the use of #emph[S#super[e];ghôl]; instead of the more regular #emph[Ṣere]; is to be explained from the character of the following syllable,—so P. Haupt who points to #he[יִקְטְלֶ֫הָ]; as compared with #he[יִקְטְלֵ֫הוּ];. In support of the view formerly adopted by us that the #he[י]; is only orthographically retained, too much stress must not be laid on the fact that it is sometimes omitted,#footnote[So in the Mêšaʿ inscription, l. 22 #he[מגדלתה]; #emph[its towers]; (along with #he[שעריה]; #emph[its gates];). Can it have been the rule to omit #he[י]; after the termination #emph[ôth];? Cf. below, n.]; thereby causing confusion in an unpointed text with the singular noun. A number of the examples which follow may be due to an erroneous assumption that the noun is a plural, where in reality it is a singular, and others may be incorrect readings. Cf. #he[דְרָכֶ֫ךָ]; #emph[thy ways]; (probably #he[דַּרְכְּךָ]; is intended), #bref("Exod 33:13");, #bref("Josh 1:8");, #bref("Ps 119:37");; for other examples, see #bref("Josh 21:11"); ff. (#he[מִגְרָשֶׁ֫הָ];; but in #bref("1 Chr 6:40"); ff. always #he[־ֶ֫ יהָ];), #bref("Judg 19:9");, #bref("1 Kgs 8:29");, #bref("Isa 58:13");, #bref("Ps 119:41, 43, 98"); (probably, however, in all these cases the #emph[sing.]; is intended); #he[אֱסָרֶ֫הָ]; #bref("Num 30:8"); (cf. v. 5); #he[מַכֹּתֶ֫הָ]; #bref("Jer 19:8");, #bref("49:17");; #he[מְבִיאֶ֫הָ]; #bref("Dan 11:6");. For the orthographic omission of #he[י]; before suffixes cf. #he[רֵעִ֫הוּ]; for #he[רֵעֵ֫יהוּ]; #emph[his friends]; #bref("1 Sam 30:26");, #bref("Prov 29:18");; #bref("Job 42:10"); (but it is possible to explain it here as a #emph[collective]; singular); #he[עֲוֹנֵ֫נוּ]; #emph[our iniquities];, #bref("Isa 64:5, 6");, #bref("Jer 14:7");; #bref("Exod 10:9");, #bref("Neh 10:1"); (#he[לְוִיֵּ֫נוּ]; from #he[לְוִיִּם]; which is always written defectively); #he[נִסְכֵּכֶם]; #bref("Num 29:33");; #he[רָעֹֽתֵכֶם]; #bref("Jer 44:9");; #he[יְדֵכֶם]; #bref("Ps 134:2");; #he[לְמִֽינֵהֶם]; #emph[after their kinds];, #bref("Gen 1:21"); (but see c), cf. #bref("4:4"); and #bref("Nah 2:8");. The #origpage("258") <orig-258>;defective writing is especially frequent in the #emph[3rd masc. sing.]; #he[־ָו];, which in #emph[Q#super[e];rê]; is almost always changed to #he[־ָיו];, e.g. #he[חִצָּו]; #emph[his arrows];, #bref("Ps 58:8");, #emph[Q#super[e];rê]; #he[חִצָּיו];. On #he[יַחְדָּו];, only three times #he[יַחְדָּיו];, cf. #xref(<p135r>)[§ 135#h(0.18em)#emph[r];];.

#ml("l") <p91l> 2. Unusual forms (but for the most part probably only scribal errors) are—#emph[Sing.]; 2nd pers. #emph[fem]; #he[־ֵיךְ]; (after #he[אַשְׁרֵי]; #emph[happy!]; #bref("Eccl 10:17");, which has become stereotyped as an interjection, and is therefore unchangeable; cf. Delitzsch on the passage); #he[־ַ֫ יְכִי]; (cf. Syr. #he[־ֵכִי];) #bref("2 Kgs 4:3");, and #super[7]; in #emph[K#super[e];th.];, #bref("Ps 103:3–5");, #bref("116:7"); (#he[־ָ֫ יְכִי]; in #emph[pause];).—In #bref("Ezek 16:31"); #he[־ַ֫ יִךְ]; (so #he[־ֵיכֶם]; in #bref("6:8");) occurs with an #emph[infin.]; ending in #he[וֹת];, the #he[וֹת]; being therefore treated as a plural ending; similarly, the plural suffix is sometimes found with the feminine ending #he[וּת]; (#bref("Num 14:33");, #bref("Isa 54:4");, #bref("Jer 3:8");, #bref("Ezek 16:15");, #bref("23:7");, as well as in #bref("16:20"); #emph[Q#super[e];rê];, and #bref("Zeph 3:20");), with the ending #emph[îth]; (#bref("Lev 5:24");, reading #he[חֲמִֽשִׁתוֹ];), and even with the ordinary feminine ending #emph[ath];; #bref("Isa 47:13");, #bref("Ezek 35:11");, #bref("Ps 9:15");, #bref("Ezra 9:15");.—Wholly abnormal is #he[מַלְאָכֵ֫כֵה]; #emph[thy messengers];, #bref("Nah 2:14");, evidently a case of dittography of the following #he[ה];: read #he[מַלְאָכַ֫יִךְ];.

#emph[3rd masc.]; #he[־ֵ֫ יהוּ]; #bref("Hab 3:10");, #bref("Job 24:23");; #he[־ֵ֫ הוּ]; #bref("1 Sam 30:26");, #bref("Ezek 43:17");, #bref("Nah 2:4");; #he[וֹ֫הִי]; (a purely Aramaic form) #bref("Ps 116:12");.—#emph[3rd fem.]; #he[־ֶ֫ יהָא]; #bref("Ezek 41:15");.

#emph[Plur.]; The strange #emph[2nd pers. masc.]; #he[תְּפוֹצֽוֹתִיכֶם]; (with #emph[î];, so Qimḥi; cf. Norzi) #bref("Jer 25:34");, is probably a mixed form combining #he[תָּפ֫וּצוּ]; and #he[הֲפִיצֽוֹתִיכֶם];; #emph[fem.]; #he[־ֵיכֶ֫נָה]; #bref("Ezek 13:20");.

#emph[3rd masc.]; #he[־ֵיהֵ֫מָה]; #bref("Ezek 40:16");; #emph[fem.]; #he[־ֵיהֶ֫נָה]; #bref("Ezek 1:11");.

3\. The termination #he[־ֵ֫מוֹ]; (also with the #emph[dual];, e.g. #bref("Ps 58:7");, #bref("59:13");), like #he[מוֹ]; and #he[־ָ֫ מוֹ];, occurs with the noun (as with the verb, #xref(<p58g>)[§ 58#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];) almost exclusively in the later poets \[namely with a substantive in the singular, #bref("Ps 21:11");, #bref("17:10, 10");, #bref("58:7");, #bref("59:13");, #bref("89:18");; with a dual or plural, #bref("Deut 32:27, 32, 37, 38");, #bref("33:29");, #bref("Ps 2:3, 3");, #bref("11:7");, #bref("35:16");, #bref("49:12");, #bref("58:7");, #bref("59:14");, #bref("73:5, 7");, #bref("83:12, 12");, #bref("140:4, 10");, #bref("Job 27:23");; after prepositions, see #xref(<p103f>)[§ 103#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];, o, notes\], and cannot, therefore, by itself be taken as an indication of archaic language. On the other hand there can be no doubt that these are revivals of really old forms. That they are consciously and artificially used is shown by the evidently intentional accumulation of them, e.g. in #bref("Exod 15:5, 7, 9");, #bref("Ps 2:3, 5");, and #bref("140:4, 10");, and also by the fact observed by Diehl (see the heading of this section) that in Ex 15 they occur #emph[only]; as verbal suffixes, in Dt 32 #emph[only]; as noun suffixes.

#ml("m") <p91m> #strong[3.]; It is clear and beyond doubt that the #emph[Yôdh]; in these suffixes with the plural noun belongs, in reality, to the ending of the #emph[construct state]; of the masculine plural. Yet the consciousness of this fact became so completely lost as to admit of the striking peculiarity (or rather inaccuracy) of appending those #emph[suffix];\-forms which include the plural ending #he[־ֵי];, even to the feminine plural in #he[וֹת]; (#he[סֽוּסוֹתֵ֫ינוּ];, #he[סֽוּסוֹתֶ֫יךָ];, etc.), so that in reality the result is a double indication of the plural.#footnote[See an analogous case in #xref(<p87s>)[§ 87#h(0.18em)#emph[s];];. Cf. also the double feminine ending in the #emph[3rd sing. perf.]; of verbs #he[ל״ה];, #xref(<p75i>)[§ 75#h(0.18em)#emph[i];];.];

#ml("n") <p91n> Such is the rule: the singular suffix, however (see b), also occurs with the ending #he[וֹת]; (probably through the influence of Aramaic), e.g. #he[עֵֽדְוֹתִי]; #bref("Ps 132:12"); (unless it be #emph[sing.]; for #he[עֵֽדוּתִי];, as, according to Qimḥi in his Lexicon, #he[תַּֽחֲנֹתִי]; #bref("2 Kgs 6:8"); is for #he[תַּֽחֲנוּתִי];); #he[מַכֹּֽתְךָ]; #bref("Deut 28:59"); (treated on the analogy of an #emph[infin.]; #he[ל״ה];); #origpage("259") <orig-259>;#he[אַֽחֲיוֹתֵךְ]; #bref("Ezek 16:52");. On the other hand #he[מִצְוֹתֶ֑ךָ]; (so Baer, Ginsb.; but Opit. #he[־ֶ֫ יךָ];) #bref("Ps 119:98");, #bref("Dan 9:5"); is merely written defectively, like #he[גַּרְגְּרֹתֶ֫ךָ]; according to Baer (not Ginsb.) in #bref("Prov 1:9");, etc. In the #emph[3rd plur.]; the use of the singular suffix is even the rule in the earlier Books (see the instances in Diehl, l. c., p. 8), e.g. #he[אֲבוֹתָם]; (#emph[their fathers];) oftener than #he[אֲבֹֽתֵיהֶם]; (this only in #bref("1 Kgs 14:15");, and in Jer, Ezr, Neh, and Ch \[in 1 K, Jer, Ezr, however, #he[אֲבוֹתָם]; is more common\]); so always #he[שְׁמוֹתָם];, #he[שְׁמוֹתָן]; #emph[their names];, #he[דּוֹרוֹתָם]; #emph[their generations];. From parallel passages like #bref("2 Sam 22:46"); compared with #bref("Ps 18:46");, #bref("Isa 2:4"); with #bref("Mic 4:3");, it appears that in many cases the longer form in #he[־ֵיהֶם]; can only subsequently have taken the place of #he[־ָם];.

#ml("o") <p91o> #strong[4.]; The following Paradigm of a masculine and feminine noun with suffixes is based upon a monosyllabic noun with one unchangeable vowel. With regard to the ending #he[־ַת]; in the #emph[constr. st.]; of the fem. it should be further remarked that the short #emph[ă]; of this ending is only retained before the #emph[grave]; suffixes #he[כֶם]; and #he[כֶן];; before all the others (the #emph[light]; suffixes) it is lengthened to #emph[ā];.

#ml("p") <p91p>

