#sec("92", [Vowel Changes in the Noun], note: []) <s92>

#ml("a") <p92a> #strong[1.]; Vowel changes in the noun may be caused (#emph[a];) by dependence on a following genitive, (#emph[b];) by connection with pronominal suffixes, (#emph[c];) by the plural and dual terminations, whether in the form of the absolute state or of the construct (before a following genitive of a noun or suffix).

#ml("b") <p92b> #strong[2.]; In all these cases, the tone of the noun is moved forward either one or two syllables, while the tone of the #emph[construct state]; may even be thrown upon the following word. In this way the following changes may arise:—

(#emph[a];) #emph[When the tone is moved forward only one place];, as is the case when the plural and dual endings #he[־ִים];, #he[וֹת]; and #he[־ַ֫ יִם]; are affixed, as well as with all monosyllabic or paroxytone suffixes, then in dissyllabic nouns the originally short vowel of the first syllable (which was lengthened as being in an open syllable before the tone) becomes #emph[Š#super[e];wâ];, since it no longer stands before the tone. On the other hand, the originally short, but tone\-lengthened vowel, of the second syllable is retained as being now the pretonic vowel; e.g. #he[דָּבָר]; #emph[word]; (ground\-form #emph[dăbăr];), plur. #he[דְּבָרִים];; with a light suffix beginning with a vowel, #he[דְּבָרִי];, #he[דְּבָרֵ֫נוּ];; plur. #he[דְּבָרַי];, #he[דְּבָרֶ֫יךָ];, etc.; #he[כָּנָף]; #emph[wing];, dual #he[כְּנָפַ֫יִם];. With an unchangeable vowel in the second syllable: #he[פָּקִיד]; #emph[overseer];, plur. #he[פְּקִידִים];; with the #emph[suffix]; of the sing. #he[פְּקִידִי];, #he[פְּקִידֵ֫נוּ];, etc.; with the #emph[suff.]; of the plur. #he[פְּקִידַי];, #he[פְּקִידֶ֫יךָ];, etc. With an unchangeable vowel in the first syllable: #he[עוֹלָם]; #emph[eternity];, plur. #he[עֽוֹלָמִים];, with #emph[suff.]; #he[עֽוֹלָמִי];, etc.#footnote[The participles #emph[Niphʿal]; #he[נִדַּֽחֲךָ]; #bref("Deut 30:4");, #he[נִדְּחוֹ]; #bref("2 Sam 14:13");, and some plurals of the participle Niph. of verbs #he[ל״א]; form an exception; cf. #xref(<p93oo>)[§ 93#h(0.18em)#emph[oo];];.];

#ml("c") <p92c> But in participles of the form #he[קֹטֵל];, with tone\-lengthened #emph[ē]; (originally #emph[ĭ];) in the second syllable, the #emph[ē]; regularly becomes #emph[Š#super[e];wâ mobile]; before a tone\-bearing affix, e.g. #he[אֹיֵב]; #emph[enemy];, plur. #he[אֹֽיְבִים];, with #emph[suff.]; #he[אֹֽיְבִי];, etc. Likewise in words of the form #he[קִטֵּל];, #he[קַטֵּל];, etc. (with #emph[ē]; in the second syllable; #xref(<p84bd>)[§ 84#super[b];#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];, l, p; #xref(<p85i>)[§ 85#h(0.18em)#emph[i];]; and k), e.g. #he[אִלֵּם]; #emph[dumb];, plur. #he[אִלְּמִים];.

#ml("d") <p92d> (#emph[b];) When the tone of the #emph[construct state];, plural or dual, is carried over to the following word, or, in consequence of the addition of the #emph[grave suffixes]; to the #emph[constr. st.]; plur. or dual, #emph[is moved forward two places]; within the word itself, in such cases the originally short vowel of the second syllable becomes #emph[Š#super[e];wâ];, while the vowel of the first syllable reverts to its original shortness, e.g. #he[דִּבְרֵי הָעָם]; #emph[the words of the people];, #he[דִּבְרֵיכֶ֫ם]; #emph[your words];, #he[דִּבְרֵיהֶ֫ם]; #emph[their words]; (in all which instances the #emph[ĭ]; of the first syllable is attenuated from an original #emph[ă];). #origpage("261") <orig-261>;

#ml("e") <p92e> In the segholate forms in the singular and mostly in the dual the suffix is appended to the ground\-form (#he[מַלְכִּי]; #emph[my king];, #he[מַלְכֵּ֫נוּ];, etc.); on the other hand, before the endings #he[־ִים];, #he[וֹת]; (sometimes also before #he[־ַ֫ יִם];) a #emph[Qameṣ]; regularly occurs,#footnote[For the rare exceptions see #xref(<p93l>)[§ 93#h(0.18em)#emph[l];]; and #xref(<p97f>)[§ 97#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];, note 2.]; before which the vowel of the first syllable then becomes vocal #emph[Š#super[e];wâ]; (#he[מְלָכִים];, #he[מְלָכוֹת];). This #emph[Qameṣ]; (on which cf. #xref(<p84aa>)[§ 84#super[a];#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];) remains even before the light suffixes, when attached to the plur. masc. (#he[מְלָכַי];, #he[מְלָכֶ֫יךָ];, etc.). On the other hand, the #emph[constr. st.]; plur. and dual, regularly, according to #emph[d];, has the form #he[מַלְכֵי];, with grave suffix #he[מַלְכֵיכֶם];, etc., #he[דַּלְתֵי]; from #he[דְּלָתַ֫יִם]; #emph[folding\-doors];.

#ml("f") <p92f> (#emph[c];) Before the #emph[Š#super[e];wâ mobile]; which precedes the suffix #he[ךָ]; when following a consonant, the #emph[a];\-sound, as a rule, is the only tone\-lengthened vowel which remains in the final syllable (being now in an open syllable #emph[before]; the tone), e.g. #he[דָּֽמְךָ֫];, #he[דְּבָֽרְךָ֫];, etc. (on the forms with #emph[ē]; in the second syllable, see #xref(<p93qq>)[§ 93#h(0.18em)#emph[qq];];); but before the grave suffixes #he[־ְכֶם]; and #he[־ְכֶן]; in the same position it reverts to its original shortness, as #he[דְּבַרְכֶם]; (#emph[d#super[e];bhărkhèm];), etc. In the same way the tone\-lengthened #emph[ā]; or #emph[ē]; of the second syllable in the #emph[constr. st.]; sing. also becomes short again, since the #emph[constr. st.]; resigns the principal tone to the following word, e.g. #he[דְּבַר אֱלֹהִים];; #he[חֲצַר הַבַּ֫יִת]; (from #he[חָצֵר];).

#ml("g") <p92g> Rem. The Masora (cf. #emph[Diqduqe ha\-ṭ#super[e];amim];, p. 37) reckons thirteen words which retain #emph[Qameṣ]; in the #emph[constr. st.];, some of which had originally #emph[â]; and therefore need not be considered. On the other hand, #he[אוּלָם]; or #he[אֻלָם]; #bref("1 Kgs 7:6");, #bref("Ezek 40:48");, etc. (in spite of the #emph[constr. st.]; plur. #he[אֻֽלַמֵּי];); #he[מִבְטָח]; #bref("Ps 65:6");, #bref("Prov 25:19");; #he[מַצָּב]; #bref("1 Sam 13:23"); (so Baer, but ed. Mant., Ginsburg, etc. #he[מַצַּב];); #he[מִשְׁקָל]; #bref("Ezra 8:30"); and #he[מַתָּן]; #bref("Prov 18:16"); are very peculiar.

#ml("h") <p92h> #strong[3.]; The vowel changes in the inflection of #emph[feminine nouns]; (§ 95) are not so considerable, since generally in the formation of the feminine either the original vowels have been retained, or they have already become #emph[Š#super[e];wâ];.

#ml("i") <p92i> Besides the vowel changes discussed above in #emph[a–g];, which take place according to the general formative laws (§§ 25–28), certain further phenomena must also be considered in the inflection of nouns, an accurate knowledge of which requires in each case an investigation of the original form of the words in question (see §§ 84–86). Such are, e.g., the rejection of the #he[ה]; of #he[ל״ה]; stems before all formative additions (cf. #xref(<p91d>)[§ 91#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];), the sharpening of the final consonant of #he[ע״ע]; stems in such cases as #he[חֹק];, #he[חֻקִּי];, etc.

#ml("k") <p92k> A striking difference between the vowel changes in the verb and noun is that in a verb when terminations are added it is mostly the second of two changeable vowels which becomes #emph[Š#super[e];wâ]; (#he[קָטַל];, #he[קָֽטְלָה];, #he[קָֽטְלוּ];), but in a noun, the first (#he[דָּבָר];, #he[דְּבָרִי];, #he[דְּבָרִים];), cf. § 27. 3. #origpage("262") <orig-262>;

#sec("93", [Paradigms of Masculine Nouns], note: [#footnote[A sort of detailed commentary on the following scheme of Hebrew declensions is supplied by E. König in his #emph[Hist.\-krit. Lehrgeb. der hebr. Spr.];, it. 1, p. 1 ff.];]) <s93>

#ml("a") <p93a> Masculine nouns from the simple stem may, as regards their form and the vowel changes connected with it, be divided into #emph[four]; classes. A synopsis of them is given on pp. #xref(<orig-264>)[264];, #xref(<orig-265>)[265];, and they are further explained below. Two general remarks may be premised:

(#emph[a];) That all feminines without a distinctive termination (#xref(<p122h>)[§ 122#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];) are treated like these masculine nouns, e.g. #he[חֶ֫רֶב]; #emph[f.]; #emph[sword];, like #he[מֶ֫לֶךְ]; #emph[m.]; #emph[king];, except that in the #emph[plural]; they usually take the termination #he[־וֹת];; thus #he[חֲרָבוֹת];, #emph[constr.]; #he[חַרְבוֹת]; (and so always before #emph[suffixes];, see #xref(<s95>)[§ 95];).

#ml("b") <p93b> (#emph[b];) That in the plural of the first three classes a changeable vowel is always retained even before the light suffixes as a lengthened pretonic vowel, whenever it also stands before the plural ending #he[־ִים];. All suffixes, except #he[כֶם];, #he[כֶן];, #he[הֶם];, #he[הֶן]; (#he[־ֵיכֶם];, #he[־ֵיכֶן];, #he[־ֵיהֶם];, #he[־ֵיהֶן];), are called #emph[light];. Cf. #xref(<p92e>)[§ 92#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];.

#align(center)[#emph[Explanations of the Paradigms (see pp. #xref(<orig-264>)[264];, #xref(<orig-265>)[265];).];];

#ml("c") <p93c> #strong[1.]; Paradigm I comprises the large class of #emph[segholate]; nouns (#xref(<p84aa>)[§ 84#super[a];#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];–#xref(<p84aa>)[#emph[a];];). In the first three examples, from a strong stem, the ground\-forms, #emph[mălk];, #emph[sĭphr];, #emph[qŭdš]; have been developed by the adoption of a helping #emph[S#super[e];ghôl]; to #he[מֶ֫לֶךְ]; (with #emph[ă]; modified to #emph[è];), #he[סֵ֫פֶר]; (#emph[ĭ]; lengthened to #emph[ē];), #he[קֹ֫דֶשׁ]; (#emph[ŭ]; lengthened to #emph[ō];).#footnote[According to P. Haupt ‘The book of Nahum’ in the #emph[Journ. of bibl. Lit.];, 1907, p. 29, the #emph[e]; in #he[סֵ֫פֶר]; and the #emph[o]; in #he[קֹ֫דֶשׁ]; are not long but accented, and hence to be pronounced #greek[σέφρ];, #greek[ὄζν]; (#he[אֹ֫זֶן];), a theory unknown at any rate to the Jewish grammarians.]; The next three examples, instead of the helping #emph[S#super[e];ghôl];, have a helping #emph[Pathaḥ];, on account of the middle (#emph[d, f];) or final guttural (#emph[e];). In all these cases the #emph[constr. st. sing.]; coincides exactly with the #emph[absolute];. The singular suffixes are added to the ground\-form; but in #emph[c]; and #emph[f]; an #emph[ŏ]; takes the place of the original #emph[ŭ];, and in #emph[d]; and #emph[f]; the guttural requires a repetition of the #emph[ă]; and #emph[ŏ]; in the form of a #emph[Ḥaṭeph]; (#he[נַֽעֲרִי];, #he[פָּֽעֳלִי];); before a following #emph[Š#super[e];wâ]; this #emph[Ḥaṭeph]; passes into a simple helping vowel (#emph[ă];, #emph[ŏ];), according to #xref(<p28c>)[§ 28#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];; hence #he[נַֽעַרְךָ];, etc.

#ml("d") <p93d> In the plural an #emph[a];\-sound almost always appears before the tone\-bearing affix #he[־ִים]; (on the analogy of forms with original #emph[a]; in the #origpage("263") <orig-263>;second syllable; cf. #xref(<p84aa>)[§ 84#super[a];#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];), in the form of a pretonic #emph[Qameṣ];, while the short vowel of the first syllable becomes vocal #emph[Š#super[e];wâ];. The original #emph[a]; of the 2nd syllable is elided in the #emph[construct state];, so that the short vowel under the first radical then stands in a closed syllable. The omission of #emph[Dageš]; in a following #emph[Begadkephath]; (#he[מַלְכֵי];, not #he[מַלְכֵּי];, etc.) is due to the loss of a vowel between #he[ל]; and #he[כ‍];. On the other hand, the pretonic #emph[Qames]; of the #emph[absolute state]; is retained before the light plural suffixes, while the grave suffixes are added to the form of the #emph[construct state];.—The ending of the #emph[absolute state]; of the dual is added, as a rule, to the ground\-form (so in #emph[a–d]; and #emph[h];, but cf. #emph[k];). The #emph[construct state]; of the dual is generally the same as that of the plural, except, of course, in cases like #emph[m];.

#ml("e") <p93e> Paradigms #emph[g]; and #emph[h]; exhibit forms with middle #emph[u]; and #emph[i]; (#xref(<p84ac>)[§ 84#super[a];#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];, γ and δ); the ground forms #emph[maut]; and #emph[zait]; are always contracted to #emph[môth];, #emph[zêth];, except in the #emph[absol. sing.];, where #emph[u]; and #emph[i]; are changed into the corresponding consonants #he[ו]; and #he[י];.

Paradigm #emph[i]; exhibits one of the numerous forms in which the contraction of a middle #emph[u]; or #emph[i]; has already taken place in the #emph[absol. sing.]; (ground\-form #emph[šauṭ];).

Paradigm #emph[k]; is a formation from a stem #he[ל״ה]; (#xref(<p84ac>)[§ 84#super[a];#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];, ε).

#ml("f") <p93f> Paradigms #emph[l];, #emph[m];, #emph[n]; are forms from stems #he[ע״ע];, and hence (see #xref(<p67a>)[§ 67#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];) originally biliteral, #emph[yam];, #emph[ʾim];, #emph[ḥuq];, with the regular lengthening to #he[יָם];, #he[אֵם];, #he[חֹק];. Before formative additions a sharpening, as in the inflection of verbs #he[ע״ע];, takes place in the second radical, e.g. #he[אִמִּי];, #he[יַמִּים];, etc. (see #xref(<p84ac>)[§ 84#super[a];#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];, β).

#align(center)[#sc[Remarks.];];

#ml("g") <p93g> 1. A. On I. #emph[a]; and #emph[d]; (ground\-form #emph[qaṭl];). In #emph[pause]; the full lengthening to #emph[ā]; generally takes place, thus #he[כָּ֫רֶם]; #emph[vineyard];, #he[נָ֫עַר];, #he[זָ֫רַע]; #emph[seed]; (from #he[זֶ֫רַע];), and so always (except #bref("Ps 48:11");), in #he[אֶ֫רֶץ]; #emph[earth]; with the article, #he[הָאָ֫רֶץ];, according to #xref(<p35o>)[§ 35#h(0.18em)#emph[o];]; (cf. also in the LXX the forms #greek[Ἀβέλ];, #greek[Ἰαφέθ]; for #he[הֶ֫בֶל];, #he[יֶ֫פֶת];). However, the form with #emph[è]; is also sometimes found in pause, along with that in #emph[ā];, e.g. #he[חֶ֫סֶד]; together with #he[חָ֫סֶד];; and very frequently only the form with #emph[S#super[e];ghôl];, e.g. #he[מֶ֫לֶךְ];, #he[דֶּ֫שֶׁא]; #emph[grass];, #he[נֶ֫צַח]; #emph[perpetuity];, #he[פֶּ֫לֶא]; #emph[a wonder];, #he[צֶ֫דֶק]; #emph[righteousness];, #he[קֶ֫דֶם]; #emph[the East];, #he[יֶ֫שַׁע]; #emph[help];, etc.—With two #emph[S#super[e];ghôls];, although with a middle guttural, we find #he[לֶ֫חֶם]; #emph[bread]; (in #emph[pause]; #he[לָ֫חֶם];) and #he[רֶ֫חֶם]; #emph[womb]; (in #emph[pause]; #he[רָ֫חֶם];), besides #he[רַ֫חַם]; #bref("Judg 5:30"); (in #emph[pause]; #he[רָ֫חַם];). A helping #emph[S#super[e];ghôl]; always stands before a final #he[א];, as #he[דֶּ֫שֶׁא];, #he[טֶ֫נֶא]; (with #emph[suff.]; #he[טַנְאֲךָ];), #he[פֶּ֫לֶא];, #he[פֶּ֫רֶא]; (also written #he[פֶּ֫רֶה];), except in #he[גַּיְא];, see #emph[v];.

#ml("h") <p93h> B. The #emph[constr. st.]; is almost always the same as the #emph[absolute];. Sometimes, however, under the influence of a final guttural or #he[ר];, Pathaḥ appears in the second syllable as the principal vowel (see below, #xref(<p93s>)[§ 93#h(0.18em)#emph[s];];), e.g. #he[גְּבַר]; #bref("Ps 18:26");; #he[זְרַע]; #origpage("264") <orig-264>;

