#align(center)[#sc[Remarks.];];

#ml("d") <p95d> 1. Paradigm #strong[I];: feminines of segholate forms. (#emph[a];) The locative of this class has the form #he[גִּבְעָ֫תָה]; #emph[towards Gibeah]; (masc. #he[גֶּ֫בַע];). In some cases, especially with an initial guttural, there is no means of deciding whether the form in question is to be referred to a #emph[qăṭl]; or a #emph[qĭṭl]; base, e.g. #he[חֶזְקָה]; #emph[strength]; (cf. #he[חֶרְפָּה]; under #emph[b];). A #emph[dual]; of this form occurs in #he[שִׁבְעָתַ֫יִם]; #emph[seven times]; (cf. #he[שֶׁ֫בַע]; #emph[seven];, fem.). Analogous to masculine forms like #he[דְּבַשׁ]; (#xref(<p93s>)[§ 93#h(0.18em)#emph[s];];) is #he[הֲדַסָּה]; #emph[myrtle];.—From masculines of the form #he[פְּרִי]; (#he[ל״ה];, cf. #xref(<p93I>)[§ 93#h(0.18em)#emph[I];];, k) arise feminines sometimes like #he[גַּֽאֲוָה];, #he[שַׁלְוָה];, #he[אַלְיָה]; (see above, #xref(<p94b>)[§ 94#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];), sometimes like #he[בְּכִית]; (#xref(<p94f>)[§ 94#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];); occasionally the final #he[ת]; is retained before the plural ending, as if it belonged to the stem (cf. #xref(<p87k>)[§ 87#h(0.18em)#emph[k];];), e.g. #he[חֲנִיתוֹת]; #emph[spears];. Forms like #he[גְּדִיָּה]; (cf. #he[אֳנִיָּה];, a #emph[qŭṭl]; form) are derived directly from the masculine forms #he[גְּדִי]; #emph[kid];, #he[אֳנִי];, #emph[a fleet];.—(#emph[b];) From a stem #he[ע״ן];, #he[חִטָּה]; #emph[wheat]; (for #he[חִנְטָה];), #emph[plur.]; #he[חִטִּים];.—(#emph[c];) From #he[עָרְלָה]; #emph[foreskin];, the #emph[plur. absol.]; is #he[עֲרָלוֹת]; (cf. #he[פְּעָלִים];, § 93, Paradigm #strong[I];, #emph[f];), #emph[constr.]; #he[עָרְלוֹת];.—(#emph[d];) Example of a feminine segholate form from a stem #he[ע״ע]; (ground\-form #emph[qŭṭl];, like #he[חַיָּה]; of the form #emph[qăṭl,];, #he[זִמָּה]; of the form #emph[qĭṭl];), with #emph[ŏ]; for #emph[ŭ];, #he[חָגָּא]; #emph[terror];, #bref("Isa 19:17"); (Aramaic orthography for #he[חָגָּה];).

#ml("e") <p95e> (#emph[e];) To the list of segholate forms with #he[ת]; fem. belong also the infinitives of verbs #he[פ״ו]; and #he[פ״ן];, which have rejected the weak consonant at the beginning, as #he[שֶׁ֫בֶת]; (from #he[יָשַׁב];), #he[דַּ֫עַת]; (from #he[יָדַע];), #he[גֶּ֫שֶׁת]; (from #he[נָגַשׁ];), as well as #he[קַ֫חַת]; (from #he[לָקַח];); cf. #xref(<p69m>)[§ 69#h(0.18em)#emph[m];]; and #xref(<p66b>)[§ 66#h(0.18em)#emph[b];]; and g. The infinitives of verbs #he[פ״ו]; are, however, also found in the form #he[דֵּעָה];, #he[לֵדָה];, #he[צֵאָה];, and of the same origin also are #he[עֵדָה]; #emph[congregation]; (from #he[יָעַד];), #he[עֵצָה]; #emph[counsel]; (from #he[יָעַץ];), #he[שֵׁנָה]; #emph[sleep]; (from #he[יָשֵׁן];), #emph[constr.]; #he[עֲדַת];, #he[שְׁנַת];, while in the #emph[constr.]; forms #he[זֵעַת]; #emph[sweat];, #bref("Gen 3:19"); (from #he[יָזַע]; #emph[to flow];), and #he[צֵאַת]; #emph[excrement];, #bref("Ezek 4:12");, the Ṣere has remained firm.

#ml("f") <p95f> From a stem #he[ע״וּ]; (cf. #he[בּוֹשׁ]; #emph[to be ashamed];) is #he[בּ֫שֶׁת]; #emph[shame];, with #emph[suffix]; #he[בָּשְׁתִּי];. #origpage("279") <orig-279>;From a stem #he[ל״ה]; (#he[דָּלָה];, cf., however, Barth, #emph[ZDMG.]; 1887, p. 607, who assumes a stem #he[ידל];) the masculine #he[דַּל]; appears to have been formed after the rejection of the final #emph[Yôdh];, and afterwards the feminine #he[דֶּ֫לֶת]; #emph[door];; but in the #emph[plural]; #he[דְּלָתוֹת];, #emph[constr.]; #he[דַּלְתוֹת];, the #he[ת]; of the termination is retained (see above, d, #he[חֲנִיתוֹת];). In a similar way #he[רְפָתִים]; #emph[stalls];, #bref("Hab 3:17");, has arisen, if it is from the stem #he[רפה];, and #he[שֹׁקֶת]; #emph[trough]; (from #he[שָׁקָה];), of which the masc. must have been #he[שֹׁק]; \= #he[שֳׁקִי];; on the other hand, the #emph[plur. constr.]; #he[שִֽׁקֲתוֹת]; #bref("Gen 30:38"); (again retaining the feminine #he[ת]; as an apparent radical) can only be an abnormal formation from the #emph[singular]; #he[שֹׁ֫קֶת];, not from a kindred form #he[שֶׁ֫קֶת]; or #he[שֵׁ֫קֶת];.

#ml("g") <p95g> 2. Paradigm II: ground\-form #emph[qăṭălăt];, etc., cf. #xref(<p94c>)[§ 94#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];, Paradigm II, #emph[a]; and #emph[b];. Analogous to the masculine forms like #he[קָטָן];, plur. #he[קְטַנִּים];, we find #he[קְטַנָּה]; #emph[parva];, etc.—The #emph[constr.]; forms, like #he[צִדְקַת]; (#emph[ṣidh#super[e];qăth];), are distinguished by the vocal #emph[Š#super[e];wâ]; (#xref(<p10d>)[§ 10#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];) from the segholate forms, like #he[כִּבְשַׂת]; (#emph[kibh\-săth];). Consequently the #emph[constr. st.]; #he[בִּרְכַּת]; #bref("Gen 28:4");, etc. (from #he[בְּרָכָה]; #emph[blessing];), and #he[חֶרְדַּת]; #bref("1 Sam 14:15");, etc. (from #he[חֲרָדָה]; #emph[a trembling];), are abnormal.—Under the influence of a guttural (see Paradigm #emph[b];) the original #emph[ă]; is retained in the first syllable in the #emph[constr. st.]; (cf. also #he[אֲדָמָה]; #emph[earth];, #he[אַדְמַת];); in other cases it is modified to #emph[S#super[e];ghôl];, e.g. #he[עֲגָלָה]; #emph[wagon];, #he[עֶגְלָתוֹ];. Frequently from an #emph[absol. st.]; in #he[־ָה]; the #emph[constr.]; is formed with the termination #he[ת];, e.g. #he[עֲטָרָה]; #emph[crown];, constr. #he[עֲטֶ֫רֶת]; (from #he[עֲטַרְתְּ];); along with #he[עֲצָרָה]; #emph[assembly];, #he[עֲצֶ֫רֶת]; is found usually, even in the #emph[absol. st.];; #he[יְבֶ֫מֶת]; (from #he[יָבָם]; #emph[levir];) before suffixes is pointed as in #he[יְבִמְתִּי];, and thus entirely agrees with #he[גְּבֶ֫רֶת]; (Paradigm I #emph[e];). From a stem #he[(אָמַן) ל״ן]; is formed #he[אֱמֶת]; #emph[truth]; (from #emph[ʾaɨmant];, and this no doubt for an original #emph[ʾămint];, #xref(<p69c>)[§ 69#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];) before suffixes #he[אֲמִתִּי];, etc.

#ml("h") <p95h> From the masc. form #he[קָטֵל]; (#emph[qăṭĭl];) are formed, according to rule, #he[גְּדֵרָה]; #emph[wall];, #he[נְבֵלָה]; #emph[corpse];, constr. #he[נִבְלַת];; #he[בְּהֵמָה]; #emph[cattle];, constr. #he[בֶּֽהֱמַת]; (for #he[בַּֽהֲמַת];), with suffix #he[בְּהֶמְתְּךָ]; #bref("Lev 19:19");. More frequently, however, the #emph[ē]; of the second syllable is retained before the termination #emph[ath]; of the #emph[constr. st.];; thus from #he[נְבֵלָה]; once #he[נְבֵֽלָתִי]; #bref("Isa 26:19");, and always #he[בְּרֵכַת]; #emph[pool];, #he[גְּזֵלַת]; #emph[prey];, #he[טְמֵאַת]; #emph[unclean];, #he[מְלֵֽאֲתִי]; #emph[full];, #bref("Isa 1:21"); (with #emph[Ḥireq compaginis];, see #xref(<p90l>)[§ 90#h(0.18em)#emph[l];];), #he[מְרֵֽרָתִי]; #bref("Job 16:13");; #he[שְׁאֵֽלָתִי]; #bref("1 Sam 1:27");, etc. (with elision of the #he[א];, #he[שְׁלָתֵךְ]; #bref("1 Sam 1:17");), also #he[שֶֽׁאֱלָתִי]; #bref("Job 6:8");. Cf. the analogous forms of the #emph[constr. st.]; #he[מַגֵּפַת]; #emph[plague];, #he[תַּרְדֵּמַת]; #emph[deep sleep];, from #he[מַגֵּפָה];, #he[תַּרְדֵּמָה];.

#ml("i") <p95i> As dual we find #he[יַרְכָתַ֫יִם]; #emph[sides]; (cf. #he[יַרְכָתוֹ]; #bref("Gen 49:13");, from the obsolete #he[יְרֵכָה];, feminine of #he[יָרֵךְ];); the #emph[constr. st.]; #he[יַרְכְּתֵי]; is perhaps to be referred to a segholate form (#he[יַרְכָּה];, cf. #he[יֶ֫רֶךְ]; as #emph[constr. st.]; of #he[יָרֵךְ];), unless the closed syllable be due to the analogy of #he[בִּרְכַּת]; and #he[חֶרְדַּת]; (see g).

#ml("k") <p95k> In the forms with simple #he[ת]; feminine the ground\-form #emph[qăṭĭlt]; is developed (#xref(<p69c>)[§ 69#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];) to #emph[q#super[e];ṭalt];, and this again regularly to #he[קְטֶ֫לֶת];. Thus the feminine of #he[חָבֵר]; #emph[companion]; is #he[חֲבֶ֫רֶת]; (with suffix #he[חֲבֶרְתָּהּ]; #bref("Mal 2:14");, cf. #he[שְׁכֶנְתָּהּ]; #bref("Exod 3:22");), of #he[גָּדֵר]; fem. #he[גְּדֶ֫רֶת]; besides #he[גְּדֵרָה];.—Of #he[ע״וּ]; stems the segholate forms #he[נַ֫חַת]; #emph[rest]; and #he[שַׁ֫חַת]; #emph[pit]; (from #he[נוּחַ];, #he[שׁוּחַ];) belong to this class; Böttcher (#emph[Gram.]; i. 411) rightly distinguished the latter from #he[שַׁ֫חַת]; #emph[corruption]; (stem #he[שָׁחַת];); in the same way also #he[נַ֫חַת]; #emph[rest]; is distinct from #he[נַ֫חַת]; #emph[a lighting down]; (stem #he[נָחַת];).

#ml("l") <p95l> The feminines of the form #emph[qăṭĭl]; from stems #he[ע״וּ];, as #he[מֵתָה]; #emph[mortua];, #he[עֵדָה]; fem. #origpage("280") <orig-280>;#emph[witness]; (from #he[מוּת];, #he[עוּד];), have likewise an unchangeable vowel in the first syllable. Cf., on the other hand, the forms from #he[פ״י]; stems mentioned above, under e, such as #he[שֵׁנָה]; #emph[sleep];, constr. st. #he[שְׁנַת];; moreover, #he[חֵמָה]; #emph[anger];, constr. st. #he[חֲמַת]; (but #he[חֵ֫מֶת]; #emph[a leathern bottle];, in #emph[pause]; #he[חֶ֑מֶת]; \[so Baer, Ginsb., but Kittel #he[חֵ׳];\] #bref("Gen 21:15");, #emph[constr. st.]; #he[חֵ֫מַת מַ֫יִם]; #bref("Gen 21:14");, perhaps from a stem #he[חמת];).

#ml("m") <p95m> The feminines of the form #emph[qăṭŭl];, like #he[עֲמֻקָּה]; (masc. #he[עָמֹק];), maintain the original #emph[ŭ]; by sharpening the following consonant (cf. #xref(<p93kk>)[§ 93#h(0.18em)#emph[kk];];); on the other hand, by appending the fem. #he[ת];, segholate forms arise like #he[נְחֹ֫שֶׁת];, before suff. #he[נְחֻשְׁתָּם];, etc. Dual #he[נְחֻשְׁתַּ֫יִם]; (see Paradigm II #emph[a];); but cf. #he[נְחָשְׁתִּי]; #bref("Lam 3:7");.

#ml("n") <p95n> A few (aramaising) feminines from #he[ל״ה]; stems (Paradigm II, #emph[c];) are found with the ending #emph[âth];, due to the rejection of the final #emph[Wāw]; or #emph[Yôdh]; and contraction of the preceding #emph[ă]; with the #emph[ă]; of the termination #emph[ăth];; thus #he[מְנָת]; #emph[portion]; (for #emph[mănăyăth]; or #emph[mănăwăth];), #he[קְצָת]; end (also #he[קָצֶה]; and #he[קָצָה];), plur. #he[מְנָיוֹת]; (#emph[constr. st.]; #bref("Neh 12:47");, #bref("13:10");) and #he[מְנָאוֹת]; (#bref("Neh 12:44");); #he[קְצָוֹת]; #bref("Exod 38:5");; cf. #bref("37:8"); and #bref("39:4"); #emph[K#super[e];th.];; on #he[גֵּֽאָיֹת]; #emph[valleys];, see #xref(<p93v>)[§ 93#h(0.18em)#emph[v];];.—#he[אוֹת]; #emph[sign]; (stem #he[אוה];) is obscured from #he[אָת];, and this is contracted from #emph[ʾâyăth]; \= #emph[ʾăwăyăth];; plur. #he[אֹתוֹת];, with the double feminine ending; cf. above, f, and #xref(<p87k>)[§ 87#h(0.18em)#emph[k];];.—The retention of the #emph[ā]; in the first syllable in #he[אָֽלָתִי];, etc., #bref("Gen 24:41");, etc., is abnormal.

#ml("o") <p95o> 3. Paradigm III, cf. the various forms in #xref(<p94d>)[§ 94#h(0.18em)#emph[d];]; and f–h. The #emph[dual]; #he[חֽוֹמֹתַ֫יִם]; #emph[two walls];, #bref("Isa 22:11");, etc., taken directly from the plur. #he[חוֹמוֹת];, for #he[חֽוֹמָתַ֫יִם];, is abnormal (cf. #xref(<p87s>)[§ 87#h(0.18em)#emph[s];];, and the proper name #he[גְּדֵֽרֹתַיִם]; #bref("Josh 15:36");).—Among the forms resembling participles #emph[Qal]; of verbs #he[ע״וּ];, such as #he[זָרָה]; (masc. #he[זָר]; from #emph[zâĭr];, hence with unchangeable #emph[â];), must be reckoned also #he[בָּמָה]; #emph[high place]; (from #he[בּוּם];), which has for its #emph[constr. st. plur.]; the pleonastic form #he[בָּֽמוֹתֵי];, or written defectively #he[בָּֽמֹתֵי]; (see #xref(<p87s>)[§ 87#h(0.18em)#emph[s];];); for this the Masora everywhere requires #he[בָּֽמֳתֵי];, which is to be read #emph[bām#super[o];thê]; (not #emph[bŏm#super[o];thê];), with an anomalous shortening of the #emph[ô]; to #he[־ֳ];; but with suffixes #he[בָּֽמוֹתַי];, etc.

#ml("p") <p95p> In a wider sense the feminines of the form #he[קַטָּל]; (#xref(<p84be>)[§ 84#super[b];#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];) belong to this class, in so far as they shorten the #emph[â]; of the second syllable before the termination #he[ת];, e.g. #he[דַּלֶּ֫קֶת]; #emph[inflammation]; (from #emph[dallăqt];), with suff. #he[צַדֶּקְתֵּךְ]; #bref("Ezek 16:52");; #he[טַבַּ֫עַת]; #emph[signet];; also fem. of the forms #he[קִטַּל]; and #he[קִטֵּל]; (#xref(<p84bc>)[§ 84#super[b];#h(0.18em)#emph[c];]; and d), as #he[אִוֶּ֫לֶת]; #emph[folly]; (for #emph[ʾiwwălt];), and of all the forms which have a changeable vowel in the second syllable, and are formed with the prefix #he[מ‍]; (#xref(<p85g>)[§ 85#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];–k), e.g. #he[מַמְלָכָה]; #emph[kingdom];, #emph[constr.]; always #he[מַמְלֶ֫כֶת];; #he[מַזְמֵרָה]; (not used in the sing.) #emph[pruning\-hook];, plur. #he[מַזְמֵרוֹת];; #he[מַשְׂכֹּ֫רֶת]; #emph[reward];, with suff. #he[מַשְׂכֻּרְתִּי];; cf. also the examples given in #xref(<p85g>)[§ 85#h(0.18em)#emph[g];]; and p, like #he[מוֹלֶ֫דֶת]; #emph[birth]; (but from #he[ל״א];, #he[מֽוֹצָאָה]; #emph[outgoing];), #he[תּוֹלֶ֫דֶת]; #emph[generation];, #he[תּֽוֹעֵבָה]; #emph[abomination];, constr. #he[תּֽוֹעֲבַת];, etc.

#ml("q") <p95q> Sometimes the plural of these forms is to be traced to a secondary form, e.g. #he[אִגֶּ֫רֶת]; #emph[a letter];, plur. #he[אִגְּרוֹת]; (as if from #he[אִגְּרָה];); also #he[יֽוֹנְקוֹת];, which is merely formed on the analogy of the other plur. fem. of participles #emph[Qal];, is to be referred to a sing. #he[יוֹנְקָה];. Cf., moreover, #he[מַֽחֲרֶ֫שֶׁת]; #emph[ploughshare];, plur. #he[מַֽחֲרֵשׁוֹת]; (as if from #he[מַֽחֲרֵשָׁה];)#footnote[#he[עַשְׁתֹּ֫רֶת]; #emph[Astarte]; (plur. #he[עַשְׁתָּרוֹת];), which was formerly included among these examples, is most probably due to an intentional alteration of the original #he[עַשְׁתֶּ֫רֶת];, like #he[מֹ֫לֶךְ]; #bref("Lev 18:21");, etc. (for #he[מֶ֫לֶךְ];), with the vowels of #he[בּ֫שֶׁת]; shame, the latter word being substituted in reading for the name of the goddess.];; on the other hand, #he[כֹּֽתָרוֹת]; #emph[capitals]; (of columns), and #he[תּֽוֹכָחוֹת]; #emph[reproofs];, are the regular plurals of #he[כֹּתֶ֫רֶת]; and #he[תּוֹכַ֫חַת];. #origpage("281") <orig-281>;

#ml("r") <p95r> In #he[כֻּתּ֫נֶת]; #emph[coat]; the original #emph[ŭ]; of the first syllable is maintained by the sharpening of the following consonant (cf. Arab. #emph[qŭṭŭn];), with suff. #he[כֻּתָּנְתִּי];, the #emph[constr. st.];, however, is #he[כְּתֹ֫נֶת]; (as also in the #emph[absol. st.]; in #bref("Exod 28:39");); plur. #he[כֻּתֳּנוֹת];, #emph[constr.]; #he[כָּתְנוֹת];.—The form #he[גֻּלְגֹּ֫לֶת]; given in Paradigm III, #emph[b]; is a #emph[Pŭlpŭl];\-form of the stem #he[גָּלַל];, cf. #he[קָדְקֹד];, #xref(<p84bp>)[§ 84#super[b];#h(0.18em)#emph[p];];.

#ml("s") <p95s> 4. To the fourth class, for which no Paradigm is required, belong all the numerous forms which in classical Hebrew have unchangeable vowels throughout, the originally short vowel of the first syllable having become #emph[Š#super[e];wâ];, owing to the tone being thrown forward. Of the forms mentioned in #xref(<s84>)[§§ 84]; and #xref(<s85>)[85]; those from #he[ע״ע]; stems especially belong to this class, as #he[מְגִלָּה]; #emph[scroll];, #he[תְּהִלָּה]; #emph[praise];, #he[תְּפִלָּה]; #emph[prayer]; (#xref(<p85i>)[§ 85#h(0.18em)#emph[i];]; and #xref(<p85q>)[#emph[q];];), as well as the feminine of the participle #emph[Hiphʿîl]; of verbs #he[ע״וּ];, e.g. #he[מְאִירָה]; #emph[enlightening]; (from #he[מֵאִיר];), and generally the feminines of #he[ע״וּ]; stems which are compounded with the #emph[preformative]; #he[מ‍];, as #he[מְנוּחָה]; #emph[rest]; (from #he[מָנוֹחַ];), see #xref(<p85l>)[§ 85#h(0.18em)#emph[l];];; from #he[ל״ה]; stems perhaps also #he[תְּעָלָה]; #emph[conduit]; (#emph[constr. st.]; #he[תְּעָלַת]; #bref("Isa 7:3");, etc.) and #he[תְּלָאָה]; #emph[travail];. Thus all these forms coincide externally with those which already, in the masculine form, have unchangeable vowels throughout (see the list of them in #xref(<p93ww>)[§ 93#h(0.18em)#emph[ww];];).

#ml("t") <p95t> 5. The feminine ending #he[־ִית]; (apart from #he[ל״ה];\-forms like #he[בְּכִית];, #xref(<p94f>)[§ 94#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];) arises from the addition of the feminine #he[ת]; to the ending #he[־ִי];, which is employed to form adjectives, etc., see #xref(<p86d>)[§ 86#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];, #xref(<p86h>)[#emph[h];];, and #xref(<p86k>)[#emph[k];];. The ending #he[וּת];, mentioned there, is attached, in segholate forms, sometimes to the ground\-form, as #he[עַשְׁתּוּת]; #bref("Job 12:5"); (v.l. #he[עַשְׁתּוֹת];), sometimes to forms with a loosely\-closed syllable, as #he[מַלְכוּת]; #emph[kingdom];; from #he[ל״ה]; stems we find forms sometimes like #he[שְׁבוּת]; #emph[captivity]; (according to others from the stem #he[שׁוּב];, like #he[לְזוּת]; #emph[perverseness]; from #he[לוּז];), sometimes like #he[בָּכוּת]; #emph[weeping];, #he[גָּלוּת]; #emph[exile];, #he[חָזוּת]; #emph[vision];; the latter retain the #emph[ā]; of the first syllable even in the #emph[constr. st.]; and before #emph[suffixes];. From a #emph[qăṭĭl];\-form is formed #he[כְּבֵדוּת]; #emph[heaviness];; from a #emph[qăṭîl];\-form #he[פְּקִדוּת];, etc.

#ml("u") <p95u> In the plural of these forms different methods of treatment may be distinguished. In some cases the whole ending #he[וּת]; is retained, as if belonging to the stem (cf. above, f), e.g. #he[אַלְמְנוּתַ֫יִךְ]; from #he[אַלְמָנוּת];, in others this ending is resolved, as in #he[מַלְכֻיּוֹת]; #bref("Dan 8:22"); (no doubt for #emph[măl#super[e];khuwwôth];), and #he[עֵֽדְוֺת]; #emph[ʿēdh#super[e];wōth];, from #he[עֵדוּת]; #emph[testimony];, but only with suffixes, #he[עֵֽדְוֺתֶ֫יךָ]; #bref("Ps 119:14");, etc.; #he[עֵֽדְוֺתָיו]; #bref("1 Kgs 2:3");, etc.

#sec("96", [Nouns of Peculiar Formation], note: []) <s96>

In the following Paradigms,#footnote[The only omissions from these Paradigms are #he[אֶחָד];, #he[חָם];, and #he[חָמוֹת]; (on which see the remarks), and all forms which are not found in the O.T.]; pp. #xref(<orig-282>)[282]; to #xref(<orig-284>)[284];, a number of frequently used nouns are arranged, whose flexion presents more or less striking peculiarities. These peculiarities, however, are almost always subordinate to the usual phonetic laws, and the usual designation of the nouns as #emph[irregular]; is, therefore, not justified, when once the ground\-forms are properly recognized on which the present forms are based. #origpage("282") <orig-282>;

