#bref("Zech 11:7");; and especially before #he[מִן]; (#he[מֵ‍];) #bref("Gen 3:22");, #bref("Exod 30:14");, #bref("Num 16:15");, #bref("Judg 17:5");, #bref("1 Sam 9:3");, #bref("Ezek 18:10");; fem. #he[אַחַת]; #emph[una]; (for #he[אַחַדְתְּ];, according to #xref(<p19d>)[§ 19#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];), in #emph[pause]; #he[אֶחָֽת];. Once #he[חַד]; masc. (by aphaeresis, #xref(<p19h>)[§ 19#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];), #bref("Ezek 33:30");, as in Aramaic; plur. #he[אֲחָדִים]; #emph[some];, but also #emph[iidem];.

#he[אָחוֹת]; #emph[sister];, from #emph[ʾăḥăwăt]; or #emph[ʾăḥăyăt];, with elision of the #he[ו]; or #he[י];, and with the #emph[â];, which has arisen from #emph[ăă];, obscured to #emph[ô];.#footnote[This explanation of #he[אָחוֹת]; (and #he[חָמוֹת]; q. v.) still seems to us more probable than the assumption that the fem. ending #emph[ăth]; is lengthened to compensate for the loss of the 3rd radical (so Wellhausen, #emph[Skizzen];, vi. 258), or that the form is derived from #emph[ʾaḥâ];, the old\-semitic #emph[constr. st.]; of the accusative, with #he[ת]; feminine (so Barth, #emph[ZDMG.]; 1899, p. 598).]; In #bref("Num 6:7"); #he[אַחֹתוֹ]; stands for #he[אֲחֹתוֹ]; (with virtual sharpening of the #he[ח];). The plur. #emph[absol.]; (#he[אֲחָיוֹת];) does not happen #origpage("284") <orig-284>;

