to occur. In #bref("Ezek 16:52"); #he[אַֽחֲיוֹתֵךְ]; occurs (for #he[אַחְיֹתַ֫יִךְ];). In the forms #he[אֲחוֹתַי]; #bref("Josh 2:13"); #emph[K#super[e];th.];, #he[אֲחוֹתַ֫יִךְ]; #bref("Ezek 16:51, 55, 61"); (to be read also in verse 45 for #he[אֲחוֹתֵךְ];, which has been erroneously assimilated to the singular occurring in vv. 48, 49, 56), and #he[אֲחֽוֹתֵיכֶם]; #bref("Hos 2:3"); (for which, however, read #he[אֲחֽוֹתְכֶם];), the third radical has been entirely lost.

#he[אִישׁ]; #emph[man];, according to the common opinion either incorrectly lengthened for #he[אֵשׁ]; (from #emph[ʾišš];, with assimilation of the #emph[Nûn]; of the ground\-form #emph[ʾinš];, which again has been attenuated from #emph[ʾanš]; from the stem #he[אָנַשׁ];), or softened directly from #emph[ʾinš];. It is, however, probable that a separate stem (#he[אישׁ]; #emph[to be strong];?) is to be assumed for the singular#footnote[So already Gesenius in his #emph[Thes. linguae Hebr.];, i. 83 f., and recently again Friedr. Delitzsch, #emph[Prolegg.];, p. 160 ff., Praetorius in Kuhn’s #emph[Orient. L.\-B.];, 1884, p. 196; König, #emph[Lehrgeb];., ii. 38; while Nöldeke (#emph[ZDMG.]; 1886, p. 739 f.), against Delitzsch, would connect both #he[אִישׁ]; and #he[נָשִׁים]; with the stem #he[אנשׁ];.];; consequently the stem #he[אָנַשׁ]; #emph[to be sociable];, #origpage("285") <orig-285>;would be connected only with the plur. #he[אֲנָשִׁים]; (#he[אִישִׁים]; is found only in #bref("Isa 53:3");, #bref("Ps 141:4");, #bref("Prov 8:4");).

#he[אָמָה]; #emph[slave, handmaid];; with the plur. #he[אֲמָהוֹת];, with consonantal #he[ה];, cf. in Aram. #he[אֲבָהָן]; #emph[fathers];, and similarly in Phoen. #he[דלהת]; from #he[דלת];, also Arab. #emph[ʾabahât]; (fathers), #emph[ʾummahât]; (mothers), with an artificial expansion into a triliteral stem.

#he[אִשָּׂה]; #emph[woman];, probably for #he[אִנְשָׁה];; from #he[אָנַשׁ]; i.e. not (as Aram. #he[אִתְּתָא]; shows) #he[אָנַשׁ]; #emph[to be sociable]; (see above, on #he[אִישׁ];) but #he[אָנַשׁ]; #emph[to be weak]; (Arab. #emph[ʾănŭṯă];). So De Lagarde, #emph[Uebersicht];, p. 68; König, #emph[Lehrgeb.];, ii. 159 f. The form #he[אֵ֫שֶׁת]; (for #emph[ʾišt];, with #he[ת]; fem., from #emph[ʾišš];, after rejection of the doubling and lengthening of the #emph[ĭ]; to #emph[ē];) occurs in #bref("Deut 21:11");, #bref("1 Sam 28:7");, #bref("Ps 58:9");, even in #emph[absol. st.]; \[cf., however, below, #xref(<s130>)[§ 130];. 4, 5\].—In #bref("Ps 128:3"); #he[אֶשְׁתְּךָ]; is found for #he[אִשְׁתְּךָ];. Instead of the plur. #he[נָשִׁים];, we find in #bref("Ezek 23:44"); #he[אִשֹּׁת];.#footnote[Friedr. Delitzsch (in his Babylonian glosses to Baer’s text of Ezekiel, p. xi) on #bref("Ezek 23:44"); remarks that in Assyro\-Babylonian the plur. of #emph[aššatu]; (woman) is #emph[aššâti];, corresponding, therefore, to #he[אִשּׁוֹת];, not to the ordinary plur. #he[נָשִׁים];. The #emph[a]; of #he[נָשִׁים]; (instead of #emph[i]; as in Arab. or #emph[e]; as in Syr.) is to be explained with Barth (#emph[Orient. Studien zu Ehren Th. Nöldekes];, Giessen, 1906, p. 792) from the natural connection of the ideas #emph[‘men’]; and #emph[‘women’];, #he[נָשִׁים]; and #he[אֲנָשִׁים];.];

#he[בַּ֫יִת]; #emph[house];, locative #he[בַּ֫יְתָה];, #he[הַבַּ֫יְתָה];, in #emph[pause]; #he[בָּ֫יְתָה];, #he[הַבָּ֫יְתָה];, #emph[constr.]; #he[בֵּ֫יתָה];, plur. #he[בָּֽתִּים]; (but in #bref("Deut 6:11");, #bref("1 Chr 28:11"); #he[בָּתִּים]; without #emph[Metheg];), pronounced #emph[bâttîm];. The explanation of the Dageš in the #he[ת]; is still a matter of dispute. The Syriac #emph[bâttîn];, however, shows that the #emph[Dageš]; is original, and belongs to the character of the form.#footnote[This disposes of the traditional view that the #emph[Dageš]; (after a firm Metheg, see #xref(<p16f>)[§ 16#h(0.18em)#emph[f];]; ζ) only serves to distinguish if from #he[בָּתִים]; #emph[passing the night];, ptcp. Qal of #he[בּוּת];, a stem which never occurs in the O.T. According to P. Haupt the stem is #he[בא]; #emph[to go in];, #he[ת]; therefore being the feminine termination, as in #emph[bint daughter];, and the original form #emph[baʾtu, bātu]; (#emph[entrance];) is preserved in the plural #emph[bāttim]; where the #emph[tt]; is to be explained as due to the analogy of trisyllabic stems. In the singular #emph[bāt]; passed into #emph[bēt]; (?), and this was resolved into #emph[bait];, as #emph[Y#super[e];rūšālēm]; into #emph[Y#super[e];rūšālayim];.]; According to Wright, #emph[Comparative Grammar];, p. 88, #he[בָּֽתִּים]; is simply contracted from #emph[bai\-tîm]; (as #he[אָן]; from #he[אַ֫יִן];, #he[עֵינָם]; from #he[עֵינָיִם];, etc.), and the Dageš, therefore, is #emph[lene];; König, #emph[Lehrgeb];., ii. 56, proposes the name #emph[Dageš forte orthoconsonanticum];; on the other hand Rahlfs, #emph[ThLZ.]; 1896, col. 587, suggests that the #he[י]; is assimilated to the #he[ת];, while Philippi, #emph[ZDMG.]; xlix, p. 206, assumes for the plural a stem distinct from that of the singular. A definite solution is at present impossible. The incorrectness of the formerly common pronunciation #emph[bottîm]; is sufficiently shown by the Babylonian punctuation (see #xref(<p8g>)[§ 8#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];, note 3), which leaves no doubt as to the #emph[â];.

#he[בֵּן]; #emph[son]; (#bref("Gen 30:19"); #he[בֵּֽן־שִׁשִּׁי];) #emph[constr.]; usually #he[בֶּן־]; (also with a conjunctive accent as an equivalent for #emph[Maqqeph];, #bref("Gen 17:17");, #bref("Isa 8:2");, etc., #bref("1 Chr 9:21");; even with smaller disjunctives, especially in the combination #he[מִבֶּן];, #bref("Exod 30:14");, #bref("Lev 27:3");, etc. \[#he[מִבֶּן־]; only after #he[וְאִם]; and before #he[חֹ֫דֶשׁ];, also in #bref("Isa 51:12");; see Strack on #bref("Exod 30:14");\]), rarely #he[בִּן־]; (#bref("Deut 25:2");, #bref("Jonah 4:10"); twice, #bref("Prov 30:1");, and so always in the combination #he[בִּן־נוּן];, and in the proper names #he[בִּנְיָמִין]; \[but #he[בֶּן־יִמִינִי]; #emph[Benjamite];\] and #he[בִּן־יָקֶה]; #bref("Prov 30:1");), once #he[בְּנִי]; (cf. #xref(<p90l>)[§ 90#h(0.18em)#emph[l];];) #bref("Gen 49:11");, and #he[בְּנוֹ]; (#xref(<p90o>)[§ 90#h(0.18em)#emph[o];];) #bref("Num 23:18");, #bref("24:3, 15");.—In #bref("Gen 49:22"); #he[בֵּן];, for which #he[בֶּן־]; ought to be read, is intended by the Masora for the #emph[absol. st.];, not the #emph[constr.]; #origpage("286") <orig-286>;#he[בַּת]; #emph[daughter]; (from #emph[bant];, and this again, according to the law stated in #xref(<p69c>)[§ 69#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];, for #emph[bint];, fem. of #he[בֵּן];), with suff. #he[בִּתִּי]; for #he[בִּנְתִּי];. Plur. #he[בָּנוֹת];, from the sing. #he[בָּנָה];, comp. #he[בָּנִים]; #emph[sons];.

#he[חָם]; #emph[husband’s father];, only with suff. #he[חָמִיךְ];, #he[חָמִ֫יהָ];; and #he[חָמוֹת]; #emph[husband’s mother];, only with suff. #he[חֲמוֹתֵךְ];, #he[חֲמוֹתָהּ];. Cf. #he[אָב];, #he[אָח];, and especially #he[אָחוֹת];.

#he[יוֹם]; #emph[day]; (Arab. #emph[yaum];),#footnote[Cf. Nöldeke, #emph[Beitröge];, p. 58, #emph[yaum];, probably an extension of a biliteral word which has survived in #he[יָמִים];, #he[יְמֵי];. Barth, however, #emph[Orient. Studien];, p. 791 (see above on #he[אִשָּׂה];), sees in #he[יָמִים];, #he[יְמֵי];, #he[יְמוֹת]; new formations in Hebrew, caused by the naturally close connection and association of these plurals with #he[שְׁנִים];, #he[שְׁנֵי];, #he[שְׁנוֹת]; #emph[years];, to which they became assimilated in form. The view that #he[יוֹם]; is merely an incorrect obscuring of #he[יָם];, and therefore distinct from the Arab. #emph[yaum];, is contradicted by the invariable spelling #he[יוֹם];, etc., notwithstanding the spelling #he[ובים]; (\=#he[וּבְיֹם]; ?) in the Siloam inscription, line 3 (cf. #xref(<p7f>)[§ 7#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];), and #he[מִיֹּמָ֑יִם]; #bref("Hos 6:2");. Cf. also the note on #xref(<p100g>)[§ 100#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];.]; dual #he[יוֹמַ֫יִם];; the plur. #he[יָמִים]; is probably from a different sing. (#he[יָם]; #emph[yām];), #emph[constr.]; #he[יְמֵי]; and (poetically) #he[יְמוֹת];, #bref("Deut 32:7");, #bref("Ps 90:15");.

#he[כְּלִי]; #emph[vessel];, in #emph[pause]; #he[כֶּ֫לִי]; (with suff. #he[כֶּלְיְךָ]; #bref("Deut 23:25");) from #he[כָּלָה]; #emph[to contain];, plur. #he[כֵּלִים]; (as if from #he[כֵּל];, #he[כֵּ֫לֶה];; according to König, ii. 63, shortened from #emph[kilyîm];).

#he[מַ֫יִם]; #emph[water];; on the plur. cf. #xref(<p88d>)[§ 88#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];.

#he[עִיר]; #emph[city];. The plur. #he[עָרִים]; is scarcely syncopated from #he[עֲיָרִים];, as it is pointed in #bref("Judg 10:4"); (no doubt erroneously, in imitation of the preceding #he[עֲיָרִים]; #emph[ass colts];), but from a kindred sing. #he[עָר];, which still occurs in proper names.

#he[פֶּה]; #emph[mouth];, #emph[constr, st.]; #he[פִּי]; (for original #he[פֵּי]; \= #he[פֵּה];?). Its origin is still disputed. According to Gesenius and König (ii. 103), #he[פֶּה]; stands for #he[פֵּאֶה]; (ground\-form #emph[piʿay];) from #he[פָּאָה]; #emph[to breathe, to blow];; according to Olshausen, for #he[פַּי];, from a stem #he[פָּיָה]; or #he[פָּוָה];. But parallel with the Hebrew #he[פֶּה]; are Assyr. #emph[pû];, Arab. #emph[fû, fam, famm, fumm];, bibl. Aram. #he[פֻּם];, #he[פֻּמָּא];, Syr. #emph[pûm, pûmā];, so that Barth, #emph[ZDMG.]; xli, p. 634, assumes two forms of development from the same stem (#he[פמו];), namely #emph[fm]; and #emph[fw];. #he[פִּי]; #emph[my mouth];, from #emph[pi\-y];; for #he[פִּיהֶם]; we find in #bref("Ps 17:10");, #bref("58:7");, #bref("59:13"); #he[פִּ֫ימוֹ];. The supposed plur. #he[פִּים]; #bref("1 Sam 13:21"); is generally explained as a contraction from #he[פִּיִּים];, but the text is altogether corrupt. The plur. #he[פִּיּוֹת];, for the #emph[edges]; of a sword, occurs in #bref("Prov 5:4");; reduplicated #he[פִּֽיפִיוֹת]; #bref("Isa 41:15");, #bref("Ps 149:6");.

#he[רֹאשׁ]; #emph[head]; (obscured from #he[רָאשׁ];\=#emph[răʾš];); plur. #he[רָאשִׁים]; (for #he[רְאָשִׁים];, #xref(<p23c>)[§ 23#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];); #he[רֹאשָׁיו]; only in #bref("Isa 15:2");.

#he[שֶׂה]; #emph[a head of small cattle]; (#emph[sheep]; or #emph[goat];), #emph[constr. st.]; #he[שֵׂה];, with suff. #he[שְׂיֵ֫הוּ]; #bref("1 Sam 14:34"); and #he[שֵׂיוֹ]; #bref("Deut 22:1");, according to König, ii. 131, from a ground\-form #emph[siʾay];, but according to De Lagarde, #emph[Uebersicht];, 81 f., from a stem #he[ושי]; (#he[שֶׂה];\=#emph[say];\=#emph[wĭsay];).

#he[שֵׁם]; #emph[name];, #emph[constr.]; generally #he[שֵׁם]; (only six times #he[שֶׁם־];); cf. #he[בֵּן];.

#he[שָׁמַ֫יִם]; #emph[heaven]; (#xref(<p88d>)[§ 88#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];).

#sec("97", [Numerals. (a) Cardinal Numbers], note: []) <s97>

#align(center)[Brockelmann, #emph[Sem. Sprachwiss];., p. 116 ff.; #emph[Grundriss];, i. 484 ff.];

#ml("a") <p97a> #strong[1.]; The formation of the cardinal numbers from 3 to 10 (on 1 and 2 see below) has this peculiarity, that numerals connected with a masculine #origpage("287") <orig-287>;substantive take the feminine form, and those with a feminine substantive take the masculine form. The common explanation of this strange phenomenon used to be that the primary form of the numeral was an abstract noun in the feminine (cf. #xref(<p122p>)[§ 122#h(0.18em)#emph[p];];). This was originally attached in the #emph[constr. st.]; to the word qualified, then came to be also used in apposition to it, and finally was placed after it like an adjective. The consequence of the appositional, and finally adjectival, construction was, that for numerals connected with feminine nouns a special shorter form came to be used, while the original forms, with the abstract feminine ending, were used in connection with masculine nouns, after as well as before them.

On this view the historical process would have been that originally the abstract numerals (like Latin #emph[trias, decas];, Greek πεντάς, δεκάς, etc.) were placed in the #emph[constr. st.]; before masculines and feminines alike, e.g. #he[שְׁל֫שֶׁת בָּנִים]; #emph[trias filiorum];, #he[עֲשֶׂ֫רֶת נָשִׁים]; #emph[decas mulierum];. A trace of this earlier usage was seen in the examples mentioned under #emph[c];, like #he[שְׁל֫שֶׁת נָשִׁים];.—Further, it was possible to say #he[שְׁלשָׁה בָנִים]; #emph[trias];, sc. #emph[filii];, as well as #he[בָּנִים שְׁלשָׁה]; #emph[filii, trias];. From this second appositional construction it was only a step to the treatment of the abstract numeral as an adjective, #emph[filii tres];. Similarly the subsequently shortened forms of the abstract numeral, which were used in connection with feminines, might stand either in the #emph[constr. st.]; before, or in apposition before or after the word numbered, thus #he[שְׁלשׁ בָּנוֹת]; #emph[trias filiarum];, or #he[שָׁלשׁ בָּנוֹת]; #emph[trias];, sc. #emph[filiae];, or #he[בָּנוֹת שָׁלשׁ]; #emph[filiae, trias];, or adjectivally #emph[filiae tres];.

A different and much more intelligible explanation of the striking disagreement between the gender of the numeral and that of the word numbered has recently been given by Reckendorf, #emph[Die syntaktischen Verhältnisse des Arabischen];, pt. ii, Leiden, 1898, p. 265 ff. He also considers that the earliest forms were abstract numerals which were placed in the #emph[constr. st.]; before the noun numbered, the latter depending on them in the genitive. The original form, however, of the abstract numerals from 3 to 9 is not the feminine, but the masculine, used for both genders, as it still is in the tens, 20, 30, etc. The feminine abstract numeral was first distinguished by a special form in the numbers from 13 to 19 (see further, below) when connected with masculines, and this distinction was afterwards extended to the numbers from 3 to 10. This explanation does not affect the view stated above that the appositional and adjectival use of the abstract numerals was only adopted later in addition to their use in the genitive construction.

The differentiation of the numerals (originally of common gender) into masculine and feminine forms in the second decade, was occasioned, according to Reckendorf, by the use of the abstract feminine #he[עֶשְׂרֵה]; in compounds. #origpage("288") <orig-288>;So long as it was felt that #he[שְׁלשׁ עֶשְׂרֵה]; simply meant #emph[the three of the decade];, the gender of the noun numbered made no difference. When, however, the consciousness of this meaning became weakened and the combination of units and tens came to be felt as a copulative rather than a genitive relation, it seemed suitable to connect only feminine nouns with the feminine form #he[עֶשְׂרֵה];. New forms were therefore invented, both of the units and the tens, for use with masculine nouns. The former, however, no longer had the form of the #emph[constr.]; but of the absolute state, clearly showing that the consciousness of the original syntactical relation in #he[שְׁלשׁ עֶשְׂרֵה];, etc., was lost. On the other hand, after the extension of these new formations to the first decade, the new feminine forms readily came to be used also in the genitive construction (and therefore in the #emph[constr. st.];) on the analogy of the earlier masculine forms.

Of the first two numerals, #he[אֶחָד];, #emph[one];, with its fem. #he[אַחַת]; (see § 96), may be recognized, from its form and use, as an adjective, although even so it admits of such combinations as #he[אַחַד הֶֽהָרִים]; #emph[unus e montibus];. The numeral two, as would be expected, appears as an abstract in the #emph[dual];, but, like the other numerals, can also stand in apposition to the noun numbered. In form it always agrees with the gender of its noun. Accordingly, the numerals from 1 to 10 are as follows:

