#origpage("289") <orig-289>;On the connective forms #he[שְׁבַע];, #he[תְּשַׁע];, cf. the analogous forms in #xref(<p93h>)[§ 93#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];.

#ml("c") <p97c> The other Semitic languages also exhibit the same peculiarity in the external differentiation of the numerals from 3 to 10 as regards gender. The fem. form of the numeral abstracts is only rarely found in connection with feminine nouns,#footnote[In the vulgar dialects of Arabic, and in Ethiopic, the feminine form of the numeral is by far the more common. This form appears also in Hebrew, when the number is regarded in the abstract, as in the multiplicatives (see #xref(<p97h>)[§ 97#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];).]; e.g. #he[שְׁל֫שֶׁת נָשִׁים]; #bref("Gen 7:13");, #bref("1 Sam 10:3");, #bref("Job 1:4");, #bref("Ezek 7:2"); #emph[K#super[e];th.];; probably also #bref("Josh 17:11");, where we should read with Dillmann #he[שׁ׳ הַנְּפוֹת];. In apposition, #bref("Zech 3:9");, #bref("4:2");, cf. #bref("Jer 36:23");. From what was said above, under #emph[a];, it follows that these cases are not a return to original usage, but only an intrusion of the form used before masculines into the sphere of the feminine. Conversely in #bref("Gen 38:24"); #he[שְׁלשׁ חֳדָשִׁים]; (but in the Samaritan #he[שְׁל֫שֶׁת];).—For #he[שִׁבְעָה]; #emph[seven];, there occurs in #bref("Job 42:13"); the strange form #he[שִׁבְעָ֫נָה];, according to Ewald \[#emph[Ausführl, Lehrb.];#super[8];, § 269 #emph[b];\] an old feminine substantive (German #emph[ein Siebend];, #emph[a set of seven];), but more probably a scribal error.

#ml("d") <p97d> #strong[2.]; The numerals from 11 to 19 are formed by placing the units, without the #emph[copula];, before the number ten (in the form #he[עָשָׂר]; masc., #he[עֶשְׂרֵה]; fem.), but without the two words being joined into one. As was said above, under #emph[a];, and as is proved by the use of #he[אַחַד];, #he[אַחַת]; in the numeral 11, the feminine numerals from 13 to 19 are to be regarded as #emph[construct]; forms in a genitive connection. The connective forms of the masculine abstracts, like #he[שְׁל֫שֶׁת];, etc., are not admitted in combination with #he[עָשָׂר];, since they are merely in apposition, and not in a genitive relation (see the rare exceptions at the end of #emph[e];). On the other hand #he[שְׁנֵי]; and #he[שְׁתֵּי]; in the numeral 12 are undoubtedly true #emph[constructs];, like #he[אַחַד]; and the fem. numerals 13–19. But instead of #he[שְׁנֵי]; (#bref("Exod 28:21");, #bref("Josh 3:12"); and four other places) and #he[שְׁתֵּי]; (#bref("Josh 4:8"); and three times in Ezek.), we generally find #he[שְׁנֵים]; and #he[שְׁתֵּים];. Two explanations have been given of these forms: (1) that the #emph[K#super[e];thîbh]; really intends #he[שְׁנַ֫יִם];, #he[שְׁתַּ֫יִם];, in the #emph[absol. st.];, which was first introduced in the case of #he[שְׁנַ֫יִם];, on the analogy of #he[עֲשָׂרָה];, etc., and then extended to #he[שְׁתַּ֫יִם];; the Masora, however, required #he[שְׁנֵי];, #he[שְׁתֵּי]; (but see below), and therefore pointed #he[שְׁנֵים];, #he[שְׁתֵּים]; as a #emph[Q#super[e];rê perpetuum]; (see § 17).—(2) that the #emph[absolute]; forms #he[שְׁנַ֫יִם];, #he[שְׁתַּ֫יִם]; (introduced on the analogy of #he[שְׁלשָׁה];, etc.) were contracted to #he[שְׁנֵים];, #he[שְׁתֵּים]; to facilitate the pronunciation of the duals when closely #origpage("290") <orig-290>;connected with #he[עָשָׂר]; and #he[עֶשְׂרֵה];, and that the contraction is founded on an early and correct tradition. The second explanation is supported by the large number of examples of #he[שנים]; (66) and #he[שתים]; (34). It would be strange if the Masora required the alteration of the far commoner forms on account of isolated instances of #he[שְׁנֵי]; and #he[שְׁתֵּי];. As a matter of fact even in regard to the latter forms the tradition often varies between #he[שְׁנֵי]; and #he[שְׁנַ֫יִם];, etc., cf. e.g. Ginsburg on #bref("Josh 3:12");. We cannot therefore assume a #emph[Q#super[e];rê perpetuum];.

#ml("e") <p97e> Accordingly the numbers from 11 upwards are—

#align(center)[

#gtable(columns: 3, [11.], [#he[אַחַד עָשָׂר];], [#he[אַחַת עֶשְׂרֵה];], [], [#he[עַשְׁתֵּי];#footnote[#he[עַשְׁתֵּי];, which remained for a long time unexplained, was recognized (first by J. Oppert) in the Assyro\-Babylonian inscriptions in the form #emph[ištin]; or #emph[ištên];; cf. Friedr. Delitzsch, #emph[Assyrische Grammatik];, p. 203, and P. Haupt, in the #emph[American Journal of Philology];, viii. 279. Accordingly, #he[עַשְׁתֵּי עָשָׂר]; is a compound, like the Sansk. #emph[êkâdaçan];, ἕνδεκα, #emph[undecim]; (analogous to the combination of units and tens in the numerals from 12 to 19), and is used at the same time in the composition of the feminine numeral eleven. On the gradual substitution of #he[עַשְׁתֵּי ע׳]; for #he[אַחַד ע׳]; and #he[אַחַת ע׳]; see Giesebrecht in #emph[ZAW.]; 1881, p. 226; #he[עַשְׁתֵּי ע׳]; occurs only in Jer., Ez., in the prologue to Deuteronomy (1:3), in the Priestly Code, and in passages undoubtedly post\-exilic, so that it may very well be a loan\-word from the Babylonian.]; #he[עָשָׂר];], [#he[עַשְׁתֵּי עֶשְׂרֵה];], [12.], [#he[שְׁנֵים עָשָׂר];], [#he[שְׁתֵּים עֶשְׂרֵה];], [], [#he[שְׁנֵי עָשָׂר];], [#he[שְׁתֵּי עֶשְׂרֵה];], [13.], [#he[שְׁלשָׁה עָשָׂר];], [#he[שְׁלשׁ עֶשְׂרֵה];]);

];

etc., on the analogy of the last. These numerals regularly have only the above form. In regard to their syntax, cf. #xref(<p134f>)[§ 134#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];.

Very rarely the units appear in the #emph[masc.]; in the #emph[constr. st.];, as #he[חֲמֵ֫שֶׁת עָשָׂר]; #emph[fifteen];, #bref("Judg 8:10");, #bref("2 Sam 19:18");; #he[שְׁמנַת עָשָׂר]; #emph[eighteen];, #bref("Judg 20:25");.—Connected by #he[וְ]; we find #he[עֲשָׂרָה וַֽחֲמִשָּׁה]; in #bref("Exod 45:12");.

#ml("f") <p97f> #strong[3.]; The tens from 30 to 90 are expressed by the plural forms of the units (so that the plural here always stands for #emph[ten times]; the unit), thus, #he[שְׁלשִׁים]; 30, #he[אַרְבָּעִים]; 40, #he[חֲמִשִּׁים]; 50, #he[שִׁשִּׁים]; 60, #he[שִׁבְעִים]; 70, #he[שְׁמֹנִים]; 80, #he[תִּשְׁעִים]; 90. But #emph[twenty]; is expressed by #he[עֶשְׂרִים];, plur. of #he[עֶ֫שֶׂר]; #emph[ten];.#footnote[For #he[עֶשְׂרִים];, #he[שִׁבְעִים];, #he[תִּשְׁעִים]; (from the #emph[segholates]; #he[עֶ֫שֶׂר];, #he[שֶׁ֫בַע];, #he[תֵּ֫שַׁע];), we should expect #emph[ʿ#super[a];sārîm, š#super[e];bhāʿîm, t#super[e];šāʿîm];. Is this very unusual deviation from the common formation (see above, #xref(<p93l>)[§ 93#h(0.18em)#emph[l];];, #emph[o];, #emph[r];) connected with the special meaning of these plurals, or are these survivals of an older form of the plural of segholates?]; These numerals are all of common gender, and do not admit of the #emph[construct state];.—In compound numerals, like 22, 23, 44, etc., the units #origpage("291") <orig-291>;may precede (#emph[two and twenty];, as in Arabic and English), e.g. #bref("Num 3:39");, #bref("26:14");. Very frequently, however, the reverse order is found (#emph[twenty and two];, as in Syriac, cf. French and English #emph[twenty\-two];), e.g. #bref("1 Chr 12:28");, #bref("18:5");.#footnote[According to the conclusions of König (#emph[De Criticae Sacrae Argumento];, p. 61, and #emph[Lehrgeb.];, ii. p. 215 ff.), the smaller number more commonly precedes in Ezek. and the Priestly Code, but the larger always elsewhere. S. Herner (#emph[Syntax der Zahlwörter im A.T.];, Lund, 1893, p. 71 ff.) arrives at the same conclusion by a full examination of the statistics; cf. also his remarks on König in #emph[ZAW.]; 1896, p. 123, and König’s reply, #emph[ibid.];, p. 328 f.]; In all cases the units and tens are connected by the #emph[copula];, ordinarily #he[וְ];, but #he[וָ]; before numerals with the tone on the penultima, #he[וַ]; before #he[־ֲ];, #he[וּ]; before #emph[Š#super[e];wâ];; see #xref(<p104d>)[§ 104#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];, #emph[e];, #emph[g];.

#ml("g") <p97g> The remaining numerals are the substantives—

#align(center)[

#gtable(columns: 2, [200], [#he[מָאתַ֫יִם]; dual (contracted from #he[מְאָתַ֫יִם];; cf. #xref(<p23c>)[§ 23#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];).], [300], [#he[שְׁלשׁ מֵאוֹת]; plur. (but in #bref("2 Kgs 11:4, 9, 10, 15");, #emph[K#super[e];th.]; #he[הַמְּאָיוֹת];).], [1000], [#he[אֶ֫לֶף]; masc.], [2000], [#he[אַלְפַּ֫יִם]; dual.], [3000], [#he[שְׁל֫שֶׁת אֲלָפִים]; plur., and so on (except #he[עֲשָׂרָה אֲלָפִים]; in #bref("2 Sam 18:3");, #bref("2 Kgs 24:14"); #emph[K#super[e];th.];; elsewhere always #he[עֲשֶׂ֫רֶת אֲלָפִים];).], [10000], [#he[רְבָבָה];, in the later books the aramaising#footnote[Cf. Kautzsch, #emph[Die Aramaismen im A.T.]; (Halle, 1902), p. 79 f.]; forms #he[רִבּוֹ];, #he[רִבּוֹא];, #he[רִבּוֹת]; (properly #emph[multitude];, cf. μυριάς).], [20000], [#he[רִבֹּתַ֫יִם]; dual (see below, h); but #he[שְׁתֵּי רִבּוֹת]; #bref("Neh 7:70"); (also #he[שְׁתֵּי רִבּוֹא]; #bref("Neh 7:71");).], [40000], [#he[אַרְבַּע רִבּוֹא]; #bref("Neh 7:66");.], [60000], [#he[שֵֽׁשׁ־רִבּאֹות]; #bref("Ezra 2:69"); (Baer and Ginsburg #he[רִבֹּאוֹת];, as in #bref("Dan 11:12");). #he[אַלְפֵי רְבָבָה]; #emph[thousands of myriads];, #bref("Gen 24:60");.]);

];

#ml("h") <p97h> Rem. 1. The dual form which occurs in some of the units has the meaning of our ending \-#emph[fold];, e.g. #he[אַרְבַּעְתַּ֫יִם]; #emph[fourfold];, #bref("2 Sam 12:6");; #he[שִׁבְעָתַ֫יִם]; #emph[sevenfold];, #bref("Gen 4:15, 24");, #bref("Isa 30:26");, #bref("Ps 12:7");, #bref("79:12"); (cf. #xref(<p134r>)[§ 134#h(0.18em)#emph[r];];). The dual #he[רִבֹּתַ֫יִם]; #bref("Ps 68:18"); (explained by #he[אַלְפֵי שִׁנְאָן]; #emph[thousands of duplication];) is not meant to be taken in the sense of #emph[two myriads]; or #emph[twice the number of myriads];, but in a multiplicative sense.#footnote[Cf. D. H. Müller, ‘Die numeralia multiplicativa in den Amarnatafeln u. im Hebr.,’ #emph[Semitica];, i, Wien, 1906, p. 13 ff.];—Besides the plural which denotes the tens, there are also the plurals #he[אֲחָדִים]; #emph[some];, also #emph[iidem];, and #he[עֲשָׂרוֹת]; #emph[decades]; (not #emph[decem];) #bref("Exod 18:21, 25");.

#ml("i") <p97i> 2. The #emph[suffixes]; to numerals are, as with other nouns, properly genitives, although they are translated in English as nominatives, e.g. #he[שְׁלָשְׁתְּכֶם]; #emph[your triad];, i.e. #emph[you three];, #bref("Num 12:4");; #he[חֲמִשָּׁיו]; #emph[his fifty]; (i.e. the 50 belonging to him) #bref("2 Kgs 1:9–13");, and #he[חֲמִשֶּׁ֫יךָ]; #bref("2 Kgs 1:10, 12");. #origpage("292") <orig-292>;

#sec("98", [Numerals. (b) Ordinal Numbers], note: []) <s98>

#ml("a") <p98a> The ordinal numbers from 2 to 10 are formed from the corresponding cardinals by adding the termination #he[־ִי]; (#xref(<p86h>)[§ 86#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];), before which another #he[־ִי]; also is generally inserted between the second and third radicals. They are as follows: #he[שֵׁנִי]; #emph[second];, #he[שְׁלִישִׁי];, #he[רְבִיעִי]; (like #he[רֶ֫בַע];, #he[רֹ֫בַע];, #he[רִבֵּעִים];, without the prosthetic #he[א];, which appears in #he[אַרְבַּע];, etc.), #he[חֲמִישִׁי]; or #he[חֲמִשִׁי]; (which, according to Strack, is always to be read for #he[חֲמִשִּׁי];), #he[שִׁשִּׁי];, #he[שְׁבִיעִי];, #he[שְׁמִינִי];, #he[תְּשִׁיעִי];, #he[עֲשִׂירִי];. The ordinal #emph[first]; is expressed by #he[רִאשׁוֹן]; (cf. #xref(<p27w>)[§ 27#h(0.18em)#emph[w];];), from #he[רֹאשׁ]; #emph[head, beginning];, with the termination #he[וֹן]; (#xref(<p86f>)[§ 86#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];). On the use of #he[אֶחָד]; as an ordinal in numbering the days of the month, cf. #xref(<p134p>)[§ 134#h(0.18em)#emph[p];];; in such cases as #bref("Gen 1:5");, #bref("2:11");, the meaning of #emph[first]; is derived solely from the context.

#ml("b") <p98b> The feminine forms have the termination #he[־ִית];, more rarely (and only in the case of 3 and 10) #he[־ִיָּה];. They are employed also to express fractions, e.g. #he[חֲמִשִׁית]; #emph[fifth]; or #emph[fifth part];, #he[עֲשִׂירִית]; and #he[עֲשִֽׂירִיָּה]; #emph[tenth part];. Side by side with these, in the same sense, there are also forms like #he[רֹ֫בֵע]; and #he[רֶֹ֫בַע]; #emph[a quarter];, #he[חֹ֫מֶשׁ]; #emph[a fifth part];, and with the afformative #he[וֹן];, #he[עִשָּׂרוֹן]; (plur. #he[עֶשְׂרוֹנִים];) #emph[a tenth part];; these are to be regarded as abstracts, and are denominatives from the cardinal numbers. Cf. finally #he[שָׁבוּעַ]; #greek[ἑβδομάς];, #emph[a week];; #he[עָשׂוֹר]; #emph[a decade]; (of days), and also the #emph[tenth day];.

On the expression of the other relations of number, for which the Hebrew has no special forms, see the Syntax, #xref(<p134q>)[§ 134#h(0.18em)#emph[q];]; and r.

#bookhead(2, [Chapter IV. The Particles], note: []);

#origpage("293") <orig-293>;

#sec("99", [General View], note: []) <s99>

#align(center)[Brockelmann, #emph[Grundriss];, i. 492 f.];

#ml("a") <p99a> #strong[1.]; The particles, which in general express the secondary modifications of thought in speech, the closer relation of words to one another, and the mutual connection of sentences, are for the most part either borrowed or derived from noun\-forms, sometimes also from pronouns and verbs (#xref(<p30s>)[§ 30#h(0.18em)#emph[s];];). #emph[Primitive]; particles (apart from a few demonstrative forms, see #xref(<p100i>)[§ 100#h(0.18em)#emph[i];];) can only be so called in the sense defined in #xref(<p81f>)[§ 81#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];.

#ml("b") <p99b> #strong[2.]; So far as the origin of the particles can be discovered with certainty, they are either (1) #emph[borrowed]; from other parts of speech; i.e. certain forms of the noun, pronoun, or verb, with more or less loss of their original meaning, have come to be employed as particles; cf. in the Indo\-Germanic languages, e.g. the Latin #emph[certo, falso, partim, verum, causa];, the German #emph[statt, anstatt, wegen, weg];, and the English #emph[instead, away];; or (2) #emph[derived]; from other parts of speech, either (#emph[a];) by the #emph[addition]; of formative syllables, as #he[יוֹמָם]; #emph[by day];, from #he[יוֹם]; (cf., however, #xref(<p100g>)[§ 100#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];); or most commonly (#emph[b];) by #emph[abbreviations]; effected in various ways, the extent of their mutilation being in proportion to the frequency of their use, so that in some cases (see below) the original stem has become wholly unrecognizable.

Cf. in German #emph[gen];, from #emph[gegen, Gegend; seit];, from #emph[Seite; weil]; (originally a particle of time, like our #emph[while];), from #emph[Weile];.

Still more violent abbreviations occur in Greek, Latin, and the Romance languages, e.g. ἀπό, #emph[ab];, #emph[a];; ἐξ, #emph[ex, e; ad];, Fr. #emph[à];; #emph[aut];, Fr. #emph[ou];, Ital. #emph[o];; #emph[super];, Ital. #emph[su];.#footnote[Even short phrases are contracted into one word: Lat. #emph[forsitan];, from #emph[fors sit an];, #greek[δηλονότι];, #greek[δηλαδή];, Fr. #emph[peut\-être];, Eng. #emph[prithee]; from #emph[I pray thee];.—In Chinese most of the particles are verbs or nouns; e.g. #emph[iù]; (to give), also the sign of the dative; #emph[ı̀]; (to make use of), #emph[to, for];; #emph[nëi]; (the interior), #emph[in];.];

#ml("c") <p99c> The greatest shortening occurs in those particles which have entirely lost the character of an independent word, by being reduced to a single consonant with its vowel (generally short) or #emph[Š#super[e];wâ];. According to the laws of syllable formation in Hebrew (#xref(<p26m>)[§ 26#h(0.18em)#emph[m];];), #origpage("294") <orig-294>;such particles cannot stand by themselves, but are united, as prefixes, with the following word (§ 102), very much like the preformatives of the imperfect (#xref(<p47a>)[§ 47#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];–#emph[d];).

#ml("d") <p99d> The view that this shortening of whole words to single letters has actually taken place in the gradual course of linguistic development is rendered highly probable by the fact that similar abbreviations in later Hebrew and in Aramaic, i.e. as the development of the original Semitic speech progresses, become more and more striking and frequent. Thus the Biblical Aramaic #he[דִּי]; becomes at a later period #he[דְּ];; in modern Arabic, e.g. #emph[hallaq]; (now) is from #emph[halwaqt];; #emph[lêš]; (why?) from #emph[li\-ayyi\-šaiĭn];, etc. Cf. also the analogous cases mentioned above from the Western languages. Nevertheless, the use of the simplest particles is found already in the earliest periods of the Hebrew language, or, at any rate, in the earliest documents which have come down to us.

#ml("e") <p99e> #strong[3.]; Less frequently particles are formed by #emph[composition];; as #he[מַדּוּעַ]; #emph[wherefore?]; for #he[מַה־יָּדוּעַ]; #emph[quid edoctus];? (τί μαθών;) or #emph[quid cognitum?];; #he[בִּלְעֲדֵי]; (from #he[בַּל]; and #he[עֲדֵי];) #emph[besides];; #he[מִלְמַ֫עְלָה]; (from #he[מִן];, #he[לְ];, #he[מַ֫עְלָה];) #emph[from above, above];.

More frequent is the combination of two words into one without contraction, e.g. #he[אַֽחֲרֵי־כֵן];, #he[אַף־כִּי];, #he[כִּי־אִם];, #he[כִּֽי־עַל־כֵּן];; cf. also the compounds of #he[אֵי]; with demonstrative pronouns, as #he[אֵֽי־מִזֶּה]; #emph[from what?];; #he[אֵי לָזֹאת]; #emph[wherefore?]; \[R.V. #emph[how];\]. See the lexicon under #he[אֵי];.

#sec("100", [Adverbs], note: []) <s100>

On demonstrative adverbs cf. Brockelmann, #emph[Grundriss];, i. 323; on interrogative adverbs, #emph[ibid.];, i. 328; on adverbs in general, i. 492 ff.

#ml("a") <p100a> #strong[1.]; The negative #he[לֹא]; #emph[not];, and a few particles of place and time, as #he[שָׁם]; #emph[there];, are of obscure origin.

#ml("b") <p100b> #strong[2.]; Forms of other parts of speech, which are used adverbially without further change, are—

(#emph[a];) Substantives with prepositions, e.g. #he[בִּמְאֹד]; (with might) #emph[very];; #he[לְבַד]; #emph[alone]; (prop. #emph[in separation];, Fr. #emph[à part];), with suffix #he[לְבַדִּי]; #emph[I alone];; #he[מִבַּ֫יִת]; #emph[from within, within];; cf. also #he[כְּאֶחָד]; (as one) #emph[together];, #he[לְעֻמַּת]; and #he[מִלְּעֻמַּת]; (originally #emph[in connection with];) #emph[near to, corresponding to, like];, etc., cf. #xref(<p161b>)[§ 161#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];.

#ml("c") <p100c> (#emph[b];) Substantives in the accusative (the adverbial case of the Semites, #xref(<p118m>)[§ 118#h(0.18em)#emph[m];];), cf. τὴν ἀρχήν, δωρεάν, e.g. #he[מְאֹד]; (might) #emph[very];, #he[אֶ֫פֶס]; (cessation) #emph[no more];, #he[הַיּוֹם]; (the day) #emph[today]; (cf. #xref(<p126b>)[§ 126#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];), #he[מָחָר];#footnote[Generally derived from the #emph[ptcp. Puʿal]; #he[מְאָחָר]; #emph[m#super[e];ʾŏḥār]; (#emph[\=m#super[e];ʾoḥḥār];) and hence to be read #emph[mŏḥār]; (cf. #he[מָֽחֳרָת]; #emph[morning];); but according to P. Haupt (notes to Esther, p. 159) from #he[יוֹם אַחַר];.]; #emph[tomorrow];, #he[יַ֫חַד]; (union) #emph[together];. Several of these continued to be used, though rarely, as substantives, e.g. #he[סָבִיב];, plur. #he[סְבִיבִים]; and #he[סְבִיבוֹת];, #emph[circuit];, as adverb #origpage("295") <orig-295>;#emph[circum, around];; others have quite ceased to be so used, e.g. #he[כְּבָר]; (length) #emph[long ago]; \[Aram.: only in Ec.\]; #he[עוֹד]; (repetition, duration) #emph[again]; or #emph[further];.

#ml("d") <p100d> (#emph[c];) Adjectives, especially in the feminine (corresponding to the Indo\-Germanic neuter), e.g. #he[רִֽאשׁוֹנָה]; #emph[primum, formerly]; (more frequently #he[בָּרִֽאשׁוֹנָה];, also #he[לָרִאשׁוֹנָה];); #he[רַבָּה]; and #he[רַבַּת]; \[both rare\] #emph[multum, much, enough];; #he[נִפְלָאוֹת]; #emph[wonderfully]; (properly #emph[mirabilibus];, sc. #emph[modis];), #he[יְהוּדִית]; #emph[Jewish];, i.e. in the Jewish language.

#ml("e") <p100e> (#emph[d];) Verbs in the infinitive absolute, especially in #emph[Hiphʿîl];, which are likewise to be regarded as accusatives (#xref(<p113h>)[§ 113#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];), e.g. #he[הַרְבֵּה]; (prop. #emph[a multiplying];) #emph[much]; \[frequent\], #he[לְהַרְבֵּה]; \[rare and late\] #emph[in multitude];; #he[הַשְׁכֵּם]; (#emph[mane faciendo];) #emph[early];; #he[הַֽעֲרֵב]; (#emph[vespere faciendo];) #emph[in the evening];.

#ml("f") <p100f> (#emph[e];) Pronouns and numerals, e.g. #he[זֶה]; (prop. #emph[there\=at this place];) #emph[here];, #he[הֵ֫נָּה]; #emph[here, hither]; (also of time, #he[עַד־הֵ֫נָּה]; #emph[till now];, cf. the late and rare #he[עֲדֶן]; and #he[עֲדֶ֫נָּה];\=#he[עַד־הֵן];); #he[אַחַת];, #he[שְׁתַּ֫יִם];, #he[שֶׁ֫בַע];, #he[מֵאָה]; #emph[once, twice, seven times, a hundred times];; #he[שֵׁנִית]; #emph[for the second time];.

#ml("g") <p100g> #strong[3.]; Some adverbs are formed by the addition of formative syllables (most frequently #he[־ָם];) to substantives or adjectives, e.g. #he[אָמְנָם]; and #he[אֻמְנָם]; #emph[truly]; (from #he[אֹמֶן]; #emph[truth];); #he[חִנָּם]; (by favour) #emph[gratis]; (from #he[חֵן]; #emph[gratia];); #he[רֵיקָם]; #emph[in vain];, #emph[frustra];, but also #emph[empty]; (from #he[רֵיק]; #emph[empty, emptiness, vanum];), #bref("Ruth 1:21");, parallel with the #emph[fem.]; #he[מְלֵאָה]; #emph[full];; #he[יוֹמָם]; #emph[by day]; (from #he[יוֹם];)#footnote[Is this #he[־ָם]; an instance of the locative or temporal termination (cf. especially #he[צהרם];) mentioned in #xref(<p88c>)[§ 88#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];? Nöldeke, #emph[ZDMG.]; xl. p. 721, considers #he[יוֹמָם]; a secondary substantival form (used adverbially like #he[לַ֫יְלָה]; #emph[noctu];), corresponding to the Phoenician and Aramaic #he[ימם];, Syr. #emph[ʾimāmā];; cf. on the other hand, König, ii. 255, who follows Olshausen in maintaining that the #emph[ām]; is an adverbial termination.];; with #emph[ô]; in the last syllable, #he[פִּתְאֹם];, for #he[פִּתְעֹם];, #emph[in a twinkling, suddenly]; (from #he[פֶּ֫תַע]; #emph[a twinkling];, the #emph[ô]; being probably obscured from an original #emph[â];).#footnote[#he[דּוּמָם]; #emph[silent]; (an adjective in #bref("Isa 47:5");, #bref("Lam 3:26");; a substantive in #bref("Hab 2:19");), which was formerly included under this head, is better taken, with Barth (#emph[Nominalbildung];, p. 352, Rem. 2), as a participle formed like #he[שׁוֹבָב];, #he[עוֹלָל];, so that #he[דּוּמָם]; (perhaps assimilated to #he[דּוּמָה];) stands for original #he[דּוֹמָם];.];—Moreover, cf. #he[אֲחֹֽרַנִּית]; #emph[backward];, and #he[קְדֹֽרַנִּית]; #emph[darkly attired];, #bref("Mal 3:14");. In both these cases, the formative syllable #emph[an]; has been first attached to the stem, and then the feminine ending #emph[îth];, which is elsewhere used to form adverbs, has been added to it.

#ml("h") <p100h> The termination #he[־ָם]; occurs also in the formation of substantives, e.g. #he[אוּלָם]; #emph[porch];, and hence the above adverbs may equally well be regarded as nouns used adverbially, so that #he[־ָם];, #he[־ֹם];, would correspond to #he[־ָן];, #he[וֹן]; (§ 85, Nos. 53, 54), cf. #he[בְּפִתְאֹם]; (with prep.) #emph[suddenly];, #bref("2 Chr 29:36");. According to others, this #emph[am]; is an obsolete accusative ending, to be compared with the indeterminate accusative sing. in #emph[ăn]; in Arabic. #origpage("296") <orig-296>;

#ml("i") <p100i> #strong[4.]; A number of forms standing in very close relation to the demonstrative pronoun may be regarded as primitive adverbs, since they arise directly from a combination of demonstrative sounds. Some of these have subsequently suffered great mutilation, the extent of which, however, can now very rarely be ascertained with certainty. Such are e.g. #he[אָז]; #emph[then];, #he[הֵ֫נָּה]; #emph[here]; (according to Barth, #emph[Sprachwiss. Abhandlungen];, p. 16, formed from the two demonstrative elements #emph[hin]; and #emph[na];), #he[כֵּן];, #he[כָּ֫כָה]; #emph[thus]; (cf. #he[אֵיכָה];, #he[אֵֽיכָכָה];, #emph[how?];), #he[אַךְ]; #emph[only];, #he[אָכֵן]; #emph[truly]; (on all these adverbs, see the Lexicon), and especially the interrogative #he[הֲ]; (#emph[Hē interrogativum];), e.g. #he[הֲלֹא]; (#bref("Deut 3:11"); #he[הֲלֹה];) #emph[nonne?];, #he[הֲגַם]; #emph[num etiam?]; This #emph[Hē interrogativum]; is perhaps shortened from #he[הַל];, which is still used in Arabic, and, according to the view of a certain school of Masoretes, occurs also in Hebrew in #bref("Deut 32:6");.#footnote[The separation of the #he[ה]; at the beginning of #bref("Deut 32:6");, expressly noticed by Qimḥi (ed. Rittenb., p. 40 b) as an unique instance, is perhaps a protest against admitting a particle #he[הַל];.];

#ml("k") <p100k> The #he[ה]; interrogative takes—(1) #emph[Ḥaṭeph\-Pathaḥ]; generally before non\-gutturals (even before #he[ר];), with a firm vowel, e.g. #he[הֲשַׂ֫מְתָּ]; #emph[hast thou set];? see the interrogative clause, #xref(<p150c>)[§ 150#h(0.18em)#emph[c];]; (#he[הַיִּיטַב]; #bref("Lev 10:19"); is an exception).

#ml("l") <p100l> (2) Before a consonant with #emph[Š#super[e];wâ];, usually #emph[Pathaḥ]; without a following #emph[Dageš forte];, e.g. #he[הַֽבְרָכָה]; #bref("Gen 27:38");, cf. #bref("18:17");, #bref("29:5");, #bref("30:15");, #bref("34:31");; less frequently (in about ten passages), #emph[Pathaḥ]; with a following #emph[Dageš forte];, e.g. #he[הַבְּדֶרֶךְ]; #emph[num in via];, #bref("Ezek 20:30");, #he[הַלְּבֶן]; #bref("Gen 17:17");, #bref("18:21");, #bref("37:32");, #bref("Num 13:19");, #bref("Job 23:6");; even in #he[ר];, #bref("1 Sam 10:24");, #bref("17:25");, #bref("2 Kgs 6:32");.

#ml("m") <p100m> (3) Before gutturals, not pointed with either #emph[Qameṣ]; or #emph[Ḥaṭeph\-Qameṣ];, it takes #emph[Pathaḥ];, e.g. #he[הַֽאֵלֵךְ]; #emph[shall I go];?, #he[הַֽאַתָּה]; #emph[num tu];?, #he[הַאִם]; #emph[num si];; #he[הַֽאֶרְצֶה]; #bref("Mal 1:13");; also in #bref("Judg 6:31"); read #he[הַֽאַתֶּם]; (not #he[הָֽא׳];), likewise #he[הַ]; in #bref("Judg 12:5");, #bref("Jer 8:19");, #bref("Neh 6:11");.—In #he[הָאִישׁ]; #bref("Num 16:22");, the Masora intends the article; read #he[הַאִישׁ];, and cf. #bref("Deut 20:19");; in #bref("Eccl 3:21"); read #he[הַֽעֹלָה]; and #he[הֲיֹרֶדֶת];; the article is a correction due to doctrinal considerations.

#ml("n") <p100n> (4) The #he[ה]; takes #emph[S#super[e];ghôl]; before gutturals pointed with #emph[Qameṣ]; or (as in #bref("Judg 9:9"); ff.) #emph[Ḥaṭeph\-Qameṣ];, e.g. #he[הֶֽאָמוּר]; #bref("Mic 2:7");; #he[הֶאָֽנֹכִי]; #bref("Job 21:4");; #he[הֶהָֽיְתָה]; #bref("Joel 1:2");; #he[הֶֽהָשֵׁב]; #bref("Gen 24:5"); (cf. the analogous instances in #xref(<p22c>)[§ 22#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];, #xref(<p35k>)[§ 35#h(0.18em)#emph[k];];, #xref(<p63k>)[§ 63#h(0.18em)#emph[k];];). The place of this interrogative particle is always at the beginning of the clause \[but see #bref("Job 34:31");, #bref("Neh 13:27");, #bref("Jer 22:15");, where one or more words are prefixed for emphasis\].

#ml("o") <p100o> #strong[5.]; Some adverbs occur also in connection with suffixes, thus #he[יֶשְׁךָ]; #emph[thou art there];, 3rd sing. masc. #he[יֶשְׁנוֹ];#footnote[This form, which occurs in #bref("Deut 29:14");, #bref("1 Sam 14:39");, #bref("23:23");, #bref("Esth 3:8");, is textually very doubtful, and cannot be supported by the equally doubtful #he[קָבְנוֹ]; (for #he[קֻבֶּ֫נּוּ];) #bref("Num 23:13");. Most probably, with Stade, #emph[Gramm.];, § 370 b, and P. Haupt, #emph[SBOT.]; Numbers, p. 57, line 37, we should read #he[יֵשֶׁ֫נּוּ];.]; (but see note below), 2nd plur. masc. #he[יֶשְׁבֶם];; #he[אֵינֶ֫נִּי]; #emph[I am not];, 2nd sing. #he[אֵֽינְךָ];, fem. #he[אֵינֵךְ];, 3rd sing. #he[אֵינֶ֫נּוּ];, fem. #he[אֵינֶ֫נָּה];, 2nd plur. #he[אֵֽינְכֶם];, 3rd plur. masc. #he[אֵינָם];.—Also #he[עוֹדֶ֫נִּי]; #emph[I am yet]; (#he[עוֹדִי]; only in #he[בְּעוֹדִי]; and #he[מֵֽעוֹדִי];), #he[עֽוֹדְךָ];, #he[עוֹדָךְ];, #he[עוֹדֵ֫ינוּ]; (#bref("Lam 4:17"); #emph[Q#super[e];rê];; #he[עוֹדֶ֫ינָה]; #origpage("297") <orig-297>;#emph[K#super[e];th.];; the oriental school \[see above, p. 38, note 2\] recognize only the reading #he[עוֹדֵ֫ינוּ];), #he[עוֹדָם];.—#he[אַיֶּ֫כָּה]; #emph[where art thou?];, #he[אַיּוֹ]; #emph[where is he?];, #he[אַיָּם]; #emph[where are they?]; The same applies to #he[הֵן]; (#he[הֶן־];) and #he[הִנֵּה]; #emph[behold!]; (prop. #emph[here, here is];; see #xref(<p105b>)[§ 105#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];), only in #bref("Gen 19:2"); #he[הִנֶּה־נָּא];; with suffixes, #he[הִנְנִי];, once #he[הִנֶּ֫נִּי]; (#bref("Gen 22:7"); with #emph[Munaḥ];), in #emph[pause]; #he[הִנֵּ֫נִי]; #emph[behold me]; (#emph[here am I];), #he[הִנְּךָ]; (#emph[pause]; #he[הִנֶּ֫ךָּ]; #bref("Ps 139:8");), #he[הִנָּךְ];, #he[הִנּוֹ]; and #he[הִנֵּ֫הוּ]; \[both very rare\], #he[הִנְנוּ]; (#emph[behold us];), and #he[הִנֶּ֫נוּ]; (in #emph[pause]; #he[הִנֵּ֫נוּ];), #he[הִנְּכֶם];, #he[הִנָּם];; \[see more fully in the Lexicon, p. 243\].

#ml("p") <p100p> The usual explanation of these suffixes (especially of the forms with #emph[Nûn energicum];) as verbal suffixes, which ascribes some power of verbal government even to forms originally substantival (e.g. #he[יֶשְׁנוֹ]; #emph[there is, he is];), is at least inadmissible for forms (like #he[אַיּוֹ];, #he[בְּעוֹדִי];) which are evidently connected with noun\-suffixes; even for the other forms it is questionable. Brockelmann suggests that the #he[ן]; in connection with these particles is a survival from #he[הנה]; corresponding to the Arab. #emph[ʾánna]; which introduces dependent clauses.

#sec("101", [Prepositions], note: []) <s101>

#align(center)[Brockelmann, #emph[Grundriss];, i. 494 ff.];

#ml("a") <p101a> #strong[1.]; All words, which by usage serve as prepositions, were originally substantives, namely :

(#emph[a];) Substantives in the accusative and in the construct state, so that the noun governed by them is to be considered as in the genitive, and in Arabic actually has the genitive ending, cf. in German #emph[statt dessen, kraft dessen];, in Greek τούτου χάριν, in Latin #emph[huius rei causa];, or #emph[gratia, montis instar];.#footnote[In the examples which follow, the meaning of the noun is added in parentheses, and, when it is actually in use \[though it is mostly in such cases very rare\], is marked with an asterisk.—On a similar use in other languages, see W. von Humboldt, #emph[Über die Kawisprache];, iii, p. 621.]; Cf. #he[אַחַ֫ר]; (hinder part\*) #emph[behind, after]; (#emph[Milʿêl]; in #he[אַ֫חַר כֵּן]; #bref("Lev 14:36");, #bref("Deut 21:13");, #bref("1 Sam 10:5");; #he[אַ֫חַר זֶה]; #bref("2 Chr 32:9");); #he[אֵ֫צֶל]; (side) #emph[close by];; #he[בֵּין]; (intermediate space\*) #emph[between];; #he[בְּעַד];, #he[בַּ֫עַד]; (distance#footnote[So also J. Hoch de Long, #emph[Die hebr. Präpos];. #he[בְּעַד];, Lpz. 1905.];) #emph[behind, around];; #he[זוּלַת];, or with #emph[Ḥireq compaginis]; #he[זֽוּלָתִי]; (removal, want) #emph[except];; #he[יַ֫עַן]; (purpose) #emph[on account of];; #he[מוּל]; (#he[מוֹל]; only in #bref("Deut 1:1");) #emph[before, over against];; #he[מִן־]; (separation; cf. #xref(<p119v>)[§ 119#h(0.18em)#emph[v];];) #emph[from, out of];; #he[נֶ֫גֶד]; (coming in front, that which is over against) #emph[before, over against];; #he[עַד־]; (progress, duration\*) #emph[during, until];; #he[עַל־]; (height, upper part\*) #emph[upon, over];; #he[עִם־]; (connection?) #emph[with];; it is doubtful whether this is to be derived from the same stem as #he[עֻמַּת];, #he[לְעֻמַּת]; #emph[near, beside, like];; #he[תַּ֫חַת]; (under part\*) #emph[under, instead of];.

#ml("b") <p101b> (#emph[b];) Substantives in the construct state, but to be regarded as in the genitive, since they depend on prepositions (especially the inseparable), e.g. #he[לִפְנֵי]; (in the face of\*) #emph[before];; #he[כְּפִי];, #he[לְפִי]; (according to the mouth, #origpage("298") <orig-298>;i.e. the command of\*) #emph[according to];; #he[בִּגְלַל]; (in the concern of) #emph[on account of];; #he[לְמַ֫עַן]; (for the purpose of) #emph[on account of];.

#ml("c") <p101c> #strong[2.]; Substantives used adverbially very frequently become prepositions in this way, e.g. #he[בְּלִי];, #he[בִּבְלִי];, #he[מִבְּלִי];, #he[בִּלְתִּי];, #he[בְּאֵין];, #he[בְּאֶ֫פֶס]; (with cessation) #emph[without];, #he[בְּעוֹד]; (in the duration of) #emph[during];; #he[בְּדֵי];, #he[כְּדֵי]; (according to the requirement of) #emph[for, according to];.

#sec("102", [Prefixed Prepositions], note: []) <s102>

#ml("a") <p102a> #strong[1.]; Of the words mentioned in § 101, #he[מִן־]; #emph[from, out of];, frequently occurs as a prefix (#xref(<p99c>)[§ 99#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];), with its #emph[Nûn]; assimilated to the following consonant (by means of #emph[Dageš forte];), e.g. #he[מִיַּ֫עַר]; #emph[out of a forest];.

#ml("b") <p102b> Rem. The separate #he[מִן־]; (always with a following #emph[Maqqeph];) is usual (but not necessary, cf. #bref("Judg 20:14"); with verse 15, #bref("Ezek 43:6");, etc.) only before the article, e.g. #he[מִן־הָאָ֫רֶץ];, and sometimes occurs before the softer consonants, e.g. #he[מִן־אָז]; #bref("Jer 44:18");, #he[מִן־בְּנֵי]; #bref("Joel 1:12");, #bref("1 Chr 5:18");; cf. #bref("Exod 18:14");, #bref("Lev 1:14");, #bref("14:30");, #bref("Judg 7:23");, #bref("10:11");, #bref("19:16");, #bref("Ps 104:7"); (#bref("2 Kgs 23:36"); before #he[ר];; also before #he[ק]; in #bref("Ps 18:49");), and elsewhere in the later books (as in Aramaic)#footnote[König, #emph[Einleitung ins A.T.];, p. 393 (cf. also the almost exhaustive statistics in his #emph[Lehrgebäude];, ii. 292 ff.), enumerates eight instances of #he[מִן]; before a word without the article in 2 Samuel and Kings, and forty\-five in Chronicles.];; there is besides a poetic by\-form #he[מִנִּי]; (cf. #xref(<p90m>)[§ 90#h(0.18em)#emph[m];];) and #he[מִנֵּי]; #bref("Isa 30:11");. Its form is most commonly #he[מִ‍·]; #emph[with a following Dageš];, which may, however, be omitted in letters which have #emph[Š#super[e];wâ]; (cf. #xref(<p20m>)[§ 20#h(0.18em)#emph[m];];). With a following #he[י]; the #he[מִ‍]; is, as a rule, contracted to #he[מִי];, e.g. #he[מִידֵי];\=#he[מִיְּדֵי]; or #he[מִֽיְדֵי]; (but cf. #he[מִיְּשֵׁנֵי]; #bref("Dan 12:2");; #he[מִיְּרֻשָּֽׁתְךָ]; #bref("2 Chr 20:11");); before gutturals it becomes #he[מֵ‍]; (according to #xref(<p22c>)[§ 22#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];), e.g. #he[מֵֽאָדָם];, #he[מֵעָם];; before #he[ח]; the #he[מִ‍]; occurs with the guttural virtually sharpened in #he[מִחוּץ]; #emph[on the outside];, and in #he[מִחוּט]; #bref("Gen 14:23");; before #he[ה]; in #he[מִֽהְיוֹת]; (cf. #xref(<p28b>)[§ 28#h(0.18em)#emph[b];]; and #xref(<p63q>)[§ 63#h(0.18em)#emph[q];];. The closed syllable here is inconsistent with the required virtual sharpening of the #he[ה];; probably #he[מִֽהְיוֹת]; is merely due to the analogy of #he[לִֽהְיוֹת];); similarly #bref("Isa 14:3"); before #he[ר];; but in #bref("1 Sam 23:28");, #bref("2 Sam 18:16"); #he[מִרְּדֹף]; is to be read, according to #xref(<p22s>)[§ 22#h(0.18em)#emph[s];];.

#ml("c") <p102c> #strong[2.]; There are also three other particles, the most commonly used prepositions and the particle of comparison, which have been reduced by abbreviation (#xref(<p99c>)[§ 99#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];) to a single prefixed consonant with #emph[Š#super[e];wâ]; (but see below, and #xref(<p103e>)[§ 103#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];), namely :

#he[בְּ]; \[poet. #he[בְּמוֹ];\] #emph[in, at, with];.

#he[לְ]; \[poet. #he[לְמוֹ];\] #emph[towards];, (belonging) #emph[to, for];, Lat. #emph[ad.];

#he[כְּ‍]; \[poet. #he[כְּמוֹ];\] #emph[like, as, according to]; (no doubt the remnant of a substantive with the meaning of #emph[matter, kind, instar];).

#ml("d") <p102d> With regard to the pointing it is to be observed that—

(#emph[a];) The #emph[Š#super[e];wâ mobile];, with which the above prefixes are usually pronounced, has resulted from the weakening of a short vowel (an original #emph[ă];, according to #emph[f];)#footnote[Jerome (see Siegfried, #emph[ZAW.]; iv. 79) almost always represents #he[בְּ]; by #emph[ba.];];; the short vowel is regularly retained before #emph[Š#super[e];wâ];: before #emph[Š#super[e];wâ simplex]; #origpage("299") <orig-299>;in the form of an #emph[ĭ];, attenuated from #emph[ă];: before a #emph[Ḥaṭeph]; the prefix takes the vowel of the #emph[Ḥaṭeph];, e.g. #he[לִפְרִי]; #emph[for fruit];, #he[כַּֽאֲרִי]; #emph[as a lion];, #he[בָּֽעֳנִי]; #emph[bŏʿ#super[o];nî];, #emph[in affliction]; (sometimes with the syllable subsequently closed, cf. #xref(<p28b>)[§ 28#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];, and the infinitives with #he[לַ]; #xref(<p63i>)[§ 63#h(0.18em)#emph[i];];): before weak consonants it follows the rule given in #xref(<p24c>)[§ 24#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];, e.g. #he[לִֽיהוּדָה]; for #he[לִיְ׳];. When the prefixes #he[בְ];, #he[וְ];, #he[כְ‍];, #he[לְ];, precede #he[אֱלֹהִים]; #emph[God];, the #emph[Š#super[e];wâ]; and #emph[Ḥaṭeph S#super[e];ghôl]; regularly coalesce in #emph[Ṣērê];, e.g. #he[בֵּֽאלֹהִים];, etc., for #he[בֶּֽאֱל׳];; so with suffixes #he[וֵאֽלֹהָיו];, etc. (once also in the sing. #he[לֵֽאלֹהוֹ]; #bref("Hab 1:11");); also regularly #he[לֵאמֹר]; #emph[to say];, for #he[לֶֽאֱמֹר];, see #xref(<p23d>)[§ 23#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];.

#ml("e") <p102e> (#emph[b];) When the prefixes precede the article, the #he[ה]; is almost always dropped, and they take its vowel. See further in #xref(<p35n>)[§ 35#h(0.18em)#emph[n];];.

#ml("f") <p102f> (#emph[c];) Immediately before the tone\-syllable, i.e. before monosyllables and dissyllables with the tone on the penultima (in the fore\-tone), they take #emph[Qameṣ]; (undoubtedly a lengthening of an original #emph[ă];, cf. #xref(<p26e>)[§ 26#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];, #xref(<p28a>)[§ 28#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];), but only in the following cases:

(#emph[aa];) #he[לָ]; before infinitives of the above\-mentioned forms, as #he[לָתֵת]; #emph[to give];, #he[לָדִין]; #emph[to judge];, #he[לָבֹז]; #emph[to plunder];, #he[לָגֹז]; #emph[to shear];, #he[לָחֹג]; #emph[to keep a festival];, #he[לָלֶ֫דֶת]; #emph[to bring forth];, #he[לָלֶ֫כֶת]; #emph[to go];, #he[לָקַ֫חַת]; #emph[to take];, except when the infinitive (as a #emph[nomen regens];) is closely connected with another word (especially its subject, #xref(<p115e>)[§ 115#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];), and consequently, as being in a sort of #emph[constr. state];, loses the principal tone, e.g. #he[לְצֵאת]; #bref("Exod 19:1");, #he[לְשֶׁ֫בֶת]; #bref("Gen 16:3");, and so always #he[לְבֹא חֲמָת]; #bref("Num 13:21");, etc. (in such cases as #he[לָֽתֶת־חֶ֫רֶב]; #bref("Exod 5:21"); the #emph[ā]; is protected by the secondary tone; before infinitives of verbs #he[ע״וּ];, the #he[לָ]; is retained even in close connection; cf. #bref("Ezek 21:20, 25");, #bref("22:3"););

#ml("g") <p102g> (#emph[bb];) before many pronominal forms, e.g. #he[בָּזֶה]; (so also in #bref("1 Sam 21:10");; not #he[בַּזֶּה];), #he[לָזֶה];, #he[כָּזֶה];, #he[לָזֹאת]; (in close connection, however, #he[לְזֹאת]; #bref("Gen 2:23");; #he[כְּזֹאת]; #bref("Gen 45:23");); #he[כָּאֵ֫לֶּה]; #emph[as these];; and especially #he[בָּכֶם];, #he[לָכֶם];, #he[כָּכֶם]; (#he[כָּכֵם];) and #he[בָּהֶם];, #he[לָהֶם];, #he[כָּהֵם]; (#he[כָּהֶם];), see #xref(<p103e>)[§ 103#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];;

#ml("h") <p102h> (#emph[cc];) #he[לָ]; before monosyllables or fore\-toned nouns in such combinations as #he[פֶּה לָפֶה]; #emph[mouth to mouth];, #bref("2 Kgs 10:21");, #he[בֵּין מַ֫יִם לָמָ֫יִם]; #emph[between waters and waters];, #bref("Gen 1:6");; #he[לָטֹ֫רַח]; #emph[for a trouble];, #bref("Isa 1:14");, but always before the principal pause. The instructive example in #bref("Deut 17:8"); also shows that the punctuation #he[לָ]; is only possible with at least the lesser pause after it; in #bref("Isa 28:10, 13"); the #he[לָ]; is twice repeated, even before the small and smallest disjunctives;

#ml("i") <p102i> (#emph[dd];) in certain standing expressions, which have become stereotyped almost as adverbs, e.g. #he[לָעַד]; #emph[to eternity];, #he[לָרֹב]; #emph[in multitude];, #he[לָבֶ֫טַח]; #emph[in security];, #he[לָנֶ֫צַח]; #emph[to eternity];, but #he[לְנֵ֫צַח נְצָחִים]; #emph[to all eternity];, #bref("Isa 34:10");. Cf. also #he[לָנֶ֫פֶשׁ]; #emph[for the dead];, #bref("Lev 19:28");, #bref("Num 5:2");, #bref("9:10");.

#ml("k") <p102k> (#emph[d];) With the interrogative #he[מָה]; they are pointed as in #he[בַּמֶּה];; in #emph[pause]; and before #he[א]; as in #he[בַּמָּה]; #emph[by what?]; (before a following relative clause, as in #bref("Eccl 3:22");, #he[בְּמֶה];; cf. Delitzsch, #emph[Jesaia];, 4th ed., on #bref("Isa 2:22");); #he[כַּמָּה]; #emph[how much];? but also #he[כַּמֶּה]; #bref("1 Kgs 22:16");, in close connection, and at a greater distance from the #emph[pause];. The #emph[S#super[e];ghôl]; in these forms arises from a modification of the original #emph[ă];, while the #he[מ‍]; is sharpened in order to maintain the original #emph[ă]; of the prefixes.

#ml("l") <p102l> When #he[לְ]; (prop. #emph[la];) is united to #he[מָה];, it takes, according to #xref(<p49f>)[§ 49#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];, #emph[g];, the form #he[לָ֫מָּה]; (#bref("Job 7:20"); #he[לָ֫מָה];, #bref("1 Sam 1:8"); #he[לָ֫מֶה];, all #emph[Milʿêl];, and hence the #emph[ă]; in the tone is lengthened to #emph[ā];) #emph[for what? why?]; Before the gutturals #he[א];, #he[ה];, #he[ע];, #he[לָמָ֫ה]; is used for euphonic reasons (exceptions #bref("1 Sam 28:15");, #bref("2 Sam 14:31");, #bref("Jer 15:18");, before #he[ה];; #bref("2 Sam 2:22");, #origpage("300") <orig-300>;#bref("Ps 49:6");, before #he[א];); #he[לָ֫מָּה];, however, remains before #he[ח];. Before letters which are not gutturals, #he[לָמָ֫ה]; is found in #bref("Ps 42:10");, #bref("43:2"); (immediately after a tone\-syllable).

#ml("m") <p102m> Rem. The divine name #he[יְהֹוָה];, which has not its original vowels (#he[יַהְוֶה];) but those of #he[אֲדֹנָי]; (see #xref(<p17c>)[§ 17#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];), except that the #he[י]; has simple not compound #emph[Š#super[e];wâ];, takes the prefixes also, after the manner of #he[אֲדֹנָי];, thus #he[וַֽיהוָֹה];, #he[לַֽיהוָֹה];, #he[בַּֽיהוָֹה];, #he[מֵֽיְהֹוָה]; (since they are to be read #he[וַֽאדֹנָי];, #he[לַֽאדֹנָי];, #he[בַּֽאדֹנָי];, #he[מֵֽאֲדֹנָי];); for the #he[א]; of #he[אֲדֹנָי];, as of #he[אֲדֹנִי];, #he[אֲדֹנִים];, etc. (see below), #emph[quiesces]; after the prefixes #he[בַּ];, #he[כַּ‍];, #he[לַ];, #he[וַ];, but is #emph[audible]; after #he[מֵ‍]; (for #he[מִן];), #he[שֶׁ]; (no instance in the O.T.), and #he[הָ]; (in #he[הָֽאֲדֹנִים]; #bref("Deut 10:17");, #bref("Ps 136:3");, the article, not #he[הַ]; interrog., is intended; the only example with #he[ה]; interrog., #bref("Jer 8:19");, is to be pointed #he[הַֽיהוָֹה];, i.e. #he[הַֽאדֹנָי];, not #he[הַיְהֹוָה];). Hence the rule, #he[משֶׁה מוֹצִיא]; #emph[Moses brought out]; (i.e. #he[מ‍];, #he[שׁ];, #he[ה]; make the #he[א]; audible), #he[וְכָלֵב מַכְנִיס]; #emph[and Caleb brought in];, (i.e. #he[ו];, #he[כ‍];, #he[ל];, #he[ב]; allow it to quiesce).#footnote[Another #emph[vox memor.]; is #he[כָּל־בּוֹ נֶֽעֶלָם]; #emph[all is hidden in him];.];—As regards the other plural forms of #he[אָדוֹן];, elision of the #he[א]; always takes place after #he[בַּ];, #he[וַ];, #he[כַּ‍];, #he[לַ];, except in the form #he[אֲדֹנֵי];, thus #he[לַֽאדֹנָיו];, #he[לַֽאדֹנֶ֫יךָ];, etc.; but #he[לַֽאֲדֹנֵי];, etc., #he[לַֽאֲדֹנֵ֫ינוּ];, etc., #he[לַֽאֲדֹֽנֵיהֶם];.

#sec("103", [Prepositions with Pronominal Suffixes and in the Plural Form], note: []) <s103>

#ml("a") <p103a> #strong[1.]; As all prepositions were originally nouns (#xref(<s101>)[§ 101];) in the accusative, they may be united with the noun\-suffixes (#xref(<p91b>)[§ 91#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];–#emph[l];), e.g. #he[אֶצְלִי]; (prop. at my side) #emph[by me];, #he[אִתִּי]; (in my proximity) #emph[with me];, #he[תַּחְתָּם]; (in their place) #emph[instead of them];, like the Latin #emph[mea causa];, #emph[for my sake];.

#ml("b") <p103b> Rem. 1. The preposition #he[אֵת]; (usually #he[אֶת־];) #emph[near, with];, is distinguished from #he[אֵת]; (see below, and #xref(<p117a>)[§ 117#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];, note 4), the sign of the definite accusative (#xref(<p117a>)[§ 117#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];), in its connection with suffixes, by a difference of pointing, the former making #he[אִתִּי];, #he[אִתְּךָ];, in #emph[pause]; #he[אִתָּךְ];, 2nd fem. #he[אִתָּךְ]; (#bref("Isa 54:10"); #he[אִתֵּךְ];), #he[אִתּוֹ];, #he[אִתָּהּ];, #he[אִתָּ֫נוּ];, #he[אִתְּכֶם];, #he[אִתָּם]; (also in the later books, especially in Kings, and always in Jer. and Ezek., incorrectly #he[אוֹתִי]; #emph[with me];; #he[מֵֽאוֹתָךְ]; #emph[from thee];, #bref("1 Kgs 20:25");; #he[מֵֽאֹתוֹ]; #emph[from him];, #bref("1 Kgs 22:7");; #he[אֹתָם]; #emph[with them];), while the latter retains its #emph[ô]; (obscured from #emph[â];) before the light suffixes, but before grave suffixes is pointed with #emph[S#super[e];ghôl];. This S#super[e];ghôl is to be explained, with Praetorius, #emph[ZDMG.]; lv. 369 f., as the modification of an #emph[ă]; which again was shortened from original #emph[â]; (in #emph[ʾâthî];, #emph[ʾâthô];, etc.) in a closed syllable (#emph[ʾăth\-hem];, etc.). The same shortening and modification of the original #emph[â]; takes place before words in close connection, hence #he[אֶת־כֹּל];, etc. When not in close connection, the toneless #he[אֶת]; becomes tone\-long #he[אֵת];, e.g. #he[אֵת הַשָּׁמַ֫יִם]; #bref("Gen 1:1");. Hence the following forms arise:—

#align(center)[

#gtable(columns: 6, [1.], [], [#he[אֹתִי]; #emph[me];.], [], [#he[אֹתָ֫נוּ]; #emph[us];.], [], [], [#emph[m.];], [#he[אֹֽתְךָ];, #emph[pause]; #he[אֹתָךְ];], [], [#he[אֶתְכֶם]; #emph[you];.], [2.], [], [], [#emph[thee];], [], [], [], [#emph[f.];], [#he[אֹתָךְ]; . . . . .], [], [. . . . . .], [], [], [#emph[m.];], [#he[אֹתוֹ]; #emph[him];.], [], [#he[אֹתָם];, rarely #he[אֶתְהֶם];], [#emph[them];], [3.], [], [], [], [], [], [], [#emph[f.];], [#he[אֹתָהּ]; #emph[her];.], [], [#he[אֶתְהֶן];, rarely #he[אֹתָן];], []);

];

#origpage("301") <orig-301>;Less common are the #emph[plene]; forms #he[אוֹתִי];, #he[אֽוֹתְךָ]; (#bref("Num 22:33"); #he[אֹֽתְכָה]; before #he[ה];), #he[אוֹתָךְ]; (#bref("Exod 29:35");, #he[אֹתָ֫כָה];), #he[אוֹתוֹ];, #he[אוֹתָהּ];, #he[אוֹתָ֫נוּ];, #he[אוֹתָם];. Moreover, for #he[אֶתְכֶם]; we find #he[אֽוֹתְכֶם]; #bref("Josh 23:15");; for #he[אֹתָם];, five times #he[אֶתְהֶם]; (#bref("Gen 32:1");, #bref("Exod 18:20");, etc.), and in #bref("Ezek 23:45"); #he[אֽוֹתְהֶם];; for #he[אֶתְהֶן]; (#bref("Gen 19:8");, etc. \[13 times\]), #he[אֹתָן]; (only found in #bref("Ezek 16:54");; #bref("Exod 35:26"); #he[אֹתָ֫נָה];; #bref("Ezek 34:21"); #he[אוֹתָ֫נָה];), and #he[אֽוֹתְהֶן]; #bref("Ezek 23:47");.—No instance of the 2nd fem. plur. #he[אֶתְכֶן]; occurs in the O.T.; in #bref("Song 2:7");, etc., #he[אֶתְכֶם]; is used instead.

#ml("c") <p103c> 2. The preposition #he[עִם־]; #emph[with]; (with suffixes on the model of stems #he[ע״ע];, #he[עִמִּי];, #he[עִמְּךָ]; \[#bref("1 Sam 1:26"); #he[עִמְּכָה];\], in #emph[pause]; #he[עִמָּךְ];; 2nd fem. #he[עִמָּךְ];; #he[עִמּוֹ];, #he[עִמָּהּ];) is united with the suffixes #he[נוּ];, #he[כֶם];, and #he[הֶם]; by a (pretonic) #emph[Qameṣ];, which causes the sharpening of the #emph[Mêm]; to be distinctly audible: #he[עִמָּ֫נוּ];, #he[עִמָּכֶם];, #he[עִמָּהֶם]; (so in #bref("Num 22:12");, #bref("Deut 29:16");, both in principal pause, and often in very late passages, otherwise #he[עִמָּם]; is generally used). In the first person, besides #he[עִמִּי];, we also find #he[עִמָּדִי]; (probably from original #he[ענדי];; cf. Arab. #emph[ʿinda];, beside, with).

#ml("d") <p103d> 3. It is but seldom that prepositions occur with verbal suffixes, as #he[תַּחְתֵּ֫נִי]; #bref("2 Sam 22:37, 40, 48"); (for which #bref("Ps 18:37, 40, 48"); #he[תַּחְתַּי];), #he[תַּחְתֶּ֫נָּה]; #bref("Gen 2:21"); and #he[בַּֽעֲדֵ֫נִי]; #bref("Ps 139:11"); (here probably for the sake of the rhyme with #he[יִשׁוּפֵּ֫נִי];).#footnote[#emph[Fînî]; and #emph[bînî]; (in me), in vulgar Arabic for #emph[fiyya]; and #emph[bî];, are compared by Socin. Brockelmann, #emph[ZA.]; xiv. 347, note 1, suggests that #he[תחתני];, #he[תחתנה];, #he[בעדני]; are later formations on the model of #he[מִמֶּ֫נִּי]; when its origin from the reduplication of the preposition had become obscured, but see below, #emph[m];.];

#ml("e") <p103e> #strong[2.]; When pronominal suffixes are added to the prefixes (§ 102), there appears occasionally, especially in the case of the shorter suffixes, an endeavour to lengthen the preposition, so as to give it more strength and body. Hence to #he[בְּ]; is appended the syllable #he[מוֹ]; (see below, #emph[k];), and #he[בְּ]; and #he[לְ]; take at least a full vowel, #he[בָּ]; and #he[לָ]; (#xref(<p102d>)[§ 102#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];, #emph[f];).—The following deviations from the analogy of the noun with suffixes are to be noticed (#emph[a];) in the pausal forms #he[בָּךְ];, #he[לָךְ];, #he[אֹתְךְ];, #he[אִתָּךְ];, #he[עִמָּךְ]; (not #emph[bèkhā];, etc.); (#emph[b];) in the similar forms with the suffix of the 2nd sing. fem. (not #emph[bēkh];, etc.) and in #he[בָּ֫נוּ];, #he[לָ֫נוּ];, #he[עִמָּ֫נוּ];, etc. (not #emph[bēnû];, etc.).

#ml("f") <p103f> (#emph[a];) #emph[#he[לְ]; with Pronominal Suffixes];.

