#gtable(columns: 4, [1. c. #emph[to me];.], [#he[לִי];], [#emph[to us];.], [#he[לָ֫נוּ];], [2. m. #emph[to thee];.], [#he[לְךָ]; (#he[לְכָה];), in #emph[pause]; #he[לָךְ];], [#emph[to you];.], [#he[לָכֶם];], [f.], [#he[לָךְ];.........], [], [\[#he[לָכֶן];#footnote[#he[לָכֶן]; does not occur in the O.T., by a mere accident, no doubt; #bref("Ezek 13:18"); #he[לָכֶ֫נָה];.];\] #he[לָכֶ֫נָה];], [3. m. #emph[to him];.], [#he[לוֹ];], [#emph[to them];], [#he[לָהֶם];, #he[לָהֵ֫מָּה]; poet. #he[לָ֫מוֹ];], [], [], [], [\[53 times\]#footnote[The question whether #he[לָ֫מוֹ]; can also stand for the sing. #he[לוֹ];, which Rödiger and recently W. Diehl (#emph[Das Pronomen pers. suff.... des Hebr.];, p. 20 f.) and P. Haupt (#emph[SBOT.]; on #bref("Prov 23:20");, a contraction of #emph[la\-humû];) have altogether denied, must be answered in the affirmative unless we conclude with Diehl and Haupt that #emph[all]; the instances concerned are due to corruptions of the text. It is true that in such places as #bref("Gen 9:26, 27");, #bref("Deut 33:2");, #bref("Isa 30:5");, #bref("Ps 73:10"); (all #emph[in]; or immediately before the principal pause; in #bref("Deut 33:2"); with #emph[Zaqeph qaṭon]; at least) #he[לָ֫מוֹ]; can be better explained as plural (in reference to collective nouns); and in #bref("Isa 53:8"); for #he[נֶ֫גַע לָ֫מוֹ]; we should read with the LXX #he[נִגַּע לַמָּ֫וֶת];. On the other hand, in #bref("Isa 44:15"); its explanation as plural would be extremely forced. Even then there would remain—presuming the traditional text to be correct—#he[פָּנֵ֫ימוֹ]; #bref("Ps 11:7"); and #he[כַּפֵּ֫ימוֹ]; #bref("Job 27:23");, as well as #he[עָלֵ֫ימוֹ];, three times, #bref("Job 20:23");, #bref("27:23"); (beside #he[עָלָיו];), and especially #bref("Job 22:2");. In all these places the most extreme exegetical artifices can only be avoided by simply admitting a singular suffix (\=#he[פָּנָיו];, #he[כַּפָּיו];, #he[עָלָיו];).—On the question of the antiquity of the suffixes in #he[מוֹ]; see #xref(<p91l>)[§ 91#h(0.18em)#emph[l];];.];], [f. #emph[to her];.], [#he[לָהּ];], [], [#he[לָהֶן];,#footnote[The form #he[לָהֵן]; in #bref("Ruth 1:13"); is Aramaic (\=#emph[therefore];).];#he[לָהֵ֫נָּה];]);

];

#origpage("302") <orig-302>;

#ml("g") <p103g> #he[בְּ]; takes suffixes in the same manner: #he[בִּי];, #he[בְּךָ]; (#bref("Exod 7:29");, #bref("2 Sam 22:30");, #bref("Ps 141:8"); #he[בְּכָה];, as in #bref("Gen 27:37");, #bref("2 Sam 18:22");, #bref("Isa 3:6"); #he[לְכָה]; \[for 2nd fem. #he[לָךְ]; the #emph[K#super[e];thîbh]; #he[לכי]; occurs in #bref("2 Kgs 4:2");, #bref("Song 2:13");, cf. #xref(<p91e>)[§ 91#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];\]), #he[בּוֹ];, etc.; except that for the 3rd plur., besides #he[בָּהֶם]; (especially in the later books) and #he[בָּהֵ֫מָּה]; (only in #bref("Exod 30:4");, #bref("36:1");, #bref("Hab 1:16");; #he[לָהֵ֫מָּה]; only in #bref("Jer 14:16");), the form #he[בָּם]; is also used; and for the feminine, besides #he[בָּהֵ֫נָּה]; (three times), #he[בָּהֵן]; is found fifteen times, and #he[בָּהֶן]; only in #bref("1 Sam 31:7");, #bref("Isa 38:16");, #bref("Ezek 42:14");.—According to the Masora, #he[לֹא]; is found fifteen times for #he[לוֹ]; (as conversely in #bref("1 Sam 2:16");, #bref("20:2"); #he[לוֹ]; for #he[לֹא];), e.g. #bref("Exod 21:8");, #bref("1 Sam 2:3");, #bref("Isa 9:2");, #bref("Ps 100:3"); (and, as has been conjectured, also #bref("Job 41:4");); cf. Delitzsch on #bref("Ps 100:3");.—In #bref("Num 32:42");, #bref("Zech 5:11");, #bref("Ruth 2:14");, the Masora requires #he[לָהֿ]; instead of #he[לָהּ]; (in all three places before a following tone\-syllable; cf. #xref(<p23k>)[§ 23#h(0.18em)#emph[k];];, and the analogous cases of the loss of #emph[Mappîq]; in #xref(<p58g>)[§ 58#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];, #xref(<p91e>)[§ 91#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];).

