#ml("k") <p103k> The syllable #he[מוֹ]; (in Arabic #emph[mâ]; #he[מָא];\=Heb. #he[מָה]; #emph[what];) in #he[כָּמ֫וֹנִי]; (probably from #he[כְּמָה אֲנִי];, prop. #emph[according to what I];, for #emph[as I];) is, in poetry, appended to the three simple prefixes #he[בְּ];, #he[כְּ‍];, #he[לְ];, even without suffixes, so that #he[בְּמוֹ];, #he[כְּמוֹ];, #he[לְמוֹ]; appear as independent words, equivalent in meaning to #he[בְּ];, #he[כְּ‍];, #he[לְ];. Poetry is here distinguished from prose by the use of longer forms; in the case of #he[מִן];, on the other hand, it prefers the shorter, which resemble the Syriac and Arabic.

#ml("l") <p103l> The form #he[כָּהֶם];, enclosed in brackets above, occurs only in #bref("2 Kgs 17:15"); (in #emph[pause];), #he[כָּהֵ֫מָּה]; only in #bref("Jer 36:32"); (in #emph[pause];); #he[כָּהֵן]; (Baer following Qimḥi #he[כָּהֶן];) only in #bref("Ezek 18:14");. Cf. Frensdorff, #emph[Massora Magna];, p. 234 ff.—For #he[כָּכֶם]; #emph[as ye];, Qimḥi requires #he[כָּכֵם]; (invariably or only in #bref("Job 16:4");?); in #bref("Josh 1:15");, #bref("Judg 8:2");, #bref("Ezra 4:2"); Baer gives #he[כָּכֶם];.

#ml("m") <p103m> With regard to #he[מִן]; with suffixes, #he[מִמֶּ֫נִּי]; #emph[from me]; is usually explained as arising, by a reduplication of #he[מִן];, from an original #he[מנמני];, just as #he[מִמֶּ֫נּוּ]; #emph[from him];, from #he[מנמנ\-הו];, identical in form with #he[מִמֶּ֫נּוּ];#footnote[The Babylonian Masora writes #he[מִמֵּ֫נוּ]; (to distinguish it from the 3rd sing.), which is justly blamed by Ibn Ezra.]; #emph[from us];, from #he[מנמנ\-נו];, while #he[מִמֶּ֫נָּה]; #emph[from her];, goes back to #he[מנמנה];. Far simpler, however, is Mayer Lambert’s explanation (#emph[REJ.]; xxiii. 302 ff.), that #he[מִמֶּ֫נִּי];, etc., have arisen from #he[מִנֶּנִּי];, etc., and that the forms of the suffixes are to be explained on the analogy of #he[אֵינֶ֫נִּי];, #he[עוֹדֶ֫נּוּ];, #he[תַּחְתֶּ֫נָּה];, #xref(<p100o>)[§ 100#h(0.18em)#emph[o];];.—The bracketed form #he[מִנֵּ֫הוּ];, for which Baer, following Qimḥi and others, writes #he[מִנֶּ֫הוּ];, occurs only in #bref("Ps 68:24");, and is there regarded by Delitzsch, Hupfeld, and others (following Simonis) as a substantive (#he[מֵן];\=#emph[portion];). The expression #he[מִן־הוּא]; (for #he[מִמֶּ֫נּוּ];?) #bref("Isa 18:2, 7"); is very strange.—#he[מֵהֵ֫מָּה]; occurs only in #bref("Jer 10:2");, #bref("Eccl 12:12"); (#bref("Job 11:20"); #he[מִנְּהֶ֑ם];); #he[מֵהֶן]; (so Baer and Ginsburg, following the best authorities, instead of the ordinary reading #he[מֵהֵן];) only in #bref("Ezek 16:47, 52");. #origpage("304") <orig-304>;

#ml("n") <p103n> #strong[3.]; Several prepositions, especially those which express relations of space and time, are (like the German #emph[wegen];) properly plural nouns (for the reason, see #xref(<p124a>)[§ 124#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];), and are, therefore, joined with the pronominal suffixes in the form of the plural #emph[construct state];, just like other plural nouns (#xref(<p91g>)[§ 91#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];). On the other hand, the apparent connection of #he[אֶל־];, #he[עַד־];, #he[עַל־]; with plural suffixes is explained from the ground\-forms of those prepositions (from stems #he[ל״ה];) #he[אֱלַי]; (#he[אֲלַי];), #he[עֲדַי];, #he[עֲלַי]; (contracted to #he[אֱלֵי];, #he[אֲלֵי];, etc.).#footnote[The reference of these forms to original plurals has been again expressly supported by De Lagarde, #emph[Symmicta];, ii. 101 ff.; #emph[Nachrichten der G. g. G.];, 1881, p. 376, cf. #emph[Mittheilungen];, 1884, p. 63; also #emph[GGA];. 1884, p. 280 f. According to Barth, #emph[ZDMG.]; xlii. p. 348 ff., and #emph[Nominalbildung];, p. 375 ff., #he[תַּחְתֶּ֫יךָ];, etc., was only formed on the analogy of #he[עָלֶ֫יךָ];, etc., and #he[אַֽחֲרֶ֫יךָ]; etc., only on the analogy of #he[לִפְנֵי];, etc., since the real plural forms ought to be #he[תְּחָתֶ֫יךָ];, #he[אֲחָרֶ֫יךָ];, etc.; cf., however, König, #emph[Lehrgebäude];, ii. 305 f.];

#ml("o") <p103o> Without suffixes these prepositions are—

#he[אַחַר];, more frequently #he[אַֽחֲרֵי]; (prop. #emph[hinder parts];) #emph[behind, after];.

#he[אֶל־];,#footnote[On the use of this particle see #xref(<p119g>)[§ 119#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];.]; poet. \[4 times in Job\] also #he[אֱלֵי]; (#emph[region, direction];), #emph[towards, to, according to];.

#he[בֵּין]; (#emph[interval];) #emph[between];; the suffixes indicating the singular are added to the singular #he[בֵּין];, thus #he[בֵּינִי];, #he[בֵּינְךָ];, etc. (#bref("Gen 16:5"); #he[בֵּינֶ֫יךָ];, the second #emph[Yôdh]; is, however, marked with a point as critically doubtful; #he[בֵּינָיו];, which occurs three times, is only the Masoretic #emph[Q#super[e];rê]; for #he[בֵּינוֹ];, which is found e.g. in #bref("Gen 30:36");). On the other hand, the suffixes indicating a plural are attached to the plural forms #he[בֵּינֵי]; or #he[בֵּינוֹת];.

#he[סָבִיב]; (#emph[circuit];) #emph[around];, as a preposition, always has the plural form, sometimes masc. #he[סְבִיבֶ֫יךָ];, etc. \[10 times\], but much more frequently in the fem. #he[סְבִיבוֹת]; (#emph[surroundings];). In #bref("Ezek 43:17"); #he[סָבִיב אוֹתָהּ]; is a corruption of #he[סְבִיבֹתֶ֫יהָ];; \[in #bref("1 Kgs 6:5"); #he[סָבִיב אֶת]; also is so contrary to usage, that it must be due to some textual error\].

#he[עַד־]; (#emph[continuation, duration];, from #he[עָדָה];) #emph[as far as, unto];, poet. #he[עֲדֵי]; \[12 times\]. In #bref("Job 32:12"); #he[עָֽדֵיכֶם];, with the #emph[ā]; retained in the secondary tone, is abnormal. Also in #bref("2 Kgs 9:18"); for #he[עַד־הֵם]; read #he[עָֽדֵיהֶם];.

#he[עַל־]; #emph[upon, over]; (cf. the rare subst. #he[עָל]; #emph[height]; \[see Lexicon\], from #he[עָלָה]; #emph[to ascend];), poet. #he[עֲלֵי]; \[40 times, and 2 #emph[Q#super[e];rê];\].

#he[תַּ֫חַת]; #emph[under]; (prop. #emph[what is beneath];). On #he[תַּחְתֵּ֫נִי];, etc.; cf. above, #emph[d];. #origpage("305") <orig-305>;#emph[With Suffixes.];

#ml("p") <p103p>

