ַּחְתֵּיהֶן];], [#he[אֲלֵיהֶן]; & #he[אֲלֵהֶן];], [], [#he[עֲלֵיהֶן];]);

];

#sec("104", [Conjunctions], note: []) <s104>

#ml("a") <p104a> #strong[1.]; The conjunctions serve to connect sentences, and to express their relations one to another. They may be either—

(#emph[a];) Original pronouns, e.g. the demonstrative #he[כִּי]; #emph[that, because, for];.

(#emph[b];) Original substantives, which afterwards were reduced to the rank of pronouns, adverbs, or conjunctions; so perhaps #he[אֲשֶׁר]; (see § 36), which is sometimes used to express the general idea of relation, sometimes as a relative pronoun (properly a demonstrative), but in many cases stands simply for #he[כִּי];; also #he[אַל־]; (#emph[nothing];), #emph[that not];; #he[פֶּן־]; #emph[that not]; (the Greek μή of #emph[prohibition];), etc. To these may be added the adverbial combination of substantives with prepositions, e.g. #he[בְּטֶ֫רֶם]; #origpage("306") <orig-306>;(#emph[in the not yet];) #emph[earlier, before];, for which #he[מִטֶ֫רֶם]; is also used. On the combination of two particles to express complex ideas (e.g. #he[אַף־כִּי]; #emph[added to this, that\=much more];), see the Syntax, #xref(<p163f>)[§ 163#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];.

#ml("b") <p104b> (#emph[c];) Prepositions, which with the addition of the conjunction #he[אֲשֶׁר]; or #he[כִּי]; together form one single conjunction, e.g. #he[יַ֫עַן אֲשֶׁר]; #emph[because];, prop. #emph[on account of the fact that];; #he[אַחַר אֲשֶׁר];, and more frequently #he[אַֽחֲרֵי אֲשֶׁר];, #emph[after that];; #he[כַּֽאֲשֶׁר]; #emph[according as]; (with #he[כְּ‍];); #he[עֵ֫קֶב כִּי]; and #he[עֵ֫קֶב אֲשֶׁר]; #emph[in consequence of the fact that, for the reason that, because];. Sometimes, however, the conjunction in such cases is omitted, and the preposition itself used as a conjunction, e.g. #he[עַל־]; (for #he[עַל־אֲשֶׁר];) #emph[although];, #bref("Job 16:17");.

So, at any rate, according to our linguistic principles. It would, however, be more correct to say, that instead of the intermediary #he[אֲשֶׁר]; the whole of the succeeding sentence is regarded as #emph[one]; substantival idea, under the immediate government of the preposition. In the same way, all prepositions governing the gerund in English may be paraphrased by conjunctions with the finite verb, see §§ 114 and 115, #emph[passim];.

#ml("c") <p104c> #strong[2.]; Besides those already mentioned, there are certain other small words now used as conjunctions, of which the derivation or original meaning is altogether obscure, thus #he[אוֹ]; #emph[or];, #he[אִם־]; #emph[if]; (also #emph[or]; before the second member of a double question), #he[אַף]; #emph[also];, #he[וְ]; #emph[and];, and others.

#ml("d") <p104d> Rem. The pointing of the #he[וְ]; (originally #he[וַ];, as still before #emph[Ḥaṭeph Pathaḥ]; and—with a following #emph[Dageš forte];—in #emph[wāw consecutive]; of the imperfect; cf. #xref(<p49f>)[§ 49#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];) is in many respects analogous to that of the prefixes #he[בְּ];, #he[כְּ‍];, #he[לְ]; (#xref(<p102d>)[§ 102#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];–#emph[i];), but as being a weak consonant, the #emph[wāw copulative]; has some further peculiarities:

(#emph[a];) Usually it takes simple #emph[Š#super[e];wâ]; (#he[וְ];).

(#emph[b];) Before words which begin with a guttural having a #emph[compound Š#super[e];wâ];, it takes the vowel with which the #emph[Š#super[e];wâ]; is compounded (according to #xref(<p28b>)[§ 28#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];), e.g. #he[וַֽחֲכַם]; #emph[and be thou wise];, #he[וַֽעֲבָדִים]; #emph[and servants];, #he[וֶֽעֱזוּז]; #emph[and strength];, #he[וֶֽאֱכֹל]; #emph[and eat thou];, #he[וָֽחֳלִי]; #emph[and sickness];. On #he[וֵאֽלֹהִים];, #he[וֵאֽלֹהַי]; etc., see #xref(<p102d>)[§ 102#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];; on #he[וַֽאדֹנַי];, etc., see #xref(<p102m>)[§ 102#h(0.18em)#emph[m];];; on such cases as #he[וַעְצֹר]; #bref("Job 4:2");, cf. #xref(<p28b>)[§ 28#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];.

#ml("e") <p104e> (#emph[c];) Before words with simple #emph[Š#super[e];wâ]; under the first consonant (except in the cases under #emph[f];), the #emph[Wāw]; becomes the vowel #emph[û]; (cf. #xref(<p26a>)[§ 26#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];), e.g. #he[וּלְכֹל]; #emph[and to all];, so also (except in the case under #emph[g];) before the cognate labials #he[ב];, #he[מ‍];, #he[פ];, hence #he[וּמֶ֫לֶךְ];. On the cases in which #emph[simple Š#super[e];wâ]; has become a #emph[Ḥaṭeph]; after #he[וּ]; #emph[copulative]; (e.g. #he[וּֽזֲהַב]; #bref("Gen 2:12");), cf. #xref(<p10g>)[§ 10#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];.

#ml("f") <p104f> (#emph[d];) With a following #he[יְ]; the #he[וְ]; coalesces to form #he[וִי]; according to #xref(<p24b>)[§ 24#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];, as #he[וִיהִי]; #emph[and let him be];. On the peculiar punctuation of the #emph[wāw copulative]; before forms with initial #emph[Š#super[e];wâ]; from #he[הָיָה]; #emph[to be]; and #he[חָיָה]; #emph[to live]; (e.g. #he[וִֽהְיִיתֶם]; #bref("Josh 8:4");, #he[וֶֽחְיֵה]; #bref("Gen 20:7");), cf. #xref(<p63q>)[§ 63#h(0.18em)#emph[q];];.

#ml("g") <p104g> (#emph[e];) Immediately before the tone\-syllable it frequently takes #emph[Qameṣ];, like #he[בְּ];, #he[כְּ‍];, #he[לְ]; (see #xref(<p102f>)[§ 102#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];), but in most cases only at the end of a sentence or clause (but cf. also #he[וָבֹא]; #bref("2 Kgs 22:30");), e.g. #he[וָמֵ֖ת]; #bref("Exod 21:12"); (on the other hand, in verse 20 #origpage("307") <orig-307>;#he[וּמֵ֖ת]; is in closer logical connection with what follows); #bref("2 Kgs 7:4"); #he[וָמַ֣תְנוּ שָׁ֔ם];, #he[וָמָ֑תְנוּ]; and #he[וָמָֽתְנוּ];; #bref("Ruth 3:3"); #he[וָסַכְתְּ];; #bref("Ps 10:15"); #he[וָ֝רָע];; #bref("1 Sam 9:4"); #he[וָאַ֔יִן];; #bref("2 Sam 13:26"); #he[וָלֹ֔א];; #bref("Ezek 47:9"); #he[וָחָ֔י];; cf. also (with #emph[Ṭiphḥa];) #bref("Gen 33:13");, #bref("2 Sam 15:12");. The very frequent connection of nouns expressing kindred ideas, by means of #he[וָ];, is due simply to considerations of rhythm, for even in such cases the #emph[Wāw]; must immediately precede the tone\-syllable, which must be marked by a disjunctive accent, e.g. #he[תֹּ֫הוּ וָבֹ֫הוּ]; #bref("Gen 1:2");, #he[יוֹם וָלַ֫יְלָה]; #bref("Gen 8:22"); (see also the previous examples); #bref("Gen 13:14"); (thrice); #bref("Exod 25:3"); #he[זָהָב וָכֶ֫סֶף];; #bref("Ps 96:7"); #he[כָּבוֹד וָעֹז];; #bref("Ps 76:7"); #he[וְרֶ֫כֶב וָסוּס];; #bref("Gen 7:13"); #he[נֹחַ וְשֵׁם־וְחָם וָיֶ֫פֶת];; #bref("1 Kgs 21:10"); #he[אֱלֹהִים וָמֶ֑לֶךְ];; #he[כֹּה וָכֹה]; #emph[thus and thus];; #bref("Esth 1:8"); #he[אִישׁ־וָאִֽישׁ]; at the end of the verse, but in #bref("Ps 87:5"); #he[אִישׁ וְאִישׁ]; in spite of the #emph[D#super[e];ḥi]; with the second #he[אִישׁ];, because it is closely connected with the following predicate. Also with three words #he[פַּ֫חַד וָפַ֫חַת וָפָ֑ח]; #bref("Isa 24:17");. On the other hand, the rapid pronunciation #he[וְ]; occurs before a conjunctive accent (and, when farther removed from the principal pause, even with the smaller disjunctives, in spite of a following tone\-syllable), e.g. #he[צֹאן וְעֶ֣בֶד]; #bref("Gen 32:6");; cf. #bref("Gen 31:40");, #bref("Lev 7:23");, #bref("Deut 2:21");, and among the examples given above, #bref("Gen 7:13"); and #bref("Ps 76:7");. (Exceptions: #he[וָקֵ֥דְמָה]; #bref("Gen 13:14");, where evidently the #he[וָ]; is intended to ensure the slow and solemn recitation of the promise, but also #he[וָזִ֥יף]; #bref("Josh 15:55");, #he[וָעֵ֣תֶר]; #bref("19:7");, #he[וָבֶ֥טֶן]; #bref("19:25");, all immediately before the pause.) For the same rhythmical reason #he[וְ]; (not #he[וָ];) is used regularly with certain monosyllables which, by their nature, lean more closely upon the following word, thus #he[וְזֶה];, #he[וְאֵת];, #he[וְגַם];, #he[וְלֹא]; (to be distinguished from #he[וָלֹ֔א]; #emph[if not];, with #emph[Zaqeph gadol];, #bref("2 Kgs 5:17");), and others.

#sec("105", [Interjections], note: []) <s105>

#ml("a") <p105a> #strong[1.]; Among the interjections some (as in all languages) are simply natural sounds, or, as it were, vocal gestures, called forth involuntarily by certain impressions or sensations, e.g. #he[אֲהָהּ]; (#bref("Ezek 30:2"); #he[הָהּ];), #he[אָח]; #emph[ah!]; #he[הֶאָח]; #emph[aha!]; (cf. this #he[אָח]; also in #he[אַחְלַי]; and #he[אַֽחֲלֵי]; #emph[utinam!];), #he[אָֽנָּ֫א]; #bref("Exod 32:31");, etc. (#bref("Gen 50:17"); #he[אָ֣נָּ֫א];) #emph[ah!]; (from #he[אָהּ]; and #he[נָא];), otherwise written #he[אָֽנָּ֫ה]; #bref("2 Kgs 20:3");, #bref("Jonah 1:14");, #bref("Ps 116:4");; also #he[הַס]; (in pause #he[הָס];, even in the plural #he[הַ֫סּוּ]; #emph[hold your peace!]; #bref("Neh 8:11");) #emph[hush!]; #he[הוֹי]; (#bref("Amos 5:16"); #he[הוֹ־הוֹ];) #emph[ha! woe!]; #he[אוֹי];, #he[א֫וֹיָה]; (#bref("Ps 120:5");), #he[אִי]; (in #he[אִילוֹ]; #bref("Eccl 4:10");; #he[אִי־לָךְ]; #bref("10:16");) #emph[woe!];

#ml("b") <p105b> #strong[2.]; Others, however, originally expressed independent ideas, and become interjections only by rapid pronunciation and by usage, e.g. #he[(הֵא) הֵן]; or #he[הִנֵּה]; #emph[behold!]; (prop. #emph[here];); #he[רְאֵה]; #emph[behold!]; (prop. imperative); #he[הָ֫בָה];, plur. #he[הָבוּ]; (prop. #emph[give];, imperative of #he[יָהַב];; as to the tone, cf. #xref(<p69o>)[§ 69#h(0.18em)#emph[o];];), #emph[come];, the Latin #emph[age];, #emph[agite!]; #he[לְכָה]; (also #he[לְךָ];), #he[לְכוּ]; (prop. #emph[go];, imperative of #he[הָלַךְ];) with the same meaning#footnote[#he[רְאֵה]; (#bref("Deut 1:8");), #he[הָ֫בָה]; and #he[לְכָה]; are also used in connection with the feminine and the plural, which proves that they have become quite stereotyped as interjections.];; #he[חָלִ֫ילָה]; #emph[far be it!]; (prop. #emph[ad profanum!];) #origpage("308") <orig-308>;#he[בִּי]; (see the Lexicon) #emph[I beseech, hear me!]; #he[נָא]; #emph[pray!];#footnote[#he[נָא]; serves to express the most various shades of expression, which are discussed in the various parts of the syntax. It is used especially (#emph[a];) after the #emph[imperative];, either in commands or entreaty, see #xref(<p110d>)[§ 110#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];; (#emph[b];) with the #emph[imperfect];, either in the cohortative (#xref(<p108b>)[§ 108#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];) or jussive (#xref(<p109b>)[§ 109#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];); (#emph[c];) once with #emph[perfect];, #bref("Gen 40:14");; (#emph[d];) after various particles: #he[הִנֵּה־נָא]; #emph[behold now];; particularly after the conjunctions #he[אַל]; and #he[אִם];: #he[אַל־נָא]; #emph[ne quaeso]; and #he[אִם־נָא]; #emph[if now];, #greek[εἴπερ, εἴποτε]; #emph[if];, in a deprecatory sense, expressive of politeness or modesty. In #bref("Num 12:13"); #he[נָא]; stands after a noun; but we ought certainly to read #he[אַל־נָא];.—In polite language this particle is used constantly in all these ways, #bref("Gen 18:3, 4");, #bref("19:7, 8, 19");, and #bref("50:17");.]; used to emphasize a demand, warning, or entreaty, and always placed after the expression to which it belongs.#footnote[Against the usual view which regards #he[נָא]; as a hortatory particle (\=#emph[up! come!]; analogous to the original imperatives #he[הָ֫בָה]; and #he[לְכָה]; and the Ethiopic #emph[năʿâ];, properly #emph[hither];, also #emph[come!];), P. Haupt, in the #emph[Johns Hopkins University Circulars];, xiii, no. 114, p. 109, justly observes that we should then expect the particle to be #emph[prefixed]; to the imperative, etc. He proposes to describe #he[נָא]; as an emphatic particle. Haupt’s suggested identification of this #he[נָא]; with the Assyrian, Arabic, and Ethiopic particle #emph[mā]; (which is also an enclitic of emphasis), and ultimately with the interrogative #emph[mā];, we shall not discuss here.];

#bookhead(1, [Third Part: Syntax], note: [#footnote[Recent works on Hebrew syntax are: A. B. Davidson, #emph[Introductory Heb. Gram.];, vol. ii, #emph[Heb. Syntax];, Edinburgh, 1894; Ed. König. #emph[Hist.\-compar. Syntax der hebr. Sprache];, Lpz. 1897 (see above, #xref(<p3f>)[§ 3#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];). Important contributions to Hebrew syntax are also contained in H. Reckendorf’s work #emph[Die syntakt. Verhältnisse des Arab.];, 2 pts., Leiden, 1895, 1898, of which we have already made use in #xref(<p97a>)[§ 97#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];. Cf. also the same author’s very instructive discussions #emph[Ueber syntakt. Forschung];, Munich, 1899.];]);

#bookhead(2, [Chapter I. The Parts of Speech], note: []);

#bookhead(3, [I. Syntax of the Verb], note: []);

#bookhead(3, [A. Use of the Tenses and Moods], note: [#footnote[Cf. the sketch of the tenses and moods used in Hebrew in § 40; and on the general characteristics of the perfect and imperfect see the note on #xref(<p47a>)[§ 47#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];; also Driver, #emph[A Treatise on the Use of the Tenses in Hebrew]; (Oxford, 1874; 3rd ed. 1892); Bennett, ‘Notes on the Use of the Hebrew Tenses’ (#emph[Hebraica];, 1886, vols. ii, iii). A partial modification of the accepted definition of the Semitic perfect and imperfect was proposed by J. A. Knudtzon, #emph[Om det saakaldte Perfektum og Imperfektum i Hebraisk];, Kristiania, 1890; of which a summary entitled ‘Vom sogenannten Perf. und Imperf. im Hebr.’ appeared in the #emph[Transactions of the Oriental Congress at Stockholm];, section sémitique #emph[b];, p. 73 ff. (Leiden, 1893). Cf. also Knudtzon’s articles, ‘Zur assyrischen und allgemein semitischen Grammatik’ in the #emph[Zeitschrift für Assyriologie];, especially vi. 422 ff. and vii. 33 ff.];]);

#origpage("309") <orig-309>;

#sec("106", [Use of the Perfect], note: []) <s106>

#ml("a") <p106a> The perfect serves to express actions, events, or states, which the speaker wishes to represent from the point of view of completion, whether they belong to a determinate past time, or extend into the present, or, while still future, are pictured as in their completed state.

The definition formerly given here (‘the perfect serves to express #emph[completed]; actions’) applies, strictly speaking, only to some of the varieties of the perfect discussed under #emph[b];–#emph[p];: hence the above modification based on the arguments of Knudtzon (for the title see note 2, and cf. further #xref(<p107a>)[§ 107#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];).

More particularly the uses of the perfect may be distinguished as follows:—

#ml("b") <p106b> #strong[1.]; To represent actions, events, or states, which, after a shorter #origpage("310") <orig-310>;or longer duration, were terminated in the past, and hence are finally concluded, namely :

(#emph[a];) Corresponding to the perfect proper in Latin and the English perfect definite, in assertions, negations, confirmations, interrogations, etc., e.g. #bref("Gen 18:15"); #emph[then Sarah denied, saying, I laughed not]; (#he[לֹא צָחַ֫קְתִּי];)......; #emph[and he said, Nay, but thou didst laugh]; (#he[צָחָקְתְּ];); #bref("Gen 3:11"); #he[מִי הִגִּיד לְךָ]; #emph[who told thee....?]; Cf. #bref("3:13, 14, 17, 22");. Also pointing to some undefined time in the past, e.g. #bref("Isa 66:8"); #he[מִֽי־שָׁמַע כָּזֹאת]; #emph[who hath (ever yet) heard such a thing?];

#ml("c") <p106c> Rem. In opposition to this express use of the perfect to emphasize the completion of an event, the imperfect is not infrequently used to emphasize that which is still future, e.g. #bref("Josh 1:5"); #emph[as I was]; (#he[הָיִיתִי];) #emph[with Moses, so will I be]; (#he[אֶֽהְיֶה];) #emph[with thee;]; #bref("Josh 1:17");, #bref("Exod 10:14");, #bref("Deut 32:21");, #bref("1 Kgs 2:38");, #bref("Isa 46:4, 11");, #bref("Joel 2:2");, #bref("Eccl 1:9");.

#ml("d") <p106d> (#emph[b];) As a simple #emph[tempus historicum]; (corresponding to the Greek aorist) in narrating past events, e.g. #bref("Gen 4:4"); #emph[and Abel, he also brought]; (#he[הֵבִיא];), etc.; #bref("Gen 7:19"); #emph[the waters did prevail]; (#he[גָּֽבְרוּ];), etc.; #bref("Job 1:1"); #emph[there was a man]; (#he[אִישׁ הָיָה];) #emph[in the land of Uz];, etc.; even in relating repeated actions, #bref("1 Sam 18:30");.

#ml("e") <p106e> Rem. As the above examples indicate, the perfect of #emph[narration]; occurs especially at the head of an entire narrative (#bref("Job 1:1");; cf. #bref("Dan 2:1");) or an independent sentence (e.g. #bref("Gen 7:11, 13");), but in co\-ordinate sentences, as a rule, only when the verb is separated from the copulative #he[ו]; by one or more words (cf. above #bref("Gen 4:4"); and #bref("7:19");). In other cases, the narrative is continued in the imperfect consecutive, according to #xref(<p111a>)[§ 111#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];. The direct connection of the narrative perfect with #he[ו]; copulative (not to be confounded with the perfect consecutive proper, § 112) agrees rather with Aramaic syntax (cf. Kautzsch, #emph[Gramm. des Biblisch\-Aram.];, § 71, 1 b). On the examples (which are in many respects doubtful) in the earlier texts, see #xref(<p112pp>)[§ 112#h(0.18em)#emph[pp];];–uu.

#ml("f") <p106f> (#emph[c];) To represent actions, etc., which were already completed in the past, at the time when other actions or conditions took place (pluperfect),#footnote[Cf. P. Haupt in the #emph[Notes on Esther];, 9:2.]; e.g. #bref("1 Sam 28:3"); #emph[now Samuel was (long since) dead];#footnote[Incorrectly, e.g. in the Vulgate, #emph[Samuel autem mortuus est... et Saul abstulit magos];, etc.];... #emph[and Saul had put away]; (#he[הֵסִיר];) #emph[those that had familiar spirits... out of the land];. Both these statements, being as it were in parentheses, merely assign a reason for the narrative beginning at verse 6. Cf. #bref("1 Sam 9:15");, #bref("25:21");, #bref("2 Sam 18:18");.—#bref("Gen 20:18"); (#emph[for the Lord had fast closed up];, etc.); #bref("27:30");, #bref("31:19, 34");, #bref("Deut 2:10");; and in a negative statement, #bref("Gen 2:5"); #emph[for the Lord God had not]; (up to that time) #emph[caused it to rain];, etc. This is especially frequent, from the nature of the case, in relative, causal, and temporal clauses, when the main clause contains a tense referring to the past, e.g. #bref("Gen 2:2"); #emph[and he rested... from all his work which he had made]; (#he[עָשָׂה];); #bref("Gen 7:9");, #origpage("311") <orig-311>;19:27, etc.; 29:10 #emph[now when Jacob had seen Rachel]; (#he[כַּֽאֲשֶׁר רָאָה];)..., #emph[Jacob went near];, etc.; so also in clauses which express the completion or incompleteness of one action, etc., on the occurrence of another, as in #bref("Gen 24:15");, #bref("27:30");, etc.; cf. #xref(<p164b>)[§ 164#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];, with the note, and #emph[c];.

#ml("g") <p106g> #strong[2.]; To represent actions, events, or states, which, although completed in the past, nevertheless extend their influence into the present (in English generally rendered by the present):

(#emph[a];) Expressing facts which were accomplished long before, or conditions and attributes which were acquired long before, but of which the effects still remain in the present (present perfect), e.g. #bref("Ps 10:11"); #he[הִסְתִּיר פָּנָיו]; #emph[he hath hidden his face]; (#emph[and still keeps it hidden];); #bref("Ps 143:6"); #he[פֵּרַ֫שְׂתִּי]; #emph[I have spread forth my hands]; (#emph[and still keep them spread forth];). This applies particularly to a large number of perfects (almost exclusively of intransitive#footnote[With regard to the great but very natural preponderance of intransitive verbs (expressing an existing state), cf. the lists in Knudtzon (see above, p. 309, note 2), pp. 117 and 122 in the Danish text.]; verbs, denoting affections or states of the mind) which in English can be rendered only by the present, or, in the case mentioned above under #emph[f];, by the imperfect.#footnote[Cf. #emph[novi, odi, memini];; οἶδα, μέμνημαι, ἔοικα, δέδορκα, κέκραγα; in the New Testament, ἤλπικα, ἠγάπηκα.]; Thus, #he[יָדַ֫עְתִּי]; #emph[I know]; (prop. #emph[I have perceived, have experienced];) #bref("Job 9:2");, #bref("10:13");, #he[לֹא יָדַ֫עְתִּי]; #emph[I know not]; #bref("Gen 4:9");, etc.; on the other hand, e.g. in #bref("Gen 28:16");, #bref("Num 22:34");, the context requires #emph[I knew not];; #he[זָכַ֫רְנוּ]; #emph[we remember]; #bref("Num 11:5");; #he[מֵֽאֲנָה]; #emph[she refuseth]; #bref("Job 6:7");; #he[עָלַץ]; #emph[it exulteth];; #he[שָׂמַ֫חְתִּי]; #emph[I rejoice]; #bref("1 Sam 2:1");; #he[בִּקֵּשׁ]; #emph[he requireth]; #bref("Isa 1:12");; #he[קִוִּ֫יתִי]; #emph[I wait]; #bref("Gen 49:18");, #bref("Ps 130:5"); (parallel with #he[הוֹחָֽ֫לְתִּי];); #he[חָפַ֫צְתִּי]; #emph[I delight]; #bref("Ps 40:9"); (mostly negative, #bref("Isa 1:11");, etc.); #he[אָהַ֫בְתִּי]; #emph[I love]; #bref("Gen 27:4");; #he[שָׂנֵ֫אתִי]; #emph[I hate]; #bref("Ps 31:7");; #he[מָאַ֫סְתִּי]; #emph[I despise]; #bref("Amos 5:21");; #he[תִּֽעֲב֫וּנִי]; #emph[they abhor me]; #bref("Job 30:10");; #he[בָּטַ֫חְתִּי]; #emph[I trust]; #bref("Ps 25:2");; #he[חָסִ֫יתִי]; #emph[I put my trust]; #bref("Ps 31:2");; #he[צָדַ֫קְתִּי]; #emph[I am righteous]; #bref("Job 34:5");; #he[פָּקַ֫דְתִּי]; #emph[I have decided to requite]; #bref("1 Sam 15:2");.—We may further include a number of verbs which express bodily characteristics or states, such as #he[גָּדַ֫לְתָּ]; #emph[thou art great]; #bref("Ps 104:1");; #he[קָטֹ֫נְתִּי]; #emph[I am little]; #bref("Gen 32:11");; #he[גָּֽבְהוּ]; #emph[they are high]; #bref("Isa 55:9");; #he[רָֽחֲקוּ]; #emph[they stand aloof]; #bref("Job 30:10");; #he[טֹ֫בוּ]; #emph[they are goodly]; #bref("Num 24:5");; #he[נָאווּ]; #emph[they are beautiful]; #bref("Isa 52:7");; #he[זָקַ֫נְתִּי]; #emph[I am old]; #bref("Gen 18:13");; #he[יָגַ֫עְתִּי]; #emph[I am weary]; #bref("Ps 6:7");; #he[שָׂבַ֫עְתִּי]; #emph[I am full]; #bref("Isa 1:11");, etc.

#ml("h") <p106h> Rem. To the same category probably belong also the perfects after #he[עַד־מָתַי]; #bref("Exod 10:3"); #emph[how long hast thou]; already #emph[been refusing]; (and refusest still...? which really amounts to #emph[how long wilt thou refuse];?), #bref("Ps 80:5");, #bref("Prov 1:22"); (co\-ordinate with the imperf.), and after #he[עַד־אָ֫נָה]; #bref("Exod 16:28");, #bref("Hab 1:2");.

#ml("i") <p106i> (#emph[b];) In direct narration to express actions which, although really only in process of accomplishment, are nevertheless meant to be repre\- #origpage("312") <orig-312>;sented as already accomplished in the conception of the speaker, e.g. #he[הֲרִמֹ֫תִי]; #emph[I lift up]; (my hand in ratifying an oath) #bref("Gen 14:22");; #he[נִשְׁבַּ֫עְתִּי]; #emph[I swear]; #bref("Jer 22:5");; #he[הַֽעִדֹ֫תִי]; #emph[I testify]; #bref("Deut 8:19");; #he[יָעַ֫צְתִּי]; #emph[I counsel]; #bref("2 Sam 17:11"); (but in a different context in ver. 15, #emph[I have counselled];); #he[אָמַ֫רְתִּי]; (prop. #emph[I say];) #emph[I decide]; (#emph[I consider as hereby settled];) #bref("2 Sam 19:30");; #emph[I declare]; #bref("Job 9:22");, #bref("32:10");.

#ml("k") <p106k> (#emph[c];) To express facts which have formerly taken place, and are still of constant recurrence, and hence are matters of common experience (the Greek #emph[gnomic aorist];), e.g. #bref("Ps 9:11"); #emph[for thou, Lord, hast not forsaken]; (#he[לֹא־עָזַ֫בְתָּ];) #emph[them that seek thee];. Cf. ver. 13, also #bref("Ps 10:3");, #bref("119:40"); and #bref("Gen 49:11"); (#he[כִּבֵּס];).

#ml("l") <p106l> Rem. In almost all the cases discussed in No. 2 (included under the English present) the imperfect can be used instead of the perfect, wherever the action or state in question is regarded, not as already completed, but as still continuing or just taking place (see #xref(<p107a>)[§ 107#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];). Thus, #he[לֹא יָכֹ֫לְתִּי]; #emph[I am not able]; #bref("Ps 40:13"); and #he[לֹא אוּכַל]; #bref("Gen 31:35"); have practically the same meaning. Hence also it very frequently happens that the imperfect corresponds to such perfects in poetic or prophetic parallelism, e.g. #bref("Isa 5:12");, #bref("Ps 2:1"); f., #bref("Prov 1:22");, #bref("Job 3:17");.

#ml("m") <p106m> #strong[3.]; To express #emph[future]; actions, when the speaker intends by an express assurance to represent them as finished, or as equivalent to accomplished facts:

(#emph[a];) In contracts or other express stipulations (again corresponding to the English present, and therefore closely related to the instances noted under #emph[i];), e.g. #bref("Gen 23:11"); #emph[the field I give]; (#he[נָתַ֫תִּי];) #emph[thee];; cf. ver. 13 and #bref("48:22");, #bref("2 Sam 14:21");, #bref("24:23");, #bref("Jer 40:4");; in a threat, #bref("1 Sam 2:16");, #bref("2 Sam 5:6"); (unless, with Wellhausen, #he[יְסִירֻ֫ךָ]; is to be read).—Especially in promises made by God, #bref("Gen 1:29");, #bref("15:18");, #bref("17:20");, #bref("Judg 1:2");.

#ml("n") <p106n> (#emph[b];) To express facts which are undoubtedly imminent, and, therefore, in the imagination of the speaker, already accomplished (#emph[perfectum confidentiae];), e.g. #bref("Num 17:27"); #he[הֵן גָּוַ֫עְנוּ אָבַ֫דְנוּ כֻּלָּ֫נוּ אָבָֽ֫דְנוּ]; #emph[behold, we perish, we are undone, we are all undone];. #bref("Gen 30:13");, #bref("Isa 6:5"); (#he[נִדְמֵ֫יתִי]; #emph[I am undone];#footnote[Cf. the similar use of ὄλωλα (διέφθορας, #emph[Il.]; 15. 128) and #emph[perii!]; On the kindred use of the perfect in conditional sentences, cf. below, #emph[p];.];), #bref("Prov 4:2");. Even in interrogative sentences, #bref("Gen 18:12");, #bref("Num 17:28");, #bref("23:10");, #bref("Judg 9:9, 11");, #bref("Zech 4:10"); (?), #bref("Prov 22:20");.#footnote[In #bref("Gen 40:14"); #emph[a perf. confidentiae]; (after #he[כִּי אִם];; but cf. #xref(<p163d>)[§ 163#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];) appears to be used in the expression of an earnest desire that something may happen (#emph[but have me in thy remembrance];, etc.). Neither this passage, however, nor the use of the perfect in Arabic to express a wish or imprecation, justifies us in assuming the existence of a #emph[precative]; perfect in Hebrew. In #bref("Job 21:16");, #bref("22:18");, also, translate #emph[the counsel of the wicked is far from me];. Cf. Driver, #emph[Tenses];#super[3];, p. 25 f. In #bref("Isa 43:9"); either #he[נִקְבְּצוּ]; is #emph[imperative]; (see #xref(<p51o>)[§ 51#h(0.18em)#emph[o];];) or we must read #he[יִקָּֽבְצוּ];, corresponding to #he[יֵאָֽסְפוּ]; which follows.]; This use of the perfect occurs most frequently in prophetic language (#emph[perfectum propheticum];). The prophet so trans\- #origpage("313") <orig-313>;ports himself in imagination into the future that he describes the future event as if it had been already seen or heard by him, e.g. #bref("Isa 5:13"); #emph[therefore my people are gone into captivity]; (#he[גָּלָה];); #bref("9:1"); ff., #bref("10:28");, #bref("11:9"); (after #he[כִּי];, as frequently elsewhere); #bref("19:7");, #bref("Job 5:20");, #bref("2 Chr 20:37");. Not infrequently the imperfect interchanges with such perfects either in the parallel member or further on in the narrative.

#ml("o") <p106o> (#emph[c];) To express actions or facts, which are meant to be indicated as existing in the future in a completed state (#emph[futurum exactum];), e.g. #bref("Isa 4:4"); #he[אִם רָחַץ]; #emph[when he has washed away];\=#emph[when he shall have washed away]; (an #emph[imperfect]; follows in the co\-ordinate sentence; cf. the conditional sentences in #xref(<p107x>)[§ 107#h(0.18em)#emph[x];];); #bref("Isa 6:11"); (after #he[עַד אֲשֶׁר אִם];, as in #bref("Gen 28:15");, #bref("Num 32:17");; also #bref("2 Sam 17:13"); after #he[עַד אֲשֶׁד];, #bref("Gen 24:19"); after #he[עַד אִם]; and elsewhere frequently after temporal conjunctions); #bref("Mic 5:2"); (#he[יָלָ֑דָה];); #bref("Gen 43:14"); #he[וַֽאֲנִי כַּֽאֲשֶׁר שָׁכֹ֫לְתִּי שָׁכָֽלְתִּי]; #emph[and I];—#emph[if I am bereaved]; (#emph[orbus fuero];), #emph[I am bereaved];, an expression of despairing resignation. Cf. #bref("Prov 23:15");, #bref("Esth 4:16");.

#ml("p") <p106p> #strong[4.]; To express actions and facts, whose accomplishment in the past is to be represented, not as actual, but only as possible (generally corresponding to the Latin imperfect or pluperfect subjunctive), e.g. #bref("Gen 31:42"); #emph[except the God of my father];... #emph[had been with me, surely now hadst thou sent me away empty]; (#he[שִׁלַּחְתָּ֑נִי];); #bref("Gen 43:10");, #bref("Exod 9:15"); (#he[שָׁלַ֫חְתִּי]; #emph[I had]; almost #emph[put forth];, etc.); #bref("Num 22:33");, #bref("Judg 13:23");, #bref("14:18");, #bref("1 Sam 13:13"); (#he[הֵכִּין];); #bref("2 Kgs 13:19");; so frequently after #he[כִּמְעַט]; #emph[easily, almost];, #bref("Gen 26:10");, #bref("Isa 1:9"); (where #he[כִּמְעַט]; is probably to be connected with the word after it), #bref("Ps 73:2");, #bref("94:17");, #bref("119:87");, #bref("Prov 5:14");. Cf. also #bref("Job 3:13");, #bref("23:10"); (#he[בְּחָנַ֫נִי];), #bref("Ruth 1:12"); (#emph[if I should think];, etc.; cf. #bref("2 Kgs 7:4");); in the apodosis of a conditional sentence, #bref("1 Sam 25:34");.—So also to express an unfulfilled desire, #bref("Num 14:2"); #he[לוּ מַ֫תְנוּ]; #emph[would that we had died...!]; (#he[לוּ]; with the imperfect would mean #emph[would that we might die!]; #bref("1 Sam 14:30");). Finally, also in a question indicating astonishment, #bref("Gen 21:7"); #he[מִי מִלֵּל]; #emph[who would have said...? quis dixerit?]; #bref("Ps 73:11");.

#sec("107", [Use of the Imperfect], note: [#footnote[Cf. the literature cited above, p. 309, note 2.];]) <s107>

#ml("a") <p107a> The imperfect, as opposed to the perfect, represents actions, events, or states which are regarded by the speaker at any moment as still continuing, or in process of accomplishment, or even as just taking place. In the last case, its occurrence may be represented as certainly imminent, or merely as conceived in the mind of the speaker, or simply as desired, and therefore only contingent (the modal use of the imperfect). #origpage("314") <orig-314>;Knudtzon (see above, Rem. on #xref(<p106a>)[§ 106#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];), comparing the Ass.\-Bab. usage, would prefer the term #emph[present]; rather than imperfect, on the ground that the tense expresses what is either actually or mentally present. In any case, the essential difference between the perfect and imperfect consists, he argues, in this, that the perfect simply indicates what is actually complete, while the imperfect places the action, etc., in a more direct relation to the judgement or feeling of the speaker.

More precisely the imperfect serves—

#strong[1.]; In the sphere of #emph[past time];:

#ml("b") <p107b> (#emph[a];) To express actions, etc., which #emph[continued]; throughout a longer or shorter period,#footnote[Cf. the #emph[Mêšaʿ]; inscription, l. 5, #he[כי יאנף כמש בארצה]; #emph[for Chemosh was angry with his land];. As Driver, #emph[Tenses];, 3rd ed., § 27, 1 a, remarks, this vivid realization of the accomplishment of the action is especially frequent in poetic and prophetic style.]; e.g. #bref("Gen 2:6"); #emph[a mist went up]; continually (#he[יַֽעֲלֶה];), 2:25, 37:7, 48:10, #bref("Exod 1:12");, #bref("8:20");, #bref("13:22");, #bref("15:6");, 12, 14, 15, #bref("Num 9:15"); f. 20 f., 23 #super[7];, #bref("Judg 2:1");, #bref("5:8");, #bref("1 Sam 3:2");, #bref("13:17"); f., #bref("2 Sam 2:28");, #bref("23:10");, #bref("1 Kgs 3:4");, #bref("7:8");, #bref("21:6");, #bref("Isa 1:21");, #bref("6:4"); (#he[יִמָּלֵא];), 17:10 f., 51:2 x, #bref("Jer 13:7");, #bref("36:18");, #bref("Ps 18:7");, 14, 17 ff.38 ff., 24:2, 32:4, 5 (#he[אוֹדִֽיעֲךָ];), 47:5, 68:10, 12, 104:6 ff., 106:19, 107:18, 29, 139:13, #bref("Job 3:11");, #bref("4:12");, 15 f., 10:10 f., 15:7 f.—very frequently alternating with a perfect (especially with a frequentative perfect; cf. #bref("Num 9:15");–23 and #xref(<p112e>)[§ 112#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];), or when the narration is continued by means of an imperfect consecutive.#footnote[According to the Masora such imperfects occur in Is 10#super[13 #emph[bis];]; (where, however, #he[וְאָסִיר]; might also mean #emph[I am wont to remove];, etc.), #bref("Isa 48:3");, #bref("57:17");, #bref("Ps 18:38");a, also (according to #xref(<p49c>)[§ 49#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];) in #bref("2 Sam 1:10"); and #bref("Ezek 16:10");. In some other cases #he[וְ]; is no doubt a dogmatic emendation for #he[וָ]; (#emph[imperf. consec.];) in order to represent historical statements as promises; cf. #bref("Isa 42:6");, #bref("43:28"); \[contrasted with #bref("42:25");\], #bref("51:2 bis");, #bref("63:3ff."); and the note on #xref(<p53p>)[§ 53#h(0.18em)#emph[p];];.];

#ml("c") <p107c> Rem. 1. The imperfect is frequently used in this way after the particles #he[אָז]; #emph[then];, #he[טֶ֫רֶם]; #emph[not yet];, #he[בְּטֶ֫רֶם]; #emph[before];, #he[עַד־]; #emph[until];, e.g. #bref("Exod 15:1"); #he[אָז יָשִֽׁיר־משֶׁה]; #emph[then sang Moses];, etc.; #bref("Num 21:17");, #bref("Deut 4:41");, #bref("Josh 10:12");, #bref("1 Kgs 3:16");, #bref("8:1");, #bref("Ps 126:2");, #bref("Job 38:21");. (The #emph[perfect]; is used after #he[אָז]; when stress is to be laid on the fact that the action has really taken place, and not upon its gradual accomplishment or duration in the past, e.g. #bref("Gen 4:26"); #he[אָז הוּחַל]; #emph[then began];, etc.; #bref("Gen 49:4");, #bref("Exod 15:15");, #bref("Josh 22:31");, #bref("Judg 5:11");, #bref("Ps 89:20");.)#footnote[After #he[אָז]; #emph[then]; (to announce future events) the imperf. is naturally used in the sense of a future, #bref("Gen 24:41");, #bref("Exod 12:48");, #bref("Mic 3:4");, #bref("Zeph 3:9");, #bref("Ps 51:21");.]; After #he[טֶ֫רֶם]; e.g. #bref("Gen 19:4"); #he[טֶ֫רֶם יִשְׁכָּ֫בוּ]; #emph[before they lay down];; #bref("Gen 2:5");, #bref("24:45");, #bref("1 Sam 3:3");, 7, always in the sense of our #emph[pluperfect];. (In #bref("Gen 24:15"); instead of the perf. #he[כִּלָּה];, the imperf. should be read, as in verse 45; so also in #bref("1 Sam 3:7"); \[#he[יִגָּלֶה];\] an imperf. is co\-ordinated with #he[ידע];). After #he[בְּטֶ֫רֶם]; (sometimes also simply #he[טֶ֫רֶם]; #bref("Exod 12:34");, #bref("Josh 3:1");), e.g. #bref("Jer 1:5"); #he[בְּטֶ֫רֶם תֵּצֵא]; #emph[before thou camest forth];; #bref("Gen 27:33");, #bref("37:18");, #bref("41:50");, #bref("Ruth 3:14"); (perhaps also in #bref("Ps 90:2"); an imperf. was intended instead of #he[יֻלָּ֫דוּ];; cf. Wellhausen on #bref("2 Sam 3:2");; but note also #bref("Prov 8:25");, in a similar context, #emph[before the mountains were settled];, #he[הָטְבָּ֑עוּ];, the predicate being separated from #he[בְּטֶ֫רֶם]; by #he[הָרִים];, as in #bref("Ps 90:2");). After #he[עַד־]; #bref("Josh 10:13");, #bref("Ps 73:17"); (#emph[until I went];), #bref("2 Chr 29:34");; on the other #origpage("315") <orig-315>;hand, with the perf., e.g. #bref("Josh 2:22");. As after #he[אָז];, so also after #he[טֶ֫רֶם];, #he[בְּטֶ֫רֶם];, and #he[עַד־]; the imperf. may be used, according to the context, in the sense of our #emph[future];, e.g. #bref("2 Kgs 2:9");, #bref("Isa 65:24");, #bref("Job 10:21");; after #he[עַד־]; e.g. #bref("Isa 22:14");. The imperf. is used in the sense of our #emph[present]; after #he[טֶ֫רֶם]; in #bref("Exod 9:30");, #bref("10:7");.

#ml("d") <p107d> 2. Driver (#emph[Tenses];#super[3];, p. 35 f.) rightly lays stress upon the inherent distinction between the #emph[participle]; as expressing #emph[mere]; duration, and the #emph[imperfect]; as expressing #emph[progressive]; duration (in the present, past, or future). Thus the words #he[וְנָהָר יֹצֵא]; #bref("Gen 2:10"); represent the river of Paradise as going out of Eden in a continuous, uninterrupted stream, but #he[יִפָּרֵד];, which immediately follows, describes how the parting of its waters is always taking place afresh. In the same way #he[יַֽעֲלֶה]; #bref("Gen 2:6"); represents new mists as constantly arising, and #he[יִמָּלֵא]; #bref("Isa 6:4"); new clouds of smoke. Also those actions, etc., which might be regarded in themselves as single or even momentary, are, as it were, broken up by the imperfect into their component parts, and so pictured as gradually completing themselves. Hence #he[תִּבְלָעֵ֫מוֹ]; #bref("Exod 15:12"); (after a #emph[perf.]; as in verse 14) represents the Egyptians, in a vivid, poetic description, as being swallowed up one after another, and #he[יַנְחֵ֫נִי]; #bref("Num 23:7"); the leading on by stages, etc.

#ml("e") <p107e> (#emph[b];) To express actions, etc., which were #emph[repeated]; in the past, either at fixed intervals or occasionally (the #emph[modus rei repetitae];), e.g. #bref("Job 1:5"); #emph[thus did]; (#he[יַֽעֲשֶׂה];) #emph[Job continually]; (after each occasion of his sons’ festivities); 4:3 f., 22:6 f., 23:11, 29:7, 9, 12 f., #bref("Gen 6:4");, #bref("29:2");, #bref("30:38");, #bref("42:31");, 39 (#emph[I used to bear]; the loss of it), #bref("Exod 1:12");, #bref("19:19");, #bref("33:7"); ff. (#he[יִקַּח]; #emph[used to take]; every time), 40:36 ff., #bref("Num 9:17"); f. 20 ff., 11:5, 9, #bref("Judg 6:4");, #bref("14:10");, #bref("21:25");, #bref("1 Sam 1:7");, #bref("2:22");, #bref("9:9");, #bref("13:19");, #bref("18:5");, #bref("27:9");, #bref("2 Sam 1:22");, #bref("12:3");, #bref("13:18");, #bref("1 Kgs 5:25"); (of tribute repeated year by year), 10:5, 13:33, 14:28, #bref("2 Kgs 4:8");, #bref("8:29");, #bref("13:20");, #bref("25:14");, #bref("Jer 36:23");, #bref("Ps 42:5");, #bref("44:3");, #bref("78:15, 40");, #bref("103:7");, #bref("Esth 2:14");; even in a negative dependent clause, #bref("1 Kgs 18:10");.

#ml("f") <p107f> #strong[2.]; In the sphere of #emph[present time];, again

(#emph[a];) To express actions, events, or states, which are #emph[continued]; for a shorter or longer time,#footnote[It is not always possible to carry out with certainty the distinction between #emph[continued]; and #emph[repeated]; actions. Some of the examples given under #emph[f]; might equally be referred to #emph[g];.]; e.g. #bref("Gen 37:15"); #he[מַה־תְּבַקֵּשׁ]; #emph[what seekest thou?]; 19:19 #he[לֹא־אוּכַל]; #emph[I cannot];; 24:50, 31:35, #bref("Isa 1:13");. Other examples are #bref("Gen 2:10");, #bref("24:31");, #bref("1 Sam 1:8");, #bref("11:5");, #bref("1 Kgs 3:7");, #bref("Ps 2:2");, and in the prophetic formula #he[יֹאמַר יְהֹוָה]; #emph[saith the Lord];, #bref("Isa 1:11, 18");, etc., cf. #bref("40:1");. So especially to express facts known by experience, which occur at all times, and consequently hold good at any moment, e.g. #bref("Prov 15:20"); #emph[a wise son maketh a glad father];; hence especially frequent in Job and Proverbs. In an interrogative sentence, e.g. #bref("Job 4:17"); #emph[is mortal man just before God?]; In a negative sentence, #bref("Job 4:18");, etc.

#ml("g") <p107g> (#emph[b];) To express actions, etc., which may be #emph[repeated]; at any time, including therefore the present, or are #emph[customarily]; repeated on a given occasion (cf. above, #emph[e];), e.g. #bref("Deut 1:44"); #emph[as bees do]; (are accustomed to #origpage("316") <orig-316>;do); #bref("Gen 6:21");, #bref("32:33");, #bref("43:32");, #bref("Judg 11:40");, #bref("1 Sam 2:8");, #bref("5:5");, #bref("20:2");, #bref("2 Sam 15:32");, #bref("Isa 1:23");, #bref("3:16");, #bref("Ps 1:3");. So again (see #emph[f];) especially to express facts known by experience which may at any time come into effect again, e.g. #bref("Exod 23:8"); #emph[a gift blindeth]; (#he[יְעַוֵּר];), etc.; #bref("Gen 2:24");, #bref("22:14");, #bref("Isa 32:6");, #bref("Amos 3:7");, #bref("Mal 1:6");, #bref("Job 2:4");, etc. Of the same kind also is the imperfect in such relative clauses (see § 155), as #bref("Gen 49:27"); #emph[Benjamin is]; #he[זְאֵב יִטְרָף]; #emph[a wolf that ravineth]; (properly, is accustomed to ravin). Finally, compare also the formulae #he[יֵֽאָמֵר]; #emph[it is]; (wont to be) #emph[said]; (to introduce proverbial expressions) #bref("Gen 10:9");, #bref("22:14");, etc.; #he[לֹא־יֵֽעָשֶׂה כֵן]; #emph[it is not]; (wont to be) #emph[so done]; (and hence #emph[may not, shall not be];, see #emph[u];), #bref("Gen 29:26");, #bref("20:9");, #bref("34:7");, #bref("2 Sam 13:12");.

#ml("h") <p107h> (#emph[c];) To express actions, etc., which although, strictly speaking, they are already finished, are regarded as still lasting on into the present time, or continuing to operate in it, e.g. #bref("Gen 32:30"); #emph[wherefore is it that thou dost ask]; (#he[תִּשְׁאַל];) #emph[after my name?]; #bref("24:31");, #bref("44:7");, #bref("Exod 5:15");, #bref("2 Sam 16:9");. In such cases, naturally, the perfect is also admissible, and is sometimes found in the same formula as the imperfect, e.g. #bref("Job 1:7"); (#bref("2:2");) #he[מֵאַ֫יִן תָּבֹא]; #emph[whence comest thou]; (just now)? but #bref("Gen 16:8"); (cf. #bref("42:7");) #he[אֵֽי־מִזֶּה בָאתְ]; #emph[whence camest thou?]; The imperfect represents the coming as still in its last stage, whereas the perfect represents it as an accomplished fact.

#ml("i") <p107i> #strong[3.]; In the sphere of #emph[future time];. To express actions, etc., which are to be represented as about to take place, and as continuing a shorter or longer time in the future, or as being repeated; thus:

(#emph[a];) From the standpoint of the speaker’s present time, e.g. #bref("Exod 4:1"); #emph[they will not believe]; (#he[יַֽאֲמִ֫ינוּ];) #emph[me, nor hearken]; (#he[יִשְׁמְעוּ];) #emph[unto my voice: for they will say]; (#he[יֹֽאמְרוּ];), etc., #bref("6:1");, #bref("9:5");, etc.

#ml("k") <p107k> (#emph[b];) In dependent clauses to represent actions, etc., which from some point of time in the past are to be represented as future, e.g. #bref("Gen 43:7"); could we in any wise know #emph[that he would say]; (#he[יֹאמַר];)? #bref("2:19");, #bref("43:25");, #bref("Exod 2:4");, #bref("2 Kgs 3:27"); #he[אֲשֶׁר־יִמְלֹךְ]; #emph[qui regnaturus erat];; #bref("13:14");, #bref("Jonah 4:5");, #bref("Job 3:3");, #bref("Eccl 2:3");, #bref("Ps 78:6"); #emph[that the generation to come might know];, #he[בָּנִים יִוָּ֫לֵדוּ]; #emph[the children which should be born]; (#emph[qui nascituri essent];; the imperfect here with the collateral idea of the occurrence being repeated in the future).

#ml("l") <p107l> (#emph[c];) To represent a #emph[futurum exactum];; cf. #bref("Isa 4:4");, #bref("6:11"); (co\-ordinated with a perfect used in the same sense, see #xref(<p106o>)[§ 106#h(0.18em)#emph[o];];); so also sometimes after the temporal particles #he[עַד];, #bref("Ps 132:5");, and #he[עַד אֲשֶׁר]; #emph[until];, #bref("Gen 29:8");, #bref("Num 20:17");, etc.

#ml("m") <p107m> #strong[4.]; Finally to the sphere of future time belong also those cases in which the (modal) imperfect serves to express actions, events, or states, the occurrence of which is to be represented as willed (or not #origpage("317") <orig-317>;willed), or as in some way conditional, and consequently only contingent. More particularly such imperfects serve—

#ml("n") <p107n> (#emph[a];) As an expression of will, whether it be a definite intention and arrangement, or a simple desire, namely :

(1) Sometimes in positive sentences in place of the cohortative (cf. e.g. #bref("Ps 59:17"); with verse 18; #bref("2 Sam 22:50"); with #bref("Ps 18:50");; #bref("Judg 19:11");, etc.), of the imperative (#bref("Isa 18:3");), or of the jussive (which, however, in most cases, does not differ from the ordinary form of the imperfect), e.g. #he[תֵּֽרָאֶה]; #emph[let it appear]; #bref("Gen 1:9");, #bref("41:34");, #bref("Lev 19:2");, 3, #bref("2 Sam 10:12"); (and so frequently in verbs #he[ל״ה];; cf. #xref(<p109a>)[§ 109#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];, note 2); #bref("Zech 9:5"); (#he[תָּחִיל];); #bref("Ps 61:7"); (#he[תּוֹסִיף];); #bref("Prov 22:17"); (#he[תָּשִׁית];); #bref("23:1");, #bref("Job 6:23"); (co\-ordinated with the imperative), #bref("10:20"); #emph[K#super[e];th.];; so probably also #he[יָדִין]; #emph[let him judge!]; #bref("Ps 72:2");.—So also in the 1st pers., to express a wish which is asserted subsequently with reference to a fixed point of time in the past, e.g. #bref("Job 10:18"); #he[אֶגְּוַע]; #emph[I ought to]; \[not #emph[should]; as A.V., R.V.\] #emph[have];, (then, immediately after being born) #emph[given up the ghost];; cf. verse 19 #he[אֶֽהְיֶה]; and #he[אוּבָֽל]; #bref("Lev 10:18");, #bref("Num 35:28");. Even to express an obligation or necessity according to the judgement of another person, e.g. #bref("Job 9:29"); #he[אֶרְשָׁע]; #emph[I am to be guilty];, #bref("12:4");. Cp. #bref("Job 9:15");, #bref("19:16");; in a question, #bref("Ps 42:10");, #bref("43:2");.

#ml("o") <p107o> (2) To express the definite expectation that something will not happen. The imperfect with #he[לֹא]; represents a more emphatic form of prohibition than the jussive#footnote[As stated in #xref(<p46a>)[§ 46#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];, a prohibition cannot be expressed by #he[אַל־]; and the imperative.]; with #he[אַל־]; (cf. #xref(<p109c>)[§ 109#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];), and corresponds to our #emph[thou shalt not do it!]; with the strongest expectation of obedience, while #he[אַל־]; with the jussive is rather a simple warning, #emph[do not that!]; Thus #he[לֹא]; with the imperfect is especially used in enforcing the divine commands, e.g. #he[לֹא תִּגְנֹב]; #emph[thou shalt not steal]; #bref("Exod 20:15");; cf. verses 3, 4, 5, 7, 10 ff. So #he[לֹא]; with the 3rd pers. perhaps in #bref("Prov 16:10");.

#ml("p") <p107p> Rem. The #emph[jussive];, which is to be expected after #he[אַל־];, does not, as a rule (according to #emph[n];, and #xref(<p109a>)[§ 109#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];, note 2), differ in form from the simple imperfect. That many supposed jussives are intended as simple imperfects is possible from the occurrence after #he[אַל־]; of what are undoubtedly imperfect forms, not only from verbs #he[ל״ה]; (cf. #xref(<p109a>)[§ 109#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];, note 2), but also from verbs #he[ע״וּ];, to express a prohibition or negative wish, #he[אַל־תַּבִּיט]; #bref("Gen 19:17");, #he[אַל־תָּסוּר]; #bref("Josh 1:7");, #he[אַל־נָא יָשִׂים]; #bref("1 Sam 25:25");. Even with the 1st pers. plur. (after an imperative) #he[וְאַל־נָמוּת]; #emph[that we die not];, #bref("1 Sam 12:19");. Also to express the conviction that something cannot happen, #he[אַל־יָנוּם]; #emph[he will not slumber];,#footnote[To regard this as an optative (so Hupfeld) is from the context impossible. It is more probably a strong pregnant construction, or fusion of two sentences (such as, #emph[do not think he will slumber!];). Verse 4 contains the objective confirmation, by means of #he[לֹא]; with the imperf., of that which was previously only a subjective conviction.]; #bref("Ps 121:3");; cf. #bref("Jer 46:6");, #bref("2 Chr 14:10");. #origpage("318") <orig-318>;

#ml("q") <p107q> (3) In dependent clauses after final conjunctions (#xref(<p165b>)[§ 165#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];), as #he[אֲשֶׁר];, #bref("Gen 11:7"); (#he[אֲשֶׁר לֹא יִשְׁמְעוּ]; #emph[that they may not understand];); #he[בַּֽעֲבוּר]; #bref("Gen 21:30");, #bref("27:4");, 19, #bref("Exod 9:14");, etc.; #he[לְמַ֫עַן אֲשֶׁר]; #bref("Num 17:5");; #he[לְמַ֫עַן]; #bref("Deut 4:1");, #bref("Ps 51:6");, #bref("78:6");, and #he[יַעַ֫ן אֲשֶׁר];#footnote[But #he[יַעַ֫ן אֲשֶׁר]; in a causal sense (#emph[because, since];), e.g. #bref("Judg 2:20"); (as #he[אֲשֶׁר]; #bref("Gen 34:27");) is followed by the perfect. On #bref("Josh 4:24"); see above, #xref(<p74g>)[§ 74#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];.]; #bref("Ezek 12:12");, #emph[in order that];#footnote[R.V. #emph[because he shall not see.];\]];; #he[לְבִלְתִּי]; #emph[that];... #emph[not];, #bref("Exod 20:20");, #bref("2 Sam 14:14");; also after #he[פֶּן־]; #emph[that not, lest];, #bref("Gen 3:22");, #bref("11:4");, #bref("19:15");, etc.#footnote[In #bref("2 Kgs 2:16"); #he[פֶּן־]; occurs with the perf. in a vivid presentment of the time when the fear is realized and the remedy comes too late. (In #bref("2 Sam 20:6");, since a #emph[perfect consec.]; follows, read with Driver #he[יִמְצָא];.)];; cf. also the instances introduced by #he[וְלֹא]; in #xref(<p109g>)[§ 109#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];.—In #bref("Lev 9:6"); such an imperfect (or jussive? see the examples in #xref(<p109f>)[§ 109#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];) is added to the expression of the command by an asyndeton, and in #bref("Lam 1:19"); to the principal clause simply by #he[וְ];: #emph[while they sought them food]; #he[וְיָשִׁ֫יבוּ אֶת־נַפְשָׁם]; #emph[to refresh their souls]; (cf. also #bref("Lam 3:26");, #emph[it is good and let him hope];, i.e. #emph[that he should hope];); so after an interrogative clause, #bref("Exod 2:7");. Finally also in a relative clause, #bref("Ps 32:8"); #he[בְּדֶ֫רֶךְ־זוּ תֵלֵךְ]; #emph[in the way which thou shouldst go];.

#ml("r") <p107r> (#emph[b];) To express actions, etc., which are to be represented as possibly taking place or not taking place (sometimes corresponding to the #emph[potential]; of the classical languages, as also to our periphrases with #emph[can, may, should];#footnote[By this, of course, is not meant that these finer distinctions were consciously present to the Hebrew mind. They are rather mere expedients for making intelligible to ourselves the full significance of the Semitic imperfect.];). More particularly such imperfects are used—

#ml("s") <p107s> (1) In a permissive sense, e.g. #bref("Gen 2:16"); #emph[of every tree of the garden]; (#he[אָכֹל תֹּאכֵל];) #emph[thou mayest freely eat]; (the opposite in verse #bref("17");); #bref("3:2");, #bref("42:37");, #bref("Lev 21:3");, 22, #bref("Job 21:3");. In the 1st pers. #bref("Ps 5:8");, #bref("22:18"); (#emph[I may];, or #emph[can, tell];); in a negative sentence, e.g. #bref("Ps 5:5");.

#ml("t") <p107t> (2) In interrogative sentences, e.g. #bref("Prov 20:9"); #he[מִֽי־יֹאמַר]; #emph[quis dixerit?]; Cf. #bref("Gen 17:17");, #bref("18:14");, #bref("31:43");, #bref("1 Sam 11:12");, #bref("2 Kgs 5:12"); #he[הֲלֹֽא־אֶרְחַץ בָּהֶם]; #emph[may I not wash in them?]; #bref("Isa 33:14");, #bref("Ps 15:1");, #bref("24:3");, #bref("Eccl 5:5");. So especially in a question expressing surprise after #he[אֵיךְ];, e.g. #bref("Gen 39:9"); #emph[how then can I...?]; #bref("44:34");, #bref("Isa 19:11");, #bref("Ps 137:4");, and even with regard to some point of time in the past, looking forward from which an event might have been expected to take place, e.g. #bref("Gen 43:7"); #he[הֲיָדוֹעַ נֵדַע]; #emph[could we in any wise know...?]; Cf. #bref("2 Sam 3:33"); (#he[יָמוּת]; #emph[was Abner to die as a fool];, i.e. #emph[was he destined to die...?];), and so probably also #bref("Gen 34:31"); (#emph[should he deal...?];). Very closely connected with this is the use of the imperfect—

#ml("u") <p107u> (3) In a consecutive clause depending on an interrogative clause, e.g. #bref("Exod 3:11");, #emph[who am I]; (#he[כִּי אֵלֵךְ];) #emph[that I should]; (#emph[ought, could];) #emph[go?]; #bref("16:7");, #bref("Num 11:12");, #bref("Judg 9:28");, #bref("1 Sam 18:18");, #bref("2 Kgs 8:13");, #bref("Isa 29:16");, #bref("Job 6:11");, #bref("21:15");, similarly after #he[אֲשֶׁר]; #bref("Gen 38:18");, #bref("Exod 5:2");. #origpage("319") <orig-319>;

#ml("v") <p107v> Rem. In passages like #bref("1 Sam 11:5");, #bref("Ps 8:5");, #bref("114:5");, the context shows that the imperfect corresponds rather to our present. In such sentences the perfect also is naturally used in referring to completed actions, e.g. #bref("Gen 20:10");, #bref("Judg 18:23");, #bref("2 Sam 7:18");, #bref("Isa 22:1");.

#ml("w") <p107w> (4) In #emph[negative sentences]; to express actions, etc., which cannot or should not happen, e.g. #bref("Gen 32:13"); #he[אֲשֶׁר לֹא־יִסָּפֵר מֵרֹב]; #emph[which cannot be numbered for multitude];; #bref("20:9"); #emph[deeds]; (#he[אֲשֶׁר לֹא־יֵֽעָשׂוּ];) #emph[that ought not to be done]; (cf. above, #emph[g];); #bref("Ps 5:5");.

#ml("x") <p107x> (5) In #emph[conditional clauses]; (the #emph[modus conditionalis]; corresponding to the Latin present or imperfect conjunctive) both in the protasis and apodosis, or only in the latter, #bref("Ps 23:4"); #he[גַּם כִּֽי־אֵלֵךְ... לֹֽא־אִירָא רָע]; #emph[yea, though I walk]; (or #emph[had to walk];)... #emph[I fear]; (or #emph[I would fear];) #emph[no evil];; #bref("Job 9:20"); #emph[though I be righteous, mine own mouth shall condemn me];. After a perfect in the protasis, e.g. #bref("Job 23:10");. Very frequently also in an apodosis, the protasis to which must be supplied from the context, e.g. #bref("Job 5:8"); #emph[but as for me, I would seek unto God]; (were I in thy place); #bref("3:13");, 16, #bref("14:14"); f., #bref("Ps 55:13");, #bref("Ruth 1:12");. However, some of the imperfects in these examples are probably intended as jussive forms. Cf. #xref(<p109h>)[§ 109#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];.

#sec("108", [Use of the Cohortative], note: []) <s108>

#ml("a") <p108a> The cohortative, i.e. according to #xref(<p48c>)[§ 48#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];, the 1st pers.#footnote[For the few examples of cohortatives in the 3rd sing., see #xref(<p48d>)[§ 48#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];.]; sing. or plur. of the imperfect lengthened by the ending #he[־ָה];,#footnote[But verbs #he[ל״ה];, according to #xref(<p75l>)[§ 75#h(0.18em)#emph[l];];, even in the cohortative, almost always have the ending #he[־ֶה];; cf. e.g. in #bref("Deut 32:20"); #he[אֶרְאֶה]; after #he[אַסְתִּ֫ירָה];.]; represents in general an endeavour directed expressly towards a definite object. While the corresponding forms of the indicative rather express the mere announcement that an action will be undertaken, the cohortative lays stress on the determination underlying the action, and the personal interest in it.

Its uses may be divided into—

#ml("b") <p108b> #strong[1.]; The cohortative standing alone, or co\-ordinated with another cohortative, and frequently strengthened by the addition of the particle #he[נָא];:

(#emph[a];) To express self\-encouragement, e.g. #bref("Exod 3:3"); #he[אָסֻ֫רָה־נָּא וג׳]; #emph[I will turn aside now, and see...!]; So especially as the result of inward deliberation (in soliloquies), e.g. #bref("Gen 18:21");, #bref("32:21"); (rarely so used after #he[אַל־];, #bref("Gen 21:16"); #emph[let me not look];...#emph[!]; #bref("Jer 18:18");), and also as a more or less emphatic statement of a fixed determination, e.g. #bref("Isa 5:1"); #emph[I will sing];#footnote[R.V. #emph[let me sing];.\]];...#emph[!]; #bref("5:6");, #bref("31:8");. Cf. also #bref("Gen 46:30"); #emph[now let me die]; (#emph[I am willing to die];), #origpage("320") <orig-320>;#emph[since I have seen thy face];; and #bref("Ps 31:8");. In the 1st pers. plur. the cohortative includes a summons to others to help in doing something, e.g. #bref("Ps 2:3"); #he[נְנַתְּקָה]; #emph[come! let us break asunder!]; etc., and #bref("Gen 11:3");.

#ml("c") <p108c> (#emph[b];) To express a wish, or a request for permission, that one should be allowed to do something, e.g. #bref("Deut 2:27"); #he[אֶעְבְּרָה]; #emph[may I be allowed to pass through]; (#emph[let me pass through];)! #bref("Num 20:17"); #he[נַעְבְּרָה־נָּא]; #emph[may we be allowed to pass through!]; #bref("Jer 40:15"); #emph[let me go, I pray thee!]; etc.; #bref("2 Sam 16:9");; so after #he[לֹא]; #bref("2 Sam 18:14");; after #he[אַל־]; #bref("2 Sam 24:14");, #bref("Jer 17:18");, #bref("Ps 25:2"); (#he[אַל־אֵב֫וֹשָׁה]; #emph[let me not be ashamed];; cf. #bref("Ps 31:2");, 18, 71:1); 69:15. After #he[אַל־נָא]; #bref("Jonah 1:14");.

#ml("d") <p108d> #strong[2.]; The cohortative in dependence on other moods, as well as in conditional sentences: (#emph[a];) In dependence (with #emph[wāw copulative];; #bref("Ps 9:15"); after #he[לְמַ֫עַן];) on an imperative or jussive to express an intention or intended consequence, e.g. #bref("Gen 27:4"); #emph[bring it to me];, #he[וְאֹכֵ֑לָה]; #emph[that I may eat];, prop. #emph[then will I eat];; #bref("Gen 19:5");, #bref("23:4");, #bref("24:56");, #bref("27:25");, #bref("29:21");, #bref("30:25"); f., 42:34, 49:1, #bref("Deut 32:1");, #bref("Hos 6:1");, #bref("Ps 2:8");, #bref("39:14");, #bref("Job 10:20"); #emph[Q#super[e];rê];; #bref("Isa 5:19"); #emph[and let the counsel of the Holy One of Israel draw nigh and come];, #he[וְנֵדָֽעָה]; #emph[that we may know]; (#emph[it];)! #bref("Gen 26:28");, #bref("1 Sam 27:5");. Also after negative sentences, #bref("Gen 18:30");, 32, #bref("Judg 6:39");, and after interrogative sentences, #bref("1 Kgs 22:7");, #bref("Isa 40:25");, #bref("41:26");, #bref("Amos 8:5");.

#ml("e") <p108e> (#emph[b];) In conditional sentences (with or without #he[אִם];) to express a contingent intention, e.g. #bref("Job 16:6"); #he[אִם־אֲדַבְּרָה]; #emph[should I determine to speak, my grief is not assuaged];, #he[וְאַחְדְּלָה]; #emph[and should I forbear, what am I eased?]; without #he[אִם]; #bref("Job 19:18");, #bref("30:26"); (where, however, #he[וָאיחלה]; is probably intended); #bref("Ps 73:16"); (unless #he[וָֽאֲח׳]; should be read), 139:8 f. After the 3rd person, #bref("Job 11:17"); #emph[though it be dark];, etc. So perhaps also #bref("2 Sam 22:38"); #he[אֶרְדְּפָה]; #emph[if I determined to pursue, then]; ..., but cf. #bref("Ps 18:38");.

#ml("f") <p108f> (#emph[c];) Likewise in the apodosis of conditional sentences, e.g. #bref("Job 31:7"); f. #emph[if my step hath turned out of the way]; ..., #he[אֶזְרְעָה]; #emph[then let me sow];; cf. 16:4 f. #emph[I also could speak as ye do, if ...!]; So even when the condition must be supplied from the context, e.g. #bref("Ps 40:6"); #emph[else would I declare and speak of them];; 51:18 #emph[else would I]; (#emph[gladly];) #emph[give it];, i.e. if thou didst require it (cf. the precisely similar #he[וְאֶשָּׂא]; #bref("Ps 55:13");); #bref("Job 6:10");. In the 1st plur. #bref("Jer 20:10");. To the same category belong the cohortatives after the formula expressing a wish #he[מִֽי־יִתֵּן];, #he[מִֽי־יִתְּנֵ֫נִי];, e.g. #bref("Jer 9:1"); #emph[oh, that I had]; ..., #he[וְאֶֽעֶזְבָה]; #emph[then]; (i.e. if I had) #emph[should I]; (or #emph[would I];) #emph[leave my people];, etc.; #bref("Judg 9:29");; without #emph[Wāw]; #bref("Isa 27:4");, #bref("Ps 55:7");, #bref("Job 23:4"); (cf. also verse 7).

#ml("g") <p108g> Rem. 1. The question, whether a resolution formed under compulsion (a #emph[necessity];) is also expressed by the cohortative (so, according to the prevailing opinion, in #bref("Isa 38:10"); #he[אֵלֵ֫כָה];; #bref("Jer 3:25");, #bref("4:19");, 21, 6:10, #bref("Ps 55:3");, 18 (?); 57:5, where, however, with Hupfeld, #he[שָֽׁכְבָה]; should be read; 77:7, 88:16, and in the 1st plur. #bref("Isa 59:10");), is to be answered in the sense that in these examples the cohortative #emph[form]; is used after its meaning has become entirely lost, merely for the sake of its fuller sound, instead of the ordinary imperfect. This view is strongly #origpage("321") <orig-321>;supported by the rather numerous examples of cohortative forms after #emph[wāw consec.]; of the imperfect (cf. #xref(<p49e>)[§ 49#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];, as also #bref("Ps 66:6"); #he[שָׁם נִשְׂמְחָה]; #emph[there did we rejoice];#footnote[Analogous to this cohortative (as equivalent to the imperfect) after #he[שָׁם]; is the use of the historic imperf. after #he[אָז];, #xref(<p107c>)[§ 107#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];.];; #bref("Ps 119:163"); #he[וָֽאֲתַעֵ֑בָה];; #bref("Prov 7:7");), which can likewise only be explained as forms chosen merely for euphony, and therefore due to considerations of rhythm.

#ml("h") <p108h> 2. The cohortative is strange after #he[עַד־]; #bref("Ps 73:17"); #emph[until I went];... #he[אָבִ֫ינָה]; #emph[I considered their latter end];; possibly a pregnant construction for ‘until I made up my mind, saying, I will consider’, etc. (but #he[אָבִ֫ינָה]; #bref("Prov 7:7"); is still dependent on the preceding #he[וָ];); #he[עַד־אַרְגִּ֫יעָה]; #bref("Prov 12:19"); is at any rate to be explained in the same way (in #bref("Jer 49:19");, #bref("50:44"); we have #he[כִּי־א׳]; with a similar meaning), #emph[as long as I]; (intentionally) #emph[wink with the eyelashes]; (shall wink). On the other hand, in #bref("Exod 32:30"); #he[אֲכַפֵּר]; is to be read, with the Samaritan, instead of #he[אֲכַפְּרָה]; after #he[אוּלַי];.

#sec("109", [Use of the Jussive], note: []) <s109>

#ml("a") <p109a> As the #emph[cohortative]; is used in the 1st pers., so the #emph[jussive]; is especially found in the 2nd and 3rd pers. sing. and plur. to express a more or less definite desire that something should or should not happen (cf. for its form, which frequently coincides with that of the ordinary #emph[imperfect];,#footnote[With regard to verbs #he[ל״ה];, it is true that the full form of the imperfect is frequently used with the meaning of the jussive (as also for the cohortative, see #xref(<p108a>)[§ 108#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];, note 2), e.g. #he[אַל־יִרְאֶה]; #bref("Job 3:9"); (but previously #he[יְקַו]; #emph[let it look for!];): especially #emph[in]; (#bref("Neh 2:3");) and immediately #emph[before]; the principal pause, #bref("Gen 1:9"); #he[תֵּֽרָאֶה];; #bref("Judg 6:39"); #he[יִהְֽיֶה];, but previously #he[יְהִי־נָא];; #bref("Isa 47:3"); #he[תֵּֽרָאֶה];, previously #he[תִּגָּל];; #bref("Ps 109:7");. On the attempt to distinguish such jussives from the imperfect by means of a special meaning #he[־ֵה];, see #xref(<p75hh>)[§ 75#h(0.18em)#emph[hh];];.]; #xref(<p48f>)[§ 48#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];, #emph[g];). More particularly its uses may be distinguished as follows:

1\. The #emph[jussive]; standing alone, or co\-ordinated with another jussive:

#ml("b") <p109b> (#emph[a];) In affirmative sentences to express a command, a wish (or a blessing), advice, or a request; in the last case (the optative or precative) it is frequently strengthened by the addition of #he[נָא];. Examples: #bref("Gen 1:3"); #he[יְהִי אוֹר]; #emph[let there be light!]; #bref("Gen 1:6");, 9, 11, etc. (the creative commands); #bref("Num 6:26"); #emph[the Lord lift up his countenance upon thee, and give thee peace!]; cf. verse 25. After particles expressing a wish, #bref("Gen 30:34"); #he[לוּ יְהִי]; #emph[I would it might be;]; #bref("Ps 81:9"); #he[אִם־תִּשְׁמַע־לִי]; #emph[if thou wouldest hearken unto me!]; As a humble request, #bref("Gen 44:33");... #he[יֵֽשֶׁב־נָא עַבְדְּךָ... וְהַנַּ֫עַר יַ֫עַל]; #emph[let thy servant, I pray thee, abide];, etc., #emph[and let the lad go up, etc.];, #bref("Gen 47:4");.

#ml("c") <p109c> (#emph[b];) In negative sentences to express prohibition or dissuasion, warning, a negative wish (or imprecation), and a request. The prohibitive particle used before the jussive (according to #xref(<p107o>)[§ 107#h(0.18em)#emph[o];];) is almost always #he[אַל־]; (in negative desires and requests frequently #origpage("322") <orig-322>;#he[אַל־נָא];); e.g. #bref("Exod 34:3"); #he[אִישׁ אַל־יֵרָא]; #emph[neither let any man be seen!]; #bref("Prov 3:7"); #emph[be not]; (#he[אַל־תְּהִי];) #emph[wise in thine own eyes!]; #bref("Job 15:31"); #he[אַל־יַֽאֲמֵן]; #emph[ne confidat];. In the form of a request (prayer), #bref("Deut 9:26"); #he[אַל־תַּשְׁחֵת]; #emph[destroy not!]; #bref("1 Kgs 2:20");, #bref("Ps 27:9");, #bref("69:18");.

#ml("d") <p109d> Rem. 1. The few examples of #he[לֹא]; with the jussive could at most have arisen from the attempt to moderate subsequently by means of the jussive (voluntative) form what was at first intended to be a strict command (#he[לֹא]; with imperf. indic.); probably, however, they are either cases in which the defective writing has been misunderstood (as in #bref("1 Kgs 2:6");, #bref("Ezek 48:14");), or (as in #bref("Gen 24:8");) instances of the purely rhythmical jussive form treated below, under #emph[k];. Moreover, cf. #he[לֹא יוֹסֵף]; #bref("Joel 2:2"); and from the same verb #bref("Gen 4:12"); (unless it is to be referred to #emph[h];) and #bref("Deut 13:1");. The same form, however, appears also to stand three times for the cohortative (see below), and in #bref("Num 22:19"); for the ordinary imperfect (but see below, #emph[i];). Thus it is doubtful whether an imaginary by\-form of the ordinary imperf. is not intended by the Masora in all these cases, and whether consequently #he[יוֹסִף];, etc., should not be restored.—On #he[לֹֽא־תָחוֹס עֵֽינְךָ];, etc., #bref("Deut 7:16");, #bref("13:9");, etc., #bref("Ezek 5:11");, etc., cf. #xref(<p72r>)[§ 72#h(0.18em)#emph[r];];, according to which #he[תָחוּס]; should probably be read in every case.—The jussive appears in the place of the cohortative after #he[לֹא]; #bref("1 Sam 14:36"); (#he[וְלֹֽא־נַשְׁאֵר]; co\-ordinated with two cohortatives), #bref("2 Sam 17:12");; cf. #bref("Isa 41:23"); #emph[K#super[e];th.]; (#he[ונרא];, i.e. #he[וְנֵ֫רֶא];, after another cohortative); also (see above) #he[לֹא אֹסֵף]; #bref("Deut 18:16");, #bref("Hos 9:15");, and even without #he[לֹא]; #bref("Ezek 5:16");.

#ml("e") <p109e> 2. #he[אַל־]; with the jussive (or imperf., cf. #xref(<p107p>)[§ 107#h(0.18em)#emph[p];];) is used sometimes to express the conviction that something cannot or should not happen; cf. #bref("Isa 2:9"); (where, however, the text is very doubtful) #he[וְאַל־תִּשָּׂא לָהֶם]; #emph[and thou canst not possibly forgive them]; \[R.V. #emph[therefore forgive them not];\]; #bref("Ps 34:6");, #bref("41:3");, #bref("50:3");, #bref("121:3"); (#he[אַל־יִתֵּן];); #bref("Prov 3:25");, #bref("Job 5:22"); #he[אַל־תִּירָא]; #emph[neither needest thou be afraid];; 20:17, 40:32.

#ml("f") <p109f> 2. The jussive depending on other moods, or in conditional sentences:

(#emph[a];) Depending#footnote[This does not include the cases in which the jussive is not logically dependent on a preceding imperat., but is merely co\-ordinated, e.g. #bref("Gen 20:7");, #bref("Ps 27:14");, etc.]; (with #emph[Wāw];) on an imperative or cohortative to express an intention or an assurance of a contingent occurrence, e.g. #bref("Gen 24:51"); #emph[take her and go, and let her be]; (#he[וּתְהִי]; prop. #emph[and she will be];)...; 30:3, 31:37, 38:24, #bref("Exod 8:4");, #bref("9:13");, #bref("10:17");, #bref("14:2");, #bref("Josh 4:16");, #bref("Judg 6:30");, #bref("1 Sam 5:11");, #bref("7:3");, #bref("1 Kgs 21:10");, #bref("Ps 144:5");, #bref("Prov 20:22");, #bref("Job 14:6");. Also after interrogative sentences, which include a demand, #bref("Esth 7:2"); (say) #emph[what is thy desire];..., #he[וְתֵעָשׂ]; #emph[and it shall]; (i.e. in order that it may) #emph[be granted!]; #bref("1 Kgs 22:20");, #bref("Isa 19:12");, #bref("Job 38:34");f. Depending on a cohortative, e.g. #bref("Gen 19:20"); #he[אִמָּֽלְטָה נָּא שָׁ֫מָּה]; #emph[oh, let me escape thither];...#he[וּתְחִי נַפְשִׁי]; #emph[that my soul may live;]; even after a simple imperf. (cf. below, #emph[g];), #bref("1 Kgs 13:33"); #emph[whosoever would, he consecrated him]; ... #he[וִיהִי]; #emph[that he might be a priest]; (read #he[כֹּהֵן];) #emph[of the high places];, but probably the LXX reading #he[וַיְהִי]; is to be preferred. #origpage("323") <orig-323>;

#ml("g") <p109g> Rem. In #bref("2 Chr 35:21"); a negative final clause with #he[וְאַל־]; is dependent on an imperative, #emph[forbear from]; (meddling with) #emph[God];... #emph[that he destroy thee not];. As a rule, however, negative final clauses are attached to the principal sentence by means of #he[וְלֹא]; and a following imperfect; so after an imperative, #bref("Gen 42:2");, #bref("1 Kgs 14:2");, #bref("18:44");; after a jussive, #bref("Exod 30:20");, #bref("Neh 6:9");; after a perfect consec., #bref("Exod 28:35");, 43, 30:12, #bref("Num 18:5");; after #he[לֹא]; with an imperfect, #bref("Lev 10:6");, #bref("Num 18:3");, #bref("Deut 17:17"); #emph[neither shall he multiply wives unto himself]; (#he[וְלֹא יָסוּר לְבָבוֹ];) #emph[that his heart turn not away];; #bref("1 Sam 20:14");, #bref("2 Sam 21:17");, #bref("Jer 11:21");; after #he[אַל־]; with jussive, #bref("Lev 10:9");, #bref("11:43");, #bref("16:2");, #bref("2 Sam 13:25");, #bref("Jer 25:6");, #bref("37:20");, #bref("38:24"); f.; after the asseverative #he[אִם]; with the impft., #bref("Gen 14:23");; even after a simple imperfect, #bref("Jer 10:4"); #emph[with nails];... #emph[they fasten it]; (#he[וְלֹא יָפִיק];) #emph[that it move not];; after a participle, #bref("Job 9:7");.

#ml("h") <p109h> (#emph[b];) Frequently in conditional sentences (as in Arabic), either in the protasis or in the apodosis, cf. #bref("Ps 45:12"); #he[יִתְאָו]; #emph[should he desire];... #emph[then];...; 104:20 #he[תָּ֫שֶׁת... וִיהִי]; #emph[if thou makest darkness, then it is night];; so also in the protasis, #bref("Exod 22:4");, #bref("Lev 15:24");, #bref("Isa 41:28");, #bref("Ezek 14:7"); (#he[וְיַ֫עַל];), #bref("Job 34:29");; in the apodosis, #bref("Exod 7:9"); #emph[then will it]; (not, #emph[then shall it];) #emph[become a serpent];; #bref("Prov 9:9"); after an imperat. in the protasis; #bref("Job 10:16");, #bref("13:5");, #bref("22:28");. In a negative apodosis, #bref("Gen 4:12"); (#he[לֹֽא־תֹסֵף];, but see above, #emph[d];). In #bref("2 Kgs 6:27"); #he[אַל־יֽוֹשִׁעֵךְ]; (#emph[if the Lord do not help thee];, etc.) is to be explained as a jussive in a negative protasis.

#ml("i") <p109i> Rem. Undoubtedly this use of the jussive (in conditional sentences) is based on its original #emph[voluntative]; meaning; let something be so and so, then this or that must happen as a consequence. Certain other examples of the #emph[jussive];, however, show that in the consciousness of the language the #emph[voluntative]; has in such cases become weakened almost to a #emph[potential]; mood, and hence the #emph[jussive]; serves to express facts which may happen #emph[contingently];, or may be expected, e.g. #bref("Num 22:19"); (#he[מַה־יֹּסֵף];, but cf. above, #emph[d];); #bref("Job 9:33"); #emph[there is no daysman betwixt us, that might lay]; (#he[יָשֵׁת];, hence plainly a #emph[subjunctive\=qui ponat];; also in #bref("Num 23:19"); #he[וִֽיכַזֵּב]; #emph[that he should lie]; is probably intended as a jussive); #bref("Eccl 5:14");; so after interrogative sentences, #bref("Jer 9:11"); #emph[who is the wise man];, #he[וְיָבֵן]; #emph[qui intelligat hoc];?; #bref("Hos 14:10");.

#ml("k") <p109k> Moreover, in not a few cases, the jussive is used, without any collateral sense, for the ordinary imperfect form, and this occurs not alone in forms, which may arise from a misunderstanding of the defective writing, as #bref("Deut 28:21");, 36, 32:8, #bref("1 Kgs 8:1");, #bref("Isa 12:1");, #bref("Mic 3:4");, #bref("5:8");, #bref("Ps 11:6");, #bref("18:12");, #bref("21:2"); #emph[Q#super[e];rê]; (#he[מַה־יָּ֫גֶל];, #emph[K#super[e];th.]; #he[יָגִיל];), 25:9, 47:4, 90:3, 91:4, 107:29, #bref("Prov 15:25");, #bref("Job 13:27");, #bref("15:33");, #bref("18:9");, #bref("20:23");, #bref("37:22");, #bref("33:11");, #bref("36:14");, #bref("38:24");, #bref("Eccl 12:6"); (verse 7 #he[יָשֹׁב];, but immediately afterwards #he[תָּשׁוּב];), #bref("Dan 8:12");, —but also in shortened forms, such as #he[יְהִי]; #bref("Gen 49:17"); (Sam. #he[יִהְיֶה];), #bref("Deut 28:8");, #bref("1 Sam 10:5");, #bref("2 Sam 5:24");, #bref("Hos 6:1");, #bref("11:4");, #bref("Amos 5:14");, #bref("Mic 1:2");, #bref("Zeph 2:13");, #bref("Zech 9:5");, #bref("Ps 72:16"); f. (after other jussives), 104:31, #bref("Job 18:12");, #bref("20:23");, 26, 28, 27:8, 33:21, 34:37, #bref("Ruth 3:4");. This use of the jussive can hardly be due merely to poetic licence, but is rather to be explained on rhythmical grounds. In all the above\-cited examples, in fact, the jussive stands at the beginning of the sentence (and hence removed as far as possible from the principal tone), in others it is immediately before the principal pause (#bref("Isa 42:6");, #bref("50:2");, #bref("Ps 68:15");, #bref("Prov 23:25");, #bref("Job 24:14");, #bref("29:3");, #bref("40:19");), or actually #emph[in]; pause (#bref("Deut 32:18");, #bref("Job 23:9");, 11, #bref("Lam 3:50");), and is then a simply rhythmical shortening due to the strong influence of the tone. Moreover, since the jussive in numerous cases is not distinguished in form from the imperfect (#xref(<p48g>)[§ 48#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];), it is frequently doubtful which of the two the writer intended. This especially applies to those cases, in which a #emph[subjunctive]; is to be expressed by one or other of the forms (cf. #xref(<p107k>)[§ 107#h(0.18em)#emph[k];]; and #emph[m];–#emph[x];). #origpage("324") <orig-324>;

#sec("110", [The Imperative], note: []) <s110>

#align(center)[Mayer Lambert, ‘Sur la syntaxe de l’impératif en hébreu,’ in #emph[REJ.]; 1897, p. 106 ff.];

#ml("a") <p110a> #strong[1.]; The imperative,#footnote[On the close relation between the imperative and jussive (both in meaning and form), cf. #xref(<s46>)[§ 46]; and #xref(<p48i>)[§ 48#h(0.18em)#emph[i];];.]; which, according to § 46, is restricted to the 2nd pers. sing. and plur., and to #emph[positive]; commands, etc., may stand either alone, or in simple co\-ordination (as in #bref("1 Kgs 18:44");, #bref("Isa 56:1");, #bref("65:18");) with other imperatives:

(#emph[a];) To express real commands, e.g. #bref("Gen 12:1"); #emph[get thee out of thy country];; or (like the jussive) mere admonitions (#bref("Hos 10:12");) and requests, #bref("2 Kgs 5:22");, #bref("Isa 5:3");; on the addition of #he[נָא]; see below, Rem. 1. The imperative is used in the sense of an ironical challenge (often including a threat) in #bref("1 Kgs 2:22"); #emph[ask for him the kingdom also];; 22:15, #bref("Judg 10:14");, #bref("Isa 47:12"); (with #he[נָא];), #bref("Jer 7:21");, #bref("Ezek 20:39");, #bref("Amos 4:4");, #bref("Job 38:3");f., 40:10ff., #bref("Lam 4:21");. The imperative has a concessive sense in #bref("Nah 3:15"); (though thou #emph[make thyself many];, etc.), and in the cases discussed under #emph[f];, e.g. #bref("Isa 8:9"); f., 29:9.

#ml("b") <p110b> (#emph[b];) To express permission, e.g. #bref("2 Sam 18:23"); after previous dissuasion, (then) #emph[run]; (as far as I am concerned)! #bref("Isa 21:12");, #bref("45:11");.

#ml("c") <p110c> (#emph[c];) To express a distinct assurance (like our expression, #emph[thou shalt have it];)#footnote[Like the threatening formulae in the Latin comic writers, e.g. #emph[vapula];, Ter. Phorm. v. 6, 10\=#emph[vapulare te iubeo];, Plaut. Curc. vi. 4, 12.]; or promise, e.g. #bref("Isa 65:18"); #emph[but be ye glad];, etc. (i.e. ye will have continually occasion to be glad); and #bref("Isa 37:30");, #bref("Ps 110:2");; in a threat, #bref("Jer 2:19");. So especially in commands, the fulfilment of which is altogether out of the power of the person addressed, e.g. #bref("Isa 54:14"); #emph[be far from anxiety]; (meaning, thou needst not fear any more); #bref("Gen 1:28");, etc. (for other examples, such as #bref("1 Kgs 22:12");, #bref("2 Kgs 5:13");, see below, #emph[f];). Most clearly in the case of the #emph[imperative Niphʿal]; with a passive meaning, e.g. #bref("Gen 42:16"); #he[וְאַתֶּם הֵאָֽסְרוּ]; #emph[and ye shall be bound];; #bref("Deut 32:50");, #bref("Isa 49:9"); (#bref("Isa 45:22");, see below, #emph[f];).

#ml("d") <p110d> Rem. 1. The particle #he[נָא]; #emph[age!]; (§ 105) is frequently added to the imperative, as to the jussive, sometimes to soften down a command, or to make a request in a more courteous form (see above, #emph[a];), #bref("Gen 12:13");, #bref("24:2");, sometimes to strengthen an exhortation uttered as a rebuke or threat (#bref("Num 16:26");, #bref("20:10");) or in ridicule (#bref("Isa 47:12");).

#ml("e") <p110e> 2. The imperative after the desiderative particle #he[לוּ]; #bref("Gen 23:13"); (at the end of verses 5 and 14 also read #he[לוּ]; for #he[לוֹ]; and join it to the following imperative) is due to an anacoluthon. Instead of the imperfect which would be expected here after #he[לוּ];, the more forcible imperative is used in a new sentence.

#ml("f") <p110f> #strong[2.]; The imperative in logical dependence upon a preceding imperative, jussive (or cohortative), or an interrogative sentence, serves to #origpage("325") <orig-325>;express the distinct assurance or promise that an action or state will ensue as the certain consequence of a previous action. So especially:

(#emph[a];) The imperative when depending (with #emph[wāw copulative];) upon another imperative. In this case the first imperative contains, as a rule, a condition, while the second declares the consequence which the fulfilment of the condition will involve. The imperative is used for this declaration, since the consequence is, as a matter of fact, intended or desired by the speaker (cf. #emph[divide et impera];), e.g. #bref("Gen 42:18"); #he[זֹאת עֲשׂוּ וִֽחְיוּ]; #emph[this do, and live];, i.e. thus shall ye continue to live. #bref("Gen 17:1");, #bref("1 Kgs 22:12");, #bref("2 Kgs 5:13");, #bref("Isa 36:16");, #bref("45:22"); (#he[וְהִוָּֽשְׁעוּ];), #bref("Jer 6:16");, #bref("Amos 5:4");, 6, #bref("Ps 37:27");, #bref("Prov 3:3");f., 4:4, 7:2, 13:20 #emph[K#super[e];th.];, #bref("Job 2:9");, #bref("2 Chr 20:20");; in #bref("Jer 25:5");, #bref("Job 22:21"); #he[נָא]; is added to the first imperative. In other cases, the first imperative contains a mocking concession, the second an irrevocable denunciation, e.g. #bref("Isa 8:9"); #he[רֹ֫עוּ עַמִּים וָחֹ֫תּוּ]; (continue to) #emph[make an uproar, O ye peoples, and ye shall be broken in pieces];; cf. verse 9 #emph[b];.

#ml("g") <p110g> Rem. 1. If a promise or threat dependent on an imperative be expressed in the 3rd pers. then the jussive is naturally used instead of the 2nd imperative #bref("Isa 8:10");, #bref("55:2");.

#ml("h") <p110h> 2. In #bref("Prov 20:13"); the second imperative (containing a promise) is attached by asyndeton; elsewhere two imperatives occur side by side without the copula, where the second might be expected to be subordinated to the first, e.g. #bref("Deut 2:24"); #he[הָחֵל רָשׁ]; (where #he[רָשׁ]; is virtually, as it were, an object to #he[הָחֵל];) #emph[begin, take in possession]; for #emph[to take in possession]; (cf., however, #bref("Judg 19:6"); #he[הֽוֹאֶל־נָא וְלִין]; #emph[be content, I pray thee, and tarry all night];, and on this kind of co\-ordination in general, cf. #xref(<p120d>)[§ 120#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];). But such imperatives as #he[לֵךְ]; (#he[לְכוּ];), #he[קוּם]; (#he[ק֫וּמוּ];), when immediately preceding a second imperative, are for the most part only equivalent to interjections, #emph[come! up!];

#ml("i") <p110i> (#emph[b];) The imperative, when depending (with #emph[wāw copulative];) upon a jussive (cohortative), or an interrogative sentence, frequently expresses also a consequence which is to be expected with certainty, and often a consequence which is intended, or in fact an intention; cf. #bref("Gen 20:7"); #emph[and he shall pray for thee];, #he[וֶחְֽיֵה]; #emph[and thou shalt live];; cf. #bref("Exod 14:16");, #bref("2 Kgs 5:10");, #bref("Job 11:6");, #bref("Ps 128:5"); #emph[the Lord bless thee ... so that]; (or #emph[in order that];) #emph[thou seest];, etc.; #bref("Ruth 1:9");, #bref("4:11");; after a cohortative, #bref("Gen 12:2");, #bref("45:18");, #bref("Exod 3:10"); #he[וְהוֹצֵא]; #emph[that thou mayest bring forth];; #bref("Exod 18:22");, #bref("1 Sam 12:17");, #bref("1 Kgs 1:12");; #bref("Jer 35:15"); (after imperative and jussive); after an interrogative sentence, #bref("2 Sam 21:3"); #emph[wherewith shall I make atonement];, #he[וּבָֽרְכוּ]; #emph[that ye may bless];, etc.—In #bref("Num 5:19"); the imperative without #he[וְ]; (in 32:23 with #he[וְ];) is used after a conditional clause in the sense of a definite promise.

#ml("k") <p110k> Rem. The #emph[2nd sing. masc.]; occurs in addressing feminine persons in #bref("Judg 4:20"); (#he[עֲמֹד];, according to Qimḥi an infinitive, in which case, however, the infinitive absolute #he[עָמֹד]; should be read; but probably we should simply read #he[עִמְדִי]; with Moore), #bref("Mic 1:13"); and #bref("Zech 13:7"); (after #he[עוּרִי];); and in #bref("Isa 23:1");, the #emph[2nd plur. masc.]; (On #origpage("326") <orig-326>;the four forms of the #emph[2nd fem. plur. imperative]; in #bref("Isa 32:11");, erroneously explained here in former editions, see now #xref(<p48i>)[§ 48#h(0.18em)#emph[i];];). In #bref("Nah 3:15"); the interchange of masc. and fem. serves to express totality (the nation in all its aspects). Cf., moreover, #xref(<p145p>)[§ 145#h(0.18em)#emph[p];]; on other noticeable attempts to substitute the corresponding masculine forms for the feminine.

#sec("111", [The Imperfect with Wāw Consecutive], note: []) <s111>

#ml("a") <p111a> #strong[1.]; The #emph[imperfect]; with #emph[wāw consecutive]; (#xref(<p49a>)[§ 49#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];–#emph[g];) serves to express actions, events, or states, which are to be regarded as the temporal or logical sequel of actions, events, or states mentioned immediately#footnote[On an apparent exception (the #emph[imperf. consec.]; at the beginning of whole books) see #xref(<p49b>)[§ 49#h(0.18em)#emph[b];]; note.]; before. The #emph[imperfect consecutive]; is used in this way most frequently as the #emph[narrative tense];, corresponding to the Greek #emph[aorist]; or the Latin #emph[historic perfect];. As a rule the narrative is introduced by a perfect, and then continued by means of imperfects with #emph[wāw consecutive]; (on this interchange of tenses cf. #xref(<p49a>)[§ 49#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];, and especially #xref(<p112a>)[§ 112#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];), e.g. #bref("Gen 3:1"); #emph[now the serpent was]; (#he[הָיָה];) #emph[more subtil... and he said]; (#he[וַיֹּאמֶר];) #emph[unto the woman;]; #bref("4:1");, #bref("6:9ff.");, #bref("10:9f.");, #bref("15:19");, #bref("11:12ff. 27ff.");, #bref("14:5f.");, #bref("15:1f.");, #bref("16:1f.");, #bref("21:1ff.");, #bref("24:1f.");, #bref("25:19ff.");, #bref("36:2ff.");, #bref("37:2");.

#ml("b") <p111b> Rem. 1. To this class belong some of the numerous #emph[imperfects consec.]; after various expressions of time, whenever such expressions are equivalent in meaning to a perfect#footnote[Cf. #bref("Isa 45:4");, where the #emph[imperf. consec.]; is joined to an abrupt statement of the cause, and #bref("Job 36:7");, where it is joined to an abrupt statement of the place.]; (namely #he[הָיָה]; #emph[it came to pass];), e.g. #bref("Isa 6:1"); #emph[in the year that king Uzziah died, I saw]; (#he[וָֽאֶרְאֶה];), etc.; #bref("Gen 22:4");, #bref("27:34");, #bref("Judg 11:16");, #bref("1 Sam 4:19");, #bref("17:57");, #bref("21:6");, #bref("Hos 11:1");; on the use of #he[וַיְהִי]; to connect expressions of time, see below, #emph[g];.—It is only in late books or passages that we find the simple #emph[perfect]; in a clause following an expression of time, as #bref("1 Sam 17:55"); (cf. Driver on the passage), #bref("2 Chr 12:7");, #bref("15:8");, etc., #bref("Dan 10:11");, #bref("15:19");; the #emph[Perfect]; after #he[וְ]; and the subject, #bref("2 Chr 7:1");.

#ml("c") <p111c> 2. The continuation of the narrative by means of the imperfect consec. may result in a series of any number of such imperfects, e.g. there are forty\-nine in #bref("Gen 1");. As soon, however, as the connecting #emph[Wāw]; becomes separated from the verb to which it belongs, by the insertion of any word, the perfect necessarily takes the place of the imperfect, e.g. #bref("Gen 1:5"); #emph[and God called]; (#he[וַיִּקְרָא];) #emph[the light Day, and the darkness he called]; (#he[וְלַח֫שֶׁךְ קָרָא];) #emph[Night];; verse 10, 2:20, 11:3 and frequently.

#ml("d") <p111d> 3. Of two co\-ordinate #emph[imperfects consecutive]; the former (as equivalent to a temporal clause) is most frequently subordinate in sense to the latter, e.g. #bref("Gen 28:8f."); #he[וַיַּרְא עֵשָׂו... וַיֵּ֫לֶךְ]; #emph[when Esau saw that..., he went];, etc.; so also, frequently #he[וַיִּשְׁמַע];, etc., #bref("Gen 37:21");, etc. On the other hand, a second #emph[imperfect consecutive]; is seldom used in an explanatory sense, e.g. #bref("Exod 2:10"); (#he[וַתֹּ֫אמֶר]; #emph[for she said];); cf. #bref("1 Sam 7:12");. Other examples of the imperfect consecutive, which apparently represent a progress in the narrative, in reality only refer to the same time, or explain what precedes, see #bref("Gen 2:25"); (#he[וַיִּֽהְיוּ]; #emph[they were];; but #bref("Josh 4:9");, #bref("1 Kgs 8:8"); #emph[they are];); #bref("Gen 36:14"); (#he[וַתֵּ֫לֶד];), 36:32 (#he[וַיִּמְלֹךְ];), #bref("1 Kgs 1:44");. #origpage("327") <orig-327>;

#ml("e") <p111e> 4. The imperfect consecutive sometimes has such a merely #emph[external]; connection with an immediately preceding perfect, that in reality it represents an antithesis to it, e.g. #bref("Gen 32:31"); #emph[and]; (#emph[yet];) #emph[my life is preserved];; #bref("2 Sam 3:8"); #emph[and yet thou chargest me];; #bref("Job 10:8");, #bref("32:3");; similarly in dependence on noun\-clauses, #bref("Prov 30:25ff.");

#ml("f") <p111f> #strong[2.]; The introduction of independent narratives, or of a new section of the narrative, by means of an #emph[imperfect consecutive];, likewise aims at a connection, though again loose and external, with that which has been narrated previously. Such a connection is especially often established by means of #he[וַיְהִי]; (καὶ ἐγένετο) #emph[and it came to pass];, after which there then follows either (most commonly) an #emph[imperfect consecutive]; (#bref("Gen 4:3");, 8, 8:6, 11:2, #bref("Exod 12:29");, #bref("13:17");, etc.), or #emph[Wāw]; with the perfect (separated from it), #bref("Gen 7:10");, #bref("15:12");, #bref("22:1");, #bref("27:30");, or even a perfect without #emph[Wāw]; (#bref("Gen 8:13");, #bref("14:1");f., 40:1, #bref("Exod 12:41");, #bref("16:22");, #bref("Num 10:11");, #bref("Deut 1:3");, #bref("1 Sam 18:30");, #bref("2 Kgs 8:21");, etc.), or finally a noun\-clause introduced by #emph[Wāw];, #bref("Gen 41:1");.

#ml("g") <p111g> Rem. 1. This loose connection by means of #he[ויהי];#footnote[Exhaustive statistics of the use of #he[ויהי]; in its many and various connexions are given by König in #emph[ZAW.]; 1899, p. 260 ff.]; is especially common, when the narrative or a new section of it begins with any expression of time, see above, #emph[b];; cf., in addition to the above\-mentioned examples (e.g. #bref("Gen 22:1"); #emph[and it came to pass after these things, that God did prove Abraham];), the similar cases in #bref("Gen 19:34");, #bref("21:22");, #bref("1 Sam 11:11");, #bref("Ruth 1:1");. Elsewhere the statement of time is expressed by #he[בְּ]; or #he[כְּ‍]; with an infinitive (#bref("Gen 12:14");, #bref("19:17");, 29 39:13, 15:18f., #bref("Judg 16:25");) or by an independent sentence with the perfect (equivalent to a pluperfect, cf. #xref(<p106f>)[§ 106#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];), e.g. #bref("Gen 15:17");, #bref("24:15");, #bref("27:30");, or by a temporal clause introduced by #he[כִּי]; #emph[when];, #bref("Gen 26:8");, #bref("27:1");, #bref("Judg 16:16");, #he[כַּֽאֲשֶׁר]; #emph[when];, #bref("Gen 12:11");, #bref("20:13");, #he[מֵאָז]; #emph[from the time that];, #bref("Gen 39:5");; or, finally, by a noun\-clause (cf. #xref(<p116u>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[u];];), e.g. #bref("2 Kgs 13:21"); #he[וַיְהִי הֵם קֹֽבְרִים אִישׁ]; #emph[and it came to pass, as they were]; (just) #emph[burying a man]; (prop. they burying), #emph[that];...; #bref("Gen 42:35");, #bref("2 Kgs 2:11"); (the apodosis in both these cases being introduced by #he[וְהִנֵּה];); #bref("1 Sam 7:10");, #bref("2 Sam 13:30");, #bref("2 Kgs 6:5");, 26, 19:37 (\=#bref("Isa 37:38");).—In #bref("1 Sam 10:11");, #bref("11:11");, #bref("2 Sam 2:23");, #bref("15:2"); a noun standing absolutely follows #he[וַיְהִי]; (as the equivalent of a complete sentence; see below, #emph[h];), and then an #emph[imperfect consecutive]; follows.

#ml("h") <p111h> 2. Closely related to the cases noticed in #emph[g]; are those in which the #emph[imperfect consecutive];, even without a preceding #he[ויהי];, introduces the apodosis either— (#emph[a];) to whole sentences, or (#emph[b];) to what are equivalent to whole sentences, especially to nouns standing absolutely. As in certain cases of the #emph[perfect consecutive]; (see #xref(<p112x>)[§ 112#h(0.18em)#emph[x];];), so the #emph[imperfect consecutive]; has here acquired a sort of independent force. Cf. for (#emph[a];) #bref("1 Sam 15:23"); #emph[because thou hast rejected the word of the Lord];, #he[וַיִּמְאָֽסְךָ]; #emph[he hath rejected thee]; (cf. #bref("Num 14:16");, #bref("Isa 48:4");, where the causal clause precedes in the form of an infinitive with preposition), #bref("Exod 9:21");; for (#emph[b];) #bref("Gen 22:24"); #he[וּפִֽילַגְשׁוֹ]; #emph[and]; (as to) #emph[his concubine];..., #he[וַתֵּ֫לֶד]; #emph[she bare];, etc.; #bref("Exod 38:24");, #bref("Num 14:36");f., #bref("1 Sam 14:19");, #bref("17:24");, #bref("2 Sam 4:10");, #bref("19:41"); #emph[K#super[e];th.];, 21:16, #bref("1 Kgs 9:20");f., 12:17, #bref("2 Kgs 25:22");, #bref("Jer 6:19");, #bref("28:8");, #bref("33:24");, #bref("44:25");#footnote[Cf. the #emph[Mêšaʿ]; inscription, l. 5 (#emph[Omri];) #emph[the king of Israel];, #he[ויענו]; #emph[he oppressed Moab];, etc.—The peculiar imperfect consecutive in #bref("Gen 30:27"); b (in the earlier editions explained as equivalent to an object\-clause) arises rather from a pregnant brevity of expression: #emph[I have observed and]; have come to the conclusion, #emph[the Lord hath blessed me];, etc.—In #bref("Gen 27:34"); read, with LXX, #he[וַיְהִי]; before #he[כִּשְׁמֹעַ];.];—In #bref("1 Kgs 15:13");, #bref("2 Kgs 16:14"); the preceding noun, used absolutely, is even regarded as the object of the following imperfect consecutive, and is therefore introduced by #he[אֶת־];. #origpage("328") <orig-328>;

#ml("i") <p111i> #strong[3.]; The #emph[imperfect consecutive]; serves, in the cases treated under #emph[a–h];, to represent either expressly, or at least to a great extent, a #emph[chronological]; succession of actions or events; elsewhere it expresses those actions, etc., which represent the logical consequence of what preceded, or a result arising from it by an inherent necessity. Thus the #emph[imperfect consecutive]; is used—

#ml("k") <p111k> (#emph[a];) As a final summing up of the preceding narrative, e.g. #bref("Gen 2:1");, #bref("23:20"); #he[וַיָּ֫קָם הַשָּׂדֶה וג׳]; #emph[so]; (in this way) #emph[the field became]; (legally) #emph[the property of Abraham];, etc.; #bref("1 Sam 17:50");, #bref("31:6");.

#ml("l") <p111l> (#emph[b];) To express a logical or necessary consequence of that which immediately precedes, e.g. #bref("Gen 39:2");, #bref("Job 2:3"); #emph[and he still holdeth fast his integrity];, #he[וַתְּסִיתֵ֫נִי וג׳]; #emph[so that thou thus]; (as it now appears) #emph[groundlessly movedst me against him];; #bref("Ps 65:9"); #emph[so that they are afraid]; ...; even a consequence which happens conditionally, #bref("Jer 20:17"); #he[וַתְּהִי]; #emph[so that my mother should have been]; ... Another instance of the kind perhaps (if the text be correct) is #bref("Jer 38:9"); #he[וַיָּ֫מָת]; #emph[so that he dies]; (must die).

#ml("m") <p111m> Rem. Such consecutive clauses frequently occur after interrogative sentences, e.g. #bref("Isa 51:12"); #emph[who art thou]; (i.e. art thou #emph[so helpless];), #he[וַתִּֽירְאִי]; #emph[that thou art]; (#emph[must needs be];) #emph[afraid];? #bref("Ps 144:3"); (cf. #bref("Ps 8:5");, where in a very similar context #he[כִּי]; #emph[that]; is used with the imperfect); #bref("Gen 12:19"); (#he[וָֽאֶקַּח];); 31:27 #he[וָֽאֲשַׁלֵּֽחֲךָ]; #emph[so that I might have sent thee away];.

#strong[4.]; As regards the range of time it is to be carefully noticed—

#ml("n") <p111n> (#emph[a];) That the #emph[imperfect consecutive]; may represent all varieties in the relations of tense and mood, which, according to #xref(<p107a>)[§ 107#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];, follow from the idea of the imperfect;

#ml("o") <p111o> (#emph[b];) That the more precise determination of the range of time to which an #emph[imperfect consecutive]; relates must be inferred in each case from the character of the preceding tense (or tense\-equivalent), to which it is attached, in a more or less close relation, as temporal or logical sequence. Thus the #emph[imperfect consecutive]; serves—

#ml("p") <p111p> (1) To represent actions, events, or states, which are #emph[past]; (or were repeated in past time), when it is united with tenses, or their equivalents, which refer to an actual past.

#ml("q") <p111q> Cf. the examples given above, under #emph[a]; and #emph[f];, of the imperfect consecutive as an historic tense. The imperfect consecutive also frequently occurs as the continuation of a perfect (#emph[preterite];) in a subordinate clause; e.g. #bref("Gen 27:1");, #bref("Num 11:20");, #bref("Deut 4:37");, #bref("1 Sam 8:8");, #bref("1 Kgs 2:5");, #bref("11:33");, #bref("18:13");, etc.; also in #bref("Isa 49:7"); #he[וַיִּבְחָרֶ֫ךָּ]; is the continuation of a preterite, contained, according to the sense, in the preceding #he[אֲשֶׁר נֶֽאֱמָן];.—In #bref("Job 31:26");, 34 the imperfect consecutive is joined to an imperfect denoting the past in a conditional sentence. An imperfect consecutive occurs in dependence on a perfect which has the sense of a pluperfect (#xref(<p106f>)[§ 106#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];), e.g. in #bref("Gen 26:18");, #bref("28:6");f., 31:19, 34 (#emph[now Rachel had taken the teraphim];, #he[וַתְּשִׂמֵם]; #emph[and had]; #origpage("329") <orig-329>;#emph[put them];, etc.); #bref("Num 14:36");, #bref("1 Sam 28:3");, #bref("2 Sam 2:23");, #bref("Isa 39:1");. Finally there are the cases in which an infinitival or participial construction representing past time, according to #xref(<p113r>)[§ 113#h(0.18em)#emph[r];];, #xref(<p116x>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[x];];, is taken up and continued by an imperfect consecutive.

#ml("r") <p111r> (2) To represent #emph[present]; actions, etc., in connection with tenses, or their equivalents, which describe actions and states as being either present or lasting on into the present (continuing in their effect); so especially,

(α) In connection with the present perfects, described in #xref(<p106g>)[§ 106#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];, e.g. #bref("Ps 16:9"); #emph[therefore my heart is glad]; (#he[שָׂמַח];) #emph[and my glory rejoiceth]; (#he[וַיָּ֫גֶל];); #bref("Isa 3:16"); (parallel with a simple imperfect). Cf. also such examples as #bref("Ps 29:10"); #he[וַיֵּ֫שֶׁב]; (prop. he sat down, #emph[and has been enthroned]; ever since), #bref("Ps 41:13");.

#ml("s") <p111s> (β) In connection with those perfects which represent experiences frequently confirmed (see #xref(<p106k>)[§ 106#h(0.18em)#emph[k];];), e.g. #bref("Job 14:2"); #emph[he cometh up]; (#he[יָצָא];) #emph[like a flower, and is cut down]; (#he[וַיִּמָּל];); #emph[he fleeth]; (#he[וַיִּבְרַח];) #emph[also as a shadow];, #he[וְלֹא יַֽעֲמוֹד]; #emph[and continueth not];; #bref("Job 20:15");, #bref("24:2");, 11, #bref("Isa 40:24");, #bref("Prov 11:2");.

#ml("t") <p111t> (γ) In connection with imperfects which, in one of the ways described in § 107. 2, are used in the sense of the present; e.g. #bref("Job 14:10"); #emph[but man dieth]; (#he[יָמוּת];) #emph[and becometh powerless]; (#he[וַיֶּחֱֽלָשׁ];), etc., i.e. remains powerless; #bref("Job 4:5");, #bref("Hos 8:13");, #bref("Hab 1:9");f., #bref("Ps 55:18");, #bref("90:3");, #bref("Job 5:15");, #bref("7:18");, #bref("11:3"); (#emph[when thou mockest];), 12:25, 34:24, 37:8 (parallel with a simple imperfect); 39:15. In the apodosis of a conditional sentence, #bref("Ps 59:16");, so also after an interrogative imperfect, #bref("1 Sam 2:29");, #bref("Ps 42:6"); (#he[וַתֶּֽהֱמִי]; for which in verse 12 and in 43:5 we have #he[וּמַה־תֶּֽהֱמִי]; #emph[and why art thou disquieted?];).

#ml("u") <p111u> (δ) In dependence on participles, which represent what at present continues or is being repeated, e.g. #bref("Num 22:11");, #bref("1 Sam 2:6");, #bref("2 Sam 19:2"); #emph[behold the king weepeth]; (#he[בֹּכֶה];) #emph[and mourneth]; (#he[וַיִּתְאַבֵּל];) #emph[for Absalom];; #bref("Amos 5:8");, #bref("9:5");f., #bref("Nah 1:4");, #bref("Ps 34:8");, #bref("Prov 20:26");, #bref("Job 12:22ff.");, but cf. e.g. #bref("Job 12:4"); #he[קֹרֵא לֶֽאֱלוֹהַּ]; #emph[who called upon God];, #he[וַיַּֽעֲנֵ֫הוּ]; #emph[and he answered him];.

#ml("v") <p111v> (ε) In dependence on other equivalents of the present, as in #bref("Isa 51:12");, #bref("Ps 144:3"); (see above, #emph[m];); #bref("Job 10:22");. So especially as the continuation of an infinitive, which is governed by a preposition (cf. #xref(<p114r>)[§ 114#h(0.18em)#emph[r];];), #bref("Isa 30:12");, #bref("Jer 10:13");, #bref("Ps 92:8");, etc.

#ml("w") <p111w> (3) To represent #emph[future]; actions, etc., in dependence on—(α) an imperfect which refers to the future, #bref("Ps 49:15");, #bref("94:22");f.;—(β) a perfect consecutive, or those perfects which, according to #xref(<p106n>)[§ 106#h(0.18em)#emph[n];];, are intended to represent future events as undoubtedly certain, and therefore as though already accomplished (#emph[perf. propheticum];); cf. #bref("Isa 5:15"); (parallel with a simple imperfect separated from #he[ו];); 5:16 (cf. 2:11, 17, where the same threat is expressed by the perfect consecutive); 5:25, 9:5, 10f., #origpage("330") <orig-330>;13#super[15.17 ff.];, 22:7 ff., #bref("Joel 2:23");, #bref("Mic 2:13");, #bref("Ezek 33:4");, 6, #bref("Ps 7:13");, #bref("64:8"); ff.;—(γ) a future participle, #bref("Jer 4:16");.#footnote[Also in #bref("Jer 51:29"); the imperfects consecutive are attached to the threat virtually contained in the preceding imperatives. On the other hand #he[וַיָּחֵ֫לּוּ]; #bref("Hos 8:10"); would be very remarkable as expressing a future; the text is, however, certainly corrupt, and hence the Cod. Babyl. and the Erfurt MS. 3 endeavour to remedy it by #he[וְיח׳];, and Ewald reads #he[וְיָחִלוּ];.—In #bref("Ezek 28:16"); (cf. #bref("Jer 15:6");f.) #he[וָֽאֲחַלֶּלְךָ]; appears to announce an action irrevocably determined upon, and therefore represented as already accomplished; cf. the prophetic perfects in verse 17 ff.];

#ml("x") <p111x> Rem. An imperfect consecutive in dependence on a perfect or imperfect, which represents an action occurring only conditionally, is likewise used only in a hypothetical sense, e.g. #bref("Job 9:16"); #he[אִם־קָרָ֫אתִי וַיַּֽעֲנֵ֫נִי]; #emph[if I had called, and he had answered me, yet]; ...; #bref("Ps 139:11"); #he[וָֽאֹמַר]; #emph[if I should say]; (previously, in verse 8 f., hypothetical imperfects are used).—In #bref("Isa 48:18");f. an imperfect consecutive occurs in dependence on a sentence expressing a wish introduced by #he[לוּא]; #emph[utinam]; (#he[וַיְהִי]; #emph[and it];, or #emph[so that it were];, equivalent to #emph[then should it be];). Cf. also the examples mentioned above, under #emph[l]; (#bref("Jer 20:17");) and #emph[m]; (#bref("Gen 31:27");), where the imperfect consecutive expresses facts occurring #emph[contingently.];

#sec("112", [The Perfect with Wāw Consecutive], note: []) <s112>

G. R. Berry, ‘Waw consecutive with the perfect in Hebrew,’ in #emph[Bibl. Lit.];, xxii. (1903), pp. 60–69.

#ml("a") <p112a> #strong[1.]; The perfect, like the imperfect (§ 111), is used with #emph[wāw consecutive]; (cf. #xref(<p49a>)[§ 49#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];; on the external differentiation of the perfect consecutive by a change in the position of the tone, see #xref(<p49h>)[§ 49#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];) to express actions, events, or states, which are to be attached to what precedes, in a more or less close relation, as its #emph[temporal]; or #emph[logical]; consequence. And as, according to #xref(<p111a>)[§ 111#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];, the narrative which begins with a perfect, or its equivalent, is continued in the imperfect consecutive, so, vice versa, the perfect consecutive forms the regular continuation to a preceding imperfect, or its equivalent.

#ml("b") <p112b> Rem. 1. This alternation of perfect and imperfect or their equivalents is a striking peculiarity of the #emph[consecutio temporum]; in Hebrew. It not only affords a certain compensation for the lack of forms for tenses and moods, but also gives to Hebrew style the charm of an expressive variety, an action conceived as being still in progress (#emph[imperfect];, etc.), reaching afterwards in the #emph[perfect]; a calm and settled conclusion, in order to be again exhibited in movement in the imperfect, and vice versa.#footnote[It is difficult to give a proper explanation of this phenomenon (according to #xref(<p49a>)[§ 49#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];, note, to be found only in the Canaanitish group of languages), when we have given up the theory of a special #emph[wāw conversivum]; in the unscientific sense mentioned in #xref(<p49b>)[§ 49#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];, note, at the end, and if we accept the fact that the #emph[perfect]; and #emph[imperfect consecutive]; cannot possibly be used in a way which contradicts their fundamental character as described in §§ 106 and 107. In other words, even the #emph[perfect consecutive]; originally represents a finally completed action, etc., just as the #emph[imperfect consecutive]; represents an action which is only beginning, becoming or still continuing, and hence in any case incomplete. The simplest view is to suppose, that the use of the #emph[perfect consecutive]; originated from those cases, in which it had to express the conclusion (or final consequence) of an action which was continued (or repeated) #emph[in past time]; (see the examples above), and that this use was afterwards extended to other cases, in which it had to represent the temporal or logical consequence of actions, etc., still in progress, and thus in the end a regular interchange of the two tenses became recognized.]; The strict regularity of this #origpage("331") <orig-331>;alternation belongs indeed rather to the higher style, and even then it depends upon the view and intention of the speaker, whether he wishes the action, etc., to be regarded as the logical consequence of what has preceded, or as simply co\-ordinate with it, and so in the same tense.

#ml("c") <p112c> 2. A succession of any number of other #emph[perfects consecutive]; may be co\-ordinated with a #emph[perfect consecutive]; (cf. e.g. #bref("Ezek 14:13");, #bref("Amos 5:19");, #bref("Ruth 3:3");, four perfects in each case, #bref("Isa 8:7"); five, #bref("Exod 6:6");f. eight). It is true, however, of the perfect (as conversely of the #emph[imperfect];, #xref(<p111c>)[§ 111#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];), that as soon as the #emph[Wāw]; is separated by any intervening word from the verb to which it belongs, an #emph[imperfect]; necessarily takes the place of the #emph[perfect];, e.g. #bref("Gen 12:12"); #emph[when the Egyptians shall see thee, they shall say]; (#he[וְאָֽמְרוּ];), #emph[This is his wife: and they will kill me]; (#he[וְהָֽרְנוּ אֹתִי];) #emph[but thee they will save alive]; (#he[וְאֹתָךְ יְחַיּוּ];).

#ml("d") <p112d> #strong[2.]; The perfect consecutive, like the imperfect consecutive, always belongs to the period of time expressed by the preceding tense, or its equivalent, with which it is connected as the temporal or logical consequence. The particular cases may be classed under three heads: (#emph[a];) the perfect consecutive in #emph[immediate]; dependence (see #emph[e];), (#emph[b];) in loose connection (see #emph[x];) with the preceding, and (#emph[c];) the perfect consecutive at the beginning of the apodosis to other sentences, or their equivalents (see #emph[ff];).

#ml("e") <p112e> #strong[3.]; The perfect consecutive in immediate dependence on the preceding tense, or its equivalent, serves

(#emph[a];) As a frequentative tense to express #emph[past]; actions, etc., i.e. actions repeatedly brought to a conclusion in the past, and follows tenses, or their equivalents, representing actions which have #emph[continued]; or been #emph[repeated in the past];:

(α) After a simple imperfect, e.g. #bref("Gen 2:6"); #he[אֵד יַֽעֲלֶה]; #emph[there went up a mist]; (again and again) #emph[from the earth];, #he[וְהִשְׁקָה]; #emph[and watered]; (as it were, #emph[and ever watered afresh];), etc. This frequentative use of the perfect consecutive is equally evident after frequentative imperfects, #bref("Gen 2:10"); (#he[וְהָיָה]; #emph[and it became]; again every time; #he[וַיְהִי]; would mean, and it became so once for all); 29:2f. (four perfects consecutive referring to actions repeated daily); #bref("Exod 33:7");–11 #he[יִקַּח]; #emph[he used to take]; at each new encampment #emph[the tent];, #he[וְנָטָה]; #emph[and to pitch it]; again every time #emph[without the camp];; notice, among the numerous frequent. perff. consec., the imperf. in vv. 7, 8, 9, 11, always in a frequentative sense; 34:34f., #bref("Num 9:19");, 21 (among several simple imperfects), 10:17, #bref("Judg 2:19");, #bref("1 Sam 2:19"); #he[תַּֽעֲשֶׂה]; #origpage("332") <orig-332>;#emph[she used to make];... #he[וְהַֽעַלְתָה]; #emph[and brought it to him from year to year];; #bref("27:9"); (#he[וְלָקַח];), #bref("1 Kgs 14:28");, #bref("2 Kgs 3:25");, #bref("12:15"); (in verses 16#super[f.]; imperfects occur again). So also in dependent sentences, #bref("Gen 6:4"); (#he[וְיָֽלְדוּ]; as a continuation of #he[יָבֹאוּ];), #bref("Job 31:29");.#footnote[Also in #bref("Ezek 44:12"); (where Stade, #emph[ZAW.]; v. 293, would read #he[שֵֽׁרְתוּ]; and #he[וַיִּֽהְיוּ];) the unusual tenses may have been intentionally chosen: #emph[because they continually ministered and so always became afresh];...];

#ml("f") <p112f> (β) After an imperfect consecutive, e.g. #bref("Exod 39:3"); (Samaritan #he[וקצצו];), #bref("1 Sam 5:7"); (? see #xref(<p112rr>)[§ 112#h(0.18em)#emph[rr];];), #bref("7:16");, #bref("2 Sam 15:2, 5");, #bref("16:13"); #emph[and he threw stones at him];, #he[וְעִפַּר]; #emph[and east dust]; continually; #bref("12:16");, #bref("2 Kgs 6:10");, #bref("12:11");, #bref("Jer 37:15");, #bref("Job 1:5");.

#ml("g") <p112g> Rem. The frequentative perfect consecutive is sometimes joined even with imperfects consecutive which simply express one #emph[single]; action or occurrence in the past; thus #bref("Exod 18:26");, #bref("40:31"); f., #bref("1 Sam 1:4");, #bref("2 Sam 15:1"); f., #bref("1 Kgs 14:27"); (cf. verse 28); #bref("1 Kgs 18:4");, #bref("2 Kgs 12:10");. For other examples of a loosely connected frequentative perfect consecutive, see below, #xref(<p112dd>)[#emph[dd];];.

#ml("h") <p112h> (γ) After a perfect, #bref("Gen 37:3"); (#he[וְעָשָׂה לוֹ];, i.e. as often as he needed a new garment)#footnote[Driver, on this passage, rightly refers to #bref("1 Sam 2:19");.];; #bref("Gen 31:7");, #bref("Num 11:8");, #bref("1 Sam 16:14");, #bref("2 Kgs 3:4");, #bref("Ps 22:6");;#footnote[#bref("Amos 4:7"); would also come under this head, if #he[וְהִמְטַרְתִּ֫י]; is really intended, and the statement refers to the past; #he[מָנַ֫עְתִּי]; might, however, also be a perfect expressing positive assurance (#xref(<p106m>)[§ 106#h(0.18em)#emph[m];];), and the passage would then come under #xref(<p112s>)[#emph[s];];.]; in interrogative sentences, #bref("1 Sam 26:9"); #emph[who has ever];, etc.; #bref("Ps 80:13");, #bref("Job 1:1, 4");, #bref("Ruth 4:7");.

#ml("i") <p112i> (δ) After an infinitive, #bref("Amos 1:11"); #he[עַל־רָדְפוֹ]; #emph[because he did pursue his brother];, #he[וְשִׁחֵת]; #emph[and #emph[(on each occasion)]; did east off all pity]; (then an imperfect consecutive); after an infinitive absolute, #bref("Josh 6:13");, #bref("2 Sam 13:19");, #bref("Jer 23:14");.

#ml("k") <p112k> (ε) After a participle, #bref("Isa 6:3"); (#he[וְקָרָא];), etc., frequentative, as a continuation of #he[עֹֽמְדִים];, verse 2); #bref("1 Sam 2:22");, #bref("2 Sam 17:17");.#footnote[That #he[וְהָֽלְכָה];, etc., are frequentatives (#emph[the maidservant used to go repeatedly and tell them];) may be seen from #he[יֵֽלֵכוּ]; (necessarily an imperfect, since it is separated from #he[וְ]; by #he[הֵם];) and #he[יֽוּכְלוּ];; on the other hand in verse 18 #he[וַיַּרְא]; and #he[וַיֵּֽלְכוּ]; of actions which happened only once.];

#ml("l") <p112l> (ζ) After other equivalents of tenses, e.g. #bref("Gen 47:22"); #emph[the priests had a portion from Pharaoh];, #he[וְאָֽכְלוּ]; #emph[and did eat]; (year by year), etc.; #bref("1 Kgs 4:7");.

#ml("m") <p112m> (#emph[b];) To express #emph[present]; actions, etc., as the temporal or logical consequence of actions or events which continue or are repeated in the present, especially such as have, according to experience, been at all times frequently repeated, and may be repeated at any time:

(α) After a simple imperfect, e.g. #bref("Gen 2:24"); #emph[therefore a man leaves]; (#he[יַֽעֲזֹב]; #emph[is accustomed to leave];) ... #he[וְדָבַק]; #emph[and cleaves];, etc., here, as frequently elsewhere, clearly with the secondary idea of purpose, i.e. in order to cleave; #bref("Isa 5:11"); (if #he[וְהָיָה]; is to be taken as a continuation #origpage("333") <orig-333>;of #he[יַדְלִיקֵם];); #bref("Isa 28:28");, #bref("Jer 12:3");, #bref("Hos 4:3");, #bref("7:7");, #bref("Ps 90:6");, #bref("Job 14:9");; also in dependent clauses, #bref("Lev 20:18");, #bref("Isa 29:8, 11f.");, #bref("Amos 5:19");.

#ml("n") <p112n> (β) After a participle, as the equivalent of a sentence representing a contingent action, etc., e.g. #bref("Exod 21:12"); #he[מַכֵּה אִישׁ וָמֵת]; (instead of #he[מַכֵּה]; there is in verse 20, etc. #he[וְכִי יַכֶּה אִישׁ];) #emph[if one smite a man and #emph[(so that)]; he die];, etc., #bref("Exod 21:16");, #bref("Isa 29:15");, #bref("Amos 6:1");, #bref("Hab 2:12");.

#ml("o") <p112o> (γ) After an infinitive absolute, #bref("Jer 7:9"); f. #emph[will ye steal, murder, and commit adultery]; (simple infinitives absolute; cf. #xref(<p113ee>)[§ 113#h(0.18em)#emph[ee];];), #he[וּבָאתֶם]; #emph[and then come and stand before me... and say];, etc.; cf. below, #xref(<p112u>)[#emph[u];];.

#ml("p") <p112p> (#emph[c];) To express #emph[future]; actions, etc., as the temporal or logical consequence of tenses, or their equivalents, which announce or require such future actions or events. Thus—

(α) After imperfects in the sense of a simple future, e.g. #bref("Amos 9:3"); f. #he[מִשָּׁם אֲחַפֵּשׂ וּלְקַחְתִּים]; #emph[I will search and take them out thence];, etc.; #bref("Gen 4:14");, #bref("40:13");, #bref("Exod 7:3");, #bref("1 Sam 17:32");, #bref("2 Kgs 5:11");, #bref("Job 8:6"); f. (also with a change of subject, #bref("Gen 27:12");, #bref("Judg 6:16");, etc.); and in interrogative sentences, #bref("Gen 39:9");, #bref("Exod 2:7");, #bref("2 Sam 12:18");, #bref("2 Kgs 14:10");, #bref("Amos 8:8");, #bref("Ps 41:6");; cf. also #bref("Ruth 1:11");; in sentences expressing a wish, #bref("2 Sam 15:4");; as well as in almost all kinds of dependent clauses. Also in conditional clauses after #he[אִם־]; #bref("Gen 32:9");, #bref("Exod 19:5");, #bref("1 Sam 1:11");, or #he[כִּי]; #bref("Gen 37:26");, or #he[הֵן]; #bref("Jer 3:1");; in final clauses after #he[לְמַ֫עַן]; #bref("Gen 12:13");, #bref("Num 15:40");, #bref("Isa 28:13");; after #he[אֲשֶׁר]; #bref("Deut 2:25");, or #he[פֶּן־]; #bref("Gen 3:22");, #bref("19:19");, #bref("32:12");, #bref("Isa 6:10");, #bref("Amos 5:6");; in temporal clauses, #bref("Isa 32:15");, #bref("Jer 13:16");; and in relative clauses, #bref("Gen 24:14");, #bref("Judg 1:12");, #bref("1 Sam 17:26");.

#ml("q") <p112q> (β) After the jussive (or an imperfect in the sense of a jussive or optative) or cohortative, with the same or a different subject, e.g. #bref("Gen 1:14"); f. #he[יְהִי מְאֹרֹת... וְהָיוּ]; #emph[let there be lights... and let them be];, etc.; #bref("Gen 24:4");, #bref("28:3");, #bref("31:44");, #bref("1 Kgs 1:2");, #bref("22:13");, #bref("Ruth 2:7");, #bref("1 Chr 22:11");; after a jussive expressing an imprecation, #bref("Ps 109:10");.

#ml("r") <p112r> (γ) After an imperative, also with the same or a different subject, e.g. #bref("2 Sam 7:5"); #he[לֵךְ וְאָֽמַרְתָּ]; #emph[go and tell]; (that thou mayst tell), etc., and often, #emph[perf. consec.]; after #he[לֵךְ]; (as also the #emph[perf. consec.]; of #he[אָמַר]; and #he[דִּבֶּר]; very frequently follows other imperatives); #bref("Gen 6:14");, #bref("8:17");, #bref("27:43"); f., #bref("1 Sam 15:3");, 18, #bref("1 Kgs 2:36");, #bref("Jer 48:26");.

#ml("s") <p112s> (δ) After perfects which express a definite expectation or assurance (cf. #xref(<p106m>)[§ 106#h(0.18em)#emph[m];]; and #emph[n];), e.g. #bref("Gen 17:20"); #he[הִנֵּה בֵּרַ֫כְתִּי אֹתוֹ וְהִפְרֵתִי אֹתוֹ]; #emph[behold, I have blessed him, and will make him fruitful];, etc.; #bref("Isa 2:11");, #bref("5:14");; on #bref("Amos 4:7"); see above, note 3 on #emph[h];; in an interrogative sentence, #bref("Judg 9:9");, #bref("11:13");.

#ml("t") <p112t> (ε) After a participle, e.g. #bref("Gen 7:4"); #emph[for yet seven days];, #he[אָֽנֹכִי מַמְטִיר]; #emph[and I will cause it to rain];... #he[וּמָחִ֫יתִי]; #emph[and I will]; (i.e. in order to) #emph[destroy];, etc.; #bref("Jer 21:9");; also with a different subject, #bref("Gen 24:43"); f. #emph[the]; #origpage("334") <orig-334>;#emph[ maiden which cometh forth]; (#he[הַיֹּצֵאת];)... #he[וְאָֽמַרְתִּי אֵלֶ֫יהָ]; #emph[to whom I shall say];..., #he[וְאָֽמְרָה]; #emph[and she]; (then) #emph[shall say];, etc. This use of the perfect consecutive is especially frequent after a participle introduced by #he[הִנֵּה];, e.g. #bref("Gen 6:17"); f.; with a different subject #bref("1 Kgs 20:36");, #bref("Amos 6:14");; after a complete noun\-clause introduced by #he[הִנֵּה]; (cf. § 140), #bref("Exod 3:13"); #emph[behold, I come]; (i.e. if I shall come)... #he[וְאָֽמַרְתִּי לָהֶם]; #emph[and shall say unto them];..., #he[וְאָֽמְרוּ]; #emph[and they]; (then) #emph[shall say];, etc.; #bref("1 Sam 14:8"); ff., #bref("Isa 7:14");, #bref("8:7"); f., 39:6.

#ml("u") <p112u> (ζ) After an infinitive absolute, whether the infinitive absolute serves to strengthen the finite verb (see #xref(<p113t>)[§ 113#h(0.18em)#emph[t];];), e.g. #bref("Isa 31:5");, or is used as an emphatic substitute for a cohortative or imperfect (#xref(<p113dd>)[§ 113#h(0.18em)#emph[dd];]; and #emph[ee];), e.g. #bref("Lev 2:6");, #bref("Deut 1:16");, #bref("Isa 5:5");, #bref("Ezek 23:46"); f.

#ml("v") <p112v> (η) After an infinitive construct governed by a preposition (for this change from the infinitive construction to the finite verb, cf. #xref(<p114r>)[§ 114#h(0.18em)#emph[r];];), e.g. #bref("1 Sam 10:8"); #he[עַד־בּוֹאִי אֵלֶ֫יךָ וְהֽוֹדַעְתִּי לְךָ]; #emph[till I come unto thee]; (prop. until my coming) #emph[and show thee];, etc.; #bref("Gen 18:25");, #bref("27:45");, #bref("Judg 6:18");, #bref("Ezek 39:27");; cf. #bref("1 Kgs 2:37");, 42.

#ml("w") <p112w> Rem. To the same class belong #bref("1 Sam 14:24");, where the idea of time precedes, #emph[until it be evening and]; until #emph[I be avenged];, etc., and #bref("Isa 5:8");, where the idea of place precedes, in both cases governed by #he[עַד־];.

#ml("x") <p112x> #strong[4.]; The very frequent use of the perfect consecutive in direct dependence upon other tenses (see above, #emph[d];–#emph[v];) explains how it finally obtained a kind of independent force—especially for the purpose of announcing future events—and might depend loosely on sentences to which it stood only in a wider sense in the relation of a temporal or logical consequence. Thus the perfect consecutive is used—

(#emph[a];) To announce #emph[future]; events, etc., in loose connection with a further announcement, e.g. #bref("Gen 41:30"); #he[וְקָמוּ]; and two co\-ordinate perfects consecutive, equivalent to #emph[but then shall arise];, etc.; frequently so after #he[הִנֵּה]; with a following substantive (#bref("1 Sam 9:8");), or a participial clause (cf. the analogous instances above, under #emph[t];), e.g. #bref("1 Sam 2:31"); #emph[behold, the days come];, #he[וְגָֽדַעְתִּי]; #emph[that I will cut off];, etc.; #bref("Isa 39:6");, #bref("Amos 4:2");, #bref("8:11");, #bref("9:13");, and very often in Jeremiah; after an expression of time, #bref("Exod 17:4");, #bref("Isa 10:25");, #bref("29:17");, #bref("Jer 51:33");, #bref("Hos 1:4");. Further, when joined to a statement concerning present or past facts, especially when these contain the reason for the action, etc., expressed in the perfect consecutive; cf. #bref("Isa 6:7"); #emph[lo, this hath touched thy lips];, #he[וְסָר]; #emph[therefore thine iniquity shall be taken away];, etc. (not copulative #emph[and it is taken away];, since it is parallel with a simple imperfect), #bref("Gen 20:11");, #bref("26:22");, #bref("Judg 13:3"); (here in an adversative sense); #bref("Hos 8:14");. In loose connection with a noun\-clause, a long succession of perfects consecutive occurs in #bref("Exod 6:6"); ff. Also in #bref("Amos 5:26"); #he[וּנְשָׁאתֶם]; may be an announcement #emph[yea, ye shall take up];; but cf. below, #emph[rr];. #origpage("335") <orig-335>;

#ml("y") <p112y> Rem. 1. Very frequently the announcement of a future event is attached by means of #he[וְהָיָה];#footnote[On the various combinations with #he[וְהָיָה]; see König’s statistics in #emph[ZAW.]; xix. 272 ff.]; #emph[and it shall come to pass]; (cf. the analogous continuation in the past by means of #he[וַיְהִי];, § 111, 2), after which the event announced (sometimes after a long parenthesis) follows in one or more (co\-ordinate) perfects consecutive, #bref("Gen 9:14");, #bref("12:12"); (#he[וְהָיָה כִי];\=#emph[if];, as in 46:33, #bref("Exod 1:10");, #bref("22:26"); and frequently), #bref("1 Kgs 18:12");, #bref("Isa 14:3"); f., #bref("Amos 8:9");; or in the imperfect, #bref("Gen 4:14");, #bref("Isa 2:2");, #bref("3:24");, #bref("4:3");, #bref("7:18");, 21 ff. (cf. 29:8); or in the jussive, #bref("Lev 14:9");. It very rarely happens that the verb which is thus loosely added, agrees in gender and number with the following subject, as in #bref("Num 5:27");, #bref("Jer 42:16"); #he[וְהָֽיְתָה]; (before #he[הַחֶ֫רֶב];), and in #bref("Jer 42:17"); #he[וְיִהְיוּ]; (before #he[כָּל־הָֽאֲנָשִׁים];).

#ml("z") <p112z> 2. The jussive form #he[וִיהִי]; occurs (in the sense described in #emph[y];) instead of #he[וְהָיָה]; in #bref("1 Sam 10:5");, #bref("2 Sam 5:24"); (#bref("1 Chr 14:15");), #bref("1 Kgs 14:5");, #bref("Ruth 3:4");, although in the first three places a jussive is wholly inadmissible in the context, and even in #bref("Ruth 3:4"); (where an admonition follows) #he[וְהָיָה]; would be expected (see below, #emph[bb];). In #bref("1 Kgs 14:5"); the form is a textual error, and the pointing should simply be #he[וַיְהִי];. In the other passages #he[ויהי]; (always before an infinitive with a preposition) stands at the beginning of the sentence at an unusually long distance from the principal tone, and hence is certainly to be explained according to #xref(<p109k>)[§ 109#h(0.18em)#emph[k];];, except that in #bref("1 Sam 10:5");, etc., the simply rhythmical jussive form takes the place, not of the full imperfect form, but (exceptionally) of the perfect consecutive.

#ml("aa") <p112aa> (#emph[b];) To introduce a command or wish: #bref("Deut 10:19"); #emph[love ye therefore the stranger];; #bref("1 Sam 6:5");, #bref("24:16");, #bref("1 Kgs 2:6"); (in #bref("Gen 40:14"); the precative perfect consecutive, as elsewhere the cohortative, jussive, and imperative, is strengthened by means of #he[נָא];). So, also, in loose connection with participial and other noun\-clauses (see above, #emph[x];), #bref("Gen 45:12"); f., #bref("1 Kgs 2:2"); f., #bref("Ruth 3:3"); f., 3:9.—In #bref("Gen 17:11"); the perfect consecutive (#he[וּנְמַלְתֶּם]; #emph[and ye shall be circumcised];, etc.) is used to explain a preceding command.

#ml("bb") <p112bb> Rem. As in the cases mentioned above under #emph[y];, the connection may be made by means of #he[וְהָיָה];. Thus with a following perfect consecutive, e.g. #bref("Gen 46:33");, #bref("47:24");, #bref("Judg 4:20");. Cf. also #bref("Gen 24:14");, where the real wish, at least as regards the sense, is contained in the next sentence.

#ml("cc") <p112cc> (#emph[c];) To introduce a question, whether in loose connection with another interrogative sentence (see above, #emph[p];), e.g. #bref("Gen 29:15"); #emph[art thou my brother]; (equivalent to, #emph[Surely thou art];), #he[וַֽעֲבַדְתַּ֫נִי]; #emph[and shouldest thou then serve me for naught];? or with a positive statement, e.g. #bref("Exod 5:5"); (#he[וְהִשְׁבַּתֶּם]; #emph[will ye then make them rest?];); #bref("Num 16:10");, #bref("1 Sam 25:11");, and (if it is #emph[Mil#super[e];raʿ];) #bref("Ps 50:21"); (#he[וְהֶֽחֱרַשְׁתִּי];).

#ml("dd") <p112dd> (#emph[d];) To introduce actions frequently repeated (hence analogous to the numerous examples of a frequentative perfect consecutive, above, under #emph[e];), e.g. #bref("1 Sam 1:3"); (#he[וְעָלָה]; of annual festival journeys); 13:21 (where, however, the text appears radically corrupt); 27:9 (#he[וְהִכָּה];, i.e. every time, therefore continued by means of #he[וְלֹא יְחַיֶּה];); #bref("1 Kgs 5:7"); (#he[וְכִלְכְּלוּ];, #origpage("336") <orig-336>;parallel with a simple imperfect); 9:25, #bref("Jer 25:4");, #bref("Hos 12:11");, #bref("Dan 8:4");.—In #bref("Job 1:4"); f. a series of frequentative perfects consecutive is interrupted by an imperfect consecutive, while a simple imperfect (as the #emph[modus rei repetitae];) forms the conclusion. In #bref("Jer 6:17"); a similar perfect is expressly marked, by placing the tone on the final syllable (according to #xref(<p49h>)[§ 49#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];), as parallel with the real perfects consecutive.

#ml("ee") <p112ee> Rem. The loose connection of #emph[tempora frequentativa]; by #he[וְהָיָה]; (cf. the Rem. on #emph[y]; and #emph[bb];) is also very common in this sense; thus with a following perfect consecutive, #bref("Gen 30:41"); f. (but in verse 42#emph[#super[a];];, where the verb is separated from the #emph[Wāw]; by an insertion, we find #he[לֹא יָשִׂים]; #emph[he used not to put them in];, according to #xref(<p107e>)[§ 107#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];); #bref("Gen 38:9");, #bref("Exod 17:11");, #bref("33:7"); ff. (see above, #emph[e];), #bref("Num 21:9");, #bref("Judg 6:3");, #bref("19:30");, #bref("1 Sam 16:23"); (followed by five perfects consecutive); #bref("2 Sam 15:5");; with a following imperfect (as the #emph[modus rei repetitae];), #bref("Judg 2:19");, #bref("2 Sam 14:26");.—In #bref("Judg 12:5"); f. #he[והיה];, contrary to what would be expected, is continued by means of the imperfect consecutive, and in #bref("1 Sam 13:22"); by #he[וְלֹא]; with the perfect (instead of the imperfect).

#ml("ff") <p112ff> #strong[5.]; Further, the perfect consecutive is very frequently employed with a certain emphasis to introduce the apodosis after sentences (or their equivalents) which contain a condition, a reason, or a statement of time. Such an apodosis, as in the cases already treated, may represent either future events, or commands and wishes, or even events which have been often repeated in the past. Thus—

(#emph[a];) The perfect consecutive occurs in the apodosis to conditional sentences#footnote[In a number of the examples of this kind the protasis is already loosely connected by means of #he[והיה];, and hence some of them had to be already mentioned above, under #emph[y];, #emph[bb];, #emph[ee];.]; (#xref(<p159g>)[§ 159#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];, #emph[o];, #emph[s];):

(α) After #he[אִם]; with the imperfect, e.g. #bref("2 Kgs 7:4");b #he[אִם־יְמִיתֻ֫נוּ וָמָֽתְנוּ]; #emph[if they kill us];, (well then) #emph[we shall but die];; here the perfect consecutive is used obviously with greater emphasis than the imperfect (#he[נִחְיֶה];) which immediately precedes; #bref("Gen 18:26");, #bref("24:8");, 41, 32:9, #bref("Num 30:15");, #bref("Judg 4:20");, #bref("1 Sam 1:11");, #bref("20:6");, #bref("1 Kgs 3:14");, #bref("Nah 3:12");, #bref("Eccl 4:11");.

#ml("gg") <p112gg> (β) After #he[אִם]; with the perfect (in the sense of a #emph[futurum exactum];), #bref("Num 5:27");, #bref("2 Kgs 5:20");, 7#super[4#emph[a];];, #bref("Isa 4:4"); f.; as precative apodosis after #he[אִם־נָא]; with the #emph[perf. preteritum];, #bref("Gen 33:10");; as a frequentative perfect consecutive, to represent past events in the apodosis after #he[אִם]; with a perfect, #bref("Gen 38:9");, #bref("Num 21:9");, #bref("Judg 6:3");, #bref("Job 7:4");; after #he[אִם]; with imperfect, #bref("Gen 31:8");.

#ml("hh") <p112hh> (γ) After #he[כִּי]; (#emph[in case, suppose that];) with the imperfect, #bref("Gen 12:12");, #bref("Exod 18:16");, #bref("Judg 13:17");, #bref("Isa 58:7");, #bref("Ezek 14:13");.#footnote[In #bref("1 Sam 24:19"); a question appears to be expressed by the perfect consecutive, #emph[for if a man find his enemy, will he let him go well away?]; Probably, however, with Klostermann, #he[וּמִי]; should be read for #he[וְכִי];.]; Frequentative with reference to the past, after #he[כִּי]; with frequentative perfect, #bref("Judg 2:18");, #bref("Job 7:13");f. #origpage("337") <orig-337>;

#ml("ii") <p112ii> (δ) After #he[אֲשֶׁר]; with the imperfect, #bref("Gen 44:9"); #he[אֲשֶׁר יִמָּצֵא אִתּוֹ... וָמֵת]; #emph[with whomsoever... it be found, let him die];; with the perfect, #bref("Exod 21:13"); #emph[and if a man lie not in wait];, etc.; #bref("Judg 1:12");.

#ml("kk") <p112kk> (ε) Very frequently after a perfect consecutive (one or more) containing the condition, e.g. #bref("Gen 44:29"); #he[וּלְקַחְתֶּם גַּם־אֶת־זֶה];... #he[וְהֽוֹרַדְתֶּם]; and if ye take (or #emph[shall have taken];) #emph[this one also... ye shall bring down];, etc.; cf. #bref("Gen 33:13");, #bref("42:38");, #bref("44:4");, 22, 47:30, #bref("Num 30:12");, #bref("Ruth 2:9");, and probably also #bref("Ezek 39:28");.—Also frequentative in reference to the past, e.g. #bref("1 Sam 17:34"); f. #he[וּבָא הָֽאֲרִי... וְיָצָ֫אתִי]; #emph[and when there came]; (as sometimes happened) #emph[a lion... I went out];, etc.; #bref("Exod 33:10");, #bref("Num 10:17"); ff., #bref("1 Kgs 18:10");, #bref("Jer 20:9"); (the perfects consecutive being regularly continued in the apodosis by #he[וְלֹא]; with an imperfect#footnote[In all these examples (not only in the frequentative perfects consecutive) the original idea of the perfect, which also underlies the perfect consecutive, comes out very distinctly. #bref("Gen 44:29"); (see above) implies in the mind of the speaker, If it ever shall have come to this, that ye #emph[have taken]; this one also, then #emph[ye have]; thereby brought me down to Sheol.];).

#ml("ll") <p112ll> Rem. The perfect consecutive may be used also in the protasis to express a condition when the employment of the perfect consecutive in the apodosis has become impossible, owing to an emphatic word having to stand before it; thus in #bref("Ezek 14:14"); on account of #he[הֵ֫מָּה];; 33:4 on account of #he[דָּמוֹ];.—In #bref("1 Sam 14:52"); the imperfect consecutive, contrary to what might be expected, stands in the apodosis, #emph[and when Saul saw any... valiant man, he took him unto him];, where #he[וַיַּֽאַסְפֵ֫הוּ]; suggests the special case, rather than a repeated occurrence; cf. #bref("2 Sam 15:2");. Conversely, in #bref("1 Sam 2:16"); (#he[וַיֹּאמֶר]; perhaps a mere mistake for #he[וְאָמַר];), 17:35 b an imperfect consecutive stands in the protasis.

#ml("mm") <p112mm> (ζ) After various equivalents of sentences, which contain a condition; thus, after a substantive standing absolutely, or a participle (a #emph[casus pendens];), #bref("Gen 17:14"); #he[וְעָרֵל זָכָר... וְנִכְרְתָה וג׳]; #emph[and the uncircumcised male]; (in case such an one be found), #emph[he shall be cut off];, etc.; cf. #bref("Gen 30:32");, #bref("Exod 12:15");, #bref("2 Sam 14:10");, #bref("Isa 6:13");, and (after an infinitive with a preposition) #bref("2 Sam 7:14");; in a wider sense also #bref("Exod 4:21");, #bref("9:19");, #bref("12:44");, #bref("Isa 9:4");, #bref("56:5");.

#ml("nn") <p112nn> (#emph[b];) The perfect consecutive serves as the apodosis to causal clauses; thus e.g. after #he[יַ֫עַן כִּי]; with the perfect, #bref("Isa 3:16"); f.; after #he[יַ֫עַן אֲשֶׁר]; with perfect, #bref("1 Kgs 20:28");; after #he[עֵ֫קֶב]; with perfect, #bref("Num 14:24");; also after what are equivalent to causal clauses, e.g. #bref("Ps 25:11"); (#he[לְמַ֫עַן שִׁמְךָ... וְסָֽלַחְתָּ]; #emph[for thy name’s sake];... #emph[pardon];...); #bref("Isa 37:29"); after #he[יַ֫עַן]; with an infinitive.

#ml("oo") <p112oo> (#emph[c];) The perfect consecutive occurs as the apodosis to temporal clauses or their equivalents, e.g. #bref("1 Sam 2:15"); #he[בְּטֶ֫רֶם יַקְטִרוּן אֶת־הַחֵ֫לֶב... וּבָא נַ֫עַר הַכֹּהֵן]; #emph[before they burnt the fat, the priest’s servant came]; (used to come), etc., hence a frequentative perfect consecutive relating to the past, as in #bref("Exod 1:19");; also after participial clauses (#xref(<p116w>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[w];];), e.g. #bref("1 Sam 2:13"); f. #he[כָּל־אִישׁ זֹבֵחַ זֶ֫בַח... וּבָא]; #emph[when#emph[(ever)]; any man offered sacrifice, then came];, #origpage("338") <orig-338>;etc. (so #bref("Judg 19:30");, #bref("2 Sam 20:12");), with a frequentative perfect consecutive. The perfect consecutive is very frequently used to announce future actions or events after simple expressions of time of any kind; thus #bref("Gen 3:5");, #bref("Exod 32:34"); (after #he[בְּיוֹם]; with the infinitive), cf. also such examples as #bref("Gen 44:31");, #bref("Judg 16:2");, #bref("Josh 6:10");, #bref("1 Sam 1:22");, #bref("16:23"); (numerous frequentative perfects consecutive after the infinitive with a preposition; so #bref("2 Sam 15:5");, see above, #emph[ee];); #bref("1 Sam 20:18");, #bref("2 Sam 14:26");, #bref("15:10");, #bref("Isa 18:5");; moreover, #bref("Exod 17:4");, #bref("Isa 10:25");, #bref("29:17");, #bref("37:26");; even after single disconnected words, e.g. #bref("Exod 16:6"); #he[עֶ֫רֶב וִֽידַעְתֶּם]; #emph[at even]; (when it becomes evening) #emph[then ye shall know];; cf. verse 7, #bref("Lev 7:16");, #bref("1 Kgs 13:31");, #bref("Prov 24:27");.

#ml("pp") <p112pp> #strong[6.]; Finally there still remains a number of passages which cannot be classed with any of those hitherto mentioned. Of these, some are due to the influence of Aramaic modes of expression, while in others the text is evidently corrupt.#footnote[Mayer Lambert, #emph[REJ.]; xxvi. 55, is probably right in pointing some of these forms as #emph[infin. abs.]; instead of #emph[perfects];.]; In a few instances we can do no more than merely call attention to the incorrectness of the expression. (We are not of course concerned here with the cases—usually occurring in dependent clauses—in which a 2nd pers. #emph[perf.]; with Wāw copulative is simply co\-ordinate with what precedes, as in #bref("Gen 28:6");, and probably #bref("Num 21:15");, #bref("Deut 33:2");.)

(#emph[a];) The influence of the Aramaic construction of the perfect with #he[וְ]; as the narrative tense, instead of the Hebrew imperfect consecutive (cf. Kautzsch, #emph[Gramm. des Bibl.\-Aram.];, #xref(<p71b>)[§ 71#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];), is certainly to be traced in #emph[Qoheleth];, and sporadically in other very late books,#footnote[In the whole of #emph[Qoheleth]; the imperfect consecutive occurs only in #bref("1:17"); and #bref("4:1, 7");. Several of the perfects with #he[וְ]; can no doubt be explained as frequentatives, e.g. #bref("1:13");, #bref("2:5, 9, 11, 13, 15");, 5:18, compared with #bref("6:2");; but this is impossible in such passages as #bref("9:14"); ff. In Ezra, Driver reckons only six examples of the historical perfect with #he[וְ];, in Nehemiah only six, and in Esther six or seven.]; perhaps also in a few passages in the books of Kings, which are open to the suspicion of being due to later interpolation; so probably #bref("1 Kgs 12:32"); #he[וְהֶֽעֱמִיד];; #bref("2 Kgs 11:1"); #emph[K#super[e];th.]; #he[וראתה];; 14:14 #he[וְלָקַח]; (in the parallel passage, #bref("2 Chr 25:24");, the word is wanting); #bref("2 Kgs 23:4"); #he[וְנָשָׂא];, etc.; verse 10 #he[וְטִמֵּא];, etc.; verse 12 #he[וְהִשְׁלִיךְ];, etc.; verse 15 #he[וְשָׂרַף];, etc.#footnote[Stade in #emph[ZAW.]; v. 291 ff. and in #emph[Ausgewählte akad. Reden];, Giessen, 1899, p. 194 ff. and appendix p. 199, discusses, in connection with #bref("2 Kgs 12:12");, a number of critically questionable perfects with #he[וְ];. He considers that the whole section, #bref("2 Kgs 23:4"); from #he[וְנָשָׂא]; to verse 5 inclusive, is to be regarded as a gloss, since the continuation of an imperfect consecutive by means of a perfect with #he[וְ]; never occurs in pre\-exilic documents, except in places where it is due to corruption of the original text. The theory of frequentative perfects consecutive (even immediately after imperfects consecutive), which has been supported above, under #emph[f]; and #emph[g];, by a large number of examples, is quite inconsistent with the character of the action in #bref("2 Kgs 23:5"); #he[וְהִשְׁבִּית];, verse 8 #he[וְנָתָץ];, and verse 14 #he[וְשִׁבַּר];.]; Cf. also #bref("Ezek 37:2");, 7, 10.

#ml("qq") <p112qq> (#emph[b];) The text is certainly corrupt in #bref("Isa 40:6"); (read with the LXX and Vulgate #origpage("339") <orig-339>;#he[וָאֹֽמַר];); #bref("Jer 38:28");, where the narrative breaks off in the middle of the sentence; 40:3 (#he[וְהָיָה];, etc., wanting in the LXX); also in #bref("Judg 7:13"); #he[וְנָפַל הָאֹהֶל]; is altogether redundant; in #bref("1 Sam 3:13"); read, with Klostermann, the #emph[2nd sing. masc.]; instead of #he[והגדתי];; in #bref("1 Kgs 21:12"); #he[וְהֹשִׁ֫יבוּ]; is, no doubt, incorrectly repeated from verse 9, where it is an imperative.

#ml("rr") <p112rr> Of other questionable instances, (α) the following, at any rate, may also be explained as frequentatives, #bref("Gen 21:25");, #bref("49:23");, #bref("Exod 36:38");, #bref("38:28");, #bref("39:3");, #bref("1 Sam 5:7");, #bref("17:20");, #bref("24:11"); (but even so #he[וְאָֽמְרוּ]; would be expected); #bref("2 Kgs 23:12");, #bref("Isa 28:26"); (parallel with an imperfect); #bref("Amos 5:26"); (unless it is rather, #emph[yea, ye shall take up];; see above, #emph[x];); #bref("Ps 26:3");, #bref("Ezek 8:36");.

#ml("ss") <p112ss> (β) A longer or constant continuance in a past state is perhaps represented by the perfect with #he[וְ]; (as a variety of the frequentative perfect with #he[וְ];), in #bref("Gen 15:6");, #bref("34:5");, #bref("Num 21:20");, #bref("Josh 9:12");, #bref("22:3");b, #bref("Isa 22:14");, #bref("Jer 3:9");. But the unusual #emph[perfects consec.]; in #bref("Josh 15:3");–11, 16:2–8 (ultimately parallel with an imperf. as in 17:9, 18:20), 18:12–21, 19:11–14.22.26–29.34, are without doubt rightly explained by Bennett (#emph[SBOT.];, Joshua, p. 23) as originally containing the directions either of God to Joshua or of Joshua to the people; cf. the evident trace of this in 15:4b. A redactor transformed the directions into a description but left the #emph[perfects consec.];, which are to be explained as in #emph[aa];. In the same way #he[וְהָיוּ]; #bref("Exod 36:29"); is most simply explained as repeated from 26:25.

#ml("tt") <p112tt> (γ) The following are due to errors in the text, or to incorrect modes of expression: #bref("Exod 36:29"); f., #bref("Judg 3:23");,#footnote[Or does #he[ונעל];, as a frequentative, imply fastening with several bolts? It is, at all events, to be noticed, that in #bref("2 Sam 13:18"); also #he[ונעל]; follows an imperfect consecutive.]; 16:18 (read #he[וַיַּֽעֲלוּ];), #bref("1 Sam 4:19");, #bref("17:38");, #bref("2 Sam 16:5");, #bref("19:18"); f. (read #he[צָֽלְחוּ]; and #he[וַיַּֽעַבְרוּ];), #bref("1 Kgs 3:11"); (where #he[ושאלת]; is, no doubt intentionally, assimilated to the four other perfects); 13:3, 20:21; #bref("2 Kgs 14:7"); (where, with Stade, #he[וְאֶת־הַסֶּ֫לַע תּפַשׁ]; should be read); 14:14, 18:4 (where, at any rate, #he[וְשִׁבַּר]; might be taken as a frequentative, but not #he[וכרת];, etc.; evidently the perfects are co\-ordinated only in form with #he[הוּא הֵסִיר];); 18:36, 21:15, 24:14, #bref("Jer 37:15"); (where #he[וְהִכּוּ];, but not #he[וְנָֽתְנוּ];, might be frequentative); #bref("Ezek 9:7"); (omit #he[וְיָֽצְאוּ]; with Stade, and read #he[וְהַכּוּ];); 20:22 (#he[והשבתי]; #emph[Milʿêl]; before an imperfect consecutive); #bref("Amos 7:4"); (#he[וְאָכְלָה]; after an imperfect consecutive); #bref("Job 16:12");.

#ml("uu") <p112uu> Finally, in #bref("1 Sam 1:12");, #bref("10:9");, #bref("17:48");, #bref("25:20");, #bref("2 Sam 6:16");, #bref("2 Kgs 3:15");, #bref("Jer 37:11");, #bref("Amos 7:2"); #he[וַיְהִי]; is to be read throughout instead of #he[וְהָיָה];, but in #bref("Gen 38:5"); #he[וְהִיא]; with the LXX.

#bookhead(3, [B. The Infinitive and Participle], note: []);

#sec("113", [The Infinitive Absolute], note: []) <s113>

Cf. the dissertation of J. Kahan, and, especially, the thorough investigation by E. Sellin, both entitled, #emph[Ueber die verbal\-nominale Doppelnatur der hebräischen Participien und Infinitive];, etc., Lpz. 1889; F. Prätorius, ‘Ueber die sogen. Infin. absol. des Hebr.’ in #emph[ZDMG.]; 1902, pp. 546 ff.

#ml("a") <p113a> #strong[1.]; The infinitive absolute is employed according to § 45 to emphasize the idea of the verb #emph[in the abstract];, i.e. it speaks of an action (or state) without any regard to the agent or to the circumstances of time and mood under which it takes place. As the #emph[name]; of an action the infinitive absolute, like other nouns in the stricter sense, #origpage("340") <orig-340>;may form part of certain combinations (as a subject, predicate, or object, or even as a genitive,#footnote[The infinitive absolute can never be joined with a genitive or a pronominal suffix.]; see below); but such a use of the infinitive absolute (instead of the infinitive construct with or without a preposition) is, on the whole, rare, and, moreover, open to question on critical grounds. On the other hand, the infinitive absolute frequently exhibits its character as an expression of the #emph[verbal idea]; by taking an object, either in the accusative or even with a preposition.

#ml("b") <p113b> Examples of the use of the infinitive absolute:—

(#emph[a];) As subject, #bref("Prov 25:27"); #he[אָכֹל דְּבַשׁ הַרְבּוֹת לֹא טוֹב]; #emph[it is not good to eat much honey];; #bref("Jer 10:5");, #bref("Job 6:25");, #bref("Eccl 4:17");; epexegetically, after a demonstrative pronoun, #bref("Isa 58:5"); f., #bref("Zech 14:12");.

#ml("c") <p113c> (#emph[b];) As predicate, #bref("Isa 32:17"); #emph[and the effect of righteousness]; (is) #he[הַשְׁקֵט וָבֶ֫טַח]; #emph[quietness]; (prop. to #emph[find rest];) #emph[and confidence];.

#ml("d") <p113d> (#emph[c];) As object, #bref("Isa 1:17"); #he[לִמְדוּ הֵיטֵב]; #emph[learn to do well];; #bref("Isa 7:15");, #bref("Prov 15:12");, #bref("Job 9:18");; according to the sense also #bref("Jer 9:23"); 23:14, as well as #bref("Isa 5:5"); (#he[הָסֵר]; and #he[פָּרֹץ]; virtually depend on the idea of the wish contained in #he[עֹשֶׂה];); #bref("Isa 22:13");, where a long series of infinitives absolute is governed by #he[הִנֵּה];, and 59:13 (six infinitives governed by #he[יְדַֽעֲנוּם]; in verse 12); #bref("Deut 28:56"); is strange since the object precedes the infinitive absolute which governs it,#footnote[Perhaps #he[הַצֵּג]; according to #xref(<p53k>)[§ 53#h(0.18em)#emph[k];]; should be explained as an infinitive construct, or should be written #he[הַצִּג];.]; also #bref("Isa 42:24");, where the statement of place precedes the infinitive absolute.—In #bref("Jer 9:4");, #bref("Job 13:3"); the infinitive absolute as the object of the verb is placed before it for the sake of emphasis (with the verb negatived by #he[לֹא]; in #bref("Isa 57:20");, #bref("Jer 49:23");), so also in #bref("Lam 3:45"); where it is the remoter object and co\-ordinated with a substantive.

#ml("e") <p113e> (#emph[d];) As genitive, #bref("Isa 14:23"); #he[בְּמַטְאֲטֵא הַשְׁמֵד]; #emph[with the besom of destruction];; so perhaps also 4:4 #he[בְּרוּחַ בָּעֵר];; cf. further, #bref("Prov 1:3");, #bref("21:16");. The infinitive absolute is never used in immediate connection with prepositions#footnote[#he[ואחרי שָׁתֹה]; #bref("1 Sam 1:9"); is impossible Hebrew, and as the LXX shows, a late addition.]; (which as being originally substantives govern the genitive), but always the infinitive construct; but if a second infinitive is co\-ordinated by #he[וְ]; with such an infinitive construct, it has the form of the infinitive absolute (since it is released from the immediate government of the preposition), e.g. #bref("1 Sam 22:13"); ... #he[בְּתִתְּךָ לוֹ לֶחֶם וְשָׁאוֹל לוֹ בֵּֽאלֹהִים]; #emph[in that thou hast given him bread ... and hast enquired of God for him];; #bref("Ezek 36:3");; #bref("1 Sam 25:26");, 33 (after #he[מִן];); after #he[לְ]; #bref("Exod 32:6");, #bref("Jer 7:18");, #bref("44:17");.

#ml("f") <p113f> (#emph[e];) Governing an accusative of the object, e.g. #bref("Isa 22:13"); #he[הָרֹג בָּקָר וְשָׁחֹם צאֹן]; #emph[slaying oxen and killing sheep];; cf. #bref("Exod 20:8");, #bref("23:30");, #bref("Deut 5:12");, #bref("Isa 37:19");, #bref("Ezek 23:30");, and of the examples in #emph[a–d];, #bref("Deut 28:56");, #bref("Isa 5:5");, #bref("58:6"); f., #bref("Prov 25:27");, etc.; followed by a preposition, e.g. #bref("Isa 7:15"); #he[מָאוֹס בָּרָע וּבָחוֹר בַּטּוֹב]; #emph[to refuse the evil and choose the good];; #bref("Prov 15:12"); (#he[הוֹכֵחַ לוֹ];).

#ml("g") <p113g> If the object be a personal pronoun, then, since the infinitive absolute can never be united with a suffix (see the note on #emph[a];), it is affixed by means of the accusative\-sign #he[אֵת]; (#he[אֹת];), e.g. #bref("Jer 9:23"); #he[וְיָדוֹעַ אֹתִי]; #emph[and knoweth me];; #bref("Ezek 36:3");. #origpage("341") <orig-341>;

#ml("h") <p113h> #strong[2.]; Analogous to the use of the infinitive absolute as the accusative of the object, mentioned in #emph[d];, is its employment as a #emph[casus adverbialis];#footnote[That this #emph[casus adverbialis]; also was originally regarded as an accusative, may be seen from classical Arabic, where an infinitive of this kind expressly retains the accusative ending. In Latin the ablative of the gerund corresponds in many ways to this use of the infinitive absolute.]; in connection with some form of the finite verb, to describe more particularly the manner or attendant circumstances (especially those of time and place) under which an action or state has taken place, or is taking place, or will take place; e.g. #bref("Jer 22:19"); #emph[he shall be buried with the burial of an ass];, #he[סָחוֹב וְהַשְׁלֵךְ]; #emph[a drawing and casting forth];, i.e. being drawn and cast forth, etc.; #bref("Gen 21:16"); (#he[הַרְחֵק]; #emph[a removing];, i.e. distant; cf. #bref("Exod 33:7");, #bref("Josh 3:16");); #bref("Gen 30:32");, #bref("Exod 30:36");, #bref("Num 6:5");, 23, 15:35 (where a subject is added subsequently; see below, #emph[gg];); #bref("Josh 3:17");, #bref("1 Sam 3:12"); (#he[הָחֵל וְכַלֵּה]; #emph[a beginning and ending];, i.e. from beginning to end); #bref("2 Sam 8:2");, #bref("Isa 7:11"); (#he[הַֽעֲמֵק]; and #he[הַגְבֵּהַּ];, prop. #emph[a making deep]; ..., and #emph[a making high];, i.e. whether thy request extend to the world below or to the height above); 57:17 (#he[הַסְתֵּר]; #emph[in hiding];, sc. my face); #bref("Jer 3:15"); (#he[דֵּעָה וְהַשְׁכֵּיל]; #emph[with knowledge and understanding];); #bref("Hab 3:13"); (#he[עָרוֹת];, for the form cf. #xref(<p75aa>)[§ 75#h(0.18em)#emph[aa];];); #bref("Zech 7:3");, #bref("Ps 35:16"); (#he[חָרֹק];, to define more precisely #he[קָֽרְעוּ]; verse 15); #bref("Job 15:3");.#footnote[Also in #bref("2 Kgs 21:13"); for #he[מָחָה וְהָפַךְ]; read with Stade and Klostermann #he[מָחֹה וְהָפֹךְ];; similarly, with Stade, #he[וְקָשֹׁה]; in #bref("Judg 4:24");; #he[וְחָזוֹק]; in #bref("Jer 23:14");, and on #bref("Isa 31:5"); cf. #emph[t];.];

#ml("i") <p113i> Rem. 1. To an adverbial infinitive absolute of this kind, there may further be added a #emph[casus adverbialis]; (the accusative of state or circumstance), or even a circumstantial clause, to define more exactly the manner in which the action is performed, e.g. #bref("Isa 20:2"); #emph[and he did so]; #he[הָלֹךְ עָרוֹם וְיָחֵף]; #emph[walking naked and barefoot];, prop. in the condition of one naked, etc.; #bref("Isa 30:14"); #emph[a breaking in pieces]; (acc. to the reading #he[כָּתוֹת];; the Masora requires #he[כָּתוּת];) #emph[without sparing];.

#ml("k") <p113k> 2. A few infinitives of this kind, all of which are in Hiphʿîl, have, through frequent use, come to be treated by the language as simple adverbs; so especially #he[הַרְבֵּה]; (cf. #xref(<p75ff>)[§ 75#h(0.18em)#emph[ff];];) #emph[multum faciendo];, i.e. #emph[multum];, very frequently strengthened by #he[מְאֹד]; #emph[very]; and even used without connection with a finite verb (see the Lexicon); also #he[הֵיטֵב]; #emph[bene faciendo];, i.e. #emph[bene];, used especially to express the careful and thorough performance of an action (e.g. #bref("Deut 13:15");); in #bref("Deut 9:21");, #bref("27:8"); it is added epexegetically to another adverbial infinitive absolute, in #bref("Jonah 4:9"); it twice precedes the verb for the sake of emphasis. Finally, #he[הַשְׁכֵּם]; #emph[mane faciendo];, i.e. #emph[early in the morning];, then in general #emph[early]; with the additional idea of earnestness; in #bref("1 Sam 17:16"); joined with the infinitive absolute #he[וְהַֽעֲרֵב]; a denominative from #he[עֶ֫רֶב]; #emph[evening]; (#emph[morning and evening];, i.e. #emph[early and late];), elsewhere (with the exception of #bref("Prov 27:14");) always joined with the infinitive absolute of the governing verb, e.g. #bref("Jer 11:7"); #emph[for I earnestly protested]; (#he[הַֽעִדֹ֫תִי];) #emph[unto your fathers]; ... #he[הַשְׁכֵּם וְהָעֵד]; #emph[rising early and protesting];, i.e. with earnest protestation; #bref("Jer 25:3");, #bref("26:5"); (where #he[וְ]; should be omitted before #he[ה׳];); #bref("Jer 29:19");, #bref("32:33");, #bref("2 Chr 16:15");. #origpage("342") <orig-342>;

#ml("l") <p113l> #strong[3.]; The infinitive absolute occurs most frequently in immediate connection with the finite verb of the same stem, in order in various ways #emph[to define more accurately]; or #emph[to strengthen the idea of the verb];.#footnote[Cf. A. Rieder, #emph[Die Verbindung des Inf. abs. mit dem Verb. fin ... im Hebr.];, Lpz., 1872; also his #emph[Quae ad syntaxin Hebraicam ... planiorem faciendam ex lingua Graeca et Latina afferantur];, Gumbinnen (Programm des Gymnasiums), 1884. G. R. Hauschild, #emph[Die Verbindung finiter und infiniter Verbalformen desselben Stammes in einigen Bibelsprachen];, Frankfurt a. M., 1893, discussing especially the rendering of such constructions in the Greek and Latin versions.];

#ml("m") <p113m> These infinitives absolute joined immediately to the finite verb belong in a sense to the #emph[schema etymologicum]; treated in #xref(<p117p>)[§ 117#h(0.18em)#emph[p];];, i.e. they are objects of the finite verb in question, except that the infinitive absolute (as a #emph[nomen abstractum];) lays stress rather on the actual occurrence or the energy of the action (see the examples below), while the noun proper emphasizes the result or extent of the action; cf. e.g. #bref("Exod 22:22"); #he[אִם־צָעֹק יִצְעַק אֵלַי]; #emph[if]; it actually happens that #emph[he cries to me];, with #bref("Gen 27:34"); (as it were, #emph[he cried];, so that #emph[a great cry]; was heard).

We must further distinguish—

#ml("n") <p113n> (#emph[a];) The infinitive absolute used #emph[before]; the verb to #emph[strengthen]; the verbal idea, i.e. to emphasize in this way either the certainty (especially in the case of threats) or the forcibleness and completeness of an occurrence. In English, such an infinitive is mostly expressed by a corresponding adverb, but sometimes merely by putting greater stress on the verb; e.g. #bref("Gen 2:17"); #he[מוֹת תָּמוּת]; #emph[thou shalt surely die];, cf. 18:10, 18, 22:17, 28:22, #bref("1 Sam 9:6"); (#emph[cometh surely to pass];); 24:21, #bref("Amos 5:5");, #bref("7:17");, #bref("Hab 2:3");, #bref("Zech 11:17");; with the infinitive strengthened by #he[אַךְ]; #bref("Gen 44:28"); (but 27:30 #emph[and Jacob was yet scarce gone out];, etc.); #bref("Gen 43:3"); #he[הָעֵד הֵעִד בָּ֫נוּ]; #emph[he did solemnly protest unto us];; #bref("1 Sam 20:6"); #he[נִשְׁאֹל נִשְׁאַל]; #emph[David earnestly asked leave of me];; #bref("Josh 17:13");, #bref("Judg 1:28"); #he[וְהוֹרֵישׁ לֹא הֽוֹרִישׁוֹ]; #emph[and did not utterly drive them out];; especially typical instances are #bref("Amos 9:8"); #emph[I will destroy it from off the face of the earth]; #he[אֶ֫פֶס כִּי לֹא הַשְׁמֵיד אַשְׁמִיד וג׳]; #emph[saving that I will not utterly destroy];, etc.; #bref("Jer 30:11"); #emph[and will in no wise leave thee unpunished];; cf. further #bref("Gen 20:18");, #bref("1 Kgs 3:26");, #bref("Joel 1:7");, #bref("Job 13:5");.

#ml("o") <p113o> The infinitive absolute is used before the verb with less emphasis:

(1) Frequently at the beginning of the statement; cf. Driver on #bref("1 Sam 20:6");. However, in these cases a special emphasis on the following verb is sometimes intended; cf. above, #emph[n];, on #bref("Gen 43:3");, #bref("1 Sam 20:6");; also #bref("Gen 3:16");, #bref("26:28");, #bref("32:17");, #bref("1 Sam 14:28");, #bref("20:3");. Elsewhere the infinitive absolute is evidently used only as possessing a certain fullness of sound (hence for rhythmical reasons, like some uses of the separate pronoun, #xref(<p135a>)[§ 135#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];), as in #bref("Gen 15:13");, #bref("43:7");, 20, #bref("Judg 9:8");, #bref("1 Sam 10:16");, #bref("23:10");, #bref("2 Sam 1:6");, #bref("20:18");.

(2) Very frequently in conditional sentences after #he[אִם];, etc. The infinitive absolute in this case emphasizes the importance of the con\- #origpage("343") <orig-343>;dition on which some consequence depends, e.g. #bref("Exod 15:26"); #emph[if thou wilt diligently hearken];, etc., #bref("Exod 19:5");, #bref("21:5");, #bref("22:3");, 11 f. 16, 22 (see above, #emph[m];); 23:22, #bref("Num 21:2");, #bref("Judg 16:11");, #bref("1 Sam 1:11");, #bref("12:25");; after #he[לוּ]; #bref("1 Sam 14:30");.

#ml("p") <p113p> The infinitive absolute is used to give emphasis to an antithesis, e.g. #bref("2 Sam 24:24"); #emph[nay; but I will verily buy]; (#he[קָנוֹ אֶקְנֶה];) #emph[it of thee];, etc. (not receive it as a gift); #bref("Judg 15:13"); #emph[no; but we will bind thee fast ... but surely we will not kill thee];; cf. further #bref("Gen 31:30"); (thou art indeed gone\=) #emph[though thou wouldst needs be gone]; (Vulg. #emph[esto];), #emph[because thou sore longedst];, etc.; #bref("Ps 118:13");, 18, 126:6 (the second infinitive absolute as a supplement to the first—see below, #emph[r];—comes #emph[after]; the verb).—Hence also, as permissive, #bref("Gen 2:16"); f. #he[אָכֹל תֹּאכֵל]; #emph[thou mayest freely eat, but];, etc. (so that verse 16 is in antithesis to verse 17); or concessive, #bref("1 Sam 2:30"); #emph[I said indeed]; ..., 14:43.

#ml("q") <p113q> The infinitive absolute is used to strengthen a question, and especially in impassioned or indignant questions, e.g. #bref("Gen 37:8"); #he[הֲמָלֹךְ תִּמְלֹךְ עָ֫לֵינוּ]; #emph[shalt thou indeed reign over us?]; #bref("Gen 37:10");, #bref("43:7");, #bref("Judg 11:25");, #bref("1 Sam 2:27");, #bref("2 Sam 19:43");, #bref("Jer 3:1");, #bref("13:12");, #bref("Ezek 28:9");, #bref("Amos 3:5");, #bref("Zech 7:5");; but cf. also #bref("Gen 24:5"); #emph[must I needs bring again?];

#ml("r") <p113r> (#emph[b];) The infinitive absolute #emph[after the verb];, sometimes (as in #emph[n];) to intensify#footnote[In Arabic also, the intensifying infinitive regularly stands #emph[after]; the verb, but in Syriac #emph[before]; the verb.]; the idea of the verb (especially after imperatives and participles, since the infinitive absolute can never precede either, e.g. #bref("Num 11:15");, #bref("Job 13:17");, #bref("21:2");, #bref("37:2"); #he[שִׁמְעוּ שָׁמוֹעַ]; #emph[hearken ye attentively];; #bref("Jer 22:10");; after participles, e.g. #bref("Isa 22:17");, also elsewhere, e.g. #bref("Num 23:11");, #bref("24:10"); #emph[thou hast altogether blessed them];; #bref("Josh 24:10");, #bref("2 Kgs 5:11");, #bref("Dan 11:10");, and with the infinitive absolute strengthened by means of #he[גַּם]; #bref("Gen 31:15");, #bref("46:4");, #bref("Num 16:13");); sometimes to express the long #emph[continuance]; of an action; here again after an imperative, #bref("Isa 6:9"); #he[שִׁמְעוּ שָׁמוֹעַ]; #emph[hear ye continually];; after a perfect, #bref("Jer 6:29");; after a participle, #bref("Jer 23:17");; after an imperfect consecutive, #bref("Gen 19:9");, #bref("Num 11:32");.

#ml("s") <p113s> To this class belong especially those cases in which a second infinitive absolute is co\-ordinated with the first; the latter then expresses either an accompanying or antithetical action or the aim to which the principal action is directed; e.g. #bref("1 Sam 6:12"); #he[הָֽלְכוּ הָלֹךְ וְגָעוֹ]; #emph[lowing as they went]; (lowing continually; so after a participle, #bref("Josh 6:13");b #emph[Q#super[e];rê];); #bref("Gen 8:7"); #emph[it went forth to and fro];#footnote[Also in #bref("Ezek 1:14"); for the distorted form #he[רצוא]; reads simply #he[יָֽצְאוּ יָצוֹא];.];; #bref("Isa 19:22"); #emph[smiting and]; (i.e. but also) #emph[healing again];; #bref("Joel 2:26"); (see above, #emph[m];).

#ml("t") <p113t> Rem. 1. Instead of a second infinitive absolute (see above) there is sometimes found a perfect consecutive (Jos. 6:13 a and #bref("2 Sam 13:19"); \[but Stade’s #he[וְזָעוֹק]; is #origpage("344") <orig-344>;preferable\], in both places as perfect frequentative; #bref("Isa 31:5"); referring to the future, unless with Stade, #emph[ZAW.]; vi. 189, we read #he[וְהַצֵּיל]; and #he[וְהַמְלֵיט];), or an imperfect consecutive (#bref("1 Sam 19:23");, #bref("2 Sam 16:13");) or participle (#bref("2 Sam 16:5");); cf. also #emph[u];.

#ml("u") <p113u> 2. The idea of long #emph[continuance]; is very frequently expressed by the verb #he[הָלַךְ]; #emph[to go];, along with its infinitive absolute, or even by the latter alone, and this occurs not only when it can be taken in its literal sense (#emph[to go, to walk];, as in the examples given above, #bref("Josh 6:9");, 13, #bref("1 Sam 6:12");, #bref("2 Sam 3:16");, #bref("13:19");; cf. also, #bref("Isa 3:16");, where both infinitives stand #emph[before]; the verb, and #bref("Ps 126:6");, where #he[הָלוֹךְ]; precedes), but in cases where #he[הָלַךְ]; in the sense of #emph[to go on, to continue];, merely performs also the function of an adverb. The action itself is added in a second infinitive absolute, or sometimes (see above, #emph[t];) in a participle or verbal adjective. Examples, #bref("Gen 8:3"); #he[וַיָּשֻׁ֫בוּ הַמַּ֫יִם... הָלוֹךְ וָשׁוֹב]; #emph[and the waters returned... continually];; #bref("Gen 8:5");, #bref("12:9");, #bref("Judg 14:9");, #bref("2 Kgs 2:11");; with a participle following, #bref("Jer 4:16"); (unless we read #he[וּבָכֹה];, as in #bref("2 Sam 3:16");); with an adjective following, #bref("Gen 26:13");, #bref("Judg 4:24");, #bref("1 Sam 14:19");, #bref("2 Sam 5:10"); (#bref("1 Chr 11:9");), #bref("2 Sam 18:25");.#footnote[Cf. in French, #emph[Le mal va toujours croissant, la maladie va toujours en augmentant et en empirant];, ‘#emph[continually increases and becomes worse and worse];.’];

On the other hand, in #bref("1 Sam 17:41"); the participle #he[הֹלֵךְ]; is used instead of the infinitive absolute. Of a different kind are the instances in which the participle #he[הֹלֵךְ]; is used as predicate along with the co\-ordinate adjective (#bref("Exod 19:19");, #bref("1 Sam 2:26");, #bref("2 Sam 3:1");, #bref("15:12");, #bref("Esth 9:4");, #bref("2 Chr 17:12");) or participle (#bref("1 Sam 17:15");, #bref("Jonah 1:11");, #bref("Prov 4:18");, #bref("Eccl 1:6");).

#ml("v") <p113v> 3. The regular place of the negative is between the intensifying infinitive absolute and the finite verb,#footnote[Cf. Rieder, #emph[Quo loco ponantur negationes]; #he[לֹא]; #emph[et]; #he[אַל];... (#emph[Zeitschrift für Gymn.\-Wesen];, 1879, p. 395 ff.).]; e.g. #bref("Exod 5:23"); #he[וְהַצֵּל לֹֽא־הִצַּלְתָּ]; #emph[neither hast thou delivered at all];, #bref("Judg 15:13");, #bref("Jer 13:12");, #bref("30:11");; cf. #bref("Mic 1:10"); (#he[אַל];). Exceptions are #bref("Gen 3:4"); (where the negation of the threat pronounced in 2:17 is expressed in the same form of words); #bref("Amos 9:8");, #bref("Ps 49:8");.

#ml("w") <p113w> 4. With a finite verb of one of the derived conjugations, not only the infinitive absolute of the #emph[same]; conjugation may be connected (#bref("Gen 28:22"); Piʿēl; 17:13, #bref("Exod 22:3");, #bref("Ezek 14:3"); Niphʿal; #bref("Gen 40:15"); Puʿal; #bref("Hos 4:18"); Hiphʿîl; #bref("Ezek 16:4"); Hophʿal), but also (especially with Niphʿal, rarely with Piʿēl and Hiphʿîl; see Driver on #bref("2 Sam 20:18");) that of #emph[Qal]; as the simplest and most general representative of the verbal idea, #bref("2 Sam 20:18"); (with Piʿēl; but in #bref("Gen 37:33");, #bref("44:28"); #he[טֹרַף]; is a passive of Qal, #xref(<p52e>)[§ 52#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];); 46:4 (with Hiphʿîl); #bref("Exod 19:13");, #bref("21:20");, #bref("2 Sam 23:7");, #bref("Isa 40:30");, #bref("Jer 10:5");, #bref("Job 6:2"); (with Niphʿal); #bref("Isa 24:19"); (with Hithpoʿēl; #he[רֹ֫עָה]; in the same verse must also, according to the Masora, certainly be the infinitive absolute Qal; see #xref(<p67o>)[§ 67#h(0.18em)#emph[o];];), and so always #he[מוֹת יוּמַת]; #emph[he shall surely be put to death];. Elsewhere the infinitive absolute of a conjugation with kindred meaning is found, #bref("Lev 19:20");, #bref("2 Kgs 3:23"); Hophʿal for Niphʿal (but most probably we should read, with Driver, the #emph[infin. Niph.]; in both places, #he[הִפָּדֵה]; and #he[הֵֽחָרֵב];); #bref("1 Sam 2:16"); (Piʿēl for Hiphʿîl, unless #he[יְקַטְּרוּן]; is to be read); #bref("Ezek 16:4"); (Hophʿal for Puʿal).#footnote[In three passages even the infinitive absolute of another stem of like sound occurs; but in #bref("Isa 28:28"); #he[אָדוֹשׁ]; is no doubt a mere textual error for #he[דּוֹשׁ];, and in #bref("Jer 8:13");, according to #xref(<p72aa>)[§ 72#h(0.18em)#emph[aa];];, we should read #he[אֹֽסְפֵם];, and in #bref("Zeph 1:2"); #he[אֹסֵף];. Barth, #emph[Nom.\-bildung];, § 49 #emph[b];, sees in #he[אָדוֹשׁ]; and #he[אָסוֹף]; infinitives #emph[Hiphʿîl];, exactly corresponding in form to #emph[ʾ#super[a];qâm];\[ā\] the Aram. infin. ʾAphʿēl of #he[קוּם];; but there is no more evidence for a #emph[Hiph.]; of #he[דּוּשׁ]; in Hebrew than for a stem #he[אָדַשׁ];.]; Finally, the infinitive absolute may #origpage("345") <orig-345>;equally well be represented by a #emph[substantive]; of kindred stem.#footnote[On these substantives (and on the use of the infinitive absolute generally as absolute object, see above, #emph[m];), cf. the #emph[schema etymologicum]; treated in connection with the government of the verb in #xref(<p117p>)[§ 117#h(0.18em)#emph[p];];, #emph[q];.]; In #bref("Isa 29:14"); the substantive intensifying the verb is found #emph[along with]; the infinitive absolute.

#ml("x") <p113x> 5. Instead of the infinitive absolute immediately connected with the finite verb, an infinitive construct form appears (cf. #xref(<p73d>)[§ 73#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];), in #bref("Num 23:25"); (#he[גַּם קֹב];; cf. #bref("Ruth 2:16"); #he[גַּם שֹׁל];); #bref("Jer 50:34"); (#he[רִיב יָרִיב];); #bref("Prov 23:1"); (#he[בִּין תָּבִין];). In the last instances the infinitive is probably assimilated to the imperfect, like the infinitive Niphʿal in the forms noticed in #xref(<p51k>)[§ 51#h(0.18em)#emph[k];]; and note.—Cf. also #bref("2 Kgs 3:24"); #he[וַיָּבֹ֫אוּ בֹא וְהַכּוֹת]; (read so with the LXX) before #he[א];, hence, no doubt due to the dislike of a hiatus; so in #bref("Ps 50:21");, #bref("Neh 1:7"); (#he[חֲבֹל];), all in rapid style; #emph[after]; the verb, #bref("Josh 7:7");, unless #he[הַֽעֲבֵיר]; is intended.

#ml("y") <p113y> #strong[4.]; Finally the infinitive absolute sometimes appears as #emph[a substitute for the finite verb];, either when it is sufficient simply to mention the verbal idea (see #emph[z];), or when the hurried or otherwise excited style intentionally contents itself with this infinitive, in order to bring out the verbal idea in a clearer and more expressive manner (see #emph[aa];).

#ml("z") <p113z> (#emph[a];) The infinitive absolute as the continuation of a preceding finite verb. In the later books especially it often happens that in a succession of several acts only the first (or sometimes more) of the verbs is inflected, while the second (or third, etc.) is added simply in the infinitive absolute. Thus after several perfects, #bref("Dan 9:5"); (cf. verse 11) #emph[we have sinned ... and have transgressed thy law];, #he[וִסוֹר]; #emph[and have turned aside]; (prop. #emph[a turning aside]; took place); so after a #emph[perfect]; #bref("Exod 36:7"); (?), #bref("1 Sam 2:28");, #bref("Isa 37:19");, #bref("Jer 14:5");, #bref("19:13");, #bref("Hag 1:6"); (four infinitives), #bref("Zech 3:4"); (but read with Wellhausen, after the LXX, #he[וְהַלְבִּ֫שׁוּ אֹתוֹ];), 7:5, #bref("Eccl 8:9");, #bref("9:11");, #bref("Esth 3:13");, #bref("9:6");, 16, 18, 12:6 ff., #bref("Neh 9:8");, 13, #bref("1 Chr 5:20");, #bref("2 Chr 28:19");;#footnote[In #bref("Ezek 7:14"); a perfect appears to be continued by means of an infinitive construct; but the text is quite corrupt; Cornill reads #he[תִּקְעוּ תָקוֹעַ הָכִ֫ינוּ הָכֵן];.]; after the #emph[perfect consecutive];, #bref("Zech 12:10");; after the #emph[perfect frequentative]; #bref("1 Kgs 9:25"); (unless #he[וְהִקְטִיר]; be intended); after the simple #emph[imperfect];, #bref("Lev 25:14");, #bref("Num 30:3");, #bref("Jer 32:44"); (three infinitives), 36:23, #bref("1 Chr 21:24");; after a #emph[cohortative];, #bref("Josh 9:20");; after the #emph[imperfect consecutive];, #bref("Gen 41:43"); (as a continuation of #he[וַיַּרְכֵּב];); #bref("Exod 8:11");, #bref("Judg 7:19");, #bref("Jer 37:21");, #bref("Neh 8:8");, #bref("1 Chr 16:36");, #bref("2 Chr 7:3");; with #he[אוֹ]; #emph[or]; after the #emph[jussive];, #bref("Deut 14:21");, #bref("Esth 2:3");, #bref("6:9");; after the #emph[imperative];, #bref("Isa 37:30");b, #bref("Amos 4:4");f.; after the #emph[participle];, #bref("Hab 2:15"); (strengthened by #he[אַף];, and regarded, like the participle itself, as an adverbial accusative); #bref("Esth 8:8");.

#ml("aa") <p113aa> (#emph[b];) At the beginning of the narrative, or at least of a new section of it. The special form of the finite verb which the infinitive absolute represents must be determined from the context. The infinitive #origpage("346") <orig-346>;absolute is most frequently used in this way, corresponding to the infinitive of command in Greek, etc.#footnote[Cf. also such infinitives in French as #emph[voir]; (page so and so, etc.), #emph[s’adresser..., se méfier des voleurs];!];:—

#ml("bb") <p113bb> (α) For an emphatic imperative,#footnote[Prätorius, #emph[op. cit.];, p. 547: the extraordinarily common use of the infinitive form #emph[qāṭōl]; in the sense of an imperative, jussive, or cohortative has long since caused it to be compared with the Arab. #emph[faʿāli];. It thus appears that the infin. #emph[qāṭōl]; in Hebrew could be used from early times as a kind of fixed, invariable word of command.]; e.g. #he[שָׁמוֹר]; (thou shalt, ye shall), #emph[observe]; #bref("Deut 5:12");; #he[זָכוֹר]; (thou shalt) #emph[remember];, #bref("Exod 13:3");, #bref("20:8"); (the full form occurs in #bref("Deut 6:17"); #he[שָׁמוֹר תִּשְׁמְרוּן];; 7:18 #he[זָכֹר תִּזְכֹּר];); #bref("Lev 2:6");, #bref("Num 4:2");, #bref("25:17");, #bref("Deut 1:16");, #bref("2 Kgs 5:10");, #bref("Isa 38:5");, #bref("Jer 2:2");, followed by a #emph[perfect consecutive];; #bref("Josh 1:13");, #bref("2 Kgs 3:16");, #bref("Isa 7:4");, #bref("14:31"); (parallel with an #emph[imperative];; in #bref("Nah 2:2"); three imperatives follow). But #he[הַבֵּיט]; #bref("Ps 142:5"); may be only an incorrect spelling of #he[הַבֵּט]; imperative.#footnote[In #bref("Ezek 21:31");, for the infinitives construct #he[הָסִיר];, #he[הָרִים];, #he[הַשְׁפִּיל]; (beside #he[הַגְבֵּהַּ];) read with Cornill the infinitives absolute #he[הָסֵר];, etc. The K#super[e];thîbh probably intends #he[הָסֵיר];, etc.];

#ml("cc") <p113cc> (β) For the jussive, #bref("Lev 6:7");, #bref("Num 6:5");, #bref("2 Kgs 11:15");, #bref("Ezek 23:46");; cf. also #bref("Prov 17:12"); (#emph[let it]; rather #emph[meet];).

#ml("dd") <p113dd> (γ) For the cohortative, #bref("Isa 22:13");b #he[אָכוֹל וְשָׁתוֹ]; (the exclamation of the mocker); #bref("Ezek 21:31");, #bref("23:30");, 46; perhaps also #bref("Jer 31:2"); (#he[הָלוֹךְ];).#footnote[In #bref("2 Sam 3:18"); the infinitive construct appears to be used instead of the cohortative, but #he[אוֹשִׁיעַ]; should certainly be read for #he[הוֹשִׁיעַ];. Also in #bref("1 Kgs 22:30"); (#bref("2 Chr 18:29");), which was formerly included under this head (I will #emph[disguise myself and go into the battle];), read #he[אֶתְחַפֵּשׂ וְאָבֹא];.];

#ml("ee") <p113ee> (δ) For the imperfect in emphatic promises, e.g. #bref("2 Kgs 4:43"); ye shall #emph[eat and leave thereof];; 19:29 (#bref("Isa 37:30");), #bref("2 Chr 31:10");; also in indignant questions, #bref("Job 40:2"); #emph[shall he that cavilleth contend with the Almighty?];#footnote[In #bref("Job 34:18"); in a similar question instead of the infinitive constr. we should rather expect the infinitive absolute (#he[הֶאָמֹר];), unless with the LXX and Vulg. the participle with the article (#he[הָֽאֹמֵר];) is to be read.]; (on the addition of the subject cf. the Rem. below); #bref("Jer 3:1"); #emph[and thinkest thou to return again to me?]; #bref("Jer 7:9"); ff. (six infinitives, continued by means of the #emph[perfect consecutive];; cf. #xref(<p112o>)[§ 112#h(0.18em)#emph[o];];).

#ml("ff") <p113ff> (ε) For any historical tense (like the Latin #emph[historic]; infinitive) in lively narration (or enumeration) and description, even of what is still taking place in present time, e.g. #bref("Hos 4:2"); #emph[swearing and breaking faith, and killing, and stealing, and committing adultery]; (in these they are busied); 10:4 (after a #emph[perfect];); #bref("Isa 21:5");, #bref("59:4");, #bref("Jer 8:15");, #bref("14:19");, #bref("Job 15:35");; cf. further #bref("Jer 32:33");, #bref("Eccl 4:2");.—In #bref("Ezek 23:30");, #bref("Prov 12:7");, #bref("15:22");, and 25:4, the infinitive absolute is best rendered by the passive. #origpage("347") <orig-347>;

#ml("gg") <p113gg> Rem. The subject is sometimes added to the infinitive absolute when it takes the place of the finite verb, e.g. #bref("Lev 6:7");, #bref("Num 15:35");, #bref("Deut 15:2");, #bref("Ps 17:5");, #bref("Prov 17:12");, #bref("Job 40:2");, #bref("Eccl 4:2");, #bref("Esth 9:1");. So, probably, also in #bref("Gen 17:10");, #bref("Exod 12:48");, although here #he[כָּל־זָכָר]; according to #xref(<p121a>)[§ 121#h(0.18em)#emph[a];]; might also be taken as #emph[an object]; with a passive verb; cf. #bref("Esth 3:13");. In #bref("1 Sam 25:26");, 33 the subject follows an infinitive absolute which is co\-ordinated with an infinitive construct, see above, #emph[e];.

#sec("114", [The Infinitive Construct], note: []) <s114>

#ml("a") <p114a> #strong[1.]; The infinitive construct, like the infinitive absolute, may also represent a #emph[nomen verbale]; (#xref(<p45a>)[§ 45#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];), but of a much more flexible character than the infinitive absolute (cf. #xref(<p113a>)[§ 113#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];). Its close relation with nouns properly so called is especially seen in the readiness with which the infinitive construct may be used for any case whatever; thus,

(#emph[a];) As the nominative of the subject, e.g. #bref("Gen 2:18"); #he[לֹא־טוֹב הֱיוֹת הָֽאָדָם לְבַדּוֹ];, literally, #emph[not good is the being of man in his separation];; #bref("Gen 30:15");, #bref("1 Sam 23:20");, #bref("Isa 7:13");, #bref("Prov 17:26");, #bref("25:7");, 24 (but cf. 21:9 #he[טוֹב לָשֶׁ֫בֶת]; in the same statement); #bref("Ps 32:9"); prop. #emph[there is not a coming near unto thee];, but the text is probably corrupt. With a #emph[feminine]; predicate, #bref("1 Sam 18:23");, #bref("Jer 2:17");.

#ml("b") <p114b> (#emph[b];) As genitive, e.g. #bref("Eccl 3:4"); #he[עֵת סְפוֹד וְעֵת רְקוֹד]; #emph[a time of mourning and a time of dancing];; #bref("Gen 2:17");, #bref("29:7");, #bref("Neh 12:46");, #bref("2 Chr 24:14");. This equally includes, according to #xref(<p101a>)[§ 101#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];, all those cases in which the infinitive construct depends on a preposition (see below, #emph[d];) \[and Driver, #emph[Tenses];, § 206\].

#ml("c") <p114c> (#emph[c];) As accusative of the object, e.g. #bref("1 Kgs 3:7"); #he[לֹא אֵדַע צֵאת וָבֹא]; #emph[I know not the going out or the coming in]; (#emph[I know not]; how #emph[to go out and come in];); #bref("Gen 21:6");, #bref("31:28");, #bref("Num 20:21");, #bref("Isa 1:14");, #bref("37:28"); (even with #he[אֵת];), #bref("Jer 6:15");, #bref("Job 15:22"); (cf. for the use of the infinitive absolute as object, #xref(<p113f>)[§ 113#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];); as accusative with a verb expressing fullness, #bref("Isa 11:9");.

#ml("d") <p114d> #strong[2.]; The construction of the infinitive with prepositions (as in Greek, ἐν τῷ εἶναι, διὰ τὸ εἶναι, etc.) may usually be resolved in English into the finite verb with a conjunction, e.g. #bref("Num 35:19"); #he[בְּפִגְעוֹ־בוֹ]; #emph[in his meeting him];, i.e. if (as soon as) he meets him; #bref("Gen 27:45"); (#he[עַד־שׁוּב];); #bref("Isa 30:12"); #he[יַ֫עַן מָֽאָסְכֶם]; #emph[because ye despise];; #bref("Jer 2:35"); #he[עַל־אָמְרֵךְ]; #emph[because thou sayest];; #bref("Gen 27:1"); #emph[and his eyes were dim]; #he[מֵרְאֹת]; #emph[from seeing];, i.e. so that he could not see.

#ml("e") <p114e> This use of the infinitive construct is especially frequent in connection with #he[בְּ]; or #he[כְּ‍]; to express time\-determinations (in English resolved into a temporal clause, as above the combination of the infinitive with #he[יַ֫עַן]; or #he[עַל־]; is resolved into a causal clause), especially after #he[וַיְהִי]; (see the #origpage("348") <orig-348>;examples, #xref(<p111g>)[§ 111#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];), e.g. #bref("1 Sam 2:27"); #he[בִּֽהְיוֹתָם בְּמִצְרַ֫יִם]; #emph[when they were in Egypt];; #bref("Gen 24:30"); #he[וַיְהִי כִרְאֹת אֶת־הַנֶּ֫זֶם... וּכְשָׁמְעוֹ וג׳]; #emph[and it came to pass, when he saw]; (prop. in the seeing) #emph[the ring];..., #emph[and when he heard]; (prop. in his hearing), etc.

#ml("f") <p114f> But by far the most frequent is the connection of the infinitive construct with #he[לְ];.#footnote[Cf. #xref(<p45g>)[§ 45#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];, according to which the close union of the #he[לְ]; with the first consonant of the infinitive (#he[לִכְתֹּב]; with a firmly closed syllable, as opposed to #he[בִּכְתֹב];, #he[כִּכְתֹב];, etc.) seems to point to the formation of a special new verbal form. Quite distinct are the few examples where the infinitive with #he[לְ]; serves to express time, as #bref("Gen 24:63"); #he[לִפְנוֹת עָ֫רֶב]; #emph[at the eventide]; (prop. at the time of the return of evening); cf. #bref("Deut 23:12");, #bref("Exod 14:27");, #bref("Judg 19:26");; #bref("2 Sam 18:29"); #emph[when Joab sent the king’s servant];.]; Starting from the fundamental meaning of #he[לְ];, i.e. #emph[direction towards something];, infinitives with #he[לְ]; serve to express the most varied ideas of purpose or aim, and very commonly also (with a weakening or a complete disregard of the original meaning of the #he[לְ];) to introduce the object of an action, or finally even (like the infinitive absolute used adverbially, #xref(<p113h>)[§ 113#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];, and the Latin gerund in #emph[\-ndo];) to state motives or attendant circumstances. See the instances in the Remarks.

#ml("g") <p114g> Rem. 1. The original meaning of the #he[לְ]; is most plainly seen in those infinitives with #he[לְ]; which expressly state a purpose (hence as the equivalent of a final clause), e.g. #bref("Gen 11:5"); #emph[and the Lord came down];, #he[לִרְאֹת אֶת־הָעִיר]; #emph[to see the city];; also with a change of subject, e.g. #bref("2 Sam 12:10"); #emph[and thou hast taken the wife of Uriah the Hittite]; #he[לִֽהְיוֹת לְךָ לְאִשָּׁה]; #emph[to be]; (i.e. that she may be) #emph[thy wife];; cf. #bref("Gen 28:4");, #bref("Jer 38:26"); (#he[לָמוּת];).—If there is a special emphasis on the infinitive with #he[לְ];, it is placed, with its complement, before the governing verb, e.g. #bref("Gen 42:9");, #bref("47:4");, #bref("Num 22:20");, #bref("Josh 2:3");, #bref("1 Sam 16:2"); with #he[בּוֹא];; #bref("Judg 15:10");, #bref("1 Sam 17:25"); with #he[עָלָה];.

#ml("h") <p114h> 2. Just as clearly the idea of aiming at a definite purpose or turning towards an object may be seen in the combination of the verb #he[הָיָה]; #emph[to be];, with #he[לְ]; and an infinitive. In fact #he[הָיָה לַֽעֲשׂוֹת]; may mean, either (#emph[a];) #emph[he was in the act of, he was about to]; (as it were, he set himself), #emph[he was ready, to do]; something, or (#emph[b];) #emph[he]; or it #emph[was appointed]; or #emph[compelled];, etc., to do the action in question. In the latter case #he[הָיָה לַֽעֲשׂוֹת]; corresponds to the Latin #emph[faciendum erat];, cf. also the English #emph[I am to go];. In both cases #he[הָיָה]; (as elsewhere when copula) is often omitted.

#ml("i") <p114i> Examples of (#emph[a];) #bref("Gen 15:12"); #he[וַיְהִי הַשֶּׁ֫מֶשׁ לָבוֹא]; #emph[and when the sun was going down]; (just about to set); #bref("2 Chr 26:5"); #he[וַיְהִי לִדְרשׁ אֱלֹהִים]; #emph[and he set himself to seek God]; (here with the secondary idea of a continuous action); with the omission of #he[הָיָה]; #bref("Isa 38:20");, #he[יְהֹוָה לְהֽוֹשִׁיעֵ֫נִי]; #emph[the Lord]; is ready #emph[to save me];; #bref("1 Sam 14:21"); (?), #bref("Jer 51:49");, #bref("Ps 25:14"); (#emph[et foedus suum manifestaturus est eis];); #bref("Prov 18:24"); (?), 19:8 (#he[לִמְצֹא]; #emph[consecuturus]; #origpage("349") <orig-349>;#emph[est];, unless we simply read #he[יִמְצָא]; with the LXX)#footnote[P. Haupt (#emph[SBOT.];, Proverbs, p. 52, lines 10 ff.; #emph[Critical Notes on Esther];, p. 170, on 7:8) considers it possible that here and in #bref("Prov 2:8");, #bref("6:24");, #bref("7:5");, #bref("16:30");, #bref("30:14");, as well as in 14:35, 17:21 before a noun, the #he[ל]; is a survival of the emphatic #he[ל]; with an #emph[imperf.];, which is especially common in Arabic. In that case #he[לִמְצֹא]; must be read #he[לִמְצָא];, i.e. #he[ל]; \+ #he[יִמְצָא];. But all the above instances can be taken as infinitives with #he[ל]; without difficulty.];; 20:25, #bref("Eccl 3:15"); #he[אֲשֶׁר לִֽהְיוֹת]; #emph[quod futurum est];; #bref("2 Chr 11:22");, #bref("12:12"); (in a negative statement); in a question, #bref("Esth 7:8"); (#emph[will he even];... ?). Cf. also #bref("1 Sam 4:19");.

#ml("k") <p114k> Of (#emph[b];) #bref("Josh 2:5"); #he[וַיְהִי הַשַּׁ֫עַר לִסְגּוֹר]; #emph[and the gate was to be shut]; (had to be shut); #bref("Isa 37:26");, #bref("Ps 109:13");.#footnote[Somewhat different are the cases where #he[הָיָה לְ]; with the infinitive (which is then used exactly as a substantive) implies #emph[to become something];, i.e. to meet with a particular fate, as #bref("Num 24:22"); (cf. #bref("Isa 5:5");, #bref("6:13");) #he[לְבָעֵר]; #emph[for wasting];, for which elsewhere frequently #he[לְשַׁמָּה]; and the like; probably also #he[לְבַלּוֹת]; #bref("Ps 49:15"); is to be explained in this way, the #he[הָיָה]; being omitted.]; Mostly with the omission of #he[הָיָה];, e.g. #bref("2 Kgs 4:13"); #he[מֶה לַֽעֲשׂוֹת לָךְ וג׳]; #emph[what is to be done for thee?]; (#he[הֲיֵשׁ לְדַבֶּר־לָךְ];) #emph[wouldest thou be]; (lit. is it to be) #emph[spoken for to the king];, etc.? #bref("2 Kgs 13:19"); #he[לְהַכּוֹת]; #emph[it was to smite]; equivalent to #emph[thou shouldest have smitten];; #bref("Isa 5:4");, #bref("Ps 32:9");, #bref("68:19"); (?), #bref("Job 30:6"); (#emph[habitandum est iis];), #bref("1 Chr 9:25");, #bref("10:13");, #bref("22:5");, #bref("2 Chr 8:13"); (?), 11:22, 19:2, 36:19 (?), #bref("Hos 9:13");, #bref("Hab 1:17");. In a question #bref("2 Chr 19:2");; after #he[לֹא]; #bref("1 Chr 5:1");, #bref("15:2");; after #he[אֵין]; #bref("1 Chr 23:26");, #bref("2 Chr 5:11"); and frequently.

#ml("l") <p114l> Of the same kind also are the cases, in which the infinitive with #he[לְ]; depends on the idea of an obligation or permission (or prohibition); especially in such forms of expression as #bref("2 Sam 18:11"); #he[עָלַי לָ֫תֶת לְךָ וג׳]; it was #emph[upon me];, i.e. it would have been #emph[my duty to give thee];, etc.#footnote[#bref("2 Sam 4:10"); (#emph[cui dandum erat mihi];) appears to be similar; it may, however, be better, with Wellhausen, to omit the #he[אֲשֶׁר];.];; cf. #bref("Mic 3:1"); (#bref("2 Chr 13:5");) #emph[it is not for you to]; (i.e. are ye not bound to)?#footnote[But in #bref("1 Sam 23:20"); after #he[וְלָ֫נוּ]; #emph[and our part shall be]; the infinitive without #he[לְ]; stands as the subject of the sentence.]; with a negative, #bref("2 Chr 26:18"); #he[לֹא לְךָ וג׳]; #emph[it pertaineth not unto thee, Uzziah, to burn incense unto the Lord, but]; only #emph[to the priests];; also #he[אֵין לְ]; with an infinitive expresses #emph[it is not permitted]; (nefas est), #emph[may not];, e.g. #bref("Esth 4:2"); #he[כִּי אֵין לָבוֹא]; #emph[for none might enter];; 8:8, #bref("1 Chr 15:2");;#footnote[Quite different of course are such cases as #bref("Isa 37:3"); #he[וְכֹחַ אַ֫יִן לְלֵדָה]; #emph[and there is not strength to bring forth];; cf. #bref("Num 20:5");, #bref("Ruth 4:4");.]; #he[אֵין לְ]; with an infinitive is used in a somewhat different sense, equivalent to #emph[it is not feasible, not possible];, e.g. in #bref("Ps 40:6");, #bref("Eccl 3:14");, #bref("2 Chr 5:11");.#footnote[In #bref("2 Sam 14:19"); #he[אִשׁ]; (\= #he[יֵשׁ]; #emph[it is, there is];) is used in a similar sense after #he[אִם];, the negative particle of asseveration, #emph[of a truth it is not]; possible #emph[to turn to the right hand or to the left];.];—With either meaning #he[לֹא]; can be used instead of #he[אֵין];, e.g. #bref("Amos 6:10"); #he[לֹא לְהַזְכִּיר]; #emph[nefas est];, #emph[to make mention]; of the name of the Lord: but #bref("Judg 1:19"); #emph[for it was not possible to drive out];, etc., perhaps, however, the text originally stood as in #bref("Josh 17:12"); #he[לֹא יָֽכְלוּ לְה׳];; #bref("1 Chr 15:2");.

#origpage("350") <orig-350>;

#ml("m") <p114m> 3. A further class comprises the very numerous cases, in which the infinitive with #he[לְ]; is used as the object#footnote[This view is based upon the fact, that in numerous expressions of this kind (see the examples above) the #he[לְ]; may be omitted, and the infinitive consequently stand as an actual accusative of the object (see above, #emph[c];). However, the connection of the verb with the object is in the latter case closer and more emphatic (hence especially adapted to poetic or prophetic diction), than the looser addition of the infinitive with #he[לְ];; thus #he[לֹא אָבוּ שְׁמוֹעַ]; #bref("Isa 28:12"); is equivalent to #emph[they desired not obeying]; (#he[לֹא אָבוּ]; also with the infin. abs. in #bref("Isa 42:24");; cf. #xref(<p113d>)[§ 113#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];); but #he[לֹא אָבוּ לִשְׁמֹעַ]; #bref("Ezek 20:8"); rather expresses #emph[they could not make up their mind as to hearkening];. When connected with #he[לְ];, the governing verb has a more independent sense than when it directly governs the accusative of the object.]; of a governing verb, hence, again, for #emph[the direction]; which an action takes. The verbs (or conjugations) which occur most frequently in this combination with #he[לְ]; and an infinitive are: #he[הֵחֵל]; (with an infinitive without #he[לְ];, e.g. #bref("Deut 2:25");, 31, #bref("Josh 3:7");), #he[הוֹאִיל]; #emph[to begin];, #he[הוֹסִיף];, #he[יָסַף]; (prop. #emph[to add];) #emph[to continue];, very frequently, even in prose, with an infinitive without #he[לְ];, as #bref("Gen 4:12");, #bref("8:10");, 12, 37:5, #bref("1 Sam 3:8");, #bref("Job 27:1");, etc.; #he[חָדַל]; #emph[to cease from, to desist];; #he[כִּלָּה]; #emph[to complete, to make an end of];; #he[תָּמַם]; #emph[to be finished];; #he[הִקְרִיב]; #emph[to come near to];, #bref("Gen 12:11");; #he[מִהֵר]; #emph[to hasten]; (with an infinitive without #he[לְ]; #bref("Exod 2:18");); #he[אָבָה]; #emph[to be willing]; (with an infinitive without #he[לְ]; #bref("Isa 28:12");, #bref("30:9");, #bref("Job 39:9");); #he[חָפֵץ]; #emph[to will, to desire];; #he[מֵאֵן]; #emph[to refuse]; (#emph[to be unwilling];); #he[בִּקֵּשׁ]; #emph[to seek];; #he[יָכֹל]; #emph[to be able]; (with an infinitive without #he[לְ];, e.g. #bref("Gen 24:50");, #bref("37:4");, #bref("Exod 2:3");, #bref("18:23");, #bref("Num 22:38");, #bref("Job 4:2");); #he[נָתַן]; with an accusative of the person in the sense of #emph[to give up to some one, to cause];, or #emph[permit]; him to do something, e.g. #bref("Gen 20:6");, #bref("Ps 16:11"); (with an infinitive abs. #bref("Job 9:18");, see #xref(<p113d>)[§ 113#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];), #he[יָדַע]; #emph[to understand]; how to do something (in #bref("Job 3:8"); #he[הָֽעֲתִידִם עֹרֵר]; is analogous); #he[לָמַד]; #emph[to learn];; #he[קִוָּה]; #emph[to wait, expect]; (with a change of subject, e.g. #bref("Isa 5:2"); #emph[and he waited for it to bring forth grapes];).

#ml("n") <p114n> We must further mention here a number of verbs in Hiphʿîl (partly denominatives), which express an action in some definite direction (cf. #xref(<p53f>)[§ 53#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];), as #he[הִגְדִּיל]; #emph[to do greatly];, #he[הִשְׁפִּיל]; #emph[to make]; (it) #emph[low];, #he[הִגְבִּיהַּ]; #emph[to make]; (it) #emph[high];, #he[הֶֽעֱמִיק]; #emph[to make]; (it) #emph[deep];, #he[הִרְחִיק]; #emph[to make]; (it) #emph[far, distant];, #he[הֵיטִיב]; #emph[to make]; (it) #emph[good]; (with an infinitive without #he[לְ]; #bref("Ps 33:3");, but #bref("1 Sam 16:17");, in the same combination, with #he[לְ];); #he[הִשְׁכִּים]; #emph[to do]; anything #emph[early]; (#bref("Ps 127:2");, along with its opposite #he[אֵחַר]; #emph[to do]; something #emph[late];, with an infinitive without #he[לְ];); #he[הִרְבָּה]; #emph[to make]; (it) #emph[much];, #he[הִפְלָא]; #emph[to make]; (it) #emph[wonderful]; (even with a passive infinitive #bref("2 Chr 26:15");),#footnote[In almost all these examples the principal idea is properly contained in the infinitive, while the governing verb strictly speaking contains only a subordinate adverbial statement, and is therefore best rendered in English by an adverb; e.g. #bref("Gen 27:20"); #emph[how is it that thou hast found it so quickly?]; (prop. how thou hast hastened to find!), #bref("Gen 31:27"); #emph[wherefore didst thou flee secretly?]; So frequently with #he[הִרְבָּה]; (\= #emph[often, abundantly];), #bref("Exod 36:5");, #bref("1 Sam 1:12");, #bref("2 Kgs 21:6");, #bref("Isa 55:7");, #bref("Amos 4:4");, #bref("Ps 78:38");, etc.; with #he[שׁוּב]; (\= #emph[again];), #bref("Deut 30:9");, #bref("1 Kgs 13:17");, #bref("Hos 11:9");, #bref("Ezra 9:14");; cf. also #bref("2 Sam 19:4");, #bref("Jer 1:12");, #bref("Jonah 4:2");, and the analogous instances in #xref(<p120g>)[§ 120#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];; also #bref("2 Kgs 2:1"); #emph[thou hast asked a hard thing];.]; etc.

#origpage("351") <orig-351>;

#ml("o") <p114o> 4. Finally, the infinitive with #he[לְ]; is very frequently used in a much looser connection to state motives, attendant circumstances, or otherwise to define more exactly. In English, such infinitive constructions (like the Latin gerund in #emph[\-do];; cf. #emph[f];) must frequently be turned by #emph[that]; or a gerund; e.g. #bref("1 Sam 12:17"); #he[לִשְׁאֹל לָכֶם מֶלֶךְ]; #emph[in asking you a king];; 14:33, 19:5, 20:36, #bref("Gen 3:22");, #bref("18:19");, #bref("34:7");, 15, #bref("Exod 23:2");, #bref("Lev 5:4");, 22, 26, 8:15, #bref("Num 14:36");, #bref("2 Sam 3:10");, #bref("1 Kgs 2:3"); f., 14:8, #bref("Jer 44:7"); f., #bref("Ps 63:3");, #bref("78:18");, #bref("101:8");, #bref("103:20");, #bref("104:14"); f., 111:6, #bref("Prov 2:8");, #bref("8:34");, #bref("18:5");, #bref("Neh 13:18");. Sometimes the infinitive with #he[לְ]; is used in this way simply by itself, e.g. #bref("1 Chr 12:8"); #emph[as the roes upon the mountains]; #he[לְמַהֵר]; (as regards hasting) #emph[in swiftness];; #bref("Gen 2:3");, #bref("2 Sam 14:25"); (#he[לְהַלֵּל];); #bref("Isa 21:1"); (#he[לַֽחֲלוֹף];); #bref("Joel 2:26");, #bref("Prov 2:2");, #bref("26:2"); and so very frequently the infinitive #he[לֵאמֹר]; #emph[dicendo]; which has become stereotyped as an adverb to introduce direct narration (in the sense of #emph[thus, as follows];).#footnote[#he[לֵאמֹר]; is very often so used after #he[וַיְדַבֵּר]; in the Priestly document (#bref("Gen 8:15");, #bref("17:3");, etc., and numberless times in the legal parts of Exod., Lev., and Num.)—a pleonasm which is not surprising considering the admittedly prolix and formal style of the document.];

#ml("p") <p114p> 5. In a number of instances—especially in the later books—the infin. constr. with #he[לְ]; appears to be attached by #emph[Wāw]; (like the infinitive absolute, #xref(<p113z>)[§ 113#h(0.18em)#emph[z];];), as the continuation of a previous finite verb. In most examples of this kind it is, however, evident that the infinitive with #he[לְ]; virtually depends on an idea of intention, effort, or being in the act of, which, according to the sense, is contained in what has preceded, while the copula, as sometimes also elsewhere, is used in an emphatic sense (#emph[and that too];); thus e.g. #bref("Exod 32:29"); (if the text be right) #emph[fill your hand today]; (sc. with an offering) #emph[for the Lord... and that to bring a blessing upon you];, i.e. that ye may be blessed; cf. #bref("1 Sam 25:31"); (otherwise in verses 26 and 33 where the infinitive absolute is used, see #xref(<p113e>)[§ 113#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];); #bref("Ps 104:21");,#footnote[When Delitzsch on #bref("Ps 104:21");, referring to #bref("Hab 1:17");, explains the infinitive with #he[לְ]; as an elliptical mode of expressing the #emph[coniugatio periphrastica]; (equivalent to #emph[flagitaturi sunt a deo cibum suum];), this is, in point of fact, certainly applicable to this and a few other places mentioned above; but all these passages, in which the infinitive with #he[וּלְ]; follows, are to be distinguished from the cases treated above under #emph[h];, where the infinitive with #he[לְ]; #emph[without Wāw]; corresponds to a Latin gerundive, or is actually used to express the #emph[coniugatio periphrastica];.]; #bref("Job 34:8");, #bref("Eccl 9:1");, #bref("Neh 8:13");, #bref("2 Chr 7:17");.—In #bref("Lev 10:10f."); #he[וּלְהַבְדִּיל]; might be regarded as an explanatory addition to the command contained in verse #bref("9b"); (\= this prohibition of wine before the service shall ye observe, and that in order to put a difference, etc.); but probably the text has been disturbed by a redactor.—In #bref("2 Chr 30:9"); #he[וְלָשׁוּב]; depends on the idea of receiving favour which lies in #he[לְרַֽחֲמִים];. On the other hand, in #bref("1 Sam 8:12"); it is sufficient to explain #emph[and in order to appoint them unto him for captains of thousands]; (sc. he will take them). In #bref("Isa 44:28"); translate #emph[and he]; (Cyrus) #emph[shall perform all my pleasure, and that in saying of Jerusalem];, etc.

#ml("q") <p114q> #strong[3.]; The period of time to which an action or occurrence represented by the infinitive construct belongs, must sometimes be inferred from the context, or from the character of the principal tenses; cf. e.g. #bref("Gen 2:4"); #emph[these are the generations of the heaven and of the earth];, #he[בְּהִבָּֽרְאָם]; #emph[when they were created]; (prop. in their being created); #bref("Judg 6:18"); #he[עַד־בֹּאִי וג׳]; #emph[until]; #origpage("352") <orig-352>;#emph[I come unto thee, and bring forth];, etc. Cf. #bref("1 Sam 18:19"); (\= #emph[when she should have been given];); #bref("2 Kgs 2:1");, #bref("Hos 7:1");.

#ml("r") <p114r> Rem. 1. The constructions of the infinitive with a preposition, described above under #emph[d];, are almost always continued in the further course of the narrative by means of the #emph[finite verb];, i.e. by an independent sentence, not by a co\-ordinate infinitive. Such a finite verb #emph[we]; regard as governed by a conjunction, which corresponds to the preposition standing before the infinitive. Thus the infinitival construction (frequently even with a change of subject) is continued by a perfect (with #he[לֹא];), #bref("Jer 9:12"); #emph[because they have forsaken]; (#he[עַל־עָזְבָם];) #emph[my law]; ... #he[וְלֹא שָֽׁמְעוּ]; #emph[and have not obeyed my voice];; #bref("Gen 39:10");, #bref("1 Sam 24:12");, #bref("Amos 1:9");; without #he[לֹא]; #bref("Job 28:25"); (perf. after #he[לְ]; and infin.); by a perfect with #he[וְ]; (cf. #xref(<p112i>)[§ 112#h(0.18em)#emph[i];]; and #emph[v];) #bref("Amos 1:11"); #he[עַל־רָדְפוֹ וג׳]; #emph[because he did pursue his brother with the sword];, #he[וְשִׁחֵת]; #emph[and did cast off]; continually #emph[all pity]; (a frequentative perfect; for examples of the perfect consecutive proper see #bref("Gen 27:45");, #bref("Judg 6:18");, #bref("1 Sam 10:8");, #bref("2 Kgs 18:32"); \[#bref("Isa 36:17");\], always after #he[עַד־בֹּאִי]; #emph[until I come];); by a simple imperfect, e.g. #bref("Prov 1:27"); (after #he[בְּ];); #bref("Isa 30:26"); (after #he[בְּיוֹם]; #emph[in the day];, a temporal phrase which has here become equivalent to a preposition); #bref("Isa 5:24"); (after #he[כְּ‍];), 10:2, 13:9, 14:25, 45:1, 49:5, #bref("1 Sam 2:8");, #bref("Prov 2:8");, #bref("5:2");, #bref("8:21"); (always after #he[לְ];)#footnote[The great frequency of examples of this kind, especially in the poetical books, is due to a striving after what is called #emph[chiasmus]; in the arrangement of the parallel members in the two halves of the verse, i.e. in the instances given, the finite verb #emph[at the end]; of the second (co\-ordinate) clause is parallel with the infinitive #emph[at the beginning]; of the first. In this way the verbal form necessarily became separated from the #he[וְ];, and consequently the imperfect had to be used instead of the perfect consecutive. Such a parallelism of the #emph[external]; and #emph[internal]; members of a verse is frequent also in other cases, and was evidently felt to be an elegance of elevated—poetic or prophetic—style.];; by an imperfect consecutive, e.g. #bref("Gen 39:18"); #emph[and it came to pass];, #he[כַּֽהֲרִימִי קוֹלִי וָֽאֶקְרָא]; #emph[as I lifted up my voice and cried, that ...];; #bref("1 Kgs 10:9");, #bref("Job 38:13"); (after #he[לְ];); #bref("1 Kgs 18:18");, #bref("Isa 38:9");, #bref("Job 38:7");, 9 ff. (after #he[בְּ];); #bref("Isa 30:12");, #bref("Jer 7:13");, #bref("Ezek 34:8"); (after #he[יַ֫עַן];).

#ml("s") <p114s> 2. The negation of an infinitive construct, on account of the predominance of the noun\-element in its character, is effected not by the verbal negative #he[לֹא]; (except in the compound #he[בְּלֹא];, which has come to be used as a preposition, #emph[without];, #bref("Num 35:23");, #bref("Prov 19:2");), but by #he[בִּלְתִּי];, originally a substantive (see the Lexicon), with #he[לְ]; prefixed (but also #bref("Num 14:16"); #he[מִבִּלְתִּי];), e.g. #bref("Gen 3:11"); #he[לְבִלְתִּי אֲכָל־מִמֶּ֫נּוּ]; #emph[not to eat of it];; in a final sense, 4:15 #emph[lest any finding him should smite him];; only in #bref("2 Kgs 23:10"); is #he[לְ]; repeated before the infinitive. In #bref("Ps 32:9"); (if the text be right) #he[בַּל]; negatives, not the infinitive, but the predicate which is understood.

#sec("115", [Construction of the Infinitive Construct with Subject and Object], note: []) <s115>

#ml("a") <p115a> #strong[1.]; Like the infinitive absolute (see #xref(<p113a>)[§ 113#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];), the character of the infinitive construct as a #emph[verbal]; noun is shown by its power of taking the case proper to its verb, and hence in transitive verbs#footnote[For examples of the accus. of the object with a pass. infin., see #xref(<p121c>)[§ 121#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];.]; #emph[the accusative of the object];, e.g. #bref("Num 9:15"); #he[בְּיוֹם הָקִים אֶת־הַמִּשְׁכָּן]; #emph[on the day the]; #origpage("353") <orig-353>;#emph[tabernacle was reared up];; #bref("1 Sam 19:1"); #he[לְהָמִית אֶת־דָּוִד]; #emph[that they should slay David];; #bref("Gen 14:17");, #bref("19:29");, #bref("Exod 38:27");, #bref("1 Kgs 12:15");, #bref("15:4");; with a negative, e.g. #bref("Lev 26:15"); #he[לְבִלְתִּי עֲשׂוֹת אֶת־כָּל־מִצְוֹתַי]; #emph[so that ye will not do all my commandments];; with the accusative of the personal pronoun, e.g. #bref("Deut 29:12"); #he[לְמַ֫עַן הָקִים־אֹֽתְךָ]; #emph[that he may establish thee];; #bref("Gen 25:26");, #bref("Jer 24:7");; with a verbal suffix, e.g. #bref("Exod 2:14"); #he[לְהָרְגֵ֫נִי]; #emph[to kill me];; #bref("Jer 38:26"); #he[לְבִלְתִּי הֲשִׁיבֵ֫נִי]; #emph[that he would not cause me to return]; (on the suffix, cf. #emph[c];). In #bref("Isa 49:6"); the object even precedes the infinitive with #he[לְ];; on this order cf. the note on #xref(<p114r>)[§ 114#h(0.18em)#emph[r];];.—If the verb governs a double accusative, the infinitive may also take the same, e.g. #bref("Gen 41:39"); #he[אַֽחֲרֵי הוֹדִ֫יעַ אֱלֹהִים֖ אֽוֹתְךָ אֶת־כָּל־זֹאת]; #emph[forasmuch as God hath showed thee all this];; #bref("Deut 21:16");.

#ml("b") <p115b> Rem. 1. The object after the infinitive construct must also always be regarded as in the accusative, even when it is not expressly introduced (as in all the above examples) by the #emph[nota accusativi]; #he[אֶת־];, and when therefore the substantive in question might easily be taken as the #emph[genitive of the object]; governed by the infinitive (the usual construction in Arabic), e.g. #bref("Prov 21:15"); #he[עֲשׂוֹת מִשְׁפָּט]; #emph[to do judgement];. Against regarding it as a genitive, which is in itself possible (#emph[the doing, the executing of judgement];), is the fact (#emph[a];) that elsewhere the #emph[nota accusativi]; is so frequently added; (#emph[b];) that in such a case the secondary forms of the infinitive, such as #he[רְאֹה]; for (#he[פָּנֶ֫יךָ];) #he[רְאֹת]; #bref("Gen 48:11"); (cf. #bref("Ps 101:3");, #bref("Prov 16:16");), would be unintelligible; (#emph[c];) that certain infinitive forms, if they were to be regarded as in the construct state, could hardly retain the pretonic Qameṣ without exception, whereas, when connected with suffixes (i.e. with real genitives; cf. #xref(<p33c>)[§ 33#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];), this Qameṣ necessarily becomes #emph[Š#super[e];wâ];; e.g. #bref("Gen 18:25"); #he[לְהָמִית צַדִּיק]; #emph[to slay the righteous]; (never as #he[לַֽהֲמִית];; cf., on the other hand, above, #he[הֲשִׁיבֵ֫נִי];); #bref("2 Kgs 21:8");, #bref("Ezek 44:30");. Similarly in such cases as #bref("Isa 3:13"); (#bref("Ps 50:4");) instead of #he[לָדִין עַמִּים]; we should rather expect #he[לְדִין];, if the infinitive were regarded as in the construct state, and #he[עַמִּים]; as the genitive. Hence also in cases like #bref("Isa 58:9"); (#he[שְׁלַח]; for #he[שְׁלֹחַ];) we must assume, with Sellin, op. cit., p. 78, a merely ‘external phonetic connection’ and not the genitive construction.

#ml("c") <p115c> 2. The verbal suffixes added to the infinitive are (with the exception of #he[לְהֽוֹצִאֵ֫הוּ]; #bref("Jer 39:14");) only the suffix of the 1st pers. sing. (besides the above examples cf. also #bref("1 Sam 5:10");, #bref("27:1");, #bref("28:9");, #bref("Ruth 2:10");, #bref("1 Chr 12:17");, etc.) and plural; e.g. #he[לְהַשְׁמִידֵ֫נוּ]; #emph[to destroy us];, #bref("Deut 1:27"); (immediately after #he[לָתֵת אֹתָ֫נוּ];, so that #he[־ֵ֫ נוּ]; is doubtless a #emph[verbal]; not a #emph[noun];\-suffix, although in form it might be either); #he[לַֽהֲמִיתֵ֫נוּ]; #bref("Num 16:13");, #bref("Judg 13:23"); (after #he[חָפֵץ];). Elsewhere the pronominal object is appended either by means of the accusative sign (e.g. #bref("Gen 25:26"); #he[בְּלֶ֫דֶת אֹתָם]; prop. #emph[in the bearing them];; #he[לָדַ֫עַת אֹתִי]; #emph[to know me];, #bref("Jer 24:7");) or in the form of a noun\-suffix (as genitive of the object). The latter occurs almost always, whenever the context excludes the possibility of a misunderstanding; e.g. #bref("1 Sam 20:33"); #he[לְהַכֹּתוֹ]; (prop. for his smiting) #emph[to smite him];, not, as the form might also mean, #emph[in order that he might smite];; cf. #bref("1 Kgs 20:35");; with the suffix of the 3rd sing. fem. #bref("Num 22:25");; of the 3rd plur. #bref("Josh 10:20");, #bref("2 Sam 21:2");, etc. Hence also the suffixes of the 2nd sing. with the infinitive, as #he[לְהַכֹּֽתְךָ]; #bref("Jer 40:14");, cf. #bref("Mic 6:13");, and even #he[גַּדֶּלְךָ]; #emph[to magnify thee];, #bref("Josh 3:7");, must certainly be regarded as #emph[nominal]; not #emph[verbal]; suffixes. The connection of the noun\-suffix, as genitive of the object, with the infinitive, #origpage("354") <orig-354>;was so fully established, that it could be used not only in such strange cases, as #bref("Gen 37:4"); #he[לֹא יָכְֽלוּ דַבְּרוֹ לְשָׁלֹם]; #emph[they could not speak to him peaceably];, cf. #bref("Zech 3:1"); #he[לְשִׂטְנוֹ]; #emph[to be an adversary to him];, but ultimately even in the 1st sing., as in #bref("Num 22:13"); #he[לְתִתִּי]; #emph[to give me leave]; \[#bref("Deut 25:7"); #he[לֹא אָבָה יַבְּמִי]; #emph[he will not perform the duty of a husband’s brother unto me];; #bref("1 Chr 4:10"); #he[לְבִלְתִּי עָצְבִּי]; #emph[that it may not grieve me!];\]

#ml("d") <p115d> 3. The power of governing like a verb is also retained in those verbal nouns which, although originally secondary forms of the infinitive, have fully acquired the value of nouns, e.g. #bref("Isa 11:9"); #he[דֵּעָה אֶת־יְהֹוָה]; (prop. to know the Lord) #emph[the knowledge of the Lord];; #he[לְיִרְאָה אֹתִי]; #emph[to fear me];, #bref("Deut 4:10");, #bref("5:26");, #bref("10:12");; an accusative follows #he[לְאַֽהֲבָה]; #bref("Deut 10:12");, 15, #bref("Isa 56:6"); (cf. also #bref("1 Kgs 10:9");, #bref("Hos 3:1");); #he[לַֽהֲנָפָה]; #bref("Isa 30:28");; #he[בְּשִׂנְאַת יְהֹוָה אֹתָ֫נוּ]; #bref("Deut 1:27");; after verbal nouns formed with the prefix #he[מ‍]; (cf. #xref(<p45e>)[§ 45#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];), #bref("Num 10:2");, #bref("Isa 13:19");, #bref("Amos 4:11");, #bref("Ezek 17:9");. The accusative of the object likewise remains after infinitives (or their secondary forms) which have the article, e.g. #bref("Gen 2:9");, #bref("Jer 22:16");, or a suffix, e.g. #bref("Gen 5:4");, etc., 28:4, 6, 29:19 f., 30:15, 38:5, #bref("2 Sam 3:11");, #bref("Isa 29:13");.

#ml("e") <p115e> #strong[2.]; The subject of the action represented by the infinitive is mostly placed immediately#footnote[In #bref("Gen 24:30"); the subject of #he[כִּרְאֹת]; is wanting (but #he[כְּשָׁמְעוֹ]; follows); the original reading was undoubtedly #he[כִּרְאֹתוֹ];, and the text is now in a state of confusion; verse 30#emph[a]; should come before verse 29#emph[b];. In #bref("Gen 19:29");, #bref("25:26");, #bref("Exod 9:16");, #bref("13:21");, #bref("1 Sam 18:19");, #bref("Jer 41:6");, #bref("Ps 42:4"); the subject, although not indicated, is easily supplied from the context. The infinitive in such cases is best rendered in English by a passive.]; after it, either in the #emph[genitive]; or #emph[nominative];. The subject is in the genitive (#xref(<p33c>)[§ 33#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];) whenever it has the form of a noun\-suffix, and also when the infinitive has the termination of the constr. st. fem. sing. (see #emph[f];); probably also in many other cases, where the infinitive in form and meaning is used more like a substantive, and accordingly governs like a noun. On the other hand, the subject of the infinitive is certainly to be regarded as a nominative, when it is separated from the infinitive by any insertion, and according to certain indications (see #emph[g];) very probably in many other instances.

#ml("f") <p115f> Rem. 1. Examples of genitives of the subject after infinitives in the connective form are #bref("Deut 1:27"); #he[בְּשִׂנְאַת יְהֹוָה אֹתָ֫נוּ]; prop. #emph[in the Lord’s hating us];; cf. 7:8, #bref("Gen 19:16");, #bref("1 Kgs 10:9");, #bref("Isa 13:19");, #bref("47:9");, #bref("Hos 3:1");, #bref("Amos 4:11");. The subject of the infinitive is probably also to be regarded as genitive in such cases as #bref("Exod 17:1"); #emph[and there was no water]; #he[לִשְׁתֹּת הָעָם]; #emph[for the people to drink]; (prop. for the drinking of the people), and in cases like #bref("Gen 16:16"); (#he[בְּלֶ֫דֶת הָגָר];); #bref("Gen 16:3");, #bref("Exod 19:1");, #bref("Num 20:3");, 4, 33:38, #bref("1 Kgs 6:1");, #bref("Ps 133:1");, #bref("2 Chr 7:3");, etc.

#ml("g") <p115g> 2. Examples in which the subject is separated from the infinitive by an insertion, and hence must certainly be regarded as a nominative, are #bref("Job 34:22"); #he[לְהִסָּ֫תֶר שָׁם פֹּֽעֲלֵי אָ֫וֶן]; #emph[that the workers of iniquity may hide themselves there]; (prop. for the hiding themselves there the workers of iniquity); cf. #bref("Gen 34:15");, #bref("Num 35:6");, #bref("Deut 19:3");, #bref("Judg 9:2");, #bref("2 Sam 24:13");, #bref("Ps 76:10");, and below, #emph[i];. The subject is likewise to be regarded as a #emph[nominative];, whenever the #emph[Lamedh]; is prefixed to the infinitive by means of a pretonic Qameṣ (cf. #emph[b]; above), e.g. #bref("2 Sam 19:20"); #he[לָשׂוּם הַמֶּ֫לֶךְ אֶל־לִבּוֹ];, #origpage("355") <orig-355>;since, if the infinitive were used as a #emph[nomen regens];, we should rather expect #he[לְשׂוּם]; according to #xref(<p102f>)[§ 102#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];. That the subject of the infinitive is regarded elsewhere also as nominative is again (see above, #xref(<p115b>)[#emph[b];];) probable, since in such forms as #he[הָנִיחַ]; #bref("Deut 25:19");, #bref("Isa 14:3");, #he[הָמִיר]; #bref("Ps 46:3");, etc., the pretonic Qameṣ is retained without exception, whereas on the analogy of #he[הֲנִיחִי]; #bref("Ezek 24:13");, #he[הֲקִימוֹ]; #bref("Jer 23:20");, etc., we should expect #he[הֲנִיחַ];, #he[הֲמִיר];, etc., if the infinitive were regarded as a #emph[nomen regens];. Or was the retention of the Qameṣ (assuming the thorough correctness of the Masoretic punctuation) rendered possible even before a following genitive, because that vowel was characteristic of the form? It is at all events certain that owing to the lack of case\-endings,#footnote[In Arabic, where the case\-endings leave no doubt as to the construction, it is equally possible to say either #emph[qatlu Zaidin]; (gen. of subj.) #emph[ʿAmran]; (acc.), literally #emph[Zaid’s killing ʿAmr];, or #emph[qatlu ʿAmrin]; (gen. of obj.) #emph[Zaidun]; (nom. of subj.), or even #emph[el\-qatlu]; (with article) #emph[Zaidun]; (nom. of subj.) #emph[ʿAmran]; (acc. of obj.).]; a distinction between the genitival and nominatival constructions could not have been consciously made in the case of most infinitives, e.g. in unchangeable forms like #he[קְטֹל];, #he[קוּם];, etc.

#ml("h") <p115h> #strong[3.]; When both a #emph[subject]; and an #emph[object]; are connected with the infinitive, the rule is, that the subject should immediately follow the infinitive, and then the object. The latter, in such a case, is necessarily in the accusative, but the subject (as in #xref(<p115e>)[#emph[e];];) may be either in the genitive or in the nominative. The noun\-suffixes again are, of course, to be regarded as genitives, e.g. #bref("Gen 39:18"); #he[כַּֽהֲרִימִי קוֹלִי]; #emph[as I lifted up my voice]; (cf. #bref("1 Kgs 13:21");, and the examples, #bref("Gen 5:4");, etc., enumerated above, under #xref(<p115d>)[#emph[d];];), and so also substantives which follow a connective form, #bref("Deut 1:27");, etc.; see above, #xref(<p115d>)[#emph[d];]; and #xref(<p115f>)[#emph[f];];.

#ml("i") <p115i> On the other hand, the subject appears necessarily to be in the nominative in such cases as #bref("Isa 10:15"); #he[כְּהָנִיף שֵׁ֫בֶט אֶת־מְרִימָיו]; #emph[as if a rod should shake them that lift it up]; (for the plur. #he[מרימיו]; cf. #xref(<p124k>)[§ 124#h(0.18em)#emph[k];];), not #he[כַּֽהֲנִיף];, as would be expected (see #xref(<p115g>)[#emph[g];]; above), if #he[שֵׁ֫בֶט]; were in the genitive; cf. #bref("2 Sam 14:13");, #bref("Job 33:17");. And so probably also in other cases, as #bref("Gen 5:1");, #bref("13:10");, #bref("Josh 14:7");, #bref("1 Kgs 13:4");, #bref("2 Kgs 23:10");, #bref("Isa 32:7");. The subject is separated from the infinitive by an insertion (and consequently must necessarily be in the nominative; see #xref(<p115g>)[#emph[g];]; above), e.g. in #bref("Jer 21:1");.

#ml("k") <p115k> Rem. Less frequently the object is placed immediately after the infinitive, and then the nominative of the subject, as a subsequent complement, e.g. #bref("Isa 20:1"); #he[בִּשְׁלֹחַ אֹתוֹ סַֽרְגוֹן]; #emph[when Sargon sent him];; #bref("Gen 4:15");, #bref("Josh 14:11");, #bref("2 Sam 18:29");, #bref("Isa 5:24");, #bref("Ps 56:1");, #bref("Prov 25:8");. In #bref("Num 24:23"); the subject follows an infinitive which has a noun\-suffix in place of the object.

#sec("116", [The Participles], note: []) <s116>

Cf. Sellin (see above at the head of #xref(<s113>)[§ 113];), p. 6 ff., and Kahan, p. 11 ff.

#ml("a") <p116a> #strong[1.]; Like the two infinitives, the participles also occupy a middle place between the noun and the verb. In form they are simple nouns, #origpage("356") <orig-356>;and most nearly related to the adjective; consequently they cannot in themselves be employed to represent definite relations of tense or mood. On the other hand, their verbal character is shown by their not representing, like the adjectives, a fixed and permanent quality (or state), but one which is in some way connected with an #emph[action]; or #emph[activity];. The #emph[participle active]; indicates a person or thing conceived as being in the continual uninterrupted #emph[exercise]; of an activity. The #emph[participle passive];, on the other hand, indicates the person or thing in a state which has been brought about by external #emph[actions];.

#ml("b") <p116b> Rem. That the language was fully conscious of the difference between a state implying action (or effected by external action) and mere passivity, is seen from the fact, that participles proper cannot be formed from the purely stative Qal, but only verbal adjectives of the form #emph[qāṭēl]; (#he[מָלֵא];, #he[כָּבֵד];, etc.) or #emph[qāṭōl]; (#he[גָּבֹהַּ];, etc.), whereas the #emph[transitive]; Qal #he[שָׂנֵא]; #emph[to hate];, although it coincides in form with the intransitive Qal (as a verb middle #emph[e];), nevertheless forms a participle active #he[שֹׂנֵא];, and participle passive #he[שָׂנוּא]; (cf. the feminine #he[שְׂנוּאָה];).—In cases where the participle proper and the verbal adjective both occur, they are by no means synonymous. When the Assyrians are called in #bref("Isa 28:11"); #he[לַֽעֲגֵי שָׂפָה]; #emph[men of stammering lips];, a character is ascribed to them which is inseparably connected with their personality. On the other hand #he[כֻּלֹּה לֹעֵג לִי]; #bref("Jer 20:7");, describes those about the prophet as continually engaged in casting ridicule upon him. Cf. also #bref("Ps 9:18"); (#he[שְׁכֵחֵי];) with 50:22 (#he[שֹֽׁכְחֵי];).

#ml("c") <p116c> On the difference between the participle as expressing #emph[simple]; duration and the imperfect as expressing #emph[progressive]; duration, cf. what has been stated above in #xref(<p107d>)[§ 107#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];. Nevertheless the participle is sometimes used—especially in the later books, cf. e.g. #bref("Neh 6:17");, #bref("2 Chr 17:11");—where we should expect the action to be divided up into its several parts, and consequently should expect the finite verb. But the substitution of the participle for the #emph[tempus historicum];, which becomes customary in Aramaic (cf. Kautzsch, #emph[Gramm. des Bibl.\-Aram.];, § 76. 2, #emph[d]; and #emph[e];), is nevertheless quite foreign to Hebrew.

#ml("d") <p116d> #strong[2.]; The period of time indicated by (#emph[a];) a participle active, either as an attribute or predicate, must be inferred from the particular context. Thus #he[מֵת]; may mean either #emph[moriens]; (#bref("Zech 11:9");), or #emph[mortuus]; (so commonly; with the article #he[הַמֵּת]; regularly \= #emph[the dead man];), or #emph[moriturus]; (#bref("Deut 4:22");); #he[בָּא]; #emph[coming, come]; #bref("Gen 18:11");, etc., #emph[venturus]; #bref("1 Sam 2:31");, etc.; #he[נֹפֵל]; #emph[falling];, but also #emph[fallen];, #bref("Judg 3:25");, #bref("1 Sam 5:3");, and #emph[ready to fall]; (threatening ruin, #bref("Isa 30:13");, #bref("Amos 9:11");). For other examples of perfect participles see #bref("Gen 27:33");, #bref("43:18"); (#he[הַשָּׁב]; #emph[that was returned];; cf. #bref("Ezra 6:21");, etc., #he[הַשָּׁבִים]; #emph[which were come again]; from the captivity); #bref("Gen 35:3");, #bref("Exod 11:5");, #bref("Zech 12:1");, #bref("Ps 137:7");, #bref("Prov 8:9");, #bref("Job 12:4"); (#he[קֹרֵא];), and see #emph[m]; below. For future participles see #bref("Gen 41:25");, #bref("1 Kgs 18:9");, #bref("Isa 5:5");, #bref("Jonah 1:3");, etc., probably also #he[לֹֽקְחֵי]; #bref("Gen 19:14");. On the #emph[futurum instans]; (esp. after #he[הִנֵּה];) see #emph[p]; below.

#ml("e") <p116e> (#emph[b];) Of the passive participles, that of #emph[Qal]; (e.g. #he[כָּתוּב]; #emph[scriptus];) always corresponds to a Latin or Greek perfect participle passive, those of the other conjugations, especially #emph[Niphʿal];, sometimes to #origpage("357") <orig-357>;a Latin gerundive (or to an adjective in #emph[\-bilis];), e.g. #he[נוֹרָא]; #emph[metuendus];, #emph[to be feared];, #bref("Ps 76:8");, etc.; #he[נֶחְמָד]; #emph[desiderandus]; (#emph[desiderabilis];) #bref("Gen 3:6");, #bref("Ps 19:11");, etc.; #he[נִבְרָא]; #emph[creandus]; #bref("Ps 102:19");; #he[נוֹלָד];, usually #emph[natus];, but also (like #he[הַיּוּלָּד]; #bref("Judg 13:8");) #emph[procreandus, nasciturus]; #bref("1 Kgs 13:2");, #bref("Ps 22:32");; #he[נַֽעֲרָץ]; #emph[terribilis]; #bref("Ps 89:8");; #he[נִתְעָב]; #emph[abominable]; #bref("Job 15:16");; #he[נֶחְשָׁב]; #emph[aestimandus]; #bref("Isa 2:22");; #he[הַנֶּֽאֱכֶ֫לֶת]; #emph[that may be eaten]; (an animal) #bref("Lev 11:47");. In #emph[Puʿal]; #he[מְהֻלָּל]; #emph[laudandus];, #emph[worthy to be praised]; #bref("Ps 18:4");. In #emph[Hophʿal];, #bref("2 Sam 20:21"); #he[מֻשְׁלָךְ];; #bref("2 Kgs 11:2"); #he[הַמּֽוּמָתִים];; #bref("Isa 12:5"); #emph[Q#super[e];rê]; #he[מוּדַ֫עַת];.#footnote[Such examples as #he[נוֹרָא];, #he[נֶחְמָד];, #he[מְהֻלָּל]; show plainly the origin of this gerundive use of the participle passive. A person or thing #emph[feared, desired];, or #emph[praised]; at all times is shown thereby to be #emph[terrible, desirable];, or #emph[praiseworthy];, and therefore also #emph[to be feared];, etc.];

#ml("f") <p116f> #strong[3.]; The participles active, in virtue of their partly verbal character, possess the power of governing like verbs, and consequently, when used in the absolute state, may take after them an object either in the accusative, or with the preposition with which the verb in question is elsewhere usually construed, e.g. #bref("1 Sam 18:29"); #he[אֹיֵב אֶת־דָּוִד]; #emph[hating David];; #bref("Gen 42:29");; with the suffix of the accusative, e.g. #he[עֹשֵׂ֫נִי]; #emph[that made me]; #bref("Job 31:15");; #he[מִי רֹאֵ֫נוּ]; #emph[who seeth us?]; #bref("Isa 29:15"); (in #bref("Isa 47:10"); #he[רֹאָ֫נִי]; is abnormal); #he[רֹדֵם]; #emph[ruling them]; #bref("Ps 68:28");, sometimes also with the article, e.g. #bref("Ps 18:33"); #he[הַֽמְאַזְּרֵ֫נִי]; #emph[that girdeth me]; (LXX ὁ κραταιῶν με); #bref("Deut 8:14");–16, 13:6, 11, 20:1, #bref("2 Sam 1:24");, #bref("Isa 9:12"); (where, however, Cheyne omits the article), 63:11, #bref("Ps 81:11");, #bref("103:4");, #bref("Dan 11:6");; followed by a preposition, e.g. #bref("1 Kgs 9:23"); #he[הָֽרֹדִים בָּעָם]; #emph[which bare rule over the people];; #bref("2 Kgs 20:5"); #he[הִֽנְנִי רֹפֵא לָךְ]; #emph[behold, I will heal thee];. #footnote[On the other hand, in #bref("Isa 11:9"); #emph[as the waters]; #he[לַיָּם מְכַסִּים]; #emph[covering the sea];, the #he[ל]; serves only to introduce the object preceding the participle \[cf. the Arabic parallels cited by Driver, #emph[Tenses];, § 135, 7 #emph[Obs.];\]. Cf. Hab. 2:14.];

By an exhaustive examination of the statistics, Sellin (see the title at the head of § 113), p. 40 ff., shows that the participle when construed as a #emph[verb]; expresses a single and comparatively transitory act, or relates to particular cases, historical facts, and the like, while the participle construed as a #emph[noun]; (see #emph[g];) indicates repeated, enduring, or commonly occurring acts, occupations, and thoughts.

So also the verbal adjectives of the form #emph[qāṭēl]; may take an accusative of the person or thing, if the finite verb from which they are derived governs an accusative, e.g. #bref("Deut 34:9"); #he[מָלֵא רוּחַ חָכְמָה]; #emph[full of the spirit of wisdom];; #bref("Ps 5:5"); #he[חָפֵץ רֶ֫שַׁע]; #emph[that hath pleasure in wickedness];.

#ml("g") <p116g> As a sort of noun the participle may, however, also exercise the same government as a noun, being in the construct state, and followed by the object of the action in the genitive (see #xref(<p89a>)[§ 89#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];; and cf. #xref(<p128x>)[§ 128#h(0.18em)#emph[x];];), e.g. #bref("Ps 5:12"); #he[אֹֽהֲבֵי שְׁמֶ֫ךָ]; #emph[that love thy name];; cf. #bref("Ps 19:8"); f.; also when a verbal adjective, e.g. #bref("Gen 22:12"); and often #he[יְרֵא אֱלֹהִים]; #emph[one fearing]; #origpage("358") <orig-358>;#emph[God];; #bref("Hab 2:15");; with an infinitive, #bref("Ps 127:2");; with a noun\-suffix (which, according to #xref(<p33c>)[§ 33#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];, also represents a genitive), e.g. #bref("Gen 4:14"); #he[כָּל־מֹֽצְאִי]; #emph[whosoever findeth me]; (prop. my finder; cf. #he[עֹשִׂי]; #emph[my maker];); 12:3 #he[מְבָֽרֲכֶ֫יךָ]; #emph[that bless thee];, #he[מְקַלֶּלְךָ]; #emph[that curseth thee]; (but read either #he[מְקַלְלֶ֫יךָ];, or #he[מְבָֽרֶכְךָ]; in the preceding clause); 27:29, #bref("1 Sam 2:30");, #bref("Isa 63:13");, #bref("Ps 18:49");. In #bref("Jer 33:22"); read #he[מְשָֽׁרְתִים אֹתִי];.#footnote[When, as in #bref("Job 40:19");, the participle with the noun\-suffix #he[הָֽעֹשׂוֹ]; #emph[he that made him];, also has the article (cf. #xref(<p127i>)[§ 127#h(0.18em)#emph[i];];), the anomaly is difficult to understand, since a word determined by a genitive does not admit of being determined by the article.—No less remarkable is the use of the constr. st. of the participle before the accusative in #bref("Jer 33:22"); #he[מְשָֽׁרְתֵי אֹתִי]; #emph[that minister unto me]; (for which there is #he[מְשָֽׁרְתַי]; in verse 21). In #bref("Amos 4:13"); an accusative of the product follows the genitive of the object, #he[עֹשֵׂה שַׁחַר עֵיפָה]; #emph[maker of the morning into darkness];. In #bref("Jer 2:17"); #he[בְּעֵת מֽוֹלִכֵךְ]; is supposed to mean #emph[at the time when he led thee];; perhaps the perfect (#he[הוֹל׳];) should be read as in 6:15. In #bref("Ezek 27:34");, the ancient versions read #he[עַתָּ];(#he[ה];) #he[נִשְׁבַּרְתְּ]; #emph[now thou art broken];, instead of the difficult #he[עֵת נִשְׁבֶּ֫רֶת];. In #bref("1 Kgs 20:40"); read #he[עֹשֶׂה]; before #he[הֵ֫נָּה וָהֵ֫נָּה];.];

#ml("h") <p116h> Rem. To the class of objective genitives belong also specifications of place after the participles #he[בָּא]; #emph[iniens]; and #he[יֹצֵא]; #emph[egrediens];, since the verbs #he[בּוֹא]; and #he[יָצָא];, in the sense of #emph[ingredi, egredi];, can be directly connected with an accusative; e.g. #bref("Gen 23:10");, 18 #he[בָּאֵי שַׁ֫עַר עִירוֹ]; #emph[that went in at the gate of his city];; #bref("Lam 1:4");; after #he[יֹֽצְאֵי]; #bref("Gen 9:10");, #bref("34:24");, #bref("46:26");, etc.—In poetic language the participle in the construct state may be connected not only with a genitive of the object, but also with any other specifications (especially of space) which otherwise can only be made to depend on the verb in question by means of a preposition; cf. #bref("Isa 38:18");, and frequently, #he[יֽוֹרְדֵי־בוֹר]; #emph[they that go down into the pit]; (the grave); #bref("Ps 88:6"); #he[שֹֽׁכְבֵי קֶ֫בֶר]; #emph[that lie in the grave];; #bref("Deut 32:24"); (#bref("Mic 7:17");); #bref("1 Kgs 2:7");, #bref("2 Kgs 11:5");, 7, 9 #emph[those that came in]; (or #emph[went out];) #emph[on the sabbath];, #bref("Prov 2:7");, #bref("1 Chr 5:18");, etc.; instead of the construction with #he[מִן־];, e.g. #bref("Isa 59:20"); (#emph[those who turn from transgression];), #bref("Mic 2:8"); (cf. #xref(<p72p>)[§ 72#h(0.18em)#emph[p];];).

#ml("i") <p116i> These genitives of nearer definition appear also in the form of a noun\-suffix, e.g. #bref("Ps 18:40");, 49 #he[קָמַי]; (for #he[קָמִים עָלַי];) #emph[that rise up against me];; cf. #bref("Exod 15:7");, #bref("Deut 33:11");, #bref("Ps 44:6");, #bref("Exod 32:25");, #bref("Isa 1:27"); #he[שָׁבֶ֫יהָ]; #emph[her converts];; #bref("Ps 53:6"); (#he[חֹנָךְ];); #bref("Prov 2:19"); #he[כָּל־בָּאֶ֫יהָ]; #emph[all that go unto her];; the construction is especially bold in #bref("Isa 29:7"); #he[כָּל־צֹבֶ֫יהָ וּמְצֹֽדָתָהּ]; #emph[all that fight against her and her stronghold]; (for #he[כָּל־הַצֹּֽבְאִים עָלֶ֫יהָ וְעַל־מ׳];); #bref("Ps 102:9"); even with a participle Poʿal, #he[מְהֽוֹלָלַי]; #emph[they that are mad against me]; (?), but read perhaps with Olshausen #he[מְחֽוֹלְלַי]; #emph[who pierce me];.—In #bref("Isa 1:30"); #emph[as a terebinth]; #he[נֹבֶ֫לֶת עָלֶ֫הָ]; #emph[fading as regards its leaf];, it remains doubtful whether #he[נֹבֶ֫לֶת]; is in the absolute state, and consequently #he[עָלֶ֫הָ]; in the accusative, or whether it is to be regarded as construct state, and #he[עָלֶ֫הָ]; as the genitive. In the latter case it would be analogous to #bref("Prov 14:2"); (see #emph[k];).

#ml("k") <p116k> #strong[4.]; The passive participles also may either be in the absolute state, and take the determining word in the accusative,#footnote[On the proper force of this accusative when retained in the passive construction cf. below, #xref(<p117cc>)[§ 117#h(0.18em)#emph[cc];];, etc., and #xref(<p121c>)[§ 121#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];, #emph[d];. So also #bref("Neh 4:12"); is to be understood, #emph[and the builders]; were #he[אִישׁ חַרְבּוֹ אֲסוּרִים עַל־מָתְנָיו]; #emph[girded every one with his sword on his side, and building];.]; or may be connected #origpage("359") <orig-359>;with it in the construct state, e.g. #bref("Judg 18:11");, #bref("1 Sam 2:18");, #bref("Ezek 9:2"); #he[לָבוּשׁ בַּדִּים]; #emph[clothed in linen];, cf. verse 3 #he[הַלָּבֻשׁ הַבַּדִּים];; (even with a suffix #he[קָרוּעַ כֻּתָּנְתּוֹ]; #emph[rent as regards his coat]; #bref("2 Sam 15:32");; with the participle following #bref("Judg 1:7");); but #bref("Ezek 9:11"); #he[לְבוּשׁ הַבַּדִּים]; #emph[the one clothed with linen];; #bref("2 Sam 13:31"); #he[קְרֻעֵי בְגָדִים]; #emph[rent in respect of clothes];, equivalent to #emph[with their clothes rent]; (cf. #bref("Jer 41:5");); #bref("Num 24:4");, #bref("Deut 25:10");, #bref("Isa 3:3");, #bref("33:24");, #bref("Joel 1:8");, #bref("Ps 32:1"); (#he[נְשׂוּי־פֶּ֫שַׁע]; #emph[forgiven in respect of transgression];, #he[כְּסוּי חֲטָאָה]; #emph[covered in respect of sin];); with a suffix to the noun, #bref("Prov 14:2"); #he[נְלוֹז דְּרָכָיו]; #emph[he that is perverse in his ways];.

#ml("l") <p116l> Rem. The passive participle occurs in the construct state before a genitive of the #emph[cause];, e.g. in #bref("Isa 1:7"); #he[שְׂרֻפוֹת אֵשׁ]; #emph[burnt with fire];; cf. #bref("Gen 41:6");, #bref("Exod 28:11");, #bref("Deut 32:24");; before a genitive denoting the #emph[author];, e.g. #bref("Gen 24:31"); #he[בְּרוּךְ יְהֹוָה]; #emph[blessed of the Lord]; (but #bref("Ps 115:15"); #he[בְּרוּכִים לַיהוָֹה];, see #xref(<p121f>)[§ 121#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];); cf. #bref("Isa 53:4");, #bref("Ps 22:7");, #bref("Job 14:1"); (15:14, 25:4); hence also with noun\-suffixes (which are accordingly genitive) #bref("Prov 9:18"); #he[קְרֻאֶ֫יהָ]; #emph[her invited ones];, i.e. those invited by her; cf. 7:26, #bref("Ps 37:22");.

#ml("m") <p116m> #strong[5.]; The use of the participle as predicate is very frequent in noun\-clauses (which, according to #xref(<p140e>)[§ 140#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];, describe established facts and states), in which the period of time intended by the description must again (see above, #emph[d];) be inferred from the context. Thus:

#ml("n") <p116n> (#emph[a];) As #emph[present];, in speaking of truths which hold good at all times, e.g. #bref("Eccl 1:4"); #he[דּוֹר הֹלֵךְ וְדוֹר בָּא]; #emph[one generation goeth, and another generation cometh; and the earth abideth]; (#he[עֹמָ֫דֶת];) #emph[for ever];; cf. verse 7; also to represent incidental (continuous) occurrences which are just happening, #bref("Gen 3:5");, #bref("16:8"); (#emph[I am fleeing];); 32:12, #bref("Exod 9:17");, #bref("1 Sam 16:15");, #bref("23:1");, #bref("2 Kgs 7:9");, #bref("Isa 1:7");; when the subject is introduced by the emphatic demonstrative #he[הִנֵּה]; #emph[behold!]; (#xref(<p100o>)[§ 100#h(0.18em)#emph[o];]; and #xref(<p105b>)[§ 105#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];), e.g. #bref("Gen 16:11"); #he[הִנָּךְ הָרָה]; #emph[behold, thou art with child];, etc.; 27:42; frequently also in circumstantial clauses (connected by #emph[Wāw];), cf. #xref(<p141e>)[§ 141#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];, e.g. #bref("Gen 15:2");, etc.

#ml("o") <p116o> (#emph[b];) To represent #emph[past]; actions or states, sometimes in independent noun\-clauses, e.g. #bref("Exod 20:18"); #he[וְכָל־הָעָם רֹאִים אֶת־הַקּוֹלֹת]; #emph[and all the people saw the thunderings];, etc.; #bref("1 Kgs 1:5");; in negative statements, e.g. #bref("Gen 39:23");#super[ #emph[a];];; sometimes in relative clauses, e.g. #bref("Gen 39:23");#super[ #emph[b];];, #bref("Deut 3:2"); (cf. also the frequent combination of the participle with the article as the equivalent of a relative clause, e.g. #bref("Gen 32:10"); #he[הָֽאֹמֵר]; #emph[which saidst];; 12:7, 16:13, 35:1, 3, 36:35, 48:16, #bref("2 Sam 15:31");, etc.); sometimes again (see #emph[n];) in #emph[circumstantial]; clauses, especially those representing actions or states which occurred simultaneously with other past actions, etc., e.g. #bref("Gen 19:1"); #emph[and the two angels came to Sodom]; #he[וְלוֹט יֹשֵׁב]; #emph[and]; (i.e. #emph[while];) #emph[Lot sat];, etc.; 18:1, 8, 16, 22, 25:26, #bref("Judg 13:9");, #bref("2 Chr 22:9");; also with the subject introduced by #he[הִנֵּה]; 37:7, 41:17. (On #he[הֹלֵךְ]; with a following adjective or participle to express an action constantly or occasionally recurring, cf. #xref(<p113u>)[§ 113#h(0.18em)#emph[u];];.)

#ml("p") <p116p> (#emph[c];) To announce #emph[future]; actions or events, e.g. #bref("1 Kgs 2:2");, #bref("2 Kgs 4:16"); #emph[at this season when the time cometh round];, #he[אַתְּ חֹבֶ֫קֶת בֵּן]; #emph[thou shalt embrace a son];; so after a specification of time, #bref("Gen 7:4");, #bref("15:14");, #bref("17:19");, #bref("19:13");, #bref("Hag 2:6"); (but in #bref("Isa 23:15");, where, after #he[וְהָיָה]; we should rather expect a perfect consecutive, it is better to explain #origpage("360") <orig-360>;#he[וְנִשְׁכַּ֫חַת];, with Qimḥi, as the 3rd sing. fem. of the perfect; on the form, cf. #xref(<p44f>)[§ 44#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];); or in relative clauses, #bref("Gen 41:25");, #bref("Isa 5:5"); #emph[what I am doing];, i.e. am in the act of doing; in a deliberative question, #bref("Gen 37:30");; but especially often when the subject is introduced by #he[הִנֵּה]; (especially also if the subject be attached as a suffix to #he[הִנֵּה]; as #he[הִנְנִי];, #he[הִנְּךָ];, etc.), if it is intended to announce the event as imminent, or at least near at hand (and sure to happen), when it is called #emph[futurum instans];, e.g. #bref("Gen 6:17");, #bref("15:3");, #bref("20:3");, #bref("24:13"); f., 48:21, 50:5, #bref("Exod 3:13");, #bref("8:25");, #bref("9:3");, #bref("34:10");, #bref("Josh 2:18");, #bref("Judg 7:17");, #bref("9:33");, #bref("1 Sam 3:11");, #bref("2 Kgs 7:2");, #bref("Isa 3:1");, #bref("7:14");, #bref("17:1");, #bref("Jer 30:10");, #bref("Zech 2:13");, #bref("3:8");; with a participle passive, #bref("2 Sam 20:21");: cf. also #xref(<p112t>)[§ 112#h(0.18em)#emph[t];];.

#ml("q") <p116q> Rem. 1. As the above examples show, a noun\-clause with a participle as predicate may have for its subject either a substantive or a personal pronoun; in both cases the participle, especially if there be a certain emphasis upon it, may precede the subject. Also in noun\-clauses introduced by #he[הִנֵּה]; the subject may be either a substantive, or (e.g. #bref("Gen 37:7");) a separate personal pronoun, or a suffix attached to #he[הִנֵּה];. In the same way, the subject may also be introduced by #he[יֵשׁ]; (#emph[est];, see the Lexicon) with a suffix, and in negative sentences by #he[אֵין]; (#emph[non est];) with a suffix, e.g. #bref("Judg 6:36"); #he[אִם־יֶשְׁךָ מוֹשִׁיעַ]; #emph[if thou wilt save];; #bref("Gen 43:5"); #he[אִם־אֵֽינְךָ מְשַׁלֵּחַ]; #emph[if thou wilt not send];; #bref("1 Sam 19:11");.—In such cases as #bref("Isa 14:27"); #he[יָדוֹ הַנְּטוּיָה]; #emph[the stretched out hand is his];, #he[הַנְּטוּיָה]; is not, like #he[נְטוּיָה]; in 9:11, 16, etc., the predicate (in which case the participle could not take the article), but the subject; cf. #bref("Gen 2:11");, #bref("45:12");, #bref("Isa 66:9");, #bref("Ezek 20:29");, #bref("Zech 7:6"); (cf. #xref(<p126k>)[§ 126#h(0.18em)#emph[k];];), where the participle with the article likewise refers to the present, also #bref("Num 7:2");, #bref("Deut 3:21");, #bref("4:3");, etc., #bref("1 Sam 4:16");, where it refers to the past. In #bref("1 Kgs 12:8"); and 21:11 even in relative clauses after #he[אֲשֶׁר];.

#ml("r") <p116r> 2. To give express emphasis to an action continuing in the #emph[past];, the perfect #he[הָיָה]; in the corresponding person is sometimes added to the participle, and similarly the imperfect #he[יִֽהְיֶה]; (or the jussive #he[יְהִי];, or the imperfect consecutive) is used to emphasize an action continuing in the #emph[future];, e.g. #bref("Job 1:14"); #he[הַבָּקָר הָיוּ חֹֽרְשׁוֹת]; #emph[the oxen]; (cows) #emph[were plowing];; #bref("Gen 37:2");, #bref("39:22");, #bref("Exod 3:1");, #bref("Deut 9:24");, #bref("Judg 1:7");, #bref("1 Sam 2:11");, #bref("2 Sam 3:6");; the same occurs with a passive participle, e.g. #bref("Josh 5:5");, #bref("Zech 3:3");; #he[יִֽהְיֶה]; with a participle is found e.g. in #bref("Isa 2:2");; the jussive in #bref("Gen 1:6");, #bref("Ps 109:12");;#footnote[A jussive is practically to be supplied also in the formulae of blessing and cursing, #he[בָּרוּךְ]; #emph[blessed be ...]; #bref("Gen 9:26");, etc.; #he[אָרוּר]; #emph[cursed art thou]; ... 3:14, etc.]; and #he[ויהי]; with a participle in #bref("Judg 16:21");, #bref("Neh 1:4");.

#ml("s") <p116s> 3. The #emph[personal pronoun]; which would be expected as the subject of a participial clause is frequently omitted, or at least (as elsewhere in noun\-clauses, cf. #bref("Isa 26:3");, #bref("Ps 16:8");, #bref("Job 9:32");) the pronoun of the 3rd pers. #he[הוּא];, e.g. #bref("Gen 24:30");, #bref("37:15");, #bref("38:24");, #bref("41:1");, #bref("1 Sam 10:11");, #bref("15:12");, #bref("Isa 29:8"); (the participle always after #he[הִנֵּה];); cf., moreover, #bref("Gen 32:7");, #bref("Deut 33:3");, #bref("1 Sam 17:25");, #bref("20:1");, #bref("Isa 33:5");, #bref("40:19");, #bref("Ps 22:29");, #bref("33:5");, #bref("55:20");, #bref("Job 12:17");, 19 ff., 25:2, 26:7.—#he[הִיא]; is omitted in #bref("Lev 18:28");; #he[הֵ֫מָּה]; in #bref("Isa 32:12");, #bref("Ezek 8:12");, #bref("Neh 9:3");; in a relative clause, #bref("Gen 39:22");, #bref("Isa 24:2");.—The personal pronoun of the 2nd pers. masc. (#he[אַתָּה];) is omitted in #bref("Hab 2:10");; the 2nd fem. (#he[אַתְּ];) in #bref("Gen 20:16"); (where, however, for the participle #he[וְנֹכַ֫חַת]; the 2nd fem. perf. #he[וְנֹכַ֫חַתְּ]; is to be read); the pronoun of the 1st sing. in #bref("Hab 1:5"); (?), #bref("Zech 9:12");, #bref("Mal 2:16");; the 2nd plur. (#he[אַתֶּם];) #bref("1 Sam 2:24"); (if the text be right), 6:3, #bref("Ezek 13:7"); (?). But these passages are all more or less doubtful.

#ml("t") <p116t> Of a different kind are the cases in which some undefined subject is to be supplied with the participle; e.g. #bref("Isa 21:11"); #he[אֵלַי קֹרֵא]; #emph[there is one calling unto me]; (\= one calleth; #xref(<p144d>)[§ 144#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];); cf. #bref("Isa 30:24");, #bref("33:4");.—So with participles in the plur., e.g. #bref("Exod 5:16"); (#he[אֹֽמְרִים]; sc. the taskmasters); #bref("Jer 38:23"); (in 33:5 the text is corrupt), #bref("Ezek 13:7"); (?), 36:13, 37:11 (equivalent to #emph[sunt qui dicant];). #origpage("361") <orig-361>;

#ml("u") <p116u> 4. We must mention as a special class those noun\-clauses which occur at the beginning of a period, and are intended to lay stress upon the fact that the first action still continues on the occurrence of the second (always introduced by #he[וְ];); e.g. #bref("Job 1:16"); f. #he[עוֹד זֶה מְדַבֵּר וְזֶה בָא]; #emph[he was yet speaking, and]; (\=#emph[when];) #emph[another came];, etc.#footnote[The independent noun\-clause here lays stress upon the simultaneous occurrence (and consequently the overlapping) of the events far more forcibly than could be done by a subordinate expression of time (as e.g. #he[וַיְהִי בְדַבְּרוֹ];). In English it may be represented by #emph[scarcely had he finished speaking when];. ... As the above examples show, the apodosis also frequently consists of a noun\-clause.];; cf. #bref("Gen 29:9");, #bref("1 Sam 9:11, 27");, #bref("20:36");, #bref("1 Kgs 14:17"); #emph[she was entering the threshold of the house, when the child died];; #bref("2 Kgs 2:23");, #bref("4:5");, #bref("Dan 9:20"); f.; also in #bref("Judg 19:22");, #bref("1 Sam 9:14");, #bref("17:23");, #bref("1 Kgs 1:42");, #bref("Job 1:18"); f., in all which passages the apodosis is introduced by #he[וְהִנֵּה];.—On the other hand, in #bref("1 Kgs 1:14"); the noun\-clause itself is introduced by #he[הִנֵּה]; (as in verse 22 by #he[וְהִנֵּה];), and denotes an action only just impending.#footnote[At the same time the preceding #he[עוֹד]; #emph[still]; shows that what is announced is not merely a future event, but a future event #emph[contemporaneous]; with something else; the case thus entirely differs from the examples given in #xref(<p112t>)[§ 112#h(0.18em)#emph[t];];, where #he[הִנֵּה]; refers to the following participle, while here it belongs properly to the apodosis, before which it is therefore generally placed; see the examples.]; Finally, when the whole sentence is introduced by means of #he[וַיְהִי]; (cf. #xref(<p111g>)[§ 111#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];), and the apodosis by #he[וְהִנֵּה];, #bref("Gen 42:35");, #bref("2 Kgs 2:11");, #bref("13:21");; without #he[הִנֵּה]; in the apodosis, #bref("1 Sam 7:10");, #bref("2 Kgs 19:37"); (#bref("Isa 37:38");).

#ml("v") <p116v> #emph[Participles active];, which are used in the sense of the perfect participle, and also #emph[participles passive];, in accordance with their meaning, express in such noun\-clauses a state still continuing on the occurrence of the principal action, e.g. #bref("Gen 38:25"); #he[הִוא מוּצֵאת וְהִיא שָֽׁלְחָה]; #emph[she was being brought forth, when she sent];, etc.; cf. #bref("Gen 50:24");. \[See further in Driver, #emph[Tenses];, §§ 166–169.\]

#ml("w") <p116w> 5. Different from the examples treated in #emph[u]; and #emph[v]; are the instances in which a participle (either alone or as the attribute of a noun) stands at the beginning of the sentence as a #emph[casus pendens]; (or as the subject of a #emph[compound noun\-clause];, see #xref(<p143c>)[§ 143#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];) to indicate a condition, the contingent occurrence of which involves a further consequence; e.g. #bref("Gen 9:6"); #he[שֹׁפֵךְ דַּם הָֽאָדָם בָּֽאָדָם דָּמוֹ יִשָּׁפֵךְ]; #emph[shedding man’s blood];, i.e. if any one sheddeth man’s blood, #emph[by man shall his blood be shed];; #bref("Exod 21:12");, #bref("Ps 75:4");, #bref("Prov 17:14");, #bref("Job 41:18");; so especially if #he[כָּל־]; #emph[every]; precedes the participle, #bref("Gen 4:15");, #bref("1 Sam 3:11"); (#bref("2 Kgs 21:12");), #bref("2 Sam 5:8"); (#emph[whosoever smiteth];), #bref("1 Chr 11:6");. The apodosis is very often introduced by #he[וְ]; (#emph[wāw apodosis];), e.g. #bref("Exod 12:15"); (with a following perfect consecutive), #bref("Num 35:30");; #bref("1 Sam 2:13"); #he[כָּל־אִישׁ זֹבֵחַ זֶ֫בַח וּבָא נַ֫עַר הַכֹּהֵן]; #emph[when any man offered sacrifice, the priest’s servant came];, etc.; #bref("2 Sam 14:10"); (participle with article); 22:41 (where, however, the text is to be emended in accordance with #bref("Ps 18:41");); #bref("2 Sam 23:3"); f., #bref("Prov 23:24"); #emph[K#super[e];th.];; 29:9.—As in the instances discussed under #emph[u];, such sentences are sometimes preceded by #he[וַיְהִי];, cf. #bref("1 Sam 10:11");, #bref("11:11");, #bref("2 Sam 2:23"); #he[וַיְהִי כָּל־הַבָּא]; #emph[and it came to pass, that as many as came];, etc. \[or by #he[וְהָיָה];, frequentative, #bref("Judg 19:30");\].—On the other hand, #he[וְהַנִּשְׁבֶּ֫רֶת]; #bref("Dan 8:22"); is a mere catchword (equivalent to #emph[and as for that which was broken];) to call to mind the contents of verse 8.

6\. On the use of the participle after the infinitive absolute #he[הָלוֹךְ]; cf. #xref(<p113u>)[§ 113#h(0.18em)#emph[u];];.

#ml("x") <p116x> 7. Almost as a rule the participial construction beginning a sentence (like the infinitival constructions according to #xref(<p114r>)[§ 114#h(0.18em)#emph[r];];) is continued by means of a finite verb with or without #he[וְ];, before which the English construction requires us to supply the relative pronoun implied in the participle; thus, #origpage("362") <orig-362>;continued by means of a perfect, #bref("Isa 14:17"); #he[שָׂם תֵּבֵל כַּמִּדְבָּר וְעָרָיו הָרָ֑ס]; #emph[that made the world as a wilderness, and overthrew the cities thereof];#footnote[On the parallelism between the #emph[external]; and #emph[internal]; members, which appears here and in many other examples of this kind, see the note on #xref(<p114r>)[§ 114#h(0.18em)#emph[r];];.];; 43:7, #bref("Ezek 22:3");, #bref("Ps 136:13"); ff., #bref("Prov 2:17");; by a perfect without #emph[Wāw];, #bref("Gen 49:11");; by a simple imperfect (as the #emph[modus rei repetitae]; in the present), #bref("Isa 5:23");, #bref("46:6");, #bref("Prov 7:8");, #bref("Job 12:17");, 19 ff., 24:21; by an imperfect without #emph[Wāw];, e.g. #bref("1 Sam 2:8");, #bref("Isa 5:8");, #bref("Prov 2:14");, #bref("19:26");; by an imperfect consecutive, #bref("Gen 27:33");, #bref("35:3");, #bref("1 Sam 2:6");, #bref("Jer 13:10"); (after several participles), #bref("Ps 18:33");, #bref("136:10");f.

#bookhead(3, [C. The Government of the Verb], note: []);

#sec("117", [The Direct Subordination of the Noun to the Verb as Accusative of the Object. The Double Accusative], note: []) <s117>

#align(center)[L. Kaila, #emph[Zur Syntax des in verbaler Abhängigkeit stehenden Nomens im alttest. Hebr.];, Helsingfors, 1906.];

#ml("a") <p117a> #strong[1.]; The simplest way in which a noun is subordinated to a verbal form is by the addition of an accusative of the object to a transitive verb.#footnote[The verb in question may either have been originally transitive, or only have become transitive by a modification of its original meaning. Thus the vocalization shows that #he[חָפֵץ]; (#emph[to have pleasure];, usually with #he[בְּ];) #emph[to desire];, #he[מָלֵא]; (#emph[to be full]; of something, also transitive) #emph[to fill];, were originally intransitive. Cf. also such cases as #he[בָּכָה]; #emph[to weep]; (generally with #he[עַל־];, #he[אֶל־]; or #he[לְ];), but also #emph[to bewail]; with an accusative; #he[יָשַׁב]; #emph[to dwell]; (usually with #he[בְּ];), but also #emph[to inhabit]; with an accusative (cf. further, under #emph[u];).—The examples are different in which verbs of motion such as #he[בּוֹא]; #emph[intrare];, also #emph[aggredi];, #he[יָצָא]; #emph[egredi]; (cf. #xref(<p116h>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[h];]; above), #he[שׁוּב]; #emph[redire];, #bref("Isa 52:8");, take an accusative of the aim of the motion, while #he[בּוֹא]; according to the Old Semitic usage, even takes an accusative of the person (at least in poetry, equivalent to #he[בּוֹא אֶל־]; in prose).]; In the absence of case\-endings,#footnote[On traces of these endings, especially the remains of a former accusative ending in #emph[a];, cf. #xref(<p90c>)[§ 90#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];.]; this accusative can now be recognized only from the context, or by the particle #he[אֶת־]; (#he[אֵת];, before suffixes also #he[אֹת];, #he[אוֹת];)#footnote[#he[אֶת־]; (toneless owing to the following Maqqeph), and #he[אֵת]; (with a tone\-long #emph[ē];, #he[אֵֽת־]; only in #bref("Job 41:26");), #he[אֹת]; or #he[אוֹת]; before the light suffixes (on all these forms cf. #xref(<p103b>)[§ 103#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];: the underlying form #emph[āth]; was obscured in Hebrew to #emph[ôth];, shortened to #emph[ăth]; before suffixes beginning with a consonant and then modified to #he[אֶת־];, whence finally the secondary form #he[אֵת]; with the tone), Phoenician #he[אית]; i.e. probably #emph[iyyāth]; (for the Phoenician form, cf. G. Hoffmann, #emph[Einige phönik. Inschriften];, Göttingen, 1889, p. 39 f.), Punic #emph[yth]; or (according to Euting) pronounced even as a mere prefixed #emph[t];, Arabic, before suffixes, #emph[ʾiyyâ];, Aram. #he[יָת];, #he[יַת];. It was no doubt originally a substantive, meaning #emph[essence, substance, self]; (like the Syriac #emph[yāth];; on the other hand, any connection with the Hebrew #he[אוֹת];, Syriac #emph[ʾātā];, Arabic #emph[ʾāyat];, #emph[a sign];, must, with Nöldeke, #emph[ZDMG.]; xl. 738, be rejected), but now united in the construct state with a following noun or suffix stands for the pronoun #emph[ipse];, αὐτός. In common use, however (cf. Wilson, ‘The particle #he[את]; in Hebrew,’ #emph[Hebraica];, vi. 2, 3, and the precise statistics of the use of #he[את]; on p. 140 ff.), it has so little force (like the oblique cases αὐτοῦ, αὐτῷ, αὐτόν, sometimes also #emph[ipsius, ipsum];, and the Germ. #emph[desselben];, etc.) that it merely serves to introduce a determinate object; #he[אֵת הַשָּׁמַ֫יִם]; prop. αὐτὸν τὸν οὐρανόν (cf. αὐτὴν Χρυσηΐδα, Iliad i. 143) is no stronger than the simple #he[הַשָּׁמַ֫יִם]; τὸν οὐρανόν. Cf., further, P. Haupt on #bref("Prov 18:24"); in his Rainbow Bible, and also in the #emph[Notes on Esther];, p. 191.]; prefixed to it. The use of this #emph[nota accusativi]; #origpage("363") <orig-363>;is, however, somewhat rare in poetry, and even in prose it is not invariably necessary but is restricted to those cases in which the accusative of the object is more closely determined by being a proper name, or by having the article, or by a following determinate genitive (hence also by the suffixes), or in some other way (see below, #emph[c];), e.g. #bref("Gen 4:1"); #emph[and she bare]; #he[אֶת־קַ֫יִן]; #emph[Cain];; #bref("6:10");, #bref("1:1"); #emph[God created]; #he[אֵת הַשָּׁמַ֫יִם וְאֵת הָאָ֫רֶץ]; #emph[the heaven and the earth]; (but #bref("2:4"); #he[אֶ֫רֶץ וְשָׁמַ֫יִם];); #bref("1:25"); #emph[and God made]; #he[אֶת־חַיַּת הָאָ֫רֶץ]; #emph[the beast of the earth];; #bref("2:24");.

#ml("b") <p117b> Rem. 1. The rare occurrence of the #emph[nota accusativi]; in poetic style (e.g. it never occurs in #bref("Exod 15:2–18");, #bref("Deut 32");, #bref("Judg 5");, #bref("1 Sam 2");, etc., though it is frequent in the late Psalms) may be explained from the fact that in this as in other respects (cf. #xref(<p2q>)[§ 2#h(0.18em)#emph[q];];) poetry represents a somewhat more archaic stage of the language than prose. The need of some external means of indicating the accusative could only have been felt after the case\-endings had become wholly extinct. Even then the #he[את]; would probably have been used at first to indicate only an object placed #emph[before]; the verb (when it followed, it was already sufficiently characterized by its position as depending on the verb), or proper names.#footnote[Thus, in Dt 33, #he[את]; occurs only in verse 9 (twice, with an object preceding the verb), in Gn 49 in the blessing of Jacob only in verse 15 with a co\-ordinate second object (consequently farther removed from the verb). Of the thirteen instances of #he[את]; in the #emph[Mêšaʿ]; inscription, seven stand directly and four indirectly before proper names.]; Finally, however, the #emph[nota accusativi]; became so customary everywhere in prose, that even the pronominal object was expressed rather by #he[את]; with suffixes than by verbal suffixes, even when none of the reasons mentioned under #emph[e]; can be assigned for it; cf. Giesebrecht in #emph[ZAW.]; 1881, p. 258 ff., and the statistics of H. Petri, cited above at the head of § 58. Such examples as #he[כְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר צִוָּה אֹתוֹ אֱלֹהִים]; #bref("Gen 6:22"); in the #emph[Priestly Code];, beside #he[בְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר־צִוָּ֫הוּ יְהֹוָה]; 7:5 in the #emph[Jahvist];, are especially instructive.

#ml("c") <p117c> 2. As accusatives determined in other ways, we have in the first place to consider the collectives introduced by #he[כֹּל]; #emph[entirety];, without a following article or determinate genitive, inasmuch as the meaning of #he[כֹּל]; includes a determinative sense, cf. e.g. #bref("Gen 1:21");, 30, 8:21, #bref("Deut 2:34");, #bref("2 Kgs 25:9");. #he[אֶת־כֹּל]; is used absolutely in #bref("Gen 9:3");, cf. 39:23; similarly, #he[מִי]; is determinate of itself, since it always denotes a person, hence #he[אֶת־מִי]; #emph[quem?]; e.g. #bref("Isa 6:8");, #bref("37:23");, etc., but never #he[אֶת־מָה]; #emph[quid?]; So also the relative #he[אֲשֶׁר]; in the sense of #emph[eum qui]; or #emph[quem];, etc., e.g. #bref("1 Sam 16:3");, or #emph[id quod];, #bref("Gen 9:24");, etc. Cf. also such examples as #bref("Josh 2:10");, #bref("1 Sam 24:19");, where #he[אֵת אֲשֶׁר]; is equivalent to #emph[the circumstance, that];, etc.—Elsewhere #he[אֵת]; stands before nouns which are determinate in sense, although the article is omitted, which according to #xref(<p126h>)[§ 126#h(0.18em)#emph[h];]; is very frequently the case in poetic or otherwise elevated style; thus #bref("Lev 26:5");, #bref("Josh 24:14");, 15, #bref("Isa 41:7"); (to distinguish the object from the subject); 50:4 (with the first of two accusatives, also for the sake of clearness); #bref("Ezek 13:20");, #bref("43:10");, #bref("Prov 13:21"); (where the #he[צַדִּיקִים]; are to be regarded as a distinct class); #bref("Job 13:25"); #origpage("364") <orig-364>;(unless, with Beer and others, we read #he[וְאִם]; for #he[וְאֶת־];); also #bref("Eccl 7:7"); may be a quotation of an ancient maxim.

#ml("d") <p117d> On the other hand #he[אֵת]; occurs very seldom in prose before a noun actually or apparently undetermined. In #bref("1 Sam 24:6"); #he[כָּנָף]; is more closely defined by means of the following relative clause; in #bref("2 Sam 4:11"); #he[אִישׁ צַדִּיק]; refers to Ishbosheth (as if it were #emph[him, who was an innocent man];); in #bref("1 Kgs 6:16"); #he[עֶשְׂרִים אַמָּה]; refers to the particular twenty cubits. In #bref("Exod 21:28"); (otherwise in verse 29) perhaps the #he[אֶת־]; is used in order to avoid the combination #he[שׁוֹר אִישׁ]; (as in #bref("Num 21:9"); to avoid the cacophony #he[נָשַׁךְ הַנָּחָשׁ אִישׁ];?); in #bref("Lev 7:8"); and 20:10 the accusatives are at any rate defined by the context.—In #bref("Num 16:15"); #he[אֶת־אַחַד מֵהֶם]; probably means #emph[even a single one]; (and then #emph[ipso facto]; a definite one) #emph[of them];, as also in #bref("1 Sam 9:3"); #he[אֶת־אַחַד מֵֽהַנְּעָרִים]; may refer to some definite one of the men\-servants. In #bref("Gen 21:30"); we should read #he[אֶת־שֶׁ֫בַע הַכְּבָשׂת]; with the Samaritan, since the seven lambs have been already mentioned; in #bref("Exod 2:1"); translate with Meyer, #emph[Die Israeliten];, p. 79, #he[אֶת־בַּת־לֵוִי]; #emph[the daughter of Levi];; in #bref("Exod 28:9"); read #he[הַשֹּׁ֫הַם]; with the Samaritan; in #bref("Lev 20:14"); #he[אֶת־אִשָּׁה]; is probably a scribal error due to #he[וְאֶת־אִמָּה];; in #bref("1 Sam 26:20"); read #he[נַפְשִׁי]; with the LXX for #he[פַּרְעשׁ אֶחָד];; in #bref("2 Sam 5:24"); read #he[הַצְּעָדָה]; as in #bref("1 Chr 14:15");; in #bref("2 Sam 15:16"); the #he[אֶת־]; is incorrectly inserted from 20:3, where it refers to the women already mentioned; in #bref("2 Sam 18:18"); read #he[הַמַּצֶּ֫בֶת];, or omit both #he[אֶת־]; and #he[אֲשֶׁר]; with the LXX and Lucian; in #bref("1 Kgs 12:31"); omit #he[אֶת־];; in #bref("2 Kgs 23:20"); probably #he[אֶת־עַצְמוֹתָם]; is to be read; in #bref("2 Kgs 25:9"); the text is corrupt. In #bref("Ezek 16:32"); #he[אֶת־זָרִים]; might refer to #emph[the strangers in question];; but see Smend on the passage.

#ml("e") <p117e> 3. The pronominal object #emph[must]; be represented by #he[את]; with a suffix (instead of a verbal suffix), when (#emph[a];) it precedes the verb, e.g. #bref("Num 22:33"); #he[אֹֽתְכָה הָרַ֫גְתִּי וְאוֹתָהּ הֶֽחֱיֵ֫יתִי]; #emph[I had slain thee and saved her alive];; #bref("Gen 7:1");, #bref("Lev 22:28");, #bref("1 Sam 8:7");, #bref("Isa 43:22");, #bref("57:11");, #bref("Jer 4:17");, 22, 7:19; (#emph[b];) when a suffix is already attached to the verb, and as a rule when a second accusative with #he[וְ]; follows, e.g. #bref("2 Sam 15:25"); #he[וְהִרְאַ֫נִי אֹתוֹ]; #emph[and he will show me it];; #bref("Exod 17:3"); #he[לְהָמִית אֹתִי וְאֶת־בָּנַי]; #emph[to kill us and our children];; #bref("Num 16:32");, #bref("1 Sam 5:11");, #bref("2 Sam 14:16"); (but cf. also #bref("Deut 11:6");, #bref("15:16");, etc., and Driver on #bref("1 Sam 5:10");); (#emph[c];) after an infinitive absolute, see above #xref(<p113a>)[§ 113#h(0.18em)#emph[a];]; note; (#emph[d];) after an infinitive construct, when it is immediately followed by the subject, e.g. #bref("Gen 41:39");, or when the combination of a suffix with the infinitive might lead to a misunderstanding, e.g. #bref("Gen 4:15"); #he[לְבִלְתִּי הַכּֽוֹת־אֹתוֹ]; #emph[lest one should smite him];, etc., where #he[לְבִלְתִּי הַכּוֹתוֹ]; might also mean #emph[lest he should smite];.

#ml("f") <p117f> 4. The pronominal object is very frequently omitted, when it can be easily supplied from the context; so especially the neuter accusative referring to something previously mentioned (the English #emph[it];) after #emph[verba sentiendi]; (#he[שָׁמַע];) and #emph[dicendi];, e.g. #bref("Gen 9:22");, etc., #he[וַיַּגֵּד]; #emph[and he told]; (it); also after #he[נָתַן]; #emph[to give];, #bref("Gen 18:7");, #bref("24:41");, etc., #he[לָקַח]; #emph[to take];, #he[הֵבִיא]; #emph[to bring];, #he[שִׂים]; #emph[to lay];, #bref("Gen 9:23");, etc., #he[מָצָא]; #emph[to find];, #bref("Gen 31:33");, etc. A personal object is omitted, e.g. in #bref("Gen 12:19");, #bref("24:51"); (after #he[לָקַח];.—The omission of the plural object is remarkable, because it leaves an opportunity for a misunderstanding, in #bref("Gen 37:17"); #he[שָׁמַ֫עְתִּי אֹֽמְרִים];#footnote[According to the ordinary rules of syntax (cf. #xref(<p116t>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[t];];) we should translate, #emph[I heard men who said];, etc.]; #emph[I heard]; them #emph[saying];; perhaps, however, we should read #he[שְׁמַעְתִּים]; with the Samaritan.

#ml("g") <p117g> 5. In common formulae the substantival object is also sometimes omitted #origpage("365") <orig-365>;(an elliptical expression); thus e.g. #he[כָּרַת]; #bref("1 Sam 20:16");, etc. (see the Lexicon) stands for #he[כָּרַת בְּרִית]; like the English #emph[to close]; (sc. a bargain) with any one; #he[נָטַר]; #emph[to keep]; (sc. #he[אַף]; #emph[anger];) equivalent to #emph[to be resentful];, #bref("Ps 103:9");, etc.; so also #he[שָׁמַר]; #bref("Jer 3:5"); (beside #he[נָטַר];); #he[נָשָׂא]; for #he[נָשָׂא קוֹל]; #emph[to lift up the voice];, #bref("Isa 3:7");; #he[נָשָׂא לְ]; for #he[נָשָׂא עָוֹן לְ]; #emph[to take away any one’s sin]; (to forgive), #bref("Gen 18:24");, 26, #bref("Isa 2:9");; #he[שָׁלַח]; #emph[to put forth]; (sc. #he[יָד]; #emph[the hand];) equivalent to #emph[to reach after something];, #bref("2 Sam 6:6");, #bref("Ps 18:17");.

#ml("h") <p117h> 6. #emph[Verba sentiendi]; may take a second object, generally in the form of a participle or adjective and necessarily indeterminate, to define more exactly the action or state in which the object is perceived, e.g. #bref("Num 11:10"); #he[וַיִּשְׁמַע משֶׁה אֶת־הָעָם בֹּכֶה]; #emph[and Moses heard the people weeping];; #bref("Gen 7:1"); #he[אֽתְךָ רָאִ֫יתִי צַדִּיק]; #emph[thee have I seen righteous];. Frequently, however, the second object is expressed by a separate clause. This is especially frequent with #he[רָאָה]; #emph[to see];, e.g. #bref("Gen 1:4"); #emph[and God saw the light, that it was good];; #bref("Gen 6:2");, #bref("12:14");, #bref("13:10");, #bref("49:15");, #bref("Exod 2:2");, #bref("Ps 25:19");, #bref("Prov 23:31");, #bref("Job 22:12");, #bref("Eccl 2:24");, #bref("8:17");; so with #he[יָדַע]; #emph[to know];, #bref("Exod 32:22");, #bref("2 Sam 3:25");, #bref("17:8"); (with two objects); #bref("1 Kgs 5:17");.

#ml("i") <p117i> 7. In certain instances #he[את]; serves apparently to introduce or to emphasize a nominative. This cannot be regarded as a reappearance of the original substantival meaning of the #he[את];, since all unquestionable examples of the kind belong to the later Books of the Old Testament. They are rather (apart from textual errors or other explanations) cases of virtual dependence on an implied #emph[verbum regens]; understood. The constant use of #he[את]; to indicate a clause governed by the verb, necessarily led at length to the use of #he[את]; generally as a defining particle irrespective of a governing verb. So in the Hebrew of the Mishna#footnote[Cf. Weiss, #he[משפט לשון המשנה]; (Vienna, 1867), p. 112.]; (see above, #xref(<p3a>)[§ 3#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];) #he[אֹתוֹ]; and #he[אֹתָהּ]; are prefixed even to a nominative without any special emphasis.

#ml("k") <p117k> Naturally the above does not apply to any of the places in which #he[את]; is not the #emph[nota accusativi];, but a preposition (on #he[את]; #emph[with];, cf. #xref(<p103b>)[§ 103#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];), e.g. #bref("Isa 57:15");, #bref("1 Sam 17:34"); (#he[וְאֶת־הַדּוֹב]; #emph[and that, with a bear];; #he[אֶת־]; here, however, has probably been interpolated from verse 36, where it is wanting); nor the places in which the accusative is subordinate to a passive (according to #xref(<p121c>)[§ 121#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];) or to a verb of wanting as in #bref("Josh 22:17"); and #bref("Neh 9:32");, see below, #emph[z];. In #bref("Ezek 43:17"); #he[סָבִיב]; #emph[about]; governs like a verb, being followed by #he[אוֹתָהּ];.

#ml("l") <p117l> Other cases are clearly due to attraction to a following relative pronoun in the accusative (#bref("Ezek 14:22");, #bref("Zech 8:17");; but #bref("Hag 2:5");a, to #he[ממצרים];, must be omitted, with the LXX, as a later addition), or the accusative depends on a verbal idea, virtually contained in what has gone before, and consequently present to the speaker’s mind as governing the accusative. Thus #bref("Num 3:26"); (the verbal idea contained in #he[ומשמרת]; verse 25 is #emph[they had to take charge of];); in #bref("Josh 17:11"); #he[ויהי לְ]; implies #emph[it was given up]; or #emph[they gave him];; #bref("1 Sam 26:16"); #emph[see where]; is equivalent to #emph[search now for];; in #bref("2 Sam 11:25"); #he[אל־ירע בעיניך]; is used in the sense of #emph[noli aegre ferre];#footnote[So also in #bref("1 Sam 20:13"); the Qal (#he[יִיטַב];) is, with Wellhausen, to be read instead of the #emph[Hiphʿîl];.];; #bref("Jer 36:33"); #emph[and]; he had #emph[the brazier before him];; in #bref("Eccl 4:3"); a verb like #emph[I esteem]; is mentally supplied before #he[אֵת אֲשֶׁר];. On #bref("Josh 22:17");, #bref("Neh 9:32");, see below, #emph[aa];.—Aposiopesis occurs in #bref("Deut 11:2"); #emph[for not your children]; (do I mean); still more boldly in #bref("Zech 7:7");, where either #he[שְׁמַעְתֶּם]; or #he[תִּשְׁמְעוּ]; (#he[תַּֽעֲשׂוּ];) is to be supplied.

#ml("m") <p117m> Setting aside a few undoubtedly corrupt passages#footnote[Thus #bref("1 Sam 26:16");, where #he[וְאֵי]; is to be read for #he[וְאֶת];; #bref("1 Kgs 11:25");, where at present the predicate of the relative clause is wanting; in #bref("2 Kgs 6:5"); the #he[את]; is probably derived from a text which read the #emph[Hiphʿîl]; instead of #he[נָפַל];. In #bref("Jer 23:33"); instead of the artificial explanation #emph[what a burden]; (is, do ye ask?) we should read with the LXX and Vulg. #he[אַתֶּם הַמַּשָּׂא]; #emph[ye are the burden];. In #bref("Ezek 10:22"); #he[מַרְאֵיהֶם וְאוֹתָם]; is unintelligible; in 37:19 read with Hitzig #he[אֶל־]; for #he[את];; in #bref("Hag 2:17"); for #he[אתכם]; read with the LXX #he[שֻֽׁבְכֶם]; \[or #he[אֵינְכֶם];; for the #he[אֶל]; cf. #bref("2 Kgs 6:11");, #bref("Jer 15:1");, #bref("Ezek 36:9");\].]; there still remain the #origpage("366") <orig-366>;following examples, in which #he[אֶת־]; in the later Hebrew manner (almost in the sense of the Latin #emph[quod attinet ad];) introduces a noun with more or less emphasis, #bref("Num 3:46");, #bref("5:10");, #bref("35:6");, #bref("Judg 20:44");, 46, #bref("Ezek 17:21");, #bref("20:16");, #bref("35:10");, #bref("44:3");, #bref("Neh 9:19");, 34, #bref("Dan 9:13");, #bref("2 Chr 31:17");.—In #bref("Ezek 47:17");–19 (cf. also 43:7) it is simplest to emend #he[זֹאת]; for #he[אֶת־];, according to verse 20. However, even the LXX, who have ταῦτα only in verse 18, can hardly have known any other reading than #he[את];; consequently in all these passages #he[את]; must be regarded as virtually dependent on some governing word, such as #emph[ecce]; (LXX 43:7 ἑώρακας), and 47:17 ff. as equivalent to #emph[thou shalt have]; as a border, etc.

#ml("n") <p117n> 8. Another solecism of the later period is finally the introduction of the object by the preposition #he[לְ]; (prop. #emph[in relation to, in the direction of];), as sometimes in Ethiopic#footnote[Dillmann, #emph[Grammatik der äthiopischen Sprache];, p. 349.]; and very commonly in Aramaic.#footnote[With regard to Biblical Aramaic, see Kautzsch’s #emph[Grammatik des Bibl.\-Aram.];, p. 151 f. In other ways, also, a tendency may be observed in later Hebrew to make use of the looser connection by means of prepositions instead of the closer subordination of the noun in the accusative.]; Less remarkable is this looser connection of the object with a participle, as with #he[אָכַל]; #bref("Lam 4:5");, #he[אִסֵּף]; #bref("Num 10:25");, #he[זָקַף]; #bref("Ps 145:14"); (but cf. 146:8), #he[צָרַר]; #bref("Num 25:18");, #he[הִשְׂגִּיא]; and #he[שָׁטַח]; #bref("Job 12:23");; #emph[before]; the participle #bref("Isa 11:9");.—To introduce an object #emph[preceding]; the finite verb #he[לְ]; is employed in #bref("Job 5:2"); (cf. also #bref("Dan 11:38");); also #emph[after]; #he[אָהֵב]; #bref("Lev 19:18");, 34; #he[הֶֽאֱרִיךְ]; #bref("Ps 129:3");; #he[הִבְדִּיל]; #bref("Ezra 8:24");, #bref("2 Chr 25:10");; #he[הֵבִין]; #bref("Job 9:11");; #he[בֵּרַךְ]; #bref("1 Chr 29:20"); (immediately before with an accusative); #he[הִגְלָה]; #bref("1 Chr 5:26");; #he[דָּרַשׁ]; #bref("Ezra 6:21");, #bref("1 Chr 22:19");, #bref("2 Chr 17:13");; #he[הֶֽחֱיָה]; #bref("Gen 45:7");, where, however, read #he[פְּלֵיטָה]; with the LXX for #he[לפליטה]; and take #he[לָכֶם]; as a #emph[dativus commodi];; #he[הִלֵּל]; #bref("1 Chr 16:36");, #bref("2 Chr 5:13");; #he[הָרַג]; #bref("2 Sam 3:30");, #bref("Ps 135:11"); (verse 10 with accusative), 136:19 f.; #he[חָבַשׁ]; (#emph[to bind up];) #bref("Isa 61:1"); (#bref("Ezek 34:4"); #emph[before]; the verb); #he[יָדַע]; #bref("Ps 69:6");; #he[כִּבֵּד]; #bref("Ps 86:9");; #he[לָקַח]; #bref("Jer 40:2");, #bref("2 Chr 23:1");; #he[הִמְלִיךְ]; and #he[מָשַׁח]; #bref("1 Chr 29:22");; #he[נֵהַל]; #bref("2 Chr 28:15");; #he[סָמַךְ]; #bref("Ps 145:14");; #he[עָזַב]; #bref("1 Chr 16:37");; #he[הֶֽעֱלָה]; #bref("Ezek 26:3");; #he[פִּתַּח]; #bref("Ps 116:16");; #he[רָדַף]; #bref("Job 19:28");; #he[הִצְדִּיק]; #bref("Isa 53:11");; #he[שָׂכַר]; #bref("2 Chr 24:12"); (previously accusatives); #he[שִׂים]; #bref("1 Sam 22:7"); (but probably #he[וְכֻלְּכֶם]; is to be read); #he[הֵשִׁיב]; (in the connection, #he[הֵשִׁיב דָּבָר לְ];) #bref("2 Chr 10:6"); (but verse 9 and #bref("1 Kgs 12:9"); with an accusative); #he[שִׁחֵת]; #bref("Num 32:15");, #bref("1 Sam 23:10");; #he[שִׁית]; #bref("Ps 73:18");; #he[שָׁלַח]; #bref("Ezra 8:16");, #bref("2 Chr 2:12");, #bref("17:7");; #he[שָׁמַר]; #bref("1 Chr 29:18");, #bref("2 Chr 5:11");.

#ml("o") <p117o> 9. Sometimes the verb, on which an accusative of the object really depends, is contained only in sense in the verb which apparently governs, e.g. #bref("Isa 14:17"); #he[אֲסִירָיו לֹא־פָתַח בָּֽיְתָה]; #emph[his prisoners he let not loose]; nor sent them back #emph[to their home];. On this #emph[constructio praegnans]; in general, see #xref(<p119ff>)[§ 119#h(0.18em)#emph[ff];];.

#ml("p") <p117p> #strong[2.]; With the proper accusatives of the object may also be classed what is called the #emph[internal]; or #emph[absolute object]; (also named #emph[schema etymologicum]; or #emph[figura etymologica];), i.e. the addition of an object in the form #origpage("367") <orig-367>;of a noun derived from the same stem,#footnote[On a kindred use of the infinitive absolute as an #emph[internal object];, see above, #xref(<p113w>)[§ 113#h(0.18em)#emph[w];];.]; e.g. #bref("Ps 14:5"); #he[פָּֽחֲדוּ פַ֫חַד]; #emph[they feared a fear]; (i.e. they were in great fear), #bref("Prov 15:27");; also with the object preceding, e.g. #bref("Lam 1:8"); #he[חֵטְא חָֽטְאָה יְרוּשָׁלַ͏ִם]; #emph[Jerusalem hath sinned a sin];; with a double accusative (see below, #emph[cc];), e.g. #bref("1 Kgs 1:12"); #he[אִֽיעָצֵךְ נָא עֵצָה]; #emph[let me, I pray thee, give thee counsel];; #bref("1 Kgs 1:12");.#footnote[Cf. βουλὰς βουλεύειν, Iliad x. 147.];

#ml("q") <p117q> Rem. (#emph[a];) Strictly speaking the only cases of this kind are those in which the verbal idea is supplemented by means of an #emph[indeterminate]; substantive (see the examples above). Such a substantive, except in the case of the addition of the internal object to denominative verbs (see below), is, like the infinitive absolute, never altogether without force, but rather serves like it to strengthen the verbal idea. This strengthening is implied in the indeterminateness of the internal object, analogous to such exclamations as, #emph[this was a man!];#footnote[The Arab grammarians assign to the indeterminate cases generally an intensive sense in many instances; hence the commentators on the Qorân usually explain such cases by adding #emph[and what ...!]; see #xref(<p125b>)[§ 125#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];.]; Hence it is intelligible that some intensifying attribute is very frequently (as in Greek usually) added to the internal object, e.g. #bref("Gen 27:34"); #he[וַיִּצְעַק צְעָקָה גְדֹלָה וּמָרָה עַד־מְאֹד]; #emph[he cried]; (with) #emph[an exceeding great and bitter cry];; cf. the Greek νοσεῖν νόσον κακήν, ἐχάρησαν χαρὰν μεγάλην (Matt. 2:10); #emph[magnam pugnare pugnam, tutiorem vitam vivere];, etc.

Examples of an internal object #emph[after]; the verb, and without further addition, are #bref("Exod 22:5");, #bref("2 Sam 12:16");, #bref("Isa 24:22");, #bref("35:2");, #bref("42:17");, #bref("Ezek 25:15");, 26:15, 27:35, #bref("Mic 4:9");, #bref("Zech 1:2");, #bref("Prov 21:26");; with an intensifying attribute, #bref("Gen 27:33");, #bref("Exod 32:31");, #bref("Judg 15:8");, #bref("2 Sam 13:36");, #bref("1 Kgs 1:40"); (cf. #bref("Jonah 4:6");, #bref("1 Chr 29:9");); #bref("Isa 21:7");, #bref("45:17");, #bref("Jonah 1:10");, #bref("Zech 1:14");, #bref("8:2");a, #bref("Dan 11:3");; along with an object proper the internal object occurs with an attribute in #bref("Gen 12:17");, #bref("2 Sam 13:15");; cf. also #bref("Isa 14:6");, #bref("Jonah 4:1");.—An internal object without an attribute #emph[before]; the verb: #bref("Isa 24:16");, #bref("Jer 46:5");, #bref("Hab 3:9");, #bref("Job 27:12");; with an attribute #emph[before]; the verb: #bref("Jer 14:17");, #bref("Zech 1:15"); (cf. also #bref("Gen 30:8");, #bref("Jer 22:19");, #bref("30:14");, #bref("Ps 139:22");). Instead of the substantive which would naturally be expected, another of kindred meaning is used in #bref("Zech 8:2");.

#ml("r") <p117r> (#emph[b];) Only in a wider sense can the #emph[schema etymologicum]; be made to include cases in which the denominative verb is used in connection with the noun from which it is derived, e.g. #bref("Gen 1:11");, #bref("9:14");, #bref("11:3");, #bref("37:7");, #bref("Ezek 18:2");, #bref("Ps 144:6");, probably also #bref("Mic 2:4");, or where this substantive, made determinate in some way, follows its verb, e.g. #bref("Gen 30:37");, #bref("Num 25:11");, #bref("2 Kgs 4:13");, #bref("13:14");, #bref("Isa 45:17");, #bref("Lam 3:58");,#footnote[Also in #bref("Ps 13:4"); #emph[lest I sleep the]; sleep of #emph[death];, #he[הַמָּ֫וֶת]; is only used pregnantly for #he[שְׁנַת הַמָּ֫וֶת]; (cf. #bref("Jer 51:39");), as #he[צְדָקוֹת]; #bref("Isa 33:15"); for #he[דֶּ֫רֶךְ צְדָקוֹת];. On the similar use of #he[הֹלֵךְ תָּמִים]; in #bref("Ps 15:2");, see #xref(<p118n>)[§ 118#h(0.18em)#emph[n];];.]; and, determinate at least in sense, #bref("Jer 22:16");; or precedes it, as in #bref("2 Kgs 2:16");, #bref("Isa 8:12");, #bref("62:5");, #bref("Zech 3:7");; cf. also #bref("Exod 3:9");. In both cases the substantive is used, without any special emphasis, merely for clearness or as a more convenient way of connecting the verb with other members of the sentence.

#ml("s") <p117s> #strong[3.]; Verbs which denote #emph[speaking]; (#emph[crying out, weeping];), or any external act, frequently take a direct accusative of the organ or means by which the action is performed. In this case, however, the accusative must be more closely determined by an attributive adjective or a noun in the genitive. This fact shows the close relation between these accusatives and the #emph[internal objects]; treated under #emph[p];, which also, #origpage("368") <orig-368>;according to #emph[q];, mostly take an intensifying attribute. On the other hand, they must not be regarded as adverbial (instrumental) accusatives, nor are they to be classed with the second (neuter) subjects treated below in #xref(<p144l>)[§ 144#h(0.18em)#emph[l];];.

#ml("t") <p117t> Examples of the accusative following the verb are #he[וָֽאֶזְעַק קֽוֹל־גָּדוֹל]; #emph[and I cried a loud voice];, i.e. with a loud voice, #bref("Ezek 11:13");, #bref("2 Sam 15:23"); (after the proper object, #bref("Deut 5:19");, #bref("1 Kgs 8:55");); #bref("Ps 109:2"); #emph[they have spoken unto me]; #he[לְשׁוֹן שֶׁ֫קֶר]; #emph[a tongue of deceit];, i.e. with a lying tongue; #bref("Prov 10:4"); #emph[he]; becometh #emph[poor]; #he[עֹשֶׂה כַף־רְמִיָּה]; #emph[dealing a slack hand];, i.e. who dealeth with a slack hand; cf. the German #emph[eine schöne Stimme singen];, to sing a fine voice, #emph[eine tüchtige Klinge schlagen];, to smite a trusty sword, #emph[Schlittschuhe laufen];, to run skates (i.e. to skate), and our #emph[to write a good hand, to play ball];, etc.—Examples of the accusative preceding are #he[שִׂפְתֵי רְנָנוֹת יְהַלֶּל־פִּי]; #emph[my mouth shall praise]; with #emph[joyful lips];, #bref("Ps 63:6");; cf. #bref("Ps 12:3");, where a #emph[casus instrumenti]; with #he[בְּ]; follows the accusative.

#ml("u") <p117u> #strong[4.]; Many verbs originally intransitive (sometimes even in form; see #emph[a];, note 2) may be used also as transitives, in consequence of a certain modification of their original meaning, which has gradually become established by usage; cf. e.g. #he[רִיב]; #emph[to strive];, but also with an accusative #emph[causam alicuius agere]; (so even in #bref("Isa 1:17");, etc.; elsewhere with #he[לְ]; of the person for whom one strives); #he[יָכֹל]; absolutely #emph[to be able];, with an accusative #emph[to prevail over]; any one; #he[חָפֵץ]; #emph[to be inclined]; and #he[רָצָה]; #emph[to have pleasure]; (usually with #he[בְּ];), with an accusative to #emph[wish for some one]; or #emph[something];; #he[שָׁכַב]; #emph[cubare];, then in the sense of #emph[concumbere];, originally joined with #he[עִם־]; #emph[cum];, but quite early also with the accusative, equivalent to #emph[comprimere]; (#emph[feminam];), etc. So in #bref("2 Sam 13:14");, etc., unless in all or some of the passages the preposition #he[אֵת]; is intended, e.g. #he[אִתָּהּ]; for #he[אֹתָהּ];; in the earlier passages #he[עִם־]; is the more usual.

#ml("v") <p117v> Rem. 1. It is certainly difficult to decide whether some verbs, which were afterwards used absolutely or joined with prepositions, were not nevertheless originally #emph[transitive];, and consequently it is only the supposed original meaning, usually assigned to them in English, which causes them to appear #emph[intransitive];.#footnote[Thus e.g. #he[עָנָה]; #emph[to reply to]; (ἀμείβεσθαί τινα), #emph[to answer any one];; #he[צִוָּה]; #emph[to command]; (iubere aliquem); #he[זָכַר]; #emph[to remember];; #he[קִוָּה]; (also with #he[לְ];) #emph[to wait for any one]; (to expect any one); #he[בִּשַׂר]; #emph[to bring glad tidings to any one]; (see the Lexicon); #he[נָאַף]; and #he[נִאֵף]; #emph[to commit adultery]; (adulterare matronam); #he[עָבַד]; #emph[to serve]; (colere); #he[עָרַב]; #emph[to become surety for]; ..., and many others.]; In that case there is of course no syntactical peculiarity to be considered, and a list of such verbs would at the most be requisite only for practical purposes. Moreover, it is also possible that certain verbs were originally in use at the same time both as transitive and intransitive, e.g. perhaps #he[לָבֵשׁ]; #emph[to be clothed]; along with #he[לָבַשׁ]; #emph[to put on]; (a garment). Finally the analogy of certain transitives in constant use may have led to intransitives of kindred meaning being also united directly with the accusative, so that, in other words, whole classes of verbs came to be regarded in a particular aspect as transitives. See below, #emph[y];. #origpage("369") <orig-369>;

#ml("w") <p117w> 2. The modification of the original meaning becomes especially evident when even reflexive conjugations (#emph[Niphʿal, Hithpaʿēl];, etc.) take an accusative (cf. § 57, note 2); e.g. #he[נִבָּא]; #emph[to prophesy];, #bref("Jer 25:13");; #he[נָסַב]; (prop. #emph[to put oneself round];) #emph[to surround];, #bref("Judg 19:22");; #he[נִלְחַם]; #emph[to fight];, #bref("Ps 109:3"); (where, however, the #emph[Qal]; #he[וַיִּלְחֲמ֫וּנִי]; should be read; cf. #bref("Ps 35:1");); also #he[הִתְגַּלַּח]; #emph[to shave]; (something) #emph[for oneself];, Num 6:19; #he[הִתְנַחֵל]; #emph[to take]; some one #emph[for oneself as a possession];, #bref("Isa 14:2");; #he[הִתְנַכֵּל]; #emph[to make]; some one #emph[an object of craft];, #bref("Gen 37:18");; #he[הִתְנַצֵּל]; #emph[to strip]; a thing #emph[off oneself];, #bref("Exod 33:6");; #he[הִתְעַבֵּר]; #emph[to bring on oneself the anger]; of any one, #emph[to anger]; him; #he[הִתְבּוֹנֵן]; #emph[to consider]; something, #bref("Job 37:14");; #he[הִתְפָּרֵק]; #emph[to break]; something #emph[off from oneself];, #bref("Exod 32:3");. In #bref("Gen 34:9"); after #he[הִתְחַתְּנוּ]; #emph[make ye marriages];, read #he[אִתָּ֫נוּ]; instead of #he[אֹתָ֫נוּ];. Cf. #xref(<p54f>)[§ 54#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];.

#ml("x") <p117x> 3. So also it is only owing to a modification of the original meaning of a verb (except where the expression is incorrect, and perhaps derived from the popular language), when sometimes the remoter object (otherwise introduced by #he[לְ];) is directly subordinated in the form of an accusative suffix, e.g. #bref("Zech 7:5"); #he[הֲצוֹם צַמְתֻּ֫נִי אָ֫נִי]; #emph[did ye fast at all unto me, even to me?]; as though to say, have ye #emph[be\-fasted]; me? have ye reached me with your fasting? Still more strange is #bref("Job 31:18"); #he[גְּדֵלַ֫נִי כְאָב]; #emph[he]; (the orphan) #emph[grew up to me as to a father];; cf. #bref("Isa 27:4");, #bref("65:5");, #bref("Jer 31:3");, and in Aramaic #bref("Dan 5:6");; but #he[אֶ֫רֶץ הַנֶּ֫גְב נְתַתָּ֫נִי]; #bref("Josh 15:19"); is to be regarded as a double accusative after a verb of #emph[giving];, see #emph[ff];. In #bref("1 Sam 2:25"); read #he[וּפִלְלוּ]; for #he[וּפִלְלוֹ];; in #bref("Isa 44:21");, instead of the Niphʿal, read #he[תִּנְשֵׁ֫נִי];; in #bref("Ezek 29:3"); either #he[עֲשִׂיתִיו]; is to be read with Olshausen or #he[עֲשִׂיתִים]; (and previously #he[יְאֹרָי];) with Smend; in #bref("Ps 42:5"); #he[אֶדַּדֶּה]; or #he[אֲדַדֵּם];; in #bref("Ps 55:23"); (where König takes #he[יְהָֽבְךָ]; as #emph[he has given it to thee];) we must certainly assume a substantive #he[יְהָב]; (\= #emph[fate];?).

#ml("y") <p117y> 4. Whole classes of verbs, which, according to #emph[v]; above, are regarded as #emph[transitive];, either on account of their original meaning or (for the sake of analogy) by a modification of that meaning, are—

(#emph[a];) Verba #emph[induendi]; and #emph[exuendi];, as #he[לָבַשׁ]; #emph[to put on];, #he[פָּשַׁט]; #emph[to put off]; a garment, #he[עָדָה]; #emph[to put on]; ornaments, #emph[to adorn oneself with]; (cf. also #he[מְשֻׁבָּצִים זָהָב]; #emph[enclosed in gold];, #bref("Exod 28:20");). Also in poetic expressions such as #bref("Ps 65:14"); #he[לָֽבְשׁוּ כָּרִים הַצֹּאן]; #emph[the pastures are clothed with flocks];, cf. #bref("Ps 109:29");; 104:2 (#he[עָטָה];); 65:14b (#he[עָטַף];), etc.#footnote[From the idea of #emph[covering oneself with]; something, we might also, if necessary, explain #bref("Exod 30:20"); #he[יִרְחֲצוּ מַיִם]; #emph[they shall wash themselves with water];; but the reading is simply to be emended to the ordinary #he[בַּמַּיִם];.];

#ml("z") <p117z> (#emph[b];) Verba #emph[copiae]; and #emph[inopiae]; (also called verba #emph[abundandi]; and #emph[deficiendi];), as #he[מָלֵא];, #emph[to be full of]; something, #bref("Exod 8:17");; here, and also frequently elsewhere, construed with #he[אֶת־];, and hence evidently with an accusative; #bref("Gen 6:13");; with a personal object, #bref("Exod 15:9"); #emph[my lust shall be satisfied upon them];; with an accusative #emph[preceding]; the verb for the sake of emphasis, e.g. #bref("Isa 1:15"); #emph[your hands]; #he[דָּמִים מָלֵ֫אוּ]; #emph[are full of blood];, cf. #bref("Isa 22:2");; so also the #emph[Niph.]; #he[נִמְלָא]; #emph[to fill oneself with]; something, e.g. #bref("Gen 6:11");, #bref("Exod 1:7"); (where the object is connected by #he[את];); #bref("Isa 2:7"); f., 6:4, #bref("Prov 3:10");; #he[נִזְרַע]; #emph[to be fructified with];, #bref("Num 5:28");; #he[שָׁרַץ]; #emph[to swarm with];, #bref("Gen 1:20");, 21 #bref("Exod 7:28");; #he[שָׂבַע]; (#he[שָׂבֵעַ];) #emph[to be full of];, #bref("Isa 1:11");, #bref("Joel 2:19");, #bref("Prov 12:11");; #he[גָּבַר]; #emph[to become strong, to wax mighty in]; something, #bref("Job 21:7");; #he[פָּרַץ]; #emph[to overflow with]; something, #bref("Prov 3:10"); (with the object preceding); #he[יָרַד]; prop. #emph[to descend];, poetically also #emph[to pour down, to overflow with]; something (cf. in Greek προρέειν ὕδωρ, δάκρυα στάζειν), e.g. #bref("Lam 3:48"); #he[פַּלְגֵי מַ֫יִם תֵּרַד עֵינִי]; #emph[mine eye runneth]; #origpage("370") <orig-370>;#emph[down (with) rivers of water];; 1:16, #bref("Jer 9:17");, #bref("13:17");, #bref("Ps 119:136");; so also #he[הָלַךְ]; #emph[to run over with, to flow with];, #bref("Joel 4:18");; #he[נָזַל]; #emph[to gush out with];, #bref("Jer 9:17");; #he[נָטַף]; #emph[to drop, to overflow with];, #bref("Judg 5:4");, #bref("Joel 4:18");a; #he[פָּרַח]; #emph[to break forth];, #bref("Exod 9:9");; #he[שָׁטַף]; #emph[to overflow];, but also (transitively) #emph[to overflow with];, probably in #bref("Isa 10:22");; #he[נוּב]; #emph[to bud with];, #bref("Prov 10:31");; so perhaps also #he[עָבַר]; #emph[to pass over, to overflow with];, #bref("Jer 5:28");; #he[יָצָא]; #emph[to go forth]; with, #bref("Amos 5:3");.—Especially bold, but still on the analogy of the above examples, is #bref("Isa 5:6");, where it is said of a vineyard #he[וְעָלָה שָׁמִיר וָשָׁ֫יִת]; #emph[but it shall come up]; (it shall be overgrown) #emph[with briers and thorns];; cf. #bref("Prov 24:31");, and still more boldly, #bref("Isa 34:13");.

#ml("aa") <p117aa> With the opposite idea, #he[חָסֵר]; #emph[to be in want of, to lack];, #bref("Gen 18:28");; #he[שָׁכֹל]; #emph[to be bereaved of]; (as though it were #emph[to lose];), #bref("Gen 27:45");.—In #bref("Josh 22:17"); even #he[הַמְעַט־לָ֫נוּ]; (prop. #emph[was there too little for us of ...?];) as being equivalent to a verbum inopiae (\= #emph[had we too little of]; ...?) is construed with an accusative; cf. #bref("Neh 9:32");.

#ml("bb") <p117bb> (#emph[c];) Several verbs of #emph[dwelling];; the accusative in this case expresses either the place or the thing #emph[at]; which or #emph[with]; which any one tarries; thus #bref("Gen 4:20");, #bref("Ps 22:4"); after #he[יָשַׁב];, cf. #xref(<p118g>)[§ 118#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];; #bref("Judg 5:17");, #bref("Isa 33:14"); after #he[גּוּר];; #bref("Ps 57:5"); after #he[שָׁכַב];; #bref("Ps 68:7");, #bref("Prov 8:12");, #bref("Isa 33:16"); with #he[שָׁכַן];; or even the person (the people) with whom any one dwells or is a guest, as #bref("Ps 5:5");, #bref("120:5"); after #he[גּוּר];, #bref("Gen 30:20"); after #he[זָבַל];, #bref("Ps 68:19"); with #he[שָׁכַן];.

#ml("cc") <p117cc> #strong[5];. #emph[Two accusatives]; (usually one of the person and one of the thing) are governed by—

(#emph[a];) The causative conjugations (#emph[Piʿēl];, #emph[Hiphʿîl];, sometimes also #emph[Pilpel];, e.g. #he[כִּלְכֵּל]; #bref("Gen 47:12");, etc.) of verbs which are simply transitive in #emph[Qal];, and hence also of #emph[verba induendi]; and #emph[exuendi];, etc. (cf. above #emph[a]; and #emph[u];, and also #emph[y];, #emph[z];), e.g. #bref("Exod 33:18"); #he[הַרְאֵ֫נִי נָא אֶת־כְּבֹדֶ֫ךָ]; #emph[show me, I pray thee, thy glory];. Thus very frequently #he[הוֹדִיעַ]; #emph[to cause]; some one #emph[to know]; something; #he[לִמַּד]; #emph[docere aliquem aliquid];, etc.; cf. further, #bref("Gen 41:42"); #he[וַיַּלְבֵּשׁ אֹתוֹ בִגְדֵי־שֵׁשׁ]; #emph[and he caused him to put on vestures of fine linen]; (#emph[he arrayed him in vestures];, etc.); cf. in the opposite sense, #bref("Gen 37:23"); (both accusatives after #he[הִפְשִׁיט]; introduced by #he[אֶת];); so with #he[מִלֵּא]; #emph[to fill, to fill up]; with something, #bref("Gen 21:19");, #bref("26:15");, #bref("Exod 28:3");; #he[אִזֵּר]; #emph[to gird]; some one with something, #bref("Ps 18:33");; #he[עִטֵּר]; #emph[to crown];, #bref("Ps 8:6");, etc.; #he[חִסֵּר]; #emph[to cause]; some one #emph[to lack]; something, #bref("Ps 8:6");; #he[הֶֽאֱכִיל]; #emph[to feed]; some one with something, #bref("Exod 16:32");; #he[הִשְׁקָה]; #emph[to make]; some one #emph[drink]; something, #bref("Gen 19:32"); ff.

#ml("dd") <p117dd> (#emph[b];) Many verbs (even in #emph[Qal];) which express an influence upon the object through some external means. The latter, in this case, is attached as a second object. They are especially—

#ml("ee") <p117ee> (α) Verbs which express #emph[covering, clothing, overlaying];, #he[חָגַר]; #bref("Exod 29:9");, #he[צִפָּה]; #bref("Exod 26:29");, etc., #he[טוּחַ]; #bref("Ezek 13:10"); ff., #he[עָטַר]; #bref("Ps 5:13");; cf. also #he[רָגַם אֶבֶן]; #bref("Josh 7:25");, etc.; hence also verbs which express #emph[sowing]; (#he[זָרַע]; #bref("Judg 9:45");, #bref("Isa 17:10");, 30:23), #emph[planting]; (#bref("Isa 5:2");), #emph[anointing]; (#bref("Ps 45:8");) #emph[with]; anything.

#ml("ff") <p117ff> (β) Expressions of #emph[giving];, thus #he[נָתַן]; #bref("Josh 15:19"); where the accusative of the thing precedes; #emph[endowing];, #he[זָבַד]; #bref("Gen 30:20");; and its opposite #emph[taking away];, as #he[קָבַע]; #bref("Prov 22:23");; #he[בֵּרַךְ]; #emph[to bless some one with something];, #bref("Gen 49:25");, #bref("Deut 15:14");; #emph[to give graciously];, #he[חָנַן]; #bref("Gen 33:5");; #emph[to sustain]; (i.e. to support, to maintain, to furnish) #emph[with]; anything, #origpage("371") <orig-371>;e.g. #bref("Gen 27:37");, #bref("Ps 51:14"); (#he[סָמַךְ];); #bref("Judg 19:5"); (#he[סָעַד];); #emph[to do something to one];, #he[גָּמַל]; #bref("Gen 50:15");, 17, #bref("1 Sam 24:18");; cf. also #he[קִדֵּם]; #emph[to come to meet any one with]; something, #bref("Ps 21:4");, #he[שִׁלַּם]; #emph[to repay some one]; with something (with two accusatives, #bref("Ps 35:12");, #bref("Prov 13:21");), and for the accusative of the person cf. εὖ, κακῶς πράττειν τινά. In a wider sense we may also include such phrases as #emph[they hunt every man his brother with a net];, #bref("Mic 7:2");; #emph[to shoot at one with arrows];, #bref("Ps 64:8"); (though this is against the accents); #bref("Prov 13:24"); #emph[seeks him early]; (with) #emph[discipline];, i.e. #emph[chastises him betimes];, etc.

#ml("gg") <p117gg> (γ) Expressions of #emph[asking]; some one #emph[for]; something, #emph[desiring]; something #emph[from]; some one (#he[שָׁאַל]; #bref("Deut 14:26");, #bref("Ps 137:3");); #emph[answering]; any one anything (#he[עָנָה]; #bref("Mic 6:5");, etc.; cf. in the other conjugations #he[הֵשִׁיב דָּבָר]; prop. #emph[verbum reddere];, with an accusative of the person, #bref("1 Kgs 12:6");, etc., also in the sense of #emph[announcing];; sometimes also #he[הִגִּיד]; #emph[to declare]; something #emph[to]; some one, #bref("Job 26:4");, etc., for #he[הִגִּיד לְ];); #he[צִוָּה]; #emph[to enjoin a person]; something, #bref("Exod 34:32");, #bref("Deut 1:18");, #bref("32:46");, #bref("Jer 7:23");.

#ml("hh") <p117hh> (δ) Expressions which mean #emph[to make, to form, to build something out of]; something; in such cases, besides the accusative of the object proper, another accusative is used for the material of which the thing is made, e.g. #bref("Gen 2:7"); #he[וַיִּ֫יצֶר יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהִים אֶת־הָֽאָדָם עָפָר מִן־הָֽאֲדָמָה]; #emph[and the Lord formed man of the dust of the ground];; so with #he[יָצַר]; also in #bref("1 Kgs 7:15");; further #bref("Exod 38:3"); #he[כָּל־כֵּלָיו עָשָׂה נְח֫שֶׁת]; #emph[all the vessels thereof made he of brass]; (for another explanation of the accusative #he[נְח֫שֶׁת]; \[#emph[into brass];\], linguistically possible but excluded by the context, see below, #emph[ii]; with #emph[kk];); cf. #bref("Exod 25:18");, 28, 26:1, 14 f.29, 27:1, 36:8, #bref("1 Kgs 7:27");; with a preceding accusative of the material, #bref("Exod 25:29");, #bref("29:2");, #bref("Deut 27:6"); #he[אֲבָנִים שְׁלֵמוֹת תִּבְנֶה אֶת־מִזְבַּח יְהֹוָה]; #emph[of unhewn stones shalt thou build the altar of the Lord];.

#ml("ii") <p117ii> (#emph[c];) Verbs which express #emph[making, preparing, forming into]; anything, along with the object proper, take a second accusative of the product, e.g. #bref("Gen 27:9"); #he[אֶֽעֱשֶׂה אֹתָם מַטְעַמִּים]; #emph[I will make them]; (the kids) into #emph[savoury meat];; cf. #bref("Gen 6:14");, 16, Ex 26#super[1 #emph[b];];, 30:25, 32:4, #bref("Isa 44:15");, #bref("Hos 8:4");, #bref("1 Kgs 18:32"); #he[וַיִּבְנֶה אֶת־הָֽאֲבָנִים מִזְבֵּחַ]; #emph[and he built the stones]; (into) #emph[an altar];; cf. 10:12. So also #he[אָפָה];, with two accusatives, #emph[to bake]; something #emph[into]; something, #bref("Exod 12:39");, #bref("Lev 24:5");; #he[שִׂים]; (prop. #emph[to set up for]; something, cf. #bref("Gen 27:37");, #bref("28:18");, #bref("Ps 39:9");, and similarly #he[הֵרִים]; #bref("Gen 31:45");) #emph[to change into]; something, #bref("Josh 8:28");, #bref("Isa 50:2");, #bref("51:10");, #bref("Mic 1:7");, #bref("4:13");; with two accusatives of the person (#emph[to appoint, promote]; any one to the position of a...), #bref("Isa 3:7");; #he[נָתַן]; is also used in the same sense with two accusatives, #bref("Gen 17:5");, and #he[שִׁית]; #bref("1 Kgs 11:34");; as a rule, however, the description of the office, and also frequently of the product, is introduced by #he[לְ]; #emph[to];, #xref(<p119t>)[§ 119#h(0.18em)#emph[t];];; also #he[שִׁית]; #emph[to make a thing so and so]; (#bref("Isa 5:6");, #bref("26:1");; with a personal object, #bref("Ps 21:7");,#footnote[Cf. a very pregnant expression of this kind in #bref("Ps 21:13"); #he[כִּי תְשִׁיתֵ֫מוֹ שֶׁ֫כֶם]; #emph[for thou shalt make them]; (as) #emph[a neck];, i.e. #emph[thou shalt cause them to turn their necks]; (backs) #emph[to me];; similarly #bref("Ps 18:41"); (#bref("2 Sam 22:41");, #bref("Exod 23:27");); #he[אֹֽיְבַי נָתַ֫תָּה לִּי עֹרֶף]; #emph[thou hast given mine enemies unto me as a back];; cf. #bref("Jer 18:17");.]; 91:9); #he[הֶחְשִׁיךְ]; #emph[to make dark];, #bref("Amos 5:8");. Of the same class also are instances like #bref("Job 28:2"); #he[אֶ֫בֶן יָצוּק נְחוּשָׁה]; #emph[a stone they smelt into brass];; #bref("1 Kgs 11:30"); #he[וַיִּקְרָעֶ֫הָ שְׁנֵים עָשָׂר קְרָעִים]; #emph[and rent it]; (the garment) #emph[into twelve pieces];; cf. #bref("Isa 37:26");, accusative of the product before the object proper, after #he[לְהַשְׁאוֹת]; #emph[to lay waste];. #origpage("372") <orig-372>;On a second object with #emph[verba sentiendi]; (as #he[יָדַע]; #emph[to know]; something #emph[to be]; something, #bref("Eccl 7:25");; #he[רָאָה]; #emph[to see, find to be];, #bref("Gen 7:1");; #he[חָשַׁב]; #emph[to esteem]; one #emph[to be]; something, #bref("Isa 53:4");, elsewhere always construed with #he[לְ]; or #he[כְּ‍];), cf. #emph[h];.

#ml("kk") <p117kk> Rem. At first sight some of the examples given above appear to be identical in character with those treated under #emph[hh];; thus it is possible, e.g. in #bref("1 Kgs 18:32");, by a translation which equally suits the sense, #emph[he built from the stones an altar];, to explain #he[מִזְבֵּחַ]; as the nearer object and #he[אֶת־הָֽאֲבָנִים]; as an accusative of the material, and the construction would then be exactly the same as in #bref("Deut 27:6");. In reality, however, the fundamental idea is by no means the same. Not that in the living language an accusative of the material in the one case, and in the other an accusative of the product were consciously distinguished. As Driver (#emph[Tenses];, § 195) rightly observes, the remoter accusative in both cases is, strictly speaking, in apposition to the nearer. This is especially evident in such examples as #bref("Exod 20:25"); #emph[thou shalt not build them]; (the stones of the altar) #he[גָּזִית]; as #emph[hewn stones];, cf. also #bref("Gen 1:27");. The main point is, which of the two accusatives, as being primarily affected (or aimed at) by the action, is to be made the more prominent; and on this point neither the position of the words (the nearer object, mostly determinate, as a rule follows immediately after the verb), nor even the context admits of much doubt. Thus in #bref("1 Kgs 18:32"); the treatment of the stones is the primary object in view, the erection of the altar for which they were intended is the secondary; in #bref("Deut 27:6"); the case is reversed.

#ml("ll") <p117ll> (#emph[d];) Finally, the second accusative sometimes more closely determines the nearer object by indicating the part or member specially affected by the action,#footnote[Analogous to this is the σχῆμα καθ’ ὅλον καὶ κατὰ μέρος in Greek epic poetry, e.g. ποῖόν σε ἔπος φύγε ἕρκος ὀδόντων.]; e.g. #bref("Ps 3:8"); #emph[for thou hast smitten all mine enemies]; #he[לֶ֫חִי]; (as to) #emph[the cheek bone];, equivalent to #emph[upon the cheek bone];; cf. #bref("Gen 37:21"); #emph[let us not smite him]; #he[נֶ֫פֶשׁ]; in #emph[the life];, i.e. let us not kill him; #bref("Deut 22:26");, #bref("2 Sam 3:27");; also with #he[שׁוּף]; #bref("Gen 3:15");; with #he[רָעָה]; #bref("Jer 2:16");; in poetry the object specially concerned is, by a bold construction, even placed first, #bref("Deut 33:11"); (with #he[מָחַץ];).

#sec("118", [The Looser Subordination of the Accusative to the Verb], note: []) <s118>

#ml("a") <p118a> #strong[1.]; The various forms of the looser subordination of a noun to the verb are distinguished from the different kinds of the accusative of the object (§ 117) by their specifying not the persons or things directly affected by the action, but some #emph[more immediate circumstance]; under which an action or an event takes place. Of such circumstances the most common are those of #emph[place, time, measure, cause];, and finally the #emph[manner]; of performing the action. These nearer definitions are, as a rule, placed #emph[after]; the verb; they may, however, also precede it.

#ml("b") <p118b> Rem. That the cases thus loosely subordinated to the verb are to be regarded as #emph[accusatives]; is seen first from the fact that in certain instances the #emph[nota accusativi]; (#he[את];) is prefixed; secondly from the fact that in one form of #origpage("373") <orig-373>;the #emph[casus loci]; a termination (#he[־ָה];) is employed, in which (according to #xref(<p90c>)[§ 90#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];) the old accusatival ending is preserved; and finally from the consistency with which classical Arabic puts these nearer definitions in the accusative (which may be recognized by its form) even under circumstances in which one would be rather inclined to expect a nominative in apposition.

#ml("c") <p118c> The relation subsisting between the circumstantial accusative and the accusative of the object is especially apparent when the former (as e.g. in a statement of the goal after a verb of motion) is immediately connected with its verb. But even the more loosely connected circumstantial definitions are certainly to be regarded as originally objects of a governing word habitually omitted, only that the consciousness of this closer government was at length lost, and the accusative more and more acquired an independent value as a #emph[casus adverbialis];.

#ml("d") <p118d> #strong[2.]; The accusative serves to define more precisely the #emph[place]; (#emph[accus. loci];), either (#emph[a];) in answer to the question #emph[whither?]; after verbs of motion,#footnote[So commonly in Sanskrit; in Greek only poetically, e.g. Iliad i. 317 κνίση δ᾽ οὐρανὸν ἷκεν: in Latin, e.g. #emph[rus ire, Romam proficisci];.]; or (#emph[b];) in answer to the question #emph[where?]; after verbs of #emph[being, dwelling, resting];, etc. (but also after transitive verbs, see the examples), or finally (#emph[c];) to define more precisely the #emph[extent]; in space, in answer to the question #emph[how far? how high? how much?];, etc.

#ml("e") <p118e> Instead of the simple accusative, the locative (see above, #xref(<p90c>)[§ 90#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];) #footnote[Hence e.g. in #bref("1 Sam 9:26"); the Masora requires #he[הַגָּ֫גָה]; instead of the #emph[K#super[e];th.]; #he[הַגָּג];.]; is frequently found in the cases mentioned under #emph[f]; (sometimes also in those under #emph[g];) or the preposition #he[אֶל־];,#footnote[So in #bref("Judg 19:18"); for #he[אֶת־בֵּית י׳]; the better reading is #he[אֶל־בּ׳];.]; especially before persons as the aim of the movement, or #he[בְּ];, usually, to express being #emph[at]; a place.

#ml("f") <p118f> Examples of (#emph[a];): #he[נֵצֵא הַשָּׂדֶה]; #emph[let us go out into the field];, #bref("1 Sam 20:11");; cf. #bref("Gen 27:3");, #bref("31:4");, #bref("Job 29:7");; #he[לָלֶ֫כֶת תַּרְשִׁישׁ]; #emph[to go to Tarshish];, #bref("2 Chr 20:36");; cf. #bref("Gen 10:11");, #bref("13:9");, #bref("24:27");, #bref("26:23");, #bref("31:21");, #bref("Exod 4:9");, #bref("17:10");, #bref("Judg 1:26");, #bref("2 Kgs 11:19");, #bref("Nah 1:8"); (?), #bref("Ps 134:2");; with #he[לָקַח]; #bref("Num 23:14");; with #he[נָתַן]; #bref("Josh 6:24");; with the #emph[accus. loci]; emphatically preceding (cf. Driver on #bref("1 Sam 5:8");), #bref("1 Kgs 2:26");, #bref("Isa 23:12");, #bref("Jer 2:10");, #bref("20:6");, #bref("32:5");; with #he[בּוֹא]; (in the sense of #emph[aggredi];, equivalent to #he[בּוֹא עַל־];, cf. #xref(<p117a>)[§ 117#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];, note 2) the #emph[personal]; aim also is poetically added in the accusative, #bref("Ezek 32:11");, #bref("38:11");, #bref("Prov 10:24");, #bref("28:22");, #bref("Job 15:21");, #bref("20:22");; but in the last passage it is better taken as an accusative of the object (cf. the German #emph[einen ankommen, überkommen];). See also #bref("Num 10:36"); (where #he[שׁוּב]; can hardly be transitive); #bref("Judg 11:29");, #bref("1 Sam 13:20"); (where, however, #he[אֶל־]; has probably fallen out after #he[ישראל];; so Strack).—Finally, cf. also the use of #he[אֲשֶׁר]; for #he[אֲשֶׁר ... שָׁ֫מָּה]; #emph[whither];, #bref("Num 13:27");.—The #emph[accus. loci]; occurs after a passive, e.g. #bref("Gen 12:15");.

#ml("g") <p118g> Examples of (#emph[b];): #bref("Gen 38:11"); #emph[remain a widow]; #he[בֵּית אָבִיךְ]; #emph[in thy father’s house];; cf. #bref("Gen 24:23");, #bref("1 Sam 17:15");, #bref("2 Sam 2:32");, #bref("Isa 3:6");, #bref("Hos 12:5");, #bref("Mic 6:10");, #bref("2 Chr 33:20");; #he[פֶּ֫תַח הָאֹ֫הֶל]; #emph[in the tent door];, #bref("Gen 18:1");, 10, 19:11, and frequently. As observed by Driver on #bref("1 Sam 2:29");, accusatives of this kind are almost without exception (but cf. #bref("1 Kgs 8:32");, #bref("Isa 16:2");, #bref("28:7");, #bref("2 Chr 33:20");) connected with a noun in the genitive. In all the above examples, however, the accusative may have been preferred to the natural construction with #he[בְּ]; (which is not rare even with #he[בֵּית]; and #he[פֶ֫תַח];) for euphonic reasons, in order to avoid the combination of such sounds as #he[בְּב׳]; and #he[בְּפ׳];; cf., moreover, #bref("Gen 2:14");, #bref("4:16");, #bref("Exod 18:5");, #bref("Lev 6:8"); (#he[הַמִּזְבֵּחַ]; instead of the usual #he[הַמִּזְבֵּ֫חָה]; #origpage("374") <orig-374>;#bref("Exod 29:13");, etc.); #bref("Deut 1:2");, 19,#footnote[In #bref("Ps 2:12"); #he[דֶּ֫רֶךְ]; is not to be taken as an #emph[accus. loci]; (#emph[on the way];), but as an #emph[accus. of respect (with regard to the way)];; see below, #emph[m];.]; #bref("2 Sam 17:26");, #bref("1 Kgs 7:8");, #bref("Prov 8:3");, #bref("9:14");. On #bref("Isa 1:30"); see #xref(<p116i>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[i];];; on #he[יָשַׁב];, with the #emph[accus. loci];, see #xref(<p117bb>)[§ 117#h(0.18em)#emph[bb];];. On the other hand, in #bref("Deut 6:3");, according to the LXX, a verb of #emph[giving]; has dropped out before #he[אֶ֫רֶץ];.

#ml("h") <p118h> Examples of (#emph[c];): #bref("Gen 7:20"); #emph[fifteen cubits upward did the waters prevail];; #bref("Gen 31:23");, #bref("41:40"); #he[רַק הַכִּסֵּא אֶגְדַּל מִמֶּ֫ךָּ]; #emph[only in the throne will I be greater than thou];; #bref("Deut 1:19"); #emph[we went]; (through) #emph[all that great and terrible wilderness];; cf. #bref("Job 29:3");. Of the same kind also are such cases as #bref("Exod 16:16"); (#emph[according to the number of your persons];, for which elsewhere #he[לְמִסְפַּר־]; is used); #bref("1 Sam 6:4"); (with the accus. preceding); 6:18, #bref("2 Sam 21:20");, #bref("Job 1:5");.—A statement of weight is put in the accusative in #bref("2 Sam 14:26");.

#ml("i") <p118i> #strong[3.]; The accusative is employed to determine more precisely the #emph[time]; (#emph[accus. temporis];), (#emph[a];) in answer to the question #emph[when?]; e.g. #he[הַיּוֹם]; #emph[the day];, i.e. #emph[on the day]; (in question), #emph[at that time];, but also #emph[on this day];, i.e. #emph[today];, or finally #emph[by day];, equivalent to #he[יוֹמָם];, like #he[עֶ֫רֶב]; #emph[at evening];, #he[לַ֫יְלָה]; #emph[noctu];, #he[בֹּ֫קֶר]; #emph[in the morning, early];, #bref("Ps 5:4");, etc., #he[צָֽהֳרַ֫יִם]; #emph[at noonday];, #bref("Ps 91:6");; #he[יוֹם אֶחָד]; #emph[on one and the same day];, #bref("Gen 27:45");; #he[שֵׁנָא]; #emph[in sleep];, #bref("Ps 127:2");; #he[תְּחִלַּת קְצִיר שְׂעֹרִים]; (#emph[Q#super[e];rê]; #he[בִּתְ׳];) #emph[at the beginning of barley harvest];, #bref("2 Sam 21:9");; in stating a date, #bref("Gen 11:10");, #bref("14:4"); #emph[in the thirteenth year];.

#ml("k") <p118k> (#emph[b];) In answer to the question #emph[how long?]; e.g. #bref("Gen 3:14");, etc., #he[כָּל־יְמֵי חַיֶּ֫יךָ]; #emph[all the days of thy life];; 7:4 #emph[forty days and forty nights];; 7:24, 14:4, 15:13, 21:34, 29:18, #bref("Exod 20:9"); (#emph[for six days];); 23:15, 31:17; #he[עֽוֹלָמִים]; #emph[for ever];, #bref("1 Kgs 8:13");; also with the accusative made determinate, #bref("Exod 13:7"); #he[אֵת שִׁבְעַת הַיָּמִים]; #emph[throughout the seven days]; in question, mentioned immediately before; cf. #bref("Judg 14:17");, #bref("Deut 9:25");.

#ml("l") <p118l> #strong[4.]; The accusative is sometimes used of abstract ideas to state the reason (#emph[accus. causae];), e.g. #bref("Isa 7:25"); #emph[thou shalt not come thither]; #he[יִרְאַת שָׁמִיר]; #emph[for fear of briers];.

#ml("m") <p118m> #strong[5.]; Finally the accusative is used very variously (as an #emph[accus. adverbialis]; in the narrower sense), in order to describe more precisely the #emph[manner]; in which an action or state takes place. In English such accusatives are mostly rendered by #emph[in, with, as, in the form]; or #emph[manner of ..., according to, in relation to, with regard to];. For more convenient classification we may distinguish them as—

#ml("n") <p118n> (#emph[a];) Adjectives expressing #emph[state];, placed #emph[after]; the verb to describe more accurately some bodily or other external condition, e.g. #bref("Isa 20:2"); #emph[walking]; #he[עָרוֹם וְיָחֵף]; #emph[naked and barefoot];; cf. verse 3, 8:21, #bref("Gen 15:2");, #bref("33:18"); (#he[שָׁלֵם];), #bref("Judg 8:4");, #bref("Mic 1:8");, #bref("Ps 107:5"); (but in 15:2 #he[תָּמִים]; is rather a substantive directly dependent on #he[הוֹלֵךְ]; \= #emph[he that walketh]; in #emph[uprightness];; cf. #xref(<p117r>)[§ 117#h(0.18em)#emph[r];];, note); #bref("Job 30:28");. After an accusative, e.g. #bref("Deut 15:18");; to specify some mental state, e.g. #bref("Gen 37:35"); (#he[אָבֵל];).—#emph[Before]; the verb (and then with a certain emphasis), #bref("Amos 2:16");, #bref("Job 1:21");, #bref("Eccl 5:14");; #bref("Lev 20:20");, #bref("Job 19:25");, #bref("27:19");, #bref("31:26"); #origpage("375") <orig-375>;(unless #he[יָקָר]; be a substantive); #bref("Ruth 1:21"); (#he[מְלֵאָה]; parallel with the adverb #he[רֵיקָם];). In #bref("Mic 2:7"); the text is clearly corrupt.

#ml("o") <p118o> Those examples are especially instructive in which the adjective expressing a state, although referring to several, is nevertheless used in the singular, e.g. #bref("Job 24:10"); #he[עָרוֹם הִלְּכוּ]; #emph[naked];, i.e. in the condition of one naked, #emph[they go about];; cf. verse 7 and 12:17. In #bref("Isa 20:4"); the singular occurs #emph[after]; a plural object, and in #bref("Isa 47:5"); the #emph[masc.]; after the 2nd sing. #emph[fem.]; imperative, which clearly proves that the term expressing the state is not conceived as being in apposition, but as an indeclinable adverb.

#ml("p") <p118p> (#emph[b];) Participles, again either #emph[after]; the verb, #bref("Num 16:27");, #bref("Jer 2:27");, #bref("43:2");, #bref("Ps 7:3");, #bref("Job 24:5");, #bref("Song 2:8");, or #emph[before]; it, #bref("Gen 49:11");, #bref("Isa 57:19");, #bref("Ezek 36:35");, #bref("Ps 56:2");, #bref("92:14");, #bref("Prov 20:14");; cf. also the substantival use of the participles Niphʿal #he[נֽוֹרָאוֹת]; #emph[in a fearful manner]; (#bref("Ps 139:14");) and #he[נִפְלָאוֹת]; #emph[in a wonderful manner];, #bref("Job 37:5");, #bref("Dan 8:24");.—Also participles in connection with genitives, as #he[מִתְהַלֵּךְ]; #bref("Gen 3:8"); (cf. also #he[בָּאָה]; #bref("1 Kgs 14:6");), are to be regarded as expressing a state and not as being in apposition, since in the latter case they would have to take the article.—In #bref("2 Sam 13:20");, #bref("1 Kgs 7:7"); and #bref("Hab 2:10"); the #emph[explicative Wāw]; (equivalent to #emph[and that too];) is also prefixed to the participle. In #bref("Ps 69:4"); for #he[מְיַחֵל]; read #he[מִיַּחֵל];.—On #bref("1 Kgs 11:8");, #bref("2 Kgs 10:6");, #bref("19:2");, #bref("Hag 1:4");, cf. the note on #xref(<p131h>)[§ 131#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];.

#ml("q") <p118q> (#emph[c];) Substantives#footnote[Cf. above, #xref(<p100c>)[§ 100#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];, on certain substantives which have completely become adverbs; and #xref(<p113h>)[§ 113#h(0.18em)#emph[h];]; and #emph[k]; on the adverbial use of the infinitive absolute.]; in the most varied relations: thus, as describing an external state, e.g. #bref("Mic 2:3"); #he[וְלֹא תֵֽלְכוּ רוֹמָה]; #emph[neither shall ye walk haughtily]; (as opposed to #he[שְׁחוֹחַ]; #bref("Isa 60:14");); #bref("Lev 6:9"); (accus. before the verb\=as #emph[unleavened cakes];), #bref("Deut 2:9");, #bref("4:11");, #bref("Judg 5:21");, #bref("Isa 57:2");, #bref("Prov 7:10");, #bref("Job 31:26");, #bref("Lam 1:9");; as stating the position of a disease, #bref("1 Kgs 15:23"); #emph[he was diseased]; #he[אֶת־רַגְלָיו]; in #emph[his feet]; (#bref("2 Chr 16:12"); #he[בְּרַגְלָיו];), analogous to the cases discussed in #xref(<p117ll>)[§ 117#h(0.18em)#emph[ll];]; and #xref(<p121d>)[§ 121#h(0.18em)#emph[d];]; (#emph[d];); as describing a spiritual, mental, or moral state, e.g. #bref("Num 32:14");, #bref("Josh 9:2"); (#he[פֶּה אֶחָד]; #emph[with one accord];, #bref("1 Kgs 22:13");; cf. #bref("Exod 24:3");, #bref("Zeph 3:9");), #bref("1 Sam 15:32");, #bref("2 Sam 23:3");, #bref("Isa 41:3"); (unless #he[שָׁלוֹם]; is adjectival, and the passage is to be explained as in #emph[n];); #bref("Jer 31:7");, #bref("Hos 12:15");, #bref("14:5");, #bref("Ps 56:3");, #bref("58:2");, #bref("75:3");, #bref("Prov 31:9");, #bref("Job 16:9");, #bref("Lam 1:9");; #bref("Lev 19:16");, etc., in the expression #he[הָלַךְ רָכִיל]; #emph[to go up and down]; as #emph[a tale\-bearer];; also #he[בֶּ֫טַח]; #emph[unawares];, #bref("Gen 34:25");, #bref("Ezek 30:9");; #he[מֵֽישָׁרִים]; #emph[uprightly];, #bref("Ps 58:2");, #bref("75:3"); (in both places #emph[before]; the verb); as stating the age, e.g. #bref("1 Sam 2:33"); (if the text be right) #he[יָמ֫וּתוּ אֲנָשִׁים]; #emph[they shall die]; as #emph[men];, i.e. #emph[in the prime of life];; cf. #bref("1 Sam 2:18"); (#he[נַ֫עַר];), #bref("Isa 65:20");, and #bref("Gen 15:16");; as specifying a number more accurately, #bref("Deut 4:27");, #bref("1 Sam 13:17");, #bref("2 Kgs 5:2");, #bref("Jer 31:8"); \[in #bref("Jer 13:19"); #he[שְׁלוֹמִים]; #emph[wholly]; (?) is corrupt; read #he[גָּלוּת שְׁלֵמָה]; with LXX for #he[הָגְלָת שׁ׳];\]; as stating the consequence of the action, #bref("Lev 15:18");, etc.

#ml("r") <p118r> The description of the external or internal state may follow, in poetry, in the form of a comparison with some well\-known class, e.g. #bref("Isa 21:8"); #he[וַיִּקְרָא אַרְיֵה]; #emph[and he cried]; as #emph[a lion];; cf. #bref("Ps 22:14");, #bref("Isa 22:18"); (#he[כַּדּוּר]; #emph[like a ball];); #bref("Isa 24:22");, #bref("Zech 2:8");, #bref("Ps 11:1"); (unless #he[צִפּוֹר]; be vocative); 58:9#super[ #emph[b];]; (unless the force of the preceding #he[כְּ‍]; is carried on, as in #bref("Ps 90:4");); #bref("Ps 144:12");, #bref("Job 24:5"); (#he[פְּרָאִים];, before the verb); 41:7 #emph[shut up together]; as with #emph[a close seal];.#footnote[It is, as a matter of fact, permissible to speak of the above examples as #emph[comparatio decurtata];, but it must not be assumed that the comparative particle #he[כְּ‍];, which is otherwise regularly prefixed (see #emph[s];), has actually dropped out.];

#ml("s") <p118s> #strong[6.]; To the expressions describing a state belong finally those nouns which are introduced by the comparative particle #he[כְּ‍];,#footnote[On the use of #he[כְּ‍]; as a prefix, cf. #xref(<p102c>)[§ 102#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];.]; since the #he[כְּ‍]; is to #origpage("376") <orig-376>;be regarded as originally a substantive#footnote[Schwabe (#emph[#he[כְּ‍]; nach seinem Wesen und Gebrauch im alttestam. Kanon gewürdigt];, Halle, 1883) contests this explanation (which is defended especially by Fleischer and is certainly indisputable). He, with Gesenius and Ewald, places #he[כְּ‍]; as a preposition on the same footing as #he[בְּ]; and #he[לְ];, and believes it to be probably connected with the stem #he[כּוּן]; as well as with #he[כִּי]; and #he[כֵּן];. The above view of #he[כְּ‍]; as a substantive of course does not imply that the language as we have it is still in every case conscious of the substantival meaning.—On #he[כְּ‍]; in numerical statements, in the sense of #emph[about, nearly];, see the Lexicon.]; in the sense of #emph[amount, kind]; (#emph[instar];), standing in the accusative (so that #he[כְּ‍]; is equivalent to #emph[as a kind of, after the manner of, according to];), while the following noun represents a genitive governed by the #he[כְּ‍];. From this, which is the proper meaning of the #he[כְּ‍];, may be explained its power of representing a great many pregnant relations, which in English can only be rendered by the help of prepositions.#footnote[It would be altogether unsuitable here also (see above, note 2 on #emph[r];) to assume a loss of the preposition. Such examples as #bref("Isa 1:26"); (#he[כְּבָרִֽאשֹׁנָה]; and #he[כְּבַתְּחִלָּה];), #bref("Lev 26:37"); (#he[כְּמִפְּנֵי];) are to be explained from the fact that here the preposition and substantive had already become simply #emph[one]; word before the #he[כְּ‍]; was prefixed. We find also #he[כְּעַל]; #bref("Isa 59:18");, #bref("63:7");, #bref("Ps 119:14");, and #bref("2 Chr 32:19");; cf. Driver on #bref("1 Sam 14:14"); (#he[כְּבַֽחֲצִי];), where the text is wholly corrupt.]; Thus the comparison may refer to—

#ml("t") <p118t> (#emph[a];) The #emph[place];, e.g. #bref("Isa 5:17"); #he[כְּדָבְרָם]; #emph[after the manner of];, i.e. #emph[as in their pasture];; 23:15 as (it is said) #emph[in the song of the harlot];; 28:21, 29:7 #he[כַּֽחֲלוֹם]; #emph[as in a dream];.

#ml("u") <p118u> (#emph[b];) The #emph[time];, especially in the combination #he[כְּיוֹם]; #emph[after the manner of the day];, equivalent to #emph[as in the day];, #bref("Isa 9:3");, #bref("Hos 2:5");; #he[כִּימֵי]; #emph[as in the days of]; ..., #bref("Isa 51:9");, #bref("Hos 2:17");, #bref("9:9");, #bref("12:10");, #bref("Amos 9:11");; cf. moreover, #bref("Lev 22:13");, #bref("Judg 20:39");, #bref("Isa 17:6");, #bref("Job 5:14");, #bref("29:2");, and the expressions #he[כְּיוֹם בְּיוֹם]; #emph[as day by day\=as in the former days];, #bref("1 Sam 18:10");; #he[כְּפַ֫עַם בְּפַ֫עַם]; #emph[as at other times];, #bref("1 Sam 3:10");, etc.; #he[כְּשָׁנָה בְשָׁנָה]; #emph[as in former years];, #bref("2 Kgs 17:4");; cf. #xref(<p123c>)[§ 123#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];. Of a different character is the use of #he[כְּ‍]; as a simple particle of time, e.g. #bref("Gen 18:10"); #he[כָּעֵת חַיָּה]; #emph[at this time]; (not #emph[about the time];), when it lives again, i.e. at the end of a year; #he[כָּעֵת מָחָר]; #emph[tomorrow at this time];; cf. #bref("Isa 23:5");, and the frequent connection of #he[כְּ‍]; with the infinitive construct to express a definite time (in the sense of a pluperfect), #bref("Gen 12:14");, #bref("27:34");, #bref("Exod 9:29");, etc.

#ml("v") <p118v> (#emph[c];) The #emph[person];, e.g. #bref("Gen 34:31"); #emph[should he deal with our sister as]; with #emph[a harlot?];

#ml("w") <p118w> (#emph[d];) The #emph[thing];, e.g. #bref("Isa 10:14");, #bref("Ps 33:7");, #bref("Job 28:5"); #he[כְּמוֹ־אֵשׁ]; #emph[as a fire];, i.e. #emph[as it were by fire]; (cf. #bref("Isa 1:25"); #he[כַּבֹּר]; #emph[as]; with #emph[lye];); #bref("Job 29:23"); #he[כַּמָּטָר]; #emph[as]; for #emph[the rain]; (they waited for me); #bref("Job 38:14"); (#emph[as]; in #emph[a garment];); 38:30 #he[כְּאֶבֶן]; #emph[as]; to #emph[stone]; (the waters are solidified in freezing).

#ml("x") <p118x> Rem. According to the earlier grammarians, #he[כְּ‍]; is sometimes used pleonastically, i.e. not to indicate a similarity (as in #bref("Lev 14:35"); #emph[as it were];, i.e. #emph[something like];), but simply to introduce the predicate (#emph[Kaph veritatis];), e.g. #bref("Neh 7:2"); #emph[for he was]; #he[כְּאִישׁ אֱמֶת]; #emph[a faithful man];; cf. #bref("1 Sam 20:3"); #he[כְּפֶשַׂע];, #bref("Lam 1:20"); #he[כַּמָּ֫וֶת];. Such a pleonasm is of course out of the question. At the most a #emph[Kaph veritatis]; can only be admitted in the sense that the comparison is sometimes introduced by #he[כְּ‍]; with a certain emphasis (equivalent to #emph[in every respect like];); thus #he[כְּאִישׁ אֱמֶת]; in #bref("Neh 7:2"); means simply #emph[of the nature of a faithful man];, i.e. as only a faithful man can be; cf. #bref("Num 11:1");, #bref("Isa 1:7");, #bref("13:6");, #bref("Hos 4:4");, #bref("5:10");, #bref("Obad 11");, #bref("Job 24:14");, #bref("27:7");, #bref("Lam 1:20");, #bref("2:4");; also #he[כִּמְעַט]; in such passages as #bref("Ps 105:12"); #emph[yea, very few];; but e.g. in #bref("Isa 1:9"); #emph[only just, a very small];... #origpage("377") <orig-377>;

#sec("119", [The Subordination of Nouns to the Verb by means of Prepositions], note: []) <s119>

#ml("a") <p119a> #strong[1.]; In general. As is the case with regard to the looser subordination of nouns to the verbal idea (§ 118), so also their subordination by means of prepositions is used to represent the more immediate circumstances (of place, time, cause, purpose, measure, association, or separation) under which an action or event is accomplished. In the case of most prepositions some idea of a relation of #emph[space]; underlies the construction, which then, in a wider sense, is extended to the ideas of time, motive, or other relations conceived by the mind.

On the origin of the prepositions and the original case\-relation in which they stand to the nouns governed by them, cf. § 101, where a list of the prepositions is given with their original meanings. Cf. also § 102 on the prefixes, and § 103 on the union of prepositions with suffixes.

#ml("b") <p119b> #strong[2.]; A not unimportant part is played in Hebrew by the #emph[compounding]; of prepositions to represent more accurately the relations of place, which either precede or follow the action. In the former case #he[מִן־];, and in the latter (which is not so frequent) #he[אֶל־]; occurs before other prepositions of place; cf. e.g. #bref("Amos 7:15"); #emph[the Lord took me]; #he[מֵאַֽחֲרֵי הַצֹּאן]; #emph[from behind the flock];; #bref("2 Kgs 9:18"); #emph[turn thee]; #he[אֶל־אַֽחֲרָי]; #emph[to behind me];, i.e., #emph[turn thee behind me];; #he[מֵעִם־];, #he[מֵאֵת]; #emph[from being with]; ..., as in French #emph[de chez, d’après quelqu’un];.#footnote[In other cases French, as well as English and German, can only emphasize #emph[one]; of the two combined ideas; thus, such expressions as #emph[il prend le chapeau sur la table];, German and English #emph[er nimmt den Hut vom Tisch];, #emph[he takes his hat from the table];, all regard the action from one point of view only; the Hebrew here brings out both aspects of it by means of #he[מֵעַל־]; #emph[from upon];, cf. e.g. #bref("Isa 6:6");.]; For further examples, see #emph[c];.

#ml("c") <p119c> Rem. 1. We must #emph[not]; regard as combined prepositions in the above sense either those #emph[substantives]; which have become prepositions only by their union with prefixes, as #he[לִפְנֵי]; #emph[before];, #he[מִפְּנֵי];, #he[לְמַ֫עַן]; #emph[on account of]; (but e.g. #he[מִלִּפְנֵי]; #emph[from before];, #bref("Gen 4:16");, etc., is such a compound); nor #emph[adverbs];, which are also formed by combining words which were originally substantives (also used as prepositions) with prepositions, as #he[מִחוּץ]; #emph[without];, #he[מִתַּ֫חַת]; in the sense of #emph[below];,#footnote[Hence not to be confounded with #he[מִתַּ֫חַת]; #emph[from under];, in such examples as #bref("Prov 22:27");, which is a real compound preposition. In the above\-mentioned adverbs also the #he[מִן־]; was #emph[originally]; by no means pleonastic; #he[מִתַּ֫חַת]; denotes properly the locality, regarded primarily as a place #emph[from beneath]; which something proceeds, and so on. This original sense of the #he[מִן־];, however, has become so much obscured by its regular combination with words of place to form independent adverbs, that it is even prefixed (evidently only on the analogy of such common adverbs as #he[מֵעַל־];, #he[מִתַּ֫חַת];) in cases where it is really inadmissible, owing to the meaning of the adverb, e.g. in #he[מִבַּלְעֲדֵי];, #he[מִלְּבַד]; #emph[without];, cf. also such examples as #he[מִבְּלִי];, #he[מִמּוּל];, #he[מִנֶּ֫גֶד];, #he[מִשָּׁם]; (#emph[there];), etc. Since a #he[מִן־]; is not usually repeated after #he[מִלְּבַד];, it appears as if #he[מִלְּבַד]; by a transposition of the #he[מִן־]; stood for the usual #he[לְבַד מִן־];. In reality, however, the preposition which forms the adverb into a preposition is omitted here, as in #he[מֵעַל];, #he[מִתַּ֫חַת]; without a following #he[לְ]; (see above). Properly #he[מִלְּבַד]; has a purely adverbial meaning\=#emph[taken by itself];, like #he[מִלְּמַ֫עְלָה מִמַּ֫עַל]; (Syriac #emph[men leʿēl];) #emph[above]; (adv.), as distinguished from #he[מִמַּ֫עַל לְ]; or #he[מֵעַל־לְ]; (Syriac #emph[leʿēl men];), #emph[over, upon something];.—Also #he[לְמִן־]; #emph[from ... onward]; is not for #he[מִן־לְ];, but the #he[לְ]; serves merely (just like the Latin #emph[usque]; in #emph[usque a, usque ad, usque ex];) to indicate expressly the starting\-point, as an exact #emph[terminus a quo]; (of place or time).]; #he[מֵעָל]; #origpage("378") <orig-378>;#emph[above]; (so also in #bref("Gen 27:39");, #bref("49:25");, not #emph[from above];). These adverbs of place, however, may become prepositions by the addition of #he[לְ];, e.g. #he[מִחוּץ לְ]; #emph[outside as regards]; ..., i.e. #emph[outside of something];, in #bref("1 Kgs 21:13"); even after a transitive verb of motion; #he[מִתַּ֫חַת לְ]; #emph[below as regards]; ..., i.e. #emph[under something]; (cf. #he[עַד־מִתַּ֫חַת לְ]; #emph[until they came under]; ..., #bref("1 Sam 7:11");), #he[מֵעַל לְ]; #emph[over something];, etc.; #he[לְבַד]; prop. #emph[in separation];; #he[לְבַד מִן־]; #emph[in separation from];, i.e. #emph[apart from, besides];. Only rarely in such a case is the #he[לְ]; omitted for the sake of brevity, e.g. #bref("Job 26:5"); #he[מִתַּ֫חַת מַיִם]; #emph[beneath]; the #emph[waters];; #bref("Neh 3:28"); (#he[מֵעַל־];).

#ml("d") <p119d> 2. Real combinations of prepositions (each retaining its full force) occur—

(#emph[a];) With #he[מִן־];, in #he[מֵאַחַר];, #he[מֵאַֽחֲרֵי]; (see above) #emph[from behind]; something; #he[מֵאֵת]; and #he[מֵעִם]; #emph[from with]; (see above); #he[מִבֵּין]; or #he[מִבֵּינוֹת]; #emph[from between]; something (with motion in either direction, see e.g. #bref("Gen 49:10");); #he[מִלִּפְנֵי]; #emph[from before]; (see above); sometimes also #he[מִמּוּל]; #bref("Lev 5:8");, etc.; #he[מֵעַל־]; #emph[from upon];, i.e. #emph[off from];; #he[מִתַּ֫חַת]; #emph[away from under]; (see footnote 2 on p. 377).

#ml("e") <p119e> (#emph[b];) With #he[אֶל־];, in #he[אֶל־אַֽחֲרֵי]; #emph[to behind];, #he[אֶל־בֵּינוֹת]; #emph[to between];; #he[אֶל־מִבֵּית לְ]; #emph[forth between]; #bref("2 Kgs 11:15");; #he[אֶל־מִחוּץ לְ]; #emph[forth without];, i.e. #emph[out in front of];, #bref("Num 5:3");; #he[אֶל־תַּ֫חַת]; #emph[down under];.#footnote[Also in #bref("1 Sam 21:5"); #he[אֶל־תַּ֫חַת]; by a pregnant construction is virtually dependent on the idea of #emph[coming into];, contained in the preceding #he[אֵין־];.];—In #bref("Job 5:5"); the two prepositions of motion are combined in a peculiarly pregnant construction, #he[אֶל־מִצִּנִּים]; (he goes thither and takes it) #emph[out of the thorns];, i.e. he taketh it #emph[even out of the thorns];, but the text is hardly correct.

#ml("f") <p119f> #strong[3.]; A general view of the union of certain verbs, or whole classes of verbs, with particular prepositions, especially in explanation of certain idioms and pregnant expressions.#footnote[A summary of #emph[all]; the relations and senses in which a preposition may be used, belongs not to the Grammar but to the Lexicon.];

#ml("g") <p119g> (#emph[a];) #he[אֶל־]; (#he[אֱלֵי];)#footnote[Cf. Mitchell, ‘The preposition #emph[el];,’ in the #emph[Journal of the Society of Biblical Literature and Exegesis];, 1888, p. 143 ff., and especially A. Noordtzij, #emph[Het hebreeuwsche voorzetsel]; #he[אל];, Leiden, 1896, a thorough examination of its uses, and especially of the relation between #he[אֶל־]; and #he[עַל־];.]; #emph[towards];, properly an expression of #emph[motion]; or at least #emph[direction]; towards something (either in the sense of #emph[up to];\=#he[עַד];, or #emph[into];\=#he[אֶל־תּוֹךְ];), is used after verbs not only in answer to the question #emph[whither?]; but by a specially pregnant construction, in answer to the question #emph[where?]; e.g. #bref("Jer 41:12"); #emph[they]; #origpage("379") <orig-379>;#emph[found him]; #he[אֶל־מַ֫יִם רַבִּים]; #emph[by the great waters];; cf. #bref("Deut 16:6");, #bref("1 Kgs 13:20");, and a still more remarkable instance in 8:30 #he[אֶל־מְקוֹם שִׁבְתְּךָ אֶל־הַשָּׁמַ֫יִם];. This combination of #emph[two]; different ideas, of motion #emph[to]; a place and being or acting #emph[in]; the place (very plainly seen in #bref("Deut 16:6"); #emph[but to the place which the Lord thy God shall choose];... shalt thou bring thine offering and #emph[there shalt thou sacrifice];, etc.), is the same as the Greek use of εἰς, ἐς for ἐν, the Latin #emph[in potestatem, in amicitiam ditionemque esse, manere]; (Cic. Verr. 5, 38; Div. 2, 14, etc.); cf. also the common German expressions #emph[zu]; Hause, #emph[zu]; Leipzig sein, #emph[zu]; Bette liegen, etc.

#ml("h") <p119h> (#emph[b];) #he[בְּ];.#footnote[Cf. Wandel, #emph[De particulae Hebr.]; #he[בְּ]; #emph[indole, vi, usu];, Jena, 1875.]; Underlying the very various uses of this preposition is either the idea of being or moving within some definite region, or some sphere of space or time (with the infinitive, a simultaneous action, etc.), or else the idea of #emph[fastening on]; something, #emph[close connection with]; something (also in a metaphorical sense, following some kind of pattern, e.g. the advice or command of some one #he[בִּדְבַר פ׳];, #he[בַּֽעֲצַת פ׳];, or in a comparison, as in #bref("Gen 1:26"); #he[בְּצַלְמֵ֫נוּ כִדְמוּתֵ֫נוּ]; #emph[in our image, after our likeness];; cf. 1:27, 5:1, 3), or finally the idea of #emph[relying]; or #emph[depending]; upon..., or even of merely #emph[striking]; or #emph[touching]; something.

#ml("i") <p119i> Thus the use of #he[בְּ]; is explained—

(1) In the sense of #emph[among]; (in the domain of), e.g. #bref("Mic 7:2"); #he[יָשָׁר בָּֽאָדָם אָ֫יִן]; #emph[there is none upright among men];; in the sense of #emph[consisting of];, in specifying the constituents of a collective idea, e.g. #bref("Gen 7:21"); #emph[and all flesh died];... #emph[in]; (\=consisting of) #emph[fowl];, etc. 8:17, 9:10, #bref("Hos 4:3");. Also after ideas of #emph[appearing, manifesting oneself, representing, being];, in the sense of #emph[as, in the capacity of]; (prop. in the sphere, after the manner of, see above), #emph[consisting of];..., #emph[tanquam];, the #he[בְּ]; #emph[essentiae]; of the earlier grammarians, corresponding to the Greek ἐν, the Latin #emph[in];,#footnote[e.g. #emph[res in praeda captae];, i.e. things taken as spoil; see Nägelsbach, #emph[Lat. Stilistik];, § 123:4. On the Hebrew #he[בְּ]; #emph[essentiae];, see Hauschild in the #emph[Festschrift zur Einweihung des Goethegymn];. Frankf. a. M. 1897, p. 163.]; and the French #emph[en];, e.g. #bref("Exod 6:3"); #emph[I appeared unto Abraham];... #he[בְּאֵל שַׁדָּי]; #emph[as El Shaddai];; #bref("Job 23:13"); #he[וְהוּא בְאֶחָד]; #emph[but he is]; (manifests himself as) #emph[one];, i.e. he remains always the same; #bref("Deut 26:5");, #bref("28:62"); #he[בִּמְתֵי מְעָט]; #emph[in the condition of being few];, cf. 10:22 #emph[to the number of seventy];; #bref("Isa 40:10");, #bref("Ps 39:7");.—Cf. also such examples as #bref("Exod 18:4"); (#bref("Ps 35:2");, #bref("146:5");) #he[בְּעֶזְרִי]; #emph[as my help];; #bref("Deut 26:14"); #emph[being unclean];; #bref("Isa 28:16"); #emph[in Sion]; (i.e. #emph[I make Sion a foundation];); #bref("Ezek 20:41"); #emph[as a sweet savour];; #bref("Prov 3:26");, perhaps also #bref("Exod 3:2"); #emph[in]; (i.e. #emph[as];) #emph[a flame of fire];; #bref("Isa 66:15"); #emph[with]; (i.e. #emph[like];) #emph[fire];; #bref("Ps 31:22");, #bref("37:20"); (102:4). For the origin of all these forms of expression #bref("Ps 54:6"); is especially instructive, since #he[אֲדֹנָי בְּסֹֽמְכֵי נַפְשִׁי]; is not meant to refer to the Lord as #emph[belonging to]; the #he[סֹֽמְכִים];, but only to ascribe to him a similar character, i.e. #emph[the Lord is one who upholds my soul];; so also #bref("Ps 99:6");, #bref("118:7");, #bref("Judg 11:35"); \[the plur. as in #xref(<p124g>)[§ 124#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];–i\].#footnote[Other instances formerly cited here (#bref("Isa 26:4");, #bref("Ps 55:19");, where #he[בְּ]; is used before the subject) as well as #bref("Ps 68:5"); #he[בְּיָהּ שְׁמוֹ]; #emph[Jah is his name];, are textually very uncertain. Cf. Cheyne, #emph[SBOT.]; Isaiah, p. 173, on #bref("Isa 26:4");.];—Cf. Gesenius, #emph[Thes. Linguae Hebr.];, i. 174 f., and Delitzsch on #bref("Ps 35:2");.

#ml("k") <p119k> (2) To introduce the object after transitive verbs, which denote #emph[touching, striking, reaching to]; (thus to some extent a fastening on, see above) something, in English sometimes rendered by #emph[at, on];, etc., and in German generally by compounds with #emph[an];, e.g. #emph[anfassen];\=#he[אָחַז בְּ];, #emph[anrühren];\=#he[נָגַע בְּ];, etc. To the same category belongs also the construction of verbs denoting #emph[authority]; (#he[מָלַךְ];, #he[מָשַׁל];, #he[נָגַשׂ];, #he[רָדָה];, the last prop. #emph[to tread on];...) with #he[בְּ];, inasmuch as the exercise of the authority is regarded as a laying hold of the person ruled; so also, the introduction of the object by #he[בְּ]; after certain #emph[verba dicendi];, or when the mental action is to be represented as extending #emph[to]; some one or something: e.g. #origpage("380") <orig-380>;#he[קָרָא בְ]; to call #emph[on]; some one, #he[נִשְׁבַּע בְּ]; #emph[iurare per aliquem];, #he[שָׁאַל בְּ]; #emph[to enquire of]; some one. Again; #he[רָאָה בְ]; #emph[to look upon];, #he[שָׁמַע בְּ]; #emph[to hearken to]; (but cf. also #emph[m];), generally with the secondary idea of participation, or of the pleasure with which one sees or hears anything, especially pleasure at the misfortunes of others, hence #he[רָאָה בְ]; #emph[to see his desire on any one #emph[or]; anything];; cf. however, #bref("Gen 21:16"); #emph[let me not look upon the death of the child];; #bref("1 Sam 6:19"); #emph[because they had looked #emph[\[irreverently\]]; at the ark of the Lord];.

Closely related to this is the use of #he[בְּ];:

#ml("l") <p119l> (3) To introduce the person or thing, which is the object of a #emph[mental]; act, e.g. #he[הֶֽאֱמִין בְּ]; #emph[to trust in]; (to cleave trustingly #emph[to];) somebody or something; #he[בָּטַח בְּ]; #emph[to have confidence in];...; #he[שָׂמַח בְּ]; #emph[to rejoice in]; or #emph[at]; something, etc.; #he[דִּבֶּר בְּ]; #emph[to speak of (about)]; some one or something, #bref("Deut 6:7");, #bref("1 Sam 19:3f.");, etc.

#ml("m") <p119m> (4) The idea of an action as extending #emph[to]; something, with at the same time the secondary idea of participation #emph[in]; something, underlies finally the #emph[partitive]; use of #he[בְּ];, e.g. #he[אָכַל בְּ]; #emph[to share in eating]; something, #bref("Exod 12:43ff.");, #bref("Lev 22:11");; also simply #emph[to eat, to taste of]; something, #bref("Judg 13:16");, #bref("Job 21:25");; so also #he[לָחַם בְּ]; #emph[to eat of];, and #he[שָׁתָה בְ];#footnote[To be distinguished from #he[שָׁתָה בְ];\=#emph[to drink from]; (a cup, etc., #bref("Gen 44:5");, #bref("Amos 6:6");), as in Arabic and Aramaic (#bref("Dan 5:2");). Cf. also #greek[ἐν ποτηρίοις]; (Xen. Anab. vi. 1, 4), #greek[ἐν χρυσώμασι πίνειν]; (#bref("1 Esd 3:6");), #emph[venenum in auro bibitur];, Seneca, Thyestes 453, and the French #emph[boire dans une tasse];.]; #emph[to drink of]; something, #bref("Prov 9:5");; #he[שָׁמַע בְּ]; #emph[to hear]; a whisper #emph[of something];, #bref("Job 26:14");; #he[מָצָא בְּ]; #emph[they found]; remaining #emph[of her]; only..., #bref("2 Kgs 9:35");; #he[נָשָׂא בְ]; #emph[to bear a share of]; something, #bref("Num 11:17");, #bref("Ezek 18:20");, #bref("Job 7:13");. Cf. also #he[חָלַק בְּ]; #emph[to give a share of]; something, #bref("Job 39:17");; #he[בָּנָה בְ]; #emph[to do building to];, #bref("Neh 4:4");.

#ml("n") <p119n> (5) With the idea of #emph[touching, striking against anything]; is naturally connected that of proximity and vicinity #emph[near];, and further that of association #emph[with]; something; cf. #bref("Gen 9:4"); #he[בְּנַפְשׁוֹ]; #emph[with the life thereof];; 15:14, 32:11 #he[בְּמַקְלִי]; #emph[with my staff];. Sometimes #he[בְּ]; combined with a verb of motion (#emph[to come with]; something), expresses the idea of #emph[bringing];, e.g. #bref("Judg 15:1"); #emph[Samson visited his wife with a kid];, i.e. he brought her a kid; #bref("Deut 23:5");, #bref("Ps 66:13");, #bref("1 Chr 15:19"); ff., 16:6.

#ml("o") <p119o> (6) From the idea of connection with something, being accompanied by something (see #emph[n];), is developed, finally, the #emph[instrumental]; use of #he[בְּ];, which represents the means or instrument (or even the personal agent), as something #emph[with which]; one has associated himself in order to perform an action; cf. #bref("Mic 4:14"); #he[בַּשֵּׁ֫בֶט]; #emph[they smite with the rod];; #bref("Isa 10:24");; #bref("Ps 18:30"); #he[בְּךָ]; #emph[by thee]; (so also 44:6, parallel with #he[בְּשִׁמְךָ];); #bref("Isa 10:34");, #bref("Hos 1:7");, #bref("12:14");; cf. also #he[עָבַד בְּ]; #emph[to labour by means of]; some one, i.e. to cause him to labour at it, #bref("Exod 1:14");, etc. On #he[בְּ]; with the passive to introduce the means or the author, see #xref(<p121f>)[§ 121#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];.

#ml("p") <p119p> A variety of the #he[בְּ]; #emph[instrumenti]; is #he[בְּ]; #emph[pretii]; (the price being considered as the #emph[means]; of acquiring a thing), cf. #bref("Gen 23:9");, #bref("29:18"); (#he[בְּרָחֵל];); 30:16, 33:19, 34:15 (#he[בְּזֹאת]; #emph[on this]; condition); 37:28; also, in a wider sense, #bref("Gen 18:28"); #he[בְּ]; #emph[for the sake of];; #bref("1 Sam 3:13");.

#ml("q") <p119q> Rem. The use of #he[בְּ]; #emph[instrumenti]; to introduce the object is peculiar in such expressions as #bref("Ps 44:20"); #emph[and thou coveredst over us]; #he[בְּצַלְמָ֫וֶת]; #emph[with the shadow of death];; #bref("Job 16:10"); #he[פָּֽעֲרוּ עָלַי בְּפִיהֶם]; #emph[they have opened wide their mouth against me]; (prop. #emph[have made an opening with their mouth];); cp. #bref("Ps 22:8");, #bref("Exod 7:20"); #emph[he lifted up]; #he[בַּמַּטֶּה]; #emph[the rod];; #bref("Lev 16:4"); #he[חָגַר]; and #he[צָנַף]; followed by #he[בְּ];; #bref("Josh 8:18");, #bref("Lam 1:17");. Analogous to some English expressions we find both #emph[to gnash the teeth];, #bref("Ps 35:16");, and #emph[to gnash with the teeth];, #bref("Job 16:9");; #emph[to wink the eye];, #bref("Prov 10:10");, and #emph[to wink with the eye];, #bref("Prov 6:13");; #emph[shake the head];, #bref("Ps 22:8");, and #emph[to shake with the head];, #bref("Jer 18:16");, #bref("Job 16:4");.—In all these instances #origpage("381") <orig-381>;the verb (intransitive) construed with #he[בְּ]; has a greater independence, and consequently more emphasis than the verb construed with a direct accusative; the latter conveys a sort of necessary specification of the action, while the noun introduced by #he[בְּ]; is used rather as a merely adverbial complement. An instructive example of this is #he[נָתַן קוֹל]; #emph[vocem emittere];, #emph[to utter a voice];, also #emph[to thunder];, while in #he[נָתַן בְּקוֹלוֹ]; #bref("Ps 46:7"); (68:34, #bref("Jer 12:8");), #he[נָתַן]; has an independent sense \= #emph[he thundered with his voice]; (i.e. mightily).

#ml("r") <p119r> (#emph[c];) #he[לְ];#footnote[Cf. Giesebrecht, #emph[Die hebr. Präpos. Lamed];, Halle, 1876.]; #emph[to];, a very general expression of #emph[direction towards]; anything, is used to represent the most varied #emph[relations]; of an action or state with regard to a person or thing. On the use of #he[לְ]; as a periphrasis for the #emph[genetivus possessoris]; or #emph[auctoris]; (the idea of belonging to), see § 129; on #he[לְ]; with the passive, to introduce the author or the cause, see #xref(<p121f>)[§ 121#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];; on #he[לְ]; in a purely local sense (e.g. #he[לִימִֽינְךָ]; #emph[at thy right hand];, prop. #emph[towards thy right hand];), or temporal (e.g. #he[לָעֶ֫רֶב]; #emph[at evening];, etc.) or distributive, see the Lexicon.

The following uses of #he[לְ]; properly belong to the government of the verb:

#ml("s") <p119s> (1) As a #emph[nota dativi];#footnote[Just as in the Romance languages the Latin preposition #emph[ad]; (Italian #emph[a];, before vowels #emph[ad];, French #emph[à];, Spanish #emph[á];) and in English #emph[to]; are used as a periphrasis for the dative.—On the introduction of the nearer object by #he[לְ];, cf. #xref(<p117n>)[§ 117#h(0.18em)#emph[n];];.]; to introduce the remoter object; also

(2) To introduce the #emph[dativus commodi];. This #emph[dativus commodi]; (or #emph[incommodi];, e.g. #bref("Ezek 37:11");) is used—especially in colloquial language and in later style—in the form of a pronoun with #he[לְ];, as an apparently pleonastic #emph[dativus ethicus];, with many verbs, in order to give emphasis to the significance of the occurrence in question #emph[for]; a particular subject. In this construction the person of the pronoun must always agree with that of the verbal form.#footnote[Such expressions as the analogous English #emph[he plucked me ope his doublet, but me no buts];, and the like, are accordingly inadmissible in Hebrew.]; By far the most frequent use of this #he[לְ]; is with the pronoun of the 2nd person after imperatives, e.g. #he[לֶךְ־לְךָ]; #emph[go, get thee away];, #bref("Gen 12:1");, #bref("22:2");, #bref("Deut 2:13"); (also in the feminine, #bref("Song 2:10");, 13); #he[נְטֵה לְךָ]; #emph[turn thee aside];, #bref("2 Sam 2:21");; #he[סְעוּ לָכֶם]; #emph[take your journey];, #bref("Deut 1:7");; #he[עִבְרוּ לָכֶם]; #emph[pass ye over];; #he[בְּרַח־לְךָ]; #emph[flee]; (to save thyself), #bref("Gen 27:43");; #he[עֲלִי־לָךְ]; #emph[get thee up];, #bref("Isa 40:9");; #he[פְּנוּ לָכֶם]; #emph[turn you];, #bref("Deut 1:40");; #he[שׁוּבוּ לָכֶם]; #emph[return ye];, #bref("Deut 5:27");; #he[ק֫וּמִי לָךְ]; #emph[rise up];, #bref("Song 2:10");; #he[שְׁבוּ לָכֶם]; #emph[abide ye];, #bref("Gen 22:5");; #he[חֲדַל לְךָ]; #emph[forbear thee];, #bref("2 Chr 35:21"); (in the plural, #bref("Isa 2:22");); #he[הָ֫בוּ לָכֶם]; #emph[take you];, #bref("Deut 1:13");, #bref("Josh 18:4");, #bref("Judg 20:7");, #bref("2 Sam 16:20");, and so almost regularly #he[הִשָּׁ֫מֶר לְךָ]; (see above, #xref(<p51n>)[§ 51#h(0.18em)#emph[n];];) #emph[cave tibi!]; and #he[הִשָּֽׁמְלוּ לָכֶם]; #emph[take heed to yourselves];; #he[דְמֵה לְךָ]; #emph[be thou like];, #bref("Song 2:17"); (cf. verse 9), 8:14, is remarkable; after a perfect consecutive, #bref("1 Kgs 17:3");, #bref("1 Sam 22:5");; after an imperfect consecutive, e.g. #bref("Isa 36:9"); #he[וַתִּבְטַח לְךָ]; #emph[and puttest thy trust];.—In the 3rd person, e.g. #he[וַתֵּ֫שֶׁב לָהּ]; #emph[and sat her down];, #bref("Gen 21:16");, cf. 22:5, #bref("Exod 18:27");, #bref("Ps 120:6");, #bref("123:4");, #bref("Job 6:19");; even after a participle, #bref("Hos 8:9");.—In the 1st person plural, #bref("Ezek 37:11");.

#ml("t") <p119t> (3) To introduce the result after verbs of making, forming, changing, appointing #emph[to]; something, esteeming #emph[as]; something; in short, in all those cases in which, according to #xref(<p117ii>)[§ 117#h(0.18em)#emph[ii];];, a second #emph[accusative]; may also be used.

#ml("u") <p119u> (4) In loose connection with some verbal idea in the sense of #emph[in reference to, with regard to];... (#xref(<p143e>)[§ 143#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];); so after a #emph[verbum dicendi];, #bref("Gen 20:13");; #bref("1 Kgs 10:23");, cf. #origpage("382") <orig-382>;#bref("Isa 36:9");; even before the verb, #bref("Jer 9:2");.—To the same class belongs also the #emph[Lamedh inscriptionis]; (untranslatable in English, and hardly more than a mere quotation\-mark) which introduces the exact wording of an inscription or title; thus #bref("Isa 8:1"); #emph[write upon it];... (the words) #he[מַהֵר שָׁלָל וגו׳]; (cf. verse 3, where the #he[לְ]; naturally is not used); #bref("Ezek 37:16");.

#ml("v") <p119v> (#emph[d];) #he[מִן];, originally (according to #xref(<p101a>)[§ 101#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];) #emph[separation];,#footnote[Cf. O. Molin, #emph[Om prepositionen]; min #emph[i Bibelhebreisken];, Upsala, 1893, and especially N. Zerweck, #emph[Die hebr. Praep.]; min, Leipzig, 1893, who, instead of the partitive meaning (formerly accepted by us also), more correctly takes ‘separation’ as the starting\-point of its various uses.]; represents both the idea of #emph[distance, separation]; or #emph[remoteness from]; something, and that of #emph[motion away from]; something, hence also #emph[descent, origin from]; a place, #bref("Amos 1:1");.

#ml("w") <p119w> (1) From the idea of #emph[separation]; is naturally derived on the one hand the sense of (#emph[taken];) #emph[from among];..., #emph[e numero];, e.g. #bref("Gen 3:1"); #emph[subtil as none other of the beasts];, etc.; cf. 3:14, #bref("Deut 33:24");, #bref("1 Sam 15:33");, #bref("Judg 5:24"); (so especially after the idea of choosing #emph[out of];#footnote[All the #emph[partitive]; uses of #he[מִן]; also come most naturally under this idea of separation #emph[out of]; a larger class. Thus #he[מִן]; is used in the sense of #emph[some, something];, and even #emph[one];, in such expressions as #emph[and he slew... also]; #he[מִשָּׂרֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל]; (#emph[divers];) #emph[of the princes of Israel];, #bref("2 Chr 21:4");; #he[מִכָּל־]; #bref("Lev 4:2");; #bref("1 Kgs 18:5");; #he[מִדַּם הַפָּר]; #emph[some of the blood of the bullock];, #bref("Exod 29:12");, etc.; #bref("Job 27:6"); #emph[my heart doth not reproach me]; #he[מִיָּמַי]; #emph[for any];, i.e. for one, #emph[of my days];; 38:12 #he[מִיָּמֶ֫יךָ]; #emph[one of thy days];, i.e. #emph[ever in thy life]; (this explanation is confirmed by #bref("1 Kgs 1:6");; cf. also #bref("1 Sam 14:45");, #bref("25:28");). In this way also, the frequently misunderstood Hebrew (and Arabic) idiom is to be explained, by which #he[מִן]; before #he[אֶחָד];, #he[אַחַת]; is equivalent to #emph[ullus];; e.g. #bref("Lev 4:2"); #emph[and shall do]; #he[מֵֽאַחַת מֵהֵ֫נָּה]; #emph[any one of these things];; 5:13, #bref("Deut 15:7");, #bref("Ezek 18:10");; so before a #emph[nomen unitatis]; (see #xref(<p122t>)[§ 122#h(0.18em)#emph[t];];), #bref("1 Sam 14:45"); (#bref("2 Sam 14:11");, #bref("1 Kgs 1:52");) #he[מִשַּֽׂעֲרַת רֹאשׁוֹ]; #emph[not one hair of his head];.—#he[מִן־]; is used in the sense of the Arabic #emph[min el\-beyān]; or explicative #emph[min]; (often to be simply translated by #emph[namely];), e.g. in #bref("Gen 7:22"); #emph[of all that was];, i.e. #emph[so far as it was];, probably also #bref("Gen 6:2"); (\=#emph[whomsoever they chose];).]; a larger class, #bref("1 Sam 2:28");; cf. #bref("Exod 19:5");, etc.), and on the other hand, the sense of #emph[without]; (separated, free from...), e.g. #bref("Isa 22:3"); #he[מִקֶּ֫שֶׁת אֻסָּ֫רוּ]; #emph[without the bow]; (i.e. without one needing to bend a bow against them) #emph[they were made prisoners];; cf. #bref("Jer 48:45"); #he[מִכֹּחַ]; #emph[without strength];; #bref("Hos 6:6");, as the first half\-verse shows, not #emph[more than burnt offerings]; (as R.V.), but #emph[and not burnt offerings];; #bref("Mic 3:6");, #bref("Ps 52:5");, #bref("Job 11:15");, #bref("19:26");, #bref("21:9");, also such examples as #bref("Num 15:24"); #emph[far from the eyes];, i.e. unobserved by the congregation; #bref("Prov 20:3");.

#ml("x") <p119x> Here also belongs the use of #he[מִן]; after the ideas of #emph[restraining, withholding from, refusing to]; any one, frequently in pregnant expressions, which we can render only by complete final or consecutive clauses, e.g. #bref("1 Sam 15:23"); #emph[he hath rejected thee]; #he[מִמֶּ֫לֶךְ]; #emph[away from]; (being) #emph[king];, instead of #he[מִֽהְיוֹת מ׳]; (as in verse 26), #emph[that thou be no longer king];; cf. #bref("1 Kgs 15:13");, #bref("Isa 17:1"); #he[מֵעִיר]; #emph[so that it is no longer a city];; #bref("Jer 17:16");, #bref("Job 28:11"); #emph[he bindeth the streams]; #he[מִבְּכִי]; #emph[that they trickle not];; #bref("Gen 16:2");, #bref("23:6"); #he[מִקְּבֹר]; #emph[that thou shouldst not bury thy dead];; #bref("Isa 24:10");.

#ml("y") <p119y> The #he[מִן]; has a still more pregnant force in those examples in which the idea of precluding from anything is only indirectly contained in the preceding verb, e.g. #bref("Gen 27:1"); #emph[his eyes were dim]; #he[מֵֽרְאֹת]; #emph[away from seeing];, i.e. #emph[so that he could not see];; #bref("Isa 7:8"); #emph[Ephraim shall be broken in pieces]; #he[מֵעָם]; #emph[that it be not a people]; (just as in #bref("Isa 23:1");, #bref("Jer 48:2");, 42, #bref("Ps 83:5");); #bref("Lev 26:13");, #bref("Isa 5:6");, #bref("49:15");, #bref("54:9");, #bref("Ezra 2:62"); (for other pregnant constructions with #he[מִן]; see below, #emph[ff];)#footnote[On the use of #he[מִן]; to express the comparative, which likewise depends on the idea of #emph[distance from...];, cf. below, #xref(<p133a>)[§ 133#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];; on #he[מִן]; as expressing the distance of time from a fixed limit, in the sense of #emph[after];, e.g. #bref("Ps 73:20"); #he[מֵהָקִיץ]; #emph[after awaking]; (cf. ἐξ ἀρίστου, #emph[ab itinere];), or #emph[after the lapse of...];, e.g. #bref("Gen 38:24");, #bref("Hos 6:2");, and very frequently #he[מִקֵּץ]; #emph[from the end of];, i.e. #emph[after the lapse of...];, see the Lexicon; also for the use of #he[מִן]; to represent #emph[resting beside]; anything, like the Latin #emph[prope abesse ab];...];; on #he[מִבְּלִי]; and #he[מֵאֵין]; #emph[without];, cf. #xref(<p152y>)[§ 152#h(0.18em)#emph[y];];. #origpage("383") <orig-383>;

#ml("z") <p119z> (2) On the sense of #emph[motion away from]; anything depends the use of #he[מִן]; after such ideas as #emph[to take away from, to beware, to be afraid of, to flee, to escape, to hide oneself from]; (cf. καλύπτω ἀπό, #emph[custodire ab];), sometimes again in pregnant expressions, e.g. #bref("Isa 33:15");. On the idea of #emph[starting from]; anything depends finally the very frequent #emph[causative]; use of #he[מִן]; #emph[on account of, in consequence of]; (cf. our #emph[that comes from];...), #emph[prae];, e.g. #he[מֵרֹב]; #emph[for multitude];, #bref("1 Kgs 8:5");.

#ml("aa") <p119aa> (#emph[e];) #he[עַל־];.#footnote[Cf. Budie, #emph[Die hebr. Präpos];. #emph[ʿAl]; (#he[עַל];), Halle, 1882.]; The two original local meanings of this preposition are #emph[upon]; (ἐπί)#footnote[Since the placing #emph[upon]; anything is an addition to it, #he[עַל־]; also implies #emph[in addition to]; something, cf. #bref("Gen 28:9"); (31:50); 30:40, 32:12 (probably a proverbial saying\=#emph[mother and children];); #bref("Deut 22:6");. Also #he[עַל]; #emph[notwithstanding]; is no doubt properly #emph[in addition to];, e.g. #bref("Job 10:7"); #emph[although thou knowest];, prop. #emph[in addition to thy knowing];.—From the original meaning #emph[upon]; is also derived that of #emph[on account of]; (prop. #emph[upon the ground of];) and #emph[in agreement with, according to];, since the pattern is regarded as the foundation #emph[upon]; which a thing stands or rests.]; and #emph[over]; (ὑπέρ, #emph[super];).

(1) From the original meaning #emph[upon]; is explained the use of #he[עַל־]; after ideas of #emph[commanding, commissioning]; (#he[פָּקַד עַל־];), etc., inasmuch as the command, obligation, etc., is #emph[laid upon]; the object. The construction is self\-evident in the case of #emph[to lie, rest, lean, rely, press upon]; something; cf. also, for the last, such examples as #bref("Isa 1:14");, #bref("Job 7:20");, #bref("23:2");, and especially #bref("2 Sam 18:11"); #he[וְעָלַי]; prop. #emph[upon me]; would it have been, it would have been incumbent #emph[upon me];, etc.

#ml("bb") <p119bb> (2) From the original meaning #emph[over]; is explained the use of #he[עַל־]; after ideas of #emph[covering, protecting, guarding]; #he[כָּסָה עַל־];, #he[גָּנַן עַל־];; also the combinations #he[רִחַם עַל־]; #emph[to have compassion upon];..., #he[חוּס עַל־];, #he[חָמַל עַל־]; #emph[to spare]; some one, arise from the idea of a compassionate or protective #emph[bending over]; something. Cf. also #he[נִלְחַם עַל־]; #bref("Judg 9:17"); \= #emph[to fight for]; some one, i.e. in his defence.

#ml("cc") <p119cc> (3) Moreover #he[עַל־]; is used after verbs of #emph[standing]; and #emph[going];, to express a towering #emph[over]; some one or something, sometimes in phrases, in which the original local idea has altogether fallen into the background, and which are therefore to be rendered in English by means of other prepositions (#emph[by, with, before, near];), e.g. #bref("Gen 41:1");, etc., #emph[Pharaoh... stood]; #he[עַל־הַיְאֹר]; #emph[by the Nile]; (above the water level; cf. #bref("Ps 1:3");), and so especially #he[עָמַד עַל־]; in the pregnant sense #emph[to stand serving before]; some one (prop. #emph[over]; one who sits or reclines at table) #bref("Zech 4:14"); (cf. #bref("Isa 6:2");, where #he[מִמַּ֫עַל לְ]; is used for #he[עַל־];); #he[הִתְיַצֵּב עַל־]; #emph[to present oneself]; by command #emph[before]; some one, #bref("Job 1:6");, etc. Cf. also #he[עַל־יַד];, #he[עַל־יְדֵי]; (#bref("Job 1:14");) #emph[near, at (on) the side]; of some one or something.

#ml("dd") <p119dd> (4) From the original meaning #emph[above]; (not, as formerly explained, #emph[on to]; something, #emph[at]; something) there arise finally all the various constructions with #he[עַל־]; in the sense of #emph[towards, against];. The original idea (which in many of these constructions has become wholly unrecognizable) starts from the view that the assailant endeavours to take up his position #emph[over]; the person attacked, so as to reach him from above, or to #emph[over]; power him; cf. especially #he[קוּם עַל־]; to rise up #emph[over];, i.e. #emph[against]; some one, then with a transference of #origpage("384") <orig-384>;thought applied to any kind of hostile approach, #he[נִלְחַם עַל־]; #emph[to fight against...];, #he[חָנָה עַל־]; #emph[to encamp against...];, #he[נֶֽאֱסַף עַל־]; #emph[to be gathered together, to assemble against]; (#bref("Mic 4:11");; cf. #bref("Ps 2:2");), etc.; even after verbs which express a mental action, e.g. #he[חָשַׁב רָעָה עַל־]; #emph[to imagine evil against]; any one, etc.

#ml("ee") <p119ee> #strong[4.]; Sometimes a preposition appears to be under the immediate government of a verb, which, by its meaning, excludes such a union. In reality the preposition is dependent on a verb (generally a verb of motion), which, for the sake of brevity, is not expressed, but in sense is contained in what is apparently the governing verb.

#ml("ff") <p119ff> Various examples of this #emph[constructio praegnans]; have been already noticed above in #emph[x]; and #emph[y]; under #he[מִן־];; for #he[מִן־]; cf. also #bref("Ps 22:22"); #he[וּמִקַּרְנֵי רֵמִים עֲנִיתָ֫נִי]; #emph[and thou hast answered]; and saved #emph[me from the horns of the wild oxen]; (in #bref("Isa 38:17");, which Delitzsch translates by #emph[thou hast loved]; and delivered #emph[my soul from the pit];, read #he[חָשַׂ֫כְתָּ]; with the LXX); #bref("Gen 25:23");, #bref("2 Sam 18:19");, #bref("Job 28:12");; cf. also #he[זָנָה מִן־]; #bref("Ps 73:27"); #emph[to go a whoring from]; any one i.e. #emph[to be unfaithful to him];; #he[רָשַׁע מִן־]; #bref("Ps 18:22"); \= #emph[to depart wickedly from God];; #he[חָרַשׁ מִן־]; #bref("Ps 28:1"); #emph[to be silent from]; one (to turn away in silence); cf. #bref("Job 13:13"); \[; so with #he[מֵעַל]; #bref("Job 30:17");, 30\].

#ml("gg") <p119gg> Pregnant constructions with #he[אַחֲרֵי];: #bref("Num 14:24"); equivalent to #he[וַיְמַלֵּא לָלֶ֫כֶת אַֽחֲרָ֑י]; #emph[and he made full to walk]; i.e. walked fully #emph[after me];; in #bref("1 Sam 13:7"); read with the LXX #he[חָֽרְדוּ מֵאַֽחֲרֶיו]; #emph[they trembled];, i.e. #emph[went trembling away from him];; with #he[אֶל־]; #bref("Gen 43:33"); #he[תָּמַהּ אֶל־]; #emph[to turn in astonishment to]; some one (cf. #bref("Isa 13:8");); #he[דָּרַשׁ אֶל־]; #bref("Isa 11:10");, etc., #emph[to turn inquiringly to]; some one; #he[הֶֽחֱרִישׁ אֶל־]; #bref("Isa 41:1"); #emph[to turn in silence to]; some one; #he[חָרַד אֶל־]; #bref("Gen 42:28"); #emph[to turn trembling to]; some one (cf. #he[חָרַד לִקְרַאת]; #emph[to come trembling to meet];, #bref("1 Sam 21:2"); \[also with #he[שאג];, #he[הריץ];, #he[שמח]; and other verbs, #bref("Judg 14:5");, #bref("15:14");, #bref("19:3");; see Lexicon\]); cf. further #bref("Jer 41:7");, #bref("Ps 7:7");, #bref("2 Chr 32:1");; with #he[בְּ]; #bref("Ps 55:19"); #emph[he hath redeemed]; and hath put #emph[my soul in peace];, exactly like #bref("Ps 118:5");; with #he[לְ]; #bref("Ps 74:7"); #emph[they have profaned]; and cast... #emph[even to the ground];; cf. 89:40.

#ml("hh") <p119hh> #strong[5.]; In poetic parallelism the governing power of a preposition is sometimes extended to the corresponding substantive of the second member;#footnote[Similarly the force of a negative is sometimes extended to the parallel member; see #xref(<p152z>)[§ 152#h(0.18em)#emph[z];];.]; e.g. #he[בְּ]; #bref("Isa 40:19");, #bref("48:14"); #emph[he shall perform his pleasure]; #he[בְּבָבֶל]; #emph[on Babylon, and his arm]; shall be #he[כַּשְׂדִּים]; (for #he[בַּכַּשְׂדִּים];) #emph[on the Chaldaeans];; #bref("Job 15:3");; #he[לְ]; #bref("Isa 28:6");, #bref("42:22"); (but probably #he[לְ]; has fallen out after another #he[ל];), #bref("Ezek 39:4");, #bref("Job 34:10"); (perhaps also #bref("Gen 45:8");; #he[מֹשֵׁל]; may, however, be taken here as a second accusative according to #xref(<p117ii>)[§ 117#h(0.18em)#emph[ii];];); #he[לְמַ֫עַן]; #bref("Isa 48:9");; #he[מִן־]; #bref("Isa 58:13");, #bref("Ps 141:9"); (unless #he[וּמִמֹּֽקְשׁוֹת]; is to be read); #he[עַד־]; #bref("Isa 15:8");; #he[תַּ֫חַת]; #bref("Isa 61:7");.

#ml("ii") <p119ii> #strong[6.]; Adverbs which have acquired a substantival value are sometimes governed by prepositions, e.g. #he[אֶל־חִנָּם]; #emph[in vain];, #bref("Ezek 6:10");; #he[אַחֲרֵי־כֵן]; #emph[after this];; #he[בְּכֵן]; (#bref("Eccl 8:10");, #bref("Esth 4:16");) #emph[then, on this condition];; #he[לָכֵן]; and #he[עַל־כֵּן]; #emph[therefore];; #he[עַד־כֵּן]; #emph[hitherto];. #origpage("385") <orig-385>;

#sec("120", [Verbal Ideas under the Government of a Verb. Co\-ordination of Complementary Verbal Ideas], note: []) <s120>

#ml("a") <p120a> #strong[1.]; When a #emph[relative]; verb (incomplete in itself) receives its necessary complement in the form of a verbal idea, the latter is, as a rule, subordinated in the infinitive construct (with or without #he[לְ];), less frequently in the infinitive absolute, in a few instances in the form of a participle (or verbal adjective), or finally in the imperfect without the copula. In these combinations the principal idea is very frequently represented by the subordinate member of the sentence, while the governing verb rather contains a mere definition of the manner of the action; cf. #emph[d]; and #emph[g]; below, and #xref(<p114n>)[§ 114#h(0.18em)#emph[n];];, note 2.

#ml("b") <p120b> (#emph[a];) On the subordination of an infinitive construct as an accusative of the object, and as the complement of relative verbal ideas, see above, #xref(<p114c>)[§ 114#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];, and the numerous examples given in #xref(<p114m>)[§ 114#h(0.18em)#emph[m];];; on the infinitive absolute as object, see #xref(<p113d>)[§ 113#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];.—The complement in the form of a participle (as in Greek, and also frequently in Syriac) occurs in #bref("Isa 33:1"); #he[כַּֽהֲתִֽמְךָ שׁוֹדֵד]; (cf. for the form, #xref(<p67v>)[§ 67#h(0.18em)#emph[v];];) #emph[when thou hast ceased as a spoiler];, i.e. to spoil; #bref("Jer 22:30"); #he[לֹא יִצְלַח... יֹשֵׁב]; #emph[he shall never prosper, sitting];, i.e. so as to sit, etc.; #bref("Jonah 1:6"); #emph[what meanest thou, sleeping?]; i.e. that thou sleepest;#footnote[In #he[יֹדֵעַ מְנַגֵּן]; #bref("1 Sam 16:16");, which appears to be a case of this kind, two different readings are combined, #he[יֹדֵעַ לְנַגֵּן]; and the simple #he[מְנַגֵּן];.]; by a verbal adjective, #bref("1 Sam 3:2"); #emph[now his eyes]; #he[הֵחֵ֫לּוּ כֵהוֹת]; #emph[had begun being dim];, i.e. to wax dim (unless we read #he[כְּהוֹת];\=#he[לִכְהוֹת];, cf. #xref(<p114m>)[§ 114#h(0.18em)#emph[m];];); by a substantive, #bref("Gen 9:20"); #emph[and Noah began]; to be #emph[an husbandman]; (omitting the article before #he[אֲדָמָה];).

#ml("c") <p120c> (#emph[b];) Examples of the subordination of the complementary verbal idea in the imperfect#footnote[This kind of subordination is frequent in Arabic and in Syriac (cf. e.g. the Peshiṭtâ, Luke 18:13); as a rule, however, a conjunction (corresponding to our #emph[that];) is inserted. Cf. moreover, the Latin #emph[quid vis faciam?]; Terence; #emph[volo hoc oratori contingat];, Cicero, Brut. 84; and our #emph[I would it were; I thought he would go];.]; (in English usually rendered by #emph[to, in order to]; or #emph[that];) are—(1) with both verbs in the same person: after the perfect, #bref("Isa 42:21"); #he[יְהֹוָה חָפֵץ... יַגְדִּיל]; #emph[it pleased the Lord... to magnify];, etc.; #bref("Job 30:28");, #bref("32:22"); #he[לֹא יָדַ֫עְתִּי אֲכַנֶּה]; #emph[I know not to give flattering titles];; after a perfect consecutive, #bref("1 Sam 20:19"); (where for #he[תֵּרֵד]; we should read with the LXX #he[תִּפָּקֵד];); after an imperfect, #bref("Ps 88:11");, #bref("102:14");, #bref("Job 19:3");, #bref("24:14");; after an imperf. consec., #bref("Job 16:8");; after a participle, #bref("Isa 5:11");a.—(2) with a difference in the persons: after a perfect, #bref("Lev 9:6"); #emph[this is the thing]; #he[אֲשֶׁר־צִוָּה יְהֹוָה תַּֽעֲשׂוּ]; #emph[which the Lord commanded]; (that) #emph[ye should do];; a negative imperfect follows #he[צִוָּה]; in #bref("Lam 1:10");; after the imperfect, #bref("Isa 47:1"); (#super[5];) #he[כִּי לֹא תוֹסִ֫יפִי עוֹד יִקְרְאוּ־לָךְ]; #emph[for thou shalt no more continue]; (that) #emph[they call thee];, i.e. thou shalt no longer be called, etc.; #bref("Hos 1:6"); #he[לֹא אוֹסִיף עוֹד אֲרַחֵם]; #emph[I will no longer continue]; (and) #emph[have mercy];, i.e. #emph[I will no more have mercy];; #bref("Isa 52:1");, #bref("Prov 23:35");.—#bref("Num 22:6"); #emph[peradventure I shall prevail]; (that) #emph[we may smite them, and]; (that) #emph[I may drive them out of the land]; (#he[אוּכַל]; may, however, be a scribal error for #he[נוּכַל];, due to the preceding #he[אוּלַי];, and in that case the example would belong to No. 1); after a participle, #origpage("386") <orig-386>;#bref("2 Sam 21:4");.—A perfect is possibly subordinated in #bref("Lam 1:10");; but the explanation of #he[בָּ֫אוּ]; as a relative clause is preferable.

#ml("d") <p120d> #strong[2.]; Instead of subordination (as in the cases mentioned in #emph[a];–#emph[c];), the #emph[co\-ordination]; of the complementary verbal idea in the finite verb (cf. above, #emph[c];) frequently occurs, either—

(#emph[a];) With the second verb co\-ordinated in a form exactly corresponding to the first (but see below, #emph[e];) by means of #he[וְ]; (#he[וַ];, #he[וָ];).#footnote[Cf. the English colloquial expression #emph[I will try and do it];.]; As a rule, here also (see above, #emph[a];) the principal idea is introduced only by the second verb, while the first (especially #he[שׁוּב];, #he[יָסַף];#footnote[Of a different kind are the cases in which #he[יָסַף]; with a negative is co\-ordinated with a verb to emphasize the non\-recurrence of the action; cf. #bref("Num 11:25"); #emph[they prophesied and added not];, sc. #emph[to prophesy];, i.e. #emph[but they did so no more];; #bref("Deut 5:19");, #bref("Job 27:19"); (reading #he[וְלֹא יֹאסִיף];).];, #he[הוֹסִיף];) contains the definition of the manner of the action, e.g. #bref("Gen 26:18"); #he[וַיָּ֫שָׁב וַיַּחְפֹּר]; #emph[and he returned and digged];, i.e. he digged again; #bref("2 Kgs 1:11");, 13; in the perfect consecutive, #bref("Isa 6:13");; with #he[הוֹסִיף];, e.g. #bref("Gen 25:1"); #emph[and Abraham added and took a wife];, i.e. again took a wife; #bref("Gen 38:5"); and frequently; with #he[הוֹאִיל]; in the jussive, #bref("Job 6:9");; in the imperative (cf. #xref(<p110h>)[§ 110#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];), #bref("Judg 19:6"); #he[הֽוֹאֶל־נָא וְלִין]; #emph[be content, I pray thee, and tarry all night]; (cf. the English #emph[he was persuaded and remained];, for #emph[to remain];); #bref("2 Sam 7:29");; with #he[מִהֵר]; #bref("Gen 24:18");, 20, etc.; with #he[חִמַּד]; #bref("Song 2:3");.

#ml("e") <p120e> Rem. 1. Instead of an exact agreement between co\-ordinate verbal forms, other combinations sometimes occur, namely imperfect and perfect consecutive (cf. #xref(<p112d>)[§ 112#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];), e.g. #bref("Deut 31:12"); #emph[that they]; #he[יִלְמְדוּ וְיָרְֽאוּ אֶת־יְהֹוָה]; #emph[may learn, and fear the Lord];, i.e. #emph[to fear the Lord];; #bref("Isa 1:19");, #bref("Hos 2:11");, #bref("Esth 8:6");, #bref("Dan 9:25");b; perfect and imperfect, #bref("Job 23:3"); (#emph[O that I knew how I might find him];); perfect and imperfect consecutive, #bref("Josh 7:7");, #bref("Eccl 4:1");, 7; jussive and imperative, #bref("Job 17:10");; cf., finally, #bref("Gen 47:6"); #he[וְאִם־יָדַ֫עְתָּ וְיֶשׁ־בָּם]; #emph[and if thou knowest and there are among them];, etc., i.e. that there are among them.

#ml("f") <p120f> 2. Special mention must be made of the instances in which the natural complement of the first verb is suppressed, or is added immediately after in the form of an historical statement, e.g. #bref("Gen 42:25"); #emph[then Joseph commanded and they filled];#footnote[Cf. the analogous examples in Kautzsch’s #emph[Gramm. des Bibl. Aram.];, § 102.]; (prop. that they should fill, and they filled...; cf. the full form of expression in #bref("Gen 50:2");); a further command is then added by means of #he[לְ]; and the infinitive; #bref("Exod 36:6");; another instance of the same kind is #bref("Gen 30:27"); #emph[I have divined and the Lord hath blessed me];, etc., i.e. that the Lord hath blessed me for thy sake.

#ml("g") <p120g> (#emph[b];) With the second verb (which, according to the above, represents the principal idea) attached #emph[without the copula];#footnote[To be distinguished, of course, from the cases in which two equally important and independent verbs are used together without the copula in vigorous poetic imagery, e.g. #bref("Exod 15:9");, #bref("Job 29:8");, etc.]; in the same mood, etc. In this construction (cf. #xref(<p110h>)[§ 110#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];) the imperatives #he[קוּם]; (#he[ק֫וּמָה];, #he[ק֫וּמִי];, #origpage("387") <orig-387>;etc.) and #he[לֵךְ]; (#he[לְכָה];, #he[לְכִי];, etc.) are exceedingly common with the sense of interjections, before verbs which express a movement or other action, e.g. #he[קוּם הִתְהַלֵּךְ]; #emph[arise, walk];, #bref("Gen 13:17");, #bref("19:15");, #bref("27:43");; in the plural, #bref("Gen 19:14");; #bref("Exod 19:24"); #he[לֶךְ־רֵד]; #emph[go, get thee down];; #bref("1 Sam 3:9");; with a following cohortative, #bref("1 Sam 9:10"); #he[לְכָה נֵלֵכָ֑ה]; #emph[come, let us go];; #bref("Gen 31:44"); and frequently.—Also with #he[שׁוּב]; (a periphrasis for #emph[again];) in the perfect, #bref("Zech 8:15");; in the imperfect, #bref("Mic 7:19");, #bref("Ps 7:13");, #bref("59:7");, #bref("71:20");; in the jussive, #bref("Job 10:16");; in the cohortative, #bref("Gen 30:31");; in the imperative, #bref("Josh 5:2");, #bref("1 Sam 3:5"); #emph[lie down again];; #he[הוֹאִיל]; (sometimes to express the idea of #emph[willingly]; or #emph[gladly];) in the perfect, #bref("Deut 1:5");, #bref("Hos 5:11");; in the imperative, #bref("Job 6:28");; #he[הִרְבָּה];\=#emph[much];, #bref("1 Sam 2:3"); #he[אַל־תַּרְבּוּ תְדַבְּרוּ גְּבֹהָה]; #emph[do not multiply]; and #emph[talk];, i.e. talk not so much #emph[arrogancy];; in the imperative, #bref("Ps 51:4");; #he[הֵחֵל];, #bref("Deut 2:24"); #he[הָחֵל רָשׁ]; #emph[begin, possess];; #he[יָכֹל];, #bref("Lam 4:14"); #he[בְּלֹא יֽוּכְלוּ יִגְּעוּ];, #emph[without men’s being able to touch];, etc.; #he[מַהֵר];\=#emph[quickly];, in the perfect, #bref("Ps 106:13");; in the imperative, #bref("Gen 19:22");, #bref("Judg 9:48");, #bref("Esth 6:10");.—Other examples are: #bref("Hos 9:9"); #he[הֶֽעֱמִיק];\=#emph[deeply, radically];; #bref("Zeph 3:7"); #he[הִשְׁכִּים];\=#emph[early]; (even in the participle, #bref("Hos 6:4");, #bref("13:3");); #bref("Isa 29:4"); #he[שָׁפֵל];\=#emph[low];, cf. #bref("Jer 13:18");; #bref("Josh 3:16"); #he[תָּמַם];\=#emph[wholly];; #bref("Ps 112:9"); #he[פִּוַּר];\=#emph[plentifully];.

#ml("h") <p120h> Rem. This co\-ordination without the copula belongs (as being more vigorous and bolder) rather to poetic or otherwise elevated style (cf. e.g. #bref("Isa 52:1");, #bref("Hos 1:6");, #bref("9:9"); with #bref("Gen 25:1");, etc.). Asyndeton, however, is not wanting even in prose; besides the above examples (especially the imperatives of #he[קוּם]; and #he[הָלַךְ]; #bref("Gen 30:31");, #bref("Deut 1:5");, #bref("2:24");, #bref("Josh 3:16");, #bref("1 Sam 3:5");) cf. also #bref("Neh 3:20");, #bref("1 Chr 13:2");. For special reasons the verb representing the principal idea may even come first; thus #bref("Isa 53:11"); #he[יִרְאֶה יִשְׂבָּע]; #emph[he shall see, he shall be satisfied]; (sc. with the sight), for the satisfaction does not come until after the enjoyment of the sight; #bref("Jer 4:5"); #he[קִרְאוּ מַלְאוּ]; #emph[cry, fill];, i.e. cry with a full (loud) voice.

#sec("121", [Construction of Passive Verbs], note: []) <s121>

#align(center)[Blake, ‘The internal passive in Semitic,’ #emph[JAOS]; xxii.];

#ml("a") <p121a> #strong[1.]; Verbs which in the active take #emph[one]; accusative (either of the proper object, or of the #emph[internal]; object, or of some other nearer definition; cf. #xref(<p117a>)[§ 117#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];, #emph[p];, #emph[u];) may in the passive, according to our mode of expression, be construed #emph[personally];, the object of the active sentence now becoming the subject, e.g. #bref("Gen 35:19"); #he[וַתָּ֫מָת רְחֵל וַתִּקָּבֵר]; #emph[and Rachel died, and was buried];, etc. The passive, however, is also used #emph[impersonally]; (in the 3rd sing. masc.), either absolutely, as #bref("Deut 21:3");f., #bref("Isa 16:10");, #bref("Ezek 16:34"); (with a dative added, #bref("2 Sam 17:16");, #bref("Isa 53:5");, #bref("Lam 5:5");), or, more frequently, with the object of the active construction still subordinated in the accusative,#footnote[When this is not recognizable either by the #emph[nota accusativi];, or by its disagreement with the passive form in gender, number, and person, it naturally cannot be determined whether the construction is really impersonal. The construction itself can only be explained by supposing that while using the passive form the speaker at the same time thinks of some author or authors of the action in question, just as on the theory of the Arab grammarians a #emph[concealed agent]; is included in every passive. This accounts for the possibility (cf. #xref(<p144g>)[§ 144#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];) of using the active without a specified subject as a periphrasis for the passive.]; e.g. #bref("Gen 27:42"); #he[וַיֻּגַּד לְרִבְקָה אֶת־דִּבְרֵי עֵשָׂו]; #emph[and there were told]; (i.e. one told) #emph[to Rebekah the words of Esau];; #bref("2 Sam 21:11");, #bref("1 Kgs 18:13");. #origpage("388") <orig-388>;

#ml("b") <p121b> Other examples are: after Niph., #bref("Gen 4:18"); #he[וַיִּוָּלֵד לַֽחֲנוֹךְ אֶת־עִירָד]; #emph[and unto Enoch was born Irad]; (cf. #bref("Num 26:60");, and after an infinitive, #bref("Gen 21:5");); #bref("Gen 17:5");, #bref("21:8"); (after an infinitive); 29:27 (unless #he[וְנִתְּנָה]; is 1st plur. cohortative); #bref("Exod 21:28");, #bref("25:28");, #bref("Lev 6:13");, #bref("Num 7:10"); (after an infinitive); 26:55 (cf. verse 53); #bref("Deut 20:8"); (where, however, for #he[יִמַּס]; the Hiph. #he[יַמֵּס]; should be read, according to 1:28); #bref("Josh 7:15");, #bref("Isa 16:10");; with the object preceding, #bref("Exod 13:7");, #bref("Lev 2:8");, #bref("19:20");, #bref("Num 16:29");, #bref("Dan 9:24");.#footnote[In #bref("2 Kgs 18:30"); #he[יִנָּתֵן]; is to be read or #he[אֶת־]; is to be omitted, as in the parallel passage #bref("Isa 36:15");.];— Also after Puʿal, #bref("Jer 50:20");; #emph[before]; Puʿal, #bref("Isa 14:3"); (#he[אֲשֶׁר]; equivalent to the internal object #he[עֲבֹדָה];\=#emph[which they have caused to be served by thee];); #bref("Job 22:9");; according to the Masoretic text also #bref("Gen 46:22");, where, however, the Samaritan and LXX read #he[יָֽלְדָה]; for #he[יֻלַּד];; the Samaritan in #bref("Gen 35:26"); and 46:27 also reads #he[יָֽלְדוּ];, and this (or #he[יֻלַּד];) should certainly be read instead of #he[יֻלְדוּ]; in #bref("2 Sam 21:22");.—After Hoph., #bref("Exod 10:8");, #bref("27:7");, #bref("Lev 10:18");, #bref("16:27");, #bref("Num 32:5");, #bref("1 Kgs 2:21");, #bref("Prov 16:33");, #bref("Job 30:15");; after the infinitive Hoph., #bref("Gen 40:20");, #bref("Ezek 16:4"); f., 27:7; #emph[before]; Hoph., #bref("Isa 17:1");, #bref("21:2");, #bref("Hos 10:6");, #bref("Zech 13:6");; after the infinitive #emph[Hothpaʿel];, #bref("Lev 13:55"); f.

#ml("c") <p121c> #strong[2.]; Verbs which in the active take two accusatives (#xref(<p117cc>)[§ 117#h(0.18em)#emph[cc];];) retain in the passive construction at least #emph[one]; accusative, namely that of the second or remoter object, while the nearer object now becomes the subject. Thus, corresponding to #he[אֲשֶׁר אַרְאֶ֫ךָּ]; #emph[which I will show thee]; (#bref("Gen 12:1");) the passive is #he[אֲשֶׁר אַתָּה מָרְאֶה]; (#bref("Exod 25:40");) #emph[which thou hast been shown];, i.e. which has been shown to thee; cf. #bref("Exod 26:30"); (but in #bref("Lev 13:49"); with an accusative of the person); #bref("Job 7:3");. In #bref("Ps 22:16"); #he[מֻדְבָּק מַלְקוֹחָ֑י]; depends on an assumed transitive #he[הִדְבִּיק]; governing two accusatives (\= #emph[my tongue is made to cleave to my jaws];); also in #bref("Isa 1:20");, #he[חֶ֫רֶב תְּאֻכְּלוּ]; #emph[ye shall be devoured with the sword];, #he[חֶרֶב]; is not an #emph[accus. instrumenti];, but most probably an accusative of the object retained from the active construction.#footnote[In the active, the sentence would be #emph[I will cause the sword to devour you];; by the rule stated above, under #emph[c];, this would become in the passive, #emph[the sword]; (nom.) #emph[shall be made to devour you]; (acc.). Instead of this, the remoter object is here made the subject, and the nearer object is retained in the accusative. Otherwise, the only possible explanation would be, according to the Arabic idiom, #emph[to cause one to devour the sword]; (remoter object), i.e. to give him over to it. It would then be simplest to read #he[תֹּֽאכְלוּ];.];

#ml("d") <p121d> Rem. 1. Examples of the retention of the second accusative are—(#emph[a];) with #emph[verba induendi]; and #emph[exuendi]; (#xref(<p117cc>)[§ 117#h(0.18em)#emph[cc];];), #bref("Ps 80:11");, #he[כָּסּוּ הָרִים צִלָּהּ]; #emph[the mountains were covered with the shadow of it]; (the vine); #bref("Prov 19:23");. So also some of the examples in #xref(<p116k>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[k];]; of passive participles of these verbs, #bref("Judg 18:11");, #bref("1 Sam 2:18");, #bref("17:5");, #bref("1 Kgs 22:10");, #origpage("389") <orig-389>;#bref("Ezek 9:2");, 3;#footnote[Analogous to #he[הַלָּבוּשׁ הַבַּדִּים]; #emph[who was clothed in linen];, #bref("Ezek 9:3");, would be #he[וְהַנּוֹתָר אֶת־הֶהָמוֹן הַזֶּה]; #bref("2 Chr 31:10");; but we must certainly read there #he[וַנּוֹתֵר]; with the LXX.—Still less can #bref("Ps 87:3"); be so explained, #he[נִכְבָּדוֹת]; being not an accusative, but the subject of a noun\-clause. On the other hand, #he[שָׁלוּחַ]; #bref("1 Kgs 14:6"); may be explained with Ewald in the sense of #emph[being charged with]; something, so that, like #he[צִוָּה];, it may be construed with an accusative.]; with the accusative preceding, #bref("Neh 4:12");.—(#emph[b];) with #emph[verba copiae]; and #emph[inopiae];, #bref("Exod 1:7");, #bref("Isa 38:10"); (equivalent to #emph[I must forego the residue of my years];); #bref("Isa 40:20");.—(#emph[c];) an accusative of the result (#xref(<p117ii>)[§ 117#h(0.18em)#emph[ii];];) with the passive, #bref("Isa 6:11");, #bref("Zech 14:4");, #bref("Job 28:2");; with the accusative preceding, #bref("Isa 24:12");, #bref("Mic 3:12"); (#bref("Jer 26:18");), #bref("Job 15:7");, #bref("22:16");.#footnote[In reality #he[וַיָּרֻם]; #bref("Exod 16:20");, 26 (#emph[it became putrid];) is equivalent to a passive (#emph[it was changed];), to which #he[תּֽוֹלָעִים]; is added as an accusative of the result.]; Also in #bref("Ezek 40:17"); and 46:23, the accusative preceding #he[עָשׂוּי]; (in 41:18 following it) can only be taken as the accusative of the result; some general idea, such as that of #emph[place];, is to be understood as the subject of #he[עָשׂוּי];.—(#emph[d];) an accusative of the member or part specially affected by the action (#xref(<p117ll>)[§ 117#h(0.18em)#emph[ll];];), #bref("Gen 17:11");, #bref("14:24");, #bref("Judg 1:7"); (accusative #emph[before]; part. pass.); #bref("2 Sam 15:32"); (accusative with suffix after the part. pass.).

#ml("e") <p121e> 2. Both accusatives are retained in an unusual manner after the passive of a #emph[verbum implendi]; in #bref("Num 14:21");; instead, however, of the #emph[Niph.]; #he[וְיִמָּלֵא]; the #emph[Qal]; (which is sometimes used transitively elsewhere) should simply be read with the LXX; similarly in #bref("Ps 72:19");, although there the LXX also translate the passive.

#ml("f") <p121f> #strong[3.]; The efficient cause (or personal agent) is, as a rule, attached to the passive by #he[לְ]; (thus corresponding to the Greek and Latin dative), e.g. #bref("Gen 25:21"); #he[וַיֵּעָ֫תֶ֫ר לוֹ יְהֹוָה]; #emph[the Lord let himself be intreated by him];; cf. #bref("Lev 26:23");, #bref("Ps 73:10"); and the blessing #he[בָּרוּךְ הוּא לַֽיהוָֹה]; #emph[blessed be he of the Lord]; #bref("Ruth 2:20");; cf. #bref("Gen 14:19");, #bref("Judg 17:2");b, #bref("1 Sam 15:13");; also in the plural, #bref("1 Sam 23:21"); (#bref("2 Sam 2:5");, #bref("Ps 115:15");).—#emph[Before]; the verb, #bref("Prov 14:20"); and frequently; less commonly by #he[מִן־]; (called #he[מִן־]; of origin\=#emph[coming from];), e.g. #bref("Gen 9:11");; #emph[before]; the verb, #bref("Ps 37:23");, #bref("Job 24:1");; by #he[בְּ]; (#emph[instrumenti];) \[rarely, König § 106\], #bref("Gen 9:6"); (#he[בָּֽאָדָם]; #emph[by man];); #bref("Num 36:2");, #bref("Isa 14:3"); b \[but ?\=#emph[wherewith it was worked]; (#xref(<p52e>)[§ 52#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];) #emph[with thee];; cf. #bref("Deut 21:3");, König § 106; and see #he[עָבַד בְּ]; in the Lexicon\], #bref("Hos 14:4");, always to introduce a personal agent.—On the connection of the passive participle with a genitive of the agent, cf. #xref(<p116l>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[l];];.

#bookhead(3, [II. Syntax of the Noun], note: []);

#sec("122", [Indication of the Gender of the Noun], note: []) <s122>

Cf. F. Schwabe, #emph[Die Genusbestimmung des Nomens im bibl. Hebr.];, Jena, 1894, and especially the thorough investigation by K. Albrecht, ‘Das Geschlecht der hebr. Hauptwörter,’ in #emph[ZAW.]; 1895, p. 313 ff., and 1896, p. 61 ff. H. Rosenberg, ‘Zum Geschlecht der hebr. Hauptwörter,’ in #emph[ZAW.]; 1905, p. 325 ff. (supplementing Albrecht’s work by a treatment of the gender of many nouns in the Mishna); and his ‘Notizen aus der tannaitischen Literatur...’ #emph[ZAW.]; 1908, p. 144 ff.

#ml("a") <p122a> #strong[1.]; According to #xref(<p80a>)[§ 80#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];, Hebrew, like the other Semitic languages, distinguishes only a #emph[masculine]; and #emph[feminine]; gender. To indicate the #origpage("390") <orig-390>;latter a special feminine ending is generally used (#xref(<p80b>)[§ 80#h(0.18em)#emph[b];]; and #xref(<p87i>)[§ 87#h(0.18em)#emph[i];];) both in the singular and plural (see, however, #xref(<p87p>)[§ 87#h(0.18em)#emph[p];];), its use being most consistent in adjectives and participles; cf. #xref(<p87r>)[§ 87#h(0.18em)#emph[r];];. The employment of these special endings is most natural when by means of them the feminine names of persons or animals are distinguished from the masculine of the same stem and the same formation, e.g. #he[אָח]; #emph[brother];, #he[אָחוֹת]; #emph[sister];; #he[עֶלֶם]; #emph[a young man];, #he[עַלְמָה]; #emph[a young woman, maid];; #he[פָּר]; #emph[iuvencus];, #he[פָּרָה]; #emph[iuvenca];; #he[עֵגֶל]; #emph[vitulus];, #he[עֶגְלָה]; #emph[vitula];. On the other hand, the feminine plays an important part in denoting the gender of whole classes of ideas (see below, #emph[p];, etc.), which the Hebrew regards as feminine. The language, however, is not obliged to use the feminine ending either for the purpose of distinguishing the #emph[sex]; of animate objects (see #emph[b];), or as an indication of the (#emph[figurative];) gender of inanimate things which are regarded as feminine (see #emph[h];).

#ml("b") <p122b> #strong[2.]; The distinction of #emph[sex]; may be effected even without the feminine ending, (#emph[a];) by the employment of words of different stems for the masculine and feminine; (#emph[b];) by the different construction (either as masculine or feminine) of the same word (#emph[communia];). But the distinction may also, (#emph[c];) in the case of names of animals, be entirely neglected, all examples of a species being included under one particular gender, either masculine or feminine (#emph[epicoena];).

#ml("c") <p122c> Examples of (#emph[a];) are: #he[אָב]; #emph[father];, #he[אֵם]; #emph[mother];; #he[אַ֫יִל]; #emph[ram];, #he[רָחֵל]; #emph[ewe];; #he[תַּ֫יִשׁ]; #emph[he\-goat];, #he[עֵז]; #emph[she\-goat];; #he[חֲמוֹר]; #emph[he\-ass];, #he[אָתוֹן]; #emph[she\-ass];; #he[אַרְיֵה]; #emph[lion];, #he[לָבִיא]; #emph[lioness];. Sometimes with the feminine ending as well, e.g. #he[עֶ֫בֶד]; #emph[male slave, man\-servant];, #he[אָמָה]; or #he[שִׁפְחָה]; #emph[female slave, maid];; #he[חָתָן]; #emph[bridegroom];, #he[כַּלָּה]; #emph[bride];.

#ml("d") <p122d> Of (#emph[b];): #he[גָּמָל]; #emph[camel];. Plur. #he[גְּמַלִּים]; construed as masculine, #bref("Gen 24:63");; as feminine, #bref("Gen 32:16");; #he[בָּקָר]; collect. #emph[oxen];, #bref("Exod 21:37");, construed as masculine, but in #bref("Gen 33:13");, #bref("Job 1:14"); as feminine. In #bref("Jer 2:24"); the construction of #he[פֶּ֫רֶה]; #emph[wild ass];, changes directly from the masculine (intended as epicene) to the feminine. Cf. the Greek ὁ, ἡ παῖς· ὁ, ἡ βοῦς.

#ml("e") <p122e> Of (#emph[c];): analogous to the epicene nouns of other languages, many species of animals which are strong and courageous, are regarded in Hebrew as always masculine, while the weak and timid are feminine; cf. ὁ λύκος, ἡ χελιδών, and the German #emph[der]; Löwe, #emph[der]; Adler, etc., but #emph[die]; Katze, #emph[die]; Taube, etc. Similarly in Hebrew, e.g. #he[אַלּוּף]; #emph[ox]; (#bref("Ps 144:14"); even referring to cows when pregnant), #he[דֹּב]; #emph[bear];, #bref("Hos 13:8"); #he[דּוֹב שַׁכּוּל]; (#emph[a bear that is bereaved of her whelps];; cf., however, #bref("2 Kgs 2:24");, #bref("Isa 11:7");), #he[זְאֵב]; #emph[wolf];, #he[כֶּ֫לֶב]; #emph[dog];, all masculine; but #he[אַרְנֶ֫בֶת]; #emph[hare];, #he[יוֹנָה]; #emph[dove];, #he[חֲסִידָה]; #emph[stork];, #he[דְּבוֹרָה]; #emph[bee];, #he[נְמָלָה]; #emph[ant];, etc., feminine.

#ml("f") <p122f> Rem. 1. Masculine nouns which either have a separate feminine form or might easily form one, are but seldom used as epicene; such are, #he[חֲמוֹר]; #emph[ass];, #bref("2 Sam 19:27"); for #he[אָתוֹן];; #he[אַיָּל]; #emph[hart];, #bref("Ps 42:2"); for #he[אַיָּלָה];. In #bref("Gen 23:3"); ff. #he[מֵת]; #emph[a dead body];, refers more especially to the body of a woman; #he[אָמוֹן]; #emph[a master workman];, in #bref("Prov 8:30"); refers to wisdom (#he[חָכְמָה]; feminine, cf. Plin. 2, 1 #emph[natura omnium artifex];; and our #origpage("391") <orig-391>;use of #emph[friend, teacher, servant, neighbour];, either as masculine or feminine; in German, #emph[Gemahl];#footnote[So in early Arabic, #emph[baʿl]; (lord) and #emph[zauǵ]; (conjux) are used both for #emph[maritus]; and #emph[uxor];; #emph[ʿarūs]; for #emph[bridegroom]; and #emph[bride];; the later language, however, distinguishes the feminine from the masculine in all these cases generally by the ending #emph[a]; (#emph[at];). In early Arabic also the feminine ending is commonly omitted in such participles as #emph[ḥāmil, bāṭin]; (gravida), and the like, which from the nature of the case can only be used of females. Thus also #he[אֹמֵן];, at least in #bref("Num 11:12"); (#bref("Isa 49:23");?), probably means #emph[nurse]; (for #he[אֹמֶ֫נֶת]; #bref("2 Sam 4:4");, etc.), not #emph[nursing\-father];.]; spouse, also for fem. #emph[Gemahlin];, etc.).

#ml("g") <p122g> 2. Of words denoting persons #he[נַ֫עַר]; παῖς, according to the formerly common opinion, was in early times used as epicene (see, however, above, #xref(<p2n>)[§ 2#h(0.18em)#emph[n];];). The use of the plural #he[נְעָרִים]; in #bref("Job 1:19"); and #bref("Ruth 2:21"); in the sense of #emph[young people]; (of both genders) does not, however, prove this. In this and in similar cases (cf. e.g. #he[אֹתָם]; #bref("Gen 1:27"); and #he[אֶתְהֶם]; 32:1) the masculine as #emph[prior gender]; includes the feminine.#footnote[The Arab grammarians call this use of the masculine plural and dual (e.g. #emph[el\-abawāni];, the two fathers, i.e. #emph[parentes];) #emph[taghlîb]; or #emph[the making]; (the masculine) #emph[prevail]; (over the feminine).—Cf. M. Grünert, #emph[Die Begriffs\-Präponderanz und die Duale a potiori im Altarab.];, Vienna, 1886.];

#ml("h") <p122h> #strong[3.]; The following classes of ideas are usually regarded as feminine,#footnote[The masculine gender is attributed ‘by the Hebrews and the Semites generally to whatever is dangerous, savage, courageous, respected, great, strong, powerful ...; the feminine to whatever is motherly, productive, sustaining, nourishing, gentle, weak, ... subject, etc.’ (Albrecht, #emph[ZAW.]; 1896, p. 120 f.).]; although the substantives which express them are mostly without the feminine ending:#footnote[When, on the other hand, words #emph[with]; a feminine\-ending, such as #he[קֶ֫שֶׁת]; #emph[a bow]; (stem #he[קוש];), #he[עֵת]; #emph[time]; (see the Lexicon), are sometimes construed as masculine, this is owing probably in some cases to a misunderstanding of the formation of the word, the #he[ת]; of the feminine being regarded as a radical.];

(#emph[a];) Names of #emph[countries]; and #emph[towns];, since they are regarded as the mothers#footnote[Cf. #emph[a city and a mother]; (#he[אֵם];) #emph[in Israel];, #bref("2 Sam 20:19");. In the same way #he[אֵם]; (like μήτηρ, #emph[mater];) on Phoenician coins stands for #emph[mother\-city];, μητρόπολις. The same figure is used in such expressions as #emph[sons of Zion];, #bref("Ps 149:2");; #emph[sons of Babylon];, #bref("Ezek 23:15");, etc., as also in speaking of the suburbs of a city as its #emph[daughters];, e.g. #bref("Josh 15:45"); ff., etc.—The comparison of Jerusalem to a woman is especially frequent in allegorical descriptions, e.g. #bref("Ezek 16:23");, #bref("Lam 1:1");, etc.]; and nurses of the inhabitants; e.g. #he[אַשּׁוּר]; #emph[Assyria];, #he[אֱדֹם]; #emph[Idumaea];, #he[צֹר]; #emph[Tyre];; cf. also such expressions as #he[בַּת בָּבֶל];, #he[בַּת צִיּוֹן]; #emph[daughter of Babylon, daughter of Zion];, etc. On the other hand appellatives which are originally masculine, remain so when used as place\-names, e.g. #bref("Amos 5:5"); #he[בֵּית־אֵל];, #he[הַגִּלְגָּל];, etc.

#ml("i") <p122i> Rem. The same proper nouns, which as names of countries are regarded as feminine, are frequently used also as names of the people, and may then, like national names in other languages, be construed as masculine (the national name almost always being used also as the personal name of the supposed ancestor of the people); thus #he[יְהוּדָה]; masc. #bref("Isa 3:8");, etc., #emph[Judaei];; but #origpage("392") <orig-392>;#bref("Isa 7:6");, fem., #emph[Judaea];; #he[אֱדֹם]; masc., #emph[Idumaei];, #bref("Num 20:20");; fem., #emph[Idumaea];, #bref("Jer 49:17");. Nevertheless, it sometimes happens that by a very common transference of thought (just as we say #emph[Turkey concludes peace];) these names are construed as feminine, even when they denote not the country but the inhabitants; so #he[יְהוּדָה]; #bref("Lam 1:3");; cf. #bref("Gen 41:8");, #bref("Exod 10:7");, #bref("12:33");, #bref("1 Sam 17:21");, #bref("2 Sam 8:2");, #bref("24:9");, #bref("Isa 7:2");, #bref("21:2");, #bref("42:11");, #bref("Jer 50:10");, #bref("Job 1:15");. Hence the frequent personification of nations (as well as of countries and towns, see #emph[h];, note 5) as female beings, e.g. #bref("Isa 50:1");, #bref("54:1"); ff., and the use of the expressions #he[בַּת בָּבֶל]; #bref("Isa 47:1"); ff., #he[בַּת צִיּוֹן]; etc. (see above) as collective poetical personifications of the people.

#ml("k") <p122k> (#emph[b];) Appellative nouns, which denote a #emph[circumscribed space];, such as #he[אֶ֫רֶץ]; #emph[earth, land];, #he[תֵּבֵל]; #emph[world];, #he[שְׁאֹל]; #emph[the abode of the dead];, #he[כִּכָּר]; #emph[circle]; (of the Jordan valley), #he[עִיר]; #emph[a town];, #he[בְּאֵר]; #emph[a well];, #he[צָפוֹן]; #emph[the north];, #he[תֵּימָן]; #emph[the south];.

#ml("l") <p122l> In the majority of nouns denoting place the gender is variable, e.g. #he[אֹ֫רַח]; and #he[דֶּ֫רֶךְ]; #emph[a way]; (usually feminine; the masculine gender only begins to predominate with Ezekiel; cf. Albrecht, l. c., 1896, p. 55), #he[גַּיְא]; (#he[גַּי];) #emph[valley];, #he[גַּן]; #emph[garden]; (fem. #bref("Gen 2:15");, unless #he[לְעָבְדֹה];, etc., is to be read), #he[הֵיכָל]; #emph[palace, temple];, #he[חָצֵר]; #emph[court];, #he[כֶּ֫רֶם]; #emph[vineyard];, #he[שַׁ֫עַר]; #emph[door];,#footnote[#he[מַֽחֲנֶה]; #emph[camp]; is feminine only when it is a collective, denoting the persons in a camp.]; etc.; also #he[מָקוֹם]; #emph[place];, at least in #bref("Gen 18:24"); (referring to Sodom), #bref("Job 20:9");, and #bref("2 Sam 17:12"); #emph[K#super[e];thîbh];, is construed as feminine. The mountains and hills commanding the surrounding country are almost without exception masculine (see Albrecht, l. c., p. 60 f.).

#ml("m") <p122m> (#emph[c];) The names of #emph[instruments, utensils];, and (on the same analogy) #emph[members]; and #emph[parts of the body]; in man or beast, since these are all regarded as subservient and subordinate (consequently as feminine).

#ml("n") <p122n> Thus #he[חֶ֫רֶב]; #emph[sword];, #he[יָתֵד]; #emph[tent\-peg];, #he[כַּד]; #emph[bucket];, #he[כּוֹס]; #emph[cup];, #he[נַ֫עַל]; #emph[shoe];, #he[עֶ֫רֶשׂ]; #emph[bed];, etc.; in other cases, as #he[אֲרוֹן]; #emph[chest, ark]; (with the article #he[הָֽאָרוֹן];), #he[תַּנּוּר]; #emph[oven];, the gender is variable. (‘Instruments for binding or holding, girdles and the like, as constraining and mastering, are masculine,’ Albrecht, l. c., p. 89.)—Also #he[אֹ֫זֶן]; #emph[ear]; (and in general, members occurring in pairs, Albrecht, l. c., p. 73 f.), #he[אֶצְבַּע]; #emph[finger]; (and so probably #he[בֹּ֫הֶן]; #emph[thumb, great toe];), #he[יָד]; and #he[כַּף]; #emph[hand];, #he[יָמִין]; #emph[right hand];, #he[רֶ֫גֶל]; #emph[foot];, #he[בֶּ֫רֶךְ]; #emph[knee];, #he[יָרֵךְ]; #emph[thigh];, #he[כָּתֵף]; #emph[shoulder];, #he[לְחִי]; #emph[cheek];, #he[בֶּ֫טֶן]; #emph[belly];, #he[כָּנָף]; #emph[wing];, #he[קֶ֫רֶן]; #emph[horn];, #he[שֵׁן]; #emph[tooth];; as a rule also #he[זְרוֹעַ]; #emph[arm]; (masc. #bref("Isa 17:5");, etc.), #he[לָשׁוֹן]; #emph[tongue]; (masc. #bref("Ps 22:16");, #bref("Prov 26:28");, etc.), #he[עַ֫יִן]; #emph[eye]; (masc. #bref("Zech 3:9");, etc.), #he[שׁוֹק]; #emph[thigh]; (masc. #bref("Exod 29:27");).#footnote[#he[אַף]; #emph[nose];, #he[גִּיד]; #emph[sinew];, #he[זָנָב]; #emph[tail];, #he[חֵךְ]; #emph[palate];, #he[כָּבֵד]; #emph[liver];, #he[לֵב];, #he[לֵבָב]; #emph[heart];, #he[מֵעִים];, #he[רַֽחֲמִים]; #emph[bowels];, #he[מֵ֫צַח]; #emph[forehead];, #he[עוֹר]; #emph[skin];, #he[עֹרֶף]; #emph[back of the neck];, #he[פֶּה]; #emph[mouth];, #he[צַוָּאר]; #emph[neck];, #he[רֹאשׁ]; #emph[head];, #he[שְׁכֶם]; #emph[shoulder];, also #he[רֶ֫חֶם]; #emph[womb];, except in #bref("Jer 20:17");, are invariably construed as masculine.—#he[עֶ֫צֶם]; #emph[bone]; is common.];

#ml("o") <p122o> (#emph[d];) Certain names of natural forces or substances are feminine, being probably regarded as instruments, while in the names of the heavens, the heavenly bodies and natural phenomena, the masculine generally predominates (cf. Albrecht, l. c., p. 323 ff.); thus feminine are #he[שֶׁ֫מֶשׁ]; #emph[sun]; (but often also #emph[masc.];, #bref("Ps 19:6");, #bref("104:19");); #he[אֵשׁ]; (Ethiopic #emph[ʾĕsât];) #origpage("393") <orig-393>;#emph[fire]; (rarely #emph[masc.];); #he[נֹ֫גַהּ]; #emph[brightness];, #he[אֶ֫בֶן]; #emph[a stone];, as a rule also #he[רוּחַ]; #emph[wind, spirit];; #he[נֶ֫פֶשׁ]; #emph[breath, soul];; also #he[אוֹר]; #emph[light]; in #bref("Jer 13:16");, #bref("Job 36:32");, and others.

#ml("p") <p122p> #strong[4.]; The following classes of ideas, which are also regarded as feminine in Hebrew (see above, #emph[h];), are usually indicated by the feminine #emph[form];, notwithstanding their occasional transference to masculine persons (see #emph[r]; and #emph[s];):

#ml("q") <p122q> (#emph[a];) Abstracts#footnote[Cf. the list of masculine and feminine abstracts in Albrecht, l. c., 1896, p. 111 ff.]; (sometimes along with masculine forms from the same stem, as #he[נְקָמָה]; #emph[vengeance];, as well as #he[נָקָם];, #he[עֶזְרָה]; #emph[help];, as well as #he[עֵ֫זֶר];), e.g. #he[אֱמוּנָה]; #emph[firmness, faithfulness];, #he[גְּבוּרָה]; #emph[strength];, #he[גְּדוּלָה]; #emph[greatness];, #he[מְלֵאָה]; #emph[fullness];, #he[מֶמְשָׁלָה]; #emph[dominion];, etc. Similarly, the feminine (sing. and plur.) of adjectives and participles is used substantivally in the sense of the Latin and Greek #emph[neuter];, e.g. #he[נְכוֹנָה]; #emph[stedfastness];, #bref("Ps 5:10");, #he[טוֹבָה]; #emph[goodness];, #he[רָעָה]; #emph[evil];, #bref("Gen 50:20");, #he[נְקַלָּה]; #emph[a light thing]; (i.e. a trifling thing), #bref("Jer 6:14");; so especially in the plural, e.g. #he[גְּדֹלוֹת]; #emph[great things];, #bref("Ps 12:4");; #he[הַנֶּֽהֱרָסוֹת]; #emph[the ruined places];, #bref("Ezek 36:36");, along with #he[הַנְּשַׁמָּה]; #emph[that which was desolate];, #he[טֹבוֹת]; #emph[kindnesses];, #bref("2 Kgs 25:28");, #he[נְכֹחוֹת]; #emph[uprightness, honesty];, #bref("Isa 26:10");, #he[נְעִימוֹת]; #emph[amoena];, #bref("Ps 16:11"); (but in verse 6 in the same sense #he[נְעִימִים];), #he[נִפְלָאוֹת]; #emph[wonderful things];, #bref("Exod 34:10"); and frequently, #he[קָשׁוֹת]; #emph[hard things, roughly]; #bref("Gen 42:7");, 30 (but cf. also #he[רֵיקִים]; #emph[vain things];, #bref("Prov 12:11");, #bref("28:19");). Cf. moreover, the very frequent use of #he[זֹאת];, #he[הִיא]; (as well as #he[זֶה]; and #he[הוּא];), #bref("Judg 14:4");, #bref("Ps 118:23");, etc., in the sense of #emph[hoc, illud]; (also #he[הֵ֫נָּה]; equivalent to #emph[illa];, #bref("Isa 51:19");): also the use of the feminine form of the verb in #bref("Isa 7:7"); #he[לֹא תָקוּם וְלֹא תִֽהְיֶה]; #emph[it shall not stand, neither shall it come to pass];; cf. #bref("Jer 10:7");; so too the suffixes #bref("Gen 15:6");, #bref("Exod 10:11");, #bref("Job 38:18");, referring back to a whole statement.#footnote[While in all these instances it is simplest to speak of the #emph[feminine]; in Hebrew as being used #emph[for the neuter]; (which in Latin, Greek, and German is commonly employed for similar purposes), it must yet not be forgotten that since the language is wholly wanting in #emph[neuters];, the Semitic mind regarded the above\-mentioned forms primarily as actual feminines. Hence the Arab commentators are accustomed to explain the feminines of adjectives and participles (which would be neuter in Latin, etc.) by supplying a feminine substantive.];

#ml("r") <p122r> (#emph[b];) Titles and designations of office, properly a subdivision of the abstract ideas treated above, under #emph[q];, and specially noticed here only on account of their peculiar transference to concrete male persons. Thus we have #he[קֹהֶ֫לֶת]; #bref("Eccl 1:1");, etc. (as a title of Solomon), properly no doubt #emph[that which takes part in]; or #emph[speaks in a religious assembly];, hence LXX ἐκκλησιαστής, i.e. #emph[concionator, preacher];; the proper names #he[סֹפֶ֫רֶת]; #bref("Ezra 2:55");, #bref("Neh 7:57");, and #he[פֹּכֶ֫רֶת]; #bref("Ezra 2:57");, #bref("Neh 7:59");, and the foreign word #he[פֶּחָה]; #emph[viceroy];; in the plural #he[כְּנָוֹת]; prop. #emph[cognomina];, then #emph[like\-named, colleagues];; #he[פְּרָעוֹת]; #emph[princes]; (if this be the true meaning).#footnote[This use of the feminine form is far more frequent in Arabic, Ethiopic, and Aramaic; cf. e.g. in Arabic #emph[ḥalîfa]; (fem. from #emph[ḥalîf];, following after, taking the place of) in the sense of #emph[the]; successor or representative (of Muḥammad), and #emph[ʿallāma]; (#emph[great wisdom];) as a title of learned men. Analogous to this is the Latin #emph[magistratus, magistracy];, for #emph[magistrate];, and our #emph[his Majesty, Excellency, Highness];, etc.]; All these words, in accordance with their meaning, are construed as masculine (in #bref("Eccl 7:27"); instead of #he[אָֽמְרָה ק׳]; the words should rather be divided as #he[אָמַר הַקּ׳];; cf. 12:8). #origpage("394") <orig-394>;

#ml("s") <p122s> Abstract ideas include also—

(#emph[c];) #emph[Collectives]; in the fem. form,#footnote[Cf. in Greek ἡ ἵππος, #emph[the cavalry]; (as well as τὸ ἱππικόν), ἡ κάμηλος, Hdt. 1, 80, etc., #emph[the camel corps];.]; generally fem. participles used substantivally, especially as the comprehensive designation of a number of #emph[persons];, e.g. #he[אֹֽרְחָה]; (fem. of #emph[travelling];), prop. #emph[the travelling]; (company), i.e. travelling persons (a caravan); #he[גּוֹלָה]; (fem. of #he[גֹּלֶה]; #emph[one going into exile];) #emph[the company of exiles]; (also frequently used of those who had returned home again); #he[יוֹשֶׁ֫בֶת]; (that which inhabits) i.e. #emph[the population];, #bref("Isa 12:6");, #bref("Mic 1:11"); f.; #he[אֹיֶ֫בֶת]; (prop. that which is hostile) #emph[the enemy];, #bref("Mic 7:8");, 10 (cf. #bref("Mic 4:6"); f. #emph[the halting, cast off, driven away];, i.e. #emph[those who halt];, etc.); #he[דַּלָּה]; (the abject) #emph[the poorest sort];; of living beings which are not persons, cf. #he[חַיָּה]; (that which lives) in the sense of #emph[cattle, beasts];; #he[דָּגָה]; #emph[a shoal of fish];, #bref("Gen 1:26"); (but in #bref("Jonah 2:2"); as a #emph[nomen unitatis];, cf. #emph[t];, for #he[דָּג]; #emph[a fish];, which in verses 1 and 11 is used as the #emph[nomen unitatis];). Cf., moreover, #he[נְבֵלָה]; #emph[dead body];, #bref("Isa 26:19");, etc. (construed as masculine), for #emph[a heap of dead bodies];.—On the collective poetic personification of a nation, by means of #he[בַּת]; #emph[daughter];, in #he[בַּת בָּבֶל];, #he[בַּת עַמִּי]; (equivalent to #he[בְּנֵי עַמִּי];) #emph[my countrymen];, see above, #emph[i];.

#ml("t") <p122t> (#emph[d];) Conversely the feminine form of substantives is sometimes used (as in Arabic) as a #emph[nomen unitatis];, i.e. to indicate a #emph[single]; example of a class which is denoted by the masculine form; cf. #he[אֳנִי]; #emph[a fleet]; (#bref("1 Kgs 9:26");), #he[אֳנִיָּה]; #emph[a]; single #emph[ship]; (#bref("Jonah 1:3"); ff.); #he[צַ֫יִד]; #emph[hunting, game];, #he[צֵידָה]; #bref("Gen 27:3"); #emph[K#super[e];th.]; (#he[צָ֑יִד]; #emph[Q#super[e];rê];) #emph[a piece of venison];; #he[שֵׂעָר]; #emph[hair]; (coll.), #he[שַֽׂעֲרָה]; #emph[a]; single #emph[hair]; (#bref("Judg 20:16");; in the plural, #bref("Ps 40:13");, #bref("69:5");); #he[שִׁיר]; #emph[a poem];, frequently collective, #he[שִׁירָה]; #emph[a]; single #emph[song];; so probably also #he[תְּאֵנָה]; #emph[a fig]; (the corresponding masculine #emph[tîn]; is collective in Arabic); #he[שֽׁוֹשַׁנָּה]; #emph[a lily]; (also #he[שׁוֹשָׁן];); #he[לְבֵנָה]; #emph[a brick]; (Arab. #emph[libina];, but #emph[libin]; collective), etc.

#ml("u") <p122u> (#emph[e];) The feminine is also used for things #emph[without life]; (as being weaker or less important), which are named from their resemblance to #emph[organic]; things expressed by the corresponding masculine form; cf. #he[יָרֵךְ]; #emph[side]; (of the body), #emph[thigh];, #he[יְרֵכָה]; or #he[יַרְכָּה]; #emph[back part, border]; (of a country, house, etc.); #he[מֵ֫צַח]; #emph[forehead];, #he[מִצְחָה]; #emph[greaves];. On a similar distinction between the masculine for natural, and the feminine for artificial objects, see #xref(<p87o>)[§ 87#h(0.18em)#emph[o];];.

#ml("v") <p122v> Rem. The juxtaposition of the masculine and feminine from the same stem serves sometimes to express entirety; e.g. #bref("Isa 3:1"); #he[מַשְׁעֵן וּמַשְׁעֵנָה]; #emph[stay and staff];, i.e. every kind of support (unless we omit verse #bref("1b"); as a gloss and take #emph[staff]; as \= #emph[staff\-bearer, official];; the list of officials begins in verse 2); cf. #bref("Isa 16:6");, #bref("Prov 8:13");. For similar groupings in the case of persons, see #bref("Isa 43:6");, #bref("49:22");, #bref("60:4"); (#emph[sons and daughters];); 49:23, #bref("Eccl 2:8");.

#sec("123", [The Representation of Plural Ideas by Means of Collectives, and by the Repetition of Words], note: []) <s123>

#ml("a") <p123a> Besides the plural endings treated in #xref(<p87a>)[§ 87#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];–#emph[i];, the language employs other means to express a plurality of living beings or things:

(#emph[a];) Certain words employed exclusively in a collective sense, while the individual members of the class are denoted by special words (#emph[nomina unitatis];, but not in the same sense as in #xref(<p122t>)[§ 122#h(0.18em)#emph[t];];). #origpage("395") <orig-395>;Thus #he[בָּקָר]; #emph[cattle, oxen];#footnote[The plural form #he[בְּקָרִים]; from #he[בָּקָר]; is found only in very late Hebrew, #bref("Neh 10:37"); (where according to the Mantua edition, Ginsburg, etc., even #he[צֹאנֵ֫ינוּ]; #emph[our sheep];, is also to be read; Baer, however, has #he[צֹאנֵ֫נוּ];), and #bref("2 Chr 4:3");. In #bref("Amos 6:12"); read, with Hitzig, #he[בַּבָּקָר יָם];.]; (even joined with numerals, e.g. #bref("Exod 21:37"); #he[חֲמִשָּׁה בָקָר]; #emph[five head of cattle];), but #he[שׁוֹר]; #emph[an ox];; #he[צֹאן]; #emph[small cattle];, i.e. sheep and goats (μῆλα), cf. #bref("Job 1:3"); #he[שִׁבְעַת אַלְפֵי־צֹאן]; #emph[seven thousand sheep];; but #he[שֶׂה]; #emph[a single head of small cattle]; (a sheep or a goat). Other more or less common collectives are: #he[זִיז]; (prop. that which prowls or roams) #emph[wild beasts];, #he[טַף]; (perhaps prop. #emph[tripping];) #emph[a number of little children];; #he[דֶּ֫שֶׁא]; #emph[fresh green herb];, i.e. young plants, #he[יֶ֫רֶק]; #emph[green];, i.e. vegetation in general; #he[עוֹף]; #emph[birds, fowl];; #he[רֶ֫כֶב]; #emph[chariots]; or #emph[cavalcade];, #he[רִמָּה]; #emph[worms];, #he[רֶ֫מֶשׂ]; #emph[creeping things]; (of small creatures), #he[שֶׁ֫רֶץ]; #emph[swarming things];.

#ml("b") <p123b> (#emph[b];) The collective use of substantives which at the same time serve as #emph[nomina unitatis];; thus, #he[אָדָם]; (never in plur.) means both #emph[man]; (homo) and #emph[men]; (homines); #he[אִישׁ]; #emph[a man]; (vir) and #emph[men]; (viri); #he[אִשָּׁה]; #emph[woman]; and #emph[women]; (#bref("Judg 21:16");, #bref("1 Sam 21:6");); #he[אַרְבֶּה]; #emph[a locust];, but usually #emph[a swarm of locusts];; #he[נֶ֫פֶשׁ]; #emph[soul]; and #emph[souls]; (persons); #he[מַקֵּל]; #emph[staff]; and #emph[staves]; (#bref("Gen 30:37");); #he[עַ֫יִט]; #emph[a bird of prey]; and #emph[birds of prey];; #he[עָלֶה]; #emph[a leaf]; and #emph[foliage];; #he[עֵ֫שֶׂב]; #emph[a plant]; and #emph[plants, herbs];; #he[עֵץ]; #emph[a tree]; and #emph[trees]; (as it were #emph[foliage];); #he[פְּרִי]; #emph[fruit]; and #emph[fruits];; #he[שִׂיחַ]; #emph[a shrub]; and #emph[shrubs];; in isolated instances also nouns like #he[עֶ֫בֶד]; #emph[man\-servant];, #he[שִׁפְחָה]; #emph[maid\-servant];, #he[חֲמוֹר]; #emph[ass];, #he[שׁוֹר]; #emph[ox]; (cf. #bref("Gen 32:6");).—On the singular (especially of gentilic names) with the article (which may, however, be omitted in poetry, cf. e.g. #bref("Ps 12:2"); #he[חָסִיד];, #bref("Prov 11:14"); #he[יוֹעֵץ];) to include all individuals of the same species, cf. #xref(<p126l>)[§ 126#h(0.18em)#emph[l];];. On the special meaning of the plurals formed from certain collectives, see #xref(<p124l>)[§ 124#h(0.18em)#emph[l];];.

(#emph[c];) The feminine ending; see #xref(<p122s>)[§ 122#h(0.18em)#emph[s];];.

#ml("c") <p123c> (#emph[d];) The repetition of single words, and even of whole groups of words, especially to express entirety, or in a distributive sense. The following cases are more particularly to be noticed:

1\. The repetition of one or more words to express the idea of #emph[every, all];, as #he[יוֹם יוֹם]; #bref("Gen 39:10");, etc., #emph[day]; by #emph[day, every day];; #he[שָׁנָה שָׁנָה]; #emph[year]; by #emph[year];, #bref("Deut 14:22");; #he[אִישׁ אִישׁ]; #emph[every]; man, #bref("Exod 36:4");; with #he[בְּ]; before each, as #he[בַּבֹּ֫קֶר בַּבֹּ֫קֶר]; #bref("Exod 16:21"); #emph[every morning]; (and similarly before a group of words, #bref("Lev 24:8");), for which the #emph[distributive]; #he[לְ]; is also used, #he[לַבֹּ֫קֶר לַבֹּ֫קֶר]; #bref("1 Chr 9:27");, and with #emph[one]; plural #he[לַבְּקָרִים]; #bref("Ps 73:14");, #he[לִבְקָרִים]; #bref("Job 7:18"); parallel with #he[לִרְגָעִים]; #emph[every moment];. Somewhat different are the instances with #he[בְּ]; before the second word only, e.g. #he[יוֹם בְּיוֹם]; #emph[day]; by #emph[day];, #bref("1 Chr 12:22");; #he[שָׁנָה בְשָׁנָה]; #emph[year by year];, #bref("Deut 15:20");, #bref("1 Sam 1:7"); (but in verse 3 #he[מִיָּמִים יָמִ֫ימָה];), #he[כְּפַ֫עַם בְּפַ֫עַם]; #bref("Num 24:1");, #bref("Judg 16:20");, #bref("20:30"); f., #bref("1 Sam 3:10"); #emph[as at other times];. Also with the two words united by means of #emph[wāw copulative];, #he[אִישׁ וְאִישׁ]; #bref("Ps 87:5");, or #he[אִישׁ וָאִישׁ]; #bref("Esth 1:8");; #he[דּוֹר וָדוֹר]; #emph[all generations];, #bref("Deut 32:7");; #he[יוֹם וָיוֹם]; #bref("Esth 3:4");; cf. #bref("Esth 8:9");, #origpage("396") <orig-396>;#bref("Ezra 10:14");, #bref("1 Chr 26:13"); and often (cf. Cheyne, #emph[Bampton Lectures];, 1889, p. 479, according to whom the use of the #he[ו]; copulative with the second word is especially common in Ch and Est, and therefore belongs to the later language; Driver, #emph[Introd.];#super[6];, p. 538, No. 35); sometimes (but with the exception of #bref("Ps 45:18"); only in very late passages) with a pleonastic #he[כָּל־]; preceding, #bref("Ps 145:13");, #bref("Esth 2:11");, #bref("9:28");, #bref("2 Chr 11:12");, etc.

#ml("d") <p123d> 2. Repetition of words in an expressly #emph[distributive]; sense#footnote[Cf. in the New Testament #bref("Mark 6:39"); f. συμπόσια συμπόσια, πρασιαὶ πρασιαί (Weizsäcker, #emph[tischweise, beetweise];).]; (which may to some extent be noticed in the examples under #emph[c];) equivalent to #emph[one each];, etc., e.g. #bref("Num 14:34"); #emph[forty days]; #he[יוֹם לַשָּׁנָה יוֹם לַשָּׁנָה]; #emph[counting for every day a year];; cf. #bref("Ezek 24:6");, #bref("Exod 28:34"); (three words repeated); also with the addition of #he[לְבַד]; #emph[apart];, #he[עֵדֶר עֵדֶר לְבַדּוֹ]; #emph[every drove by itself];, #bref("Gen 32:17");; cf. #bref("Zech 12:12");. Most frequently with the addition of a numeral (for the simple repetition of numerals for the same purpose, cf. #xref(<p134q>)[§ 134#h(0.18em)#emph[q];];), and with the words not only in groups of two (#bref("Lev 24:8");, #bref("Num 13:2");, #bref("31:4");) or three (#bref("Num 7:11");, #bref("17:21");), but even of six (#bref("Exod 26:3");) or seven (#bref("Exod 25:33");, #bref("26:19");, 21, 25); in #bref("Exod 25:35"); five words even three times repeated.#footnote[These repetitions of larger groups of words belong entirely to the #emph[Priestly Code]; in the Pentateuch, and are unquestionably indications of a late period of the language. Of quite a different kind are such examples as #bref("Ezek 16:6");, where the repetition of four words serves to give greater solemnity to the promise, unless here, as certainly in 1:20, it is a mere #emph[dittography];; the LXX omit the repetition, in both passages.];

#ml("e") <p123e> 3. Repetition to express an exceptional or at least superfine quality; e.g. #bref("2 Kgs 25:15"); #emph[which were of gold, gold, of silver, silver];, i.e. made of pure gold and pure silver; #bref("Deut 2:27"); #he[בַּדֶּ֫רֶךְ בַּדֶּ֫רֶךְ]; #emph[only along by the]; high #emph[way];; cf. #bref("Num 3:8");, #bref("8:16"); #emph[they are given, given to him];, i.e. given exclusively for his service, for his very own. Also with a certain hyperbole in such examples as #bref("2 Kgs 3:16"); #he[גֵּבִים גֵּבִים]; #emph[nothing but trenches];; #bref("Gen 14:10"); #he[בֶּֽאֱרֹת בֶּֽאֱרֹת חֵמָר]; #emph[all asphalt\-pits];.—Repetition serves to intensify the expression to the highest degree in #bref("Judg 5:22"); #emph[by reason of the violent pransings of his strong ones];, #bref("Exod 8:10"); (#emph[countless heaps];), and #bref("Joel 4:14"); (#emph[countless multitudes];); cf. also #he[מְעַט מְעַט]; #bref("Exod 23:30"); #emph[by little and little, very gradually];; cf. #xref(<p133k>)[§ 133#h(0.18em)#emph[k];];.

#ml("f") <p123f> 4. Repetition with the copula to express #emph[of more than one kind];; thus #bref("Deut 25:13"); (#bref("Prov 20:10");) #he[אֶ֫בֶן וָאֶ֫בֶן]; #emph[a weight and a weight];, i.e. two kinds of weight (hence the addition #emph[great and small];); #bref("Ps 12:3"); #he[בְּלֵב וָלֵב]; #emph[with two kinds of heart];, i.e. #emph[with a double\-dealing heart];; cf. the opposite #he[בְּלֹא לֵב וָלֵב]; #bref("1 Chr 12:33");.

#sec("124", [The Various Uses of the Plural\-form], note: [#footnote[Cf. Dietrich, ‘Über Begriff und Form des hebr. Plurals,’ in the #emph[Abhandl. zur hebr. Grammatik];, Leipzig, 1846, p. 2 ff.];]) <s124>

#ml("a") <p124a> #strong[1.]; The plural is by no means used in Hebrew solely to express a number of individuals or separate objects, but may also denote them collectively. This use of the plural expresses either (#emph[a];) a combination of various external constituent parts (plurals of #emph[local extension];), or (#emph[b];) a more or less intensive focusing of the characteristics inherent in the idea of the stem (#emph[abstract plurals];, usually rendered in English by forms in #emph[\-hood, \-ness, \-ship];). A variety of the plurals described under (#emph[b];), in which the secondary idea of #emph[intensity]; or of an #emph[internal ]; #origpage("397") <orig-397>;#emph[multiplication]; of the idea of the stem may be clearly seen, is (#emph[c];) the #emph[pluralis excellentiae]; or #emph[pluralis maiestatis];.

#ml("b") <p124b> Examples of (#emph[a];): Plurals of #emph[local extension]; to denote localities in general, but especially level surfaces (the #emph[surface];\-plural), since in them the idea of a whole composed of innumerable separate parts or points is most evident, as #he[שָׁמַ֫יִם]; (#xref(<p88d>)[§ 88#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];) #emph[heaven]; (cf. also #he[מְרוֹמִים]; #emph[heights]; of heaven, #bref("Isa 33:16");, #bref("Job 16:19");; elsewhere #he[מָרוֹם];); #he[מַ֫יִם]; #emph[water];; #he[יַמִּים]; (the broad surface of the sea) poetically for #he[יָם]; #emph[sea];; #he[פָנִים]; (prop. the side #emph[turned towards]; any one, then) #emph[surface]; in general, usually #emph[face];; #he[אֲחוֹרִים]; #emph[the back];, #bref("Exod 26:12");, #bref("33:23");, etc., #he[צַוָּארִים]; #emph[neck nape of the neck];#footnote[Cf. the same use of the plural in τὰ στέρνα, τὰ νῶτα, τὰ τράχηλα, #emph[praecordia];, #emph[cervices, fauces];; on plurals of extension in general, cf. the prepositions of place and time in the plur. form, #xref(<p103n>)[§ 103#h(0.18em)#emph[n];];. #he[סְפָרִים]; is not a case in point, in the sense of #emph[letter]; (properly a sheet folded into several pages; elsewhere also #he[סֵ֫פֶר];) #bref("1 Kgs 21:8"); ff., #bref("2 Kgs 10:1");, #bref("19:14"); (#bref("Isa 37:14");; referred to afterwards by the #emph[singular]; suffix); #bref("Isa 39:1");, #bref("Jer 29:25");, #bref("32:14"); (#emph[after]; being folded, previously #he[סֵ֫פֶר];).];; also #he[מְרַֽאֲשׁוֹת]; #emph[the place at the head];, #he[מַרְגְּלוֹת]; #emph[place at the feet];; #he[עֲבָרִים]; #emph[place on the other side]; (of a river); #he[מַֽעֲמַקִּים]; #emph[depth];, #he[מֶרְחַקִּים]; (also #he[מֶרְחָק];) #emph[distance];, #he[מִשְׁכָּבִים]; #emph[bed];, #bref("Gen 49:4"); (unless, with Dillmann, it is to be explained in the sense of #emph[double bed];, i.e. #emph[torus];), #he[מִשְׁכָּנִים]; #bref("Ps 46:5");, and #he[מִשְׁכָּנוֹת]; #bref("43:3");, #bref("84:2");, #bref("132:5");, #emph[dwelling]; (perhaps also #he[אֹֽהָלִים]; #emph[encampment];, in passages like #bref("1 Sam 4:10");). The last four belong, however, to poetic style, and are better reckoned among the #emph[plurals of amplification]; treated under #xref(<p124d>)[#emph[d];];–#xref(<p124f>)[#emph[f];];. So perhaps #he[יְצֻעִים]; #emph[bed]; (#bref("Ps 63:7");, #bref("Job 17:13");; but #bref("Gen 49:4");, #bref("Ps 132:3");, etc., in the singular); probably, however, #he[יְצֻעִים]; (prop. #emph[strata];) refers to a number of coverings or pillows.

The plural of extension is used to denote a lengthened period of #emph[time]; in #he[עֽוֹלָמִים]; #emph[eternity]; (everlasting ages).

#ml("c") <p124c> Rem. The plural of extension includes also a few examples which were formerly explained as simply poetic plurals, e.g. #bref("Job 17:1"); #he[קְבָרִים לִי]; #emph[graves are]; (ready) #emph[for me];, i.e. the place where there are many of them (as it were #emph[the graveyard];) is my portion, #bref("Job 21:32");, #bref("2 Chr 16:14");; cf. #bref("2 Kgs 22:20");.

#ml("d") <p124d> Of (#emph[b];): the tolerably numerous abstract plurals, mostly of a particular form (#emph[q#super[e];ṭûlîm, qiṭṭûlîm];, etc.), may be divided into two classes. They sum up either the #emph[conditions]; or #emph[qualities]; inherent in the idea of the stem, or else the various single #emph[acts]; of which an action is composed. Cf. for the first class, #he[בְּחוּרִים]; and #he[בְּחוּרוֹת]; #emph[youth];, #he[זְקֻנִים]; #emph[old age];, #he[נְעוּרִים]; #emph[youth];; #he[בְּתוּלִים]; #emph[maidenhood];, #he[כְּלוּלוֹת]; #emph[bridal state];; #he[מְגוּרִים]; #emph[condition of a sojourner];, #he[בְּשָׂרִים]; #emph[fleshliness]; (only in #bref("Prov 14:30");), #he[חַיִּים]; #emph[life]; (the abstract idea of the qualities of a living being); #he[שִׁכּוּלִים]; #emph[childlessness];, #he[סַנְוֵרִים]; #emph[blindness];, #he[עִוְעִים]; #emph[perverseness];.

#ml("e") <p124e> There are also a number of plurals, found almost exclusively in poetry (sometimes along with the singular), which are evidently intended to intensify#footnote[Cf. A. Ember, ‘The pluralis intensivus in Hebrew,’ #emph[AJSL.]; 1905, p. 195 ff.]; the idea of the stem (plural of amplification), as #he[אוֹנִים]; #emph[might];, #bref("Isa 40:26");; #he[אֱמוּנִים]; (as well as #he[אֱמוּנָה];) and #he[אֱמוּנוֹת]; #emph[faithfulness];; #he[אַשְׁרֵי]; (according to #xref(<p93l>)[§ 93#h(0.18em)#emph[l];];, only in the construct state plural or with suffixes \= #emph[the happiness of];), #emph[happy];; #he[כּֽוֹשָׁרוֹת]; (complete) #emph[prosperity];, #bref("Ps 68:7");; #he[בִּינוֹת]; #bref("Isa 27:11"); and #he[תְּבוּנוֹת]; #bref("Isa 40:14");, etc. (keen) #emph[understanding];; #he[עֵצוֹת]; (true) #emph[counsel];, #bref("Deut 32:28");; #he[דֵּעִים]; #bref("Job 37:16"); and #he[דֵּעוֹת]; #origpage("398") <orig-398>;#bref("1 Sam 2:3"); (thorough) #emph[knowledge];; #he[בַּטֻּחוֹת]; #bref("Job 12:6"); and #he[מִבְטַחִים]; #bref("Isa 32:18"); (full) #emph[confidence];; #he[בְּרָכוֹת]; (abundant) #emph[blessing];, #bref("Ps 21:7");; #he[גְּבוּרוֹת]; (exceptional) #emph[strength];, #bref("Job 41:4");; #he[הַוּוֹת]; #bref("Ps 5:10"); (very) #emph[wickedness];; #he[חֲמוּדוֹת]; #bref("Dan 9:23"); (greatly) #emph[beloved];; #he[חֵמוֹת]; #bref("Ps 76:11");, etc.(fierce) #emph[wrath];; #he[חֲרָפוֹת]; #bref("Dan 12:2"); (utter) #emph[contempt];; #he[יְשֻׁעוֹת]; (real) #emph[help];, #bref("Isa 26:18");, etc.; #he[מַרְאֹת]; #bref("Gen 46:2"); (an important) #emph[vision];; #he[מֵֽישָׁרִים]; #emph[uprightness];; #he[תַּהְפֻּכוֹת]; #emph[perversity];; #he[נְקָמוֹת]; (complete) #emph[vengeance];, #bref("Judg 11:36");, etc.; #he[חֲשֵׁכִים]; and #he[מַֽחֲשַׁכִּים]; (thick) #emph[darkness];; #he[מִסְתָּרִים]; #emph[a]; (close) #emph[hiding\-place];; #he[נְגִידִים]; #emph[nobility];; #he[שְׁמָנִים]; #bref("Isa 28:1"); #emph[fatness];; #he[צַחְצָחוֹת]; (complete) #emph[aridity];; #he[מַמְתַּקִּים]; #emph[sweetness];; #he[מַֽחֲמַדִּים]; #emph[preciousness];; #he[שַֽׁעֲשֻׁעִים]; #emph[delight];; #he[עֲדָנִים]; and #he[תַּֽעֲנֻגִים]; #emph[pleasure];; #he[רַֽחֲמִים]; #emph[compassion];; #he[מְנוּחֹת]; #bref("Ps 23:2"); #emph[rest, refreshment];; #he[מְהוּמֹת]; #bref("Amos 3:9"); #emph[tumult];. Probably also #he[יְדִידֹת]; (heartfelt) #emph[love];, #bref("Ps 45:1");; #he[מְרֹרוֹת]; (extreme) #emph[bitterness];, #bref("Job 13:26");; #he[מִרְמוֹת]; (base) #emph[deceit];, #bref("Ps 38:13");; #he[צְדָקוֹת]; (true) #emph[righteousness];, #bref("Isa 33:15");, etc.; #he[שְׁמָחוֹת]; (the highest) #emph[joy];, #bref("Ps 16:11");. On the other hand, #he[חָכְמוֹת]; #emph[wisdom]; (#bref("Prov 1:20");, etc.) can hardly be a #emph[plural]; (\=the essence of wisdom, or wisdom personified), but is a singular (see #xref(<p86l>)[§ 86#h(0.18em)#emph[l];];).

A further extension of this plural of amplification occurs according to P. Haupt’s very probable suggestion (#emph[SBOT.]; Proverbs, p. 40, line 50, etc.) in #he[יְאֹרִים]; #emph[the great river]; (of the Nile, generally #he[יְאֹר];) #bref("Isa 7:18");, #bref("19:6"); (though with the predicate in the plural), #bref("Ezek 30:12");, #bref("Ps 78:44");, but in #bref("Isa 37:25");, #bref("Ezek 29:3"); the usual explanation, #emph[arms]; or #emph[channels of the Nile];, can hardly be avoided; also in #he[נְהָרוֹת]; #bref("Ps 24:2"); of the ocean, which encircles the earth, 137:1 of the #emph[great river];, i.e. the Euphrates, but in #bref("Isa 18:1"); #he[נַֽהֲרֵי כוּשׁ]; is evidently a numerical plural.—In #bref("Prov 16:13"); #he[מְלָכִים]; (acc. to P. Haupt\=the #emph[great king];) is very doubtful. In #he[נְשִׂיאֵי]; #bref("Ezek 19:1"); the second #emph[yôdh]; is evidently due to dittography, since #he[ישְׂרָאֵל]; follows.

#ml("f") <p124f> The summing up of the several parts of an action is expressed in #he[חֲנֻמִים]; #emph[embalming];, #he[כִּפֻּרִים]; #emph[atonement];, #he[מִלֻּאִים]; (prop. #emph[filling];, sc. of the hand) #emph[ordination to the priesthood];, #he[שִׁלֻּחִים]; #emph[dismissal];, #he[שִׁלֻּמִים]; #emph[retribution];, #he[פִּתֻּחִים]; #emph[engraving]; (of a seal, etc.); #he[אֳהָבִים]; #emph[fornication];, #he[זְנוּנִים]; #emph[whoredom];, #he[נִֽאֻפִים]; #emph[adultery];; #he[נִֽחֻמִים]; (prop. no doubt, warm compassion) #emph[consolation];, #he[תַּֽחֲנוּנים]; #emph[supplication];, #he[נְדֻדִים]; #bref("Job 7:4"); (restless) #emph[tossing to and fro];, #he[פְּלָאִים]; #emph[wonder]; #bref("Lam 1:9");, #he[עֹלֵלוֹת]; #emph[gleaning];; perhaps also #he[נְגִינִוֹת]; #bref("Ps 4:1");, #bref("6:1");, etc., if it means #emph[the playing on stringed instruments];, and #he[שַׁלְמֹנִים]; #bref("Isa 1:23"); #emph[bribery];, unless it be a plural of number.#footnote[Mayer Lambert in #emph[REJ.]; xxiv. 106 ff., enumerates no less than ninety\-five words ending in #emph[îm];, which in his opinion are to be regarded as #emph[pluralia tantum];.];

#ml("g") <p124g> Of (#emph[c];): the #emph[pluralis excellentiae]; or #emph[maiestatis];, as has been remarked above, is properly a variety of the abstract plural, since it sums up the several characteristics#footnote[The Jewish grammarians call such plurals #he[רִבּוּי הַכֹּחוֹת]; #emph[plur. virium]; or #emph[virtutum];; later grammarians call them #emph[plur. excellentiae, magnitudinis];, or #emph[plur. maiestaticus];. This last name may have been suggested by the #emph[we]; used by kings when speaking of themselves (cf. already #bref("1 Macc 10:19");, #bref("11:31");); and the plural used by God in #bref("Gen 1:26");, #bref("11:7");, #bref("Isa 6:8"); has been incorrectly explained in this way. It is, however, either #emph[communicative]; (including the attendant angels; so at all events in #bref("Isa 6:8");, cf. also #bref("Gen 3:22");), or according to others, an indication of #emph[the fullness of power and might]; implied in #he[אֱלֹהִים]; (see Dillmann on #bref("Gen 1:26");); but it is best explained as a plural of #emph[self\-deliberation];. The use of the plural as a form of respectful address is quite foreign to Hebrew.]; belonging to the idea, besides possessing the secondary sense of an #emph[intensification]; of the original idea. It is thus closely related to the plurals of amplification, treated under #xref(<se>)[];, which are mostly found in poetry. #origpage("399") <orig-399>;So especially #he[אֱלֹהִים]; #emph[Godhead, God]; (to be distinguished from the numerical plural #emph[gods];, #bref("Exod 12:12");, etc.). The supposition that #he[אֱלֹהִים]; is to be regarded as merely a remnant of earlier polytheistic views (i.e. as originally only a numerical plural) is at least highly improbable, and, moreover, would not explain the analogous plurals (see below). That the language has entirely rejected the idea of numerical plurality in #he[אֱלֹהִים]; (whenever it denotes #emph[one]; God), is proved especially by its being almost invariably joined with a singular attribute (cf. #xref(<p132h>)[§ 132#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];), e.g. #he[אֱלֹהִים צַדִּיק]; #bref("Ps 7:10");, etc. Hence #he[אֱלֹהִים]; may have been used originally not only as a numerical but also as an abstract plural (corresponding to the Latin #emph[numen];, and our #emph[Godhead];), and, like other abstracts of the same kind, have been transferred to a concrete single god (even of the heathen).

#ml("h") <p124h> To the same class (and probably formed on the analogy of #he[אֱלֹהִים];) belong the plurals #he[קְדֹשִׁים]; #emph[the Most Holy]; (only of Yahweh), #bref("Hos 12:1");, #bref("Prov 9:10");, #bref("30:3"); (cf. #he[אֱלֹהִים קְדֹשִׁים]; #bref("Josh 24:19");, and the Aram. #he[עֶלְיוֹנִין]; #emph[the Most High];, #bref("Dan 7:18");, 22, 25); and probably #he[תְּרָפִים]; (usually taken in the sense of #emph[penates];) the image of a god, used especially for obtaining oracles. Certainly in #bref("1 Sam 19:13");, 16 only #emph[one]; image is intended; in most other places a single image #emph[may]; be intended#footnote[Even in #bref("Gen 31:34");, notwithstanding the plural suffix in #he[וַתְּשִׂמֵם]; and #he[עֲלֵיהֶם];, since the construction of these abstracts as numerical plurals is one of the peculiarities of the E\-document of the Hexateuch; cf. #bref("Gen 20:13");, #bref("35:7");, and #xref(<p145i>)[§ 145#h(0.18em)#emph[i];];.];; in #bref("Zech 10:2"); alone is it most naturally taken as a numerical plural. In #bref("Eccl 5:7"); #he[גְּבֹהִים]; #emph[supremus]; (of God) is doubtful; according to others it is a numerical plural, #emph[superiores];.

#ml("i") <p124i> Further, #he[אֲדֹנִים];, as well as the singular #he[אָדוֹן];, (lordship) #emph[lord];, e.g. #he[אֲדֹנִים קָשֶׁה]; #emph[a cruel lord];, #bref("Isa 19:4");; #he[אֲדֹנֵי הָאָ֫רֶץ]; #emph[the lord of the land];, #bref("Gen 42:30");, cf. #bref("Gen 32:19");; so especially with the suffixes of the 2nd and 3rd persons #he[אֲדֹנֶ֫יךָ];, #he[אֲדֹנַ֫יִךְ]; #bref("Ps 45:12");, #he[אֲדֹנָיו];, etc., also #he[אֲדֹנֵ֫ינוּ]; (except #bref("1 Sam 16:16");); but in 1st sing. always #he[אֲדֹנִי];.#footnote[On #he[אֲדֹנָי]; (for #he[אֲדֹנִי];) as a name of God, cf. #xref(<p135q>)[§ 135#h(0.18em)#emph[q];];.]; So also #he[בְּעָלִים]; (with suffixes) #emph[lord, master]; (of slaves, cattle, or inanimate things; but in the sense of #emph[maritus];, always in the singular), e.g. #he[בְּעָלָיו]; #bref("Exod 21:29");, #bref("Isa 1:3");, etc.#footnote[Euting, #emph[Reise in Arabien];, p. 61, mentions the interesting fact that the subjects of the Emir of Ḥâyel commonly speak of their ruler as #emph[šiyûkh];, a plur. majestatis\= #emph[the great sheikh];.];

#ml("k") <p124k> On the other hand, we must regard as doubtful a number of participles in the plural, which, being used as attributes of God, resemble #emph[plurales excellentiae];; thus, #he[עֹשָׂי]; #emph[my Maker];, #bref("Job 35:10");; #he[עֹשַׂ֫יִךְ]; #bref("Isa 54:5");; #he[עֹשָׂיו]; #bref("Ps 149:2");; #he[עֹשֶׂיהָ]; #bref("Isa 22:11");; #he[נֽוֹטֵיהֶם]; #emph[stretching them out];, #bref("Isa 42:5");; for all these forms may also be explained as singular, according to #xref(<p93ss>)[§ 93#h(0.18em)#emph[ss];];.#footnote[#he[בֹּֽעֲלַ֫יִךְ];, which in #bref("Isa 54:5"); is in parallelism with #he[עֹשַׂ֫יִךְ];, must then be explained as merely formed on analogy.];—#he[נֹֽגְשָׂיו]; #bref("Isa 3:12"); might also be regarded as another instance, unless it be a numerical plural, #emph[their oppressors];; moreover, #he[מְרִימָיו]; #emph[him who lifteth it up];, #bref("Isa 10:15"); (but read probably #he[מְרִימוֹ];); #he[שֹֽׁלְחָיו]; #emph[him who sendeth him];, #bref("Prov 10:26");, #bref("22:21"); (so Baer, but Ginsburg #he[שֹֽׁלְחֶ֫ךָ];), 25:13 (in parallelism with #he[אֲדֹנָיו];). These latter plurals, however (including #he[מרימיו];), may probably be more simply explained as indicating an indefinite individual, cf. #emph[o]; below.—For #he[שֹֽׁמְרֶ֫יךָ]; #bref("Ps 121:5"); (textus receptus) and #he[בּֽוֹרְאֶ֫יךָ]; #bref("Eccl 12:1"); (textus receptus) the singular should be read, with Baer. #origpage("400") <orig-400>;

#ml("l") <p124l> Rem. 1. (#emph[a];) Coherent substances, etc., are mostly regarded as #emph[single];, and are, accordingly, almost always represented by nouns in the singular, cf. #he[אָבָק]; #emph[fine dust];, #he[אֵ֫פֶר]; #emph[ashes];, #he[בַּד]; #emph[linen];, #he[בְּדִיל]; #emph[lead];, #he[זָהָב]; #emph[gold];, #he[כֶּ֫סֶף]; #emph[silver];, #he[נְח֫שֶׁת]; #emph[brass];, #he[חָלָב]; #emph[milk];, #he[יַ֫יִן]; #emph[wine];, #he[עָפָר]; #emph[dust, the ground];, #he[עֵץ]; #emph[wood];. Plurals are, however, formed from some of these words expressing materials in order to denote separate portions taken from the whole in manufacture (#emph[plurals of the result];) or parts otherwise detached from it; thus, #he[בַּדִּים]; #emph[linen garments];; #he[כְּסָפִים]; #emph[silver pieces];, #bref("Gen 42:25");, 35; #he[נְחֻשְׁתַּ֫יִם]; (dual) #emph[fetters of brass];; #he[עֵצִים]; #emph[ligna]; (#emph[timber]; for building or #emph[sticks]; for burning); also in a wider sense, #he[בְּדִילִים]; particles of #emph[alloy]; to be separated by smelting, #bref("Isa 1:25");; #he[עֲפָרוֹת]; #emph[fragments of earth];, #bref("Prov 8:26");, cf. #bref("Job 28:6"); #he[עַפְרֹת זָהָב]; #emph[dust of gold];.

#ml("m") <p124m> (#emph[b];) To the class of #emph[plurals of the result]; belong also a few names of natural products, when represented in an artificial condition; thus, #he[חִטִּים]; #emph[wheat]; in grain (threshed wheat), as distinguished from #he[חִטָּה]; #emph[wheat]; (used collectively) in the ear; cf. the same distinction between #he[כֻּסְּמִים]; and #he[כֻּסֶּ֫מֶת]; #emph[spelt];; #he[עֲדָשִׁים]; and #he[עֲדָשָׁה]; (the singular preserved only in the Mishna) #emph[lentils];; #he[שְׂעֹרִים]; and #he[שְׂעֹרָה]; #emph[barley];; also #he[פִּשְׁתִּים]; #emph[linen];, #he[פֵּ֫שֶׁת]; (to be inferred from #he[פִּשְׁתִּי];) #emph[flax];.

#ml("n") <p124n> (#emph[c];) Finally, the distinction between #he[דָּם]; #emph[blood]; and #he[דָּמִים]; requires to be specially noticed. The singular is always used when the blood is regarded as an organic unity, hence also of menstrual blood, and the blood of sacrifices (collected in the basin and then sprinkled), and in #bref("Num 23:24"); of the blood gushing from wounds. On the other hand, #he[דָּמִים]; as a sort of plural of the result and at the same time of local extension, denotes #emph[blood which is shed];, when it appears as blood\-stains (#bref("Isa 1:15");) or as blood\-marks (so evidently in #bref("Isa 9:4");). But since blood\-stains or blood\-marks, as a rule, suggest blood shed in murder (although #he[דָּמִים]; also denotes the blood which flows at child\-birth or in circumcision), #he[דָּמִים]; acquired (even in very early passages) simply the sense of a #emph[bloody deed];, and especially of #emph[bloodguiltiness];, #bref("Exod 22:1"); f., etc.

#ml("o") <p124o> In some few cases the plural is used to denote an indefinite singular; certainly so in #bref("Deut 17:5"); #he[אֶל־שְׁעָרֶ֫יךָ]; #emph[unto #emph[one of]; thy gates];; #bref("Zech 9:9"); #he[בֶּן־אֲתֹנוֹת]; (cf. #bref("Song 2:9");); #bref("Exod 21:22"); #he[יְלָדֶ֫יהָ]; (where evidently only #emph[one]; child is thought of, though certainly in connection with a contingency which may be repeated); cf. also #bref("Eccl 4:10"); (#emph[if one of them fall];).—So probably also #bref("Gen 8:4");, #bref("1 Sam 17:43");, #bref("Dan 2:1");, #bref("Neh 3:8");, #bref("6:2");; but #emph[not]; #bref("Gen 19:29");, since the same document (#bref("Gen 13:12");) makes Lot dwell #emph[in the cities]; of the Jordan valley; in #bref("Gen 21:7"); #he[בָּנִים]; denotes the class with which the action is concerned. In #bref("Judg 12:7"); instead of the unusual #he[בְּעָרֵי גִלְעָד]; #emph[in the cities of Gilead]; (formerly explained here as #emph[in one of the cities of Gilead];) we should most probably read, with Moore (#emph[SBOT.]; Judges, p. 52), #he[בְּעִירוֹ בְּמִצְפֵּה גִלְעָד]; #emph[in his city, in Mizpeh]; (in) #emph[Gilead];.

#ml("p") <p124p> #strong[2.]; When a substantive is followed by a genitive, and the compound idea thus formed is to be expressed in the plural, this is done—

(#emph[a];) Most naturally by using the plural of the #emph[nomen regens];, e.g. #he[גִּבּוֹרֵי חַ֫יִל]; #emph[mighty men of valour]; (prop. heroes of strength), #bref("1 Chr 7:2");, 9; so also in compounds, e.g. #he[בְּנֵי יְמִינִי]; #bref("1 Sam 22:7");, as the plur. of #he[בֶּן־יְמִינִי]; #emph[Benjamite];; but also

#ml("q") <p124q> (#emph[b];) By using the plural of #emph[both]; nouns,#footnote[Cf. König, #emph[Lehrgebäude];, ii. 438 f., according to whom the plural of the principal word exercises an influence on the determining genitive.]; e.g. #he[גִּבּוֹרֵי חֲיָלִים]; #bref("1 Chr 7:5");; #origpage("401") <orig-401>;#he[וּבְבָֽתֵּי כְלָאִים]; #emph[and in prison houses];, #bref("Isa 42:22");; cf. #bref("Exod 34:1");, etc., #he[שְׁנֵיֽ־לֻחֹת אֲבָנִים]; #emph[two tables of stone]; (but #bref("Exod 31:18"); #he[לֻחֹת אֶ֫בֶן];); #bref("Num 13:32");, #bref("Deut 1:28");, #bref("Josh 5:2");, #bref("6:4");, #bref("2 Kgs 14:14");, #bref("25:23");, #bref("Isa 51:9");, #bref("Jer 41:16");, #bref("Ezra 3:3");, etc. #he[עַמֵּי הָֽאֲרָצוֹת]; #emph[the people of the country];; #bref("2 Chr 26:14");; so perhaps #he[בְּנֵי אֵלִים]; #emph[sons of God];, #bref("Ps 29:1");, #bref("89:7"); (according to others #emph[sons of gods];); or finally even

#ml("r") <p124r> (#emph[c];) By using the plural of the #emph[nomen rectum];;#footnote[Cf. Brockelmann, #emph[Grundriss];, i. 482.]; e.g. #he[בֵּית אָבוֹת]; #bref("Exod 6:14");, #bref("Num 1:2");, 4 ff., etc., as plur. of #he[בֵּית אָב]; #emph[father’s house, family];; #he[בֵּית הַבָּמוֹת]; #emph[the houses of the high places];, #bref("2 Kgs 17:29"); (also #he[בָּֽתֵּי הַבָּמוֹת]; 23:19); #he[בֵּית עֲצַבֵּיהֶם]; #emph[the houses of their idols];, #bref("1 Sam 31:9");, #bref("Ezek 46:24");; cf. also #bref("Judg 7:25"); #emph[the head of Oreb and Zeeb];, i.e. the heads, etc.

#ml("s") <p124s> Rem. When a substantive (in a distributive sense) with a suffix refers back to a plural, the singular form of the substantive suffices, since the idea of plurality is already adequately expressed by the suffix, e.g. #he[פִּימוֹ]; #emph[os]; (for #emph[ora];) #emph[eorum];, #bref("Ps 17:10");; #he[יְמִינָם]; #emph[their right hand];, #bref("Ps 144:8"); \[so in the English RV.\], for #emph[hands];.

#sec("125", [Determination of Nouns in general. Determination of Proper Names], note: []) <s125>

#align(center)[Brockelmann, #emph[Grundriss];, i. 466 ff.];

#ml("a") <p125a> #strong[1.]; A noun may either be determinate in itself, as a proper name or pronoun (see below, #emph[d]; and #emph[i];), or be made so by its context. In the latter case, the determination may be effected either by prefixing the article (see § 126), or by the connection of the noun (in the construct state) with a following determinate genitive, and consequently also (according to #xref(<p33c>)[§ 33#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];) by its union with a pronominal suffix (#xref(<p127a>)[§ 127#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];). It is to be taken as a fundamental rule, that the determination can only be effected in #emph[one]; of the ways here mentioned; the article cannot be prefixed to a proper name, nor to a noun followed by the genitive, nor can a proper name be used in the construct state. Deviations from this rule are either only apparent or have arisen from a corruption of the text.

#ml("b") <p125b> Rem. Only in a few passages is a noun made expressly #emph[indeterminate]; by the addition of #he[אֶחָד]; in the sense of our indefinite article; cf. #bref("Exod 16:33");, #bref("Judg 9:53");, #bref("13:2");, #bref("1 Sam 1:1");, #bref("7:9");, 12, #bref("1 Kgs 13:11");, #bref("19:4");, #bref("20:13");, #bref("22:9");, #bref("2 Kgs 4:1");, #bref("8:6");, #bref("12:10");, #bref("Ezek 8:8");, #bref("Dan 8:3");, #bref("10:5"); (in 8:13 #he[אֶחָד קָדוֹשׁ]; i.e. #emph[one];, namely #emph[a holy one];, is opposed to another).

#ml("c") <p125c> It is further to be noticed, that in Hebrew the phenomenon sometimes occurs, which the Arab grammarians call #emph[indeterminateness for the sake of amplification];; e.g. #bref("Isa 31:8"); #emph[and he shall flee]; #he[מִפְּנֵי־חֶ֫רֶב]; #emph[from a sword];, i.e. #emph[from an irresistible sword]; (God’s sword); cf. #bref("Isa 28:2"); #he[בְּיָד];; #bref("2 Sam 6:2"); #he[שֵׁם];; #bref("Hos 3:1"); #he[אִשָּׁה]; #emph[such a woman];, without doubt to be referred to the Gomer mentioned in cap. 1; #origpage("402") <orig-402>;#bref("Amos 6:14"); #he[גּוֹי];; #bref("Ps 77:16"); #he[בִּזְרֹעַ];; #bref("Prov 21:12"); #he[צַדִּיק];, if with Delitzsch it is to be referred to God; #bref("Job 8:10"); #he[מִלִּים]; meaning #emph[important words];, but in 15:13 #he[מִלִּין]; #emph[reproachful words];. Cf. on this point, #xref(<p117q>)[§ 117#h(0.18em)#emph[q];];, note 3, and Delitzsch, #emph[Psalmen];, ed. 4, p. 79.

#ml("d") <p125d> #strong[2.]; Real #emph[proper nouns];, as being the names of things (or persons) only #emph[once]; met with, are sufficiently determinate in themselves. Such names, therefore, as #he[יהוה];, #he[דָּוִד];, #he[יַֽעֲקֹב];, #he[כְּנַ֫עַן];, #he[סְדֹם]; do not admit of the article,#footnote[Consequently, #he[הַֽמְנַשֶּׁח]; #bref("Deut 3:13");, #bref("Josh 1:12");, etc. (in the Deuteronomist) in the combination #he[שֵׁבֶט הַֽמְנַשֶּׁה]; (for which elsewhere #he[שֵׁבֶט מְנַשֶּׁה];) is to be regarded #emph[not]; as a proper name but as a gentilic name (\= #emph[the tribe of the Manassites];), for which in #bref("Deut 29:7"); #he[שׁ׳ הַֽמְנַשִּׁי]; is used, as in 10:8 #he[שׁ׳ הַלֵּוִי]; #emph[the tribe of the Levites];, and in #bref("Judg 18:1"); #he[שׁ׳ הַדָּנִי]; #emph[the tribe of the Danites];.—In #bref("Josh 13:7"); #he[הַֽמְנַשֶּׁה]; (like gentilic names in #he[־ִי];) is even used adjectivally.]; nor can they be in the construct state. On the other hand, not only #emph[gentilic]; names (as denoting the #emph[various]; individuals belonging to the same class), but also all those proper names, of which the appellative sense is still sufficiently evident to the mind, or at least has been handed down from an earlier period of the language, frequently (often even as a rule) take the article (according to #xref(<p126e>)[§ 126#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];), and may even be followed by a genitive.

#ml("e") <p125e> Examples. Like the above\-mentioned proper names of individuals, countries, and cities, so also national names, which are identical in form with the name of the founder of the race (e.g. #he[יִשְׂרָאֵל];, #he[אֱדֹם];, #he[מוֹאָב];), are always determinate in themselves. Of gentilic names (e.g. #he[הָֽעִבְרִי]; #emph[the Hebrew];, #he[הָֽעִבְרִים]; #emph[the Hebrews];, #bref("Gen 40:15");; #he[הַכְּנַֽעֲנִי]; #emph[the Canaanite];) the plural #he[פְּלִשְׁתִּים];, even when meaning #emph[the]; Philistines, is generally used without the article (but in #bref("1 Sam 4:7");, etc., #he[הַפְּ׳];); so always #he[כַּפְתֹּרִים];.—Evident appellatives (like such modern names as #emph[the Hague];, #emph[le Havre];) are #he[הַגִּבְעָה]; #emph[the hill];, in the construct state #he[גִּבְעַת שָׁאוּל];, i.e. #emph[the Gibeah named after Saul]; to distinguish it from others; #he[הָֽרָמָה]; #emph[the height];; #he[הָעַי]; #emph[the heap];; #he[הַלְּבָנוֹן]; (prop. #emph[the white mountain];) #emph[the Lebanon];; #he[הַיְאֹר]; (prop. #emph[the river];) #emph[the Nile];, cf. #bref("Amos 8:8"); #he[כִּיאוֹר מִצְרָֽיִם]; #emph[like the river of Egypt];; #he[הַיַּרְדֵּן]; #emph[the Jordan]; (according to Seybold, #emph[Mittheil. und Nachr. des DPV.];, 1896, p. 11, probably #emph[the drinking\-place]; \[#he[ירד];, Arab. #emph[warada];, meaning orig. #emph[to go down to drink];\]).

#ml("f") <p125f> Rem. 1. In a few instances original appellatives have completely assumed the character of real proper names, and are therefore used without the article; thus #he[אֱלֹהִים]; #emph[God];, to denote the one true God (as elsewhere #he[יהוה];) #bref("Gen 1:1"); and so generally in this document of the Pentateuch up to Ex 6, elsewhere sometimes #he[הָֽאֱלֹהִים]; ὁ θεός (cf. #xref(<p126e>)[§ 126#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];); also the sing. #he[אֱלוֹהַּ]; #emph[God];, #he[עֶלְיוֹן]; #emph[the Most High];, and #he[שַׁדַּי]; #emph[the Almighty]; never take the article.—Moreover, #he[אָדָם]; #emph[Adam]; from #bref("Gen 5:1"); onwards (previously in 2:7, etc., #he[הָֽאָדָם]; #emph[the]; first #emph[man];); #he[שָׁטָן]; #emph[Satan];, #bref("1 Chr 21:1"); (but #bref("Zech 3:1");, #bref("Job 1:6");, etc., #he[הַשָּׂטָן]; #emph[the adversary];); cf. #he[אֹהֶל מוֹעֵד]; #emph[the tent of revelation]; (i.e. the tabernacle), always without the article.

#ml("g") <p125g> To the class of nouns originally appellative, which the language regards #origpage("403") <orig-403>;as proper names, and which consequently never take the article, belong also certain archaic words mostly used only by poets, such as #he[שְׁאוֹל]; #emph[Hades];, #he[תֵּבֵל]; #emph[world];, #he[תְּהוֹם]; #emph[ocean];, of the body of water which encircles the earth, #bref("Gen 1:2");, etc.: but #bref("Isa 63:13");, #bref("Ps 106:9"); #he[בַּתְּהֹמוֹת]; #emph[through the depths];, namely of the Red Sea.#footnote[That various other words, such as #he[אֱנוֹשׁ]; #emph[man];, #he[צַלְמָ֫וֶת]; #emph[deep darkness];, #he[רֹזֵן]; #emph[prince];, #he[שָׂדַי]; #emph[field];, #he[תּֽוּשִׁיָּה]; #emph[effectual working];, are always found without the article is not to be attributed to any special archaism, but is to be explained from the fact that they belong solely to poetic language, which avoids the article; in other cases, such as #he[תַּרְדֵּמָה]; #emph[deep sleep];, there is no occasion for the article in the passages we possess.];

#ml("h") <p125h> 2. When nouns which the usage of the language always treats as proper names occasionally appear to be connected with a following genitive, this is really owing to an ellipse whereby the noun which really governs the genitive, i.e. the appellative idea contained in the proper name, is suppressed. So evidently in the case of #he[יְהֹוָה צְבָאוֹת]; #emph[Yahweh]; (the God) #emph[of hosts];; the fuller form #he[יהוה אֱלֹהֵי צְבָאוֹת]; #bref("2 Sam 5:10");, etc., or #he[יהוה אֱלֹהֵי הַצְּבָאוֹת]; #bref("Amos 3:13");, etc., is a secondary expansion of the original #he[יְהֹוָה צְבָאוֹת];; #he[אֱלֹהִים צְבָאוֹת]; in #bref("Ps 59:6");, #bref("80:15");, 20, 84:9 is due to the mechanical substitution of #he[אֱלֹהִים]; for #he[יהוה]; affected in the 2nd and part of the 3rd book of the Psalms. So also in geographical names such as #he[אוּר כַּשְׂדִּים]; #emph[Ur]; (the city) #emph[of the Chaldees];, #bref("Gen 11:28");; #he[אֲרַם נַֽהֲרַ֫יִם]; #emph[Aram]; (the region) #emph[of the two rivers];; #he[בֵּית לֶ֫חֶם יְהוּדָה]; #emph[Bethlehem]; (the city) #emph[of Judah];; #he[אָבֵל בֵּית מַֽעֲכָה]; #bref("2 Sam 20:14");, etc., to distinguish it from #he[אָבֵל מַ֫יִם]; #emph[Abel by the water];, #bref("2 Chr 16:4");; #he[יָבֵישׁ גִּלְעָד]; #bref("1 Sam 11:1");, etc.; #he[יַרְדֵּן יְרֵחוֹ]; #bref("Num 22:1");, #bref("26:3");, 63, etc.; on #bref("Judg 8:32"); cf. #xref(<p128c>)[§ 128#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];; #he[צִיּוֹן קְדוֹשׁ יִשְׂרָאֵל]; #emph[the Zion of the Holy One of Israel];, #bref("Isa 60:14");; but in #bref("1 Sam 1:1"); for #he[צוֹפִים]; read #he[צוּפִי]; #emph[a Zuphite];. Some of these examples (cf. also #bref("Amos 6:2");) come very near to the actual construct state (cf. above, #he[גִּבְעַת שָׁאוּל];), since e.g. the addition of the genitive serves to distinguish the place from four others called Aram (see the Lexicon), or from another Bethlehem. Aram, Bethlehem, etc., are accordingly no longer names found only in one special sense, and therefore also are no longer proper names in the strictest sense.

#ml("i") <p125i> #strong[3.]; Of the pronouns, the personal pronouns proper (the separate pronouns, § 32) are always determinate in themselves, since they can denote only definite individuals (the 3rd person, also definite things). For the same reason the demonstrative pronouns (§ 34) are also determinate in themselves, when they stand #emph[alone]; (as equivalent to substantives), either as subject (#bref("Gen 5:29");) or as predicate (e.g. #he[זֶה הַיּוֹם]; #emph[this is the day];, #bref("Judg 4:14");; #he[אֵ֫לֶּה הַדְּבָרִים]; #emph[these are the words];, #bref("Deut 1:1");), or as object (e.g. #he[אֶת־זֹאת]; #bref("2 Sam 13:17");), or as genitive (#he[מְחִיר זֶה]; #bref("1 Kgs 21:2");), or finally when joined to a preposition (#he[לְזֹאת]; #bref("Gen 2:23");; #he[בָּזֶה]; #bref("1 Sam 16:8");, see #xref(<p102g>)[§ 102#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];).

#ml("k") <p125k> So also the personal pronouns #he[הוּא];, #he[הִיא];, #he[הֵם];, #he[הֵ֫מָּה];, #he[הֵ֫נָּה]; when they are used as demonstratives (\=#emph[is];, #emph[ea, id, ille];, etc.) are always determinate in themselves, e.g. #he[הוּא הַדָּבָר]; #emph[that is the thing];, #bref("Gen 41:28");. They #origpage("404") <orig-404>;are made determinate by the article, when they are joined like adjectives (see #xref(<p126u>)[§ 126#h(0.18em)#emph[u];];) with a determinate substantive, e.g. #he[הָאִישׁ הַזֶּה]; #emph[this man];; #he[הָֽאֲנָשִׁים הָאֵ֫לֶּה]; #emph[these men];; #he[בַּיָּמִים הָהֵ֫מָּה וּבָעֵת הַהִיא]; #emph[in those days, and in that time];, #bref("Joel 4:1");. The demonstrative, however, even in this case, is frequently used #emph[without]; the article, as being sufficiently determinate in itself (cf. #xref(<p126y>)[§ 126#h(0.18em)#emph[y];];).

#sec("126", [Determination by Means of the Article], note: []) <s126>

#ml("a") <p126a> #strong[1.]; The article (#he[הַ·];, #he[הָ];, #he[הֶ];, § 35) was originally, as in other languages (clearly in the Romance; cf. also ὁ, ἡ, τό in Homer), a demonstrative pronoun. The demonstrative force of the article, apart from its occasional use as a relative pronoun (see #xref(<p138i>)[§ 138#h(0.18em)#emph[i];];), appears now, however, only (#emph[a];) in a few standing phrases, and (#emph[b];) in a certain class of statements or exclamations.

#ml("b") <p126b> (#emph[a];) Cf. #he[הַיּוֹם]; #emph[this day, hodie]; (#xref(<p100c>)[§ 100#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];); #he[הַלַּ֫יְלָה]; #emph[this night];, #bref("Gen 19:34");; #he[הַפַּ֫עַם]; #emph[this time];, #bref("Gen 2:23");; #he[הַשָּׁנָה]; #emph[this year]; (\= in this year) #bref("Isa 37:30");, #bref("Jer 28:16");.

(#emph[b];) includes those instances in which the article, mostly when prefixed to a participle, joins on a new statement concerning a preceding noun. Although such participles, etc., are no doubt primarily regarded always as in apposition to a preceding substantive, the article nevertheless has in some of these examples almost the force of #he[הוּא]; (#he[הִיא];, #he[הֵ֫מָּה];) as the subject of a noun\-clause; e.g. #bref("Ps 19:10"); #emph[the judgements of the Lord are true];..., verse 11 #he[הַנֶּֽחֱמָדִים וג׳]; prop. #emph[the more to be desired than gold];, i.e. #emph[they are more to be desired];, or #emph[even they, that are more to be desired];,#footnote[On the analogous use of the article before participles which have a verbal suffix, as in #bref("Ps 18:33");, etc., cf. above, #xref(<p116f>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];.]; etc.; cf. #bref("Gen 49:21");, #bref("Isa 40:22"); f., 44:27 f., 46:6, #bref("Amos 2:7");, #bref("5:7");, #bref("Ps 33:15");, #bref("49:7"); (#he[הַבֹּֽטְחִים]; in the parallel half of the verse continued by a finite verb); #bref("Ps 104:3");, #bref("Job 6:16");, #bref("28:4");, #bref("30:3");, #bref("41:25"); and frequently. When such a participle has another co\-ordinate with it, the latter is used #emph[without]; the article, since according to the above it strictly speaking represents a second predicate, and as such, according to #emph[i];, remains indeterminate; e.g. #bref("Job 5:10"); #emph[who giveth]; (#he[הַנֹּתֵן];) #emph[rain];, etc., #emph[and sendeth]; (#he[וְשֹׁלֵחַ];), etc.

#ml("c") <p126c> The article is sometimes used with similar emphasis before a substantive, which serves as the subject of a compound sentence (#xref(<p140d>)[§ 140#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];); e.g. #bref("Deut 32:4"); #he[הַצּוּר תָּמִים פָּֽעֳלוֹ]; i.e. as a fresh statement (not in apposition to the preceding dative), really equivalent to #emph[he is a rock, perfect in his work]; (i.e. #emph[whose work is perfect];); cf. #bref("Ps 18:31");.

#ml("d") <p126d> #strong[2.]; The article is, generally speaking, employed to determine a substantive wherever it is required by Greek and English; thus:

(#emph[a];) When a person or thing already spoken of is mentioned again, and is consequently more definite to the mind of the hearer or reader; e.g. #bref("Gen 1:3"); #emph[and God said, Let there be light];: verse 4 #emph[and God saw the light]; (#he[אֶת־הָאוֹר];); #bref("1 Kgs 3:24"); #emph[fetch me a sword: and they brought the sword];; #bref("Eccl 9:15");. (In #bref("2 Sam 12:2"); therefore #he[לֶֽעָשִׁיר]; must be read.) #origpage("405") <orig-405>;(#emph[b];) With a title understood and recognized by every one, e.g. #he[הַמֶּ֫לֶךְ שְׁלֹמֹה]; ὁ βασιλεὺς Σαλωμών: #bref("Gen 35:8"); #emph[under the oak]; (the well\-known oak which was there).

(#emph[c];) With appellatives to denote persons or natural objects which are unique, e.g. #he[הַכֹּהֵן הַגָּדוֹל]; #emph[the high priest];, #he[הַשֶּׁ֫מֶשׁ]; #emph[the sun];, #he[הָאָ֫רֶץ]; #emph[the earth];.

#ml("e") <p126e> (#emph[d];) When terms applying to whole classes are restricted (simply by usage) to particular individuals (like ὁ ποιητής, meaning Homer) or things, e.g. #he[שָׂטָן]; #emph[adversary];, #he[הַשָּׂטָן]; #emph[the adversary, Satan];; #he[בַּ֫עַל]; #emph[lord];, #he[הַבַּ֫עַל]; #emph[Baal]; as proper name of the god; #he[הָֽאָדָם]; #emph[the]; (first) #emph[man, Adam];; #he[הָאֱלֹהִים];#footnote[On the subsequent change of #he[שָׂטָן];, #he[אָדָם];, #he[אֱלֹהִים]; into real proper names by the omission of the article, cf. above, #xref(<p125f>)[§ 125#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];.]; or #he[הָאֵל]; ὁ θεός, #emph[the one true God]; (cf. also ὁ Χριστός in the New Testament); also #he[הַנָּהָר]; #emph[the river];, i.e. #emph[Euphrates];; #he[הַכִּכָּר]; #emph[the circle];, sc. of the Jordan, #emph[the Jordan plain]; \[#bref("Gen 19:17");, etc.\].

(#emph[e];) Very often with the vocative, e.g. #bref("2 Sam 14:4"); #he[הוֹשִׁ֫עָה הַמֶּ֫לֶךְ]; #emph[help, O king];; #bref("Zech 3:8"); #he[יְהוֹשֻׁעַ הַכֹּהֵן הַגָּדוֹל]; #emph[O Joshua the high priest];; #bref("1 Sam 17:58");, #bref("24:9");, #bref("2 Kgs 9:5");; in the plural, #bref("Isa 42:18");, #bref("Joel 1:2");, 13; but cf. also #bref("Josh 10:12");, #bref("Isa 1:2");, #bref("49:13"); (#he[שָׁמַ֫יִם]; and #he[אֶ֫רֶץ];); 23:16, #bref("Hos 13:14");, #bref("Joel 1:5");, #bref("Ps 34:12");, #bref("Eccl 10:17");, #bref("11:9");, etc.#footnote[For further exceptions see Nestle, #emph[ZAW.]; 1904, p. 323 ff.]; The vocative occurs #emph[without]; the article in #bref("Isa 22:2");, since it has been already defined by a preceding accusative.

#ml("f") <p126f> Rem. Strictly speaking in all these cases the substantive with the article is really in apposition to the personal pronoun of the 2nd person, which is either expressly mentioned or virtually present (in the imperative), e.g. #bref("1 Sam 17:58"); #emph[thou, the young man];. But such passages as #bref("Isa 42:18");, where the vocative precedes the imperative, prove that in such cases the substantive originally in apposition eventually acquired the value of a complete clause.

#ml("g") <p126g> (#emph[f];) With words denoting classes (see particulars under #emph[l];).

(#emph[g];) In a peculiar way, to specify persons or things, which are so far definite as to be naturally thought of in connection with a given case, and must be assumed accordingly to be there (see #emph[q];–#emph[s];).

(#emph[h];) With adjectives (also ordinal numbers and demonstrative pronouns used adjectivally) which are joined to substantives determined in some way (see #emph[u];).

#ml("h") <p126h> Rem. The article may be omitted in #emph[poetry]; in all the above\-mentioned cases; in general it is used in poetry far less frequently than in prose. Its use or omission probably often rests on rhythmical grounds;#footnote[Cf. the useful statistics of J. Ley in the #emph[Neue Jahrbücher für Philologie und Pädagogik];, 2te Abteilung, 1891, Heft 7–9, and M. Lambert, ‘L’article dans la poésie hébr.,’ #emph[REJ.]; 37, 263 ff.]; it is sometimes omitted also for rhetorical reasons. Cf. e.g. #he[אֶ֫רֶץ]; for #he[הָאָ֫רֶץ]; #bref("Ps 2:2");; #he[מְלָכִים]; as vocative, verse 10; #he[מֶ֫לֶךְ]; for #he[הַמֶּ֫לֶךְ]; 21:2; #he[שִׁמְךָ גָּדוֹל וְנוֹרָא]; (contrary to #emph[u, v];) 99:3. In the instances in which the #he[ה]; of the article is omitted after a prefix (#xref(<p35n>)[§ 35#h(0.18em)#emph[n];];), the vowel of the article is often retained after the prefix even in poetry, e.g. #he[בַּשָּׁמַ֫יִם]; #bref("Ps 2:4");, etc. #origpage("406") <orig-406>;

#ml("i") <p126i> (#emph[i];) On the other hand, the article is always omitted when a person or thing is to be represented as indefinite (or indefinable) or as yet unknown; consequently also before the predicate, since this is from its nature always a general term, under which the subject is included, e.g. #bref("Gen 29:7"); #he[עוֹד הַיּוֹם גָּדוֹל]; #emph[as yet the day is great];, i.e. it is yet high day; 33:13, 40:18, 41:26, #bref("Isa 66:3");.

#ml("k") <p126k> Rem. 1. As exceptions to the above rule it is usual to regard those examples in which a determinate adjective or participle (equivalent to a relative clause) is used apparently as a predicate, e.g. #bref("Gen 2:11"); #he[הוּא הַסֹּבֵב]; #emph[it is the compassing];, i.e. #emph[that is it which compasseth];; 42:6, 45:12, #bref("Exod 9:27");, #bref("Deut 3:21");, #bref("8:18");, #bref("11:7");, #bref("1 Sam 4:16");, #bref("Isa 14:27");, #bref("Mal 3:2"); (cf. in Greek, e.g. St. Mat. 10:20, where Winer, #emph[Gram. des neutest. Sprachidioms];, § 58, 2, Rem., explains οἱ λαλοῦντες as a predicate with the article). In reality, however, these supposed predicates are rather subjects (acc. to #xref(<p116q>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[q];];), and the only peculiarity of these cases is that the subject is not included under a general idea, but is equated with the predicate.

2\. Sometimes the article is used with only one of two parallel words, as #bref("Nah 1:5"); #he[הָרִים]; and #he[הַגְּבָעוֹת];, #bref("2 Chr 3:17"); #he[מִיָּמִין]; and #he[מֵֽהַשְּׂמֹאול];.

#ml("l") <p126l> #strong[3.]; The use of the article to determine the #emph[class]; is more extensive in Hebrew than in most other languages. In this case the article indicates universally known, closely circumscribed, and therefore well defined classes of persons or things. The special cases to be considered are—

#ml("m") <p126m> (#emph[a];) The employment of general names as collectives in the singular, to denote the #emph[sum total]; of individuals belonging to the class (which may, however, be done just as well by the plural); e.g. #emph[the righteous, the wicked man];, #bref("Eccl 3:17");; #emph[the woman];, i.e. #emph[the female sex];, 7:26; #he[הָֽאֹיֵב]; #emph[the enemy];, i.e. #emph[the enemies]; (?) #bref("Ps 9:7");; #he[הָֽאֹרֵב]; #emph[the lier in wait];, i.e. #emph[the liers in wait];; #he[הֶֽחָלוּץ]; #emph[the armed man];, i.e. #emph[soldiers];; #he[הַֽמְאַסֵּף]; #emph[the rearguard];; #he[הַמַּשְׁחִית]; #emph[the spoiler];, #bref("1 Sam 13:17");;#footnote[But in #bref("Exod 12:23"); #he[המ׳]; is either to be explained as #emph[the destroyer]; (now mentioned for the first time) according to #emph[q];, or a particular angel is meant whose regular function it was to inflict punishments. Others again take #he[המ׳]; even in #bref("Exod 12:23"); impersonally \= #emph[destruction];.]; so also (as in English) with names of animals, when something is asserted of them, which applies to the whole species, e.g. #bref("2 Sam 17:10"); #emph[as the courage of]; #he[הָֽאַרְיֵה]; #emph[the lion];. Especially also with gentilic names, e.g. #emph[the Canaanite];, #bref("Gen 13:7"); (cf. 15:19#super[ f.];); so in English #emph[the Russian, the Turk];, etc., in Attic writers ὁ Ἀθηναῖος, ὁ Συρακόσιος, etc.

#ml("n") <p126n> (#emph[b];) Names of materials known everywhere, the elements and other words denoting classes, even though only a part and not the whole of them is considered, in which case in other languages, as e.g. in English, the article is usually omitted (cf., however, our #emph[to fall into the water, into the fire];, etc.), e.g. #bref("Gen 13:2"); #emph[and Abram was very rich]; #he[בַּמִּקְנֶה בַּכֶּסֶף וּבַזָּהָב]; #emph[in cattle, in silver and in gold];; #bref("Josh 11:9"); #emph[and he burnt their chariots]; #he[בָּאֵשׁ]; #emph[with fire];; cf. #bref("Gen 6:14");, #bref("41:42"); (unless this means, #emph[the chain]; necessarily belonging to the official dress); #bref("Exod 2:3");, #bref("31:4"); (35:32), #bref("Isa 1:22");, etc., and #he[בַּשָּׁמֶן]; #emph[with oil];#footnote[In nearly all the above examples the presence of the article is only indicated by the vowel of the prefix (#he[בַּ];, #he[כַּ‍];, #he[לַ];) and might therefore be merely due to the masoretic punctuation. There is, however, no reason to doubt the correctness of the tradition. The same is true of the examples under #emph[n]; and #emph[o];.]; very commonly in the sacrificial laws, #bref("Exod 29:2");, #origpage("407") <orig-407>;etc., and also #bref("Deut 33:24");, #bref("2 Sam 1:21");, #bref("Isa 1:6");, #bref("Ps 23:5");, etc. Similarly the article is used with terms of measurement, as #he[הָֽאֵפָה]; #bref("Exod 16:36");, etc.: #he[הַחֹמֶר]; and #he[הַבַּת]; #bref("Ezek 45:11");; #he[הָעֹ֫מֶר]; #bref("Exod 16:22");; #he[בַּחֶ֫בֶל]; #bref("2 Sam 8:2");.

(#emph[c];) The expression of abstract ideas of every kind, since they are likewise used to represent whole classes of attributes or states, physical or moral defects, etc.; e.g. #bref("Prov 25:5"); (#he[בַּצֶּ֫דֶק];); #bref("Gen 19:11"); #emph[and they smote the men];... #he[בַּסַּנְוֵרִים]; #emph[with blindness];; #bref("Amos 4:9");, etc.; but in #he[הַח֫שֶׁךְ]; #bref("Isa 60:2"); the article is no doubt due to dittography of the #he[ה];, and the parallel #he[וַֽעֲרָפֶל]; has no article.

#ml("o") <p126o> (#emph[d];) #emph[Comparisons];, since the object compared is treated not (as usually in English) individually but as a general term, e.g. #bref("Isa 1:18"); #emph[white]; #he[כַּשֶּׁ֫לֶג]; #emph[as snow];, #he[כַּצֶּ֫מֶר]; #emph[as wool];; #emph[red]; #he[כַּתּוֹלָע]; #emph[like crimson];; #bref("Isa 34:4"); #emph[and the heavens shall be rolled together]; #he[כַּסֵּפֶר]; #emph[as a scroll];; cf. #bref("Num 11:12");, #bref("Judg 8:18");, #bref("16:9"); #emph[as]; #he[פְּתִיל־הַנְּעֹרֶת]; #emph[a string of tow is broken];; #bref("1 Sam 26:20");, #bref("1 Kgs 14:15");, #bref("Isa 10:14");, #bref("24:20");, #bref("27:10");, #bref("29:8");, #bref("53:6");, #bref("Nah 3:15");, #bref("Ps 33:7");, #bref("49:15");; cf. also such examples as #bref("Gen 19:28");, #bref("Judg 14:6");, where the object compared is determined by a determinate genitive which follows (according to #xref(<p127a>)[§ 127#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];).

#ml("p") <p126p> Examples of indeterminate comparisons are rare, and perhaps due only to the Masora,—so at least in the case of singulars, while in such plurals as those in #bref("Gen 42:30");, #bref("1 Kgs 10:27");, #bref("Joel 2:4");, 7, the omission of the article may be explained by the ordinary rules. On the other hand, the article is regularly omitted when the object compared is already defined by means of an attribute (or relative clause, #bref("Jer 23:9");, #bref("Ps 17:12");), e.g. #bref("Isa 16:2"); #he[כְּעוֹף נוֹדֵד קֵן מְשֻׁלָּח]; #emph[as wandering birds];, (as) #emph[a scattered nest]; (but cf. 10:14 #he[כַּקֵּן];); 14:19, 29:5 #he[כְּמֹץ עֹבֵר]; (but #bref("Ps 1:4"); #he[כַּמֹּץ];); #bref("Jer 2:30");, #bref("Prov 27:8");, #bref("Job 29:25");, #bref("30:14");.—In comparisons with persons also the Masora seems to avoid the use of the article, as in #he[כְּגִבּוֹר]; #bref("Job 16:14"); and seven other places (#he[כַּגִּבּוֹר]; only in #bref("Isa 42:13");), #he[כְּאָב]; #bref("Job 31:18");, #he[כְּגֶ֫בֶר]; #bref("Job 38:3");, #bref("40:7");.

#ml("q") <p126q> #strong[4.]; Peculiar to Hebrew#footnote[Cf., however, analogous examples in biblical Aramaic in Kautzsch’s #emph[Gramm. des Bibl. Aram.];, § 79 #emph[f];, e.g. #bref("Dan 2:14");, #bref("3:2");, etc.]; is the employment of the article to denote a single person or thing (primarily one which is as yet unknown, and therefore not capable of being defined) as being present to the mind under given circumstances. In such cases in English the indefinite article is mostly used.

#ml("r") <p126r> Thus #bref("Amos 5:19"); #emph[as if a man did flee from a lion]; (#he[הָֽאֲרִי];, i.e. the particular lion pursuing him at the time), #emph[and a bear]; (#he[הַדֹּב];) #emph[met him];, etc., cf. 3:12, #bref("1 Kgs 20:36"); (John 10:12); also #bref("Gen 8:7"); f., 14:13 (#he[הַפָּלִיט];, i.e. #emph[one that had escaped];, the particular one who came just then; so also #bref("Ezek 24:26");, #bref("33:21");; cf. #bref("2 Sam 15:13");); #bref("Gen 15:1");, 11, 18:7 #emph[the servant];, who is regarded as being constantly at hand and awaiting his commands; cf. #bref("2 Sam 17:17"); (but #he[הַנַּ֫עַר]; #bref("Num 11:27"); is used like #he[הַפָּלִיט]; above); #bref("Gen 19:30");, unless #he[בַּמְּעָרָה]; means in #emph[the]; well\-known #emph[cave];; #he[בַּמָּקוֹם]; #bref("Gen 28:11");, according to Dillmann, upon #emph[the]; place suitable for passing the night, or the right place, but it may possibly also refer to #emph[the]; sanctuary of Bethel afterwards so sacred and celebrated; #bref("Gen 42:23");, #bref("46:2");, #bref("50:26");, #bref("Exod 2:15");, #bref("3:2");, #bref("4:20");, #bref("21:20"); (#bref("2 Sam 23:21");), #bref("Lev 23:42");, #bref("24:10"); (Samaritan #he[יִשְׂרְאֵלִי]; without the article); #bref("Num 17:11");, #bref("21:6");, 9, 25:6, #bref("Deut 19:5");, #bref("Josh 2:15");, #bref("Judg 4:18");, #bref("8:25");, #bref("13:19");, #bref("16:19");, #bref("19:29");, #bref("20:16");, #bref("1 Sam 17:34");, #bref("19:13");, #bref("21:10");, #bref("2 Sam 17:17");, #bref("1 Kgs 6:8");, #bref("13:14"); (? most #origpage("408") <orig-408>;probably a particular tree is meant); 19:9, #bref("Isa 7:14"); (#he[הָֽעַלְמָה];, i.e. #emph[the]; particular #emph[maiden];, through whom the prophet’s announcement shall be fulfilled; we should say #emph[a maiden]; \[cf. Driver on #bref("1 Sam 1:4");, #bref("6:8");, #bref("19:13");\]; #bref("Job 9:31");.

#ml("s") <p126s> So always #emph[to write in the book]; (or #emph[on the scroll];, #bref("Num 5:23");, #bref("Jer 32:10");), i.e. not in #emph[the]; book already in use, but in the book which is to be devoted to that purpose, equivalent to #emph[in a book, on a scroll];, #bref("Exod 17:14");, #bref("1 Sam 10:25");, #bref("Job 19:23");. Especially instructive for this use of the article is the phrase #he[וַיְהִי הַיּוֹם];, which does not simply refer back to the previous narrative in the sense of #emph[the same day];, but is used exactly like our #emph[one day]; (properly meaning on the particular day when it happened, i.e. #emph[on a certain day];), #bref("1 Sam 1:4");, #bref("14:1");, #bref("2 Kgs 4:8");, #bref("11:18");, #bref("Job 1:6");, 13. In #bref("Gen 39:11"); even #he[כְּהַיּוֹם הַזֶּה];.

#ml("t") <p126t> The article is sometimes used in this way before collectives in the singular, which are not meant to denote (like the examples given under #emph[l];) a whole class, but only that part of it which applies to the given case; thus #he[הָֽעֹרֵב];, #he[הַיּוֹנָה]; #bref("Gen 8:7");, #he[הַצִּרְעָה]; #bref("Exod 23:28");.

#ml("u") <p126u> #strong[5.]; When a substantive is defined by the article, or by a suffix, or by a following genitive determinate in any way (see the examples below), the attribute belonging to it (whether adjective, participle, ordinal, or demonstrative pronoun) necessarily takes the article (see, however, the Rem.), e.g. #bref("Gen 10:12"); #he[הָעִיר הַגְּדֹלָה]; #emph[the great city];; #bref("Deut 3:24"); #he[יָֽדְךָ הַֽחֲזָקָה]; #emph[thy strong hand];. A genitive following the substantive may, according to #xref(<p127a>)[§ 127#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];, be determined either by the article, e.g. #bref("1 Sam 25:25"); #he[אִישׁ הַבְּלִיַּעַל הַזֶּה]; #emph[this worthless man]; (prop. #emph[man of worthlessness];; cf. also such examples as #bref("2 Chr 36:18");, where the article is prefixed only to a second genitive following the noun); or as a proper name, e.g. #bref("Deut 11:7"); #he[מַֽעֲשֵׂה יְהֹוָה הַגָּדֹל]; #emph[the great work of the Lord];; or by a suffix, e.g. #bref("Isa 36:9"); #he[עַבְדֵי אֲדֹנִי הַקְּטַנִּים]; #emph[the least of my master’s servants];.

#ml("v") <p126v> When several attributes (whether connected by #emph[Wāw]; or not) follow a determinate substantive, each of them takes the article, e.g. #bref("Deut 10:17"); #he[הָאֵל הַגָּדֹל הַגִּבֹּר וְהַנּוֹרָא]; #emph[the great God, the mighty, and the terrible];. Cf. also #bref("Exod 3:3");, #bref("Deut 1:19");, in both of which places a demonstrative with the article also follows the adjective.#footnote[The demonstrative used adjectivally is generally placed #emph[after]; the adjective proper; in such cases as #he[עַמְּךָ הַזֶּה הַגָּדוֹל]; #bref("2 Chr 1:10"); the adjective forms a further (fresh) addition to #he[עַמְּךָ הַזֶּה];.];

Rem. 1. The article is, however, not infrequently used also—

#ml("w") <p126w> (#emph[a];) With the attribute alone, when it is added to an originally indefinite substantive as a subsequent limitation; so always with ordinal numbers after #he[יוֹם];,#footnote[Cf. Driver, #emph[Tenses];, 3rd ed., § 209; M. Lambert, #emph[REJ.]; 31, 279 f.—The omission of the article from the substantive is not to be regarded in #emph[this]; instance as an indication of late style, and consequently cannot be put forward as a proof of the late origin of the ‘Priestly Code’ (cf. Dillmann on #bref("Gen 1:31");, Holzinger, #emph[Einl. in d. Hexateuch];, p. 465, and especially Driver in the #emph[Journal of Philology];, xi. 229 f., against Giesebrecht in #emph[ZAW.]; 1881, p. 265 f.). On the other hand, the common omission of the article from the substantive before a determinate adjective (e.g. #he[כְּנֵ֫סֶת הַגְּדוֹלָה]; #emph[the great synagogue];, in the Mishna; cf. Segal, #emph[Mišnaic Hebrew];, p. 19 ff.) is certainly a later idiom.]; e.g. #bref("Gen 1:31"); (cf. 2:3, #bref("Exod 20:10");, etc.) #he[יוֹם הַשִּׁשִּׁי]; #emph[the sixth day]; (prop. #emph[a day]; namely #origpage("409") <orig-409>;#emph[the sixth];; but #he[יוֹם שֵׁנִי]; #emph[a second day];, #bref("Gen 1:8");); #bref("Exod 12:15"); #he[מִיּוֹם הָֽרִאשֹׁן]; #emph[from the first day onward]; (not before #bref("Dan 10:12"); and #bref("Neh 8:18"); is #he[מִן־הַיּוֹם הָֽרִאשׁוֹן]; used instead of it). On the other hand, the article is #emph[always]; found after #he[בְּ];, hence #he[בַּיּוֹם הַשִּׁשִּׁי];, etc., although it is possible that the original reading in these cases was #he[בְּיוֹם];, and that the article is only due to the Masora. In #bref("Judg 6:25"); the text is evidently corrupt (see verse 26).—Especially also in certain frequently recurring combinations as in particularizing the gates in #bref("Jer 38:14");, #bref("Ezek 9:2");, etc., #bref("Zech 14:10");, and courts in #bref("1 Kgs 7:8");, 12, etc., #bref("Ezek 40:28");; and very often when the attribute consists of a participle, e.g. #bref("Deut 2:23");, #bref("Judg 21:19");, #bref("1 Sam 25:10");, #bref("Jer 27:3");, #bref("46:16"); #he[חֶ֫רֶב הַיּוֹנָה]; #emph[the sword which oppresses]; (#emph[?];); #bref("Ezek 14:22");, #bref("Zech 11:2"); #emph[K#super[e];th.]; (#emph[the impenetrable forest?];) #bref("Prov 26:18");, #bref("Ps 119:21");.

#ml("x") <p126x> Of the other examples, #bref("Gen 21:29"); (where, however, the Samaritan reads #he[הכבשות];), 41:26 (but cf. verse 4), #bref("Num 11:25");, #bref("Judg 16:27");, #bref("1 Sam 17:17"); may at any rate be explained on the ground that the preceding cardinal number is equivalent to a determinant; in #bref("Gen 1:21");, #bref("28:9");, 10, etc., the substantive is already determined by #he[כָּל־];, and in #bref("1 Sam 14:29"); (#he[דְּבַשׁ];) by #he[מְעַט];.—In #bref("1 Sam 12:23");, #bref("2 Sam 12:4");, #bref("Isa 7:20"); (where, however, #he[הַשְּׂכִירָה]; might also be understood as a subsequent explanation of #he[בְּתַ֫עַר];) and #bref("Neh 9:35");, the omission of the article after the preposition is certainly due merely to the Masora. In #bref("1 Sam 16:23"); (unless #he[רוּחַ אֱלֹהִים]; is to be read twice), #bref("Zech 4:7"); (where however #he[אַתָּ הָהָר]; is probably meant), #bref("Ps 104:18"); (where a #he[ה]; precedes #he[הָרִים];, hence probably a case of haplography), the omission of the article before #he[א];, #he[ר]; (?) and #he[ה]; may be due to a regard for euphony (see #emph[z]; below). On the other hand, in #bref("1 Sam 6:18"); (read #he[הָאֶ֫בֶן הַגְּ׳];), 17:12 (#he[הַזֶּה]; is a later addition), 19:22 (cf. the LXX), #bref("Jer 17:2");, #bref("32:14");, #bref("40:3"); #emph[K#super[e];th.];, #bref("Ezek 2:3"); (read #he[גּוֹי]; or omit #he[גּוֹיִם]; with Cornill), #bref("Mic 7:11");, #bref("Ps 62:4");, either the text is corrupt, or the expression incorrect. But in #bref("2 Kgs 20:13");, #bref("Jer 6:20");, #bref("Song 7:10"); acc. to D. H. Müller (#emph[Anzeiger der Wiener Akad.];, phil\-hist. Kl. 1902, no. x) #he[הַטּוֹב]; is the genitive of a substantive, #emph[aromatic oil, sweet cane]; (in #bref("Jer 6:20"); read #he[וּקְנֵה];), #emph[like spiced wine];. In #bref("Isa 39:2"); read #he[שֶׁ֫מֶן הַטּוֹב]; and in #bref("Ps 133:2"); #he[כְּשֶׁ֫מֶן חַטּ׳];.

#ml("y") <p126y> (#emph[b];) No article with the attribute, while the substantive is determined either by the article, or a suffix, or a following genitive. Thus the article is sometimes omitted with demonstratives, since they are already to a certain extent determined by their meaning (cf. also the #emph[Mêšaʿ]; inscription, l. 3, #he[הבמת זאת]; #emph[this high place];); as with #he[הוּא]; #bref("Gen 19:33"); (evidently for euphony, and so probably often); 30:16, 32:23, #bref("1 Sam 19:10");; with #he[הִיא]; #bref("Gen 38:21");; with #he[זוּ]; #bref("Ps 12:8"); (according to the Masora #he[זוּ]; is a relative pronoun here, as always elsewhere); with #he[אֵ֫לֶּה]; #bref("1 Sam 2:23");, according to the present corrupt text (the original reading #he[כָּל־עַם יהוה]; became #he[כָּל־עַם אֱלֹהִים];, and #he[אֱלֹהִים]; was then corrupted to #he[אֵלֶּה];); so, almost without exception, when the substantive is determined only by a suffix, e.g. #bref("Josh 2:20");, #bref("Judg 6:14");, #bref("1 Kgs 10:8");, #bref("2 Kgs 1:2"); and 8:8 f., where #he[חלי];, as in #bref("Jer 10:19");, has arisen by contraction from #he[חָלְיִי];, or we should simply read #he[חָלְיִ]; (in all these passages with #he[זֶה];); #bref("Gen 24:8"); (with #he[זֹאת];); #bref("Exod 10:1");, #bref("1 Kgs 22:23");, #bref("Jer 31:21"); (with #he[אֵ֫לֶּה];).

The article is sometimes omitted also with the attributes referring to proper names,#footnote[Cf. Nöldeke, #emph[Beiträge zur semit. Sprachwiss.];, p. 48, n. 1.]; as #he[צִידוֹן רַבָּה]; #bref("Josh 11:8");, #bref("19:28");, #he[חֲמָת רַבָּה]; #bref("Amos 6:2");. Other examples are #bref("Josh 16:3");, 5, 18:13, #bref("1 Kgs 9:17"); (but in #bref("1 Chr 7:24");, #bref("2 Chr 8:5"); with the article). In #bref("Gen 7:11");, etc., #he[תְּהוֹם רַבָּה]; is also a case of this kind, #he[תְּהוֹם]; being used (almost always without the article) as a sort of proper name; cf. also #he[אֵל עֶלְיוֹן]; #emph[the most high]; #origpage("410") <orig-410>;#emph[God]; and #he[אֱלֹהִים חַי]; #emph[the living God];. In #bref("Judg 1:15"); #he[גֻּלֹּת עִלִּית]; and #he[גּ׳ תַּחְתִּית]; are strange; #bref("Josh 15:19"); has #he[גּ׳ עִלִּיּוֹת]; and #he[גּ׳ תַּחְתִּיּוֹת];.

#ml("z") <p126z> Of the remaining examples #bref("Isa 11:9"); explains itself; the direct connection of the attribute with its substantive is broken by the insertion of #he[לַיָּם];. In #bref("Ezek 34:12");, #bref("Hag 1:4"); (as Wellhausen says, a good instance of a Hebrew adjective in the stative form \= #he[וְהֵם סְפוּנִים];), #bref("Ps 143:10");, #bref("Song 6:12"); (?) the substantive is also (see above) determined by a suffix, and consequently the attribute is less closely attached; the same applies to #bref("Gen 37:2");, #bref("42:19");, #bref("43:14");, #bref("Ps 18:18");, except that in these passages the omission of the article before #he[ר];, #he[א];, #he[ע]; may at the same time be due to considerations of euphony (as also in #bref("Josh 16:1"); before #he[ע];, #bref("Num 14:37"); before #he[ר];, 28:4, #bref("Ezek 10:9"); before #he[א];, 21:19 before #he[ח];).#footnote[The same reason no doubt also favoured the omission of the article before #he[הוּא]; and #he[אֵ֫לֶּה];, see above, under #emph[y];. Also in #bref("Isa 23:7"); (#emph[is this your joyous];...?) the article is omitted before #he[עַלִּיזָה]; probably only for euphony.]; In #bref("1 Sam 13:17"); f. (#he[אֶחָד];) and #bref("2 Kgs 25:16"); (#he[שְׁנַ֫יִם]; after a determinate substantive), the attribute again, being a numeral, is determinate in itself (see above, #emph[x];); in #bref("Isa 65:2"); the #he[לֹא]; prevents the use of the article; finally, in #bref("2 Chr 26:15"); #he[בְּחִצִּים]; and #he[בַּֽאֲבָנִים]; are to be read, as in #bref("Jer 2:21"); #he[גֶּ֫פֶן]; for #he[הַגֶּ֫פֶן];, in 22:26 #he[אֶ֫רֶץ]; for #he[הָאָ׳];; in #bref("2 Sam 6:3"); omit #he[חֲדָשָׁה];, and in #bref("Ezek 39:27"); omit #he[רַבִּים];. Without any apparent reason the article is omitted in #bref("Dan 8:13"); and 11:31.

#ml("aa") <p126aa> 2. When, as in #bref("Mic 7:12"); (#he[יוֹם הוּא]; #emph[in that day?];), the article is omitted from both substantive and demonstrative, and in #bref("Ezra 3:12");, the demonstrative even precedes (#he[זֶה הַבַּ֫יִת];\=#he[הַבַּ֫יִת הַזֶּה];), this is obviously due in both cases to a radical corruption of the text (not only in the words quoted). In #bref("Josh 9:12"); #he[לַחְמֵ֫נוּ]; is either in apposition to the independent demonstrative #he[זֶה]; (\= #emph[this our bread];, etc.), as in verse 13 #he[נֹאדוֹת]; is to #he[אֵ֫לֶּה];, or they are complete sentences, #emph[this is our bread];, etc. So also in #bref("Exod 32:1"); #he[משֶׁה]; (\= #emph[that]; \[#emph[iste];\] #emph[Moses];, etc.), and in #bref("Ps 48:15"); #he[אֱלֹהִים]; are to be taken in apposition to #he[זֶה];. On #bref("Ps 68:8"); and #bref("Isa 23:13"); cf. #xref(<p136d>)[§ 136#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];.

#sec("127", [The Noun determined by a following Determinate Genitive], note: []) <s127>

#align(center)[Brockelmann, #emph[Grundriss];, i. 475.];

#ml("a") <p127a> When a genitive, determined in any way, follows a #emph[nomen regens];, it also determines the #emph[nomen regens];, which, according to #xref(<p89a>)[§ 89#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];, is always in the construct state. Moreover, every pronominal suffix attached to a substantive is, according to #xref(<p33c>)[§ 33#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];, to be considered as a genitive determinate by nature. An independent genitive may be determinate—

(#emph[a];) By its character as a #emph[proper name]; (according to #xref(<p125a>)[§ 125#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];), e.g. #he[דְּבַר יְהֹוָה]; #emph[the word of the Lord];.

(#emph[b];) By having the article, e.g. #he[אִישׁ הַמִּלְחָמָה]; (prop. the man of the war) #emph[the soldier]; (but #he[אִישׁ מִלְחָמָה]; #bref("Josh 17:1");, #emph[a soldier];); #he[אַנְשֵׁי הַמִּלְחָמָה]; #origpage("411") <orig-411>;#bref("Num 31:49");, #emph[the soldiers];; #he[דְּבַר הַנָּבִיא]; #emph[the word of the prophet];, #bref("Jer 28:9"); (but e.g., on the other hand, #he[מִצְוַת אֲנָשִׁים מְלֻמָּדָה]; #emph[a commandment of men which hath been taught];, #bref("Isa 29:13");; #he[דְּבַר־שָׁ֑קֶר]; #emph[word of falsehood];, #bref("Prov 29:12");).

(#emph[c];) By the addition of a pronominal suffix (see above), e.g. #he[בֵּֽית־אָבִי]; #emph[my father’s house];.

(#emph[d];) By construction with another genitive determined in some way, e.g. #bref("Gen 3:2"); #he[מִפְּרִי עֵֽץ־הַגָּן]; #emph[of the fruit of the trees of the garden];. Thus in #bref("Isa 10:12"); four, and in 21:17 even five, members of a series are determined by a concluding determinate genitive.

#ml("b") <p127b> Rem. 1. The above explains also the various meanings of #he[כֹּל]; (prop. a substantive in the sense of #emph[aggregate, whole];), according as it is followed by a determinate or indeterminate genitive. In the former case #he[כֹּל]; has the meaning of #emph[the entirety];, i.e. #emph[all, the whole]; (like the French #emph[tous les hommes, toute la ville];), e.g. #he[כָּל־הָאָרֶץ]; #emph[the whole]; (prop. the entirety of the) #emph[earth];, #he[כָּל־הָֽאָדָם]; #emph[all men];;#footnote[#he[הָֽאָדָם]; being a collective, cf. #he[כָּל־הָאִישׁ]; #bref("2 Sam 15:2");, #emph[all men];, #he[כָּל־הַבֵּן]; #bref("Exod 1:22"); #emph[all sons];, #he[כָּל־הַבַּת]; #emph[all daughters];; in itself #he[כָּל־הָֽאָדָם]; could also mean #emph[the whole man];.]; #bref("Exod 18:22");, #bref("Num 15:13");, #bref("Jer 4:29");, and cases like #bref("Num 4:23");, 47, 21:8 where #he[כָּל]; is followed by a singular participle with the article. On the other hand, before an indeterminate genitive #he[כֹּל]; is used in the more indefinite (individualizing) sense of #emph[of all kinds, any]; (cf. #emph[tout homme, à tout prix];), or distributively #emph[each, every];, e.g. #he[כָּל־עֵץ]; #emph[every]; (kind of) #emph[tree];, #bref("Gen 2:9");; cf. 4:22, 24:10, #bref("1 Chr 29:2");; #he[כָּל־דָּבָר]; #emph[any thing];, #bref("Judg 19:19");; #he[בְּכָל־יוֹם]; #emph[every day, every time];, #bref("Ps 7:12");.

#ml("c") <p127c> It is, however, to be observed—

(#emph[a];) That the article may in this case also (see #xref(<p126h>)[§ 126#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];) be omitted in poetic style, although the substantive is to be regarded as determinate, e.g. #he[כָּל־שֻׁלְחָנוֹת]; #emph[all]; (the) #emph[tables];, #bref("Isa 28:8");.

(#emph[b];) That the meaning #emph[every]; is frequent even before singulars used collectively; afterwards the idea of #emph[quisque]; passes naturally into that of #emph[totality];, e.g. #he[כָּל־חַי]; #emph[each living thing];, i.e. #emph[every]; (not #emph[every kind of];) #emph[living thing];; #he[כָּל־בָּשָׂר]; #emph[all flesh];, i.e. #emph[all men]; or #emph[all living creatures]; (with the article only in #bref("Gen 7:15"); before a relative clause, and in #bref("Isa 40:6");); sometimes also #he[כָּל־עֵץ]; #emph[all trees];, #he[כָּל־עוֹף]; #emph[all birds];; finally—

(#emph[c];) That before the names of members of the human body, #he[כָּל־]; frequently (as being determinate in itself) denotes the entirety, e.g. #bref("Isa 1:5"); #emph[the whole head, the whole heart]; (the sense required by the context, not #emph[every head];, etc., which the expression #emph[in itself]; might also mean); 9:11, #bref("2 Kgs 23:3");, #bref("Ezek 29:7"); #emph[all]; (i.e. #emph[the whole of];) #emph[their shoulders];... #emph[all]; (#emph[the whole of];) #emph[their loins];; 36:5.—On #he[כֹּל]; with a suffix when it follows a noun in apposition (e.g. #bref("Isa 9:8"); #he[הָעָם כֻּלּוֹ]; #emph[the people, all of it];, i.e. #emph[the whole nation];, more emphatic than #he[כָּל־הָעָם];, cf. Driver on #bref("2 Sam 2:9");), as well as when it follows absolutely in the genitive (\= #emph[all men, every one];, e.g. #bref("Gen 16:12");),#footnote[In #bref("Ezra 10:17"); instead of #he[בַּכֹּל אֲנָשִׁים]; read simply #he[בְּכָל־הָֽאֲנָשִׁים];.]; see the Lexicon, pp. 481#super[b];, 482#super[b];.

#ml("d") <p127d> 2. Gentilic names (or patronymics), derived from compound proper names (consisting of a nomen regens and genitive), are determined by inserting the article before the second part of the compound (since it contains the original #origpage("412") <orig-412>;genitive), e.g. #he[בֶּן־יְמִינִי]; (see #xref(<p86h>)[§ 86#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];) #emph[a Benjamite];, #he[בֶּן־הַיְמִינִי]; #bref("Judg 3:15");, etc., #emph[the Benjamite];; #he[בֵּֽית־הַלַּחְמִי]; #emph[the Bethlehemite];, #bref("1 Sam 16:1");, etc. (cf., however, #bref("1 Chr 27:12"); #emph[Q#super[e];rê]; #he[לַבֵּן יְמִינִי];); #he[בֵּֽית־הַשִּׁמְשִׁי]; #emph[the Beth\-shemite];, #bref("1 Sam 6:14");; #he[אֲבִי הָֽעֶזְרִי]; #emph[the Abiezrite];, #bref("Judg 6:11");, etc., cf. #bref("1 Kgs 16:34");.

#ml("e") <p127e> 3. In a few instances the nomen regens appears to be used indefinitely notwithstanding a following determinate genitive; it is not so, however, in #bref("Gen 16:7");, where the reference is to a well\-known fountain; 21:28, where in the original context there must have been some reason for #emph[the seven ewe lambs of the flock];; #bref("2 Sam 12:30"); #emph[the spoil found in the city];; but it often is so before a proper name, as in #bref("Exod 10:9"); #he[חַג יְהֹוָה]; #emph[a feast of the Lord]; (unless it is #emph[the]; spring festival), #bref("Deut 7:25");, and frequently #he[תּֽוֹעֲבַת יְהֹוָה]; #emph[an abomination unto the Lord];; cf. also #bref("Gen 46:34");, #bref("Deut 22:19"); #emph[a virgin of Israel];; #bref("1 Sam 4:12"); #emph[a man of Benjamin];; #bref("Prov 25:1");, #bref("Song 2:1");, #bref("3:9");; similarly before appellatives with the article (or before a genitive determined by a suffix, as in #bref("Lev 14:34");), #bref("1 Sam 20:20"); #emph[three arrows];; #bref("2 Sam 23:11"); #he[חֶלְקַת הַשָּׂדֶה]; #emph[a plot of the ground]; (but see #bref("Gen 33:19");, #bref("Josh 24:32");); #bref("Judg 13:6");, #bref("Jer 13:4");, #bref("41:16");, #bref("Song 1:11");, 13 f., 5:13, 7:3, 8:2. On the other hand, #he[שִׁיר הַמַּֽעֲלוֹת]; in the titles of Psalms 120 to 134 (except 121:1, #he[שִׁיר לַמַּֽעֲלוֹת];) was most probably originally the title of a collection, in the sense of #emph[‘the pilgrimage\-songs’]; (according to #xref(<p124r>)[§ 124#h(0.18em)#emph[r];];), and was subsequently added to these Psalms severally.—In #bref("Exod 20:24"); #he[בְּכָל־הַמָּקוֹם]; #emph[in all the place];, sc. of the sanctuary, is a dogmatic correction of #he[בְּכָל־מָקוֹם];, #emph[in every place];, to avoid the difficulty that several holy\-places are here authorized, instead of the one central sanctuary. In #bref("Gen 20:13"); also #he[כָּל־הַמָּקוֹם]; (unless it means #emph[in the whole place];) is remarkable, since elsewhere #emph[every place]; is always (8 times) #he[כָּל־מָקוֹם];.

#ml("f") <p127f> 4. The deviations mentioned under #emph[e];, from a fundamental rule of syntax, are in some cases open to suspicion on textual grounds, but much more doubtful are the instances in which the article is found before a noun already determined in some other way, as—

(#emph[a];) Before a noun which appears to be determined by a following independent determinate genitive. The least questionable are the instances in which the genitive is a #emph[proper name];, since these may be elliptical forms of expression like the apparent construction of proper names with a genitive, noticed in #xref(<p125h>)[§ 125#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];, e.g. #bref("Num 21:14"); #he[הַנְּחָלִים אַרְנוֹן]; #emph[the valleys];, namely the valleys #emph[of Arnon];; #bref("2 Kgs 23:17"); #he[הַמִּזְבַּח בֵּית־אֵל]; #emph[the altar];, namely the altar of #emph[Bethel]; (i.e. with the suppression of the real nomen regens, #he[מִזְבַּח]; without the article; by the pointing #he[הַמִּזְבַּח]; the Masora evidently intends to allow the choice either of reading #he[הַמִּזְבֵּחַ]; or correcting it to #he[מִזְבַּח];); #he[הָאֵל בֵּית־אֵל]; #emph[the God of Beth\-el];#footnote[According to Philippi (#emph[st. constr.];, p. 38) #he[בית־אל]; is rather a case of ‘sub\-position’ in the accusative, as also #he[הַדֶּ֫רֶךְ חֶתְלוֹן]; #bref("Ezek 47:15"); (for which, however, in 48:1 there is the correct reading #he[דֶּ֫רֶךְ חֶתְלוֹן];) #emph[by the way to Hethlon];; and in fact, #bref("Ezek 47:15"); may without difficulty be explained in this way; so #he[שֵׁשׁ]; #bref("Exod 39:27"); as an accusative of the material.]; (equivalent to #he[הָאֵל אֵל בּ׳];), #bref("Gen 31:13"); (the LXX read #he[הָאֵל הַנִּרְאֶה אֵלֶיךָ בַּמָּקוֹם]; #emph[the God who appeared to thee in the]; holy #emph[place];); #he[הַמֶּלֶךְ אַשּׁוּר]; #emph[the king of Assyria];, #bref("Isa 36:16"); (probably a scribal error due to verse 13; it does not occur in the parallel passage, #bref("2 Kgs 18:31");), cf. #bref("Josh 13:5");, #bref("2 Kgs 25:11");, #bref("Jer 38:6");, #bref("Ezek 47:15");; in the vocative, #bref("Jer 48:32");, #bref("Lam 2:13");. On the other hand, #he[שָׂרָה אִמּוֹ]; #bref("Gen 24:67"); is no doubt #origpage("413") <orig-413>;only a subsequent insertion; so also #he[יִשְׂרָאֵל]; #bref("Josh 8:33");b (cf. LXX), #bref("2 Sam 20:23");, #bref("2 Kgs 7:13");, #he[הַמֶּ֫לֶךְ]; #bref("1 Sam 26:22"); after #he[הַחֲנִית]; (simplified by the Masora to #he[חֲנִית]; #emph[Q#super[e];rê];); #he[עֲלִיַּת אָחָז]; #bref("2 Kgs 23:12");, #he[אַשּׁוּר]; #bref("Isa 36:8"); (cf. #bref("2 Kgs 18:23");), #he[הַקֹּדֶשׁ]; #bref("Ezek 46:19"); (unless the article with #he[לשכות]; is to be omitted), also #he[הַתָּמִיד]; #bref("Dan 8:13");, and #he[עֹדֵד הַנָּבִיא]; #bref("2 Chr 15:8");. In #bref("Exod 9:18"); read with the Samaritan #he[לְמִיּוֹם];; in #bref("2 Sam 19:25"); #he[לֶ֫כֶת]; might possibly be taken in apposition to #he[לְמִן הַיּוֹם];; in #bref("2 Kgs 10:1"); restore #he[אֶת־בְּנֵי];, with the LXX and Lucian, before #he[אַחְאָב];; in #bref("2 Kgs 25:19"); omit the article, as in #bref("Jer 52:25");, before #he[סֹפֵר];.

#ml("g") <p127g> A similar ellipse must also be assumed in #bref("2 Kgs 23:17"); #emph[the sepulchre is]; the sepulchre #emph[of the man of God]; (but most probably #he[קֶבֶר]; has dropped out after #he[הַקֶּבֶר];) and #bref("Ps 123:4"); (cf., however, the LXX, and observe that in the parallel member the genitive is paraphrased by #he[לְ];).—In #bref("Josh 3:14"); #he[הַבְּרִית]; (verse 17 #he[בְּרִית יהוה];) has been added to the original #he[הָֽאָרוֹן]; by a redactor; cf. similar syntactically impossible additions in verse 11 (also in #bref("1 Sam 4:3");, etc., where the LXX still had simply #he[אֲרוֹן יהוה];); in #he[הַיְתַד]; #bref("Judg 16:14"); the Masora evidently combines two different readings #he[הַיָּתֵד]; and #he[יְתַד הָאֶרֶג];; and similarly in #bref("Jer 25:26"); (where #he[הָאָ֫רֶץ]; was only subsequently introduced into the text), the two readings #he[הַמַּמְלָכוֹת]; and #he[מַמְלְכוֹת הָא׳]; are combined.—In #bref("Josh 8:11");, #bref("1 Kgs 14:24");, #bref("Jer 31:40");, #bref("Ezek 45:16"); the article, being usual after #he[כָּל־];, has been mechanically added, and so also in #bref("2 Chr 8:16"); after #he[עַד־];; in #bref("2 Kgs 9:4"); the second #he[הַנַּ֫עַר]; (instead of #he[נַ֫עַר];) is occasioned by the first; in #bref("Ezek 7:7"); #he[מְהוּמָה]; belongs as a nominative to what follows; in #bref("Ezek 8:29"); the meaning perhaps is #emph[in the chambers, in the house of the Lord];, or the article is to be omitted; in #bref("1 Chr 15:27"); the text is manifestly corrupt.

#ml("h") <p127h> Of another kind are the instances in which a determinate noun is followed by a definition of the material #emph[in apposition]; (hence, not in the genitive; cf. § 131), e.g. #bref("Zech 4:10"); #he[הָאֶ֫בֶן הַבְּדִיל]; #emph[the weight, the lead];, i.e. #emph[the leaden weight];; #bref("Exod 39:17");, #bref("2 Kgs 16:14"); (#he[הַנְּח֫שֶׁת];, both here and in verse 17, is probably only a later addition, while #he[המסגרות המכנות]; in verse 17 has arisen from a confusion of two readings, #he[מסגרות המכנות]; and #he[המסגרות מֵהמכנות];). In #bref("Jer 32:12"); also #he[הַמִּקְנָה]; (unless the article is simply to be omitted) is in apposition to #he[הַסֵּפֶר];.

#ml("i") <p127i> (#emph[b];) Before a noun with a suffix (which likewise represents a determinate genitive; see above, at the beginning of this section). This does not apply to cases in which a #emph[verbal]; (i.e. #emph[accusative];) suffix is affixed to a participle which has the article, e.g. #he[הַמַּכֵּ֫הוּ]; #bref("Isa 9:12");, #emph[the one smiting him];; in #bref("Deut 8:15");, #bref("13:6"); also #he[ךָ]; is a verbal suffix, but hardly the #he[וֹ]; in #he[הָֽעֹשׂוֹ]; for #he[הָֽעֹשֵׂ֫הוּ]; #bref("Job 40:19");, nor the #he[־ָהּ]; in #he[הַיֹּֽלְדָהּ]; #bref("Dan 11:6");; #xref(<p116g>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];. For #he[הָֽעֶרְכְּךָ]; #bref("Lev 27:23");, read #he[עֶרְכְּךָ]; as in verses 2, 3, 5, 7, 13, etc., twelve times (but cf. also the note on #xref(<p128d>)[§ 128#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];).—Of the remaining examples #he[כַּגְּבִרְתָּהּ]; #bref("Isa 24:2"); (probably an intentional alliteration with the eleven other words beginning with #he[כַּ‍];), #he[לַמַּֽעֲנֵ֫הוּ]; #bref("Prov 16:4");, and #he[בֶּֽעָרֵ֫ינוּ]; (so Baer, following the best authorities) #bref("Ezra 10:14");, rest only on the authority of the Masoretes, not of the authors. So also in #he[הָאָֽהֳלִי]; #bref("Josh 7:21");, #he[הַֽחֶצְיוֹ]; #bref("Josh 8:33"); (previously #he[חֶצְיוֹ];), #he[הֶהָֽרוֹתֶ֫יהָ]; #bref("2 Kgs 15:16"); (dittography of the #he[ה];), the article is simply to be omitted as syntactically impossible; the #he[ו]; of #he[הַדָּבְרוֹ]; #bref("Mic 2:12"); is the copula belonging to the next word. #origpage("414") <orig-414>;

#sec("128", [The Indication of the Genitive Relation by means of the Construct State], note: []) <s128>

#align(center)[Cf. especially Philippi’s work cited at the head of #xref(<s89>)[§ 89];.];

#ml("a") <p128a> #strong[1.]; The genitive relation is regularly expressed (see #xref(<s89>)[§ 89];) by the close connection of the #emph[nomen regens]; (in the construct state) with the #emph[nomen rectum]; (in the genitive). Since only #emph[one]; nomen regens can be immediately connected with a nomen rectum, it follows that the same genitive cannot depend on two or more #emph[co\-ordinate]; nouns, but a second (sometimes even a third, etc.) regens must be added with a suffix referring to the nomen rectum, e.g. #he[בְּנֵי דָוִד וּבְנֹתָיו]; #emph[the sons of David and his daughters]; (not #he[בְּנֵי וּבְנוֹת דָּוִד];); cf. #bref("1 Kgs 8:28");.#footnote[Very rare, and only possible in very rapid utterance, are such exceptions as #bref("Ezek 31:16"); (#he[מִבְחַר וְטֽוֹב־לְבָנוֹן];); #bref("Prov 16:11");.—In #bref("Isa 11:2"); #emph[the spirit of knowledge and of the fear of the Lord];, #he[דַּעַ֫ת]; may at any rate also be taken as an absolute genitive, so also #he[סֵ֫פֶר]; #bref("Dan 1:4");.]; The language also prefers to avoid a series of several co\-ordinate#footnote[In #bref("Ps 114:1"); a second genitive is added even without the copula, but the parallelism of the members renders any misunderstanding impossible.]; genitives depending upon one and the same nomen regens (such as occur in #bref("Gen 14:19");, #bref("Num 20:5");, #bref("31:54"); \[#bref("1 Chr 13:1");\], #bref("1 Sam 23:7");, #bref("2 Sam 19:6");, #bref("Isa 22:5");, #bref("Ps 5:7");, #bref("8:3");),#footnote[In almost all these instances the two (or three) genitives form one closely connected whole, as #emph[heaven]; and #emph[earth, sons]; and #emph[daughters];.]; and rather tends to repeat the nomen regens, e.g. #bref("Gen 24:3"); #he[אֱלֹהֵי הַשָּׁמַ֫יִם וֵֽאלֹהֵי הָאָ֫רֶץ]; #emph[the God of heaven and the God of the earth]; (so in #bref("Jer 8:1"); the regens is five times repeated). A lengthened series of genitives may, however, be formed by a nomen rectum serving at the same time as #emph[regens]; to a genitive depending on it (cf. #xref(<p127a>)[§ 127#h(0.18em)#emph[a];]; \[#emph[d];\]); e.g. #bref("Gen 47:9"); #he[יְמֵי שְׁנֵי חַיֵּי אֲבֹתַי]; #emph[the days of the years of the life of my fathers];; cf. #bref("Job 12:24");, where there are three genitives, #bref("Isa 10:12"); four, and 21:17 five (unless the last three are in apposition). As a rule, indeed, such an inconvenient accumulation of genitives is avoided by means of a circumlocution in the case of one of them (see #xref(<p129d>)[§ 129#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];).

#ml("b") <p128b> Rem. As the fundamental rules stated above are the necessary consequence not merely of #emph[logical]; but more especially of #emph[rhythmical]; relations (see #xref(<p89a>)[§ 89#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];), we must feel the more hesitation in admitting examples in which genitives are supposed to be loosely attached to forms other than the construct state. Some of these examples (the supposed genitives following a regens which is determined by the article) have been already discussed in #xref(<p127f>)[§ 127#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];–#emph[h];. Compare, moreover:

#ml("c") <p128c> (#emph[a];) Genitives after the absolute state, e.g. #bref("Isa 28:1"); #he[גֵּֽיא־שְׁמָנִים הֲלוּמֵי יַ֫יִן]; #emph[the fat valley of them that are overcome with wine];. The usual explanation that #he[גֵּֽיא־שְׁמָנִים]; forms #emph[one]; single idea (in German #emph[Fettigkeitstal];), on which the #origpage("415") <orig-415>;genitive #he[הֲלוּמֵי יַ֫יִן]; then depends, in reality explains nothing; the text is almost certainly corrupt. In #bref("Deut 15:18"); #he[מִשְׁנֵה]; would be expected; in #bref("Josh 3:11"); #he[הַבְּרִית]; is a later addition; in #bref("Isa 32:13"); (#he[מָשׂוֹשׂ];), and #bref("Ps 68:22"); (#he[שֵׂעָר];), the absolute for the construct state probably rests only on the authority of the Masoretes. In #bref("Judg 6:25"); ff. the text is obviously in confusion. In #bref("Judg 8:32"); (cf. 6:24) #he[בְּעָפְרָה]; should come either after #he[וַיִּקָּבֵר]; or at the end of the verse, unless, with Moore, we omit #he[אֲבִי הָֽע׳]; as a gloss (from 6:24); in #bref("Isa 63:11"); #he[משֶׁה]; is probably a gloss on #he[יְמֵי־עוֹלָם]; which has crept into the text; in #bref("2 Sam 4:2"); #he[לְאִישׁ־בּ֫שֶׁת];, according to the LXX, has dropped out before #he[בֶּן];; in #bref("Ezek 6:11"); #he[רָעוֹת]; is to be omitted with the LXX; if originally in the text, it could only be genitive (\= #emph[all abominations of evils];), not an adjective; #bref("Prov 21:6"); the text is altogether uncertain (the LXX read #he[מֽוֹקְשֵׁי]; for #he[מְבַקְשֵׁי];); in #bref("1 Chr 9:13"); the preposition #he[לְ]; (after a #he[ל];) has dropped out before #he[מְלֶאכֶת]; (cf. 12:25).—Elsewhere (#bref("Deut 3:5");, #bref("1 Kgs 4:13");, #bref("2 Chr 8:5");) the supposed genitives are to be taken rather as words of nearer definition standing in apposition, i.e. #emph[with high walls, gates, and bars];. In #bref("Jer 8:5"); #he[ירושלים]; is either in apposition to #he[העם הזה]; or is better (since not in the LXX) omitted as a gloss.

#ml("d") <p128d> (#emph[b];) Genitives after a noun with a suffix (where the suffix prevents the direct government by the #emph[nomen regens];). Thus in #bref("Lev 27:3");, 5, 6, where #he[הַזָּכָר]; after #he[עֶרְכְּךָ];#footnote[Halévy, #emph[J. A.]; xiv. 548, removes the difficulty by pointing #he[עַרְכֹּךְ];.]; might be taken, contrary to the accents, as subject of the following clause; in #bref("Lev 5:15");, 25 the suffix may refer to Moses. In #bref("Lev 6:3"); #he[מִדּוֹ בַד]; #emph[his garment];, namely the garment #emph[of linen];, unless simply in apposition, cf. #xref(<p131d>)[§ 131#h(0.18em)#emph[d];]; (or read #he[מִדֵּי];?); #bref("Lev 26:42");, where #he[בְּרִיתִי יַֽעֲקֹב וגו׳]; could at most be explained as an ellipse for #he[בְּרִיתִי בְרִית יַֽעֲקֹב];, cf. #xref(<p125h>)[§ 125#h(0.18em)#emph[h];]; (probably, however, it is a case of dittography of the #he[י];, which was repeated also before #he[אברהם];; so Valeton, #emph[ZAW.]; xii. 3); equally strange is #he[בְּרִיתִי הַיּוֹם]; #bref("Jer 33:20");, etc. On the other hand, #he[אִם יִֽהְיֶה נְבִֽיאֲכֶם יְהֹוָה]; #bref("Num 12:6"); could not possibly mean #emph[if your prophet be a prophet of the Lord];; the text is manifestly corrupt (probably #he[נְבִֽיאֲךָ מִיַּהְוֶה]; is to be read, with Marti). In #bref("Ps 45:7"); #he[כִּסְאֲךָ אֱלֹהִים]; (usually explained as #emph[thy divine throne];), #he[אלהים]; is most probably a later addition \[another suggestion is to read #he[כֵאלֹהִים]; #emph[like God];(#emph[’s throne];): cf. #xref(<p141d>)[§ 141#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];, note\]. In #bref("Jer 52:20"); two readings are probably combined, #he[לִנְחֻשְׁתָּם]; without any addition, and #he[לִנְח֫שֶׁת כָּל־הַכֵּלִים];. In #bref("Num 25:12"); #he[שָׁלוֹם]; is in apposition to #he[בְּרִיתִי];. On #he[דַּרְכֵּךְ זִמָּה]; #bref("Ezek 16:27");, cf. #xref(<p131r>)[§ 131#h(0.18em)#emph[r];];.

#ml("e") <p128e> (#emph[c];) The interposition of a word is assumed between #he[כָּל־]; (#emph[the whole];; cf. #xref(<p127b>)[§ 127#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];) and the genitive governed by it in #bref("2 Sam 1:9");, #bref("Job 27:3"); (#he[עוֹד];), and, if the text is correct, in #bref("Hos 14:3"); (#he[תִּשָּׂא];). In reality, however, in all three places the genitive relation is destroyed by the transposition of the words (instead of #he[עוֹד כָּל־];, etc.), and #he[כָּל־]; is rather to be taken adverbially (equivalent to #emph[wholly];), e.g. #bref("2 Sam 1:9"); #emph[because my life is yet wholly in me];, i.e. my whole life; cf. Philippi, #emph[Stat. Constr.];, p. 10.—On the instances in which the original construct state #he[אֵין]; #emph[non\-existence]; is used without a following genitive, see the negative sentences, #xref(<p152o>)[§ 152#h(0.18em)#emph[o];];.

#ml("f") <p128f> #strong[2.]; The dependence of the nomen rectum on the nomen regens by no means represents merely what is, properly speaking, the genitive relation (see the examples under #emph[g];–#emph[i];). Very frequently the nomen #origpage("416") <orig-416>;rectum only adds a nearer definition of the nomen regens, whether by giving the name, the genus or species, the measure, the material, or finally an attribute of it (#emph[genit. epexegeticus]; or #emph[appositionis];,#footnote[The latter term is preferred especially by König, #emph[Theol. Stud. und Krit.];, 1898, p. 528 ff.]; see the examples under #emph[k];–#emph[q];).

Examples. The #emph[nomen rectum]; represents—

#ml("g") <p128g> (#emph[a];) A #emph[subjective genitive];, specifying the possessor, author, etc., e.g. #he[בֵּית־הַמֶּ֫לֶךְ]; #emph[the king’s house];; #he[דְּבַר יְהֹוָה]; #emph[the word of the Lord];.

#ml("h") <p128h> (#emph[b];) An #emph[objective genitive];, e.g. #bref("Obad 10"); #he[מֵֽחֲמַס אָחִיךָ]; #emph[for the violence done to thy brother];#footnote[Cf. in Latin a similar use of the genitive after #emph[iniuria]; (Caes. #emph[B. G.]; 1, 30), #emph[metus (hostium, Pompeii];, etc.), #emph[spes];, and other words. In Greek, cf. εὔνοια τῶν φίλων, πίστις τοῦ θεοῦ, ὁ λόγος ὁ τοῦ σταυροῦ, 1 Cor. 1:18.]; (but in #bref("Ezek 12:19"); #he[מֵֽחֲמַס]; is followed by a subjective genitive); #bref("Prov 20:2"); #he[אֵימַת מֶ֫לֶךְ]; #emph[the terror of a king];; #bref("Gen 18:20"); #he[זַֽעֲקַת סְדֹם]; #emph[the cry concerning Sodom];; #bref("Isa 23:5"); #he[שֵׁמַע צֹר]; #emph[the report of]; (#emph[about];) #emph[Tyre];, cf. #bref("2 Sam 4:4");; #bref("Amos 8:10"); #he[אֵ֫בֶל יָחִיד]; #emph[the mourning for an only son];; #bref("Deut 20:14"); #he[שְׁלַל אֹֽיְבֶיךָ]; #emph[praeda hostibus tuis erepta];; cf. #bref("Isa 3:14");. In a wider sense this includes such examples as #he[דֶּ֫רֶךְ עֵץ הַֽחַיִּים]; #emph[the way of]; (i.e. #emph[to];) #emph[the tree of life];, #bref("Gen 3:24");; cf. #bref("Prov 7:27");, #bref("Job 38:20");; #he[דֶּ֫רֶךְ הַיָּם]; #emph[the way of]; (#emph[by];) #emph[the sea];, #bref("Isa 8:23");; #he[זִבְחֵי אֱלֹהִים]; #emph[the sacrifices of]; (i.e. #emph[pleasing to];) #emph[God];, #bref("Ps 51:19");; #he[שְׁבֻעַת יְהֹוָה]; #emph[the oath of]; (i.e. #emph[sworn before];) #emph[the Lord];, #bref("1 Kgs 2:48");; #he[דִּבְרֵי לְמוּאֵל]; #emph[the words of]; (i.e. #emph[addressed to];) L., #bref("Prov 31:1");.

#ml("i") <p128i> (#emph[c];) A #emph[partitive genitive];; this includes especially the cases in which an adjective in the construct state is followed by a general term, e.g. #he[חַכְמוֹת שָֽׂרוֹתֶיהָ]; #emph[the wisest of her ladies];, #bref("Judg 5:29");; cf. for this way of expressing the superlative, #xref(<p133h>)[§ 133#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];, and also #emph[r]; below.

#ml("k") <p128k> Merely formal genitives (#emph[genit. explicativus]; or #emph[epexegeticus, genit. appositionis];) are those added to the construct state as nearer definitions—

(#emph[d];) Of the #emph[name];, e.g. #he[נְהַר פְּרָת]; #emph[the river Euphrates];; #he[אֶ֫רֶץ כְּנַ֫עַן]; #emph[the land of Canaan];; #he[בְּתוּלַת יִשְׂרָאֵל]; #emph[the virgin Israel]; (not #emph[of Israel];), #bref("Amos 5:2");.

#ml("l") <p128l> (#emph[e];) Of the #emph[genus];, e.g. #bref("Prov 15:20"); (21:20) #he[כְּסִיל אָדָם]; #emph[a fool of a man]; (\=#emph[a foolish man];); cf. #bref("Gen 16:12");, #bref("Isa 1:4");, #bref("29:19");, #bref("Hos 13:2");, #bref("Mic 5:4");, etc.

#ml("m") <p128m> (#emph[f];) Of the #emph[species];, e.g. #he[אֲחֻזַּת קֶ֫בֶר]; #emph[a possession of a burying\-place];, i.e. #emph[hereditary sepulchre];, #bref("Gen 23:4");, etc.; #he[תְּאֵנֵי הַבַּכֻּרוֹת]; #emph[the early figs];, #bref("Jer 24:2");; #he[אֹ֫הֶל בֵּיתִי]; #emph[the tabernacle of my house];, i.e. my dwelling\-place, #bref("Ps 132:3");.

#ml("n") <p128n> (#emph[g];) Of the #emph[measure, weight, extent, number];, e.g. #he[מְתֵי מִסְפָּר]; #emph[people of number];, i.e. #emph[few in number];, #bref("Gen 34:30");, #bref("Deut 26:5");; cf. also #bref("Ezek 47:3");–5 #emph[waters of the ankles, waters of the loins, waters of swimming];, i.e. which reached up to the ankles, or loins, or necessitated swimming; but in verse 4 in apposition (?) #he[מַ֫יִם בִּרְכַּ֫יִם];.

#ml("o") <p128o> (#emph[h];) Of the #emph[material];#footnote[In the almost entire absence of corresponding adjectives (#he[אָרוּז]; #emph[made of cedar];, a denominative from #he[אֶ֫רֶז];, and #he[נָחוּשׁ]; #emph[brazen]; are the only examples), the language regularly has recourse to the above periphrasis. On the form #emph[qāṭûl];, as expressing an inherent property, cf. #xref(<p50f>)[§ 50#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];; cf. also the proper name, #he[בַּרְזִלַּי]; #emph[ferreus];.]; of which something consists, e.g. #he[כְּלִי חָ֑רֶשׂ]; #emph[a vessel of earthenware];, #bref("Num 5:17");; #he[כְּלֵי כֶ֫סֶף]; #emph[vessels of silver]; (cf. the French #emph[des vases d’or];); #he[אֲרוֹן עֵץ]; #emph[an ark of wood];, #he[שֵׁ֫בֶט בַּרְזֶל]; #emph[a rod of iron];, #bref("Ps 2:9");; cf. #bref("Gen 3:21");, #bref("6:14");, #bref("Judg 7:13");, etc. #origpage("417") <orig-417>;

#ml("p") <p128p> (#emph[i];) Of the #emph[attribute]; of a person or thing, e.g. #bref("Gen 17:8"); #he[אֲחֻזַּת עוֹלָם]; #emph[an everlasting possession];; #bref("Prov 17:8"); #emph[a precious stone];; cf. #bref("Num 28:6");, #bref("Isa 13:8");, #bref("28:4");, #bref("Ps 23:2");, #bref("31:3");, #bref("Prov 5:19");, #bref("14:5");, #bref("Job 41:19");, and the examples of the genitive with a suffix given in #xref(<p135n>)[§ 135#h(0.18em)#emph[n];];. Such a periphrasis for the expression of attributes frequently occurs, even when the corresponding adjectives are in use. Thus especially #he[קֹ֫דֶשׁ]; #emph[holiness]; very frequently serves as a periphrasis for the adjective #he[קָדוֹשׁ]; (e.g. #he[בִּגְדֵי הַקֹּ֫דֶשׁ]; #emph[the holy garments];, #bref("Exod 29:29");), since #he[קָדוֹשׁ]; is used almost exclusively in reference to persons (hence also with #he[עַם]; and #he[גּוֹי]; #emph[people];, and with #he[שֵׁם]; the #emph[name]; of a person); the only exceptions are #he[מָקוֹם קָדוֹשׁ]; #emph[holy place];, #bref("Exod 29:31");, etc.; #he[מַ֫יִם קְדשִׁים]; #emph[holy water];, #bref("Num 5:17");; #he[קָדוֹשׁ]; as the predicate of #he[יוֹם]; #emph[day];, #bref("Neh 8:10"); f., and of #he[מַֽחֲנֶה]; #emph[camp];, #bref("Deut 23:15");. So also the use of #he[צַדִּיק]; #emph[righteous]; is always confined to persons, except in #bref("Deut 4:8");; elsewhere the periphrasis with #he[צֶ֫דֶק]; or #he[צְדָקָה]; is always used, e.g. #he[מֹֽאזְנֵי צֶ֫דֶק]; #emph[just balances];, #bref("Lev 19:36");.

#ml("q") <p128q> In a wider sense this use of the genitive also includes statements of the purpose for which something is intended, e.g. #he[צֹאן טִבְחָה]; #emph[sheep for the slaughter];, #bref("Ps 44:23");; #he[מוּסַר שְׁלוֹמֵ֫נוּ]; #emph[the chastisement designed for our peace];, #bref("Isa 53:5");; cf. 51:17 (#emph[the cup which causes staggering];), #bref("Ps 116:13");; finally, also, the description of the material, with which something is laden or filled, e.g. #bref("1 Sam 16:20"); #he[חֲמֹר לֶ֫חֶם וְנֹאד יַ֫יִן]; #emph[an ass]; laden #emph[with bread and a bottle of wine]; (but probably #he[עֲשָׂרָה]; is to be read for #he[חֲמֹר];); cf. #bref("Gen 21:14");, #bref("Prov 7:20");, etc.

#ml("r") <p128r> Rem. 1. Certain substantives are used to convey an attributive idea in the construct state before a partitive genitive; thus #he[מִבְחָר]; #emph[choice, selection];, as in #bref("Gen 23:6"); #he[מִבְחַר קְבָרֵ֫ינוּ]; #emph[the choice of our sepulchres];, i.e. our choicest sepulchres; #bref("Exod 15:4");, #bref("Isa 22:7");, #bref("37:24");; other examples are, #bref("Isa 1:16"); #emph[the evil of your doings];, emphatically, for #emph[your evil doings];; #bref("Isa 17:4");, #bref("37:24"); (\=#emph[the tall cedars thereof];), #bref("Ps 139:22");, #bref("Job 15:26");.—This is the more common construction with the substantive #he[כֹּל]; #emph[entirety];, for #emph[all, the whole, every];, see #xref(<p127b>)[§ 127#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];; it is also frequent with #he[מְעַט]; #emph[a little];, for #emph[few];, #bref("1 Sam 17:28");, etc.

#ml("s") <p128s> 2. To the periphrases expressing attributive ideas (see #emph[p]; above) by means of a genitive construction may be added the very numerous combinations of the construct states #he[אִישׁ]; #emph[a man];, #he[בַּעַל]; #emph[master, possessor];, #he[בֶּן־]; #emph[son];, and their feminines and plurals (including #he[מְתֵי]; #emph[men];, used only in the plural), with some appellative noun, in order to represent a person (poetically even a thing) as possessing some object or quality, or being in some condition. In English, such combinations are sometimes rendered by single substantives, sometimes by circumlocution.

Examples:—

#ml("t") <p128t> (#emph[a];) Of #he[אִישׁ];, etc.: #he[אִישׁ דְּבָרִים]; #emph[an eloquent man];, #bref("Exod 4:10"); (but #he[אִישׁ שְׂפָתַ֫יִם]; #bref("Job 11:2"); #emph[a man of lips];, i.e. #emph[a boaster];); #he[אִישׁ לָשׁוֹן]; \= #emph[a slanderer];, #bref("Ps 140:12");; #he[אִישׁ דַּ֫עַת]; #emph[a man of knowledge];, #bref("Prov 24:5");; #he[אִישׁ חֵמָה]; #emph[a wrathful man];, #bref("Prov 15:18");; #he[אִישׁ דָּמִים]; #emph[a man of blood];, #bref("2 Sam 16:7");, #bref("Ps 5:7");; cf. further, #bref("1 Sam 16:18");, #bref("1 Kgs 2:26");, #bref("Isa 53:3");, #bref("Prov 19:6");, #bref("26:21");, #bref("29:1");, #bref("Ezra 8:18");; also #he[אֵ֫שֶׁת מִדְיָנִים]; #emph[a contentious woman];, #bref("Prov 27:15");; in the plural, e.g. #bref("Gen 6:4"); #he[אַנְשֵׁי הַשֵּׁם]; #emph[the men of renown, famous];; cf. #bref("Gen 47:6");, #bref("Isa 41:11");, #bref("Job 34:8");, 10 (#he[אַנְשֵׁי לֵבָב]; #emph[men of understanding];); with #he[מְתֵי];, e.g. #bref("Isa 5:13"); (#he[מְתֵי רָעָב]; #emph[famished men];; but read probably #he[מְזֵי רָעָב]; #emph[weak with hunger];); #bref("Ps 26:4");, #bref("Job 11:11");, #bref("22:15");.

#ml("u") <p128u> (#emph[b];) Of #he[בַּ֫עַל];, etc.: #he[בַּ֫עַל שֵׂעָר]; #emph[hairy];, #bref("2 Kgs 1:8");; #he[בַּ֫עַל הַֽחֲלֹמוֹת]; #emph[the dreamer];, #bref("Gen 37:19");; cf. #bref("Nah 1:2");, #bref("Prov 1:17");, #bref("18:9"); (#emph[a destroyer];), 22:24, 23:2 (#emph[disposed to eat, greedy];), 24:8; feminine #origpage("418") <orig-418>;#he[בַּֽעֲלַת־אוֹב]; #emph[a woman that hath a soothsaying spirit];, #bref("1 Sam 28:7");; cf. #bref("Nah 3:4");; in the plural, e.g. #he[בַּֽעֲלֵי חִצִּים]; #emph[archers];, #bref("Gen 49:23");, #he[בַּֽעֲלֵי בְרִית]; #emph[confederates];, #bref("Gen 14:13");; #he[בַּֽעֲלֵי שְׁבוּעָה]; #emph[sworn supporters];, #bref("Neh 6:18");.

#ml("v") <p128v> (#emph[c];) Of #he[בֶּן־];, etc.: #he[בֶּן־חַ֫יִל]; #emph[a hero, warrior];, #bref("1 Kgs 1:52");; #he[בֶּן־מֶ֫שֶׁק]; #emph[heir];, #bref("Gen 15:2");; #he[בֶּן־שָׁנָה]; #emph[yearling];, #bref("Exod 12:5");, etc.; #he[בֶּן־מְאַת שָׁנָה]; #emph[centum annos natus];, #bref("Gen 21:5");; #he[בֶּן־מָ֫וֶת]; #emph[worthy to die];, #bref("1 Sam 20:31"); (Luther, #bref("2 Sam 12:5"); #emph[ein Kind des Todes];); cf. #bref("Deut 25:2"); #he[בִּן־הַכּוֹת]; #emph[worthy to be beaten];. Feminine, e.g. #he[בַּת־בְּלִיַּ֫עַל]; #emph[a wicked woman];, #bref("1 Sam 1:16");; frequently also #he[אִישׁ בְּלִיַּ֫עַל];, #he[בְּנֵי ב׳];, #he[אַנְשֵׁי ב׳]; and even simply #he[בְּלִיַּ֫עַל];, like the Latin #emph[scelus]; for #emph[scelestissimus];, #bref("2 Sam 23:6");, #bref("Job 34:18");. Plural masculine, e.g. #he[בְּנֵי מֶ֑רִי]; #emph[children of rebellion];, #bref("Num 17:25");. #he[בֶּן־]; is used poetically of things without life, e.g. #bref("Isa 5:1"); #he[בֶּן־שָׁ֫מֶן]; #emph[a fat];, i.e. a fruitful (hill); #bref("Jonah 4:10"); #he[בִּן־לַ֫יְלָה]; i.e. #emph[grown in a night];; #bref("Job 41:20"); #emph[son of the bow]; (i.e. an arrow); so also #he[בְּנֵי רֶשֶׁף]; \= #emph[sparks];, #bref("Job 5:7");; #bref("Lam 3:13");; #he[בְּנוֹת]; #bref("Eccl 12:4"); #emph[the daughters of song];, probably meaning the individual notes.

There is another use of #he[בֶּן־]; or #he[בְּנֵי]; to denote membership of a guild or society (or of a tribe, or any definite class). Thus #he[בְּנֵי אֱלֹהִים]; or #he[בְּנֵי הָֽאֱלֹהִים]; #bref("Gen 6:2");, 4, #bref("Job 1:6");, #bref("2:1");, #bref("38:7"); (cf. also #he[בְּנֵי אֵלִים]; #bref("Ps 29:1");, #bref("89:7");) properly means not #emph[sons of god];(#emph[s];), but beings of the class of #he[אֱלֹהִים]; or #he[אֵלִים];; #he[בְּנֵיֽ־הַנְּבִיאִים]; #bref("1 Kgs 20:35"); (singular in #bref("Amos 7:14");) persons #emph[belonging to the guild of prophets];; #he[בֶּן־הָֽרַקָּחִים]; #bref("Neh 3:8"); #emph[one of the guild of apothecaries];, cf. 3:31 where #he[בֶּן־הַצֹּֽרְפִים]; is to be read. Similarly #he[בְּנֵי שִׁלֵּשִׁים]; #bref("Gen 50:23"); are most probably not #emph[great\-grandsons]; but #emph[grandsons];, i.e. those belonging to the third generation. Cf. also #he[בְּנֵי הַגֵּֽרְשֻׁנִּי]; #bref("Num 4:27"); f. #emph[Gershonites];, #he[בְּנֵי הַקְּהָתִים]; #bref("2 Chr 20:19");, etc., #emph[Kohathites];; #he[בְּנֵי קֶ֫דֶם]; #emph[dwellers in the East];.

#ml("w") <p128w> 3. Special mention must be made of the not infrequent idiom by which adjectives (sometimes also ordinals, see #xref(<p134o>)[§ 134#h(0.18em)#emph[o];];) are added in the genitive, like substantives, rather than as attributes in the same state, gender, and number as the noun which they qualify; thus, #bref("Isa 28:4"); #he[צִיצַת נֹבֵל]; #emph[the flower of that which fades];, for which verse 1 has #he[צִיץ נֹבֵל]; #emph[the fading flower];; cf. further, #bref("Isa 22:24");, #bref("Jer 22:17"); (?), 52:13, #bref("Ps 73:10");, #bref("74:15"); (but #he[אֵיתָן]; may be a substantive), 78:49; also the use of #he[רַע]; as a substantive, e.g. in #bref("Prov 2:14"); b, 6:24 (#he[אֵ֫שֶׁת רַע];), etc., analogous to the New Testament phrase ὁ οἰκονόμος τῆς ἀδικίας, Luke 16:8, and the French #emph[un homme de bien];.#footnote[On the other hand, in such passages as #bref("Isa 36:2"); (#bref("2 Kgs 18:17");), #bref("Zech 14:4");, #bref("Eccl 8:10");, etc., there is no apparent reason why the Masora requires the construct state instead of the absolute; hence #he[חֵיל]; #bref("Isa 36:2"); and #he[גֵּיא]; #bref("Zech 14:4"); must be intended as forms of the absolute state, shortened in consequence of their close connection.];—Finally, an adverb (treated as a substantive) may likewise be used as an epexegetical genitive; cf. #he[דְּמֵי חִנָּם]; #emph[blood shed without cause];, #bref("1 Kgs 2:31");; #bref("Prov 24:28");, #bref("26:2");; #bref("Ezek 30:16"); (#he[יוֹמָם];).

#ml("x") <p128x> #strong[3.]; The epexegetical genitives include finally the numerous nearer definitions which follow the construct state of adjectives (and of active and passive participles, or verbal adjectives, cf. #xref(<p116f>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];–#emph[l];). For, while the word of nearer definition is added to the verb in the accusative (e.g. #he[חָלָה אֶת־רַגְלָיו]; #emph[he was diseased in his feet];, #bref("1 Kgs 15:23");), it may, with participles and verbal adjectives, be either in the accusative #origpage("419") <orig-419>;(#xref(<p116f>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[f];]; and #emph[k];) or in the genitive, the case of a word depending on a noun. Such a genitive relation is usually termed an #emph[improper annexion];. The nearer definition contains a statement either of the #emph[material];, e.g. #bref("Exod 3:8");, etc., #he[אֶ֫רֶץ זָבַת חָלָב וּדְבַשׁ]; #emph[a land flowing with milk and honey];; or of the #emph[means];, e.g. #he[חַלְלֵי־חֶ֫רֶב]; #emph[slain with the sword];, #bref("Isa 22:2");; or the #emph[cause];, #bref("Song 2:5"); #emph[sick of love];; or of the #emph[scope]; of the attribute,#footnote[Cf. the Latin #emph[integer vitae scelerisque purus; tristes animi];, etc.]; e.g. #bref("Gen 39:6"); #he[יְפֵה־תֹ֫אַר]; #emph[fair of form];; cf. #bref("Gen 41:2");, 4, #bref("Exod 34:6");, #bref("Isa 1:4");, #bref("Jer 32:19");, #bref("Nah 1:3");, #bref("Ps 119:1");, #bref("Job 37:16");; or of the #emph[manner];, e.g. #bref("Ps 59:6"); #he[בֹּֽגְדֵי אָ֫וֶן]; #emph[faithless ones of wickedness]; (wickedly faithless).

#ml("y") <p128y> Especially frequent is the use of this genitive to name the part of the body described as being affected by some physical or mental condition, e.g. #bref("Ps 24:4"); #he[נְקִי כַפַּ֫יִם]; #emph[clean as regards hands];, etc.; #bref("2 Sam 9:3");, #bref("Isa 6:5");, #bref("Job 17:9");; #bref("Isa 19:10"); #he[אַגְמֵי־נָ֫פֶשׁ]; #emph[grieved in soul];; #bref("1 Sam 1:10");, #bref("Job 3:20");. Also such examples as #bref("Amos 2:16");, #bref("Prov 19:1");, where a suffix is attached to the substantive, must be regarded as instances of the genitive construction, on the analogy of #bref("Prov 14:2");, see #xref(<p116k>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[k];];.

#sec("129", [Expression of the Genitive by Circumlocution], note: []) <s129>

#ml("a") <p129a> Besides the construction of a nomen rectum dependent upon a nomen regens in the construct state (§§ 89 and 128), the connection of two nouns may also be effected otherwise, either by simply attaching the dependent noun by means of the preposition #he[לְ];, which, according to #xref(<p119r>)[§ 119#h(0.18em)#emph[r];];, expresses, besides other ideas, like that of #emph[belonging to];,#footnote[Cf. the σχῆμα Κολοφώνιον in Greek, e.g. ἡ κεφαλὴ τῷ ἀνθρώπῳ for τοῦ ἀνθρώπου (Bernhardy’s #emph[Syntax];, p. 88). The Arab grammarians distinguish a twofold genitive, one of which may be resolved by #he[לְ];, and the other by #he[מִן]; \[see Wright’s #emph[Arabic Grammar];, vol. ii, § 75 ff.\]. The #emph[de]; of the Romance languages is a development of the latter idea; the Gascon, however, says e.g. #emph[la fille à Mr. N.];, laying stress upon the idea of #emph[belonging to]; and not that of #emph[origin];, as in #emph[la fille de];... of the literary language.]; or by the addition of a relative clause (#he[אֲשֶׁר לְ];, see #emph[h]; below).

#ml("b") <p129b> 1. The introduction of a genitive by #he[לְ]; sometimes occurs even when the construction with the construct state would be equally possible, e.g. #bref("1 Sam 14:16"); #he[הַצֹּפִים לְשָׁאוּל]; #emph[the watchmen of Saul];; #bref("Ps 37:16");, #bref("2 Chr 28:18"); (where indeed the circumlocution makes the sense much plainer); as a rule, however, this use is restricted to the following cases:—

#ml("c") <p129c> (#emph[a];) To prevent a nomen regens being determined by a following determinate genitive, e.g. #bref("1 Sam 16:18"); #he[בֵּן לְיִשַׁי]; #emph[a son of Jesse]; (#he[בֶּן־יִשַׁי]; would be, according to #xref(<p127a>)[§ 127#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];, #emph[the son of Jesse];); cf. #bref("Gen 14:18");, #bref("36:12");, #bref("41:12");, #bref("Num 16:22"); (27:16), #bref("1 Sam 17:8");, #bref("2 Sam 19:21");, #bref("Ps 122:5");. Hence, regularly #he[מִזְמוֹר לְדָוִד]; (#bref("Ps 3:1");, etc.) #emph[a psalm of David]; (properly belonging to David as the author), for which #he[לְדָוִד]; #emph[of David]; is used alone elliptically in #bref("Ps 11:1");, #bref("14:1");, etc. Such a case as #he[לְדָוִד מִזְמוֹר]; (#bref("Ps 24:1");, etc.) is not to #origpage("420") <orig-420>;be regarded as a transposition, but #he[מִזְמוֹר]; is used epexegetically for the general term omitted before #he[לְדָוִד]; (as it were, a poem #emph[of David, a psalm];). Moreover, the introduction of the author, poet, etc., by this #emph[Lamed auctoris]; is the customary idiom also in the other Semitic dialects, especially in Arabic.

#ml("d") <p129d> (#emph[b];) When a genitive is to be made dependent on a nomen regens, which is itself composed of a nomen regens and rectum, and represents, as a compound, one united idea, e.g. #bref("Ruth 2:3"); #he[חֶלְקַת הַשָּׁדֶה לְבֹעַז]; #emph[the portion of field belonging to Boaz]; (#he[חֶ׳ שְׂדֵה בֹעַז]; would be #emph[the portion of the field of Boaz];); #bref("2 Kgs 5:9"); #emph[at the house\-door of Elisha];. This especially applies to the cases in which the compound regens represents a term in very common use, the fixed form of which cannot be altered, e.g. #bref("1 Kgs 14:19"); #he[עַל־סֵ֫פֶר דִּבְרֵי הַיָּמִים לְמַלְכֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל]; #emph[in the book of the chronicles of the kings of Israel];; 15:23, etc.; cf. also #bref("Josh 19:51");.

#ml("e") <p129e> (#emph[c];) When for any other reason the construction with the nomen regens in the construct state is inadmissible; cf. e.g. #bref("Lev 18:20");, where #he[שְׁכָבְתְּךָ];, on account of the suffix, cannot be used in the construct state; but #bref("Lev 15:16"); ff., etc., #he[שִׁכְבַת־זֶ֫רַע];; #bref("Judg 3:28"); #emph[the Jordan fords of Moab]; (#he[יַרְדֵּן]; as a #emph[proper name]; cannot be used in the construct state); #bref("Exod 20:5"); #emph[upon the third and upon the fourth generation of them that hate me];; #he[וְעַל־רִבֵּעִים]; must be kept in the absolute state for the sake of conformity with #he[עַל־שִׁלֵּשִׁים];, and for the same reason also #he[לַֽאֲלָפִים לְאֹֽהֲבַי];.

#ml("f") <p129f> (#emph[d];) After statements of number in such cases as #bref("Gen 8:14"); #he[בְּשִׁבְעָה וְעֶשְׂרִים יוֹם לַחֹ֫דֶשׁ]; #emph[on the seven and twentieth day of the month];; cf. 7:11, 16:3 and frequently, or as in #bref("Hag 1:1"); #he[בִּשְׁנַת שְׁתַּ֫יִם לְדָֽרְיָ֫וֶשׁ]; #emph[in the second year of Darius];; the numeral here is always one compound idea with the substantive numbered, and consequently (as in the examples under #emph[b];) does not admit of being in the constr. st. with a genitive. The same naturally applies also to such examples as #bref("1 Kgs 3:18"); #he[בַּיּוֹם הַשְּׁלִישִׁי לְלִדְתִּי]; #emph[on the third day of my giving birth]; (i.e. after my giving birth). Cf. also the standing phrase #he[בְּאֶחָד לַחֹ֫דֶשׁ]; #emph[on the first]; (day) #emph[of the month];, #bref("Gen 8:5"); and frequently.

#ml("g") <p129g> Rem. In cases like #bref("2 Sam 3:2"); #emph[and his firstborn was Amnon]; #he[לַֽאֲחִינֹ֫עַם]; #emph[of Ahinoam];, the genitive expressed by circumlocution with #he[לְ]; is in reality dependent on a regens which is omitted (#he[בֵּן לַֽאֲחִינֹ֫עַם]; #emph[a son of Ahinoam];); cf. #bref("2 Sam 3:3");, 5, #bref("1 Kgs 14:13");, #bref("Amos 5:3"); (unless #he[לְבֵית יִשְׂרָאֵל]; originally depended on #emph[thus spake the Lord];), and the remarks on #he[לְדָוִד מִזְמוֹר]; under #emph[c]; above.

#ml("h") <p129h> 2. The periphrastic expression of the genitive by means of #he[אֲשֶׁר לְ]; is used principally to state the #emph[possessor];, e.g. #bref("Gen 29:9"); #he[הַצֹּאן אֲשֶׁר לְאָבִ֫יהָ]; #emph[her father’s sheep]; (prop. #emph[the sheep which]; belonged #emph[to her father];); #bref("Gen 47:4"); and frequently. So also (according to #xref(<p128a>)[§ 128#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];) when a genitive depends on more than one substantive, e.g. #bref("Gen 40:5"); #emph[the butler and the baker who]; (belonged) #emph[to the king of Egypt]; (#he[וְאֹפֵה מֶ֫לֶךְ מִצְרַ֫יִם]; would indicate only the baker as belonging to the king); or when a genitive (as in the examples under #emph[d]; above) is added to a compound, which expresses one united idea (#bref("Ruth 4:3");); or when, as a fixed term (e.g. a title), it appears always in the same form, e.g. #bref("Song 1:1"); #he[שִׁיר הַשִּׁירִים אֲשֶׁר לִשְׁלֹמֹה]; #emph[the Song of songs, of Solomon];; #bref("1 Sam 21:8");, #bref("2 Sam 2:8");, #bref("1 Chr 11:10");; cf. also #bref("Gen 41:43");.#footnote[In New Hebrew #he[שֶׁל]; (derived from #he[שֶׁלְּ]; \= #he[אֲשֶׁר לְ];, see § 36, and cf. #bref("Song 1:6");, #bref("3:7"); #he[שֶׁלִּי];, #he[שֶׁלִּשְׁלֹמֹה];) is used like the simple relative #he[דִּי];, #he[דְּ]; in Aramaic, as an independent sign of the genitive.]; #origpage("421") <orig-421>;

#sec("130", [Wider Use of the Construct State], note: []) <s130>

#ml("a") <p130a> The construct state, which, according to #xref(<p89a>)[§ 89#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];, primarily represents only the immediate government by one substantive of the following word (or combination of words), is frequently employed in rapid narrative as a connecting form, even apart from the genitive relation; so especially—

(1) Before prepositions,#footnote[Cf. König, ‘Die Ueberwucherung des St.\-constr.\-Gebrauchs im Semit.,’ #emph[ZDMG.]; 53, 521 ff.]; particularly in elevated (prophetic or poetic) style, especially when the nomen regens is a participle. Thus before #he[בְּ];, #he[שִׂמְחַת בַּקָּצִיר]; #emph[the joy in the harvest];, #bref("Isa 9:2");, #bref("2 Sam 1:21");, #bref("Ps 136:8");f.; in participles, #bref("Isa 5:11");, #bref("9:1");, #bref("19:8");, #bref("Ps 84:7");, and especially often when #he[בְּ]; with a suffix follows the participle, e.g. #bref("Ps 2:12"); #he[כָּל־חוֹסֵי בוֹ];; cf. #bref("Nah 1:7");, #bref("Jer 8:16"); (#bref("Ps 24:1");); #bref("Ps 64:9"); (unless #he[רֹאֶה]; should be read); 98:7.#footnote[In #bref("Judg 8:11"); the article is even used before a construct state followed by #he[בְּ];, in order to determine the whole combination #he[שְׁכוּנֵי בָֽאֳהָלִים]; #emph[tent\-dwellers];, taken as one word; cf., however, the remarks in #xref(<p127f>)[§ 127#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];–#emph[i]; on similar grammatical solecisms.];—Before #he[לְ];, #bref("Hos 9:6"); (but read probably #he[מַחֲמַדֵּי כַסְפָּם];); #bref("Ps 58:5"); (before #he[לָמוֹ];); #bref("Prov 24:9");, #bref("Job 18:2");, #bref("Lam 2:18"); (before #he[לָךְ];); #bref("1 Chr 6:55");, #bref("23:28");; in participles, #bref("Ezek 38:11");, #bref("Job 24:5");; before #he[לְ]; with an infinitive, #bref("Isa 56:10");, and again before #he[לְ]; with a suffix, #bref("Gen 24:21");, #bref("Isa 30:18");, #bref("64:3");;#footnote[These are to be distinguished from the cases where #he[לְ]; follows a construct state, which in conjunction with #he[מִן]; (and the following #he[לְ];) has become a sort of preposition or adverb of place; thus, we have #he[מִבֵּית־לְ]; #bref("Exod 26:33"); (for which in #bref("Ezek 1:27"); merely #he[בֵּית לְ];) meaning simply #emph[within];; #he[מִימִין לְ]; (#bref("2 Kgs 23:13");, #bref("Ezek 10:3");) #emph[on the right hand]; (i.e. south) #emph[of];; #he[מִצְּפוֹן לְ]; (#bref("Josh 8:11");, 13, etc., #bref("Judg 2:9");) #emph[on the north of];; cf. also #bref("Josh 15:21"); and #he[לִפְנֵי מִן]; #bref("Neh 13:4");.];—before #he[אֶל־];, #bref("Isa 14:19");, #bref("Ezek 21:17");; —before #he[אֶת־]; (#emph[with];), #bref("Isa 8:6");; —before #he[מִן];, #bref("Gen 3:22");, #bref("Isa 28:9"); (a participle); #bref("Jer 23:23");, #bref("Ezek 13:2");, #bref("Hos 7:5");; —before #he[עַל־];, #bref("Judg 5:10");; —before #he[בִּלְתִּי];, #bref("Isa 14:6");; —before the #emph[nota accus];. #he[את];, #bref("Jer 33:22");; —before a locative (which in such cases also serves as a genitive), #bref("Exod 27:13");, #bref("Jer 1:15");.

#ml("b") <p130b> (2) Before #emph[wāw copulative];, e.g. #bref("Ezek 26:10");; but #he[חָכְמַת]; #bref("Isa 33:6");, #he[גִּילַת]; 35:2, and #he[שְׁכֻרַת]; 51:21 may be cases of an intentional reversion to the old feminine ending #emph[ath];, in order to avoid the hiatus (#he[וָ];)#he[־ָה וְ];.

#ml("c") <p130c> (3) When it governs the (originally demonstrative) pronoun #he[אֲשֶׁר];; so especially in the combination #he[מְקוֹם אֲשֶׁר];, #bref("Gen 39:20");, #bref("40:3");, #emph[the place where]; (prop. #emph[of that in which];) #emph[Joseph was bound];; cf. #xref(<p138g>)[§ 138#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];; or #he[בִּמְקוֹם אֲשֶׁר]; #bref("Lev 4:24");, 33, #bref("2 Sam 15:21");, #bref("1 Kgs 21:19");, #bref("Jer 22:12");, #bref("Ezek 21:35");, #bref("Hos 2:1");. We should expect #he[הַמָּקוֹם אֲשֶׁר];, #he[בַּמָּקוֹם אֲשֶׁר];, as in #bref("Gen 35:13");, etc., #emph[at the place which...];, cf. § 138; but #he[אֲשֶׁר]; is treated as a nomen rectum instead of as an attribute. #origpage("422") <orig-422>;Cf. also #he[מִיּוֹם א׳]; followed by a #emph[perfect]; in #bref("1 Sam 29:8");, and #he[יְמֵי א׳]; #bref("Lev 13:46");, #bref("Num 9:18");.#footnote[In #bref("Deut 23:5"); the construct state governs a sentence introduced by the #emph[conjunction]; #he[אֲשֶׁר]; (#he[עַל־דְּבַר אֲשֶׁר]; #emph[by reason of the fact that];, i.e. #emph[because];); so also in #bref("1 Sam 3:13");.];

#ml("d") <p130d> (4) When it governs independent sentences (cf. § 155), which virtually stand to the construct state (as nomen regens) in a sort of genitive relation, e.g. #bref("Exod 4:13"); #he[בְּיַד־תְּשְׁלָח];#footnote[]; prop. #emph[by the hand of]; him whom #emph[thou wilt send];; #bref("Num 23:3"); #he[דְּבַר מַה־יַּרְאֵ֫נִי]; #emph[the matter of]; that #emph[which he shall show me];, i.e. whatever he shall; #bref("Isa 29:1"); #he[קִרְיַת חָנָה דָוִד]; #emph[the city where David encamped];; #bref("Jer 48:36");, #bref("Ps 16:3"); (if the text be right), 65:5 (#bref("Prov 8:32");), #bref("Ps 81:6");, #bref("Job 18:21"); #emph[the place of him that knoweth not God];; #bref("Job 29:16");, #bref("Lam 1:14"); (if the text be right) #emph[into the hands]; of those against whom #emph[I cannot stand];.#footnote[Probably #bref("Gen 22:14"); is also to be so explained (contrary to the accents), and certainly (contrary to the very unnatural division of the verses) #bref("2 Chr 30:18");, which should read on thus: #he[יְהֹוָה הַטּוֹב יְכַפֵּר בְּעַד כָּל־לְבָבוֹ הֵכִין]; #emph[the good Lord pardon every one that setteth his heart to seek God];. \[See Wickes’ #emph[Accentuation of the Twenty\-one Prose Books of the Old Testament];, p. 140.\]]; In #bref("Gen 39:4"); (#he[כָּל־יֶשׁ־לוֹ];) the #he[כָּל־]; takes after it a noun\-clause, and in #bref("Exod 9:4");, still more boldly, a subst. with #he[לְ];.—Very often a #emph[time\-determination]; governs the following sentence in this way; thus #he[אַֽחֲרֵי]; followed by a perfect, #bref("Lev 25:48");, #bref("1 Sam 5:9");; #he[בְּיוֹם]; #bref("Ps 102:3"); (before a noun\-clause), #bref("Exod 6:28");, #bref("Num 3:1");, #bref("Deut 4:15");, #bref("2 Sam 22:1");, #bref("Ps 18:1");, #bref("59:17");, #bref("138:3"); (in every case before a following perfect), #bref("Ps 56:10"); (before an imperfect); #he[מִיּוֹם]; followed by the perfect, #bref("Jer 36:2");; #he[כָּל־יְמֵי]; #bref("Lev 14:46");, #bref("1 Sam 25:15");, #bref("Job 29:2"); (#he[כִּימֵי]; #emph[as in the days when];...#footnote[Cf. #bref("Nah 2:9"); #he[מִימֵי הִיא];, usually explained to mean #emph[from the days]; that #emph[she]; hath been, but the text is evidently very corrupt.];; cf. #he[כִּימוֹת]; and #he[שְׁנוֹת]; before a perfect, #bref("Ps 90:15");); #he[בְּעֵת]; before a perfect, #bref("Jer 6:15"); (cf. 49:8, 50:31); before an imperfect, #bref("Job 6:17");; #he[תְּחִלַּת]; before a perfect #bref("Hos 1:2");.

#ml("e") <p130e> (5) Connected with a following word in apposition; certainly so in such cases as #he[בְּתוּלַת בַּת־צִיּוֹן]; #emph[the virgin, the daughter of Zion];, #bref("Isa 37:22");; cf. 23:12, #bref("Jer 14:17");; also #bref("1 Sam 28:7"); #he[אֵ֫שֶׁת בַּֽעֲלַת־אוֹב]; #emph[a woman, possessor of a soothsaying spirit];; cf. #bref("Deut 21:11");.—#bref("Gen 14:10");, #bref("Judg 19:22"); (but read probably #he[אֲנָשִׁים]; with Moore, as in #bref("Deut 13:14");, #bref("Judg 20:13");, #bref("1 Kgs 21:10");); #bref("2 Kgs 10:6");, #bref("17:13"); #emph[Q#super[e];rê];; #bref("Jer 46:9");, #bref("Ps 35:16"); (?), 78:9, #bref("Job 20:17"); #emph[b]; (unless #he[נַֽהֲרֵי]; or #he[נַֽהֲלֵי]; be a gloss).

#ml("f") <p130f> Rem. Some of the above passages may also be explained by supposing that there exists a real genitive relation towards the preceding construct state, which has been, as it were, provisionally left #emph[in suspenso];, in consequence of the insertion of some interrupting word, e.g. #bref("Isa 37:22");, etc.; #bref("Job 20:17"); #emph[a];. Elsewhere (#bref("Deut 33:19");, #bref("Ps 68:34");) the #emph[nomen regens]; probably governs the following construct state directly.#footnote[So also #bref("Isa 28:16"); #emph[a corner stone of the preciousness]; (#he[יִקְרַת]; is a #emph[substantive]; not an #emph[adjective];) #emph[of a fixed foundation];, i.e. a precious corner stone of surest foundation.—In #bref("2 Sam 20:19"); the text is wholly corrupt; in #bref("Ps 119:128"); read #he[כָּל־פִּקּוּדֶ֫יךָ];.]; #origpage("423") <orig-423>;

#ml("g") <p130g> (6) The numeral #he[אַחַד]; #emph[one]; for #he[אֶחָד]; in close connection, and even with small disjunctives, e.g. #bref("Gen 3:22");, #bref("48:22");, #bref("1 Sam 9:3");, #bref("2 Sam 17:22");, #bref("Isa 27:12");, #bref("Zech 11:7");.

The character of these passages shows that the numeral here cannot be in the construct state, but is merely a rhythmical shortening of the usual (tone\-lengthened) form.

#sec("131", [Apposition], note: []) <s131>

#ml("a") <p131a> #strong[1.]; Apposition in the stricter sense is the collocation of two substantives in the same case in order to define more exactly (or to complete) the one by the other, and, as a rule (see, however, below, under #emph[g];), the former by the latter. Apposition in Hebrew (as in the other Semitic languages#footnote[On certain uses of apposition peculiar to the Semitic languages, cf. the exhaustive discussion by Fleischer, ‘Ueber einige Arten der Nominalapposition im Arab.’ (#emph[Kleine Schriften];, ii. 16); \[and see also Driver, #emph[Tenses];, Appendix IV.\]];) is by no means confined to those cases in which it is used in English or in the classical languages. It is not infrequently found when either the #emph[subordination]; of one substantive to the other or some more circumstantial kind of epexegetical addition would be expected.

#strong[2.]; The principal kinds of apposition in Hebrew are:—

#ml("b") <p131b> (#emph[a];) The collocation of #emph[genus]; and #emph[species];, e.g. #he[אִשָּׁה אַלְמָנָה]; #emph[a woman]; (who was) #emph[a widow];, #bref("1 Kgs 7:14");; #he[נַֽעֲרָה בְתוּלָה]; #emph[a damsel]; (that is) #emph[a virgin];, #bref("Deut 22:23");, 28, #bref("Judg 4:4");, #bref("19:1");, #bref("21:12");, #bref("1 Sam 30:17");, #bref("1 Kgs 1:2");; cf. #bref("Gen 13:8");, #bref("21:20"); (where, however, #he[קַשָּׁת]; is probably an explanatory gloss); #bref("Exod 24:5"); (#bref("1 Sam 11:15");), #bref("2 Sam 15:16");, #bref("1 Kgs 3:16");, #bref("5:29"); (but probably #he[סֵ֫בֶל]; should be read instead of #he[סַבָּל];); #bref("Isa 3:24"); (unless #he[מַֽעֲשֵׂה]; is to be read), #bref("Jer 20:1");. Perhaps also #he[כֹּהֵן הָרֹאשׁ]; #emph[the priest]; (who is) #emph[the chief man];, #bref("2 Kgs 25:18");, etc.; others take #he[כֹּהֵן]; as constr. st.—In #bref("2 Sam 10:7"); read #he[כָּל־צְבָא הַגִּבּ׳]; with the LXX, as in the parallel passage #bref("1 Chr 19:9"); for #he[כָּל־צָבָא הַגּ׳];, which is evidently meant to refer to the reading in 2 S.

#ml("c") <p131c> (#emph[b];) Collocation of the #emph[person]; or #emph[thing]; and the #emph[attribute];, e.g. #bref("Job 20:29"); (27:13) #he[זֶה חֵ֫לֶק־אָדָם רָשָׁע]; #emph[this is the portion of a man];, (who is) #emph[a wicked man]; (but #he[רָשָׁע]; might also be an adject.); cf. #bref("Prov 6:12");.—#bref("Lev 6:13");, #bref("16:4"); (where, however, #he[קֹ֫דֶשׁ]; is probably a gloss); #bref("Prov 22:21"); #he[אֲמָרִים אֱמֶת]; #emph[words]; (which are) #emph[truth];; (immediately after #he[אִמְרֵי אֱמֶת];) cf. #bref("1 Sam 2:13");, #bref("Mic 1:11"); (where, however, #he[בּ֫שֶׁת]; is most probably a gloss on #he[עֶרְיָה];); #bref("Zech 1:13"); (\=#emph[comfortable words];); #bref("Ps 45:5"); (?) 68:17 (cf. verse 16). In a wider sense this includes also such cases as #bref("Ps 60:5"); #he[יַ֫יִן תַּרְעֵלָה]; #emph[wine]; which is #emph[staggering]; (intoxicating drink), which causes staggering#footnote[Unless it is to be translated #emph[thou gavest us intoxication to drink as wine]; (and so in #bref("1 Kgs 22:27"); #emph[give him affliction to eat as bread];, etc.); cf. #bref("Ps 80:6"); and the analogous examples of apposition in the form of a second accusative in #xref(<p117kk>)[§ 117#h(0.18em)#emph[kk];];. Moreover, having regard to #he[יַ֫יִן הָרֶ֫קַח]; #emph[spiced wine];, #bref("Song 8:2");, and #he[עַ֫יִר פֶּ֫רֶא]; #emph[a wild ass’s colt];, #bref("Job 11:12"); (in which passages #he[יַ֫יִן]; and #he[עַ֫יִר]; must certainly be in the construct state) we cannot but ask whether the Masora does not intend the #he[יַ֫יִן]; in #bref("Ps 60:5"); to be taken as construct state (for which elsewhere #he[יֵין];).];; #bref("1 Kgs 22:27");, #bref("2 Chr 18:26"); #he[מַ֫יִם לַ֫חַץ]; (in #origpage("424") <orig-424>;#bref("Isa 30:20"); parallel with #he[לֶ֫חֶם צַר];) #emph[water]; which is #emph[affliction];, drunk in trouble (imprisonment). Still more boldly, #bref("1 Kgs 5:3"); #he[בָּקָר רְעִי]; #emph[oxen]; which were taken out of the #emph[pastures];, and #bref("1 Kgs 6:7"); #emph[undressed stones]; which come from the #emph[quarry];, probably a corruption of #he[מִמַּסָּע];. A person and a condition are in apposition in #bref("Ezek 18:6"); (unless #he[בְּנִדָּתָהּ]; is to be read).—In #bref("1 Sam 4:1"); read #he[אֶ֫בֶן הָע׳];, as in #bref("5:1");, #bref("7:12");.

#ml("d") <p131d> (#emph[c];) Collocation of the #emph[person]; (#bref("Deut 28:36");) or #emph[thing]; (form) and #emph[material];,#footnote[Cf. also the examples treated above in #xref(<p127h>)[§ 127#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];.]; or of the #emph[place]; or #emph[measure]; and its #emph[contents];, e.g. #bref("1 Chr 15:19"); #he[בִּמְצִלְתַּ֫יִם נְח֫שֶׁת]; #emph[with cymbals]; which were #emph[brass];, i.e. of brass; cf. #bref("Exod 26:25");, #bref("Dan 11:8");, #bref("1 Chr 28:15");, 18 (#emph[?];); #bref("Exod 28:17"); #emph[four rows];, namely #emph[stones]; (for which 39:10 has #he[טוּרֵי אָ֑בֶן];); cf. #bref("2 Chr 4:13");, #bref("Lev 6:3"); (see, however, #xref(<p128d>)[§ 128#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];); #bref("2 Kgs 7:1"); #he[סְאָה סֹ֫לֶת]; #emph[a seah of fine flour];; cf. #bref("2 Kgs 7:6, 18");, #bref("Gen 18:6");, #bref("Exod 16:33");, #bref("Lev 5:11");, #bref("Ruth 2:17");, #bref("1 Kgs 16:24");, #bref("2 Kgs 5:23"); #he[כִּכְּרַ֫יִם כֶּ֫סֶף]; #emph[two talents]; of #emph[silver];;#footnote[On the anomalous form #he[כִּכְּרַ֫יִם]; (instead of #he[כִּכָּרַ֫יִם];; cf. #he[כִּכָּרָ֑יִם]; immediately before), see #xref(<p88b>)[§ 88#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];.]; cf. 5:17, #bref("Exod 39:17");, #bref("Ezek 22:18"); (if the text be right). With the #emph[material]; placed before the #emph[measure];, #bref("Exod 30:23"); f.—A #emph[period of time]; and its contents are placed in apposition #he[חֹ֫דֶשׁ יָמִים]; #emph[a month of days];, i.e. a month’s time\=for a whole month, #bref("Gen 29:14");, #bref("Num 11:20");, 21, cf. #bref("Deut 21:13");, #bref("2 Kgs 15:13");, and #he[שְׁנָתַ֫יִם יָמִים]; #emph[two years’ time];, i.e. two full years, #bref("Gen 41:1");, #bref("2 Sam 13:23");, #bref("14:28");, #bref("Jer 28:3, 11");, #bref("Dan 10:2f.");.

Finally, under this head may be included all the cases in which a numeral (regarded as a substantive) is followed by the object numbered in apposition, e.g. #he[שְׁלשָׁה בָנִים]; #emph[trias]; sc. #emph[filii];, #xref(<p97a>)[§ 97#h(0.18em)#emph[a];]; and #xref(<p134b>)[§ 134#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];.

#ml("e") <p131e> (#emph[d];) Collocation of the #emph[thing]; and the #emph[measure]; or #emph[extent, number];, etc., e.g. #bref("Num 9:20"); #he[יָמִים מִסְפָּר]; #emph[days];, (a small) #emph[number];, i.e. only a few days; #he[כֶּ֫סֶף מִשְׁנֶה]; #emph[money, repetition];, i.e. twice as much money, #bref("Gen 43:12"); (unless #he[כֶּ֫סֶף]; be constr. st.); #he[מַ֫יִם בִּרְכָּ֑יִם]; #emph[water]; which was of the measure of the #emph[knees];, which reached to the knees, #bref("Ezek 47:4"); (also #he[מֵי מָתְנָ֑יִם]; #emph[water that was to the loins];, in the same verse). This likewise includes the cases in which a noun is followed in apposition by a numeral (see #xref(<p134c>)[§ 134#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];) or an adverb, originally conceived as a substantive, e.g. #bref("Neh 2:12"); #he[אֲנָשִׁים מְעַט]; #emph[men, a few];, i.e. some few men; #bref("1 Kgs 5:9"); #he[תְּבוּנָה הַרְבֵּה]; #emph[understanding, much\-making];, i.e. much understanding, unless #he[הַרְבֵּה]; is to be taken as an adverb with #he[וַיִּתֵּן];, as in #bref("2 Sam 8:8"); with #he[לָקַח];. #origpage("425") <orig-425>;

#ml("f") <p131f> (#emph[e];) Collocation of the #emph[thing]; and its #emph[name];, e.g. #he[בְּהַֽרֲרָם שֵׁעִיר]; #emph[in their mountainous district, Seir]; (perhaps only a later gloss), #bref("Gen 14:6");; #he[הָאָ֫רֶץ כְּנָ֑עַן]; #emph[the land Canaan]; (#he[כנען]; probably only a later gloss), #bref("Num 34:2");; cf. #bref("Ezra 9:1");, #bref("1 Chr 5:9"); (see under #emph[g]; below).—For examples of nouns in the construct state before a noun in apposition, see #xref(<p130e>)[§ 130#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];.

#ml("g") <p131g> Rem. 1. Only in certain combinations does the noun of nearer definition come first, e.g. #he[הַמֶּ֫לֶךְ דָּוִד];, #he[הַמֶּ֫לֶךְ שְׁלֹמֹה]; #emph[king David, king Solomon]; (less frequently #he[דָּוִד הַמֶּ֫לֶךְ]; as in #bref("2 Sam 13:39");, #bref("1 Kgs 2:17");, #bref("12:2");, #bref("2 Kgs 8:29");, #bref("9:15");, and in late Hebrew, #bref("Hag 1:1");, 15 \[cf. the Aramaic order #he[דריוש מלבא];\], and often in Chron.).—A chiasmus occurs in #bref("Isa 45:4");, the name standing after the defining noun in the first part of the verse, and before it in the parallel clause.

#ml("h") <p131h> 2. When the #emph[nota accusativi]; (#he[אֵת];, #he[אֶת־];) or a preposition precedes the first substantive, it #emph[may]; be repeated before the noun in apposition, e.g. #bref("Gen 4:2");, #bref("22:2");, #bref("24:4");, #bref("47:29");, #bref("Isa 66:21");; this usually occurs when the nearer definition precedes a #emph[proper name];. As a rule, however, the repetition does not take place (#bref("Deut 18:1");, #bref("Jer 33:18");, #bref("1 Sam 2:14");). A noun in apposition is made determinate, even after a noun with a prefix, in the ordinary way, e.g. #bref("2 Chr 12:13"); #he[בִּירֽוּשָׁלַ֫יִם הָעִיר]; #emph[in Jerusalem, the city which];, etc.#footnote[In #bref("1 Kgs 11:8"); participles after #he[לְכָל־נָשָׁיו];, as in #bref("2 Kgs 10:6"); after #he[אֶת־גְּדֹלֵי הָעִיר];, in 19:2 after a determinate accusative, and in #bref("Hag 1:4"); after #he[בְּבָֽתֵּיכֶם];, are used #emph[without]; the article; these, however, are probably to be explained not as in apposition, but according to #xref(<p118p>)[§ 118#h(0.18em)#emph[p];];.];

#ml("i") <p131i> 3. Sometimes a second adjective is used in apposition to a preceding adjective, in order to modify in some way the meaning of the first, e.g. #bref("Lev 13:19"); #he[בַּהֶ֫רֶת לְבָנָה אֲדַמְדָּ֑מֶת]; #emph[a white\-reddish]; (light red) #emph[bright spot];.

#ml("k") <p131k> 4. #emph[Permutation]; is to be regarded as a variety of apposition. It is not complementary like apposition proper (see #emph[a]; above), but rather #emph[defines]; the preceding substantive (or pronoun, see below), in order to prevent any possible misunderstanding. This includes cases like #bref("Gen 9:4"); #emph[with the life thereof]; (which is) #emph[the blood thereof];; #bref("Exod 22:30");, #bref("Deut 2:26");, #bref("1 Sam 7:9");, #bref("2 Kgs 3:4"); #emph[an hundred thousand rams, the wool];, i.e. the wool of the rams; #bref("Jer 25:15"); #emph[this cup of the wine];, that is #emph[of fury]; (but #he[הַֽחֵמָה]; is probably a gloss); #bref("Isa 42:25"); #emph[he poured upon him fury];, namely #emph[his anger];;#footnote[But #he[מַ֫יִם]; #bref("Gen 6:17"); (cf. 7:6) is to be regarded as a later gloss upon the archaic #he[מַבּוּל];.]; but especially the examples in which such a permutative is added to a preceding pronoun, namely —

#ml("l") <p131l> (#emph[a];) To a separate pronoun, e.g. #bref("Exod 7:11");; with regard to the vocative, cf. #xref(<p126f>)[§ 126#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];.

#ml("m") <p131m> (#emph[b];) To an accusative suffix, e.g. #bref("Exod 2:6"); #emph[she saw him, the child]; (unless #he[אֶת־הַיּ׳]; be a later gloss); #bref("Exod 35:5");, #bref("Lev 13:57");#super[ #emph[b];];, #bref("1 Kgs 19:21"); (where, indeed, #he[הַבָּשָׂר]; appears to be a late gloss); 21:13, #bref("2 Kgs 16:15"); #emph[K#super[e];th.];, #bref("Jer 9:14");, #bref("31:2");, #bref("Ezek 3:21");, #bref("Eccl 2:21"); (according to Delitzsch rather a double accusative).#footnote[For #he[וַיְשַׁנּוֹ]; #bref("1 Sam 21:14"); either’ #he[וַיְשַׁנֶּה]; is to be read or the #emph[K#super[e];thîbh]; is to be explained according to #xref(<p75b>)[§ 75#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];, note. Also #he[יִלְכְּדֻנוֹ]; #bref("Prov 5:22"); has hardly preserved the correct form.];

#ml("n") <p131n> (#emph[c];) To a noun\-suffix, e.g. #bref("Ezek 10:3"); #he[בְּבֹאוֹ הָאִישׁ]; #emph[when he went in, the man];; 42:14; cf. #bref("Prov 13:4"); (?), #bref("Ezek 3:12");; so also after a preposition with suffix, e.g. #bref("Eccl 4:10"); #he[אִי לוֹ הָֽאֶחָד]; #emph[woe to him, the one alone];; with a repetition of the preposition, #bref("Num 32:33");, #bref("Josh 1:2"); #he[לָהֶם לִבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל]; #emph[to them, to the children of Israel];; #bref("Judg 21:7");, #bref("Jer 51:56");, #origpage("426") <orig-426>;#bref("Ezek 42:5"); (?), #bref("Dan 11:11");, #bref("1 Chr 4:42");, #bref("2 Chr 26:14");.#footnote[But in #bref("Isa 17:6"); we should certainly divide the words differently and read #he[בִּסְעִפֵי הַפֹּֽרִיָּה];, in #bref("Jer 48:44"); read #he[אֵלֶּה]; for #he[אֵלֶ֫יהָ];, and in #bref("Prov 14:13"); #he[אַֽחֲרִית הַשִּׂמְחָה];; in #bref("Gen 2:19"); #he[נֶ֫פֶשׁ חַיָּה]; is a late gloss upon #he[לוֹ];, and in #bref("Ezek 41:25"); #he[אֶל־דַּלְתוֹת הַֽהֵיכָל]; a gloss on #he[אֲלֵיהֶן];.];—Cf. finally, #bref("Song 3:7");, where the suffix precedes the genitive periphrastically expressed by #he[שֶׁלּ׳];, as in #bref("Ezek 9:1");, where the genitive is expressed by #he[לְ];.#footnote[Some of the examples given above are textually (or exegetically) doubtful, while in the case of others, especially those from the later Books, we cannot help asking whether such a prolepsis of the genitive by means of a suffix (as e.g. #bref("Ezek 10:3");) is not due to the influence of Aramaic, in which it is the customary idiom; cf. Kautzsch’s #emph[Gramm. des Biblisch\-Aram.];, § 81 #emph[e]; and § 88.];

#ml("o") <p131o> Of a different kind are the cases in which the permutative with its proper suffix follows as a kind of correction of the preceding suffix, e.g. #bref("Isa 29:23"); #emph[when he]; (or rather) #emph[his children see];, etc. (but #he[יְלָדָיו]; is clearly a gloss); cf. #bref("Ps 83:12");; in #bref("Job 29:3"); read #he[בַּֽהֲהִלּוֹ]; (infin. Hiph.) or at least its syncopated form #he[בַּהִלּוֹ];.

#ml("p") <p131p> 5. Cases of apposition in a #emph[wider sense]; are those in which the nearer definition added to the noun was originally regarded as an #emph[adverbial accusative];; on its use with the verb and on the relative correctness of speaking of such an accusative in Hebrew, cf. #xref(<p118a>)[§ 118#h(0.18em)#emph[a];]; and #emph[m];. Owing to the lack of case\-endings, indeed, it is in many instances only by analogies elsewhere (especially in Arabic) that we can decide whether the case is one of apposition in the narrower or in the wider sense; in other instances this must remain quite uncertain. However, the following are probably cases of apposition in the wider sense:—

#ml("q") <p131q> (#emph[a];) Such phrases as #he[מִשְׁנֶה כֶ֫סֶף]; #emph[a double amount in money];, #bref("Gen 43:15");; cf. #bref("Jer 17:18");; #bref("1 Sam 17:5"); #emph[five thousand shekels in brass];, but this might also be taken (as in #emph[d];) #emph[shekels]; which were #emph[brass];; certainly such cases as #bref("Job 15:10"); #emph[older than thy father in days];, and the expression of the superlative by means of #he[מְאֹד]; (originally a substantive), e.g. #he[טוֹב מְאֹד]; #emph[very good];, #bref("Gen 1:31"); (cf. also #bref("Eccl 7:16"); #he[צַדִּיק הַרְבֵּה]; #emph[righteous over much];), and the very frequent #he[הַרְבֵּה מְאֹד]; prop. #emph[a much\-making exceedingly];, i.e. #emph[exceedingly great];, #bref("Gen 15:1");, #bref("41:49");, also #bref("Prov 23:29"); #he[פְּצָעִים חִנָּם]; #emph[wounds without cause];,#footnote[In #bref("Ps 69:5"); #he[חִנָּם]; (like #he[שֶׁ֫קֶר]; #emph[in a false way, falsely];, #bref("Ps 35:19"); and 38:20) is used as an #emph[adverbial accusative]; with a participle; cf. #xref(<p118q>)[§ 118#h(0.18em)#emph[q];];.]; perhaps also #bref("Gen 34:25"); (#he[בֶּ֫טַח];).

#ml("r") <p131r> (#emph[b];) A few examples, in which an epexegetical substantive is added to a substantive with a suffix; thus, #bref("Ezek 16:27"); #he[מִדַּרְכֵּךְ זִמָּה]; #emph[of thy conduct in lewdness]; (but it is also possible to explain it (as in #emph[c];) #emph[of thy conduct];, which is #emph[lewdness];); cf. #bref("Ezek 24:13");, #bref("2 Sam 22:33"); #he[מָֽעוּזִּי חָ֑יִל]; #emph[my fortress in strength];, i.e. my strong fortress (cf., however, #bref("Ps 18:33");); #bref("Hab 3:8");, #bref("Ps 71:7");. While even in these examples the deviation from the ordinary usage of the language (cf. #xref(<p135n>)[§ 135#h(0.18em)#emph[n];];) is strange, it is much more so in #he[חֲבֹֽלָתוֹ חוֹב]; #bref("Ezek 18:7");, i.e. according to the context #emph[his pledge]; for #emph[a debt];; #bref("Ezra 2:62"); #he[כְּתָבָם הַמִּתְיַֽחֲשִׂים];, i.e. #emph[their register];, namely of #emph[those that were reckoned by genealogy]; (but perhaps #he[הַמִּתְי׳]; is in apposition to the suffix in #he[כְּתָבָם];), also the curious combinations (mentioned in #xref(<p128d>)[§ 128#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];) of #he[בְּרִיתִי]; with a proper name (#bref("Lev 26:42");), and in #bref("Jer 33:20"); with #he[הַיּוֹם];.#footnote[But in #bref("Num 25:12"); #he[שָׁלוֹם]; may also be explained, according to #emph[c];, as really in apposition. Cf. on the whole question Delitzsch, #emph[Psalmen];, 4th ed., p. 203, note 1.]; #origpage("427") <orig-427>;

#ml("s") <p131s> 6. In #bref("Deut 33:4"); (#he[מֽוֹרָשָׁה];, perhaps #he[מוֹר׳ לִקְהִלַּת]; is to be read), 33:27 (#he[מְעֹנָה];), #bref("Judg 7:8"); (#he[צֵדָה];), the absolute state appears to be used instead of the construct to govern a following logical genitive; this, however, cannot be explained either as a special kind of apposition, or (with Hitzig) as a peculiarity of the dialect of Northern Palestine, but is merely a textual corruption. On the other hand, in #bref("Job 31:11"); #he[עָוֹן]; is evidently intended to combine the readings #he[עֲוֹן פְּלִילִים]; and #he[עָוֹן פְּלִילִי]; (as in verse 28).—The remarkable combination #he[אֱלֹהִים צְבָאוֹת]; in #bref("Ps 80:8");, 15 is due to the fact that in ψψ 42–83 #he[אֱלֹהִים]; has almost throughout been subsequently substituted by some redactor for the divine name #he[יהוה];; on #he[יהוה צְבָאוֹת]; cf. #xref(<p125h>)[§ 125#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];. In #bref("Ps 59:6");, #bref("80:5");, 20, and 84:9 #he[יהוה]; has been reinstated in the text before #he[אֱלֹהִים צְבָאוֹת];.#footnote[Without this assumption it would be inconceivable that #he[יהוה אֱלֹהֵי צְבָאוֹת]; should not have been written; that the author of these Psalms regarded #he[צְבָאוֹת]; already as an independent name of God (so Gesenius and Olshausen) is out of the question.];

#ml("t") <p131t> 7. Lastly, the nearer definition (qualification) of a noun may be effected by means of a preposition (either with a suffix or with an independent noun), but must then be distinguished from the cases in which the preposition is dependent on a verb or verbal idea, e.g. #bref("Gen 3:6"); #emph[and she gave also]; #he[לְאִישָׁהּ עִמָּהּ]; #emph[unto her husband with her]; (\= #emph[her husband who was with her];); in #bref("Gen 9:16"); (#emph[that I may remember the everlasting covenant between God and every living creature of all flesh];) and other places, the qualification of the noun is itself also qualified.

#sec("132", [Connection of the Substantive with the Adjective], note: [#footnote[On the expression of attributive ideas by substantives, cf. above, #xref(<p127h>)[§ 127#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];, and #xref(<p128o>)[§ 128#h(0.18em)#emph[o];];, with the note; #xref(<p135n>)[§ 135#h(0.18em)#emph[n];]; and #xref(<p141c>)[§ 141#h(0.18em)#emph[c];]; (substantives for adjectives as predicates of noun\-clauses) and #xref(<p152u>)[§ 152#h(0.18em)#emph[u];]; (periphrases for negative qualities). On the use of the feminine of adjectives (and participles) to express abstract ideas, see #xref(<p122q>)[§ 122#h(0.18em)#emph[q];];. It remains to mention further the employment (mostly only in poetry) of certain epithets in place of the substantives to which the quality in question belongs; e.g. #he[אָבִיר]; #emph[the strong one];, i.e. #emph[God];; #he[אַבִּיר]; #emph[the strong one];, i.e. #emph[the bull]; (in #bref("Jer 8:16");, etc., #emph[the horse];); #he[קַל]; #emph[swift];\=#emph[the runner]; (of the #emph[horse];, #bref("Isa 30:16");); #he[לְבָנָה]; #emph[alba];, i.e. #emph[luna];; #he[פֹּֽרִיָּה]; (#emph[fructifera];) #emph[a fruitful tree];, #bref("Isa 17:6"); (so #he[פֹּרָת]; #bref("Gen 49:22");); #he[רֹבֵץ]; #emph[a croucher];, i.e. #emph[a crouching beast of prey];, #bref("Gen 4:7");. Cf. also #he[רֹזֵן]; (#emph[gravis, augustus];) and #he[נָשִׂיא]; (#emph[elatus]; ?), i.e. #emph[a prince];. The use of adjectives and participles for substantives is much more extensive in Arabic. In Greek and Latin poetical language cf. such examples as ὑγρή \= #emph[the sea];; #emph[merum]; for #emph[vinum];, etc.];]) <s132>

#ml("a") <p132a> #strong[1.]; The adjective (like the participle used adjectivally), which serves as an #emph[attribute]; of a substantive, stands #emph[after]; the substantive, and agrees with it in #emph[gender]; and #emph[number];, e.g. #he[אִישׁ גָּדוֹל]; #emph[a great man];, #he[אִשָּׁה יָפָה]; #emph[a beautiful woman];. If the substantive is immediately connected with a genitive, the attribute follows the latter, since, according to § 89 and #xref(<p128a>)[§ 128#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];, the construct state and the genitive belonging to it are inseparably united, e.g. #bref("Esth 8:15"); #he[עֲטֶ֫רֶת זָהָב גְּדוֹלָה]; #emph[a great crown of gold];.— On the attribute when attached to a #emph[determinate]; substantive, see above, #xref(<p126u>)[§ 126#h(0.18em)#emph[u];];. #origpage("428") <orig-428>;

#ml("b") <p132b> Rem. 1. Where an adjectival attribute appears to stand #emph[before]; its substantive (according to the usual explanation, for the sake of special emphasis) the relation is really appositional in character; thus, #bref("Isa 10:30"); #he[עֲנִיָּה עֲנָתוֹת]; #emph[O thou poor one, Anathoth!]; (but probably #he[עֲנִ֫יהָ]; #emph[answer her];, is to be read); cf. 23:12, 53:11 (#emph[a righteous man, my servant];; but in 28:21 #he[זָר]; and #he[נָכְרִי]; are #emph[predicates]; preceding the substantives); #bref("Jer 3:6");, 10 f., #bref("Ps 18:4"); #emph[him who is worthy to be praised will I call upon];, #emph[the Lord];; 92:12 (apposition after participles).—But #he[רַבִּים]; and #he[רַבּוֹת]; #emph[many];, are sometimes placed, like numerals, before the substantive, #bref("Jer 16:16");, #bref("Neh 9:28"); (in #bref("Ps 145:7"); #he[רַב]; is a #emph[subst. regens];, in 89:51 the text is corrupt); an appositional relation can scarcely be intended in these instances.

#ml("c") <p132c> 2. In a few expressions (mostly poetic) the adjective appears not as an attribute #emph[after]; the substantive, but in the construct state governing it; so in the singular, #bref("Exod 15:16"); (unless #he[גֹּדֶל]; should be read); #bref("1 Sam 16:7"); (#emph[the height of his stature];); in the plural, #bref("1 Sam 17:40"); #he[חַלֻּקֵי אֲבָנִים]; #emph[smooth ones of]; (among) #emph[stones];, i.e. #emph[smooth stones];; #bref("Isa 35:9");, #bref("Ezek 7:24");, #bref("Ps 46:5");, and with a following collective instead of a plural, e.g. #bref("Isa 29:19"); #he[אֶבְיוֹנֵי אָדָם]; #emph[the poor among men];, i.e. #emph[poor men];; #bref("Jer 49:20");, #bref("Zech 11:7");; cf. in Latin #emph[canum degeneres];. However, in almost all these cases the adjective which is made into a #emph[regens]; is strongly emphatic, and is frequently equivalent to a superlative (see below, #xref(<p133g>)[§ 133#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];).

#ml("d") <p132d> 3. When #emph[two]; adjectives follow a feminine, sometimes only that standing next to the noun takes the feminine termination, e.g. #bref("1 Kgs 19:11"); #he[רוּחַ גְּדֹלָה וְחָזָק וגו׳]; (but read #he[גָּדוֹל];); #bref("1 Sam 15:9"); (but cf. #xref(<p75y>)[§ 75#h(0.18em)#emph[y];];); #bref("Jer 20:9");, #bref("Ps 63:2");. A similar dislike of the feminine form may also be observed in the case of verbal predicates referring to feminine subjects, cf. #xref(<p145p>)[§ 145#h(0.18em)#emph[p];]; and #emph[t];.

When an attribute qualifies several substantives of different genders, it agrees with the masculine, as being the #emph[prior gender]; (cf. #xref(<p146d>)[§ 146#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];), e.g. #bref("Neh 9:13"); #he[חֻקִּים וּמִצְוֹת טוֹבִים];; #bref("Jer 34:9");, #bref("Zech 8:5");.

When #emph[three]; attributes follow a substantive, the first two may stand without a conjunction, and the last be attached by #emph[wāw copulative];, cf. #bref("Zech 1:8");.

#ml("e") <p132e> 4. After feminines plural ending in #he[־ִים]; (#xref(<p87p>)[§ 87#h(0.18em)#emph[p];];) the adjectival attribute (in accordance with the fundamental rule stated above, under #emph[a];) takes the ending #he[וֹת];, e.g. #bref("Isa 10:14"); #he[בֵּיצִים עֲזֻבוֹת]; #emph[forsaken eggs];; #bref("Gen 32:16");. For a strange exception see #bref("Jer 29:17"); (differently in 24:2).

#ml("f") <p132f> 5. With regard to #emph[number]; it is to be remarked that—

(#emph[a];) Substantives in the #emph[dual]; are followed by adjectives (or participles) in the #emph[plural];, e.g. #bref("Ps 18:28"); (#bref("Prov 6:17");) #he[עֵינַ֫יִם רָמוֹת]; #emph[haughty eyes];; #bref("Isa 35:3");, #bref("Job 4:3"); f., cf. #xref(<p88a>)[§ 88#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];.

#ml("g") <p132g> (#emph[b];) #emph[Collective]; ideas are not infrequently joined with the #emph[plural]; of the adjective or participle (#emph[constructio ad sensum];); thus, e.g. #he[צֹאן]; #emph[sheep]; \[with #emph[fem. plur.];\], #bref("Gen 30:43");, #bref("1 Sam 25:18");; #he[עַם];\=#emph[men];, #bref("1 Sam 13:15");, #bref("Isa 9:1");; #he[כָּל־יִשְׂרָאֵל];\=#emph[all the Israelites];, #bref("1 Sam 2:14");; #he[גָּלוּת];\=#emph[the exiles];, #bref("Jer 28:4");; cf. also #he[נֶ֫פֶשׁ שְׁנָ֫יִם]; #emph[two souls];, #bref("Gen 46:27");.#footnote[But it is impossible to take #he[תְּמִימִם]; in #bref("Ezek 46:6"); as an attribute of #he[בָּקָר];; probably it is a correction intended to harmonize the passage with #bref("Num 28:11"); where #emph[two]; young bullocks are required.]; Cf. similar phenomena in the connection of collectives with plural predicates in #xref(<p145c>)[§ 145#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];.

#ml("h") <p132h> (#emph[c];) The #emph[pluralis excellentiae]; or #emph[pluralis maiestatis]; is joined, as a rule, to the singular of the attribute, e.g. #bref("Ps 7:10"); #he[אֱלֹהִים צַדִּיק];; #bref("2 Kgs 19:4");, 16 (\=#bref("Isa 37:4");, 17); #bref("Isa 19:4");; but cf. #he[אֱלֹהִים חַיִּים];#footnote[Cf. #bref("1 Sam 28:13");, where #he[אֱלֹהִים]; (in the sense of #emph[a spirit];) is followed by #he[עֹלִים]; as a second accusative; conversely in #bref("1 Sam 19:13");, 16, a singular suffix refers back to #he[תְּרָפִים]; #emph[household god]; (but not so in #bref("Gen 31:34");), as in #bref("Ps 46:4"); to the plural of amplification #he[יַמִּים]; #emph[sea];. On the other hand, it is very doubtful whether #he[רַבָּה]; #bref("Ps 78:15"); is to be regarded as an attribute of #he[תְּהֹמוֹת]; and not rather as the adverb, #emph[abundantly];.]; #bref("Deut 5:23");, #bref("1 Sam 17:26");, 36, #bref("Jer 10:10");, #bref("23:36");, perhaps also #origpage("429") <orig-429>;#bref("Exod 20:3"); #he[אֱלֹהִים אֲחֵרִים];\= #emph[another god];, and #bref("Josh 24:19"); #he[אֱלֹהִים קְדֹשִׁים]; (but cf. above, #xref(<p124g>)[§ 124#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];–#xref(<p124k>)[#emph[k];];). On the other hand, #bref("1 Sam 4:8"); is to be explained as having been said by the Philistines, who supposed that the Israelites had several gods. On the connection of #he[אֱלֹהִים]; with a plural predicate, see #xref(<p145i>)[§ 145#h(0.18em)#emph[i];];.

#strong[2.]; On the adjective (in the construct state) governing a following genitive, see #xref(<p128x>)[§ 128#h(0.18em)#emph[x];];; for the participle in the same construction, see #xref(<p116f>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];–#emph[l];.

#sec("133", [The Comparison of Adjectives. (Periphrastic Expression of the Comparative and Superlative)], note: []) <s133>

#align(center)[A. Wünsche, ‘Der Komparativ im Hebr. im Lichte der arab. Gramm.,’ in #emph[Vierteljahrsschrift für Bibelkunde];, 1904, p. 398 ff.];

#ml("a") <p133a> #strong[1.]; Hebrew possesses no special forms either for the comparative or superlative of the adjective.#footnote[There is in Arabic a special form of the adjective (the #emph[elative];) for the comparative and superlative, which in Hebrew would have the form #he[אַקְטָל];. Instances of it, perhaps, are #he[אַכְזָר]; #emph[daring, cruel];, #he[אַכְזָב]; #emph[deceptive]; (of a brook drying up), and its opposite #he[אֵיתָן]; (contracted from #emph[ʾaitan];) #emph[constantly flowing];, #emph[perennis];. These forms are, however, used without any perceptible emphasis, and cannot be regarded as more than isolated relics of an elative formation which has become obsolete, much as the Latin comparative disappears in Italian, and still more so in French, and is supplanted by the circumlocution with #emph[più];, #emph[plus];.]; In order to express a comparative, the person or thing which is to be represented as excelled in some particular quality is attached to the attributive word by the preposition #he[מִן־]; (#he[מִ‍·];), e.g. #bref("1 Sam 9:2"); #he[גָּבֹהַּ מִכָּל־הָעָם]; #emph[higher than any of the people];. The fundamental idea evidently is, #emph[tall away from all the people]; (beyond all the people); cf. #bref("Judg 14:18"); #he[מַה־מָּתוֹק מִדְּבַשׁ וּמֶה עַז מֵֽאֲרִי]; #emph[what is sweeter than honey? and what is stronger than a lion?]; #bref("Ezek 28:3");, #bref("Amos 6:2");. Frequently an infinitive appears as the object of the comparison, e.g. #bref("Gen 29:19"); #emph[it is better that I give her to thee, than that I should give her];, etc.; #bref("Exod 14:12");, #bref("Ps 118:8");f.#footnote[In #bref("Judg 11:25"); the adjective is specially intensified by repetition, #emph[art thou so much better than Balak?]; It would also be possible, however, to translate #emph[art thou really better ...?];];

#ml("b") <p133b> Rem. 1. This use of #he[מִן־]; is also very common when the attributive idea is represented by an intransitive verb, e.g. #bref("1 Sam 10:23"); #he[וַיִּגְבַּהּ מִכָּל־הָעָם]; #emph[and he was higher than any of the people];; #bref("Nah 3:8");, #bref("Job 7:6");. Elsewhere, especially after transitive verbs, #he[מִן־]; rather represents (on its different senses see #xref(<p119v>)[§ 119#h(0.18em)#emph[v];];–#xref(<p119z>)[#emph[z];];) the idea #origpage("430") <orig-430>;of a #emph[separation, distinction]; or #emph[superiority]; of one person or thing #emph[from]; or #emph[over]; others.#footnote[Cf. the Latin ablative with the comparative; also the etymology of such words as #emph[eximius, egregius];, and the Homeric ἐκ πάντων μάλιστα, Il. 4, 96; ἐκ πασέων, 18, 431.]; This is evident in such cases as #he[בָּחַר מִן־]; #emph[to choose]; something (to prefer it) #emph[before]; something else, e.g. #bref("Job 7:15");, cf. #bref("Deut 14:2"); (also #he[יִתְרוֹן... מִן־]; #emph[the excellence of... over...];, #bref("Eccl 2:13");); it is also seen in examples like #bref("Gen 37:3"); #he[וְיִשְׂרָאֵל אָהַב אֶת־יוֹסֵף מִכָּל־בָּנָיו]; #emph[now Israel loved Joseph more than all his]; (other) #emph[children];; 29:30, #bref("1 Sam 2:29");, #bref("Hos 6:6");.#footnote[On the other hand, the phrase #he[צָדַק מִן־]; expresses not a comparison, but only a relation existing between one person and another; thus, in #bref("Gen 38:26"); #he[צָֽדְקָה מִמֶּ֫נִּי]; means, #emph[she is in the right as against me];; cf. #bref("Ps 139:12");, #bref("Job 4:17");, #bref("32:2");.—In #bref("Prov 17:12"); #emph[rather]; (to meet with so and so) #emph[than];... is expressed by #he[וְאַל־]; before the second member.];

#ml("c") <p133c> 2. A somewhat different idea underlies the use of #he[מִן־]; after adjectives, or intransitive verbs possessing an attributive sense, when the thought to be expressed is that the quality is #emph[too little]; or #emph[too much]; in force for the attainment of a particular aim or object, e.g. #bref("Isa 7:13"); #he[הַמְעַט מִכֶּם]; #emph[is it a small thing]; (i.e. too little) #emph[for you to...?]; #bref("Job 15:11");; after an intransitive verb, e.g. #bref("Gen 32:11"); #emph[I am too insignificant]; (#he[קָטֹ֫נְתִּי];) #emph[for all the mercies]; (I am not worthy of...), etc.; cf. also the expressions #he[כָּבֵד מִן־]; #emph[to be too heavy for one];, #bref("Exod 18:18");, #bref("Num 11:14");, #bref("Ps 38:5");; #he[קָשָׁה מִן־]; #emph[to be too hard for one];, #bref("Deut 1:17");; #he[מָעַט מִן־]; #emph[to be too few for something];, #bref("Exod 12:4");; #he[גָּבַר מִן־]; #emph[to be too strong for one];, #bref("Ps 65:4");; #he[עָצַם מִן־]; #emph[to be too mighty for one];, #bref("Gen 26:16");; #he[רוּם מִן־]; #emph[to be too high for one];, #bref("Ps 61:3");; #he[צַר מִן־]; #emph[to be too narrow for one];, #bref("Isa 49:19");; #he[קָצַר מִן־]; #emph[to be too short for something];, #bref("Isa 50:2");, and very frequently #he[נִפְלָא מִן־]; #emph[to be too wonderful for one]; (and, consequently, inconceivable or unattainable), #bref("Gen 18:14");, #bref("Deut 17:8");, #bref("30:11");, #bref("Jer 37:17");, #bref("Prov 30:18");; in #bref("Ps 139:6"); #he[פְּלִיאָה]; in the same sense is followed by #he[מִן];.—This use is especially seen in the numerous instances in which the attribute is followed by #he[מִן־]; with an infinitive, e.g. #bref("1 Kgs 8:64"); #emph[the brazen altar... was]; #he[קָטֹן מֵֽהָכִיל]; #emph[too little to receive]; (to be able to receive) #emph[the burnt offering];, cf. #bref("Gen 4:13");, #bref("36:7"); #emph[too great for them to dwell together];; after verbs, e.g. #bref("Exod 12:4");, #bref("Isa 28:20");, #bref("Ps 40:6");. Finally, cf. #he[רַב לָכֶם מִן־];, followed by the infinitive, #emph[it is enough]; (prop. #emph[too much];) #emph[for you to...];, meaning #emph[ye have... long enough];, #bref("1 Kgs 12:28");; cf. #bref("Exod 9:28"); and #bref("Ezek 44:6"); (#he[מִן־]; followed by a substantive).#footnote[Cf. also #bref("2 Kgs 4:3");, where the idea of #emph[doing something too little]; is paraphrased by the Hiph. #he[הִמְעִיט]; \= #emph[do not too little];, sc. #he[לִשְׁאֹל]; #emph[in borrowing]; empty vessels.];

#ml("d") <p133d> In all these instances #he[מִן־]; expresses either the #emph[removal]; of a thing #emph[from]; a person, or the #emph[severance]; of the person from some aim or object; cf. also the expression #he[לֹֽא־יִבָּצֵר מֵהֶם כֹּל וגו׳]; #emph[nothing will be unattainable for them]; (prop. there shall not be cut off from them anything which, etc.), #bref("Gen 11:6");, #bref("Job 42:3");.

#ml("e") <p133e> 3. The attributive idea, on which #he[מִן־]; logically depends, must sometimes, in consequence of a pregnant use of the #he[מִן־]; (see the analogous examples in #xref(<p119ff>)[§ 119#h(0.18em)#emph[ff];];), be supplied from the context, e.g. #bref("Isa 10:10"); #he[וּפְסִֽלֵיהֶם מִירֽוּשָׁלַ֫יִם]; #emph[whose graven images]; were more numerous #emph[than]; those at #emph[Jerusalem];, etc.;#footnote[With this #emph[comparatio decurtata];, cf. the still bolder pregnant construction in #bref("Ps 4:8");, #he[מֵעֵת]; #emph[greater gladness than at the time];, etc.]; #bref("Mic 7:4"); worse #emph[than a thorn hedge];; #bref("Ps 62:10"); lighter #emph[than a breath];; #bref("Job 11:17"); clearer #emph[than the noonday];; #bref("Eccl 4:17"); better #emph[than];, etc. #origpage("431") <orig-431>;

#ml("f") <p133f> #strong[2.]; The correlative comparatives #emph[greater—less]; (#emph[older—younger];) are expressed by the simple adjective with the article (#emph[the great];, equivalent to #emph[the greater];, etc.); #bref("Gen 1:16");, #bref("19:31, 34");, #bref("27:15");, #bref("29:16, 18, 26");.

#ml("g") <p133g> #strong[3.]; To express the #emph[superlative]; it is also sufficient (see above, #emph[f];) to make the adjective determinate, either by means of the article or a following partitive genitive (or suffix); in this case the article or genitive indicates that the attribute in question belongs especially to one or more definite individuals;#footnote[Cf. also #he[עֶלְיוֹן]; #emph[the one above];, i.e. #emph[the Most High];.]; e.g. #bref("1 Sam 9:21"); #he[הַצְּעִרָה]; #emph[the least];; 16:11 #he[הַקָּטָן]; #emph[the little one];, i.e. the #emph[youngest]; of eight sons; 17:14 #emph[David was]; #he[הַקָּטָן]; #emph[the youngest, and the three great];, i.e. elder, etc.; #bref("Gen 42:13");, #bref("44:2");, #bref("Song 1:8");.—So also with a qualifying adjective, e.g. #bref("Gen 9:24"); #he[בְּנוֹ הַקָּטָן]; #emph[his youngest son];; cf. #bref("Josh 14:15");; also with a following genitive, #bref("2 Chr 21:17"); #he[קְטֹן בָּנָיו]; #emph[the youngest of his sons];: #bref("Prov 30:24"); #emph[the least upon the earth];; with suffix, #bref("Mic 7:4"); #he[טוֹבָם]; #emph[their good one];, i.e. the best of them; #bref("Jonah 3:5"); #he[מִגְּדוֹלָם וְעַד־קְטַנָּם]; #emph[from the greatest of them even to the least of them];; cf. the inverse order in #bref("Jer 6:13");, #bref("31:34");.

#ml("h") <p133h> Rem. 1. The above examples apply only to the most common #emph[relative]; attributes (#emph[great, small, good];), and to expressions which by usage easily came to be recognized as periphrases for the superlative. Other adjectives, however, when followed by a partitive genitive, also acquire the sense of a superlative; this appears from the context, e.g. #bref("Deut 33:19"); #emph[the most hidden treasures of the sand];; #bref("Judg 5:29"); #emph[the wisest among her ladies];; #bref("Isa 14:30");, #bref("19:11");, #bref("23:8"); f., 29:19, #bref("Jer 49:20");, #bref("Ezek 28:7");, #bref("Zech 11:7");, #bref("Ps 45:13");, #bref("Job 30:6"); (#emph[in the most horrible of valleys];), 41:22; probably also #bref("Ps 35:16");. On this government by the adjective generally, cf. #xref(<p132c>)[§ 132#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];.—Moreover, the combination of a substantive in the construct state with an adjective used substantivally (#xref(<p128w>)[§ 128#h(0.18em)#emph[w];];) sometimes serves as a periphrasis for the superlative, e.g. #bref("Isa 22:24"); #he[כֹּל כְּלֵי הַקָּטָן]; #emph[all the smallest vessels];. On #bref("Song 7:10"); see #xref(<p126x>)[§ 126#h(0.18em)#emph[x];];.

#ml("i") <p133i> 2. Other periphrases for the superlative are the use of a substantive in the construct state before the plural of the same word (which is naturally to be regarded as a partitive genitive; cf. our #emph[book of books];), e.g. #bref("Exod 26:33"); #he[קֹדֶשׁ הַקֳּדָשִׁים]; #emph[the most holy place];; #he[שִׁיר הַשִּׁירִים]; (#bref("Song 1:1");) #emph[the most excellent song];; cf. #bref("Gen 9:25"); (\= #emph[servus servorum, the lowest servant];); #bref("Num 3:32");, #bref("Deut 10:17"); (#bref("Ps 136:2");, 3)#footnote[#emph[God of gods, and Lord of lords];, just as the supreme god of the Babylonians is called #emph[bēl bēlī]; (Tiele, #emph[Compend. der Rel.\-Gesch.];, p. 87).];; #bref("1 Kgs 8:27");, #bref("Isa 34:10"); (cf. Gal 1:5, Rev 22:5); #bref("Jer 3:19");, #bref("Ezek 16:7");, #bref("26:7"); (#emph[king of kings];, of Nebuchadrezzar; cf. 1 Tim 6:15, Rev 17:14, 19:16, and another kind of periphrasis in #bref("Ps 95:3");); #bref("Eccl 1:2");. Similarly in #bref("Jer 6:28"); two participles are combined, and in #bref("Hos 10:15"); two substantives in the singular. Finally, the same object is attained by connecting one substantive in the construct state with another of the same stem (#he[שַׁבַּת שַׁבָּתוֹן]; #emph[a sabbath of solemn rest];, i.e. an obligatory day of rest, #bref("Exod 31:15");, etc.) or of the same meaning (e.g. #he[ח֫שֶׁךְ אֲפֵלָה]; #emph[a thick darkness];, #bref("Exod 10:22");).

#ml("k") <p133k> 3. The intensification of attributes by means of repetition belongs rather to rhetoric than to syntax, e.g. #bref("Eccl 7:24"); #he[עָמֹק עָמֹק]; #emph[exceeding deep];; #bref("1 Sam 2:3");, #bref("Prov 20:14");; the adjective is even used three times in #bref("Isa 6:3");.—Cf. the repetition of adverbs for the same purpose in #bref("Gen 7:19");, #bref("Num 14:7"); (#he[מְאֹד מְאֹד]; #emph[exceedingly];, also #he[בִּמְאֹד מְאֹד]; #bref("Exod 1:7");, etc.); #bref("Ezek 42:15");.—On the other hand, in #bref("Deut 28:43"); the repetition expresses #origpage("432") <orig-432>;a continuous progress, #emph[higher and higher... lower and lower];; in #bref("Deut 2:27"); (see #xref(<p123e>)[§ 123#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];) and 16:20 (#emph[nothing but justice];) the constancy of the action. Cf. #bref("Exod 23:30"); #he[מְעַט מְעַט]; #emph[little by little, very gradually];.#footnote[Adverbs of the same stem are connected in this way in #bref("Num 6:9");, #bref("Isa 29:5");, #bref("30:13");; of different stems in #bref("Isa 5:26"); and #bref("Joel 4:4");. In #bref("Num 12:2"); the particles #he[רַק אַךְ]; appear to be placed together for a similar purpose, equivalent to #emph[simply and solely];.];

#ml("l") <p133l> The repetition of substantives serves also as a periphrasis for the superlative in such cases as #he[לְדֹר דֹּר]; (#bref("Exod 3:15");) \= #emph[to the remotest generations];; cf. 17:16, #bref("Jer 6:14");, #bref("8:11"); (#emph[perfect peace];); #bref("Ezek 21:32"); (#he[עַוָּה]; three times);#footnote[Different in kind from the triple utterance of the same words in #bref("2 Sam 18:33");, #bref("Jer 7:4"); and 22:29, and the double exclamation in #bref("Jer 4:19"); and #bref("Lam 1:16"); (?).]; 35:7, #bref("Nah 1:2");; cf. also #bref("Hos 2:21"); f. and the emphatic combination of synonymous verbs in #bref("Isa 33:10");. Sometimes the completeness of an action or state is expressed by placing together two or even three substantives of the same stem and of similar sound, cf. #bref("Isa 22:5");, #bref("Ezek 6:14"); (33:28#super[ f.];, 35:3); 32:15, #bref("Nah 2:11");, #bref("Zeph 1:15"); (#bref("Job 30:3");, #bref("38:27");).

#sec("134", [Syntax of the Numerals], note: []) <s134>

Cf. the exhaustive statistics collected by Sven Herner, #emph[Syntax der Zahlwörter im A.T.];, Lund, 1893. E. König, ‘Zur Syntax der Zahlwörter im A.T.,’ #emph[AJSL];. xviii, 129 ff.

#ml("a") <p134a> #strong[1.]; The numerals from 2 to 10, as being originally abstract substantives,#footnote[Cf. #xref(<p97a>)[§ 97#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];, where it is shown that the masculine is the original form of the numerals (used for both genders), and that the feminine was afterwards differentiated and used with masc. nouns, primarily in the second decade and then in the first as well.]; may be connected with their substantives in three different ways. They may stand either—

(#emph[a];) In the construct state #emph[before]; the substantive (the object numbered being consequently in the genitive), e.g. #he[שְׁלשֶׁת יָמִים]; #emph[a triad of days];, i.e. three days; #he[שְׁנֵי הָֽאֲנָשִׁים]; #emph[the two men];; or

#ml("b") <p134b> (#emph[b];) In the absolute state #emph[before]; it (the object numbered being in apposition, #xref(<p131d>)[§ 131#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];), e.g. #he[שְׁלשָׁה בָנִים]; #emph[a triad];, namely #emph[sons];, i.e. three sons; #he[שְׁנַ֫יִם אֲנָשִׁים]; #emph[two men];; or

#ml("c") <p134c> (#emph[c];) In the absolute state (likewise in apposition) #emph[after]; the object numbered, e.g. #he[בָּנוֹת שָׁלוֹשׁ];. So especially in long lists, since in these the substantives naturally come first, e.g. #bref("Gen 32:15");. #bref("Num 7:17");, #bref("28:19");. Apart from such cases, the frequency of this order in the later Books is due to the fact that the character of the numeral tended more and more to become adjectival rather than substantival.#footnote[From Herner’s tables (op. cit., pp. 55–66) it appears, according to p. 68, that in the documents J, E, D of the Pentateuch, and in Jos 1–12, Judges, Samuel, Isaiah, Jeremiah, the Minor Prophets, Psalms, Megilloth, and Job, the numeral never, or very rarely, stands #emph[after]; its noun; in Kings and Ezekiel it stands several times #emph[after];; in the Priestly Code nearly always #emph[after];; in Chronicles, Ezra, Nehemiah, and Daniel, nearly as often #emph[after]; as #emph[before]; the noun. In #bref("Exod 28:10"); the Masora makes the numeral in the genitive follow the construct state of the substantive numbered; we should, however, read #he[וְאֶת־שֵׁמוֹת הַשִּׁשָּׁה];; for the omission of the article before #he[שׁ׳];, cf. #xref(<p126w>)[§ 126#h(0.18em)#emph[w];];.]; #origpage("433") <orig-433>;

#ml("d") <p134d> Rem. In #bref("Lev 24:22"); #he[אֶחָד]; follows the construct state #he[מִשְׁפַּט];, but here as in #bref("Num 15:16"); #he[מִשְׁפָּט]; should be read. In #bref("Gen 42:19"); #he[אֶחָד]; is in apposition to a substantive with a suffix (\= #emph[one of you brethren];; but verse 33 #emph[the one of you brethren];). In #bref("Num 31:28"); #he[אֶחָד]; precedes the substantive in the Aramaic manner (\= #emph[one each];).—For #he[מֵאָֽה־שָׁנָה]; (#bref("Gen 17:17");, etc.) we find regularly in the Priestly Code (except in #bref("Gen 17:17");, #bref("23:1");) #he[מְאַת שָׁנָה]; (#bref("Gen 5:3");, etc.) #emph[an hundred years];. On the connection of abstract numerals with suffixes, as #he[שְׁנֵיהֶם]; #emph[their duality];, i.e. #emph[they two];, #bref("Gen 2:25");, etc. (also with a strengthening separate pronoun, as #he[שְׁנֵ֫ינוּ אֲנַ֫חְנוּ]; #bref("1 Sam 20:42");), cf. #xref(<p97i>)[§ 97#h(0.18em)#emph[i];];.

#ml("e") <p134e> #strong[2.]; The numerals from 2 to 10 take the object numbered in the plural,#footnote[On examples such as #bref("Gen 46:27"); (#he[נֶ֫פֶשׁ שְׁנָ֫יִם]; #emph[two souls];), cf. #xref(<p132g>)[§ 132#h(0.18em)#emph[g];]; (collectives joined with the plural of the adjective).]; with very few exceptions, such as #bref("Exod 16:22"); (where #he[שְׁנֵי הָעֹ֫מֶר]; \= #emph[the double of an omer];), #bref("2 Kgs 22:1");, #bref("Ezek 45:1");, cf. #bref("2 Kgs 8:17"); and 25:17 #emph[K#super[e];th.]; The numerals from 11 to 19 generally take the plural, but with certain substantives frequently used with numerals the singular is more common (see further, under #emph[f];). The tens (from 20 to 90), when they precede, take the singular (in the accusative, cf. #xref(<p131p>)[§ 131#h(0.18em)#emph[p];];) of certain nouns frequently used with numerals (#he[אֶ֫לֶף]; #emph[a thousand];, #he[אִישׁ];, #he[יוֹם];, #he[כֹּר];, #he[נֶ֫פֶשׁ];, #he[שֶׁ֫קֶל];—but only in Ezekiel and the Priestly Code), otherwise the plural, as #he[בָּנִים];, #he[בָּנוֹת];, #he[עָרִים]; (but cf. also #bref("Judg 11:33");), etc.; on the other hand, the plural is necessary when they follow the object numbered in apposition (e.g. #he[אַמּוֹת עֶשְׂרִים]; #emph[twenty cubits];, #bref("2 Chr 3:3"); f.; with the exception of #bref("2 Sam 24:24");, only in late Books). After #he[מֵאָה]; and #he[אֶ֫לֶף]; the substantive numbered may be used either in the singular or plural, see further under #emph[g];.

#ml("f") <p134f> Rem. 1. After the numerals from 11 to 19 the singular is used, as a rule, with #he[יוֹם]; #emph[day];, #he[שָׁנָה]; #emph[year];, #he[אִישׁ]; #emph[man];, #he[נֶ֫פֶשׁ]; #emph[soul]; (person), #he[שֵׁ֫בֶט]; #emph[tribe];, #he[מַצֵּבָה]; #emph[pillar]; (#bref("Exod 24:4");), sometimes with #he[אַמָּה]; #emph[cubit];, #he[חֹ֫דֶשׁ]; #emph[month];, #he[עִיר]; #emph[city];, #he[שֶׁ֫קֶל]; #emph[shekel]; (compare our #emph[four\-year\-old, ten pound];), e.g. #bref("Deut 1:2"); #he[אַחַד עָשָׂר יוֹם]; (cf., however, such exceptions as #bref("Deut 1:23");, #bref("Josh 4:2");, etc.).—Substantives other than these are used in the plural with the numerals from 11 to 19, and the numeral may even follow the substantive, especially in later passages, as #bref("Num 7:87"); f., #bref("1 Chr 4:27");, #bref("25:5");.

#ml("g") <p134g> 2. After #he[מֵאָה]; (#he[מְאַת]; \[so almost exclusively in the Priestly Code, e.g. always #he[מְאַת אֶ֫לֶף];\], #he[מֵאוֹת];, #he[מָאתַ֫יִם];) and #he[אֶ֫לֶף]; (#he[אֲלָפִים];, #he[אַלְפֵי];, #he[אַלְפַּ֫יִם];) the substantives #he[אִישׁ];, #he[אֶ֫לֶף];, #he[אַמָּה]; (except in #bref("Ezek 40:27");), #he[יוֹם];, #he[רַגְלִי];, #he[צֶ֫מֶד]; are regularly used in the singular, generally also #he[שָׁנָה];, #he[כִּכָּר];, #he[כֹּר];, #he[שֶׁ֫קֶל]; (with the exception of #bref("Josh 7:21");, #bref("2 Sam 14:26");, etc.); cf., moreover, #bref("Gen 33:19");, #bref("24:60"); (#he[אַלְפֵי רְבָבָה];), #bref("Esth 1:1");, #bref("Judg 21:12");, #bref("Deut 7:9");, #bref("1 Kgs 5:12");, #bref("2 Chr 9:15");.—Examples of the #emph[plural]; after #he[מֵאָה]; are #bref("Gen 26:12");, #bref("1 Sam 18:25");, #bref("2 Sam 16:1");, #bref("1 Kgs 18:4");; after #he[מְאַת]; #bref("Exod 38:27");; after #he[מֵאוֹת]; #bref("Judg 15:4");, #bref("2 Sam 8:4");, #bref("1 Kgs 10:17");, #origpage("434") <orig-434>;#bref("Ezek 42:17");; after #he[מָאתַ֫יִם]; #bref("1 Sam 25:18");, #bref("1 Kgs 7:20");; after #he[אֶ֫לֶף]; #bref("1 Sam 25:2");, #bref("1 Kgs 3:4");, #bref("5:6");, #bref("2 Kgs 3:4");, #bref("Ps 90:4");; after #he[אֲלָפִים]; #bref("1 Sam 17:5");, #bref("Job 42:12");; after #he[אַלְפֵי]; #bref("Mic 6:7");; after #he[אַלְפַּ֫יִם]; #bref("Isa 36:8");.— In #bref("Dan 12:11"); the plural #he[יָמִים]; precedes the numeral #emph[twelve hundred];.

#ml("h") <p134h> #strong[3.]; Numerals compounded of tens and units (like 21, 62) take the object numbered either #emph[after]; them in the singular (in the accusative), e.g. #bref("Gen 5:20"); #he[שְׁתַּ֫יִם וְשִׁשִּׁים שָׁנָה]; #emph[two and sixty years]; (#he[שָׁנָה]; in the singular, according to #emph[e];, since it conforms to the ten immediately preceding; but also #he[שְׁלשִׁים וּשְׁמֹנֶה שָׁנָה]; #bref("Deut 2:14");), or #emph[before]; them in the plural, especially in the later Books, #bref("Dan 9:26");, etc.; or the object is repeated (but only in #bref("1 Kgs 6:1");, and the Priestly Code; sometimes even several times, e.g. #bref("Gen 23:1");, #bref("25:7");, 17 thrice) in the plural with the units, and in the singular with the tens and hundreds, e.g. #bref("Gen 12:4"); #he[חָמֵשׁ שָׁנִים וְשִׁבְעִים שָׁנָה]; #emph[seventy and five years];; #bref("Gen 23:1"); #he[מֵאָה שָׁנָה וְעֶשְׂרִים שָׁנָה וְשֶׁ֫בַע שָׁנִים]; #emph[an hundred and twenty and seven years];. Cf. #bref("Gen 5:6ff.");

#ml("i") <p134i> Rem. 1. It may further be remarked with regard to the order, that the thousand or thousands always precede the hundreds, etc., and the hundreds almost always come before the smaller numbers (in Kings and Ezekiel sometimes, and in the Priestly Code usually, after the smaller numbers), the tens in the earlier Books (documents J and D of the Pentateuch, in Joshua 1–12, Judges, Samuel, Isaiah, and also in Ezra and Nehemiah) #emph[before]; the units, but in Jeremiah, Ezekiel, the Priestly Code, Joshua 13–24 #emph[after]; the units (see Herner, op. cit., p. 73). After the hundreds the smaller number is very frequently added without #he[וְ];, especially in Ezra, Nehemiah, and Daniel.

On the syntax of the cardinals in general:—

#ml("k") <p134k> 2. The cardinals are determined by the article, when they refer back (without being connected with the object numbered; cf., however, #bref("Lev 25:10"); f., #bref("Num 16:35");, #bref("Josh 4:4");, #bref("2 Sam 23:13");) to a number or list already mentioned, e.g. #bref("Gen 2:11"); #he[שֵׁם הָֽאֶחָד פִּישׁוֹן]; #emph[the name of the one]; (the first) #emph[is Pishon];; #bref("Gen 14:9"); #emph[four kings against the five]; (enumerated in verse 2); cf. #bref("1 Chr 11:20"); f., and the determinate tens in #bref("Gen 18:29");, 31 f. A demonstrative with the article may also be added to a numeral determined in this way, e.g. #bref("Deut 19:9"); (but cf. also #bref("Gen 9:19");, #bref("22:23");, where the numeral and demonstrative are practically determinate in themselves). In the case of the numerals from 11 to 19 the article may stand either before the unit (#bref("1 Chr 25:19");, #bref("27:15");) or before #he[עָשָׂר]; (#bref("Josh 4:4");); it is used before all three members of a compound number (273) in #bref("Num 3:46");.

#ml("l") <p134l> In apposition with any determinate substantive the cardinal number is used #emph[without]; the article, not only when it precedes the substantive, as in #bref("Josh 15:14"); (#he[אֶת־שְׁלוֹשָׁה בְנֵי הָֽעֲנָק];, where #he[שְׁלוֹשָׁה]; is equivalent to a substantive determinate in itself; cf. #bref("Gen 18:28");, #bref("Josh 6:8");, 22, #bref("1 Sam 17:14");, #bref("1 Kgs 11:31");, and the passages discussed above in #xref(<p126x>)[§ 126#h(0.18em)#emph[x];];, #bref("Gen 21:29");, etc.), but also when it follows the substantive, e.g. #bref("1 Kgs 7:27");, 43 f. #he[עֶשֶׂר]; and #he[עֲשָׂרָה];; the omission of the article may here, as in the cases noticed in #xref(<p126z>)[§ 126#h(0.18em)#emph[z];];, be also due to the dislike of a hiatus, but cf. also #he[שְׁנַ֫יִם]; #bref("2 Kgs 25:16"); after a determinate substantive. The fact that it is by nature determinate would also be a very simple explanation of #he[אֶחָד]; #bref("Num 28:4");, #bref("1 Sam 13:17"); f., #bref("Jer 24:2");, #bref("Ezek 10:9");, instead of the more usual #he[הָֽאֶחָד];, and of #he[אַחַת]; #bref("1 Sam 1:2"); for #he[הָֽאַחַת];. #origpage("435") <orig-435>;

#ml("m") <p134m> Such cases as #he[שִׁבְעַת הַיָּמִים]; #bref("Judg 14:17"); (which is determined by a following determinate genitive) are explained from #xref(<p127b>)[§ 127#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];; #bref("1 Chr 9:25"); perhaps from #xref(<p126q>)[§ 126#h(0.18em)#emph[q];];; in #bref("Isa 30:26"); probably the light of all the seven days of the week is meant; on the other hand, in #bref("1 Sam 9:20"); and 25:38 the article is, with Wellhausen, to be omitted.

#ml("n") <p134n> 3. Certain specifications of #emph[measure, weight];, or #emph[time];, are commonly omitted after numerals, e.g. #bref("Gen 20:16"); #he[אֶ֫לֶף כֶּ֫סֶף]; #emph[a thousand]; (shekels) #emph[of silver];; so also before #he[זָהָב]; #bref("Gen 24:22");, #bref("1 Kgs 10:16");, #bref("Isa 7:23");, cf. #bref("Ps 119:72");. Moreover, #bref("Ruth 3:15"); #he[שֵׁשׁ שְׂעֹרִים]; #emph[six]; (ephahs) #emph[of barley];; #bref("1 Sam 10:4"); #he[שְׁתֵּי־לֶ֫חֶם]; #emph[two]; (sc. #emph[loaves];, see verse 3) #emph[of bread];, cf. 17:17 #he[עֲשָׂרָה לֶ֫חֶם];; #bref("2 Sam 16:1");, where before #he[קַ֫יִץ]; a measure, or perhaps some term like #emph[cakes];, is to be supplied.—The number of cubits is stated in the Priestly Code (#bref("Exod 26:2");, etc.) and in 1 K 6 and 7 (otherwise only in #bref("Ezek 40:5");, 21, 47:3. #bref("Zech 5:2");, #bref("1 Chr 11:23");, #bref("2 Chr 4:2"); f.) by the addition of #he[בָּֽאַמָּה]; prop. #emph[by the cubit];. Also in #bref("Exod 27:11"); the Samaritan and LXX read #he[בָּֽאַמָּה]; after #he[אֹרֶךְ];, and in 27:15 #he[אַמָּה]; after #he[עֶשְׂרֵה];.

#ml("o") <p134o> #strong[4.]; The #emph[ordinals]; above 10 have no special forms, but are expressed by the corresponding cardinals, which may then stand either before or after the object numbered, e.g. #bref("Gen 7:11"); #he[בְּשִׁבְעָה עָשָׂר יוֹם]; #emph[on the seventeenth day];; #bref("Deut 1:3"); #he[בְּאַרְבָּעִים שָׁנָה]; #emph[in the fortieth year];; cf. #bref("Gen 14:5");, #bref("2 Kgs 25:27");, and, with repetition of #he[שָׁנָה]; in a compound number, #bref("1 Kgs 6:1");; such a cardinal occurs without #he[בְּ]; (and therefore in the #emph[accus. temporis];, according to #xref(<p118k>)[§ 118#h(0.18em)#emph[k];];) in #bref("Gen 14:4"); (the Samaritan, however, has #he[ובשלש];); with the article (but without a numbered object, see under #emph[k];), #bref("1 Kgs 19:19");.#footnote[Somewhat different from this is #bref("Exod 19:15"); #emph[be ready]; #he[לִשְׁל֫שֶׁת יָמִים]; prop. #emph[after three days];, i.e. #emph[on the third day]; (in verses 11 and 16 and in #bref("Ezra 10:8"); the ordinal is used), also #bref("1 Sam 30:13"); #he[כִּי חָלִיתִי הַיּוֹם שְׁלשָׁה]; #emph[because three days agone I fell sick];, prop. #emph[today three]; (days).];—On the position of the numeral as a genitive following its noun, cf. e.g. #bref("1 Kgs 16:10"); #he[בִּשְׁנַת עֶשְׂרִים וָשֶׁבַע]; #emph[in the twenty and seventh year];, and with a determinate numeral, #bref("Exod 12:18");, #bref("Num 33:38");, #bref("Deut 15:9");. In this case, however, #he[שָׁנָה]; is very frequently repeated, e.g. #bref("Gen 7:11");, #bref("2 Kgs 13:10");; after a determinate numeral, #bref("Lev 25:10");.#footnote[All these expressions may indeed be explained by supposing that, e.g. in #bref("Lev 25:10");, the proper meaning is #emph[the year of the fifty years]; which it completed, i.e. the fiftieth year; but it is more correct to regard #he[שְׁנַת]; or #he[בִּשְׁנַת]; in such cases not as a real #emph[nomen regens];, but simply as a connective form to be explained on the analogy of the cases mentioned in #xref(<p128k>)[§ 128#h(0.18em)#emph[k];];.];

#ml("p") <p134p> Rem. In numbering days of the month and years, the cardinals are very frequently used instead of the ordinals even for the numbers from 1 to 10, e.g. #he[בִּשְׁנַת שְׁתַּיִם]; #bref("1 Kgs 15:25");; #he[בִּשְׁנַת שָׁלשׁ]; #bref("2 Kgs 18:1");, etc., cf. #bref("Deut 15:9");. The months themselves are always numbered by the ordinals (#he[בָּרִאשׁוֹן];, #he[בַּשֵּׁנִי];, etc., up to #he[בָּֽעֲשִׂירִי];), but not the days of the month, e.g. #he[בְּאֶחָד לַחֹ֫דֶשׁ]; #bref("Gen 8:5");, etc., #he[בְּאַרְבָּעָה לַחֹ֫דֶשׁ]; #bref("Zech 7:1");; #he[בַּֽחֲמִשָּׁה לַחֹ֫דֶשׁ]; #bref("Ezek 1:1");, etc., #he[בְּשִׁבְעָה לַחֹ֫דֶשׁ]; #bref("2 Kgs 25:8");, #he[בְּתִשְׁעָה לַחֹ֫דֶשׁ]; #bref("Lev 23:32"); (always, however, #he[בֶּֽעָשׂר לַחֹ֫דֶשׁ]; #emph[on the tenth day of the month];). On the #origpage("436") <orig-436>;omission of #he[יוֹם]; in all these cases see under #emph[n];; only in late passages is #he[יוֹם]; added, e.g. #bref("2 Chr 29:17"); #he[בְּיוֹם שְׁמוֹנָה לַחֹ֫דֶשׁ];; #bref("Ezra 3:6"); #he[מִיּוֹם אֶחָד לַחֹ֫דֶשׁ];.—Finally, when the year is stated by #he[בִּשְׁנַת]; governing a determinate ordinal, namely #bref("2 Kgs 17:6"); #he[בִּשְׁנַת הַתְּשִׁיעִית]; #emph[in the ninth year];; #bref("2 Kgs 25:1"); (in #bref("Jer 52:4"); #he[בְּשָׁנָה];), #bref("Jer 28:1"); #emph[K#super[e];th.];, 32:1 #emph[K#super[e];th.];, 46:2, 51:59, #bref("Ezra 7:8");; #he[בִּשְׁנַת]; in such cases is again (see note 2 on #emph[o];) to be explained according to #xref(<p128k>)[§ 128#h(0.18em)#emph[k];];. This is supported by the fact that the Masora on #bref("Jer 28:1");, #bref("32:1"); requires in the #emph[Q#super[e];rê]; #he[בַּשָּׁנָה]; for #he[בשנת];.

#ml("q") <p134q> #strong[5.]; #emph[Distributives]; are expressed either by repetition of the cardinal number, e.g. #bref("Gen 7:9");, 15 #he[שְׁנַ֫יִם שְׁנַ֫יִם]; #emph[two and two];; #bref("2 Sam 21:20"); #he[שֵׁשׁ וָשֵׁשׁ]; #emph[six each];; with the numbered object also repeated, e.g. #bref("Josh 3:12"); #he[אִישׁ אֶחָד אִישׁ אֶחָד לַשָּֽׁבֶט]; #emph[for every tribe a man];; #bref("Num 13:2");, #bref("34:18"); (#he[אֶחָד מִן];, as in #bref("Neh 11:1");, #emph[one out of every ten];); cf. #xref(<p123d>)[§ 123#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];; or a periphrasis with #he[אֶחָד לְ]; is used, #bref("Num 17:18");, #bref("Deut 1:23");, cf. #bref("Isa 6:2"); #he[לְאֶחָד]; after #emph[six wings]; twice repeated; the simple distributive #he[לְ]; is, however, sufficient (as in #he[לַבְּקָרִים];, #xref(<p123c>)[§ 123#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];), e.g. #he[לְמֵאוֹת וְלַֽאֲלָפִים]; #emph[by hundreds and by thousands];.

#ml("r") <p134r> #strong[6.]; The #emph[multiplicatives]; are expressed either (like the ordinals above 10, see under #emph[o];) by the cardinals (in the feminine, probably owing to the omission of #he[פַּ֫עַם];, #he[פְּעָמִים];; so König, #emph[Lehrgeb.];, ii. 228), as #he[שְׁתַּ֫יִם]; #emph[twice];, #bref("Job 40:5");; #he[שֶׁ֫בַע]; #emph[seven times];, #bref("Lev 26:21");, 24, #bref("Prov 24:16");; cf. also #he[אַחַת]; #emph[once];, #bref("2 Kgs 6:10");, #bref("Job 40:5");, for which in #bref("Job 33:14"); #he[בְּאַחַת];#footnote[But #he[בְּאַחַת]; #bref("Num 10:4"); is to be translated #emph[on one]; (trumpet).]; along with #he[בִּשְׁתַּ֫יִם]; (the latter also in #bref("1 Sam 18:21");); or by the #emph[dual]; of the numeral, thus #he[שִׁבְעָתַ֫יִם]; #bref("Gen 4:15"); (in verse 24 along with the cardinal 77 for 77 times); #bref("Isa 30:26");, #bref("Ps 12:7");, #bref("79:12");; #he[אַרְבַּעְתָּ֑יִם]; #bref("2 Sam 12:6");;#footnote[Probably also #he[כִּפְלַ֫יִם]; #bref("Job 11:6"); (from #he[כֶּ֫פֶל]; #emph[doubling];) does not mean #emph[doubled]; but #emph[manifold];.]; or periphrastically by #he[פַּ֫עַם]; #emph[a time]; (prop. #emph[a step];, with the article, #he[הַפַּ֫עַם]; #emph[this time];; cf. also #he[בַּפַּ֫עַם הַזֹּאת];, with #he[בְּ];, like #he[בְּאַחַת]; above), as #he[פַּ֫עַם אַחַת]; #emph[once]; (#bref("Neh 13:20"); #he[פַּ֫עַם וּשְׁתַּ֫יִם]; #emph[once and twice];), #he[פַּֽעֲמַ֫יִם]; #emph[twice];, #he[שָׁלשׁ פְּעָמִים]; (for which in #bref("Exod 23:14");, #bref("Num 22:28");, 32 #he[שָׁלשׁ רְגָלִים];) #emph[three times];; cf. #bref("Ezek 41:6"); #emph[thirty\-three times];; #bref("2 Sam 24:3"); #emph[an hundred times];; #bref("Deut 1:11"); #emph[a thousand times];; #bref("1 Kgs 22:16"); #he[עַד־כַּמֶּה פְעָמִים]; #emph[until how many times];, i.e. how often. Cf. also #he[עֲשֶׂ֫רֶת מֹנִים]; #emph[ten times];, #bref("Gen 31:7");, 14, and #he[רַבּוֹת עִתִּים]; #emph[many times];, #bref("Neh 9:28");.—In #bref("Gen 43:34");, #emph[five times]; is expressed by #he[חָמֵשׁ יָדוֹת]; (prop. #emph[five hands];),#footnote[But #he[אַרְבַּע הַיָּדוֹת]; #bref("Gen 47:24"); means #emph[the]; (other) #emph[four parts];; cf. #bref("2 Kgs 11:7");, #bref("Neh 11:1");.]; and in #bref("Exod 16:5"); the #emph[double]; is expressed by #he[מִשְׁנֶה עַל־]; (prop. #emph[a repetition over and above that which];, etc.).—Of the ordinals #he[שֵׁנִית]; is used as a numeral adverb, #bref("Gen 22:15");, etc., #emph[a second time];, cf. the Latin #emph[tertium consul];; #he[בַּשְּׁלִישִׁת]; #emph[the third time];, #bref("1 Sam 3:8");; #he[פַּ֫עַם חֲמִישִׁית]; #emph[a fifth time];, #bref("Neh 6:5");; #he[בַּשְּׁבִעִית]; #emph[at the seventh]; (time), #bref("1 Kgs 18:44");, and #he[בַּפַּ֫עַם הַשּׁ׳]; #bref("Josh 6:16");. #origpage("437") <orig-437>;

#ml("s") <p134s> Rem. The collocation of a numeral with the next above it (either in the same or in different sentences) is a rhetorical device employed in #emph[numerical sayings]; to express a number, which need not, or cannot, be more exactly specified. It must be gathered from the context whether such formulae are intended to denote only an insignificant number (e.g. #bref("Isa 17:6");, #emph[two or]; at the most #emph[three];), or a considerable number, e.g. #bref("Mic 5:4");. Sometimes, however, this juxtaposition serves to express merely an indefinite total, without the collateral idea of intensifying the lower by means of the higher number. Thus #emph[one]; and #emph[two]; are connected by #he[וְ];, #bref("Deut 32:30");, #bref("Jer 3:14");, #bref("Job 33:14");, #bref("40:5"); (without #he[וְ];, #bref("Ps 62:12");); #emph[two]; and #emph[three];, #bref("Isa 17:6"); (#bref("Sir 23:16");, #bref("26:28");, #bref("50:25");), and without #he[וְ];, #bref("2 Kgs 9:32");, #bref("Hos 6:2");, #bref("Amos 4:8");; #emph[three]; and #emph[four];, #bref("Jer 36:23");, #bref("Amos 1:3");–11, #bref("Prov 30:18");, #bref("21:29"); (#bref("Sir 26:5");), and without #he[וְ];, #bref("Prov 30:15");; #emph[four]; and #emph[five];, without #he[וְ];, #bref("Isa 17:6");; #emph[six]; and #emph[seven];, #bref("Job 5:19");, #bref("Prov 6:16");; #emph[seven]; and #emph[eight];, #bref("Mic 5:4");, #bref("Eccl 11:2");; (#emph[nine]; and #emph[ten];, #bref("Sir 25:7");).

#bookhead(3, [III. Syntax of the Pronoun], note: []);

#sec("135", [The Personal Pronoun], note: []) <s135>

#ml("a") <p135a> #strong[1.]; The #emph[separate]; pronouns,—apart from their employment as the subject in noun\-clauses (cf. #xref(<p141a>)[§ 141#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];) and the idiom mentioned under #emph[d–h];,—are used, according to #xref(<p32b>)[§ 32#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];, as a rule, only to give express emphasis to the subject; e.g. #bref("Gen 16:5");, #bref("2 Sam 24:17"); #he[אָֽנֹכִי]; i.e. #emph[I myself];, so also #he[אֲנִי]; #bref("2 Sam 12:28");, #bref("17:15"); (after the verb), #bref("Ezek 34:15");, #bref("Ps 2:6");;#footnote[Also #he[הוּא];, #he[הִיא]; #emph[he himself, she herself]; (of persons and things), e.g. #bref("Isa 7:14"); #he[אֲדֹנָי הוּא]; #emph[the Lord himself];; #bref("Esth 9:1"); #he[הַיְּהוּדִים הֵ֫מָּה]; #emph[the Jews themselves];. In the sense of #emph[the same]; (ὁ αὐτός) or (#emph[one and];) #emph[the same];, #he[הוּא]; is used in #bref("Isa 41:4");, #bref("43:10");, 13, 46:4, 48:12 (always #he[אֲנִי הוּא];), #bref("Ps 102:28"); (#he[אַתָּה הוּא];), and probably also #bref("Job 3:19");.—The position of #he[הֵ֫מָּה];, as an accusative of the object, before a perfect in #bref("1 Chr 9:22");, can at most be explained on the analogy of Aramaic (#bref("Ezra 5:12");).]; but #bref("1 Sam 10:18");, #bref("2 Sam 12:7");, #bref("Isa 45:12"); #he[אָֽנֹכִי]; #emph[I]; and none else; cf. also #he[אֲנִי אֲנִי]; #emph[I, I!]; #bref("Hos 5:14");, etc.; #he[אַתָּה]; #bref("Gen 15:15");, #bref("Judg 15:18");, #bref("1 Sam 17:56"); (as in 20:8, 22:18, #bref("Exod 18:19");, #bref("Deut 5:24");, #bref("Judg 8:21");, after the imperative); #bref("1 Kgs 21:7");; #he[אַתֶּם]; #bref("Gen 9:7");, #bref("Exod 20:19"); (after the verb, #bref("Judg 15:12");); fem. #bref("Gen 31:6");; #he[הוּא]; #bref("1 Sam 22:18");; #he[הִיא]; #bref("Gen 3:20");, #bref("Judg 14:3");; #he[הֵ֫מָּה]; #bref("Jer 5:5");.—Sometimes, however, the separate pronoun appears to be placed before the verb more on rhythmical grounds, i.e. in order to give the statement a fuller sound than that of the bare verbal form (cf. the similar use of the infinitive absolute, #xref(<p113o>)[§ 113#h(0.18em)#emph[o];];). Thus #bref("Gen 14:23");, #bref("Ps 139:2");, and most clearly in such passages as #bref("Gen 21:24");, #bref("47:30");, #bref("Exod 8:24");, #bref("Judg 6:18");, #bref("11:9");, #bref("1 Sam 12:20");, #bref("2 Sam 3:13");, #bref("21:6");, #bref("1 Kgs 2:18"); (in solemn promises). The same explanation applies to #he[אֲנִי]; at the beginning of sentences, e.g. #bref("Gen 24:45");, #bref("Hos 5:3");, #bref("10:11");, #bref("12:11");, #bref("Ps 39:11");, #bref("82:6");, #bref("Job 5:3");.#footnote[As early as the Mêšaʿ inscription (line 21 ff.) #he[אנך]; frequently stands at the beginning of a new sentence after the dividing stroke.];

#ml("b") <p135b> Rem. 1. Different from this is the pleonastic addition of the separate pronoun immediately #emph[after]; the verb (according to Delitzsch on #bref("Song 5:5"); perhaps #origpage("438") <orig-438>;a trace of popular language), e.g. #bref("1 Sam 23:22");(?), #bref("Song 5:5");, and (like other indications of the very late origin of the book) very frequently in Ecclesiastes, e.g. 1:16, 2:1, 11, 15, 3:17f. and thirteen other places; in Aramaic, #bref("Dan 5:16");.

#ml("c") <p135c> 2. Substantival subjects also are somewhat frequently resumed, and thus expressly emphasized, by the insertion of the corresponding separate pronoun of the 3rd person before the predicate is stated, e.g. #bref("Gen 3:12"); #emph[the woman whom thou gavest to be with me, she]; (#he[הִיא];) #emph[gave me];, etc.; 14:24 (#he[הֵם];); 15:4, 24:7, etc.; but #he[הוּא]; in #bref("Isa 7:14"); after the predicate and subject is equivalent to #emph[he himself];.#footnote[Analogous to this is the resumption of a noun dependent on a preposition, by means of a pronominal suffix united with the same preposition, e.g. #bref("Gen 2:17");, #bref("2 Sam 6:22");, #bref("2 Kgs 22:18");, or of an object by means of the #emph[nota accusativi]; #he[את]; with suffix, e.g. #bref("1 Sam 15:9"); (where #he[וְנִמְאֶ֫סֶת]; is certainly to be read), #bref("Isa 8:13");.];

#ml("d") <p135d> #strong[2.]; Not infrequently the separate pronoun serves to give strong emphasis to a suffix of the same person which precedes (or sometimes even to one which follows), whether the suffix be attached to a verb (as accusative) or to a noun or preposition (as genitive). In English such an emphasis on the pronoun can generally be rendered only by laying greater stress upon it, or sometimes by repeating it; cf., on the contrary, the French #emph[mon livre à moi];. The separate pronoun in such instances is not to be regarded as a #emph[casus obliquus]; (accusative or genitive), but as the subject of an independent sentence, the predicate of which must in each case be supplied according to the context.

#ml("e") <p135e> Examples of emphasis:—

(#emph[a];) On a #emph[verbal]; suffix by means of #he[אֲנִי]; (#he[אָ֫נִי];), #bref("Gen 27:34"); #he[בָּֽרֲכֵ֫נִי גַם־אָ֫נִי]; #emph[bless me, even me also]; (prop. #emph[bless me, I also]; would be blessed); #bref("Zech 7:5");; cf. also #bref("Ezek 6:3");, #bref("34:11");, 20 #he[הִנְנִי אָ֫נִי];; by #he[אַתָּה]; (#he[אָ֫תָּה];) #bref("Prov 22:19"); (but the text is most probably corrupt).—The separate pronoun #emph[precedes]; in #bref("Gen 24:27"); (#he[אָֽנֹכִי];); 49:8 (#he[אַתָּה];, not #emph[Judah, thou art he whom];, but #emph[Judah thee, thee thy brethren shall praise!];), and #bref("Eccl 2:15"); #he[גַּם אֲנִי];.

#ml("f") <p135f> (#emph[b];) On a noun\-suffix with a substantive, by means of #he[אֲנִי]; #bref("2 Sam 19:1");, #bref("Prov 23:15");; by #he[אָ֑תָּה]; #bref("1 Kgs 21:19"); #he[אֶת־דָּֽמְךָ גַּם־אָ֑תָּה]; #emph[thy blood, even thine];; by #he[הוּא]; #bref("2 Sam 17:5");, #bref("Jer 27:7");, #bref("Mic 7:3");; by #he[אֲנַ֫חְנוּ]; #bref("1 Sam 20:42");, after #he[שְׁנֵ֫ינוּ];, but without special stress; #bref("Neh 5:2"); (?); by #he[אַתֶּם]; #bref("Num 14:32");; by #he[הֵם]; #bref("Ps 38:11"); (without special stress), #he[הֵ֫מָּה]; #bref("Ps 9:7");.—The separate pronoun #emph[precedes]; in #bref("Job 21:4"); (#he[אָֽנֹכִי];); #bref("Gen 40:16");, #bref("Isa 45:12");, #bref("1 Chr 28:2"); (#he[אֲנִי];); #bref("Zech 9:11"); (#he[אַתְּ];); #bref("Josh 23:9"); (#he[אַתֶּם];); #bref("Ezek 33:17"); (#he[הֵ֫מָּה];).—In #bref("Ps 89:48");, where #he[אֲנִי]; might be taken as strengthening #he[חלד]; (equivalent in sense to #he[חֶלְדִּי];), we should read #he[אֲדֹנָי]; for #he[אֲנִי];, as in verse 51.

#ml("g") <p135g> (#emph[c];) On a suffix united with a preposition, #bref("1 Sam 25:24"); #he[בִּי אֲנִי]; #emph[upon me, upon me];; #bref("1 Kgs 1:26"); #he[לִי... אֲנִי];; #bref("2 Chr 35:21"); #he[לֹא־עָלֶ֫יךָ אַתָּה]; #emph[not against thee];; #bref("1 Sam 19:23"); #he[עָלָיו גַּם הוּא]; #emph[upon him also];; #bref("Deut 5:3"); #he[כִּי אִתָּנוּ אֲנַ֫חְנוּ]; #emph[but with us, even us];; #bref("Hag 1:4"); #he[לָכֶם אַתֶּם]; #emph[for you yourselves];; #bref("Jer 25:14"); #he[בָּם גַּם־הֵ֫מָּה];.—The separate pronoun precedes in #bref("1 Sam 12:23"); #he[אָֽנֹכִי... לִי];; #bref("1 Kgs 1:20"); #he[אַתָּה... עָלֶ֫יךָ];; #bref("Mic 5:1"); #he[אַתָּה... מִמְּךָ];, and #bref("2 Chr 28:10"); #he[אַתֶּם עִמָּכֶם];.

#ml("h") <p135h> The same principle also explains #bref("Gen 4:26"); #he[לְשֵׁת גַּם־הוּא]; #emph[to Seth, to him also]; (not #he[גַּם־לוֹ];); cf. 10:21, and #bref("Exod 35:34");, #bref("Num 4:22");. #origpage("439") <orig-439>;

#ml("i") <p135i> #strong[3.]; The #emph[oblique]; cases of the personal pronouns expressed by means of a preposition (or the #emph[nota accus.]; #he[את];) with a suffix may be used either in a demonstrative or reflexive sense,#footnote[As in Luther’s Bible #emph[jm]; (#emph[ihm];), #emph[jr]; (#emph[ihr];) for #emph[sich];, and in our version #emph[him, her]; for #emph[himself, herself];.]; as #he[לוֹ]; #emph[to him];, but also #emph[to himself];, e.g. #bref("Judg 3:16"); #emph[and Ehud made]; #he[לוֹ]; #emph[for himself a sword];, cf. #bref("Gen 33:17");; so also #he[לָהֶם]; #emph[sibi];, #bref("Isa 3:9");; #he[אֵלָיו]; #emph[unto him];, and #bref("Gen 8:9"); #emph[unto himself];; #he[אִתּוֹ]; #emph[with him];, and #bref("Gen 22:3"); #emph[with himself];; #he[עִמָּהּ]; #emph[with her];, and #bref("1 Sam 1:24"); #emph[with herself];; also apparently as a pleonastic #emph[dativus ethicus]; (see #xref(<p119s>)[§ 119#h(0.18em)#emph[s];];), #bref("Job 12:11");, #bref("13:1");.

#ml("k") <p135k> Rarely, and only when marked emphasis is intended, is the #emph[accusative]; of the reflexive pronoun represented by the #emph[nota accusativi]; #he[את]; with a suffix (this being ordinarily expressed by the reflexive conjugations #emph[Niphʿal]; and #emph[Hithpaʿēl];#footnote[#emph[Niphʿal]; according to #xref(<p51e>)[§ 51#h(0.18em)#emph[e];]; (like #emph[Hithpaʿēl]; according to #xref(<p54f>)[§ 54#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];) may also include the #emph[dative]; of the reflexive pronoun.];); thus, #he[אֹתָם]; #emph[se ipsos];, #bref("Exod 5:19");, #bref("Jer 7:19"); in sharp antithesis to #he[הַֽאֹתִי];; #bref("Ezek 34:2");, 8, 10. Cf. § 57 at the end, together with note 2.

#ml("l") <p135l> Rem. There is a similar emphasis in #bref("Isa 49:26"); on #he[בְּשָׂרָם]; and #he[דָּמָם]; in the sense of #emph[their own flesh, their own blood];. On the sometimes demonstrative, sometimes reflexive meaning of noun\-suffixes of the 3rd person singular and plural, cf. § 91, #emph[p]; and #emph[q];. For other circumlocutions to express the idea of #emph[self];, see #xref(<p139f>)[§ 139#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];.

#ml("m") <p135m> #strong[4.]; The #emph[possessive pronouns]; are, according to #xref(<p33c>)[§ 33#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];, expressed by the #emph[suffixes of the noun]; (in the genitive),#footnote[Like the substantival genitive, according to #xref(<p129h>)[§ 129#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];, the possessive pronoun may also be paraphrased by a relative clause, e.g. #bref("Ruth 2:21"); #he[הַנְּעָרִים אֲשֶׁר לִי]; #emph[the young men, which]; are #emph[to me];, i.e. #emph[my young men];; so especially, when the substantive, which should take a genitive suffix, is already followed by a genitive, e.g. #bref("1 Sam 17:40");. In this case, however, the suffix also is sometimes attached pleonastically, e.g. #bref("Song 1:6"); #he[כַּרְמִי שֶׁלִּי]; #emph[my vineyard, which]; belongs #emph[to me.]; Cf. #bref("Song 3:7");, and the analogous pleonasms in #bref("2 Sam 22:2"); (but see #bref("Ps 18:2");) and #bref("Ps 27:2");.]; which may represent either a #emph[subjective genitive];, or (like the genitives proper, #xref(<p128h>)[§ 128#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];) an #emph[objective genitive];, e.g. #he[חֲמָסִי]; #emph[the wrong done against me];, #bref("Gen 16:5");, #bref("Jer 51:35");; cf. #bref("Gen 9:2");, #bref("18:21");, #bref("27:13"); (#bref("2 Sam 16:12"); #emph[K#super[e];th.];); #bref("Gen 30:23");, #bref("39:21"); (cf. #bref("Exod 3:21");, etc.); 50:4, #bref("Exod 20:20");, #bref("21:35");, #bref("Judg 4:9");, #bref("13:12"); (#he[מַֽעֲשֵׂ֫הוּ]; #emph[the treatment of him];); #bref("Isa 56:7");, #bref("Jer 9:7");, #bref("Nah 3:19");, #bref("Prov 1:27");, #bref("24:22");, #bref("Job 20:29");, #bref("23:14");, #bref("34:6");. Cf. also such pregnant expressions as #bref("Ps 20:3"); #he[יִשְׁלַח עֶזְרְךָ]; #emph[he will send thy help]; (#emph[help for thee];), i.e. he will send thee help; #bref("Gen 30:18");, #bref("39:21");, #bref("Exod 2:9");, #bref("Isa 1:26"); (#emph[and I will restore judges for thee];); #bref("Ezek 37:15");.

When several substantives are co\-ordinated, the pronominal suffix must be attached to each singly, e.g. #bref("Gen 36:6"); #emph[and Esau took]; #he[אֶת־נָשָׁיו וְאֶת־בָּנָיו וְאֶת־בְּנֹתָיו]; #emph[his wives and his sons and his daughters];, etc.; 38:18, etc. In #bref("2 Sam 23:5"); the text is hardly correct. #origpage("440") <orig-440>;

#ml("n") <p135n> #strong[5.]; When the genitive, following a construct state, is used periphrastically to express the idea of a material or attribute (#xref(<p128o>)[§ 128#h(0.18em)#emph[o];]; and #emph[p];), the pronominal suffix, which properly belongs to the compound idea (represented by the #emph[nomen regens]; and genitive), is, like the article (§ 127), attached to the second substantive (the genitive), e.g. #he[הַר־קָדְשִׁי]; prop. #emph[the hill of my holiness];, i.e. my holy hill, #bref("Ps 2:6");, etc.; #he[עִיר קָדְשְׁךָ]; #emph[thy holy city];, #bref("Dan 9:24");; #he[אֱלִילֵי כַסְפּוֹ]; #emph[his idols of silver];, #bref("Isa 2:20");, #bref("30:22");, #bref("31:7");;#footnote[On the other hand, more explicitly in prose, #bref("Gen 44:2"); #he[אֶת־גְּבִיעִי גְבִיעַ הַכֶּ֫סֶף]; #emph[my cup, the silver cup];.]; cf. #bref("Deut 1:41");, #bref("Isa 9:3");, #bref("28:4");, #bref("41:11");, #bref("Ezek 9:1");f., #bref("Ps 41:10");, #bref("150:1");, #bref("Job 18:7"); #he[צַֽעֲדֵי אוֹנוֹ]; #emph[his steps of strength];; 38:6; after an adjective as #emph[nomen regens];, #bref("Isa 13:3"); (#bref("Zeph 3:11");) #he[עַלִּיזֵי גַֽאֲוָתִי]; #emph[my proudly exulting ones];.—On the same analogy is the use of e.g. #he[כְּלֵי מִלְחַמְתּוֹ]; #bref("Deut 1:41"); #emph[his weapons of war]; \[cf. #bref("Isa 41:12");\]; #bref("Isa 56:7"); #he[בֵּית תְּפִלָּתִי]; #emph[my house of prayer];, although the genitive here does not convey the idea of an attribute.

#ml("o") <p135o> Rem. 1. Through a weakening in the distinction of gender, which is noticeable elsewhere (cf. #xref(<p110k>)[§ 110#h(0.18em)#emph[k];];, 144 #emph[a];, 145 #emph[p];, #emph[t];, #emph[u];) and which probably passed from the colloquial language#footnote[According to Diehl (see the title at the head of #xref(<p91a>)[§ 91#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];), who adduces numerous instances on pp. 44 ff., 54 ff., 67 f., many of these cases may be set down to corruption of the traditional text, while the sudden (and sometimes repeated) change of gender in suffixes is mainly due to the influence exercised on the copyists by the Mishnic and popular Aramaic dialects, neither of which recognizes such distinctions. Such influence, however, is insufficient to explain the large number of instances of this weakening, occurring even in the earlier documents.]; into that of literature, #emph[masculine]; suffixes (especially in the plural) are not infrequently used to refer to #emph[feminine]; substantives; thus a noun\-suffix in the singular, #bref("Exod 11:6");, #bref("25:19");, #bref("Judg 11:34");;#footnote[The Masora reckons six instances of #he[מִמֶּ֫נּוּ];, where #he[מִמֶּ֫נָּה]; would be expected (#bref("Judg 11:34");, where, however, the text is most probably corrupt), #bref("Exod 25:15"); (?), #bref("Lev 6:8");, #bref("7:18");, #bref("27:9");, #bref("Josh 1:7");; almost all these passages can, however, be easily explained in other ways.]; in the plural, #bref("Gen 31:9");, #bref("32:16");, #bref("41:23");, #bref("Exod 1:21");, #bref("2:17");, #bref("Num 27:7"); (but the feminine suffix twice immediately after, and so the Samaritan also in verse 7); 36:6 (Samaritan #he[אֲבִיהֶן];, but also #he[בְּעֵֽינֵיהֶם];); #bref("Judg 19:24");, #bref("21:22");, #bref("1 Sam 6:7");, 10b (#he[בְּנֵיהֶם];); 9:20, #bref("Isa 3:16");, #bref("Ezek 23:45"); ff. (alternating with #he[הֶן];); #bref("Amos 4:1"); f. (but afterwards a feminine suffix); #bref("Job 1:14");, #bref("39:3"); (#he[חֶבְלֵיהֶם]; in parallelism with #he[יַלְדֵיהֶן];); 42:15, #bref("Song 4:2");, #bref("6:6");, #bref("Ruth 1:8"); ff. (along with feminine suffixes); #bref("Dan 1:5");, #bref("8:9");. Verbal suffixes in the singular, #bref("Exod 22:25");; in the plural, #bref("Judg 16:3");, #bref("Prov 6:21");, #bref("Job 1:15");. But #bref("Gen 26:15");, 18, 33:13, #bref("Exod 2:17");, #bref("1 Sam 6:10");#super[ #emph[a];]; are to be explained according to #xref(<p60h>)[§ 60#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];. On #he[הֵ֫מָּה]; as feminine, see #xref(<p32n>)[§ 32#h(0.18em)#emph[n];];. On the use of the masculine in general as the #emph[prior gender];, see #xref(<p122g>)[§ 122#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];.

#ml("p") <p135p> 2. The suffix of the 3rd person singular feminine (as also the separate pronoun #he[הִיא]; #bref("Num 14:41");, #bref("Josh 10:13");, #bref("Judg 14:4");) sometimes refers in a general sense to the verbal idea contained in a preceding sentence (corresponding to our #emph[it];); thus the verbal suffix, #bref("Gen 15:6");, #bref("Num 23:19");, #bref("1 Sam 11:2");, #bref("1 Kgs 11:12");, #bref("Isa 30:8");, #bref("Amos 8:10");; cf. #bref("Gen 24:14"); (#he[בָּהּ]; #emph[thereby];), 42:36, 47:26, #bref("Exod 10:11"); (#he[אֹתָהּ]; #emph[that];), #bref("Isa 47:7");. Elsewhere the suffix of the 3rd singular feminine refers to the plurals of things, e.g. #bref("2 Kgs 3:3"); #origpage("441") <orig-441>;\[but see Kittel; so 13:2, 6, 11; 10:26, but LXX #he[מַצִּבַת];\], #bref("Jer 36:23");, #bref("Job 6:20"); (if the text is correct), 39:15 (read #he[תֵּחָמֵם]; in v. 14), and to the plurals of names of animals, #bref("Isa 35:7");, #bref("Ezra 11:5");. Conversely, plural suffixes refer to collective singulars, e.g. in #bref("Gen 15:13");, #bref("Num 16:3");, #bref("1 Sam 2:8");, #bref("Zeph 2:7"); \[but read #he[עַל הַיָּם];\]; and to a verbal idea contained in the preceding clause, in #bref("Ezek 33:18");, #bref("Job 22:21"); (#he[בָּהֶם]; #emph[thereby];), #bref("Ezek 18:26");, #bref("33:19"); (#he[עֲלֵיהֶם]; #emph[on that account, thereby];).#footnote[In #bref("2 Kgs 7:10"); for #he[שֹׁעֵר]; (the LXX had #he[שַׁ֫עַר];) read #he[שֹֽׁעֲרֵי];.]; But the suffix in #he[נְתָנוֹ]; #bref("Deut 21:10"); refers to the collective idea contained in #he[אֹֽיְבֶ֫יךָ];; in #bref("Jonah 1:3"); #he[עִמָּהֶם]; refers to the sailors included in sense under the term #he[אֳנִיָּה];. In #bref("Josh 2:4"); read #he[וַתִּצְפְּנֵם];; in #bref("Isa 30:6"); (#he[מֵהֶם];), 38:16, #bref("Ps 19:5"); (#he[בָּהֶם];) the text is most probably corrupt.

#ml("q") <p135q> 3. In a few examples the force of the noun\-suffix or possessive pronoun has become so weak that the language appears to be almost entirely unconscious of it. Thus in #he[אֲדֹנָי]; #emph[my Lord];, usually explained as being from the #emph[pluralis maiestatis]; #he[אֲדֹנִים]; (#xref(<p124i>)[§ 124#h(0.18em)#emph[i];];) with the suffix of the 1st singular (always with #emph[Qameṣ]; to distinguish it from #he[אֲדֹנַי]; #emph[my lords];, #bref("Gen 19:2");; but see note below), used exclusively of God, not only in addressing him (#bref("Gen 15:2");, #bref("18:3");, #bref("Ps 35:23");), but ultimately (see, however, the note below), without any regard to the pronoun, as equivalent to #emph[the Lord];.#footnote[Cf. the same weakening of the force of the possessive pronoun in #he[רַבִּי]; prop. #emph[my master];, from the second century #text(size: 0.9em)[A.D.]; onwards #emph[the master];; so also in Syriac #he[מרי]; #emph[my lord];, and ultimately as a title #emph[the lord];; in Italian #emph[Madonna];, French #emph[Madame, Notre Dame, Monsieur, Monseigneur];, etc. It can, however, hardly be doubted that the regular distinction between #he[אֲדֹנָי]; as a holy name, and #he[אֲדֹנִי]; as an ordinary appellative is merely due to the practice of the later Rabbis. G. H. Dalman, #emph[Der Gottesname Adonaj und seine Geschichte]; (Berlin, 1889), in an exhaustive discussion, shows that apart from the book of Daniel and the eight critically doubtful passages, in which #he[אדני]; is used by God himself, there is nowhere any necessity to regard the suffix as #emph[entirely]; meaningless, since #he[אדני]; is always used either in an address #emph[to]; or (like #he[אֲדֹנִי];, which also is never a mere phrase or title) in reverent language #emph[about]; God—as the Lord of the speaker—like the Assyrian #emph[bēli\-ia];, #emph[my lord];. Against any original distinction between #he[אֲדֹנָי]; and #he[אֲדֹנִי]; it may be urged especially that when unconnected with suffixes the singular #he[אָדוֹן]; is always used of God, and not the #emph[pluralis maiestatis]; presupposed by #he[אֲדֹנָי];.]; On #he[אֲדֹנָי]; as a #emph[Q#super[e];rê perpetuum]; of the Masoretes for #he[יהוה]; see #xref(<p17c>)[§ 17#h(0.18em)#emph[c];]; and #xref(<p102m>)[§ 102#h(0.18em)#emph[m];];.

#ml("r") <p135r> A similar loss of vitality in the suffix is generally assumed in #he[יַחְדָּו]; prop. #emph[in his unitedness];, i.e. #emph[he]; etc. #emph[together];, e.g. #he[כָּל־הָעָם יַחְדָּו]; #bref("Exod 19:8");; then, without regard to the suffix, even after the 1st person #he[אֲנַ֫חְנוּ יַחְדָּו]; #bref("1 Kgs 3:18"); in reference to two women; #bref("Isa 41:1");, #bref("Job 9:32");, #bref("Neh 6:2");, 7; after the 2nd person, #bref("Isa 45:20");, etc. But the supposed pronominal suffix is perhaps rather to be explained, with Brockelmann, #emph[ZA.]; xiv. 344 f., as an old adverbial ending, which survives in the Arabic adverbs in #emph[u]; and in Assyrian.—Cf. further #he[כֻּלָּם]; prop. #emph[their entirety];, but also after the 2nd person equivalent to #emph[all together];, #bref("1 Kgs 22:28");, #bref("Mic 1:2"); (#emph[hear, ye peoples, all of you];; cf. #xref(<p144p>)[§ 144#h(0.18em)#emph[p];];), and even before the 2nd person, #bref("Job 17:10"); (in #bref("1 Sam 6:4"); read #he[לָכֶם]; with the LXX).—On the redundant suffix in #he[הָֽעֶרְכְּךָ]; #bref("Lev 27:23");, cf. #xref(<p127i>)[§ 127#h(0.18em)#emph[i];];. #origpage("442") <orig-442>;

#sec("136", [The Demonstrative Pronoun], note: []) <s136>

#ml("a") <p136a> The #emph[demonstrative pronouns]; are #he[זֶה];, fem. #he[זֹאת];, plur. #he[אֵ֫לֶּה]; (§ 34), #emph[hic, haec]; (#emph[hoc];), #emph[hi];, etc., and the personal pronoun #he[הוּא];, likewise used as a demonstrative, fem. #he[הִיא];, plur. masc. #he[הֵ֫מָּה];, fem. #he[הֵ֫נָּה]; (#xref(<p32b>)[§ 32#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];), #emph[is];, #emph[ea]; (#emph[id];), or #emph[ille];, etc., #emph[ii];, #emph[eae]; or #emph[illi];, etc. The distinction between them in usage is that #he[זֶה]; (like #emph[hic];, ὅδε) almost always points out a (new) person or thing present, while #he[הוּא]; (like #emph[is];, #emph[ille];, αὐτός, ἐκεῖνος) refers to a person or thing already mentioned or known (see the examples below).#footnote[On #he[זֶה]; and #he[הוּא]; standing separately as determinate in themselves, see #xref(<p125i>)[§ 125#h(0.18em)#emph[i];];. On the use of determinate demonstratives as adjectives, see #xref(<p126u>)[§ 126#h(0.18em)#emph[u];];.];

#ml("b") <p136b> Rem. 1. Compare the instructive examples in #bref("Gen 32:3");, #bref("Judg 7:4"); #emph[of whom I say unto thee, this]; (#he[זֶה];) #emph[shall go with thee, he]; (#he[הוּא];) #emph[shall go with thee]; (so afterwards with negatives). Moreover, #he[הַיּוֹם הַזֶּה]; #emph[this day];, i.e. the actual day on which one is speaking or writing (#bref("Gen 26:33");, etc.), but #he[הַיּוֹם הַהוּא]; the day or period of which the historian has just been speaking (#bref("Gen 15:18");, #bref("26:32");) or of which the prophet has just been foretelling (#bref("Isa 5:30");, #bref("7:18");, 20 ff.) and of which he continues to speak or foretell. Nevertheless #he[זֶה]; and #he[אֵ֫לֶּה]; are also found in certain common combinations where #he[הוּא]; and #he[הֵ֫מָּה]; would be expected, and vice versa; thus almost always #he[הַדָּבָר הַזֶּה];, plur. #he[הַדְּבָרִים הָאֵ֫לֶּה];, but #he[בַּיָּמִים הָהֵ֫מָּה]; or #he[בַּיָּמִים הָהֵם];.—With a secondary sense of contempt (like Latin #emph[iste];) #he[זֶה]; occurs, e.g. in #bref("1 Sam 10:27");, #bref("21:16");, #bref("1 Kgs 22:27");, #bref("Isa 6:10");, etc. In the sense of the neuter, #emph[this];, #he[זֹאת]; is more common than #he[זֶה];, as #bref("Isa 5:25");, #bref("43:9");, etc., but #he[הוּא]; more common than #he[הִיא];.

#ml("c") <p136c> 2. Both #he[זֶה]; and #he[הוּא]; are sometimes used almost as enclitics to emphasize interrogative words (like the Latin #emph[nam]; in #emph[quisnam];; cf. also #emph[quis tandem];); e.g. #bref("Job 38:2"); #he[מִי זֶה]; #emph[who now]; (darkeneth, etc.)...? #bref("1 Sam 17:55"); f., #bref("Isa 63:1");, #bref("Jer 49:19");, #bref("Ps 24:8");, #bref("25:12");, etc.; #he[מַה־זֶּה]; #emph[what now?]; #bref("1 Sam 10:11");; #emph[how now?]; #bref("Gen 27:20");; #emph[why now?]; #bref("Judg 18:24");; but before the verb #he[עָשָׂה]; it is usually #he[מַה־זֹּאת]; #bref("Gen 3:13");, #bref("12:18");, #bref("Exod 14:5");, #bref("Judg 15:11");; #he[לָ֫מָּה־זֶּה]; #emph[wherefore now?]; #bref("Gen 18:13");, #bref("25:22");, #bref("1 Sam 17:28");, #bref("2 Sam 12:23");, etc.—So also #he[מִי־הוּא]; #bref("Isa 50:9");, #bref("Job 4:7"); ff.; and still more emphatically #he[מִי הוּא־זֶה]; #bref("Ps 24:10");, #bref("Jer 30:21");.

#ml("d") <p136d> 3. #he[זֶה]; is likewise used as an enclitic (see #emph[c]; above): (#emph[a];) #emph[of place];, in such passages as #bref("Gen 27:21"); #he[הַֽאַתָּה זֶה וגו׳]; #emph[whether thou]; (that art here) #emph[be my son Esau?]; #bref("2 Sam 2:20"); #emph[is it thou?]; #he[הִנֵּה־זֶה]; #emph[behold, here];, #bref("1 Kgs 19:5");, #bref("Isa 21:9");;#footnote[On the other hand, it is very questionable whether #he[זֶה]; in #bref("Ps 104:25"); (#he[זֶה הַיָּם];), #bref("Isa 23:13"); (#he[זֶה הָעָם];), #bref("Judg 5:5");, #bref("Ps 68:9"); (#he[זֶה סִינַי];) can be taken, according to the common explanation, simply as a prefixed demonstrative particle (#emph[the sea yonder];, etc.). In #bref("Ps 104:25"); #he[הַיָּם]; may be in apposition to #he[זֶה];; cf. § 126 #emph[aa];, on #bref("Exod 32:1");, and #bref("Zech 5:7");, where #he[אִשָּׁה אַחַת]; is in apposition to #he[זֹאת]; depending on #he[הִנֵּה];, and also #bref("Ezek 40:45");, where #he[הַלִּשְׁכָּה]; is in apposition to #he[זֹה];; otherwise it is most naturally taken as the subject, #emph[this is the sea];. #bref("Isa 23:13");, #bref("Judg 5:5");, #bref("1 Kgs 14:14");, and #bref("Ps 68:9"); might also be explained in the same way; but in these passages the text is almost certainly corrupt. In #bref("Judg 5:5"); in fact #he[זֶה סִינַי]; is most probably to be regarded with Moore as a very early gloss, which subsequently found its way from this passage into Ps 68.]; cf. also the strengthen\- #origpage("443") <orig-443>;ing of the separate pronoun by #he[הוּא]; #bref("Isa 43:25"); (#he[אָֽנֹכִי];), #bref("1 Sam 7:28");, #bref("Isa 37:16");, #bref("Ps 44:5"); (#he[אַתָּה];), and #he[אֵ֫לֶּה הֵם]; #emph[these are];, #bref("Gen 25:16");, #bref("1 Sam 4:8");; (#emph[b];) #emph[of time];: #he[עַתָּה זֶה]; #emph[now];, #bref("1 Kgs 17:24");; #emph[just now];, #bref("2 Kgs 5:22");; and rather frequently before words denoting number, e.g. #bref("Gen 27:36"); #he[זֶה פַֽעֲמַ֫יִם]; #emph[twice, now];; cf. 31:38, #bref("2 Sam 14:2");, #bref("Job 1:12");, #bref("7:3");, #bref("19:3");; separated from the numeral in #bref("Gen 31:41"); #he[זֶה־לִּי]; elliptically for #emph[this];, i.e. this present period, #emph[is to me];, i.e. makes altogether, #emph[twenty years];, etc. The other examples are similarly elliptical.

#sec("137", [The Interrogative Pronoun], note: []) <s137>

#ml("a") <p137a> The interrogative pronoun #he[מִי]; #emph[who]; may refer either to a masculine or feminine person (#bref("Song 3:6");), or even to a plural, e.g. #he[מִי אַתֶּם]; #emph[who are ye?]; #bref("Josh 9:8");; #he[מִי־אֵ֫לֶּה]; #bref("Gen 33:5");, #bref("Num 22:9"); (more minutely, #he[מִי וָמִי]; #bref("Exod 10:8");, i.e. #emph[who exactly, who in particular?];). It is used of the neuter only when the idea of a person is implied, e.g. #he[מִֽי־שְׁכֶם]; #emph[who are the Shechemites?]; #bref("Judg 9:28");, #bref("13:17");, #bref("Gen 33:8");, #bref("Mic 1:5");; even more boldly, with the repetition of a #he[מִי]; used personally, in #bref("1 Sam 18:18");, #bref("2 Sam 7:18");.—Another interrogative is #he[אֵי־זֶה]; #emph[which, what?];; of persons only in #bref("Esth 7:5");.

#ml("b") <p137b> Moreover, #he[מִי]; may also be used in the sense of a genitive, e.g. #he[בַּת־מִי אַתְּ]; #emph[whose daughter art thou?]; #bref("Gen 24:23");, #bref("1 Sam 17:55");, 56, 58; #he[דְּבַר מִי]; #emph[whose word?]; #bref("Jer 44:28");, #bref("1 Sam 12:3");; in the accusative, #he[אֶת־מִי]; #emph[quemnam?]; #bref("1 Sam 28:11");, #bref("Isa 6:8");; with prepositions, e.g. #he[בְּמִי]; #bref("1 Kgs 20:14"); (in an abrupt question #emph[by whom?];); #he[לְמִי]; #bref("Gen 32:18");; #he[אַֽחֲרֵי מִי]; #bref("1 Sam 24:15");.—Similarly #he[מָה];, #he[מַה־];, #he[מֶה]; #emph[what?]; is used for the nominative, or accusative, or genitive (#bref("Jer 8:9");), or with prepositions, e.g. #he[עַל־מָה]; #emph[whereupon?]; #bref("Isa 1:5");, #bref("Job 38:6");; #emph[why?]; #bref("Num 22:32");, etc.; #he[עַד־מָה]; #emph[quousque?]; #bref("Ps 74:9");.#footnote[A quite different use of #he[מָה]; was pointed out (privately) by P. Haupt in #bref("Song 5:8"); #emph[will ye not tell him?]; i.e. #emph[I charge you that ye tell him];, and 7:1 \= #emph[look now at the Shulamite];, corresponding to the late Arabic #emph[mâ tarâ];, #emph[just see! mâ taqûlu];, #emph[say now!]; It has long been recognized that #he[מָה]; is used as a negative in #bref("Song 8:4");.];

#ml("c") <p137c> Rem. Both #he[מִי]; and #he[מָה]; are used also in indirect questions (on the merely relative distinction between direct and indirect questions in Hebrew, see the #emph[Interrogative Sentences];), e.g. #bref("Gen 39:8"); (but read #he[מְא֫וּמָה]; with Samar. and LXX), 43:22, #bref("Exod 32:1");.—On the meaning of #he[מִי]; and #he[מָה]; as interrogatives is based also their use as #emph[indefinite pronouns]; (equivalent to #emph[quisquis, quodcunque]; or #emph[quicquam];), e.g. #bref("Exod 32:26");, #bref("Judg 7:3");, #bref("1 Sam 20:4");, #bref("Isa 50:10"); (read #he[יִשְׁמַע]; in the apodosis), 54:15, #bref("Prov 9:4");, 16, #bref("2 Chr 36:23");; even #he[שִׁמְרוּ־מִי]; #emph[have a care, whosoever ye be];, #bref("2 Sam 18:12"); (unless #he[לִי]; is to be read, with the LXX, for #he[מִי];); so also #he[מָה]; (#emph[whatever it be];) #bref("Job 13:13");, #bref("1 Sam 19:3");, #bref("2 Sam 18:22");, 23; cf. #bref("Num 23:3"); #he[וּדְבַר מַה־יַּרְאֵ֫נִי]; #emph[and whatsoever he showeth me];. Cf. also #he[מִי אֲשֶׁר]; #emph[whosoever]; #bref("Exod 32:33");, #bref("2 Sam 20:11");, and #he[מִֽי־הָאִישׁ אֲשֶׁר]; #emph[any man who]; #bref("Deut 20:5"); ff., #origpage("444") <orig-444>;#bref("Judg 10:18");. A still further weakening of the #emph[indefinite]; use of #he[מָה]; is the combination #he[מַה־שֶּׁ·]; #emph[that which];, #bref("Eccl 1:9");, #bref("3:15"); (just like the Syriac #he[מָא דְ];); cf. #bref("Esth 8:1");, and #he[בַּל... מָה]; #bref("Prov 9:13");, #he[לֹא... מָה]; #bref("Neh 2:12");, #emph[nothing whatever];.—On #he[מְא֫וּמָה]; #emph[quicquam];, #emph[anything at all]; (usually with a negative), and as an adverb #emph[in any way];, #bref("1 Sam 21:3");, see the Lexicon.

#sec("138", [The Relative Pronoun], note: []) <s138>

#align(center)[Cf. Philippi, #emph[Stat. constr.]; (see heading of § 89), p. 71 f., and especially V. Baumann, #emph[Hebräische Relativsätze];, Leipzig, 1894.];

#ml("a") <p138a> Relative clauses are most frequently (but not necessarily; cf. #xref(<p155b>)[§ 155#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];) introduced by the indeclinable #he[אֲשֶׁר]; (see § 36).#footnote[The etymology of the word is still a matter of dispute. Against the identification of #he[אֲשֶׁר];, as an original substantive, with the Arabic #emph[ʾaṯar];, #emph[trace];, Aram. #he[אֲתַר]; #emph[place, trace];, Nöldeke urges (#emph[ZDMG.]; xl. 738) that the expression #emph[trace of];... could hardly have developed into the relative conjunction, while the meaning of #emph[place]; has been evolved only in Aramaic, where the word is never used as a relative. According to others, #he[אֲשֶׁר]; is really a compound of several pronominal roots; cf. Sperling, #emph[Die Nota relationis im Hebräischen];, Leipzig, 1876, and König, #emph[Lehrgeb.];, ii. 323 ff., who follows Ewald and Böttcher in referring it to an original #he[אֲשַׁל];. According to Hommel (#emph[ZDMG.]; xxxii. 708 ff.) #he[אֲשֶׁר]; is an original substantive, to be distinguished from #he[שֶׁ·]; and #he[שַׁ·]; (an original pronominal stem), but used in Hebrew as a #emph[nota relationis];, or (as #he[זֶה]; and #he[זוּ]; are also sometimes used, see below, #emph[g]; and #emph[h];) simply for the #emph[relative pronoun];. Baumann (op. cit., p. 44) sees in the Assyrian #emph[ša];, Phoenician, Punic, and Hebrew #he[שֶׁ];, the ground\-forms, of which the Phoenician and Punic #he[אש]; (see above, § 36 note) and the Hebrew #he[אֲשֶׁר]; are developments.]; This is not, however, a relative pronoun in the Greek, Latin, or English sense, nor is it a mere #emph[nota relationis];,#footnote[E.g. like Luther’s use of #emph[so];, in #emph[die fremden Götter, so unter euch sind];, #bref("Gen 35:2");.]; but an original #emph[demonstrative]; pronoun \[as though #emph[iste, istius];, etc.\].#footnote[This is the necessary conclusion both from the analogy of the Arabic #emph[ʾallaḏi];, which is clearly a demonstrative (like the Hebr. #he[הַלָּז];, #he[הַלָּזֶה];), and from the use of #he[זֶה]; and #he[זוּ]; as relatives.]; Hence it is used—

(1) In immediate dependence on the substantival idea to be defined, and virtually in the same case as it (hence belonging syntactically to the main clause); e.g. #bref("Gen 24:7");... #he[יְהֹוָה אֲשֶׁר לְקָחַ֫נִי... הוּא יִשְׁלַח]; #emph[the Lord, iste, he took me];... #emph[he shall send];, etc. (\= #emph[who took me];); #bref("Gen 2:2"); #emph[and God finished]; #he[מְלַאכְתּוֹ אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה]; #emph[his work, istud, he had made]; (it). Such qualifying clauses may be called #emph[dependent relative clauses];.

#ml("b") <p138b> Rem. 1. In the above examples #he[אֲשֶׁר]; in #bref("Gen 24:7"); is virtually in the nominative, in #bref("Gen 2:2"); in the accusative. A further distinction between the examples is that in #bref("Gen 24:7"); the main idea (#he[יהוה];), to which #he[אֲשֶׁר]; is added in apposition, is only resumed in the qualifying clause by the subject (#emph[he];) inherent in #origpage("445") <orig-445>;#he[לְקָחַ֫נִי];, while in #bref("Gen 2:2"); it is not resumed at all. This suppression of the retrospective pronoun#footnote[The instances in which, instead of a retrospective pronoun, the main idea itself is repeated (#bref("Gen 49:30");, #bref("50:13");, #bref("Jer 31:32");) are most probably all due to subsequent amplification of the original text by another hand.]; takes place especially when it (as in #bref("Gen 2:2");) would represent an accusative of the object, or when it would be a separate pronoun representing a nominative of the subject in a noun\-clause, e.g. #bref("Gen 1:7"); #he[הַמַּ֫יִם אֲשֶׁר מִתַּחַת לָֽרָקִיעַ]; #emph[the waters, those, under the firmament];, etc. In negative sentences, however, the retrospective pronoun is not infrequently added, e.g. #bref("Gen 17:12"); #he[הוּא];; 7:2 #he[הִיא];; #bref("1 Kgs 9:20"); #he[הֵ֫מָּה];; #bref("Deut 20:15"); #he[הֵ֫נָּה];; but cf. also #he[אֲשֶׁר הוּא חַי]; #bref("Gen 9:3");. The addition of #he[הִיא]; in a verbal clause, #bref("2 Kgs 22:13");, is unusual.

The very frequent omission of the retrospective pronoun is noticeable in cases where the predicate of the qualifying clause is a #emph[verbum dicendi];, e.g. #bref("Num 10:29"); #emph[we are journeying unto the place];, #he[אֲשֶׁר אָמַר יְהוָֹה אֹתוֹ אֶתֵּן לָכֶם]; #emph[that]; place, #emph[the Lord said]; (of it), #emph[It will I give to you];; cf. #bref("Num 14:40");, #bref("Judg 8:15");, #bref("1 Sam 9:17");, 23, 24:5, #bref("1 Kgs 8:29");, #bref("Jer 32:43");.

#ml("c") <p138c> 2. When the substantive, followed by #he[אֲשֶׁר]; and the qualifying clause, expresses an idea of #emph[place];, it may also be resumed by the adverbs of place #he[שָׁם]; #emph[there];, #he[שָׁ֫מָּה]; #emph[thither];, #he[מִשָּׁם]; #emph[thence];, e.g. #bref("Gen 13:3"); #he[עַד־הַמָּקוֹם אֲשֶׁר־הָיָה שָׁם אָֽהֳלֹה]; #emph[unto the place, that one, his tent had been there];, i.e. #emph[where his tent had been];; cf. #bref("Gen 3:23"); #he[מִשָּׁם];, #bref("Exod 21:13"); #he[שָׁ֫מָּה];. But even in this case the retrospective word may be omitted, cf. #bref("Gen 35:14");, #bref("Num 20:13");, #bref("Isa 64:10");, where #he[שָׁם]; would be expected, and #bref("Gen 30:38");, #bref("Num 13:27");, #bref("1 Kgs 12:2");, where #he[שָׁ֫מָּה]; would be expected.—When the appositional clause is added to a word of time, the retrospective pronoun is always omitted, e.g. #bref("1 Sam 20:31"); #emph[for all the days];, #he[אֲשֶׁר בֶּן־יִשַׁי חַי]; #emph[those—the son of Jesse is living]; (#emph[in them];); cf. #bref("Gen 45:6");, #bref("Deut 1:46");, #bref("9:7");, #bref("1 Kgs 11:42");; see Baumann, op. cit., p. 33.

#ml("d") <p138d> 3. If the governing substantive forms part of a statement made in the first or second person, the retrospective pronoun (or the subject of the appositional clause) is in the same person, e.g. #bref("Gen 45:4"); #emph[I am Joseph];, #he[אֲשֶׁר־מְכַרְתֶּם אֹתִי]; #emph[he—ye sold me];, i.e. #emph[whom ye sold];; #bref("Num 22:30");, #bref("Isa 49:23");; 41:8 #emph[thou, Jacob];, #he[אֲשֶׁר בְּחַרְתִּ֫יךָ]; #emph[he—I have chosen thee];; #bref("Jer 33:19");, #bref("Eccl 10:16"); f.; #bref("Gen 15:7"); #emph[I am the Lord];, #he[אֲשֶׁר הֽוֹצֵאתִ֫יךָ]; #emph[he—I brought thee out];, etc., #bref("Exod 20:2"); (#bref("Deut 5:6");).

#ml("e") <p138e> (2) Not depending (adjectivally) on a governing substantive, but itself expressing a substantival idea. Clauses introduced in this way may be called #emph[independent relative clauses];. This use of #he[אֲשֶׁר]; is generally rendered in English by #emph[he who, he whom];, etc. (according to the context), or #emph[that which];, etc., or sometimes #emph[of such a kind as]; (#emph[qualis];), cf. #bref("Exod 14:13");#super[#emph[b];];, and in a dependent relative clause #bref("Isa 7:17");. In reality, however, the #he[אֲשֶׁר]; is still a demonstrative belonging to the construction of the main clause as subject or object, or as a genitive dependent on a noun or preposition, e.g. #bref("Num 22:6"); #he[אֲשֶׁר תָּאֹר יוּאָר]; #emph[iste—thou cursest]; (him)—#emph[is cursed];, i.e. #emph[he whom thou cursest];, etc.; #bref("Exod 22:8");;#footnote[The absolute use of #he[אֲשֶׁר]; is very peculiar in the formula #he[אֲשֶׁר הָיָה דְבַר יי׳ אֶל־]; #emph[this]; (is it)—#emph[it came as the word of the Lord to];..., #bref("Jer 14:1");, #bref("46:1");, #bref("47:1");, #bref("49:34");.]; #he[אֲשֶׁר]; as object, #bref("Gen 44:1");, #origpage("446") <orig-446>;49:1, #bref("1 Sam 16:3"); ff., #bref("Mic 6:1"); (#he[אֵת אֲשֶׁר];); and even preceding the verb, e.g. #bref("Isa 52:15");, #bref("Ps 69:5");; #he[אֲשֶׁר]; as genitive, #bref("Ezek 23:28"); #emph[I will deliver thee]; #he[בְּיַד אֲשֶׁר שָׂנֵאת]; #emph[into the hand of those—thou hatest]; (them); depending on a preposition, e.g. #he[לַֽאֲשֶׁר]; #bref("Gen 44:4");, #bref("2 Kgs 10:22");; #he[בַּֽאֲשֶׁר]; #bref("Gen 21:17");, #he[בַּֽאֲשֶׁר הוּא שָׁם]; #emph[in that]; (place)—#emph[he is there];, i.e. #emph[where he is];; cf. #bref("Judg 17:8"); and #bref("Ruth 1:16"); #he[אֶל־אֲשֶׁר]; #emph[whither];;#footnote[In #bref("Zech 12:10"); also, instead of the unintelligible #he[אלי את אשר];, we should probably read #he[אֶל־אֲשֶׁר];, and refer the passage to this class.]; #bref("1 Kgs 18:12"); #he[עַל־אֲשֶׁר]; #emph[whither];; #he[מֵֽאֲשֶׁר]; #bref("Exod 5:11");.

#ml("f") <p138f> From these examples it follows that in independent relative clauses the retrospective suffix, or adverb of place, may be, and in fact generally is, omitted. As a rule, however (as in the dependent relative clause), this does not apply to cases in which the retrospective pronoun, by the construction of the sentence, depends on a preposition,#footnote[Such a strong ellipse as in #bref("Isa 31:6");, where #he[מִמֶּ֫נּוּ]; would be expected after #he[העמיקו];, is only possible in elevated poetic or prophetic language.]; e.g. #bref("Gen 44:9"); f. #he[אֲשֶׁר יִמָּצֵא אִתּוֹ... וָמֵת]; #emph[he—it]; (the cup) #emph[is found with him,—shall die]; (for the #emph[Wāw]; of the apodosis in #he[וָמֵת]; cf. #xref(<p143d>)[§ 143#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];). In such cases #he[אֲשֶׁר]; preceded by the preposition is quite anomalous, as in #bref("Gen 31:32"); #he[עִם אֲשֶׁר תִּמְצָא]; #emph[with whomsoever thou findest];, where #he[אֲשֶׁר]; is a relative pronoun in the English sense; on the other hand, in #bref("Isa 47:12"); (and probably also 56:4) #he[בַּֽאֲשֶׁר]; is to be explained (with Baumann, op. cit., p. 37) by reference to 47:15, as a demonstrative pronoun, #emph[stand now with thine enchantments..., with those—thou hast laboured]; (with them).

\[With regard to the preceding explanation of #he[אֲשֶׁר];, the student will of course understand that, in Hebrew as we know it, #he[אֲשֶׁר]; never occurs as a mere demonstrative. A particle which, whatever its origin, is uniformly used with reference to something in another, contiguous clause, will naturally have acquired in practice that force which we denote by the term ‘relative’.\]

#ml("g") <p138g> Like the original demonstrative pronoun #he[אֲשֶׁר];, the demonstratives proper #he[זֶה];, #he[זוֹ];, #he[זוּ]; (the last commonly),#footnote[The etymological equivalent #he[דִּי];, #he[דְּ]; in Aramaic is always a relative.]; and sometimes the article, are used somewhat frequently in poetic language to introduce both dependent and independent relative clauses. With regard to the construction of #he[זֶה];, etc., the remarks on #he[אֲשֶׁר];, under #emph[a]; and #emph[e];, also hold good.

Examples:—

(#emph[a];) #he[זֶה]; in apposition to a governing substantive in the nominative, #bref("Ps 104:26"); #he[לִוְיָתָן זֶה־יָצַ֫רְתָּ]; (there is) #emph[leviathan, he—thou hast formed]; (him), i.e. #emph[whom thou hast formed];; #bref("Isa 42:24"); (#he[זוּ];); in the accusative, #bref("Isa 25:9");, #bref("Ps 74:2"); (in both cases with a retrospective pronoun; #he[זוֹ]; is used without it in #bref("Ps 132:12");); in apposition to a genitive dependent on a preposition, #bref("Prov 23:22"); #he[שְׁמַע לְאָבִ֫יךָ זֶה יְלָדֶ֑ךָ]; #emph[hearken unto thy father, him—he begat thee];, i.e. #emph[who begat thee];; #bref("Ps 17:9"); (#he[זוּ];).—In #bref("Ps 104:8"); #he[אֶל־מְקוֹם זֶה יָסַ֫דְתָּ לָהֶם]; #emph[unto the place which thou hadst founded for them]; (cf. #xref(<p130c>)[§ 130#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];), #he[זֶה]; is in the genitive after the construct state #he[מְקוֹם]; #emph[to the place of that, thou hadst]; #origpage("447") <orig-447>;#emph[founded]; (it) #emph[for them];; on the same analogy we may also take, with Baumann (op. cit., p. 48), #bref("Ps 78:54"); (#he[חַר זֶה];) and #bref("Exod 15:13"); (#he[עַם־זוּ גָאָ֑לְתָּ];), 15:16, #bref("Isa 43:21");, #bref("Ps 9:16");, #bref("10:2");, #bref("31:5");, #bref("32:8");, #bref("62:12");, #bref("142:4");, #bref("143:8"); (all examples of #he[זוּ];).

#ml("h") <p138h> To introduce independent relative clauses #he[זֶה]; is used as a nominative in #bref("Job 19:19");; as accusative, #bref("Job 15:17"); and #he[זוּ]; #bref("Hab 1:11");, #bref("Ps 68:29"); (after a preposition, #he[זֶה]; #bref("Exod 13:8");; but the text is evidently corrupt).

#ml("i") <p138i> (#emph[b];) More certain examples of the use of the article as a relative pronoun (more correctly, perhaps, of the demonstrative which is otherwise used as article) are #bref("1 Chr 26:28"); #he[כֹּל הַֽהִקְדִּישׁ שְׁמוּאֵל]; #emph[all that Samuel had dedicated];, etc.; #bref("1 Chr 29:8"); (where #he[נִמְצָא]; can only be #emph[perfect Niphʿal];); #bref("2 Chr 29:36");, #bref("Ezra 10:14");. In connection with a plural, #bref("Josh 10:24"); #emph[the chiefs of the men of war]; #he[הֶהָֽלְכוּ אִתּוֹ]; #emph[who went with him];; #bref("Ezra 8:25");, #bref("10:17");, #bref("1 Chr 29:17");. Finally, in the sense of #emph[id quod];, #bref("Jer 5:13"); (where, however, we should read with the LXX #he[הַדָּבָר];). Cf. moreover, #bref("1 Sam 9:24"); #emph[the thigh]; #he[וְהֶֽעָלֶ֫יהָ]; #emph[and that which was upon it]; (but see #emph[k]; below); #bref("2 Chr 1:4"); #he[בַּֽהֵכִין]; equivalent to #he[בַּֽאֲשֶׁר הֵכִין]; #emph[to the place, that he had prepared];.

#ml("k") <p138k> In all the examples adduced except #bref("1 Sam 9:24"); (where #he[וְהָֽאַלְיָה]; should probably be read for #he[וְהֶֽעָלֶ֫יהָ];) the #he[הַ]; is followed by undoubted perfects; almost all the examples, moreover, belong to the latest Books (Ezra and Chronicles). On the other hand, another series of instances (even in the older texts) is extremely doubtful, in which the Masora likewise requires perfects, either by placing the tone on the penultima, as in #bref("Gen 18:21");, #bref("46:27");, #bref("Job 2:11"); #he[הַבָּ֫אָה];; #bref("Isa 51:10"); #he[הַשָּׂ֫מָה];; #bref("Ezek 26:17"); #he[הַֽהֻלָּ֫לָה]; #bref("Ruth 1:22");, #bref("2:6"); and 4:3 #he[הַשָּׁ֫בָה];, or by the punctuation, #bref("Gen 21:3"); #he[הַנּוֹלַד];; #bref("1 Kgs 11:9");, #bref("Dan 8:1"); #he[הַנִּרְאָה];; #bref("Isa 56:3"); #he[הַנִּלְוָה];, while no doubt the authors in all these cases intended participles (and in fact perfect participles, cf. #xref(<p116d>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];) with the article, thus #he[הַבָּאָ֫ה];, etc., #bref("Ezek 26:17"); #he[הַֽהֻלָּלָה]; for #he[הַמְהֻלָּלָה]; according to #xref(<p52s>)[§ 52#h(0.18em)#emph[s];];, and in the other examples #he[הַנּוֹלָד];, #he[הַנִּרְאֶה];, #he[הַנִּלְוֶה];.

#sec("139", [Expression of Pronominal Ideas by means of Substantives], note: []) <s139>

#ml("a") <p139a> Analogous to the periphrases for expressing materials and attributes by means of substantives (#xref(<p128o>)[§ 128#h(0.18em)#emph[o];]; and #emph[p];), is the use of substantives to represent certain kinds of pronominal ideas, for which no special expressions exist. Thus—

#ml("b") <p139b> #strong[1.]; #he[אִישׁ];, #he[אִשָּׁה]; #emph[man, woman];, are used to express—

(#emph[a];) The idea of #emph[each, every]; (in the sense of each severally) with reference to persons,#footnote[As a rule #he[אִישׁ]; is used in the particularizing sense of #emph[each man];, with the plural of the verb, e.g. #bref("Gen 44:11");; sometimes, however, as subject to a verb in the singular, e.g. #bref("Gen 44:13");.]; and even animals (#bref("Gen 15:10");), e.g. #bref("Gen 10:5");, feminine #bref("Exod 3:22");; #he[אִישׁ]; is the object, e.g. in #bref("Jer 12:15");. On #he[אִישׁ];—#he[אִישׁ]; cf. #xref(<p123c>)[§ 123#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];.

#ml("c") <p139c> In a few passages #he[אִישׁ]; in the above sense is placed for the sake of emphasis before the governing noun (always a substantive with a suffix), thus #he[מִיַּד אִישׁ אָחִיו]; #bref("Gen 9:5");, according to the usual explanation, stands for #he[מִיַּד אֲחִי אִישׁ]; #emph[at the hand of the brother of every man];. But although the explanation seems to be #origpage("448") <orig-448>;supported by #bref("Gen 42:25"); and #bref("Num 17:17");, it is inconceivable that such an inversion of #emph[nomen regens]; and #emph[rectum]; should occur. It is more likely, either that the second substantive is in apposition to #he[אִישׁ]; (thus #bref("Gen 9:5"); #emph[at the hand of every man, his brother];, \[unless it is a combination of the two readings #he[מִיַּד אִישׁ]; and #he[מִיַּד הָֽאָדָם];\]; similarly 15:10 #emph[and he laid each]; or, more exactly, #emph[one piece of it];, etc., and so probably also #bref("Num 17:17"); #emph[every one];, sc. #emph[his name];), or #he[אִישׁ]; precedes as a kind of #emph[casus pendens];, and only receives its nearer definition from the following substantive with suffix; thus #bref("Gen 41:12");, #bref("42:25"); (according to the context \= #emph[to every one in his sack];); 42:35, where #he[צְרוֹר־כַּסְפּוֹ בְּשַׂקּוֹ]; is virtually the predicate of #he[אִישׁ];; #bref("Exod 12:4");, #bref("28:21");, #bref("Num 5:10");, #bref("26:54");, #bref("2 Kgs 23:35");, and especially #bref("Zech 7:10");.#footnote[Cf. on the whole question the thorough discussion by Budde, #emph[Die bibl. Urgeschichte];, p. 283 ff.: according to him, the words in #bref("Gen 9:5"); are to be rendered #emph[at the hand of one another]; (from men mutually) #emph[will I require it];. \[In support of this view, Budde points to #bref("Zech 7:10"); #he[וְרָעַת אִישׁ אָחִיו אַל־תַּחְשְׁבוּ בִּלְבַבְכֶם];, which in the light of 8:17, #he[וְאִישׁ אֶת־רָעַת רֵעֵהוּ אַל־תַּחְשְׁבוּ בִּלְבַבְכֶם];, can only, he observes, be rendered ‘and devise not #emph[the hurt of one another]; in your heart’. So also König, #emph[Syntax];, § 33.\]];

#ml("d") <p139d> (#emph[b];) #emph[Any one, some one];, e.g. #bref("Gen 13:16");, #bref("Song 8:7");, with a negative #emph[no one];;#footnote[Cf. also #he[אֵין־אִישׁ]; #bref("Gen 39:11");. On the expression of the idea of #emph[no one]; by means of #he[אֵין]; with a following participle, see the #emph[Negative Sentences];, #xref(<p152l>)[§ 152#h(0.18em)#emph[l];];.]; so after #he[אַל־]; #bref("Exod 16:19");, 29; before #he[לֹא]; #bref("Gen 23:6"); and frequently.—Instead of #he[אִישׁ]; we sometimes find in a similar sense #he[אָדָם]; #emph[man, homo];, e.g. #bref("Lev 1:2"); (cf. #he[כְּאַחַד הָֽאָדָם]; #emph[as any one else];, #bref("Judg 16:7");, 11), #he[נֶ֫פֶשׁ]; (#emph[soul];) #emph[person];, #bref("Lev 2:1");, #bref("5:1");, etc., and in a neuter sense #he[דָּבָר]; (prop. word, thing) for #emph[anything];, #bref("Gen 18:14");, or #he[כָּל־דָּבָר]; #bref("Lev 5:2");, #bref("Num 31:23");. With a negative #he[דָּבָר]; means #emph[nothing];; thus after #he[אַל־]; #bref("Gen 19:8");; after #he[לֹא]; #bref("Eccl 8:5");.—Cf. finally, #he[מֵֽאַחַד]; #emph[any one];, #bref("Deut 15:7");; #emph[anything];, #bref("Ezek 18:10"); (but in #bref("Lev 4:2");, #bref("5:13"); #he[מֵֽאַחַת];) and the expressions noticed in #xref(<p144e>)[§ 144#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];. The latter include also instances like #bref("Ezek 18:32"); #emph[I have no pleasure]; #he[בְּמוֹת הַמֵּת]; #emph[in the death of him that dieth];, i.e. #emph[of any man];.

#ml("e") <p139e> (#emph[c];) In connection with #he[אָחִיו]; #emph[his brother]; or #he[רֵעֵ֫הוּ]; #emph[his neighbour];, #he[אִישׁ]; #emph[one];, masc. (as #he[אִשָּׁה]; #emph[one];, fem., in connection with #he[אֲחוֹתָהּ]; #emph[her sister]; or #he[רְעוּתָהּ]; #emph[her neighbour];) is used to represent the ideas of #emph[alter—alter, the one—the other];#footnote[Elsewhere #he[זֶה... זֶה]; are used in a similar sense, #bref("Exod 14:20");, #bref("Isa 6:3");; also #he[הָֽאֶחָד... הָֽאֶחָד]; #bref("2 Sam 14:6");, or the substantive is repeated, e.g. #bref("Gen 47:21"); (#emph[from one end... to the other end];).]; (in reference to persons, animals, or things without life; see the Lexicon) or the idea of #emph[one another];, e.g. #bref("Gen 13:11"); #emph[and they separated themselves]; #he[אִישׁ מֵעַל אָחִיו]; #emph[the one from the other];; #bref("Exod 26:3"); #emph[five curtains]; (#he[יְרִיעֹת]; fem.) #emph[shall be coupled together]; #he[אִשָּׁה אֶל־אֲחֹתָהּ]; #emph[one to another];.

#ml("f") <p139f> 2. #he[נֶ֫פֶשׁ]; #emph[soul, person]; expresses the idea of #emph[self];,#footnote[On the representation of this idea by pronouns, separate and suffixed, see #xref(<p135a>)[§ 135#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];, #emph[i]; and #emph[k];.]; both in the singular, #bref("Prov 19:8");, 16, 29:24, #bref("Job 18:4"); (in all cases #he[נַפְשׁוֹ]; equivalent to #emph[himself];) and in the plural, #bref("Jer 37:9");, etc. Similar to this is the use of #he[בְּקִרְבָּהּ]; #bref("Gen 18:12"); (prop. #emph[in her inward part];) in the sense of #emph[within herself];.#footnote[In a similar way the idea of #emph[self]; in Arabic, as in Sanskrit (#emph[âtman];), is paraphrased by #emph[soul, spirit];; in Arabic also by #emph[eye];; in Rabbinic by #he[גּוּף]; #emph[body];, #he[גֶּ֫רֶם]; or #he[עֶ֫צֶם]; #emph[bone];, in Ethiopic and Amharic by #emph[head];, in Egyptian by #emph[mouth, hand];, etc.; cf. also the Middle High German #emph[mîn lîp, dîn lîp];, for #emph[ich, du];. However, #he[נֶ֫פֶשׁ]; in such cases is never (not even in #bref("Isa 46:2"); #he[נַפְשָׁם]; #emph[they themselves];) a merely otiose periphrasis for the personal pronoun, but always involves a reference to the #emph[mental]; personality, as affected by the senses, desires, etc.]; #origpage("449") <orig-449>;

#ml("g") <p139g> 3. #he[עֶ֫צֶם]; #emph[bone]; (then metaphorically for #emph[substance];) expresses the idea of #emph[self, selfsame, very same];, in reference to #emph[things]; (as #he[נֶ֫פֶשׁ]; to persons, e.g. #he[בְּעֶ֫צֶם הַיּוֹם הַזֶּה]; #emph[in the selfsame day];, #bref("Gen 7:13");, cf. #bref("Josh 10:27");, #bref("Ezek 24:2");; #he[כְּעֶ֫צֶם הַשָּׁמַ֫יִם לָטֹהַר]; #emph[as it were the very heaven for clearness];, #bref("Exod 24:10");; #he[בְּעֶ֫צֶם תֻּמּוֹ]; #emph[in the very fullness of his strength]; (\= #emph[in the midst of his full strength];), #bref("Job 21:23");.

#ml("h") <p139h> 4. The simple plural of words denoting time sometimes includes also the idea of a #emph[few, some];;#footnote[#emph[Some]; in reference to persons in #bref("Exod 16:20"); is expressed by #he[אֲנָשִׁים];, and in #bref("Neh 5:2");–4 by #he[יֵשׁ אֲשֶׁר]; #emph[sunt qui];, with a participle following.]; thus #he[יָמִים]; #emph[a few days];, #bref("Gen 24:55");, #bref("40:4"); (here even of a longer period, \= #emph[for some time];); #bref("Isa 65:20");, #bref("Dan 8:27"); (on the other hand, #bref("Gen 27:44");, #bref("29:20"); #he[יָמִים אֲחָדִים];; see § 96 under #he[אֶחָד];); #he[שָׁנִים]; #emph[some years];, #bref("Dan 11:6");, 8.

#bookhead(2, [Chapter II. The Sentence], note: []);

#bookhead(3, [I. The Sentence in General], note: []);

#origpage("450") <orig-450>;

#sec("140", [Noun\-clauses, Verbal\-clauses, and the Compound Sentence], note: []) <s140>

#ml("a") <p140a> #strong[1.]; Every sentence, the subject and predicate of which are nouns or their equivalents (esp. participles), is called a #emph[noun\-clause];, e.g. #he[יְהוָֹה מַלְכֵּנוּ]; #emph[the Lord is our king];, #bref("Isa 33:22");; #he[וְאַנְשֵׁי סְדֹם רָעִים וְחַטָּאִים]; #emph[now the men of Sodom were wicked and sinners];, #bref("Gen 13:13");; #he[פֶּה לָהֶם]; #emph[a mouth is theirs];, #bref("Ps 115:5");; see further, § 141.

#ml("b") <p140b> #strong[2.]; Every sentence, the subject of which is a noun (or pronoun included in a verbal\-form) and its predicate a finite verb, is called a #emph[verbal\-clause];, e.g. #he[וַיֹּא֫מֶר אֱלֹהִים]; #emph[and God said];, #bref("Gen 1:3");; #he[וַיַּבְדֵּל]; #emph[and he divided];, 1:7; see further, § 142.

#ml("c") <p140c> Rem. In the last example the pronominal subject is at least indicated by the preformative (#he[י];), and in almost all forms of the perfect by afformatives. The 3rd pers. sing. perf. however, which contains no indication of the subject, must also be regarded as a full verbal\-clause.

#ml("d") <p140d> #strong[3.]; Every sentence, the subject or predicate of which is itself a full clause, is called a #emph[compound sentence];, e.g. #bref("Ps 18:31"); #he[הָאֵל תָּמִים דַּרְכּוֹ]; #emph[God—his way is perfect];, equivalent to #emph[God’s way is perfect];; #bref("Gen 34:8"); #he[שְׁכֶם בְּנִי חָֽשְׁקָה נַפְשׁוֹ בְּבִתְּכֶם]; #emph[my son Shechem—his soul longeth for your daughter];; see further, § 143.

#ml("e") <p140e> #strong[4.]; The above distinction between different kinds of sentences—especially between noun\- and verbal\-clauses—is indispensable to the more delicate appreciation of Hebrew syntax (and that of the Semitic languages generally), since it is by no means merely external or formal, but involves fundamental differences of meaning. Noun\-clauses with a substantive as predicate, represent something #emph[fixed, a state]; or in short, #emph[a being]; so and so; verbal\-clauses on the other hand, something #emph[moveable]; and #emph[in progress];, an #emph[event]; or #emph[action];. The latter description is indeed true in a certain sense also of noun\-clauses #origpage("451") <orig-451>;with a participial predicate, except that in their case the event or action (as distinguished from that expressed by the verbal\-clause) is of a fixed and abiding character.

#ml("f") <p140f> Rem. By the Arab grammarians every clause beginning with an independent subject is regarded as a noun\-clause, and every clause beginning with a finite verb as verbal. If a finite verb follows the noun\-subject the two together (since the verb comprises its own subject and is thus a complete verbal\-clause) form a compound noun\-sentence, just as when the predicate consists of an independent noun\-clause. Though this definition of the different kinds of sentence, which we formerly accepted (in #xref(<p144a>)[§ 144#h(0.18em)#emph[a];]; of the 22nd to the 24th German editions of this Grammar), is rejected above, #emph[a–d];, we must, nevertheless, mention here the point in which this more complicated view of the Arab grammarians may be regarded as at least relatively correct, namely, in classifying verbal\-clauses according as the subject precedes or follows the verb, a distinction which is often of great importance in Hebrew also; see further, in #xref(<p142a>)[§ 142#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];.

#sec("141", [The Noun\-clause], note: []) <s141>

#ml("a") <p141a> #strong[1.]; The #emph[subject]; of a noun\-clause (see #xref(<p140a>)[§ 140#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];) may be—

(#emph[a];) A substantive, e.g. #he[וְנָהָר יֹצֵא מֵעֵ֫דֶן]; #emph[and a river went out]; (was going out) #emph[of Eden];, #bref("Gen 2:10");.

(#emph[b];) A pronoun, e.g. #bref("Gen 7:4"); #he[אָֽנֹכִי מַמְטִיר]; #emph[I will cause it to rain];; #bref("14:18"); #he[וְהוּא כֹהֵן]; #emph[and he was priest];; #bref("2:23"); (#he[זֹאת]; before a feminine predicate, as #he[אֵ֫לֶּה]; before a plural in #bref("Exod 32:4");); #he[מִי חָכָם]; #emph[who is wise?]; #bref("Hos 14:10");.—In #bref("1 Chr 5:2"); #he[וּלְנָגִיד מִמֶּ֫נּוּ]; #emph[and of him]; one became #emph[a prince];, the subject is contained in #he[מִמֶּ֫נּוּ];.#footnote[For other remarkable instances of ellipse in the Chronicler, see Driver, #emph[Introduction];, ed. 8, p. 537, no. 27.];

#ml("b") <p141b> #strong[2.]; The #emph[predicate]; of a noun\-clause may be—

(#emph[a];) A substantive, e.g. #bref("Deut 14:1"); #he[בָּנִים אַתֶּם וגו׳]; #emph[ye are children of the Lord your God];; #bref("Gen 42:13");. Specially characteristic of the Semitic mode of expression are the cases in which both subject and predicate are substantives, thus emphasizing their identity (‘the thing is its measure, material, or equivalent’), e.g. #bref("Ezek 41:22"); #he[הַמִּזְבֵּחַ עֵץ ... וְקִֽירֹתָיו עֵץ]; #emph[the altar]; (was) #emph[wood ..., and the walls thereof]; (were) #emph[wood];, i.e. of wood. Cf. below, #emph[c];.

(#emph[b];) An adjective or participle, e.g. #bref("Gen 2:12"); #he[וּֽזֲהַב הָאָ֫רֶץ הַהִיא טוֹב]; #emph[and the gold of that land is good];; #he[וְעֶפְרוֹן יֹשֵׁב]; #emph[now Ephron was sitting];, etc., #bref("Gen 23:10");.#footnote[Cf. the numerous examples in #xref(<p116n>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[n];];–#emph[p];.]; Very frequently such noun\-clauses, attached by #emph[Wāw]; to a verbal\-clause, are used to represent a state #emph[contemporaneous]; with the principal action; cf. #emph[e]; below.

(#emph[c];) A numeral, e.g. #bref("Gen 42:13"); #he[שְׁנֵים עָשָׂר עֲבָדֶ֫יךָ]; #emph[the twelve]; (of us) #emph[are thy servants];. #origpage("452") <orig-452>;(#emph[d];) A pronoun, e.g. #bref("Gen 10:12"); (#he[הִיא];), #bref("Exod 9:27"); (#he[אֲנִי];), #bref("Gen 24:65"); (#he[מִי];), #bref("1 Kgs 9:13"); (#he[מָה];).#footnote[Why in these examples the pronouns, notwithstanding appearances to the contrary, are to be considered as predicates and not as subjects, may be seen from what has been remarked above, #xref(<p126k>)[§ 126#h(0.18em)#emph[k];];.];

(#emph[e];) An adverb or (esp. if formed with a preposition) any specification of time, place, quality, possessor, etc., which may be regarded as the equivalent of a noun\-idea, e.g. #he[שָׁם הַבְּדֹ֫לַח]; #emph[there is the bdellium];, #bref("Gen 2:12");; #he[אֵי הֶ֫בֶל]; #emph[where is Abel?]; 4:9; #he[לְעוֹלָם חַסְדּוֹ]; #emph[his mercy]; endureth #emph[for ever];, #bref("Ps 136:1"); f.; #he[עֹ֫שֶׁר בְּבֵיתוֹ]; #emph[riches]; are #emph[in his house];, #bref("Ps 112:3");; #he[לוֹ אֲנָחְ֑נוּ]; #emph[we are his];, #bref("Ps 100:3"); #emph[Q#super[e];rê];.

#ml("c") <p141c> Rem. 1. The employment of a substantive as predicate of a noun\-clause is especially frequent, either when no corresponding adjective exists (so mostly with words expressing the material; cf. #xref(<p128o>)[§ 128#h(0.18em)#emph[o];];) or when the attribute is intended to receive a certain emphasis. For in all cases there is a much greater stress upon a substantival predicate,#footnote[The same naturally applies to most of those cases which are not pure noun\-clauses, but have the substantival predicate connected with the subject by #he[הָיָה]; (e.g. #bref("Gen 1:2"); #emph[and the earth was a waste and emptiness];; cf. #bref("Ps 35:6");, #bref("Prov 8:30");, #bref("Job 3:4");) or where a preposition precedes the substantival predicate, as #bref("Ps 29:4"); #emph[the voice of the Lord is with power];, i.e. #emph[powerful];.]; since it represents something as #emph[identical]; with the subject (see above, #emph[b]; \[#emph[a];\]), than upon an adjectival or verbal predicate; cf. #bref("Song 1:10");; #bref("Ps 25:10"); #emph[all the paths of the Lord are]; #he[חֶ֫סֶד וֶֽאֱמֶת]; #emph[lovingkindness and truth]; (i.e. #emph[wholly lovingkindness];, etc.; cf. #bref("Jer 10:10");); #bref("Ezek 38:5");, #bref("Ps 10:5");, #bref("19:10");, #bref("23:5");, #bref("88:19");, #bref("Prov 3:17");,#footnote[#he[שָׁלוֹם]; here, as in #bref("Job 21:9");, is evidently a substantive after a plural subject; on the other hand, it is doubtful whether #he[שָׁלוֹם]; in such passages as #bref("Gen 43:27");, #bref("2 Sam 20:9");, #bref("Ps 120:7");, etc., is not rather to be regarded as an adjective.]; #bref("Job 22:12");, #bref("23:2");, #bref("26:13");, #bref("Ruth 3:2");. Sometimes the emphasis on the predicate is obtained by the use of the plural form (according to #xref(<p124e>)[§ 124#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];), e.g. #bref("Ps 110:3"); #emph[thy people]; are #he[נְדָבֹת]; #emph[altogether willingness];; #bref("Song 5:16");, #bref("Dan 9:23");.

#ml("d") <p141d> Sometimes the boldness of such combinations is modified by the repetition of the subject, as #emph[regens]; of the predicate, e.g. #bref("Job 6:12"); #he[אִם־כֹּחַ אֲבָנִים כֹּחִי]; #emph[is my strength the strength of stones?]; #bref("Prov 3:17");. That the language, however—especially in poetry—is not averse even to the boldest combinations in order to emphasize very strongly the unconditional relation between the subject and predicate, is shown by such examples as #bref("Ps 45:9"); #emph[myrrh and aloes and cassia]; are #emph[all thy garments]; (i.e. so perfumed with them that they seem to be composed of them); #bref("Song 1:15"); #emph[thine eyes]; are #emph[doves];, i.e. dove’s eyes (but 5:12 #he[כְּיוֹנִים];);#footnote[As a rule, in such comparisons #he[כְּ‍]; (which is then to be regarded as #emph[nominative];) stands before the predicate, e.g. #bref("Isa 63:2"); #emph[wherefore]; are #emph[thy garments]; #he[כְּדֹרֵךְ בְּגַת]; #emph[like]; those of #emph[one that treadeth in the wine\-press];? (prop. #emph[the like of one]; that treadeth, #emph[instar calcantis];); #bref("Jer 50:9");. The comparison is then much less emphatic than in the noun\-clauses cited above.]; #bref("Ps 23:5");, #bref("109:4");, #bref("Job 8:9");, #bref("12:12");, #bref("Song 2:13");. In prose, e.g. #bref("Exod 9:31");, #bref("Ezra 10:13"); #he[הָעֵת גְּשָׁמִים]; #emph[the season]; is #emph[rain showers];, i.e. #emph[the rainy season];; with a bold enallage of the number, #bref("Gen 34:30"); #he[וַֽאֲנִי מְתֵי מִסְפָּר]; #emph[and I]; (with my family) #emph[am]; persons few #emph[in number];. For similarly bold expressions with #he[הָיָה]; cf. #bref("Gen 11:1");, #bref("12:2");, #bref("Exod 17:12");, #bref("Isa 5:12");, #bref("Jer 2:28");, and again with a bold enallage of the number, #bref("Job 29:15"); #emph[I was eyes to the blind, and feet was I to the lame];, but in prose, #bref("Num 10:31"); #emph[and thou shalt be to us]; #he[לְעֵינַ֫יִם];. #origpage("453") <orig-453>;

#ml("e") <p141e> #strong[2.]; The noun\-clause connected by #emph[wāw copulative]; to a verbal\-clause, or its equivalent, always describes a state #emph[contemporaneous]; with the principal action, or (when the predicate is a transitive participle) an action represented in constant duration (cf. #xref(<p107d>)[§ 107#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];, as well as #xref(<p116n>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[n];]; and #emph[o];), e.g. #bref("Gen 19:1"); #emph[and the two angels came to Sodom at even];, #he[וְלוֹט יֹשֵׁב]; #emph[while Lot sat];, etc.; 18:1, 8, 16, 22, 25:26, #bref("Judg 13:9");, #bref("1 Sam 1:9");, #bref("2 Sam 4:7");, #bref("11:4"); (always with a participle); with an adjectival predicate, #bref("Gen 18:12");; with a substantival predicate, 18:27; with an adverbial predicate, 9:23. Not infrequently such a #emph[circumstantial]; clause indicates at the same time some contradictory fact, so that #he[וְ]; is equivalent to #emph[whereas, while, although];, e.g. #bref("Gen 15:2");, #bref("18:27");, #bref("20:3");, #bref("48:14"); (#emph[although he was the younger];); #bref("Judg 16:15"); #emph[how canst thou say, I love thee];, #he[וְלִבְּךָ אֵין אִתִּי]; #emph[whereas thine heart is not with me];? #bref("2 Sam 3:39");, #bref("Ps 28:3"); #emph[while mischief is in their hearts];. These clauses describing a state are, however, only a subdivision of the large class of circumstantial clauses, on which see § 156.

#ml("f") <p141f> #strong[3.]; As the examples given under #emph[a]; and #emph[b]; show, the syntactical relation existing between the subject and predicate of a noun\-clause is as a rule expressed by simple juxtaposition, without a #emph[copula]; of any kind. To what period of time the statement applies must be inferred from the context; e.g. #bref("1 Kgs 18:21"); #he[יְהֹוָה הָֽאֱלֹהִים]; #emph[the Lord]; is #emph[the]; true #emph[God];; #bref("1 Sam 9:19");; #bref("Isa 31:2"); #he[גַּם־הוּא חָכָם]; #emph[yet he also is wise];; #bref("Gen 42:11");; on the other hand, #bref("Gen 19:1"); #he[וְלוֹט יֹשֵׁב]; #emph[and]; (\=while) #emph[Lot was sitting];; #bref("Ezek 28:15");; #bref("Gen 7:4"); #he[אָֽנֹכִי מַמְטִיר]; #emph[I am raining];, i.e. #emph[I will rain];. Sometimes even a jussive or optative is to be supplied as predicate, #bref("Gen 27:13"); #emph[upon me]; be #emph[thy curse];; #bref("Gen 11:3");, #bref("20:13");, #bref("Exod 12:2");. Cf. #xref(<p116r>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[r];];, note.

#ml("g") <p141g> Not infrequently, however, a connection is established between subject and predicate (#emph[a];) by adding the separate pronoun of the 3rd person singular or plural, expressly resuming and therefore strengthening the subject, or (#emph[b];) (especially for the sake of a more exact specification of time) by the help of the verb #he[הָיָה];. The first of these will be a #emph[compound]; sentence, since the predicate to the main subject consists of an independent clause.

#ml("h") <p141h> Examples of (#emph[a];): #bref("Gen 41:26"); #emph[the seven good kine]; #he[שֶׁ֫בַע שָׁנִים הֵ֫נָּה]; #emph[they]; are #emph[seven years];; #bref("Deut 1:17");, #bref("4:24");; #bref("Eccl 5:18"); #he[זֹה מַתַּת אֱלֹהִים הִיא]; #emph[this];—#emph[it]; is #emph[a gift of God];; #bref("Num 3:27"); #he[אֵ֫לֶּה הֵם];; in a question, #bref("Gen 27:38");. Sometimes #he[הוּא]; is used in this way to strengthen a pronominal subject of the first or second person, and at the same time to connect it with the predicate which follows,#footnote[On a similar use of the separate pronoun of the third person in Aramaic (#bref("Dan 2:38");, #bref("Ezra 5:11");, etc.) see Kautzsch, #emph[Gramm. des Bibl. Aram.];, § 87. 3.]; e.g. #he[אָֽנֹכִי אָֽנֹכִי הוּא]; #bref("Isa 43:25"); #emph[I, even I];, am #emph[he that blotteth out];, etc.; 51:12; #he[אַתָּה הוּא]; #bref("2 Sam 7:28");, #bref("Isa 37:16");, #bref("Ps 44:5");, #bref("Neh 9:6");, 7; in an interrogative sentence, #bref("Jer 14:22");;#footnote[This is of course to be distinguished from the use of #he[הוּא]; (to be inferred from the context) as predicate in the sense of ὁ αὐτός; see above, #xref(<p135a>)[§ 135#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];, note 1; or such cases as #bref("Deut 32:39"); #emph[see now]; #he[כִּי אֲנִי הוּא]; #emph[that I, even I];, am #emph[he];; #bref("1 Chr 21:17");.]; in #bref("Jer 49:12"); #he[הוּא]; in a #emph[verbal];\-clause strengthens #he[אַתָּה];. #origpage("454") <orig-454>;

#ml("i") <p141i> Of (#emph[b];): naturally this does not apply to the examples, in which #he[הָיָה];, in the sense of #emph[to become, to fare, to exist];, still retains its full force as a verb, and where accordingly the sentence is verbal, and not a noun\-clause; especially when the predicate precedes the subject. On the other hand, such examples as #bref("Gen 1:2"); #emph[and the earth was]; (#he[הָֽיְתָה];) #emph[waste and emptiness];, can scarcely be regarded as properly verbal clauses; #he[הָֽיְתָה]; is used here really only for the purpose of referring to past time a statement which, as the description of a state, might also appear in the form of a pure noun\-clause; cf. #bref("Gen 3:1");. This is especially true of the somewhat numerous instances in which #he[הָיָה]; occurs as a connecting word between the subject and the participial predicate; e.g. #bref("Judg 1:7");, #bref("Job 1:14"); (immediately afterwards a pure noun\-clause). The imperfect of #he[הָיָה]; announces what is future in #bref("Num 14:33");, etc.; cf. #xref(<p116r>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[r];];. However, especially in the latter case, #he[הָיָה]; is not wholly without verbal force, but comes very near to being a mere copula, and this use is more frequent in the later books#footnote[According to Albrecht, #emph[ZAW.]; viii. 252, especially in Deuteronomy and in the Priestly Code.]; than in the earlier.

#ml("k") <p141k> Rem. On the employment of #he[יֵשׁ]; #emph[existence];, and #he[אַ֫יִן]; #emph[non\-existence];, which were originally substantives (on their tendency to be used as verbs, equivalent to #emph[est];, and #emph[non est];, cf. #xref(<p100o>)[§ 100#h(0.18em)#emph[o];];, and the #emph[Negative Sentences];, § 152) as a connecting link between a pronominal subject and a participial predicate (especially in conditional and interrogative sentences, #bref("Gen 24:42");, 49, 43:4, etc.), see above, #xref(<p116q>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[q];];, and the various kinds of subordinate clauses mentioned in §§ 150, 159.

#ml("l") <p141l> #strong[4.]; The natural #emph[arrangement]; of words in the noun\-clause, as describing a state, is #emph[subject—predicate];; the principal stress falls on the former since it is the object of the description. Very frequently, however (and not merely in poetry, where greater freedom is naturally allowed in the arrangement of words), the reverse order is found, i.e. #emph[predicate—subject];. The latter order #emph[must]; be used when special emphasis is laid on the predicate,#footnote[For the same reason specifications of place (e.g. #bref("Gen 4:7");) or other adverbial qualifications may stand at the beginning of the sentence.]; or when it consists of an interrogative word; thus with a substantival predicate, e.g. #bref("Gen 3:19"); #he[עָפָר אַתָּה]; #emph[dust thou art];; 4:9, 12:13 (#emph[my sister];, not my wife); 20:2, 12, 29:14, Is 6#super[3 #emph[b];];, #bref("Job 5:24");, #bref("6:12");; with an adjectival predicate, e.g. Is 6#super[3 #emph[a];];, 28:21, #bref("Jer 10:6");; with a participle, #bref("Gen 30:1");, #bref("32:12");; with an interrogative pronoun, e.g. #bref("Gen 24:65");;#footnote[The only exceptions, according to Albrecht (see the Rem. above), are #bref("Exod 16:7");, 8.]; with an adverbial interrogative, e.g. #bref("Gen 4:9");.

#ml("m") <p141m> Rem. On the above cf. the exhaustive investigations of C. Albrecht, ‘Die Wortstellung im hebr. Nominalsatze,’ #emph[ZAW.]; vii. 218 ff. and viii. 249 ff.; with a complete list of the exceptions to the order #emph[subject—predicate];, p. 254 ff. The predicate #emph[must]; precede for the reasons stated (an #emph[adjectival predicate]; is particularly emphatic when it has the force of a comparative, e.g. #bref("Gen 4:13");; the predicate expressed by means of a preposition precedes most frequently when it serves to convey the ideas of #emph[having, possessing];, e.g. #bref("Gen 18:14");, #bref("29:16");, etc.; cf. also 26:20, 31:16, 43).

#ml("n") <p141n> The predicate #emph[may]; precede: (#emph[a];) when the subject is a pronoun, for ‘the person assumed to be generally known, does not excite the same interest as #origpage("455") <orig-455>;that which is stated about him;’ (#emph[b];) ‘in order not to be a mere appendage to a subject which consists of several words,’ e.g. #bref("2 Kgs 20:19");; (#emph[c];) in interrogative sentences (with a substantival or adjectival predicate or one compounded with a preposition), e.g. #bref("1 Sam 16:4");; finally (#emph[d];) in a relative clause, when the predicate is adverbial or compounded with a preposition, as a rule closely united (by Maqqeph) with #he[אֲשֶׁר];, e.g. #bref("Gen 2:11"); #he[אֲשֶׁר־שָׁם];; 1:29 f. #he[אֲשֶׁר־בּוֹ];.

#sec("142", [The Verbal\-clause], note: []) <s142>

#ml("a") <p142a> #strong[1.]; By #xref(<p140f>)[§ 140#h(0.18em)#emph[f];]; there is an essential distinction between verbal\-clauses, according as the subject stands before or after the verb. In the verbal\-clause proper the principal emphasis rests upon the action which proceeds from (or is experienced by) the subject, and accordingly the verb naturally precedes (#emph[necessarily]; so when it is in the perf. consec. or imperf. consec.). Nevertheless, the subject does sometimes precede even in the verbal\-clause proper, in the continuation of the narrative, e.g. #bref("Gen 7:19");, #bref("1 Sam 18:1");, #bref("2 Sam 19:12");; especially so if there is special emphasis upon it, e.g. #bref("Gen 3:13"); (it is not I who am to blame, but) #emph[the serpent beguiled me];, cf. #bref("Gen 2:5");, etc.#footnote[This of course applies also to the cases, in which the subject consists of a strongly emphasized personal pronoun, e.g. #bref("Gen 32:13"); #he[אַתָּה]; #emph[thou thyself];; 33:3 #he[הוּא]; #emph[he himself];.]; In the great majority of instances, however, the position of the subject at the beginning of a verbal\-clause is to be explained from the fact that the clause is not intended to introduce a new fact carrying on the narrative, but rather to describe a #emph[state];. Verbal\-clauses of this kind approximate closely in character to noun\-clauses, and not infrequently (namely when the verbal form might just as well be read as a participle) it is doubtful whether the writer did not in fact intend a noun\-clause.

#ml("b") <p142b> The particular #emph[state]; represented in the verb may consist—

(#emph[a];) Of an act completed long before, to which reference is made only because it is necessary for understanding the sequel of the principal action. If the predicate be a perfect (as it almost always is in these cases), it is generally to be rendered in English by a pluperfect; cf. the examples discussed above in #xref(<p106f>)[§ 106#h(0.18em)#emph[f];]; (#bref("1 Sam 28:3");, etc.); also #bref("Gen 6:8"); (not #emph[Noah found grace];); 16:1, 18:17, 20:4, 24:1, 39:1 (#emph[and Joseph]; in the meanwhile #emph[had been brought down to Egypt];); 41:10, #bref("Judg 1:16");, #bref("1 Sam 9:15");, #bref("14:27");, #bref("25:21");, #bref("1 Kgs 1:1");, etc.—In a wider sense this applies also to such verbal\-clauses as #bref("Gen 2:6"); (see further, #xref(<p112e>)[§ 112#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];), since when they serve to represent an action continuing for a long period in the past, and thus to some extent a state.

#ml("c") <p142c> (#emph[b];) Of a fact, contemporaneous with the principal events or continuing as the result of them. To the former class belong all those instances in which the predicate is combined with #he[הָיָה]; (provided that #he[הָיָה]; has not, as in #bref("Gen 1:2");, #bref("3:1");, etc., been weakened to a mere copula, in which case the precedence of the subject is fully explained from the character of the clause as a noun\-clause; cf. #xref(<p141i>)[§ 141#h(0.18em)#emph[i];];, and the examples of #he[הָיָה];, etc., with a participle, #xref(<p116r>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[r];];); as an example of the second class, cf. e.g. #bref("Gen 13:12"); #he[אַבְרָם יָשַׁב בְּאֶֽרֶץ־כְּנָ֑עַן וגו׳]; #emph[Abraham]; accordingly #emph[continued to dwell in the land of Canaan];, but #emph[Lot dwelt];, etc. #origpage("456") <orig-456>;

#ml("d") <p142d> Rem. 1. The close relation between verbal\-clauses beginning with the subject and actual noun\-clauses, is seen finally from the fact that the former also are somewhat frequently added with #he[וְ]; (or subordinated) to a preceding sentence in order to lay stress upon some accompanying circumstance; on such noun\-clauses describing a #emph[state]; or #emph[circumstance];, cf. #xref(<p141e>)[§ 141#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];. This is especially the case, again, when the circumstantial appendage involves an antithesis; cf. #bref("Gen 18:18"); #emph[seeing that]; nevertheless #emph[Abraham shall surely become];, etc.; 24:56, 26:27, #bref("Isa 29:13");, #bref("Jer 14:15");, #bref("Ps 50:17");, #bref("Job 21:22");, and such examples as #bref("Gen 4:2, 4");, 29:17, where by means of #he[וְ]; a new subject is introduced in express antithesis to one just mentioned. Moreover, in the examples treated above, under #emph[b]; and #emph[c]; (#bref("1 Sam 28:3");, etc.), the subject is frequently introduced by #he[וְ];, which then corresponds to the Greek δέ, used to interpose an explanation, etc., see Winer, #emph[Gramm. des neutest. Sprachidioms];, § 53. 7 #emph[b];.

#ml("e") <p142e> 2. By a peculiar construction verbal\-clauses may be joined by means of #he[וְ]; and a following subject to participial clauses, e.g. #bref("Gen 38:25"); #he[הִיא מוּצֵאת וְהִיא שָֽׁלְחָה]; #emph[she was already brought forth, when she sent];, etc.; 44:3, 4, #bref("Judg 18:3");, #bref("19:11");, #bref("2 Sam 20:8");; for other examples, see #xref(<p116u>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[u];]; (where it is pointed out, note 1, that the apodosis also frequently appears in the form of a #emph[noun];\-clause, a further proof of the close relation between verbal\-clauses beginning with the subject and noun\-clauses proper). Without doubt there is in all these cases a kind of inversion of the principal clause and the temporal subordinate clause; the latter for the sake of greater emphasis being raised to an independent noun\-clause, while the real principal action is added as though it were an accompanying circumstance, and hence in the form of an ordinary circumstantial clause. \[Cf. Driver, #emph[Tenses];, § 166 ff.\]

#ml("f") <p142f> #strong[2.]; According to what has been remarked above, under #emph[a];, the natural #emph[order of words]; within the verbal sentence is: #emph[Verb—Subject];, or #emph[Verb—Subject—Object];. But as in the noun\-clause (#xref(<p141l>)[§ 141#h(0.18em)#emph[l];];) so also in the verbal\-clause, a variation of the usual order of words frequently occurs when any member of the sentence is to be specially emphasized by priority of position.#footnote[Not infrequently also the striving after #emph[chiasmus]; mentioned in #xref(<p114r>)[§ 114#h(0.18em)#emph[r];];, note, occasions a departure from the usual arrangement of words.]; Thus the order may be:—

(#emph[a];) #emph[Object—Verb—Subject];: #bref("Gen 30:40");, #bref("37:4");, #bref("1 Sam 15:1");, #bref("2 Kgs 23:19"); and frequently. Naturally the examples are far more numerous, in which the object precedes a verbal form which includes the subject in itself, e.g. #bref("Gen 3:10, 14, 18");, 6:16, 8:17, 9:13, #bref("Exod 18:23");, #bref("Judg 14:3");, #bref("1 Sam 18:17");, #bref("20:9");, #bref("21:10");, #bref("2 Kgs 22:8");, #bref("Prov 13:5");, etc.

(#emph[b];) #emph[Verb—Object—Subject];: #bref("Gen 21:7");, #bref("Num 5:23");, #bref("1 Sam 15:33");, #bref("2 Sam 24:16"); (but #he[המלאך]; is probably only a subsequent addition); #bref("Isa 19:13");, #bref("Ps 34:22");, #bref("Job 11:19");, etc.

(#emph[c];) #emph[Subject—Object—Verb];: #bref("Isa 3:17");, #bref("11:8");, #bref("13:18");, #bref("Hos 12:11");, #bref("Ps 6:10");, #bref("11:5");, #bref("Job 29:25");.#footnote[This sequence occurs even in prose (#bref("Gen 17:9");, #bref("23:6");, etc.); it is, however, more doubtful here than in the above prophetical and poetical passages, whether the preceding subject should not be regarded rather as the subject of a compound sentence (§ 143), the predicate of which is an independent verbal\-clause; this would explain why the verbal\-clause is usually separated from the subject by one of the greater disjunctives.—On the other hand, the sequence #emph[Subject];—#emph[Object];—#emph[Verb]; is quite common in Aramaic (e.g. #bref("Dan 2:7");, 10); cf. Gesenius, #emph[Comm.]; on #bref("Isa 42:24");, and Kautzsch’s #emph[Gramm. des Bibl. Aram.];, § 84. 1 #emph[b];. The pure Aramaic usage of placing the #emph[object]; before the #emph[infinitive]; occurs in Hebrew in #bref("Lev 19:9");, #bref("21:21");, #bref("Deut 28:56");, #bref("2 Sam 11:19");, #bref("Isa 49:6");, #bref("2 Chr 28:10");, #bref("31:7");, #bref("36:19"); (?).]; #origpage("457") <orig-457>;(#emph[d];) #emph[Object—Subject—Verb]; (very rarely): #bref("2 Kgs 5:13");, #bref("Isa 5:17");, #bref("28:17");, #bref("Ps 51:5");, #bref("Prov 13:16"); (read #he[כֹּל];).#footnote[This sequence occurs more frequently in noun\-clauses with a participial predicate, e.g. #bref("Gen 37:16");, #bref("41:9");, #bref("2 Sam 13:4");, etc., in interrogative sentences, e.g. #bref("2 Kgs 6:22");, #bref("Jer 7:19");; in all which cases the emphasized object is placed before the natural sequence of #emph[subject—predicate];. \[Cf. Driver, #emph[Tenses];, § 208.\]];

(#emph[e];) A substantival complement of the verb #he[היה]; is placed first in #bref("Isa 18:5"); #he[וּבֹסֵר גֹּמֵל יִֽהְיֶה נִצָּה]; #emph[and a ripening grape the flower becometh];.

#ml("g") <p142g> Rem. Of specifications compounded with a preposition those of #emph[place]; stand regularly after the verb, unless they are specially emphatic as e.g. #bref("Gen 19:2");, #bref("30:16");, #bref("32:5");, #bref("Mic 5:1");, #bref("Esth 9:12");; in #bref("Gen 29:25"); #he[בְּרָחֵל]; with #he[בְּ]; #emph[pretii]; precedes for the sake of emphasis. Cf., however, in #bref("Gen 35:13"); the order #emph[verb];—specification of #emph[place—subject];.—The remoter object precedes for the sake of emphasis, e.g. in #bref("Gen 13:15"); (26:3), 15:3; even before the interrogative, #bref("Gen 27:37"); (cf. #bref("Jer 22:15"); where the subject precedes an interrogative, and #bref("1 Sam 20:8");, #bref("Job 34:31"); where a prepositional specification precedes).—Prepositional specifications of #emph[time];, such as #he[בְּרֵאשִׁית]; (#bref("Gen 1:1");), #he[בְּיוֹם];, #he[בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא];, etc. (but not #he[בָּרִֽאשֹׁנָה];, nor the simple #he[רִֽאשֹׁנָה];, #he[בַּתְּחִלָּה];, #he[לְעוֹלָם];), stand, as a rule, before the verb, provided it be not in the perf. consec. or imperf. consec.; so also certain adverbs of time, such as #he[אָז];, #he[עַתָּה];, while others like #he[עוֹד];, #he[תָּמִיד]; regularly follow the verb.

#sec("143", [The Compound Sentence], note: []) <s143>

#ml("a") <p143a> A compound sentence (#xref(<p140d>)[§ 140#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];) is formed by the juxtaposition of a subject#footnote[In #bref("Gen 31:40"); a verbal\-clause (#he[הָיִ֫יתִי]; #emph[I was];) occurs instead of the subject, and is then explained by another verbal\-clause.]; (which always precedes, see #emph[c];) and

(#emph[a];) An independent noun\-clause, which (α) refers to the principal subject by means of a pronoun, e.g. #bref("Nah 1:3"); #he[יְהֹוָה בְּסוּפָה דַרְכּוֹ]; #emph[the Lord—in the storm is his way];; #bref("2 Sam 23:6");, #bref("Ps 18:31");, #bref("104:17");, #bref("125:2");, #bref("Eccl 2:14");; cf. also #bref("Gen 34:23");, where the predicate is an interrogative clause.—A personal pronoun is somewhat frequently used as the principal subject, e.g. #bref("Isa 59:21"); #he[וַֽאֲנִי זֹאת בְּרִיתִי אֹתָם]; #emph[and as for me, this is my covenant with them];, etc.; #bref("Gen 9:9");, #bref("17:4");, #bref("Isa 1:7");, #bref("1 Chr 28:2");;#footnote[In #bref("1 Chr 28:2"); (cf. also 22:7 #he[אֲנִי הָיָה עִם־לְבָבִי];) #he[אֲנִי]; might also be taken as strengthening the pronominal suffix which follows (equivalent to #emph[I myself had it in my mind];), as e.g. #bref("Ezek 33:17"); #emph[whereas their own way is not equal];; cf. #xref(<p135f>)[§ 135#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];.]; with an interrogative noun\-clause, #bref("Gen 37:30");, #bref("Job 21:4");, #bref("38:19");:—or (β) is without a retrospective suffix (in which case naturally the connection between the subject and predicate is much looser), e.g. #bref("1 Sam 20:23"); #emph[and]; as touching #emph[the matter which];, etc... #emph[behold the Lord is between thee and me for ever];; #bref("Prov 27:2");. #origpage("458") <orig-458>;

#ml("b") <p143b> (#emph[b];) An independent verbal\-clause: (α) with a retrospective suffix,#footnote[Cf. the Mêšaʿ inscription, l. 31, #emph[and Ḥoronain];, #emph[therein dwelt];, etc.]; e.g. #bref("Gen 9:6"); (cf. #xref(<p116w>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[w];];); 17:15 #emph[as for Sarai thy wife, thou shalt not call her name Sarai];; 26:15, 28:13, 34:8, #bref("Exod 30:37");, #bref("32:1");, #bref("1 Sam 2:10");, #bref("2 Kgs 10:29");, #bref("Isa 9:1");, #bref("11:10");, #bref("Ezek 33:2");, #bref("Hos 9:11");, #bref("Ps 11:4");, #bref("46:5");, #bref("65:4");, #bref("74:17");, #bref("Dan 1:17");; with a pronoun as the principal subject, #bref("Gen 24:27");; (β) without a retrospective suffix, #bref("Isa 19:17"); #emph[every one]; that #emph[mentions it]; (Judah) #emph[to it]; (Egypt), #emph[it]; (Egypt) #emph[is afraid];.

#ml("c") <p143c> Rem. 1. In all the above examples prominence is given to the principal subject (by its mere separation from the context by means of a greater disjunctive, as a #emph[casus pendens];#footnote[But this term must not (any more than that formerly used ‘the subject preceding #emph[absolutely];’) be misunderstood to mean that the principal subject is, as it were, floating in the air, and that the whole sentence results in an anacoluthon. On the contrary, to the Semitic mind, such sentences appear quite as correctly formed as ordinary noun\- and verbal\-clauses.];) in a manner which would be quite impossible in a simple noun or verbal\-clause (e.g. #bref("Nah 1:3"); if it were #he[דֶּ֫רֶךְ יְהֹוָה בְּסוּפָה];); cf. the French #emph[c’est moi qu’on a accusé];. But the statement or question contained in the clause which forms the predicate also receives greater weight. For the same purpose other members of the sentence also are sometimes placed at the beginning and resumed again by a following suffix; thus the object, #bref("Gen 13:15");, #bref("21:13");, #bref("35:12");, #bref("47:21"); (with the Samaritan and LXX read perhaps #he[הֶֽעֱבִיד];); #bref("1 Sam 25:29");; a specification of place, #bref("Gen 2:17");, #bref("2 Kgs 22:18");, etc.; a substantive with #he[לְ];, #bref("1 Sam 9:20");, #bref("2 Sam 6:23");; cf. the examples in #xref(<p135a>)[§ 135#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];.—In #bref("Num 15:29"); a dative is co\-ordinated with the #emph[casus pendens];, i.e. there is a transition to a different construction.

#ml("d") <p143d> 2. To compound sentences belong also the numerous examples already treated in the account of the tenses, where the predicate of a #emph[casus pendens]; is introduced by the #emph[wāw apodosis];. The isolation and prominence of the principal subject is in this case still more marked than in the instances treated above; on the #emph[casus pendens]; with a following imperfect consecutive (e.g. #bref("Jer 6:19");, #bref("33:24");), cf. #xref(<p111h>)[§ 111#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];; with a following perfect consecutive (e.g. #bref("Exod 4:21");, #bref("12:44");, #bref("Num 23:3");, #bref("1 Sam 25:27");, #bref("2 Sam 14:10");, #bref("Isa 9:4");, #bref("56:6"); f.), #xref(<p112t>)[§ 112#h(0.18em)#emph[t];]; and #emph[mm];; on the participle as #emph[casus pendens];, #xref(<p112oo>)[§ 112#h(0.18em)#emph[oo];]; and #xref(<p116w>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[w];];.—In #bref("Job 15:17"); #emph[wāw apodosis]; follows with the cohortative; in #bref("Job 23:12");, #bref("Ps 115:7");, the imperfect is separated by #he[לֹא]; from the #emph[wāw apodosis];; in #bref("Job 4:6"); as for #emph[thy hope];, it is #emph[the integrity of thy ways];, 36:26, #bref("Eccl 5:6");, an incomplete noun\-clause is appended by #emph[wāw apodosis];. On #emph[wāw apodosis]; after disconnected specifications of time, cf. #xref(<p112oo>)[§ 112#h(0.18em)#emph[oo];]; at the end, and #bref("Gen 40:9");, #bref("2 Sam 15:34"); #he[וְעַתָּה וַֽאֲנִי עַבְדֶּ֫ךָ]; #emph[and now]; (so far as the present is concerned) #emph[I will be thy servant];, #bref("Num 12:12");, #bref("Jer 4:1"); (#emph[me thou needest not fear];).

#ml("e") <p143e> 3. Sometimes a substantive introduced by #he[לְ]; (#emph[in respect to];; cf. #xref(<p119u>)[§ 119#h(0.18em)#emph[u];];) serves the same purpose as the #emph[casus pendens]; beginning the sentence, as #bref("Num 18:8"); (unless the #he[לְ]; here serves to introduce the object, according to #xref(<p117n>)[§ 117#h(0.18em)#emph[n];];); #bref("Isa 32:1"); (where, however, #he[וְשָׂרִים]; should most probably be read); #bref("Eccl 9:4");, #bref("1 Chr 7:1");, #bref("24:20"); ff., #bref("2 Chr 7:21");. On the other hand, #bref("Ps 16:3");, #bref("17:4");, #bref("32:6");, #bref("89:19");, #bref("119:91");, are very doubtful. The suggestion of P. Haupt (#emph[Johns Hopkins University Circulars];, xiii. no. 114; Baltimore, 1894) also deserves attention, that in passages like #bref("Eccl 9:4");, and in #he[לְכֹל]; #bref("Gen 9:10");, #bref("23:10");, #bref("Exod 27:3");, 19, #bref("Ezek 44:9");, etc., #he[לְ]; is not the preposition, but an emphasizing particle, answering to the Arab. #emph[lă, surely];; Assyrian #emph[lû];; with #he[כֹּל]; it is equivalent to #emph[in short.]; Cf. also #he[לְ־לְ]; #emph[sive—sive, et—et];, #bref("Josh 17:16");, #bref("Ezra 1:11");, Assyrian #emph[lû];—#emph[lû];. #origpage("459") <orig-459>;

#sec("144", [Peculiarities in the Representation of the Subject (especially in the Verbal\-clause)], note: []) <s144>

#ml("a") <p144a> #strong[1.]; According to § 40 ff. most forms of the finite verb include a specification of the subject in the form of #emph[personal afformatives]; (in the imperfect also in the form of preformatives). Not infrequently, however, masculine forms are used in referring to feminines, e.g. #he[וִֽידַעְתֶּם]; #bref("Ezek 23:49");; #he[עֲשִׂיתֶם]; #bref("Ruth 1:8");; in the imperfect, #bref("Joel 2:22");, #bref("Song 2:7");; in the imperative, #bref("Amos 4:1");, #bref("Zech 13:7"); (for other examples, see #xref(<p110k>)[§ 110#h(0.18em)#emph[k];];). On emphasizing the pronominal subject by the addition of the separate pronoun, see #xref(<p135a>)[§ 135#h(0.18em)#emph[a];]; and #emph[b];.

On the masculine as #emph[prior gender];, cf. #xref(<p122g>)[§ 122#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];; on similar anomalies in the use of the personal pronoun, #xref(<p135o>)[§ 135#h(0.18em)#emph[o];];, in the connection between substantive and adjective, #xref(<p132d>)[§ 132#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];, between subject and predicate, #xref(<p145p>)[§ 145#h(0.18em)#emph[p];];, #emph[t];, #emph[u];.

#ml("b") <p144b> #strong[2.]; The third person singular is often used impersonally, especially in the masculine, e.g. #he[וַֽיְהִי]; #emph[and it came to pass];, #he[וְהָיָה]; #emph[and it shall come to pass];; #he[חָרָה]; followed by #he[לוֹ];, etc., #emph[it became hot to him];, i.e. #emph[he became angry];, #bref("Gen 4:6");, etc.; #he[וַיֵּ֫צֶר לוֹ]; lit. #emph[and it became strait to him, he was distressed];, #bref("Gen 32:8");;#footnote[In Arabic and Ethiopic the masculine is commonly used in this case, in Syriac the feminine.—The forms #he[חַם]; #emph[hot];, #he[טוֹב]; #emph[good, well];, #he[מַר]; #emph[bitter];, #he[צַר]; #emph[narrow];, #he[רַע]; #emph[evil]; (frequently joined by #he[לִי];, #he[לוֹ];, etc.), which many regard as impersonal, are no doubt to be regarded in most cases not as forms of the 3rd pers. sing. perf., but, with Hupfeld on #bref("Ps 18:7");, as adjectives.]; also in the feminine, e.g. #bref("1 Sam 30:6"); (#bref("Judg 10:9");) #he[וַתֵּ֫צֶר לְדָוִד];; #bref("Judg 11:39");, #bref("Jer 7:31");, #bref("Ezek 12:25");, #bref("Job 15:32"); (unless #he[תְּמֽוּרָתוֹ]; in verse 31 be the subject); cf. also the impersonal passives, #bref("Isa 1:6"); (#he[רֻכְּכָה];), 29:6 (#he[תִּפָּקֵד];). Somewhat different are the instances in which the 3rd singular feminine occurs as the predicate of a feminine subject which is not mentioned, but is before the mind of the speaker, e.g. #bref("Isa 7:7");, #bref("14:24");, #bref("Jer 10:7");, #bref("Job 4:5");, #bref("18:15"); (in #bref("2 Kgs 24:7"); #he[כָּל־אֲשֶׁר]; is used in this way with a feminine predicate, and in #bref("Jer 19:5"); #he[אֲשֶׁר]; alone); different, too, are the instances in which the 3rd singular masculine refers to an act just mentioned, e.g. #bref("Gen 17:11"); #he[וְהָיָה]; #emph[and this]; (the circumcision) #emph[shall be a token of a covenant];, etc.

#ml("c") <p144c> Rem. The expressions for natural phenomena may be either in the 3rd sing. masculine or feminine, e.g. #he[אוֹר]; #emph[it becomes light];, #bref("1 Sam 29:10"); (but with an explicit subject, #bref("Gen 44:3");); #he[וַיֵּאוֹר]; #emph[and it became light];; so also #he[יַחְשִׁךְ]; #emph[it grows dark];, #bref("Jer 13:16");; but #he[וְחָֽשְׁכָה]; #bref("Mic 3:6");; #he[תָּעֻ֫פָה]; #emph[though there be darkness];, #bref("Job 11:17");; #he[תַּמְטִיר]; #emph[it rains];, #bref("Amos 4:7"); (where, however, the context requires the reading #he[אַמְטִיר];); #bref("Ps 50:3"); #he[נִשְׂעֲרָה]; #emph[it is tempestuous];. #origpage("460") <orig-460>;

#ml("d") <p144d> #strong[3.]; The indefinite personal subject (our #emph[they, one];, the French #emph[on];, and the German #emph[man];#footnote[In #bref("1 Sam 9:9"); #he[הָאִישׁ]; (prop. #emph[the man];) is used in exactly the same sense as our #emph[one];.];) is expressed—

(#emph[a];) By the 3rd person singular masculine, e.g. #he[קָרָא]; #emph[one]; (sc. any #emph[one who named]; it, see the Rem.) #emph[called]; (or #emph[calls];) it, #bref("Gen 11:9");, #bref("16:14");, #bref("19:22");, #bref("Exod 15:23");; #he[וַיִּקְרָא]; #bref("Gen 35:8");, 10, #bref("2 Sam 2:16");, #bref("Isa 9:5");; #he[וַיֹּא֫מֶר]; #emph[one said];, #bref("Gen 48:1");, #bref("1 Sam 16:4");;#footnote[Elsewhere in such cases #he[וַיֹּֽאמְרוּ]; usually occurs (but not in the perfect, e.g. #bref("1 Sam 23:22");), so that it is doubtful whether the present reading of #bref("Gen 48:1");, etc., would not be better explained according to #xref(<p7d>)[§ 7#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];, note. In #bref("Gen 48:2"); for the extraordinary #he[וַיַּגֵּד]; the common form #he[וַיֻּגַּד]; is to be read; so in 50:26 for #he[וַיִּישֶׂם]; (after a plural) either #he[וַיּוּשַׂם]; or the 3rd plur.; in #bref("2 Kgs 21:26"); #he[וַיִּקְבְּרוּ];.]; other examples are #bref("Gen 38:28"); #emph[one put out a hand];; #bref("Num 23:21");, #bref("1 Kgs 22:38");, #bref("Isa 6:10"); #he[וְרָפָא לוֹ]; #emph[and one heals them];; 8:4 (#he[יִשָּׂא];); 46:7 (#he[יִצְעַק];); #bref("Amos 6:12");, #bref("Mic 2:4");, #bref("Job 27:23");; by the 3rd singular feminine (#he[יָֽלְדָה];) #bref("Num 26:59");.

#ml("e") <p144e> Rem. The Jewish commentators, following the Arab grammarians, usually explain these singulars by the addition of the participle (generally determinate) of the same stem, e.g. #he[קָרָא הַקֹּרֵא];. This view is supported by the fact that such a complement sometimes occurs, e.g. #bref("Isa 16:10"); #he[יִדְרֹךְ הַדֹּרֵךְ]; #emph[the treader treads out];, for #emph[one treads out];; 28:4, 24 (#emph[doth one plow continually?];); #bref("Deut 17:6"); (#bref("Ezek 18:32");), #bref("Deut 22:8");, #bref("2 Sam 17:9"); (#bref("Ezek 33:4");), #bref("Jer 9:23");; with an indeterminate participle (as in Arabic, e.g. #emph[qāla qāʾilun];, #emph[a sayer says];, i.e. #emph[some one says];), e.g. #bref("Num 6:9");, #bref("Amos 9:1");; cf. above, #xref(<p116t>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[t];];, and, on the whole question, Driver on #bref("1 Sam 16:4");.

#ml("f") <p144f> (#emph[b];) Very frequently by the 3rd plural masculine, e.g. #bref("Gen 29:2"); #emph[for out of that well]; #he[יַשְׁקוּ]; #emph[they]; (i.e. people generally) #emph[watered the flocks];; 26:18, 35:5, 41:14, 49:31, #bref("1 Kgs 1:2");, #bref("Isa 38:16");, #bref("Hos 12:9");, #bref("Job 18:18");, #bref("34:20");, #bref("Esth 2:2");, #bref("Neh 2:7");.

#ml("g") <p144g> Rem. The 3rd plur. also is sometimes used to express an indefinite subject, where the context does not admit of a human agent or at least not of several, e.g. #bref("Gen 34:27");. In such a case the 3rd plur. comes to be equivalent to a passive, as very commonly in Aramaic (see Kautzsch’s #emph[Gramm. des Bibl. Aram.];, § 96. 1 #emph[c];); e.g. #bref("Job 7:3"); #emph[wearisome nights]; #he[מִנּוּ־לִי]; #emph[have they allotted to me]; (equivalent to #emph[were allotted to me];; to make ‘invisible powers’ the subject is a merely artificial device); #bref("Job 4:19");, #bref("6:2");, #bref("18:18");, #bref("19:26");, #bref("34:20");, #bref("Ezek 32:25");, #bref("Ps 63:11");, #bref("Prov 2:22"); (in parallelism with a passive); 9:11.

#ml("h") <p144h> (#emph[c];) By the 2nd singular masculine, e.g. #bref("Isa 7:25"); #he[לֹֽא־תָבוֹא שָׁ֫מָּה]; #emph[one will]; (or #emph[can];) #emph[not come thither]; (prop. #emph[thou wilt];...); #bref("Jer 23:37");, #bref("Prov 19:25");, #bref("30:28"); (unless the reading should be #he[תִּתָּפֵשׂ];). Cf. also #he[עַד־בֹּֽאֲךָ]; or simply #he[בֹּֽאֲךָ]; (#bref("Gen 10:19");, 30, 13:10 #he[בֹּֽאֲבָה];) prop. #emph[until thy coming];, i.e. #emph[until one comes];.

#ml("i") <p144i> (#emph[d];) By the plural of the participle, e.g. #bref("Jer 38:23"); #emph[and all thy wives and thy children]; #he[מֽוֹצִאִים]; (prop. #emph[are they bringing out];\=) #emph[they will bring out];, etc.; cf. #bref("Isa 32:12");, #bref("Ezek 13:7");, #bref("Neh 6:10"); (#emph[for some are coming to slay thee];) #origpage("461") <orig-461>;and the passages discussed above, #xref(<p116t>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[t];];.#footnote[That this form of expression also (see #emph[g];) comes to be equivalent to a passive is seen from the analogy of such Aramaic passages as #bref("Dan 4:22");, which exclude any idea of human agency. Cf. Kautzsch, #emph[Gramm. des Bibl. Aram.];, § 76. 2 #emph[e]; at the end, and in post.\-bibl. Hebrew, e.g. #emph[Pirqe Aboth]; 2, 16; 3, 5, etc.]; In #bref("1 Kgs 5:1"); the text is corrupt.

#ml("k") <p144k> (#emph[e];) By the passive, e.g. #bref("Gen 4:26"); #he[אָז הוּחַל לִקְרֹא]; #emph[then]; (#emph[was it begun];\=) #emph[began men to call upon];, etc. (but read #he[זֶה הֵחֵל]; #emph[he began];).

#ml("l") <p144l> #strong[4.]; A peculiar idiom, and one always confined to poetic language, is the not infrequent occurrence of two subjects in a verbal sentence,#footnote[Two subjects occur in a noun\-clause in #bref("Ps 83:19");.]; one of the person and the other of the thing. The latter then serves—whether it precedes or follows—to state the instrument, organ, or member by which the action in question is performed, and may be most often rendered in English by an adverb, as a nearer definition of the manner of the action. All the examples of this kind have this in common, that the subject denoting the thing takes a suffix in the same person as the personal subject.#footnote[In #bref("Exod 6:3"); #he[שְׁמִי]; is subordinated to the following passive #he[נוֹדַ֫עְתִּי]; (#xref(<p121b>)[§ 121#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];); in #bref("1 Sam 25:26");, 33 #he[יָדִי];, #he[יָֽדְךָ]; are subjects to the infinitive absolute #he[הוֹשֵׁעַ];, according to § 113 #emph[gg];. In #bref("Ps 69:11"); read #he[וָֽאֲעַנֶּה]; for #he[וָֽאֶבְכֶּה];.]; They are thus distinguished from the #emph[accusatives]; treated in #xref(<p117s>)[§ 117#h(0.18em)#emph[s];];, with which they are often confused.

#ml("m") <p144m> (#emph[a];) Examples where the subject denoting the #emph[thing]; precedes, #he[קוֹלִי אֶל־יְהֹוָה אֶקְרָא]; #emph[my voice—I cry unto the Lord];, i.e. #emph[I cry aloud unto the Lord];, #bref("Ps 3:5");, #bref("27:7");, #bref("142:2");; #he[פִּֽי־קָרָ֫אתִי]; #emph[my mouth];—#emph[I cried];, i.e. #emph[I cried aloud];, #bref("Ps 66:17"); (cf. 17:10); #bref("Isa 26:9"); #he[נַפְשִׁי]; #emph[with my soul];, i.e. #emph[fervently];, and parallel with it #he[אַף־רוּחִי];; but #he[נַפְשִׁי]; #bref("Ps 57:5"); is rather a periphrasis for the 1st pers. #emph[I];.

(#emph[b];) Where the subject denoting the #emph[thing]; follows, #he[צַֽהֲלִי קוֹלֵךְ]; #emph[cry];—#emph[thy voice]; (i.e. aloud), #bref("Isa 10:30");; so also after an imperative, #bref("Ps 17:13"); (#he[חַרְבֶּ֫ךָ];) and verse 14 (#he[יָֽדְךָ];); 60:7, 108:7 (#he[יְמִֽינְךָ];); after a perfect, #bref("Hab 3:15"); (#he[סוּסֶ֫יךָ];); after a cohortative, #bref("Ps 108:2"); (#he[אַף־כְּבוֹדִי];). The subject denoting the #emph[thing]; stands between the personal subject and the predicate in #bref("Ps 44:3"); #he[אַתָּה יָֽדְךָ];.#footnote[In several of the above examples it might naturally be supposed that the subject denoting the thing (especially when it follows the verb) is to be explained rather as a #emph[casus instrumentalis];, i.e. as an accusative, analogous to the adverbial accusatives in #xref(<p118q>)[§ 118#h(0.18em)#emph[q];];. But although it is true that the subject denoting the thing often defines more closely the manner in which the action is performed, and although in similar (but still different) examples, #bref("Ps 89:2");, #bref("109:30");, #bref("Job 19:16");, #he[פִּי]; occurs with #he[בְּ]; #emph[instrumentale];, the explanation given above must nevertheless be accepted.];

#ml("n") <p144n> Rem. 1. Sometimes (as in other languages) an action is ascribed to a subject which can only have been performed at his direction by another #origpage("462") <orig-462>;person; cf. e.g. #bref("Gen 40:22"); (41:13), 41:14, 43:34 (#emph[and he commanded to set before them];, etc.); 46:29, #bref("2 Sam 12:9");.

#ml("o") <p144o> 2. Supposed ellipses of a definite subject are due either to a misunderstanding of the passage, or to a corruption of the text. Thus in #bref("1 Sam 24:11"); after #he[וַתָּ֫חָס]; either #he[עֵינִי]; has dropped out (through confusion with #he[עָלֶ֫יךָ];) or we should read with the LXX #he[וָֽאָחֻס];. In #bref("2 Sam 13:39"); (#he[וַתְּכַל דָּוִד];) the text is obviously corrupt.

#ml("p") <p144p> 3. In poetic (or prophetic) language#footnote[In prose, #bref("Lev 2:8");; but #he[וְהִקְרִיבָהּ]; here is hardly the original reading. Different from this is #bref("Gen 26:7");, where there is a transition to direct narration.]; there sometimes occurs (supposing the text to be correct) a more or less abrupt transition from one person to another. Thus from the 2nd to the 3rd (i.e. from an address to a statement), #bref("Gen 49:4"); (?), #bref("Isa 31:6"); (?), 42:20, 52:14, 61:7, #bref("Mal 2:15"); (where, however, for #he[יִבְגֹּד]; we should undoubtedly read #he[תִּבְגֹּד];); #bref("Ps 22:9"); \[and regularly after a vocative, #bref("Isa 22:16");, #bref("47:8");, #bref("48:1");, #bref("54:1");, 11, #bref("Jer 22:16");, #bref("49:4");, 16, #bref("Amos 5:6");f., #bref("Mic 1:2"); (\=#bref("1 Kgs 22:28");), #bref("Mal 3:9");, #bref("2 Kgs 9:31");; and after #he[הוֹי]; #bref("Isa 5:8");, #bref("29:15");, #bref("Jer 22:13");\]. From the 3rd to the 2nd pers., #bref("Deut 32:15");, #bref("Isa 1:29"); (but read probably #he[חֶמְדָּתָם]; for #he[חֲמַדְתֶּם];, which has caused the insertion of #he[אֲשֶׁר];), 5:8, #bref("Jer 29:19");, #bref("Job 16:7");, cf. also #bref("Deut 32:17");. From the 1st to the 3rd pers., #bref("Lam 3:1"); (in a relative clause). In #bref("Job 13:28"); the 3rd pers. #he[וְהוּא]; is probably employed δεικτικῶς for the 1st.

#sec("145", [Agreement between the Members of a Sentence, especially between Subject and Predicate, in respect of Gender and Number], note: []) <s145>

#ml("a") <p145a> #strong[1.]; As in other languages, so also in Hebrew, the predicate in general conforms to the subject in gender and number (even when it is a pronoun, e.g. #he[זֹאת בְּרִיתִי]; #emph[this is my covenant];, #bref("Gen 17:10");). There are, however, numerous exceptions to this fundamental rule. These are due partly to the #emph[constructio ad sensum]; (where attention is paid to the meaning rather than to the grammatical form; see #emph[b];–#emph[l]; below), partly to the position of the predicate (regarded as being without gender) #emph[before]; the subject.

#ml("b") <p145b> #strong[2.]; Singular nouns which include in themselves a collective idea (#xref(<p123a>)[§ 123#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];), or which occasionally have a collective sense (#xref(<p123b>)[§ 123#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];), may readily, in accordance with their meaning, be construed with the plural of the predicate, whether it precedes or follows. This is also the case, when the collective is itself feminine but represents, exclusively or at least generally, masculine persons.

Examples:—

#ml("c") <p145c> (#emph[a];) Of collectives proper (cf. #xref(<p132g>)[§ 132#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];): (α) with the predicate preceding, #bref("Gen 30:38"); #he[תָּבֹ֫אןָ הַצֹּאן]; (cf. 30:39, 31:8 and 33:13); #bref("Judg 1:22"); f. #he[בַּ֫יִת]; representing persons belonging to the tribe; #bref("Mic 4:3"); #he[גּוֹי];; #bref("2 Kgs 25:5"); #he[חַ֫יִל]; #emph[army];; #bref("Prov 11:26"); #he[לְאוֹם]; #origpage("463") <orig-463>;#emph[the people];; #bref("Num 10:3"); #he[כָּל־הָֽעֵדָה]; #emph[all the congregation]; (cf. #bref("1 Kgs 8:5");); #bref("1 Kgs 1:40");, #bref("Isa 9:8");, #bref("25:3");, #bref("Amos 1:5"); #he[עַם];; #bref("1 Sam 17:47");, #bref("Ezra 10:12"); #he[קָהָל]; #emph[assembly];. Cf. also the construction of national names, as #he[אֲרָם]; (#xref(<p122i>)[§ 122#h(0.18em)#emph[i];];), e.g. #bref("1 Kgs 20:20"); #he[וַיָּנֻ֫סוּ אֲרָם]; #emph[and the Syrians fled];; #bref("1 Sam 4:5");.—(β) with the predicate following, #bref("1 Kgs 8:5"); #he[צֹאן וּבָקָר]; #emph[sheep and oxen];, construed with the plural in the following relative clause; #bref("Job 1:14"); #he[הַבָּקָר הָיוּ חֹֽרְשׁוֹת]; #emph[the cattle]; (cows) #emph[were ploughing];; #bref("2 Sam 3:1"); and #bref("1 Chr 10:6"); #he[בַּ֫יִת];\=#emph[family]; (in #bref("1 Sam 6:13"); #he[בֵּית שֶׁ֫מֶשׁ]; on the analogy of names of countries, is used for #emph[the inhabitants of Bethshemesh];); #bref("Hos 11:7");, #bref("Ezra 4:4"); #he[עַם];; #bref("Ps 68:11"); #he[חַיָּה]; #emph[herd]; \[if correct, figuratively for #emph[people];\]; #bref("Isa 26:19"); #he[נְבֵלָה]; #emph[dead bodies];; #bref("Isa 27:11"); #he[קָצִיר]; #emph[boughs];; #bref("1 Sam 4:1"); #he[יִשְׂרָאֵל];, preceded by a predicate in the singular.

#ml("d") <p145d> (#emph[b];) Of substantives occasionally used as collectives: (α) with the predicate preceding, #bref("Gen 34:24"); #he[זָכָר];; #bref("Judg 9:55");, #bref("15:10"); #he[אִישׁ];; #bref("Isa 16:4"); #he[רֹמֵס]; #emph[the treader down];.—(β) with the predicate following, #bref("Job 8:19"); #he[אַחֵר];\=#emph[others];; #bref("Ezek 28:3"); #he[סָתוּם]; #emph[a secret];; \[#bref("Ps 9:7");, and even after #he[זֶה]; #bref("Job 19:19");.\]

#ml("e") <p145e> (#emph[c];) Of feminines as collective terms denoting masculine persons: (α) with the predicate preceding, #bref("1 Sam 17:46"); #he[וְיֵדְעוּ כָּל־הָאָרֶץ]; #emph[that all the earth may know];, i.e. all the inhabitants of the earth; cf. #bref("Deut 9:28");, #bref("Ps 66:1");, #bref("96:1");, 9, etc.; #bref("Amos 1:8"); #he[שְׁאֵרִית]; #emph[remnant];; (#bref("Ps 33:8"); #he[כָּל־הָאָ֫רֶץ];).—(β) with the predicate following, #bref("Gen 41:57");, #bref("2 Sam 15:23");, #bref("1 Kgs 10:24");, #bref("Gen 48:6"); #he[מוֹלֶ֫דֶת]; #emph[issue];; #bref("1 Sam 2:33"); #he[כָּל־מַרְבִּית]; #emph[all the increase];; #bref("Job 30:12"); #he[פִּרְחָח]; #emph[rabble];. In #bref("Hag 2:7"); read #he[חֲמֻדֹת]; with the LXX.

#ml("f") <p145f> Examples of predicates in the singular, notwithstanding the collective meaning of the subject, occur in #bref("Gen 35:11");, #bref("Exod 10:24");, #bref("14:10");, #bref("Deut 1:39");, etc.—For examples of bold enallage of the number in noun\-clauses with a substantival predicate, see above, #xref(<p141c>)[§ 141#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];.

#ml("g") <p145g> Rem. Not infrequently the construction begins in the singular (especially when the predicate precedes; see #emph[o]; below), but is carried on, after the collective subject has been mentioned, in the plural; e.g. #bref("Exod 1:20"); #he[וַיִּ֫רֶב הָעָם וַיַּֽעַצְמוּ מְאֹד]; #emph[and the people multiplied, and waxed very mighty];; 33:4.

#ml("h") <p145h> #strong[3.]; On the other hand, plurals which have a singular meaning (#xref(<p124a>)[§ 124#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];) are frequently construed with the singular, especially the #emph[pluralis excellentiae]; or #emph[maiestatis]; (§ 124 #emph[g];–#emph[i];; on the union of these plurals with attributes, cf. #xref(<p132h>)[§ 132#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];), as #he[אֱלֹהִים]; #bref("Gen 1:1");, 3, etc. (but see the Rem.), #he[אֲדֹנִים]; #emph[master];, #bref("Exod 21:4"); #he[בְּעָלִים]; #emph[master, owner];, #bref("Exod 21:29");; cf., moreover, #he[פָּנִים]; with the singular, #bref("Job 16:16"); #emph[K#super[e];th.];, #he[רַֽחֲמִים]; #bref("Prov 12:10");.—So feminine forms with a masculine meaning are construed with a masculine predicate, e.g. #bref("Eccl 12:9"); #he[הָיָה קֹהֶ֫לֶת חָכָם]; #emph[the preacher was wise];.

#ml("i") <p145i> Rem. The construction of #he[אֱלֹהִים]; #emph[God]; with the plural of the predicate may be explained (apart of course from such passages as #bref("1 Kgs 19:2");, #bref("20:10");, where the speakers are heathen, and #he[אֱלֹהִים]; may, therefore, be a numerical plural) partly as an acquiescence in a polytheistic form of expression, partly from the peculiar usage of one of the early documents of the Hexateuch, called #emph[E]; by Wellhausen, etc., #emph[B]; by Dillmann; cf. his commentary on Numbers—Joshua, p. 618, and above, #xref(<p124g>)[§ 124#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];, note 2. So #bref("Gen 20:13"); (but in conversation with a heathen); 31:53, 35:7, cf. also #bref("Josh 24:19");. That this construction was afterwards studiously avoided from fear of misconception, is shown by such passages as #bref("Neh 9:18"); compared with #bref("Exod 32:4");, 8, and #bref("1 Chr 17:21"); compared with #bref("2 Sam 7:23");. Cf. Strack’s excursus on #bref("Gen 20:13"); in #emph[Die Genesis];, Munich, 1905, p. 77. #origpage("464") <orig-464>;

#ml("k") <p145k> #strong[4.]; Plurals of names of animals or things, and of abstracts, whether they be masculine or feminine, are frequently construed with the feminine singular of the verbal predicate#footnote[Cf. in Greek the construction of the neuter plural with the singular of the predicate τὰ πρόβατα βαίνει; in Attic Greek the plural of the predicate is allowed only when the neuter denotes actual persons, as τὰ ἀνδράποδα ἔλαβον. In Arabic also the #emph[pluralis inhumanus]; (i.e. not denoting persons) is regularly construed with the feminine singular of the attribute or predicate, as are all the #emph[plurales fracti]; (properly collective forms).]; (on the collective sense of the feminine form, cf. #xref(<p122s>)[§ 122#h(0.18em)#emph[s];];); thus #bref("Joel 1:20"); #he[בַּֽהֲמוֹת שָׂדֶה תַּֽעֲרֹג]; #emph[the beasts of the field long];; #bref("Jer 12:4"); (where the predicate precedes), cf. also #bref("Job 12:7");; names of things with the predicate preceding occur in #bref("2 Sam 24:13");, #bref("Isa 34:13");, #bref("Jer 4:14");, #bref("51:29");, #bref("Ps 18:35");, #bref("37:31");, #bref("73:2"); #emph[K#super[e];th.];, 103:5 (unless #he[הַֽמְחַדֵּשׁ]; is to be read for #he[תִּתְחַדֵּשׁ];), #bref("Job 14:19");, #bref("27:20");; with the predicate following, #bref("Gen 49:22"); (#he[בָּנוֹת];\=#emph[branches];); #bref("Deut 21:7");, #bref("1 Sam 4:15"); (#he[וְעֵינָיו קָ֫מָה];),#footnote[On the possibility of explaining forms like #he[קָ֫מָה]; as 3rd plural feminine, cf. above, #xref(<p44m>)[§ 44#h(0.18em)#emph[m];];; but this explanation would not apply to all the cases under this head, cf. #bref("Joel 1:20");, #bref("Ps 37:31");, #bref("103:5");.]; #bref("2 Sam 10:9");, #bref("Isa 59:12");, #bref("Jer 2:15"); #emph[K#super[e];th.];, 48:41, 49:24, #bref("Prov 15:22");, #bref("20:18");, #bref("Job 41:10");.#footnote[In #bref("Prov 14:1"); an abstract plural #he[חָכְמוֹת]; (to be read thus with 9:1, etc., instead of #he[חַכְמוֹת];) is construed with the singular; but cf. #xref(<p86l>)[§ 86#h(0.18em)#emph[l];];, #xref(<p124e>)[§ 124#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];, end.];

#ml("l") <p145l> #strong[5.]; Moreover, the plural of persons (especially in the participle) is sometimes construed with the singular of the predicate, when instead of the whole class of individuals, each severally is to be represented as affected by the statement. Undoubted examples of this #emph[distributive]; singular are #bref("Gen 27:29"); (#bref("Num 24:9");) #he[אֹֽרֲרֶ֫יךָ אָרוּר וּמְבָֽרֲכֶ֫יךָ בָּרוּךְ]; #emph[those that curse thee, cursed be]; every one of them, #emph[and those that bless thee, blessed be]; every one of them; #bref("Exod 31:14");, #bref("Lev 17:14"); and 19:8 (in both places the Samaritan has #he[אֹֽכְלוֹ];); #bref("Isa 3:12"); unless #he[נֹגְשָׂיו]; is to be regarded as a #emph[pluralis maiestatis]; according to #xref(<p124k>)[§ 124#h(0.18em)#emph[k];];; #bref("Prov 3:18, 35"); (?), #bref("18:21"); (?), #bref("21:27b");, #bref("27:16");, #bref("28:1b");, 28:16 #emph[K#super[e];th.];

#ml("m") <p145m> Rem. Analogous to the examples above mentioned is the somewhat frequent#footnote[In several of the above examples the text is doubtful, and hence Mayer Lambert (#emph[REJ.]; xxiv. 110) rejects the theory of distributive singulars generally. \[Cf. Driver, #emph[Jeremiah];, p. 362, on 16:7.\]]; use of suffixes in the singular (distributively) referring to plurals; cf. the #emph[verbal];\-suffixes in #bref("Deut 21:10");, #bref("28:48");, #bref("Amos 6:10");; and the #emph[noun];\-suffixes in #bref("Isa 2:8");, #bref("30:22");, #bref("Jer 31:14");, #bref("Hos 4:8"); (but since #he[ו]; follows, #he[נַפְשׁוֹ]; is undoubtedly a dittography for #he[נָֽפֶשׁ];), #bref("Zech 14:12");, #bref("Ps 5:10"); (where, however, #he[פִּימוֹ]; is clearly to be read with all the early versions); 62:5, 141:10 (?), #bref("Job 38:32");, #bref("Eccl 10:15"); \[but LXX #he[הַכְּסִיל];\]; finally, the suffixes with prepositions in #bref("Isa 2:20"); #he[אֲשֶׁר עָֽשׂוּ־לוֹ]; #emph[which they made]; each one #emph[for himself]; (according to others, #emph[which they]; (#emph[the makers];) #emph[made for him];); 5:26, 8:20, #bref("Job 24:5");, in each case #he[לוֹ];; in #bref("Gen 2:19"); #he[לוֹ]; refers to the collectives #he[חַיָּה]; and #he[עוֹף];; cf. further, #bref("Josh 24:7");, #bref("Isa 5:23"); #he[מִמֶּ֫נּוּ]; after #he[צַדִּיקִים]; (but read probably #he[צַדִּיק]; with the LXX, etc.). Conversely in #bref("Mic 1:11"); #he[עִבְרִי לָכֶם]; \[cf. #bref("Jer 13:20"); #emph[K#super[e];th.];\], but the text is undoubtedly corrupt. #origpage("465") <orig-465>;

#ml("n") <p145n> #strong[6];. Subjects in the dual are construed with the plural of the predicate, since verbs, adjectives, and pronouns, according to #xref(<p88a>)[§ 88#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];, have no dual forms; thus #he[עֵינַ֫יִם];, #bref("Gen 29:17"); #he[וְעֵינֵי לֵאָה רַכּוֹת]; #emph[and Leah’s eyes were dull];; #bref("2 Sam 24:3");, #bref("Isa 30:20");, #bref("Jer 14:6");, #bref("Mic 7:10");, #bref("Ps 18:28");, #bref("38:11"); (on the other hand, in #bref("1 Sam 4:15"); the predicate is in the feminine singular #emph[after]; the subject, and in #bref("Mic 4:11"); #emph[before]; it; on both constructions cf. #emph[k]; above); so also #he[אָזְנַ֫יִם]; #emph[ears];, #bref("2 Chr 6:40");; #he[יָדַ֫יִם]; #emph[hands];, #bref("Isa 1:15");, #bref("Job 10:8");, #bref("20:10"); (in #bref("Exod 17:12"); even with the plural masculine #he[כְּבֵדִים];; cf. #emph[p];); #he[שְׂפָתַ֫יִם]; #emph[lips];, #bref("1 Sam 1:13");, #bref("Job 27:4");; #he[שָׁדַ֫יִם]; #emph[breasts];, #bref("Hos 9:14");.

#ml("o") <p145o> #strong[7.]; Variations from the fundamental rule (see above, #emph[a];) very frequently occur #emph[when the predicate precedes the subject]; (denoting animals or things#footnote[Only rarely does an uninflected predicate precede a personal subject, as #bref("1 Sam 25:27"); (but #he[הֵבִ֫יאָה]; should probably be read, as in verse 35); #bref("Esth 9:23"); (before a plur. masc.). Such examples as #bref("Job 42:15"); are to be explained according to #xref(<p121a>)[§ 121#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];.];). The speaker or writer begins with the most simple form of the predicate, the uninflected 3rd singular masculine, and leaves us without indication as to which of the following subjects (and so which gender or number) is to define the predicate thus left temporarily indefinite.#footnote[In a certain sense this is analogous to the German #emph[es kommt ein Mann, eine Frau];, etc.]; Thus inflexions are omitted in—

(#emph[a];) The verb, with a following singular feminine, #bref("Isa 2:17"); #he[וְשַׁח גַּבְהוּת הָֽאָדָם]; #emph[and bowed down shall be the loftiness of man];; 9:18, 14:11, 28:18, 47:11; #bref("1 Sam 25:27"); (see note 1 below); #bref("1 Kgs 8:31");#super[#emph[b];];, 22:36, #bref("2 Kgs 3:26");, #bref("Jer 51:46");, #bref("Eccl 7:7");; with a following plural masc., #bref("Isa 13:22"); #he[וְעָנָה אִיִּים]; #emph[and there shall cry wolves];, etc.; #bref("Judg 13:17"); #emph[K#super[e];th.];, 20:46, #bref("1 Sam 1:2");, #bref("4:10");, #bref("2 Sam 24:15");, #bref("1 Kgs 13:33");, #bref("Jer 51:48");, #bref("Ps 124:5");, #bref("Esth 9:23"); (see note 1 below); #bref("Gen 1:14"); #he[יְהִי מְאֹרֹת]; #emph[let there be lights];; with a following plural feminine, #bref("Deut 32:35");, #bref("1 Kgs 11:3");#super[#emph[a];];, #bref("Isa 8:8");, #bref("Jer 13:18");, #bref("Mic 2:6");, #bref("Ps 57:2");; before collectives and mixed subjects, e.g. #bref("Gen 12:16");, #bref("13:5");, #bref("30:43");, #bref("32:6");, etc.; before a following dual, #bref("Isa 44:18");, #bref("Ps 73:7"); (where, however, with the LXX #he[עֲוֹנָ֫מוֹ]; should be read).

#ml("p") <p145p> Rem. 1. The instances in which a preceding predicate appears in the plural masculine before a plural (or collective singular) feminine of persons (#bref("Judg 21:21");, #bref("1 Kgs 11:3");#super[#emph[b];];), of animals (#bref("Gen 30:39"); where however #he[צאֹן]; may refer specially to male animals) or of things (#bref("Lev 26:33");, #bref("Jer 13:16");, #bref("Hos 14:7");, #bref("Ps 16:4");, #bref("Job 3:24");, #bref("Song 6:9");), or before a dual (#bref("2 Sam 4:1");, #bref("Zeph 3:16");, #bref("2 Chr 15:7");) are to be explained not on the analogy of the examples under #emph[o];, but from a dislike of using the 3rd plur. fem. imperf., for this is the only form concerned in the above examples (cf., however, #bref("Nah 3:11"); #he[תְּהִי]; instead of #he[תִּֽהְיִי];); cf. the examples of a following predicate in the 3rd plur. masc., instead of the fem., under #emph[t]; and #emph[u];, and on an analogous phenomenon in the imperative, see #xref(<p110k>)[§ 110#h(0.18em)#emph[k];];.

#ml("q") <p145q> 2. As in the case of verbs proper so also the verb #he[הָיָה];, when used as a copula, frequently remains uninflected before the subject; cf. #bref("Gen 5:23ff.");, 39:5, #origpage("466") <orig-466>;#bref("Deut 21:3"); (according to the accents); 22:23, #bref("Isa 18:5"); #he[וּבֹסֶר גֹּמֵל יִֽהְיֶה נִצָּה]; #emph[and a ripening grape the flower becometh];.

#ml("r") <p145r> (#emph[b];) The adjective in a noun\-clause, e.g. #bref("Ps 119:137"); #he[יָשָׁר מִשְׁפָּטֶ֫יךָ]; #emph[upright are thy judgements];; cf. verse 155.#footnote[This does not include such cases as #bref("Job 24:7");, 10, where #he[עָרוֹם]; is rather to be explained as an accusative denoting a state, #xref(<p118n>)[§ 118#h(0.18em)#emph[n];];.];—On the other hand, #he[רֹעֵה]; in #he[רֹעֵה צֹאן עֲבָדֶ֫יךָ]; #emph[thy servants are shepherds];, #bref("Gen 47:3");, is either an unusual orthography or simply a misspelling for #he[רֹעֵי];.

#ml("s") <p145s> Rem. 1. As soon as a sentence which begins with an uninflected predicate is carried on after the mention of the subject, the gender and number of the subsequent (co\-ordinate) predicates must coincide with those of the subject, e.g. #bref("Gen 1:14"); #he[יְהִי מְאֹרֹת... וְהָיוּ]; (see #emph[o]; above); #bref("Num 9:6");, #bref("Ezek 14:1");; cf. also #bref("Gen 30:39"); (see #emph[p]; above).

#ml("t") <p145t> 2. The dislike mentioned in #emph[p]; above, of using the feminine form (cf., further, #xref(<p144a>)[§ 144#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];, with the sections of the Grammar referred to there, and below, under #emph[u];), is exemplified sometimes by the fact that of several predicates only that which stands next to the feminine substantive is inflected as feminine (cf. the treatment of several attributes following a feminine substantive, #xref(<p132d>)[§ 132#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];); thus in #bref("Isa 14:9"); #he[רָֽגְזָה];, and afterwards #he[עוֹרֵר]; (but #he[עוֹרֵר]; is better taken as an infin. abs.\=#emph[excitando];, reading #he[הָקֵם]; for #he[הֵקִים];); 33:9 #he[אָבַל אֻמְלְלָה אֶ֫רֶץ]; #emph[mourneth, languisheth the land];. Cf. #bref("Jer 4:30");, #bref("Job 1:19");, and the examples (#xref(<p47k>)[§ 47#h(0.18em)#emph[k];];) where only the first of several consecutive forms of the 2nd sing. fem. imperf. has the afformative #emph[î];, #bref("Isa 57:8");, #bref("Jer 3:5");, #bref("Ezek 22:4");, #bref("23:32"); (#he[תִּֽהְיֶה]; after #he[תִּשְׁתִּי];); on the converse sequence of genders in imperatives, #bref("Nah 3:15");, cf. #xref(<p110k>)[§ 110#h(0.18em)#emph[k];];.—Of a different kind are instances like #bref("Lev 2:1");, #bref("5:1");, #bref("20:6");, where #he[נֶ֫פֶשׁ]; #emph[person]; (fem.) as the narrative continues, assumes (in agreement with the context) the sense of a masculine person.

#ml("u") <p145u> 3. The instances in which the gender or number of the #emph[following]; predicate appears to differ from that of the subject are due partly to manifest errors in the text, e.g. #bref("Gen 32:9"); read with the Samaritan #he[הָֽאֶחָד]; instead of #he[הָֽאַחַת];; #he[וְהָיָה]; then follows correctly; #bref("1 Sam 2:20"); read with Wellhausen #he[שָׁאֻל];, according to 1:28, instead of #he[שָׁאַל];; #bref("1 Sam 16:4"); read #he[וַיּֽאֹמְרוּ];; #bref("Ezek 18:29"); instead of #he[יִתָּכֵן]; read the plural as in verse 25; so also #bref("Ezek 20:38"); for #he[יָבוֹא];,#footnote[#he[יבוא]; probably an error for #he[יבאו];. The Masora on #bref("Lev 11:34"); reckons fourteen instances of #he[יָבֹא];, where we should expect the plural.]; and in #bref("Job 6:20"); for #he[בָּטָ֑ח];; in #bref("Lam 5:10"); read #he[נִכְמָר];, and cf. in general, #xref(<p7d>)[§ 7#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];, note; #bref("1 Chr 2:48"); read #he[יָלְֽדָה];; in #bref("Jer 48:15"); also the text is certainly corrupt. Other instances are due to special reasons. The anomalies in #bref("Isa 49:11");, #bref("Hos 14:1");, #bref("Prov 1:16"); (after #he[רַגְלָיו];), #bref("Ps 11:4"); (after #he[עֵינָיו];), 63:4, #bref("Prov 5:2");, #bref("10:21");, 32 18:6, 26:23, #bref("Job 15:6"); (all after #he[שְׂפָתַ֫יִם];), #bref("Prov 3:2"); (after #he[מִצְוֹתַי];), #bref("Ps 102:28");, #bref("Job 16:22"); (after #he[שָׁנוֹת];), #bref("Dan 11:41"); (read #he[וְרִבּוּת];), and perhaps #bref("Gen 20:17"); are also to be explained (see #emph[p];) from the dislike of the 3rd plur. fem. imperf.; moreover, in #bref("Jer 44:19");, #bref("Prov 26:23"); the plur. masc. even of a participle occurs instead of the plur. fem.—In #bref("Gen 31:8"); f. #he[יִהְֽיֶה];, after a plural subject, is explained as a case of attraction to the following singular predicate.#footnote[So also the pronoun #he[הוּא]; emphatically resuming the subject (see #xref(<p141h>)[§ 141#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];) is attracted to the predicate in number in #bref("Josh 13:14"); #he[אִשֵּׁי יְהֹוָה... הוּא נַֽחֲלָתוֹ]; #emph[the offerings of the Lord... that is his inheritance];; in number and gender, #bref("Lev 25:33"); #emph[Q#super[e];rê];; #bref("Jer 10:3");.];—In #bref("Gen 4:7"); #he[רֹבֵץ]; #origpage("467") <orig-467>;is a substantival participle (#emph[a lurker, a coucher];). In #bref("Gen 47:24"); #he[יִֽהְיֶה]; remains undefined in gender (masc.), although the noun precedes for the sake of emphasis; so also in #bref("Gen 28:22");, #bref("Exod 12:49");, #bref("28:7");, 32, #bref("Num 9:14");, #bref("15:29");, #bref("Jer 50:46");, #bref("Eccl 2:7"); (#he[הָיָה לִי]; as if the sentence began afresh, #emph[and servants born in my house... there fell to my lot]; this possession also). In #bref("Job 20:26"); #he[לֹֽא־נֻפַּח]; may (unless #he[אֵשׁ]; is regarded as masculine, #xref(<p122o>)[§ 122#h(0.18em)#emph[o];];) be taken impersonally, #emph[fire, without its being blown upon];.—In #bref("Isa 16:8"); and #bref("Hab 3:17"); the predicate in the singular is explained from the collective character of #he[שְׁדֵמוֹת]; (see #emph[h]; above); on the other hand, the masculine form of the predicate is abnormal in #bref("Ps 87:3");, #bref("Prov 2:10");, #bref("12:25");, #bref("29:25");, #bref("Job 8:7");, #bref("36:18");.

#sec("146", [Construction of Compound Subjects], note: []) <s146>

#ml("a") <p146a> #strong[1.]; When the subject is composed of a nomen regens (in the construct state) with a following genitive, the predicate sometimes agrees in gender and number not with the nomen regens, but with the genitive, when this represents the principal idea of the compound subject.#footnote[Sometimes, however, the attraction of the predicate to the genitive may be merely due to juxtaposition.]; Thus #bref("1 Sam 2:4"); #he[קֶ֫שֶׁת גִּבֹּרִים חַתִּים]; #emph[the bow of the mighty men is broken];, as if it were #emph[the mighty men with their bow are broken];; #bref("Exod 26:12");, #bref("Lev 13:9");, #bref("1 Kgs 1:41"); (but the text is clearly very corrupt), 17:16, #bref("Isa 2:11");, #bref("21:17");, #bref("Zech 8:10");, #bref("Job 15:20");, #bref("21:21");, #bref("29:10");, #bref("32:7"); (#he[רֹב שָׁנִים]; equivalent to #emph[many years];); 38:21; with the predicate preceding, #bref("2 Sam 10:9");, unless it is to be explained according to #xref(<p145k>)[§ 145#h(0.18em)#emph[k];];.

#ml("b") <p146b> Rem. 1. The cases in which #he[קוֹל]; (#emph[voice, sound];) with a following genitive stands at the beginning of a sentence, apparently in this construction, are really of a different kind. The #he[קוֹל]; is there to be taken as an exclamation, and the supposed predicate as in apposition to the genitive, e.g. #bref("Gen 4:10"); #emph[the voice of thy brother’s blood, which crieth]; (prop. #emph[as one crying];)...!\=#emph[hark! thy brother’s blood is crying];, etc.; #bref("Isa 13:4");, #bref("66:6");. In #bref("Isa 52:8"); an independent verbal\-clause follows the exclamation #emph[the voice of thy watchmen!];; in #bref("Jer 10:22"); and #bref("Song 2:8"); an independent noun\-clause; in #bref("Isa 40:3"); #he[קוֹל קֹרֵא]; #emph[the voice of one that crieth!]; i.e. #emph[hark! there is one crying]; is followed immediately by direct speech; in #bref("Mic 6:9"); #he[קוֹל]; #emph[hark!]; may be used disconnectedly (cf. the almost adverbial use of #he[קוֹל]; in #xref(<p144m>)[§ 144#h(0.18em)#emph[m];];) and #he[יְהֹוָה]; be taken as the subject to #he[יִקְרָא];.

#ml("c") <p146c> 2. When the substantive #he[כֹּל]; (#he[כָּל־];) #emph[entirety]; is used in connection with a genitive as subject of the sentence, the predicate usually agrees in gender and number with the genitive, since #he[כֹּל]; is equivalent in sense to an attribute (#emph[whole, all];) of the genitive; hence, e.g. with the predicate preceding, #bref("Gen 5:5"); #he[וַיִּֽהְיוּ כָּל־יְמֵי אָדָם]; #emph[and all the days of Adam were];, etc. (in 5:23, 9:29, #he[וַיְהִי];; but the Samaritan reads #he[ויהיו]; here also); #bref("Exod 15:20");; with the predicate following, #bref("Ps 150:6");, etc. Exceptions are, e.g. #bref("Lev 17:14"); (but cf. #xref(<p145l>)[§ 145#h(0.18em)#emph[l];];), #bref("Josh 8:25");, #bref("Isa 64:10");, #bref("Prov 16:2");, #bref("Nah 3:7");. On the other hand, in such cases as #bref("Exod 12:16"); the agreement of the #origpage("468") <orig-468>;predicate with #he[כָּל־]; is explained from the stress laid upon the latter, #he[כָּל־מְלָאכָה לֹא]; being equivalent to #emph[the whole of work]; (is forbidden).

#ml("d") <p146d> #strong[2.]; When the subject of the sentence consists of several nouns connected by #emph[wāw copulative];, usually

(#emph[a];) The predicate #emph[following]; is put in the plural, e.g. #bref("Gen 8:22"); #emph[seed time and harvest, and cold and heat... shall not cease]; (#he[לֹא יִשְׁבֹּ֫תוּ];); after subjects of different genders it is in the masculine (as the #emph[prior gender];, cf. #xref(<p132d>)[§ 132#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];), e.g. #bref("Gen 18:11"); #he[אַבְרָהָם וְשָׂרָה זְקֵנִים]; #emph[Abraham and Sarah]; were #emph[old];; #bref("Deut 28:32");, #bref("1 Kgs 1:21");.

#ml("e") <p146e> Rem. Rare exceptions are #bref("Prov 27:9"); #he[שֶׁ֫מֶן וּקְטֹ֫רֶת יְשַׂמַּח־לֵב]; #emph[ointment and perfume rejoice the heart];, where the predicate agrees in gender with the masculine #he[שֶׁ֫מֶן]; (as in #bref("Isa 51:3"); with #he[שָׂשׂוֹן];); on the other hand, in #bref("Exod 21:4"); (where #he[הָֽאִשָּׁה וִֽילָדֶ֫יהָ]; are the subjects) it agrees with #he[הָֽאִשָּׁה]; as being the principal person; in the compound sentence, #bref("Isa 9:4");, it agrees with the feminine subject immediately preceding.#footnote[Similarly with a mixed object, #bref("Gen 33:2"); #emph[he put... Leah and her children]; #he[אַֽחֲרֹנִים]; #emph[after];; #he[אַֽחֲרֹנִים]; agrees with the masculine immediately preceding.];

#ml("f") <p146f> (#emph[b];) The predicate #emph[preceding]; two or more subjects may likewise be used in the plural (#bref("Gen 40:1");, #bref("Job 3:5");, etc.); not infrequently, however, it agrees in gender and number with the first, as being the subject nearest to it. Thus the predicate is put in the singular masculine before several masculines singular in #bref("Gen 9:23");, #bref("11:29");, #bref("21:32");, #bref("24:50");, #bref("34:20");, #bref("Judg 14:5");; before a masculine and a feminine singular, e.g. #bref("Gen 3:8");, #bref("24:55"); #emph[then said]; (#he[וַיּאֹ֫מֶר];) #emph[her brother and her mother];; 33:7; before a masculine singular and a plural, e.g. #bref("Gen 7:7"); #he[וַיָּבֹא נֹחַ וּבָנָיו]; #emph[and Noah went in, and his sons];, etc.; #bref("Gen 8:18"); (where feminines plural also follow); 44:14, #bref("Exod 15:1");, #bref("2 Sam 5:21");; before collectives feminine and masculine, #bref("2 Sam 12:2");.

#ml("g") <p146g> Similarly, the feminine singular occurs before several feminines singular, e.g. #bref("Gen 31:14"); #he[וַתַּ֫עַן רָחֵל וְלֵאָה]; #emph[then answered Rachel and Leah];; before a feminine singular and a feminine plural, e.g. #bref("Gen 24:61");; before a feminine singular and a masculine singular, #bref("Num 12:1"); #he[וַתְּדַבֵּר מִרְיָם וְאַֽהֲרוֹן]; #emph[then spake Miriam and Aaron];; #bref("Judg 5:1");; before a feminine singular and a masculine plural, e.g. #bref("Gen 33:7"); (cf., on the other hand, #bref("Ps 75:4"); #he[נְמֹגִים אֶ֫רֶץ וְכָל־יֽשְׁבֶ֫יהָ]; #emph[dissolved are the earth and all the inhabitants thereof];). The plural feminine occurs before a plural feminine and a plural masculine in #bref("Amos 8:13");.—In #bref("Jer 44:25"); for #he[אַתֶּם וּנְשֵׁיכֶם]; read #he[אַתֶּם הַנָּשִׁים]; with the LXX, and cf. verse 19.

#ml("h") <p146h> (#emph[c];) When other predicates follow after the subjects have been mentioned, they are necessarily put in the plural; cf. #bref("Gen 21:32");, #bref("24:61");, #bref("31:14");, #bref("33:7");, etc., and #xref(<p145s>)[§ 145#h(0.18em)#emph[s];];. #origpage("469") <orig-469>;

#sec("147", [Incomplete Sentences], note: []) <s147>

#ml("a") <p147a> #strong[1.]; Sentences are called #emph[incomplete];, in which either the subject or the predicate or both must in some way be supplied from the context.#footnote[This does not apply to such cases as #bref("Gen 33:8");, where an infinitive with #he[לְ]; appears alone in answer to a question, the substance of the question being presupposed as a main clause; cf. also #bref("Gen 26:7");, where #he[הִיא]; must again be supplied after #he[אִשְׁתִּי];.]; Besides the instances enumerated in #xref(<p116s>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[s];]; (omission of the personal pronoun when subject of a participial clause) and the periphrases for negative attributes #xref(<p152u>)[§ 152#h(0.18em)#emph[u];];, this description includes certain (noun\-) clauses introduced by #he[הִנֵּה]; (see #emph[b]; below), and also a number of exclamations of the most varied kinds (see #emph[c]; below).

Rem. Incomplete sentences are very common in Chronicles, but are mostly due to the bad condition of the text; cf. Driver, #emph[Introd.];#super[6];, p. 537, no. 27. Thus in #bref("2 Chr 11:22");#super[#emph[b];]; restore #he[חָשַׁב];, with the LXX, before #he[לְהַמְלִיכוֹ];; in 35:21 add #he[בָּ֫אתִי];, with the LXX, after #he[הַיּוֹם]; and read #he[פְּרָת]; for #he[בֵּית];; in #bref("2 Chr 19:6"); and 28:21 the pronoun #he[הוּא]; is wanted as subject, and in 30:9 the predicate #he[יִֽהְיוּ];; cf. also the unusual expressions in #bref("1 Chr 9:33"); (#bref("Ezra 3:3");), #bref("1 Chr 15:13"); (#emph[ye were not present?];), #bref("2 Chr 15:3");, #bref("16:10");, 12 (bis), 18:3.

#ml("b") <p147b> #strong[2.]; The demonstrative particle #he[הֵן];, #he[הִנֵּה]; #emph[en, ecce]; may be used either absolutely (as a kind of interjection, cf. #xref(<p105b>)[§ 105#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];) before complete noun\- or verbal\-clauses, e.g. #bref("Gen 28:15"); #he[וְהִנֵּה אָֽנֹכִי עִמָּךְ]; #emph[and, behold! I am with thee];; 37:7, 48:21, #bref("Exod 3:13");, #bref("34:10");, or may take the pronoun, which would be the natural subject of a noun\-clause, in the form of a suffix, see #xref(<p100o>)[§ 100#h(0.18em)#emph[o];];. Whether these suffixes are to be regarded as in the accusative has been shown to be doubtful in #xref(<p100p>)[§ 100#h(0.18em)#emph[p];];. However, in the case of #he[הִנֵּה]; the analogy of the corresponding Arabic demonstrative particle #emph[ʾinna]; (followed by an accusative of the noun) is significant.#footnote[On the same analogy any substantive following #he[הִנֵּה]; would have to be regarded as originally a virtual accusative. Since, however, Hebrew does not possess case\-terminations (as the Arabic does, and uses the accusative necessarily after #emph[ʾinna];), it is very doubtful whether, and how far, substantives following #he[הִנֵּה]; were felt to be accusatives.]; If #he[הִנֵּה]; with a suffix and a following adjective or participle (see the examples in #xref(<p116p>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[p];]; and #emph[q];) forms a noun\-clause, the subject proper, to which #he[הִנֵּה]; with the suffix refers, must, strictly speaking, be supplied again before the predicate.#footnote[That these are real noun\-clauses and that the participle (e.g. #he[מֵת]; in #he[הִנְּךָ מֵת]; #bref("Gen 20:8");) cannot be taken as a second accusative (as it were #emph[ecce te moriturum];), is also shown by the analogy of Arabic, where after #emph[ʾinna]; with an accusative the predicate is expressly in the #emph[nominative];.]; Sometimes, however, the pronoun referring to the subject is wanting, and the simple #he[הִנֵּה]; takes the place of the #origpage("470") <orig-470>;subject and copula (as #bref("Gen 18:9"); #he[הִנֵּה בָאֹ֫הֶל]; #emph[behold]; she is #emph[in the tent];; 42:28), or there is no indication whatever of the predicate, so that the sentence is limited to #he[הִנֵּה]; with the suffix, as in the frequent use of #he[הִנֵּ֫נִי];, #he[הִנֶּ֫נִּי]; #emph[here am I];, in answer to an address. Elsewhere a substantive follows #he[הִנֵּה]; (or #he[הֵן]; #bref("Gen 11:6");, #bref("Job 31:35");), and #he[הִנֵּה]; then includes the meaning of a demonstrative pronoun and the copula, e.g. #bref("Gen 22:7"); #he[הִנֵּה הָאֵשׁ וְהָֽעֵצִים]; #emph[here is the fire and the wood];, etc.; 12:19 #emph[behold]; thou hast #emph[thy wife!]; #bref("Exod 24:8");; with reference to the past, e.g. #bref("Amos 7:1"); #he[וְהִנֵּה לֶ֫קֶשׁ וג׳]; #emph[and lo];, it was #emph[the latter growth];, etc. By a very pregnant construction the simple #he[הִנֵּה]; is used as the equivalent of a sentence in #bref("Job 9:19");, #emph[lo, here am I!];

#ml("c") <p147c> #strong[3.]; Examples of exclamations (threatening, complaining, triumphing, especially warlike or seditious) in which, owing to the excitement of the speaker, some indispensable member of the sentence is suppressed, are—(#emph[a];) with suppression of the predicate (which has frequently to be supplied in the form of a jussive), e.g. #bref("Judg 7:20"); #emph[a sword for the Lord and for Gideon!]; (verse 18 without #he[חֶ֫רֶב];); #bref("2 Sam 20:1"); and #bref("2 Chr 10:16"); (cf. also #bref("1 Kgs 22:36");) #emph[every man to his tents, O Israel!]; (i.e. #emph[let every man go to]; or #emph[remain in his tent];); without #he[אִישׁ]; #bref("1 Kgs 12:16");; moreover, #bref("Isa 1:28");, #bref("13:4"); (on the exclamatory #he[קוֹל]; equivalent to #emph[hark!]; cf. #xref(<p146b>)[§ 146#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];); 28:10, 29:16 (#he[הַפְכְּכֶם]; #emph[O your perversity!]; i.e. how great it is!); #bref("Jer 49:16"); (if #he[תִּפְלַצְתְּךָ]; be equivalent to #emph[terror be upon thee!];); #bref("Joel 4:14");, #bref("Mal 1:13"); (#he[הִנֵּה מַתְּלָאָה]; #emph[behold what a weariness!];); #bref("Job 22:29");; perhaps also #bref("Gen 49:4"); #he[פַּ֫חַז כַּמַּ֫יִם]; #emph[a bubbling over as water]; (sc. happened), unless it is better to supply a subject #he[אַתָּה]; (#emph[thou wast];).—(#emph[b];) With suppression of the subject, #bref("Judg 4:20");, cf. #xref(<p152k>)[§ 152#h(0.18em)#emph[k];];; #bref("Job 15:23"); #he[אַיֵּה]; #emph[where]; sc. is bread?—(#emph[c];) With suppression of both subject and predicate, #bref("Judg 7:18"); (see above); #bref("1 Kgs 12:16"); (see above); #bref("2 Kgs 9:27"); #he[גַּם אֹתוֹ]; #emph[him also!]; explained immediately afterwards by #he[הַכֻּהוּ]; #emph[smite him!]; #bref("Hos 5:8"); #emph[after thee, Benjamin!]; sc. is the enemy (differently in #bref("Judg 5:14");); #bref("Ps 6:4");, #bref("90:13");, #bref("Hab 2:6"); #he[עַד־מָתַי];; #bref("Ps 74:9"); #he[עַד־מָה];.—On #he[וָלֹא]; #emph[and if not]; (unless #he[וְלוּ]; is to be read), #bref("2 Sam 13:26");, #bref("2 Kgs 5:17");, see § 159 #emph[dd];.

#ml("d") <p147d> Rem. 1. To the class of incomplete sentences naturally belong exclamations introduced by interjections #he[אֲהָהּ];, #he[אוֹי];, #he[הוֹי];, #he[הַס];;#footnote[We do not consider here the cases in which these interjections (e.g. #he[הַם]; #bref("Judg 3:19");, #bref("Amos 6:10");) stand quite disconnectedly (so always #he[אָח]; and #he[הֶאָח];).]; cf. § 105. After the first two the object of the #emph[threat]; or #emph[imprecation]; follows regularly with #he[לְ]; (cf. #emph[vae tibi];) or #he[אֶל־]; or #he[עַל־];, e.g. #he[אוֹי לָ֫נוּ]; #emph[woe unto us!]; #bref("1 Sam 4:8");, #bref("Isa 6:5");; cf. also #he[אֲהָהּ לַיּוֹם]; #emph[alas for the day!]; #bref("Joel 1:15");; on the other hand, the object of #emph[commiseration]; (after #he[הוֹי];) follows mostly in the vocative, or rather in the accusative of exclamation (cf. #emph[vae te]; in Plautus); so in lamentation for the dead, #he[הוֹי אָחִי]; #emph[alas, my brother!]; #bref("1 Kgs 13:30");, #origpage("471") <orig-471>;#bref("Jer 22:18");; #he[הוֹי גּוֹי חֹטֵא]; #emph[ah, sinful nation!]; #bref("Isa 1:4");, #bref("5:8, 11, 18, 20, 22"); (#emph[ah! they that...];).—For #he[הַס]; cf. #bref("Hab 2:20");, #bref("Zeph 1:7");, #bref("Zech 2:17");.

#ml("e") <p147e> 2. Finally, instances of noun\-clauses shortened in an unusual manner may perhaps occur in #he[יְדֵיהֶם]; and #he[רַגְלֵיהֶם]; #bref("Ps 115:7");, for #he[יָדַ֫יִם לָהֶם]; #emph[they have hands];, etc.; cf. verses 5 and 6 #he[פֶּֽה־לָהֶם];, etc. Perhaps also #he[וּפִֽילַגְשׁוֹ]; #bref("Gen 22:24");, and #he[וְחָלְיוֹ]; #bref("Eccl 5:16"); are to be regarded in the same way, but hardly #he[נְבִֽיאֲכֶם]; #bref("Num 12:6");; cf. #xref(<p128d>)[§ 128#h(0.18em)#emph[d];]; above.

#bookhead(3, [II. Special Kinds of Sentences], note: []);

#sec("148", [Exclamations], note: []) <s148>

#ml("a") <p148a> The originally interrogative #he[מָה]; is used to introduce exclamations of wonder or indignation \= #emph[O how!]; or ridicule, #emph[why! how!]; sometimes strengthened by #he[זֶה]; or #he[זֹאת]; according to #xref(<p136c>)[§ 136#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];.—Astonishment or indignation at something which has happened is introduced by #he[אֵיךְ]; #emph[how]; (likewise originally interrogative) with the perfect; the indignant refusal of a demand by #he[אֵיךְ]; (but also by #he[מָה]; #bref("Job 31:1");) with the imperfect; an exclamation of lamentation by #he[אֵיכָה];, less frequently #he[אֵיךְ]; #emph[how!];; in #bref("Joel 1:18"); by #he[מָה];.

Examples:—

#ml("b") <p148b> #he[מָה]; (or #he[מַה־]; with a following #emph[Dagĕš];, see § 37) expressing admiration (or astonishment) before verbal\-clauses, e.g. #bref("Gen 27:20"); (#he[מַה־זֶּה];); 38:29, #bref("Num 24:5"); (#emph[how goodly are...!];); #bref("Ps 21:2");, #bref("Song 7:2");; before the predicate of noun\-clauses, e.g. #bref("Gen 28:17");, #bref("Ps 8:2");; mockingly before the verb, #bref("2 Sam 6:20"); (#emph[how glorious was...!];); #bref("Jer 22:23");, #bref("Job 26:2"); f.; indignantly, #bref("Gen 3:13"); #he[מַה־זֹּאת];; 4:10, 20:9, 31:26 #emph[what hast thou done!];

#he[אֵיךְ]; with the perfect, e.g. #bref("Gen 26:9");, #bref("Ps 73:19");; in scornful exclamation, #bref("Isa 14:4");, 12; in a lament (usually #he[אֵיכָה];), #bref("2 Sam 1:25");, 27; with the imperfect, in a reproachful question, #bref("Gen 39:9");, #bref("44:8");, #bref("Ps 11:1");, #bref("137:4");; in a mocking imitation of lament, #bref("Mic 2:4");.

#he[אֵיכָה]; with the perfect, #bref("Isa 1:21");, #bref("Lam 1:1");; with the imperfect, #bref("Lam 2:1");, #bref("4:1");.

#ml("c") <p148c> Rem. 1. The close relation between a question and an exclamation appears also in the interrogative personal pronoun #he[מִי]; in such cases as #bref("Mic 7:18"); #he[מִי־אֵל כָּמ֫וֹךָ]; #emph[who is a God like unto thee?]; and so in general in #emph[rhetorical]; questions as the expression of a forcible denial; similarly in the use of an interrogative sentence to express a wish, see #xref(<p150d>)[§ 150#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];, 151 #emph[a];.

#ml("d") <p148d> 2. A weaker form of exclamation is sometimes produced by the insertion of a corroborative #he[כִּי]; #emph[verily, surely];, before the predicate, #bref("Gen 18:20");; cf. 33:11, #bref("Isa 7:9");, and the analogous cases in the apodoses of conditional sentences, § 159 #emph[ee];.

#sec("149", [Sentences which express an Oath or Asseveration], note: []) <s149>

#ml("a") <p149a> The particle #he[אִם];, in the sense of #emph[certainly not];, and #he[אִם־לֹא]; (rarely #he[כִּי]; #bref("Gen 22:16");) in the sense of #emph[certainly];, are used to introduce promises or threats confirmed by an oath (especially after such formulae as #he[חַי־יְהֹוָה];, #origpage("472") <orig-472>;#he[חֵֽי־נַפְשְׁךָ];,#footnote[Also combined #he[חַי־יְהֹוָה וְחֵי נַפְשְׁךָ]; #bref("1 Sam 20:3");, #bref("25:26"); #emph[as the Lord liveth, and as thy soul]; (i.e. #emph[thou];) #emph[liveth!]; (Also in #bref("2 Sam 11:11"); read #he[חַי־יהוה]; instead of the impossible #he[חַיֶּ֫ךְ];). On #he[חַי]; and #he[חֵי]; in these noun\-clauses (prop. #emph[living is the Lord];, etc.), cf. #xref(<p93aa>)[§ 93#h(0.18em)#emph[aa];];, note.]; #he[חַי אָ֫נִי];, #he[בִּי נִשְׁבַּ֫עְתִּי];, etc., as well as after imprecations, see below), and also simple asseverations, e.g. #bref("1 Sam 2:30");, #bref("2 Sam 20:20");, #bref("Job 27:5"); after #he[חָלִ֫ילָה לִי]; #emph[far be it from me];, but mostly without any introductory formula.

#ml("b") <p149b> Rem. No certain explanation of these particles has yet been given. According to the usual view, phrases expressing an oath depend on the suppression of an imprecation upon oneself, e.g. #emph[the Lord do so unto me, if I do it]; equivalent to #emph[I certainly will not do it];; then naturally #he[אִם־לֹא]; properly #emph[if I do it not]; equivalent to #emph[I certainly will do it];. It is indeed difficult to understand such self\-imprecations, put into the mouth of God, as in #bref("Deut 1:34"); f., #bref("Isa 14:24");, #bref("22:14");, #bref("Jer 22:6");, #bref("Ezek 3:6");, #bref("35:6");, #bref("Ps 95:11");. Possibly, however, the consciousness of the real meaning of the formula was lost at an early period, and #he[אִם־לֹא]; simply came to express #emph[verily];, #he[אִם]; #emph[verily not];.—In #bref("1 Sam 25:22");, where, instead of a self\-imprecation, a curse is pronounced upon others, read #he[לְדָוִד]; with the LXX for #he[לְאֹֽיְבֵי דָוִד];.

Examples:—

#ml("c") <p149c> (#emph[a];) The particles #he[אִם]; and #he[אִם־לֹא]; used after the utterance of an oath and after formulae of swearing, e.g. #bref("2 Sam 11:11"); (see note on #emph[a];) #he[חַי־יְהֹוָה וְחֵי נַפְשֶׁ֫ךָ אִם־אִֽעֱשֶׂה אֶת־הַדָּבָר הַזֶּה]; #emph[as the Lord liveth, and as thy soul liveth, I will not do this thing];; #bref("1 Sam 14:45");, #bref("2 Kgs 5:16"); (after #he[חַי יְהֹוָה];; in #bref("1 Sam 14:39"); and 29:6 #he[חַי־י׳]; is followed by a simple #he[כִּי];); #bref("Song 2:7");, #bref("3:5"); (after #he[הִשְׁבַּ֫עְתִּי]; #emph[I adjure you];); cf. also #bref("Gen 14:23");, #bref("21:23");, #bref("26:29");; spoken by God, #bref("Deut 1:34"); f., #bref("1 Sam 3:14");, #bref("Ps 95:11");; similarly #he[אִם־לֹא]; #bref("Gen 24:37"); f.; spoken by God, #bref("Isa 14:24");, where #he[אִם־לֹא]; occurs first with the perfect in the sense of a prophetic perfect, #xref(<p106n>)[§ 106#h(0.18em)#emph[n];];, but in the parallel clause with the imperfect; #bref("Jer 22:6");; in #bref("Gen 31:52"); the negative oath introduced by #he[אִם־אָ֫נִי];, #he[אִם־אַתָּה]; is immediately afterwards continued by #he[לֹא]; with the imperfect.—In #bref("Ezek 34:10"); the threat introduced in verse 8 by #he[אִם־לֹא]; is, after a long parenthesis, resumed with #he[הִנְנִי];.

#ml("d") <p149d> (#emph[b];) #he[אִם]; and #he[אִם־לֹא]; after formulae of cursing, e.g. #bref("1 Sam 3:17"); #he[כֹּה יַֽעֲשֶׂה־לְּךָ אֱלֹהִים וְכֹה יוֹסִיף אִם־תְּכַחֵד מִמֶּ֫נִּי דָבָר וג׳]; #emph[God do so to thee, and more also! thou shalt not hide anything from me];, etc.; cf. #bref("1 Sam 25:22");. On the other hand, #he[כִּי]; follows the curse, in #bref("1 Sam 14:44");, #bref("1 Kgs 2:23"); (here with a perfect), and in #bref("2 Sam 3:35"); #he[כִּי אִם];; in #bref("1 Sam 25:34"); the preceding #he[כִּי]; is repeated before #he[אִם];; in #bref("1 Sam 20:13"); the purport of the asseveration is repeated (after the insertion of a conditional sentence) in the perfect consecutive.

#ml("e") <p149e> (#emph[c];) #he[אִם]; and #he[אִם־לֹא]; as simple particles of asseveration, e.g. #bref("Judg 5:8"); #he[מָגֵן אִם־יֵרָאֶה וָרֹמַח וג׳]; #emph[truly, there was not a shield and spear seen];, etc., #bref("Isa 22:14");, #bref("Job 6:28"); (in the middle of the sentence); after #he[חָלִ֫ילָה חָלִ֫ילָה]; #emph[absit];, #bref("2 Sam 20:20");; #he[אִם־לֹא]; with the imperf. #bref("Isa 5:9");, with the perfect, #bref("Job 22:20");. #origpage("473") <orig-473>;

#sec("150", [Interrogative Sentences], note: []) <s150>

H. G. Mitchell, ‘The omission of the interrogative particle,’ in #emph[Old Test. and Sem. Studies in memory of W. R. Harper];, Chicago, 1907, i, 113 ff.

#ml("a") <p150a> #strong[1.]; A question need not necessarily be introduced by a special interrogative pronoun or adverb. Frequently#footnote[Mitchell (op. cit.) restricts the number of instances to 39, of which he attributes 12 (or 17) to corruption of the text. Thus in #bref("Gen 27:24"); he would read, with the Samaritan, #he[הַֽאַתָּה]; as in verse 21, in #bref("1 Sam 16:4"); #he[הֲשָׁלֹם];, in #bref("2 Sam 18:29"); #he[הֲשָׁלוֹם]; as in verse 32; similarly he would read the interrogative particle in #bref("2 Kgs 5:26");, #bref("Ezek 11:3");, #bref("Job 40:25");, #bref("41:1");; #bref("1 Sam 30:8");, #bref("2 Kgs 9:19");, #bref("Ezek 11:13");, #bref("17:9");.]; the natural emphasis upon the words is of itself sufficient to indicate an interrogative sentence as such; cf. #bref("Gen 27:24"); #he[אַתָּה זֶה בְּנִי עֵשָׂו]; #emph[thou art my son Esau?]; (but cf. note #super[1]; below) #bref("Gen 18:12");, #bref("Exod 33:14"); (#he[פָּנַי י׳];); #bref("1 Sam 11:12"); #he[שָׁאוּל יִמְלֹךְ עָלֵ֫ינוּ]; #emph[Saul shall reign over us?]; #bref("1 Sam 22:7");, #bref("2 Sam 16:17");, #bref("18:29"); #he[שָׁלוֹם לַנַּ֫עַר]; #emph[is it well with the young man?]; (but cf. note #super[1];); #bref("1 Sam 16:4");, #bref("1 Kgs 1:24");, #bref("Isa 28:28");, #bref("Hos 4:16");, #bref("Zech 8:6"); (#emph[should it also be marvellous in mine eyes?];); #bref("Prov 5:16");. So especially, when the interrogative clause is connected with a preceding sentence by #he[וְ];, e.g. #bref("Jonah 4:11"); #he[וַֽאֲנִי לֹא אָחוּס]; #emph[and I should not have pity?]; #bref("Exod 8:22"); #emph[will they not stone us?]; #bref("Judg 11:23");, #bref("14:16");, #bref("1 Sam 20:9");, #bref("24:20");, #bref("25:11");, #bref("2 Sam 11:11");, #bref("15:20");, #bref("Isa 37:11");, 44#super[19 #emph[b];];, #bref("Jer 25:29");, #bref("45:5");, #bref("49:12");, #bref("Ezek 20:31");, #bref("Job 2:10");, #bref("10:9");; or when (as in some of the examples just given) it is negative (with #he[לֹא]; for #he[הֲלֹא]; #emph[nonne?];), #bref("2 Kgs 5:26"); (but cf. note #super[1];), #bref("Lam 3:38");. #footnote[But in #bref("1 Sam 27:10"); instead of #he[אַל־]; (which according to the usual explanation would expect a negative answer) read either #he[אֶל־מִי]; (#he[עַל־מִי];) with the LXX, or better, #he[אָן]; (#he[אָ֫נָה];) #emph[whither?]; with the Targum. In #bref("2 Sam 23:5"); read #he[חֶפְצִי הֲלֹא]; with Wellhausen.];

#ml("b") <p150b> Rem. The statement formerly made here that the interrogative particle is omitted especially before gutturals, cannot be maintained in view of Mitchell’s statistics (op. cit. p. 123 f.). The supposed considerations of euphony are quite disproved by the 118 cases in which #he[הַ]; or #he[הֶ]; occurs before a guttural.

#ml("c") <p150c> #strong[2.]; As a rule, however, the simple question is introduced by #emph[He interrogative]; #he[הֲ]; (#he[הַ];; as to its form, cf. #xref(<p100k>)[§ 100#h(0.18em)#emph[k];];–#xref(<p100n>)[#emph[n];];), #emph[ne? num?]; the disjunctive question by #he[הֲ]; (#emph[num? utrum?];) in the first clause, and #he[אִם];#footnote[Quite exceptional is the use of the particle #he[אִין]; #emph[num?]; (common in Aramaic) in #bref("1 Sam 21:9"); #he[וְאִין יֶשׁ־פֹּה]; #emph[num est hic?]; The text is, however, undoubtedly corrupt; according to Wellhausen, #emph[Text der Bücher Sam.];, the LXX express the reading #he[רְאֵה הֲיֵשׁ];; but cf. the full discussion of the passage by König, #emph[ZAW.]; xviii. 239 ff.—The above does not apply to interrogative sentences introduced by interrogative pronouns (§ 37) or by the interrogatives compounded with #he[מָה]; #emph[what?]; such as #he[כַּמָּה]; #emph[how many?]; #he[לָ֫מָּה]; #emph[why?]; (see #xref(<p102k>)[§ 102#h(0.18em)#emph[k];];), #he[מַדּוּעַ]; #emph[why?]; (#xref(<p99e>)[§ 99#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];), or by #he[אַיֵּה]; #emph[where?]; #he[אֵיךְ];, #he[אֵיכָה]; #emph[how?]; (§ 148), etc. On the transformation of pronouns and adverbs into interrogative words by means of a prefixed #he[אֵי];, see the Lexicon.]; (also #he[וְאִם];, less frequently #he[אוֹ];) #emph[an?]; in the second, e.g. #origpage("474") <orig-474>;#bref("1 Kgs 22:15"); #he[הֲנֵלֵךְ אִם נֶחְדָּ֑ל];#footnote[On the use of the #emph[imperfect]; in deliberative questions, see #xref(<p107t>)[§ 107#h(0.18em)#emph[t];];; on the #emph[perfectum confidentiae]; in interrogative sentences, see #xref(<p106n>)[§ 106#h(0.18em)#emph[n];];.]; #emph[shall we go... or shall we forbear?]; Cf. also #he[אָן]; #emph[where? whither?]; #he[אָ֫נָה]; #emph[whither];, and J. Barth, #emph[Sprachwiss. Untersuchungen];, i. 13 ff.

The particular uses are as follows:—

#ml("d") <p150d> (#emph[a];) The particle #he[הֲ]; stands primarily before the simple question, when the questioner is wholly uncertain as to the answer to be expected, and may be used either before noun\-clauses, e.g. #bref("Gen 43:7"); #he[הַעוֹד אֲבִיכֶם חַי הֲיֵשׁ לָכֶם אָח]; #emph[is your father yet alive? have ye]; another #emph[brother?]; for #he[הֲיֵשׁ]; cf. #bref("Gen 24:23");, #bref("1 Sam 9:11");; for #he[הֲכִי]; #emph[is it that?]; #bref("Job 6:22");; for #he[הֲכִי יֶשׁ־]; #emph[is there yet?]; #bref("2 Sam 9:1"); (but in #bref("2 Sam 23:19"); for #he[הֲכִי]; read #he[הִנּוֹ]; with #bref("1 Chr 11:25");); for #he[הַאֵין]; #emph[is there not?]; #bref("1 Kgs 22:7");, etc.; or before verbal\-clauses, e.g. #bref("Job 2:3"); #emph[hast thou considered]; (#he[הֲשַׂ֫מְתָּ לִבְּךָ];) #emph[my servant Job?]; In other cases #he[הֲ]; (\= #emph[num?];) is used before questions, to which, from their tone and contents, a negative answer is expected, e.g. #bref("Job 14:14"); #emph[if a man die];, #he[הֲיִחְֽיֶה]; #emph[shall he]; indeed #emph[live]; again? Sometimes a question is so used only as a rhetorical form instead of a negative assertion, or of a surprised or indignant refusal,#footnote[Analogous to this is the use of the interrogative #he[מָה]; in the sense of a reproachful remonstrance instead of a prohibition, as #bref("Song 8:4"); #he[מַה־תָּעִירוּ]; #emph[why should ye stir up?]; i.e. #emph[pray, stir not up];; cf. also #bref("Job 31:1");; see above, § 148.]; e.g. #bref("2 Sam 7:5"); #he[הַֽאַתָּה תִבְנֶה־לִּי בַיִת]; #emph[shalt thou build me an house?]; (in the parallel passage #bref("1 Chr 17:4"); #he[לֹא אַתָּה וג׳]; #emph[thou shalt not];, etc.); #bref("Gen 4:9"); #he[הֲשֹׁמֵר אָחִי אָנֹ֫כִי]; #emph[am I my brother’s keeper?]; cf. #bref("2 Kgs 5:7");, and the two passages where #he[הֲ]; is used before the infinitive (#emph[constr.]; #bref("Job 34:18");, #emph[absol.]; #bref("Job 40:2");; on both, see § 113 #emph[ee];, with the note).—On the other hand, in #bref("1 Kgs 16:31"); for #he[הֲנָקֵל]; (after #he[וַיְהִי];) read #he[הַנָּקֵל];.

#ml("e") <p150e> Rem. 1. A few passages deserve special mention, in which the use of the interrogative is altogether different from our idiom, since it serves merely to express the conviction that the contents of the statement are well known to the hearer, and are unconditionally admitted by him. Thus, #bref("Gen 3:11"); #emph[surely thou hast eaten];; #bref("Gen 27:36"); #he[הֲכִי קָרָא]; prop. #emph[is it so that one names?]; etc., i.e. #emph[of a truth he is rightly named Jacob];; #bref("Gen 29:15"); #emph[verily thou art my brother];; #bref("Deut 11:30");, #bref("Judg 4:6");, #bref("1 Sam 2:27"); #emph[I did indeed];, etc.; 20:37, #bref("1 Kgs 22:3"); #emph[ye know surely...];; #bref("Mic 3:1");, #bref("Job 20:4");.—In #bref("1 Sam 23:19"); (cf. #bref("Ps 54:2");) a surprising communication is introduced in this way (by #he[הֲלֹא];) in order to show it to be absolutely true, and in #bref("Amos 9:7"); a concession is expressed by #he[הֲלוֹא]; #emph[I have, it is true];, etc. Finally, we may include the formula of quotation #he[הֲלֹא הִיא כְתוּבָה]; #bref("Josh 10:13"); or #he[הֲלֹא־הֵם כְּתוּבִים]; equivalent to #emph[surely it is, they are]; written (the latter in #bref("1 Kgs 11:41");, #bref("14:29");, and very often elsewhere in the books of Kings and Chronicles), synonymous with the simple formula of assertion #he[הִנֵּה כְתוּבָה]; #bref("2 Sam 1:18");, and #he[הִנָּם כְּתוּבִים]; #bref("1 Kgs 14:19");, #bref("2 Kgs 15:11");, #bref("2 Chr 27:7");, #bref("32:32");.

Of very frequent occurrence also are questions introduced by #he[לָ֫מָּה];, which really contain an affirmation and are used to state the reason for a request or warning, e.g. #bref("2 Sam 2:22"); #emph[turn thee aside... wherefore should I smite thee to the ground?]; i.e. #emph[otherwise I will]; (or #emph[must];) #emph[smite];, etc.; cf. #bref("1 Sam 19:17");, and Driver on the passage; #bref("2 Chr 25:16");; also #bref("Gen 27:45");, #bref("Exod 32:12"); (#bref("Joel 2:17");, #bref("Ps 79:10");, #bref("115:2");); #bref("Song 1:7");, #bref("Eccl 5:5");, #bref("7:17");, #bref("Dan 1:10");. #origpage("475") <orig-475>;

#ml("f") <p150f> 2. The rare cases in which a #emph[simple]; question is introduced by #he[אִם]; (as sometimes in Latin by #emph[an? is it?];) are really due to the suppression of the first member of a double question; thus #bref("1 Kgs 1:27");, #bref("Isa 29:16");, #bref("Job 6:12");, #bref("39:13");.

#ml("g") <p150g> (#emph[b];) Disjunctive questions are, as a rule, introduced by #he[אִם];—#he[הֲ]; (#emph[utrum—an?];) or sometimes by #he[וְאִם];#footnote[#he[וְאִם]; occurs in #bref("Prov 27:24"); after a negative statement; we should, however, with Dyserinck read #he[וְאֵין];. Not less irregular is #he[הֲלֹא]; instead of #he[אִם לֹא]; in the second clause of #bref("Judg 14:15");, but the text can hardly be correct (cf. Moore, #emph[Judges];, New York, 1895, p. 337); in #bref("1 Sam 23:11"); the second #he[הֲ]; introduces a fresh question which is only loosely connected with the first.—In #bref("Num 17:28"); and in the third clause of #bref("Job 6:13");, #he[הַאִם]; is best taken with Ewald in the sense of #he[הֲלֹא];, since #he[אִם]; from its use in oaths (see above, #xref(<p149b>)[§ 149#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];) may simply mean #emph[verily not];.];—#he[הֲ];, e.g. #bref("Joel 1:2");, #bref("Job 21:4"); (even with #he[הֲ]; repeated after #he[וְאִם]; in a question which implies disbelief, #bref("Gen 17:17");). In #bref("Job 34:17");, #bref("40:8"); f. special emphasis is given to the first member by #he[הַאַף]; prop. #emph[is it even?]; The second member is introduced by #he[אוֹ]; #emph[or]; in #bref("2 Kgs 6:27");, #bref("Job 16:3");, #bref("38:28");, #bref("31:36"); (#bref("Mal 1:8"); #he[אוֹ הֲ];), in each case before #he[מ‍];, and hence no doubt for euphonic reasons, to avoid the combination #he[אִם מ׳];; cf. also #bref("Judg 18:19");, #bref("Eccl 2:19");.

#ml("h") <p150h> Double questions with (#he[וְאִם];) #he[אִם];—#he[הֲ]; need not always be mutually exclusive; frequently the disjunctive form serves (especially in poetic parallelism; but cf. also e.g. #bref("Gen 37:8");) merely to repeat the same question in different words, and thus to express it more emphatically. So #bref("Job 4:17"); #emph[shall mortal man be just before God? or]; (#he[אִם];) #emph[shall a man be pure before his Maker?]; #bref("Job 6:5"); f., 8:3, 10:4 f., 11:2, 7, 22:3, #bref("Isa 10:15");, #bref("Jer 5:29");. The second member may, therefore, just as well be connected by a simple #he[וְ];, e.g. #bref("Job 13:7");, #bref("15:7"); f., 38:16 f.22, 32, 39; cf. also #bref("Ps 8:5"); after #he[מָה];; #bref("Job 21:17"); f. after #he[כַּמָּה];; or even without a conjunction, #bref("Job 8:11");, #bref("22:4");; after #he[מָה]; #bref("Ps 144:3");.

#ml("i") <p150i> (#emph[c];) With regard to #emph[indirect]; questions#footnote[It should here be remarked that the distinction between direct and indirect questions cannot have been recognized by the Hebrew mind to the same extent as it is in Latin or English. In Hebrew there is no difference between the two kinds of sentence, either as regards mood (as in Latin) or in tense and position of the words (as in English). Cf. also #xref(<p137c>)[§ 137#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];.]; after verbs of inquiring, doubting, examining,#footnote[In #bref("Gen 43:6"); the #he[הַ]; after #he[לְהַגִּיד]; is explained from the fact that the latter, according to the context, implies #emph[to give information upon a question];.]; etc., simple questions of this kind take either #he[הֲ]; #emph[whether];, #bref("Gen 8:8");,#footnote[Also in #bref("Eccl 3:21"); we should read #he[הַֽעֹלָה]; and #he[הֲיֹרֶ֫דֶת]; (#emph[whether—whether];) instead of the article which is assumed by the Masora.]; or #he[אִם]; #bref("Gen 15:5");, #bref("2 Kgs 1:2");, #bref("Song 7:13");; even before a noun\-clause, #bref("Jer 5:1");; in #bref("1 Sam 20:10"); the indirect question is introduced by #he[אוֹ];, i.e. probably #emph[if perchance];. In disjunctives (#emph[whether—or];) #he[אִם];—#he[הֲ]; #bref("Num 13:18"); at the end (or #he[אִם־לֹא];—#he[הֲ]; #bref("Gen 24:21");, #bref("27:21");, #bref("37:32");, #bref("Exod 16:4");), and #he[הֲ];—#he[הֲ]; #bref("Num 13:18");, which is followed by #he[אִם];—#he[הֲ];; also #he[אוֹ];—#he[הֲ]; #bref("Eccl 2:19");. The formula #he[מִי יוֹדֵעַ אִם]; has an affirmative force, #emph[who knows whether... not];, like the Latin #emph[nescio an];, #bref("Esth 4:14");.

#ml("k") <p150k> In #bref("Jonah 1:7");, 8 the relative pronouns #he[שֶׁ·]; and #he[אֲשֶׁר]; owing to the following #he[לְמִי]; have become also interrogative, #emph[for whose cause?];

#ml("l") <p150l> (#emph[d];) #he[זֶה]; and #he[הוּא]; (cf. #xref(<p136c>)[§ 136#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];) immediately after the interrogative serve to give vividness to the question; so also #he[אֵפוֹא]; (for which #he[אֵפוֹ]; five times in Job) #emph[then, now];, #bref("Gen 27:33"); #he[מִֽי־אֵפוֹא הוּא]; #emph[who then is he?]; #bref("Judg 9:38");, #bref("Isa 19:12");, #bref("Job 17:15");; #he[אַיֵּה אֵפוֹ]; #origpage("476") <orig-476>;#emph[where then is...?]; However, #he[אֵפוֹא]; may also be placed at the end of the entire question (#bref("Exod 33:16");, #bref("Isa 22:1");; also #bref("Hos 13:10");, since either #he[אֱהִי]; is a dialectical form of #he[אַיֵּה];, or #he[אַיֵּה]; should be read instead of it) or at the beginning of the question proper, after a strongly emphasized word, as in #bref("Gen 27:37");.#footnote[On the other hand, in #bref("Job 9:24"); and 24:25 #he[אֵפוֹ]; is not prefixed to the #he[מִי];, but appended to the conditional sentence.];

#ml("m") <p150m> (#emph[e];) Sometimes one interrogative governs two co\-ordinate clauses, the first of which should rather be subordinated to the second, so that the interrogative word strictly speaking affects only the second; thus #bref("Isa 5:4"); after #he[מַדּוּעַ]; #emph[wherefore looked I... and it brought forth?]; i.e. wherefore brought it forth, while I looked, etc.; #bref("Isa 50:2");; after #he[הֲ]; #bref("Num 32:6");, #bref("Jer 8:4");, also #bref("Num 16:22"); (read #he[הַאִישׁ];); after #he[הֲלֹא]; #bref("Josh 22:20");; after #he[לָ֫מָּה]; #bref("Isa 58:3");, #bref("2 Chr 32:4");; after #he[אֶל־מִי]; #bref("Isa 40:25");.#footnote[Cf. the analogous sentences after #he[יַ֫עַן]; #emph[because];, #bref("Isa 65:12");, #bref("Jer 35:17");; after causal #he[אֲשֶׁר]; #bref("1 Sam 26:23");; after #he[כִּי]; #bref("Isa 12:1");; likewise after #he[גַּם]; § 153 at the end; after #he[פֶּן־]; #bref("Deut 8:12");–14, 25:3, #bref("Josh 6:18");, #bref("2 Sam 12:28");.]; But #he[הֲ]; #bref("Job 4:2"); and #he[הֲלֹא]; 4:21 are separated from the verb to which they belong by the insertion of a conditional clause.

#ml("n") <p150n> #strong[3.]; The affirmative answer is generally expressed, as in Latin, by repeating the emphatic word in the question (or with the second person changed to the first, #bref("Gen 24:58");, #bref("27:24");, #bref("29:5");, #bref("Judg 13:11");), #bref("Gen 29:6");, #bref("37:32"); f., #bref("1 Sam 23:11");, #bref("26:17");, #bref("1 Kgs 21:10");, #bref("Jer 37:17");. (On #he[וָיֵשׁ]; #emph[if it be so]; in the corrected text of #bref("2 Kgs 10:15");, see § 159 #emph[dd];.) As a negative answer the simple #he[לֹא]; is sometimes sufficient, as in #bref("Gen 19:2");, #bref("1 Kgs 3:22");, etc.; cf. #xref(<p152c>)[§ 152#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];; and in #bref("Judg 4:20"); the simple #he[אָֽיִן]; equivalent to #emph[no]; or #emph[no one];.

#sec("151", [Desiderative Sentences], note: []) <s151>

#ml("a") <p151a> A wish may be expressed not only by the simple imperfect (#xref(<p107n>)[§ 107#h(0.18em)#emph[n];];), cohortative (§ 108, especially with #he[נָא]; #xref(<p108c>)[§ 108#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];), jussive (§ 109; with #he[נָא]; #xref(<p109b>)[§ 109#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];), imperative (#xref(<p110a>)[§ 110#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];), perfect consecutive (#xref(<p112aa>)[§ 112#h(0.18em)#emph[aa];];) or by a simple noun\-clause (#xref(<p116r>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[r];];, note, and #xref(<p141g>)[§ 141#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];) but also in the following ways:—

#strong[1.]; By exclamations in the form of #emph[interrogative]; clauses:#footnote[The transition from a question to a wish may be seen, e.g. in #bref("Num 11:4"); #emph[who shall give us flesh to eat?]; i.e. #emph[O that we had flesh to eat!];]; especially sentences with #he[מִי]; followed by the imperfect as being the mood of that which is still unfulfilled but possible, and hence also of that which is desired, e.g. #bref("2 Sam 15:4"); #he[מִֽי־יְשִׂמֵ֫נִי שֹׁפֵט]; #emph[who maketh me judge?]; i.e. #emph[O that I were made judge!]; #bref("1 Sam 20:10");, #bref("2 Sam 23:15");. On the other hand, #he[מִי]; with the perfect (#bref("Gen 21:7");, #bref("Num 23:10");, #bref("1 Sam 26:9");, #bref("Isa 53:1");, etc.) or participle (#bref("Ps 59:8");, #bref("Prov 24:22");, etc.), rather expresses a rhetorical question, i.e. a denial, cf. #xref(<p150d>)[§ 150#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];. Especially frequent is the use of #he[מִֽי־יִתֵּן]; (prop. #emph[who gives?];) to introduce all kinds of desiderative clauses (see under #emph[b];).—In #bref("Mal 1:10"); the desiderative clause proper is co\-ordinated with an interrogative clause, #origpage("477") <orig-477>;#he[מִי גַם־בָּכֶם וְיִסְגֹּר דְּלָתַ֫יִם]; #emph[would that one were among you and would shut the doors];, i.e. O that one would shut the doors!

#ml("b") <p151b> Rem. Sometimes the original sense of #he[מִֽי־יִתֵּן]; is still plainly discernible, e.g. #bref("Judg 9:29"); #he[מִֽי־יִתֵּן אֶת־הָעָם הַזֶּה בְיָדִי]; #emph[who gives this people into my hand?]; equivalent to, O that this people were given into my hand! cf. #bref("Ps 55:7");. In these examples, however, #he[מִֽי־יִתֵּן]; is still equivalent to #emph[O had I!]; and in numerous other instances the idea of #emph[giving]; has entirely disappeared, #he[מִֽי־יִתֵּן]; having become stereotyped as a mere desiderative particle (#emph[utinam];). Its construction is either—

(#emph[a];) With the #emph[accusative]; (in accordance with its original meaning) of a substantive, #bref("Deut 28:67"); #emph[would that it were even!... morning!]; #bref("Judg 9:29");, #bref("Ps 14:7"); (53:7), 55:7; with an accusative and a following infinitive, #bref("Job 11:5");; with two accusatives, #bref("Num 11:29");, #bref("Jer 8:23");; with the accusative of an infinitive, #bref("Exod 16:3");, #bref("2 Sam 19:1"); #he[מִֽי־יִתֵּן מוּתִי אֲנִי תַחְתֶּ֫יךָ]; #emph[would that I had died for thee]; (for #he[אֲנִי]; cf. #xref(<p135f>)[§ 135#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];); of a participle, #bref("Job 31:35");; of a personal pronoun (as a suffix), #bref("Job 29:2"); (with a following #he[כְּ];; but #he[מִֽי־יִתְּנֵ֫נִי]; #bref("Isa 27:4"); and #bref("Jer 9:1"); with a following accusative is not simply equivalent to #he[מִֽי־יִתֵּן לִי];, but is properly #emph[who endows me with];, etc.; cf. #xref(<p117ff>)[§ 117#h(0.18em)#emph[ff];];).—With a still greater weakening of the original meaning #he[מִֽי־יִתֵּן]; is used with an adjective in #bref("Job 14:4"); #emph[could a clean thing but come out of an unclean!]; i.e. #emph[how can a clean thing come];, etc.; similarly in #bref("Job 31:31"); #emph[who can find one that hath not been satisfied!];

#ml("c") <p151c> (#emph[b];) With a following perfect, #bref("Job 23:3"); (cf. #xref(<p120e>)[§ 120#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];); with a perfect consecutive, #bref("Deut 5:26"); #emph[O that they had such an heart!];

#ml("d") <p151d> (#emph[c];) With a following imperfect, #bref("Job 6:8");, #bref("13:5");, #bref("14:13");; in #bref("Job 19:23"); the imperfect is twice added with #emph[Wāw]; (cf. #emph[a]; above, on #bref("Mal 1:10");).

On the cohortative in the apodosis to such desiderative clauses, cf. #xref(<p108f>)[§ 108#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];.

#ml("e") <p151e> #strong[2.]; The wish may also be expressed by the particles #he[אִם]; (#bref("Ps 81:9");, #bref("95:7");, #bref("139:19");, #bref("Prov 24:11");, #bref("1 Chr 4:10");; always with a following imperfect) and #he[לוּ]; (for which in #bref("Ps 119:5"); we have #he[אַחְלַי];, #bref("2 Kgs 5:3"); #he[אַֽחֲלֵי];, from #he[אָח]; #emph[ah!]; and #he[לַי];\=#he[לוּ];; both with a following imperfect) #emph[si, o si! utinam];.#footnote[Cf. a similar transition from a conditional to a desiderative particle, in consequence of the suppression of the apodosis, in the English, #emph[O if I had!]; and the like; e.g. #bref("Num 22:29"); #emph[if there were]; (#he[לוּ יֶשׁ־];) #emph[a sword in my hand now had I surely killed thee!];]; #he[לוּ]; is followed by the imperfect, #bref("Gen 17:18");, #bref("Job 6:2");; by the jussive, #bref("Gen 30:34"); (rather concessive, equivalent to #emph[let it be so];); by the perfect, as the expression of a wish that something might have happened in past time (cf. #xref(<p106p>)[§ 106#h(0.18em)#emph[p];];), #bref("Num 14:2"); #he[לוּ מַ֫תְנוּ]; #emph[would that we had died];; 20:3 and #bref("Josh 7:7"); (both times #he[וְלוּ];); on the other hand, #bref("Isa 48:18"); and 63:19 (both times #he[לוּא];) to express a wish that something expected in the future may already have happened.—On #he[לוּ]; with the imperative (by an anacoluthon) #bref("Gen 23:13"); cf. #xref(<p110e>)[§ 110#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];. On the perfect after #he[כִּי אִם]; #bref("Gen 40:14");, #bref("2 Kgs 5:20");, cf. #xref(<p106n>)[§ 106#h(0.18em)#emph[n];];, note 2. #origpage("478") <orig-478>;

#sec("152", [Negative Sentences], note: []) <s152>

#ml("a") <p152a> #strong[1.]; Besides the use of rhetorical questions (#xref(<p150d>)[§§ 150#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];, #xref(<p151a>)[151#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];), independent sentences are made negative by the adverbs #he[לֹא]; (#bref("Job 6:21");, where instead of the #emph[K#super[e];th.]; #he[לוֹ]; we must evidently read #he[לֹא];; perhaps preserved as a substantive) \= the Greek οὐ, #emph[not];, #he[אַל־]; \= μή (#bref("Job 24:25"); as a substantive), #he[אֵין]; (#emph[it is];) #emph[not];; #he[טֶ֫רֶם]; #emph[not yet];, #he[אֶ֫פֶס]; #emph[not];, #he[אַפְסִי]; (cf. #xref(<p90m>)[§ 90#h(0.18em)#emph[m];];) #emph[not];. The forms #he[בַּל];, #he[בְּלִי];, #he[בִּלְתִּי]; #emph[not]; belong almost entirely to poetry.—With regard to #he[לֹא]; and #he[אֵין]; the main distinction is that verbal\-clauses (rarely noun\-clauses, see #emph[e];) are regularly negatived by #he[לֹא]; (besides its use as negativing #emph[single]; words#footnote[Especially in compounds, e.g. #he[לֹא־אֵל]; lit. #emph[a no\-God]; (Germ. #emph[Ungott];) who is indeed called a god, but is not really a god, #bref("Deut 32:21");; #he[לֹא אֱלֹהַּ]; verse 17, cf. #bref("Jer 5:7");, #bref("2 Chr 13:9");; #he[לֹא־עָם]; lit. #emph[a not\-people]; (Germ. #emph[Unvolk];), #bref("Deut 32:21");; #he[לֹא דָבָר]; #emph[a nothing];, #bref("Amos 6:13");; #he[לֹא עֵץ]; lit. #emph[not\-wood];, #bref("Isa 10:15");; #he[לֹא־אִישׁ];, #he[לֹֽא־אָדָם]; lit. #emph[not\-man];, #emph[superhuman]; (of God), #bref("Isa 31:8");; #he[לֹא־צֶ֫דֶק]; #emph[unrighteousness];, #bref("Jer 22:13");, cf. #bref("Ezek 22:29");; #he[לֹא־סְדָרִים]; #emph[disorder];, #bref("Job 10:22");; #he[לֹֽא־חָמָם]; #emph[not\-violence];, 16:17; after #he[לְ]; #bref("Job 26:2"); f. (#he[לֹא־כֹחַ];, #he[לֹא־עֹז]; #emph[helplessness];, #he[לֹא חָכְמָה]; #emph[insipientia];); cf. also #bref("Isa 55:2"); #he[בְּלוֹא לְשָׂבְעָה]; #emph[for what is unsatisfying];; #bref("Ps 44:13");, #bref("Job 8:11");, #bref("15:32");, #bref("1 Chr 12:33");.—In #bref("Num 20:5"); a construct state with several genitives is negatived by #he[לֹא];.—Also #he[לֹא]; is used with an infinitive, #bref("Num 35:23");; with an adjective, #he[לֹא חָכָם]; #emph[unwise];, #bref("Deut 32:6");, #bref("Hos 13:13");; #he[לֹֽא־חָסִיד]; #emph[impius];, #bref("Ps 43:1");; #he[לֹא־עָז]; and #he[לֹֽא־עָצוּם]; #emph[not strong];, #bref("Prov 30:25"); f.; #he[לֹא־כֵן]; #emph[unsuitably];, #bref("2 Kgs 7:9");; #he[לֹא־טוֹב]; #emph[not\-good];, #bref("Isa 65:2");, #bref("Ezek 20:25");, etc.; #he[לֹא טָהוֹר]; #emph[not\-clean];, #bref("2 Chr 30:17");; with a participle, e.g. #bref("Jer 2:2"); (#emph[unsown];); 6:8, #bref("Ezek 4:14");, #bref("22:24");, #bref("Zeph 2:1");, 3:5; the Masora, however, requires #he[נֻחָ֫מָה]; in #bref("Isa 54:11");, #he[נֶֽעֱזָ֫בָה]; in 62:12, #he[נוֹשָׁ֫בָה]; in #bref("Jer 6:8");, #he[רֻחָ֫מָה]; in #bref("Hos 1:6");, i.e. always 3rd sing. fem. perf. in pause \= #emph[she was not comforted];, etc., and consequently not compounds, but either relative clauses or (#bref("Isa 54:11");, #bref("Hos 1:6");, and especially 2:25) main clauses instead of proper names.—On the above compounds generally, cf. the dissertation mentioned in #xref(<p81d>)[§ 81#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];, note 2; on their use in sentences expressing a state, to convey attributive ideas, see #emph[u]; below.];), while #he[אֵין]; is used exclusively with noun\-clauses (see the examples below).

#ml("b") <p152b> The conjunctions #he[פֶּן־]; and #he[לְבִלְתִּי]; #emph[that not];, serve to negative dependent clauses. The particular uses of these particles are as follows:—

(#emph[a];) #he[לֹא]; (less frequently #he[לוֹא];), like οὐ, οὐκ, is used regularly for the #emph[objective, unconditional]; negation, and hence is usually connected with the perfect or imperfect (as indicative); on #he[לֹא]; with the imperfect to express an unconditional prohibition, see #xref(<p107o>)[§ 107#h(0.18em)#emph[o];];; on its use with the jussive, see #xref(<p109d>)[§ 109#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];.—On #he[לֹא]; for #he[הֲלֹא]; #emph[nonne];, in interrogative sentences, cf. #xref(<p150a>)[§ 150#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];. In connection with #he[כֹּל];, #he[כָּל־]; (\= #emph[any];), #he[לֹא]; is used to express an #emph[absolute]; negation, #emph[nullus, none whatever]; (cf. the French #emph[ne... personne, ne... rien];), usually in the order #he[לֹא... כֹּל];, e.g. #bref("Gen 3:1"); #he[לֹא תֹֽאכְלוּ מִכֹּל עֵץ הַגָּן]; #emph[ye shall not eat of any tree of the garden];; 9:11, #bref("Exod 10:15");, #bref("20:10");, #bref("Lev 7:23");, #bref("Deut 8:9");, #bref("Jer 13:7");, #bref("32:17"); (#he[לֹא... כָּל־דָּבָר]; #emph[nothing at all];; cf. the same statement in the form of a rhetorical question, #bref("Jer 32:27");); #bref("Prov 12:21");, #bref("30:30"); #origpage("479") <orig-479>;#he[לֹא... מִפְּנֵי־כֹל]; and turneth #emph[not]; away #emph[for any];; #bref("2 Chr 32:15");; but cf. also the inverted order, #bref("Exod 12:16"); #he[כָּל־מְלָאכָה לֹא־יֵֽעָשֶׂה]; #emph[no manner of work shall be done];; 12:43, 15:26, 22:21, #bref("Lev 16:17");, #bref("Job 33:13");, #bref("Dan 11:37");. The meaning is different when #he[כֹּל]; by being determinate is used in the sense of #emph[whole];, e.g. #bref("Num 23:13"); #he[כֻּלּוֹ לֹא תִרְאֶה]; #emph[thou shalt not see them all];, but only a part.

Analogous to #he[לֹא... כֹּל]; is the use of #he[אִישׁ... לֹא]; #bref("Gen 23:6");, etc., in verbal\-clauses in the sense of #emph[no one at all, not a single one];. On #he[אֵין־כֹּל]; #emph[nothing at all];, see under #emph[p];.

#ml("c") <p152c> Rem. 1. The examples in which #he[לֹא]; is used absolutely as a negative answer, equivalent to #emph[certainly not! no!]; must be regarded as extremely short verbal\-clauses, e.g. #bref("Gen 19:2"); (#he[לֹא]; according to the context for #he[לֹא נָסוּר]; etc.); 23:11, 42:10, #bref("Hag 2:12");, #bref("Job 23:6");, sometimes with a following #he[כִּי]; #emph[but];, #bref("Gen 19:2"); (see above); #bref("Josh 5:14");, #bref("1 Kgs 3:22");.

#ml("d") <p152d> 2. The negation of #emph[noun];\-clauses by #he[לֹא]; (as opposed to the regular negation by #he[אֵין];) always includes a certain emphasis, since the force of the negation falls rather upon a particular word (cf. e.g. #bref("Ezek 36:32");), than upon the whole clause. In #bref("2 Sam 3:34"); #he[יָדֶ֫יךָ לֹֽא־אֲסוּרוֹת]; #emph[thy hands]; were #emph[not bound];, a participle is thus specially negatived by #he[לֹא];; cf. #bref("Ps 74:9");, where, however, #he[לֹא]; is separated from the participle by #he[אִתָּ֫נוּ];, and #bref("Job 12:3");. As a rule, noun\-clauses with a pronominal subject are thus negatived by #he[לֹא];, #bref("Gen 20:12");, #bref("Num 35:23"); (#bref("Deut 4:42");, #bref("19:4");); #bref("1 Sam 15:29");, #bref("2 Sam 21:2");, #bref("Jer 4:22");, #bref("Ps 22:7");, #bref("Job 28:14");, parallel with #he[אֵין];; generally with #he[לֹא]; before a substantival predicate, e.g. #bref("Exod 4:10"); #he[לֹא אִישׁ דְּבָרִים אָנֹ֫כִי]; #emph[I am not a man of words];; #bref("Amos 5:18");.—Noun\-clauses with a substantival subject, #bref("Gen 29:7");, #bref("Num 23:19");, #bref("Isa 22:2");, #bref("44:19");, #bref("Hag 1:2");, #bref("Ps 22:3");, #bref("Job 9:32");, #bref("18:17");, #bref("21:9");, #bref("22:16");, #bref("36:26"); (with #he[וְ]; of the apodosis); 41:2; in #bref("Job 9:33"); even #he[לֹא יֵשׁ]; #emph[non est]; is used instead of #he[אֵין];.—In #bref("Prov 18:5"); #he[לֹא]; is used before an adjectival predicate; in #bref("1 Sam 20:26"); (where a preceding noun\-clause is negatived by #he[בִּלְתִּי];) read #he[לֹא טֹהָר]; with the LXX, for #he[לֹא טָהוֹר];. On #he[לֹא]; for #he[אֵין]; in circumstantial clauses to express attributive ideas, see #emph[u]; below.

#ml("e") <p152e> 3. As a rule #he[לֹא]; stands immediately before the verb, but sometimes is separated from it (frequently to bring into special prominence another word which follows it); thus #bref("Job 22:7");, #bref("Eccl 10:10"); before the object and verb; #bref("Num 16:29"); before the subject and verb; #bref("Deut 8:9");, #bref("2 Sam 3:34");, #bref("Ps 49:18");, #bref("103:10");, #bref("Job 13:16");, #bref("34:23"); before a complementary adjunct. In #bref("Deut 32:5"); #he[לֹא]; according to the accentuation even stands at the end of the clause (#emph[they offend him not];); but undoubtedly #he[לֹא בָנָיו]; are to be taken together.—On the position of #he[לֹא]; with the infinitive absolute, see #xref(<p113v>)[§ 113#h(0.18em)#emph[v];];.

#ml("f") <p152f> (#emph[b];) #he[אַל־]; is used like μή and #emph[ne]; to express a #emph[subjective]; and #emph[conditional]; negation, and hence especially in connection with the jussive (#xref(<p109c>)[§ 109#h(0.18em)#emph[c];]; and #emph[e];) to introduce prohibitions, warnings, negative desires, and requests. On #he[אַל־]; with the imperfect, see #xref(<p107p>)[§ 107#h(0.18em)#emph[p];];; with the cohortative, see #xref(<p108c>)[§ 108#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];; on #bref("2 Kgs 6:27");, see #xref(<p109h>)[§ 109#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];.

#ml("g") <p152g> Rem. 1. #he[אַל־]; (like #he[לֹא];, see note on #emph[a]; above) may be used to form a compound word, as in #bref("Prov 12:28"); #he[אַל־מָוֶת]; #emph[not\-death]; (immortality); though all the early versions read #he[אֶל־מָוֶת];. The instances in which #he[אַל]; appears to stand absolutely, equivalent to #emph[no, certainly not]; (like μή for μὴ γένηται), e.g. #bref("Ruth 1:13"); #he[אַל בְּנֹתַי]; #emph[nay, my daughters];, and #bref("Gen 19:18");, #bref("33:10"); (#he[אַל־נָא];), are also due (see under #emph[c];) to extreme shortening of a full clause (in #bref("2 Sam 13:25"); such a clause is repeated immediately #origpage("480") <orig-480>;afterwards); thus in #bref("2 Sam 1:21");, #bref("Isa 62:2");, #bref("Ps 83:2"); #he[יְהִי]; is evidently to be supplied, and in #bref("Joel 2:13");, #bref("Amos 5:14");, #bref("Prov 8:10"); the corresponding jussive from the preceding imperatives, in #bref("Prov 17:12"); from the preceding infinitive absolute.

#ml("h") <p152h> 2. #he[אַל־];, like #he[לֹא];, regularly stands immediately before the verb, but in #bref("Isa 64:8");, #bref("Jer 10:24");, #bref("15:15");, #bref("Ps 6:2");, #bref("38:2"); before another strongly emphasized member of the sentence.#footnote[In #bref("Jer 51:3"); the pointing #he[אֶל־]; occurs twice instead of #he[אַל־];, and is thus, in the opinion of the Masoretes, equivalent to #emph[against]; him that #emph[bendeth];; but undoubtedly we should read #he[אַל־];.];

#ml("i") <p152i> (#emph[c];) #he[אֵין]; construct state (unless it be sometimes merely a contracted connective form, cf. #he[שְׁנֵים]; for #he[שְׁנַ֫יִם]; #xref(<p97d>)[§ 97#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];) of #he[אַ֫יִן]; #emph[non\-existence]; (as also the absolute state, see below) is the negative of #he[יֵשׁ]; #emph[existence];; cf. e.g. #bref("Gen 31:29"); with #bref("Neh 5:5");. As #he[יֵשׁ]; (#emph[he, she, it is, was];, etc.) includes the idea of #emph[being]; in all tenses, so #he[אַ֫יִן];, #he[אֵין]; includes the idea of #emph[not being]; in all tenses. Hence—

#ml("k") <p152k> (1) The absolute state #he[אַ֫יִן];, with an evident transition to the meaning of a verbal predicate, #emph[there does not exist];, always #emph[follows]; the word negatived, e.g. #bref("Isa 37:3"); (#bref("2 Kgs 19:3");) #he[וְכֹחַ אַ֫יִן לְלֵדָה]; #emph[and strength does not exist to bring forth];; #bref("Gen 2:5"); #he[אַ֫יִן]; #emph[was not]; present; #bref("Exod 17:7"); #he[אִם־אָֽיִן]; #emph[or is he not];? after #he[הֲיֵשׁ]; #emph[is he...];? (cf. #bref("Num 13:20");); #bref("Lev 26:37");, #bref("Num 20:5");, #bref("Judg 4:20"); (#he[אָֽיִן]; #emph[no];). In #bref("1 Sam 9:4"); and 10:14 #he[אַ֫יִן]; is used in reference to a plural; #bref("1 Kgs 18:10");, #bref("Isa 41:17");, #bref("45:21");, #bref("59:11");, #bref("Mic 7:2");, #bref("Prov 13:4");, #bref("25:14");, #bref("Job 3:9"); #he[וָאַ֫יִן]; #emph[and let there be none, let none come!]; #bref("Eccl 3:19");.—Cf. finally #he[אִם־אַ֫יִן]; #emph[if it be not so];, #bref("Gen 30:1");, #bref("Exod 32:32");, #bref("Judg 9:15");, #bref("2 Kgs 2:10");.—Quite anomalous is #he[אַ֫יִן]; #bref("Job 35:15"); before a perfect as an emphatic negation; the text, however, can hardly be correct.

#ml("l") <p152l> (2) The construct state #he[אֵין]; stands in its natural position immediately before the substantive whose non\-existence it predicates, or before the subject of the sentence which is to be negatived. To the former class belong also the very numerous instances in which #he[אֵין]; is joined to a participle, e.g. #bref("1 Sam 26:12"); #he[וְאֵין רֹאֶה וְאֵין יוֹדֵעַ וְאֵין מֵקִיץ]; #emph[and there was not one seeing];, etc., i.e. #emph[and no man saw it, nor knew it, neither did any awake];; so especially #he[וְאֵין]; with a participle in subordinate #emph[circumstantial]; or #emph[descriptive clauses];, such as #bref("Isa 5:29"); #he[וְיַפְלִיט וְאֵין מַצִּיל]; #emph[and he shall carry it away];, while there is none delivering, i.e. #emph[without any one’s delivering]; it; #bref("Ps 7:3");, etc.; #bref("Lev 26:6"); etc., #he[וְאֵין מַֽחֲרִיד]; #emph[without any one’s making you afraid];; cf. #xref(<p141e>)[§ 141#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];. #he[אֵין]; is used as the negation of an entire noun\-clause, e.g. in #bref("Gen 39:23");, #bref("Num 14:42"); #he[אֵין יְהוַֹה בְּקִרְבְּכֶם]; #emph[the Lord is not among you];; #bref("Gen 37:29"); #he[אֵֽין־יוֹסֵף בַּבּוֹר]; #emph[Joseph was not in the pit];.

#ml("m") <p152m> (3) When the subject which is to be negatived is a personal pronoun, it is joined as a suffix to #he[אֵין];, according to #xref(<p100o>)[§ 100#h(0.18em)#emph[o];];, e.g. #he[אֵינֶ֫נִּי]; #emph[I am not, was not, shall not be];; #he[אֵֽינְךָ];, fem. #he[אֵינֵךְ];, #emph[thou art not];, etc.; #he[אֵינֶ֫נּוּ];, fem. #he[אֵינֶ֫נָּה]; #emph[he, she is not];, etc.; also absolutely, #bref("Gen 42:13"); #emph[he is]; (5:24 #emph[he was];) #emph[no]; longer #emph[alive];; #he[אֵינָם]; #emph[they are not];, etc. When the accompanying predicate is a verb, it follows again (see #emph[l];) in the form of a participle, since #he[אֵין]; always introduces a noun\-clause, e.g. #bref("Exod 5:10"); #he[אֵינֶ֫נִּי נֹתֵן]; #emph[I will not give];; 8:17, #bref("Deut 1:32");.

#ml("n") <p152n> Rem. In #bref("Neh 4:17"); #he[אֵין אֲנִי]; for #he[אֵינֶ֫נִּי]; is due to its being co\-ordinate with three other (substantival) subjects; these are again expressly summed up in #he[אֵֽין־אֲנַחְנוּ];.—In #bref("Hag 2:17"); #he[אֵין אֶתְכֶם]; the pronominal complement of #he[אֵין]; appears #origpage("481") <orig-481>;to follow with the sign of the accusative;#footnote[According to De Lagarde, #emph[Novae psalterii graeci editionis specimen];, p. 26, #he[יְשׁוּעָ֫תָה]; #bref("Ps 3:3"); is also an accusative after #he[אֵין];.]; but most probably we should read with the LXX #he[שֻֽׁבְכֶם]; for #he[אֶתְכֶם];.

#ml("o") <p152o> (4) The fact that #he[אֵין]; (like #he[אַ֫יִן];) always includes the idea of a verb (#emph[is not, was not];, etc.) led finally to such a predominance of the verbal element, that the original character of #he[אֵין]; as a construct state (but cf. #emph[i]; above) was forgotten, and accordingly it is very frequently separated from its noun (substantive or participle); especially so by the insertion of shorter words (of the nature of enclitics), e.g. #he[בּוֹ]; #bref("Isa 1:6");, #he[לוֹ]; #bref("Lev 11:10");, 12, #he[לָהּ]; #bref("Gen 11:30");, #he[גַּם]; #bref("Ps 14:3");, #he[שָׁם]; #bref("Judg 18:10");, #bref("Exod 12:30");; but cf. also #bref("Ps 5:10");, #bref("6:6");, #bref("32:2");, and #he[אֵין]; used absolutely in #bref("Exod 22:2");, #bref("1 Kgs 8:9");, #bref("Ruth 4:4");.—Hence, finally, even the transposition of #he[אֵין]; and its noun became possible, e.g. #bref("Gen 40:8"); and 41:15 #he[וּפֹתֵר אֵין אֹתוֹ]; #emph[and an interpreter there is not of it];; #bref("Gen 47:13");, #bref("Judg 14:6");, #bref("1 Sam 21:2");, #bref("Isa 1:30");, #bref("Jer 30:13");, #bref("Hab 2:19");, #bref("Prov 5:17"); (#he[וְאֵין];\=#emph[neve sint];; cf. #emph[k]; above, on #bref("Job 3:9");); 30:27.—In #bref("Gen 19:31");, #bref("Exod 5:16"); #he[אֵין]; is placed between the subject and predicate.

#ml("p") <p152p> Rem. 1. Like #he[לֹא... כֹּל]; or #he[כֹּל... לֹא]; (see #emph[b]; above) so also #he[אֵין כֹּל]; expresses an #emph[absolute]; negation, e.g. #bref("Eccl 1:9"); #he[אֵין כָּל־חָדָשׁ]; #emph[there is no new thing];, etc.; #bref("2 Sam 12:3");, #bref("Dan 1:4"); (cf. also #he[אֵין מְא֫וּמָה]; #emph[there is nothing];, #bref("1 Kgs 18:43");, #bref("Eccl 5:13");); as also #he[כָּל־... אֵין]; #bref("Hab 2:19");; cf. #he[מְא֫וּמָה אֵין]; #bref("Judg 14:6");.

#ml("q") <p152q> 2. Undoubtedly akin to #he[אֵין]; in origin is the negative syllable #he[אִי]; occurring in the two compounds #he[אִי כָבוֹד]; (as a proper name, #bref("1 Sam 4:21");; Baer #he[אִֽי־כָבוֹד];) and #he[אִֽי־נָקִי]; #emph[not innocent];, #bref("Job 22:30");; but the proper name #he[אִֽיתָמָר]; is doubtful, and the fem. #he[אִיזֶ֫בֶל]; very doubtful. In Ethiopic this #he[אִי]; is the most common form of negation, prefixed even to verbs.

#ml("r") <p152r> (#emph[d];) #he[טֶ֫רֶם]; #emph[not yet];, when referring to past time is used, as a rule (#xref(<p107c>)[§ 107#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];), with the imperfect, #bref("Gen 2:5"); #he[כֹּל... טֶ֫רֶם]; #emph[none];... #emph[yet];; see #emph[b]; and #emph[p]; above; #bref("Gen 19:4");, #bref("24:45");, #bref("Josh 2:8");, #bref("1 Sam 3:3");; with the imperfect in the sense of a present, #bref("Exod 10:7"); #he[הֲטֶ֫רֶם תֵּדַע]; #emph[knowest thou not yet?]; #bref("Exod 9:30");; but cf. #bref("Gen 24:15");, and #he[בְּטֶ֫רֶם]; with the perfect in #bref("Ps 90:2"); (but see #xref(<p107c>)[§ 107#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];), #bref("Prov 8:25");.

#ml("s") <p152s> (#emph[e];) #he[אֶ֫פֶס]; (prop. a substantive, #emph[cessation];) #emph[no longer];, including the verbal idea of #emph[existing];, cf. #bref("Deut 32:36");, #bref("Isa 45:6");, 14, 46:9; used absolutely, #bref("Amos 6:10"); in the question #he[הַאֶ֫פֶס עוֹד אִישׁ]; #emph[is there none left?]; etc., #bref("2 Sam 9:3");; frequently also in the sense of #emph[non nisi];; with #he[־ִי]; paragogic (#xref(<p90m>)[§ 90#h(0.18em)#emph[m];];) #he[אַפְסִי]; #bref("Isa 47:8");, 10, #bref("Zeph 2:15"); #he[אֲנִי וְאַפְסִי עוֹד]; #emph[I]; am, #emph[and there is none else];.

#ml("t") <p152t> (#emph[f];) #he[בַּל];,#footnote[Evidently from #he[בָּלָה]; #emph[to waste away];, from which stem also #he[בְּלִי]; and #he[בֶּ֫לֶת]; (whence #he[בִּלְתִּי]; #xref(<p90m>)[§ 90#h(0.18em)#emph[m];];), originally substantives, are formed.]; in poetic and prophetic style, and with a certain emphasis,\=#he[לֹא];, is used with the imperfect, e.g. #bref("Isa 26:14");, #bref("33:20");, 23 (immediately afterwards with a perfect); #bref("Hos 7:2");, #bref("Ps 49:13");, #bref("Prov 10:30"); (but #bref("Isa 14:21"); before the jussive,\=#he[אַל־];); before an adjective, #bref("Prov 24:23");; before a preposition, #bref("Ps 16:2");, #bref("Prov 23:7");.

(#emph[g];) #he[בְּלִי]; with a perfect, #bref("Gen 31:20");, #bref("Isa 14:6");; with an imperfect, #bref("Job 41:18");; to negative a participle, #bref("Hos 7:8");, #bref("Ps 19:4");; to negative an adjective, #bref("2 Sam 1:21");.

(#emph[h];) #he[בִּלְתִּי]; to negative an adjective, #bref("1 Sam 20:26");; on #he[בִּלְתִּי]; #bref("Ezek 13:3");, see #emph[x];; on #origpage("482") <orig-482>;#he[לְבִלְתִּי]; as the regular negative with the infinitive construct, see #xref(<p114s>)[§ 114#h(0.18em)#emph[s];];; on #he[לְבִלְתִּי]; as a conjunction, see #emph[x]; below.

On #he[אִם]; as a negative particle in oaths (#emph[verily not];), see #xref(<p149c>)[§ 149#h(0.18em)#emph[c];]; above.

#ml("u") <p152u> Rem. on #he[לֹא];, #he[אֵין];, #he[בְּלִי];. To the category of negative sentences belongs also the expression of negative attributes by means of #he[לֹא];, #he[בְּלִי]; #emph[not]; (both so used almost exclusively in poetic language) or #he[אֵין]; with a following substantive, mostly in the simplest form of circumstantial clause; e.g. #bref("2 Sam 23:4"); #he[בֹּ֫קֶר לֹא עָבוֹת]; #emph[a morning]; when there are #emph[not clouds];, i.e. #emph[a cloudless morning];; cf. #bref("Job 12:24");, #bref("26:2");#super[ #emph[b];];, 38:26 (#he[לֹא־אִישׁ]; #emph[where no man is];, i.e. #emph[uninhabited];); #bref("1 Chr 2:30");, 32 #he[לֹא בָנִים]; #emph[childless];; so also #he[בְּלִי]; e.g. #bref("Job 24:10"); and #he[אֵין]; e.g. #bref("Ps 88:5"); #emph[I am as a man]; #he[אֵֽין־אֱיָל]; #emph[there is not help];, i.e. #emph[like a helpless man];; #bref("Isa 9:6"); #he[אֵֽין־קֵץ]; #emph[endless];; 47:1, #bref("Hos 7:11");; #he[אֵֽין־מִסְפָּר]; #emph[countless];, #bref("Song 6:8");, etc., but usually (#bref("Ps 104:25");, etc.) like a proper circumstantial clause (cf. #xref(<p141e>)[§ 141#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];) connected by #emph[Wāw];, #he[וְאֵֽין־מִסְפָּר];.—Less frequently such periphrases take the form of relative clauses (cf. #xref(<p155e>)[§ 155#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];), e.g. #bref("Job 30:13"); #he[לֹא עֹזֵר לָ֫מוֹ]; they #emph[for whom there is no helper];, i.e. #emph[the helpless]; (but probably #he[עֹזֵר]; is only an intrusion from 29:12, and we should read #he[עֹצֵר]; #emph[without any one’s restraining them];; in 29:12 translate #emph[the fatherless and him that had none to help him];; in #bref("Ps 72:12"); #he[וְאֵֽין־ע׳]; is used in the same sense); #bref("Hab 1:14");; with #he[אֵין]; #bref("Isa 45:9"); #emph[thy work]; is that of a man #emph[who hath no hands];; #bref("Zech 9:11"); #emph[out of the waterless pit];.#footnote[In #bref("Prov 9:13"); (perhaps also #bref("Prov 14:7");; but see Delitzsch on the passage) a verbal\-clause is used co\-ordinately in this way as a periphrasis for an adjective.];

#ml("v") <p152v> How far such compounds finally came to be regarded by the language simply as negative adjectives, may be seen partly from the fact that they (as also relative clauses analogous to the above) are frequently co\-ordinated with real adjectives, #bref("Joel 1:6");, #bref("Ps 72:12");, #bref("Job 29:12");; cf. also #bref("Isa 59:10");, where #he[כְּאֵֽין־עֵינַ֫יִם]; is parallel with #he[כַּֽעִוְרִים];; partly from their being introduced by the sign of the dative #he[לְ];, e.g. #bref("Isa 40:29"); (\= #emph[and to the powerless];); #bref("Job 26:2");#super[ #emph[a];.3];, #bref("Neh 8:10");.

#line(length: 30%, stroke: 0.5pt + muted);

#ml("w") <p152w> (#emph[i];) #he[פֶּן־]; #emph[lest, that not];, at the beginning of a clause expressing a fear or precaution, hence especially after such ideas as #emph[fearing];, #bref("Gen 32:12");, etc. (cf. δείδω μή, #emph[vereor ne];), #emph[taking heed];, frequently after #he[הִשָּׁ֫מֶר];, #he[הִשָּֽׁמְרוּ]; #bref("Gen 24:6");, #bref("31:24");, etc., #emph[taking care];, #bref("2 Kgs 10:23");, etc. Not infrequently the idea on which #he[פֶּן־]; depends, is only virtually contained in the main clause, e.g. #bref("Gen 19:19"); #emph[I cannot escape to the mountain]; (because I am afraid), #he[פֶּן־תִּדְבָּקַנִי הָֽרָעָה]; #emph[lest some evil overtake me];; #bref("Gen 26:9");, #bref("38:11");; also in #bref("Gen 44:34"); from the rhetorical question #emph[how shall I... ?]; we must understand #emph[I cannot];, governing #he[פֶּן];. This is especially the case after an appeal to do or not to do an action by which something may be prevented (in which case #he[פֶּן־]; is simply equivalent to the final #emph[ne];); cf. e.g. #bref("Gen 11:4");, #bref("19:15");, #bref("Num 20:18"); (where #he[פֶּן־]; #emph[lest]; is separated from the verb by a strongly emphasized substantive); #bref("Judg 15:12"); after #emph[swear unto me];; #bref("Prov 24:18");.—In #bref("Gen 3:22"); #emph[and now, lest he put forth his hand];, etc., #he[פֶּן־]; is to be regarded as virtually dependent on a cohortative, which immediately afterwards (verse 23) is changed into an historic tense; cf. also #bref("Gen 26:7");, #bref("31:31");, #bref("42:4"); #bref("Exod 13:17");, #bref("1 Sam 13:19");, #bref("27:11");, #bref("Ps 38:17");, in every case after #he[כִּי אָמַ֫רְתִּי];, #he[כִּי אָמַר];, etc.\=#emph[I thought];, etc., #emph[I must beware lest];, etc.

Rem. According to #xref(<p107q>)[§ 107#h(0.18em)#emph[q];];, #he[פֶּן־]; is naturally followed by the imperfect; for the exceptions, #bref("2 Sam 20:6");, #bref("2 Kgs 2:16");, see #xref(<p107q>)[§ 107#h(0.18em)#emph[q];];, note 3; cf. moreover, #bref("2 Kgs 10:23"); #he[רְאוּ פֶּן־יֶשׁ־פֹּה]; #emph[look lest there be here];, etc. #origpage("483") <orig-483>;

#ml("x") <p152x> (#emph[k];) #he[לְבִלְתִּי]; #emph[that... not];, with the imperfect, #bref("Exod 20:20");, #bref("2 Sam 14:14"); (in #bref("Jer 23:14"); read the infinitive #he[שׁוּב]; for #he[שָׁ֫בוּ];, in 27:8 #he[יָבֹ֫אוּ]; for #he[בֹּ֫אוּ];). In #bref("Ezek 13:3"); #he[בִּלְתִּי רָאוּ]; is a relative clause governed by #he[לְ];\= #emph[according to]; things which #emph[they have not seen];.

#ml("y") <p152y> #strong[2.]; Two negatives in the same sentence do not neutralize each other (as in #emph[nonnulli, non nemo];), but make the negation the more emphatic (like οὐκ οὐδείς, οὐκ οὐδαμῶς, #emph[nulli—non, nemo non];); e.g. #bref("Zeph 2:2"); (if the text is correct) #he[בְּטֶ֫רֶם לֹֽא־יָבוֹא]; #emph[before there shall]; (not) #emph[come];.#footnote[In #bref("1 Kgs 10:21"); #he[אֵין־כֶּ֫סֶף]; goes with what precedes and must be emended, with the LXX and Lucian, to #he[כִּי הַכֶּ֫סֶף];.]; This especially applies to the compounds formed by the union of #he[אֵין]; or #he[בְּלִי]; with #he[מִן־]; #emph[without]; (#xref(<p119y>)[§ 119#h(0.18em)#emph[y];];), e.g. #bref("Isa 5:9"); (6:11) #he[מֵאֵין יוֹשֵׁב]; (for which in #bref("Jer 2:15"); #he[מִבְּלִי יֹשֵׁב];), prop. #emph[without no inhabitant];, i.e. #emph[so that no inhabitant is left there];. On the other hand, in #bref("Isa 50:2"); #he[מֵאֵין מַ֫יִם]; the #he[מִן־]; is causative, #emph[because there is no water];; as also in #bref("Exod 14:11"); #he[הֲמִבְּלִי אֵין־]; #emph[is it because there were no...?]; #bref("2 Kgs 1:3");, 6, 16. In #bref("Eccl 3:11"); #he[מִבְּלִי אֲשֶׁר לֹא]; #emph[except that]; (#emph[yet so that man cannot];, etc.).

#ml("z") <p152z> #strong[3.]; The negative sometimes extends its influence from the first to a second negative sentence parallel with it (which may or may not have #emph[Wāw];); e.g. #bref("1 Sam 2:3"); #emph[talk not so much arrogancy];; #emph[let]; (not) #emph[boasting come out of your mouth];; #bref("Exod 28:43");, #bref("Lev 19:12");, #bref("22:9");, 15 f., #bref("Num 16:14");, #bref("23:19");, #bref("Deut 7:25");, #bref("Isa 23:4");, #bref("28:27");, #bref("38:18");, #bref("47:14");, #bref("Ezek 16:47");, #bref("Ps 9:19");, #bref("13:5");, #bref("35:19");, #bref("38:2");, #bref("44:19");, #bref("75:6");, #bref("Job 28:17"); (so #he[לָ֫מָּה לֹא]; #emph[why... not?]; in #bref("Job 3:11"); also affects the parallel clause).

#sec("153", [Restrictive and Intensive Clauses], note: []) <s153>

The particles #he[אַךְ];, #he[רַק]; #emph[only];, serve to introduce restrictive clauses, and #he[גַּם];, #he[אַף]; #emph[also, besides, even];, intensive clauses. It is to be observed that the force of these particles does not necessarily affect the word which immediately follows (as is the case with #he[אַךְ]; #bref("Gen 7:23");, #bref("34:15");; #he[רַק]; #bref("Gen 6:5");, #bref("Amos 3:2");; even #he[הֲרַק אַךְ]; #emph[hath he indeed only?]; #bref("Num 12:2");; #he[גַּם]; #bref("Gen 27:34");, #bref("Job 7:11");; #he[אַף]; #bref("Deut 15:17");), but very frequently extends to the whole of the following sentence. Thus with #he[אַךְ];, e.g. #bref("Num 14:9");, #bref("1 Kgs 17:13");, #bref("Prov 17:11");, #bref("Job 13:15");, #bref("14:22");, #bref("16:7");, #bref("23:6");; #he[רַק]; #bref("Gen 20:11");, #bref("24:8");, #bref("Ps 32:6");, #bref("Prov 13:10");; #he[גַּם]; #bref("Gen 27:33");, #bref("32:21"); (#he[גַּם הִנֵּה];), 44:10; #bref("1 Sam 22:7");, #bref("28:20");, #bref("Zech 9:11");, #bref("Prov 17:26");, #bref("20:11");; #he[אַף]; #bref("Job 14:3");, #bref("15:4");.—In #bref("Mal 1:10"); and #bref("Job 2:10"); #he[גַּם]; is placed before two co\-ordinate sentences, although, strictly speaking, it applies only to the second. Cf. the analogous examples in #xref(<p150m>)[§ 150#h(0.18em)#emph[m];];. #origpage("484") <orig-484>;

#sec("154", [Sentences connected by Wāw], note: []) <s154>

#ml("a") <p154a> #emph[Wāw copulativum];#footnote[For further particulars of the use of #emph[wāw copulativum];, see Gesenius’ #emph[Thesaurus];, i. 393 ff. On its use in the co\-ordination of similar tenses and moods (e.g. five imperfects consecutive in #bref("Gen 25:34");, five perfects with #he[וְגַם];) as well as of dissimilar tenses and moods, the remarks made in the treatment of the tenses will suffice. With regard to the connection of single #emph[nouns]; by #he[וְ]; (which strictly speaking is always really a contraction of so many clauses into a single sentence) the following observations may be made:— #linebreak(); #linebreak();(#emph[a];) Contrary to English usage, which in lengthy enumerations uses the #emph[and]; to connect only the last member of the series, in Hebrew #emph[polysyndeton]; is customary, as in #bref("Gen 12:16"); wāw copulativum six times, 24:35 seven times, 15:19 ff. nine times, and in #bref("Josh 7:24"); ten times. Sometimes, however, only the last two words are joined (so in a series of three members, #bref("Gen 5:32");, #bref("10:1");, #bref("11:26");, #bref("13:2");, #bref("14:1");, #bref("30:39");, etc.; the last three out of a series of four, #bref("Jer 2:26");); less frequently only the first two, #bref("Ps 45:9");; cf. #xref(<p132d>)[§ 132#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];. The formula #he[תְּמוֹל שִׁלְשׁוֹם]; #emph[yesterday]; (and) #emph[the day before yesterday];, #bref("Exod 5:8");, etc., is always without the copula. On the other hand, the #emph[constructio asyndetos]; in a series of verbs is used as a rhetorical expedient to produce a hurried and so an impassioned description; e.g. #bref("Judg 5:27"); #emph[at her feet he bowed, he fell, he lay];; #bref("Exod 15:9");, #bref("Deut 32:15");, #bref("1 Sam 15:6");, #bref("Jer 4:7");, #bref("Amos 5:21");, #bref("Ps 10:10");, #bref("14:1");, #bref("45:5");, #bref("Job 20:19");, #bref("28:4");, #bref("29:8");, #bref("Song 2:11");, #bref("5:6");, etc. #linebreak(); #linebreak();(#emph[b];) Frequently #emph[wāw copulativum]; is also #emph[explanatory]; (like #emph[isque, et—quidem];, and the German #emph[und zwar];, English #emph[to wit];), and is then called #emph[wāw explicativum];, e.g. #bref("Gen 4:4"); and (i.e. #emph[namely];) #emph[of the fat thereof]; (unless it is simply copulative); #bref("Exod 24:12");, #bref("25:12"); (#emph[to wit two];); 27:14, 28:23, #bref("Judg 17:3"); (in #he[וּמַסֵּכָה];; here as often elsewhere, to introduce an explanatory gloss, cf. #bref("Isa 17:8");, #bref("Ezek 3:15");, and especially P. Haupt, #emph[SBOT.]; Isaiah, p. 90, l. 21 ff.), #bref("1 Sam 17:34"); #emph[and that too with the bear];; #bref("2 Sam 13:20");, #bref("Isa 57:11");, #bref("Jer 17:10");, #bref("Amos 3:11");, #bref("4:10");, Ze 9:9, #bref("Prov 3:12");, #bref("Neh 8:13");, #bref("2 Chr 23:10"); (but in #bref("1 Sam 28:3"); the #he[וּ]; before #he[בְּעִירוֹ]; is to be omitted with the LXX); cf. also such combinations as #he[וְעַד];—#he[מִן]; #emph[from... and even to...];, #bref("Gen 13:3");, #bref("14:23");, #bref("19:4");, 11, etc.—In #bref("1 Sam 6:11"); (see Driver on the passage), #bref("2 Sam 1:23");, etc., #he[ו]; is equivalent to #emph[yea, and];; in #bref("Isa 32:7"); #emph[even];. #linebreak(); #linebreak();#he[וְ]; is used to express emphasis (\=#emph[and especially];), e.g. in #bref("Gen 3:16"); #he[וְהֵֽרֹנֵךְ];; #bref("Isa 2:1");, #bref("Ps 18:1");, perhaps also in #bref("Job 10:17"); #emph[yea];, a whole #emph[host];; #bref("2 Chr 16:14");.—An undoubted example of what is called #emph[wāw concomitantiae]; occurs in #bref("Job 41:12"); #emph[a seething pot]; #he[וְאַגְמֹן]; #emph[with]; burning #emph[rushes];; cf. #bref("Exod 10:10"); (#emph[with your little ones];), 12:8, #bref("Lev 1:12");, #bref("Isa 42:5");. In Arabic this #emph[wāw concom.]; is followed by the accusative. #linebreak(); #linebreak();#he[וְ—וְ]; is used in the sense of #emph[both];—#emph[and]; in #bref("Ps 76:7");, #bref("Dan 1:3");, #bref("8:13");. On #he[וְ—וְ]; as meaning #emph[sive];—#emph[sive];, cf. #xref(<p162b>)[§ 162#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];. #linebreak(); #linebreak();(#emph[c];) See the Lexicon on adverbs used in a copulative sense, such as #he[גַּם]; #emph[also, moreover];, summing up a number, e.g. #he[גַּם־שְׁנַ֫יִם]; #emph[both together];, #bref("Gen 27:45");, #bref("Prov 17:15");; #he[גַּם־כֹּל]; #emph[all together];; as an intensive #emph[and];, e.g. #bref("Gen 30:8");, #bref("37:7");, #bref("1 Sam 30:8");; cf. also such examples as #bref("1 Sam 24:12"); #emph[see, yea see!]; #he[גַּם—גַּם]; or #he[גַּם—וְגַם]; #bref("Gen 24:44");\=#emph[both];—#emph[and];; #he[גַּם]; occurs three times in #bref("Gen 24:25"); and 32:20; also #he[אַף];, which is generally still more intensive, in the sense of #emph[also, in addition to this, even];, and belongs rather to poetry, and to the later language; frequently also equivalent to a mere #emph[and];, but sometimes adversative #emph[but now];, #bref("Ps 44:10");, etc.; and #he[אַף—אַף]; (also three times), equivalent to #emph[both—and];; cf. #he[וְאַף גַּם]; #emph[and even];, #bref("Lev 26:44");; #he[אַף־כִּי]; prop. #emph[add to this also that];, equivalent to #emph[not to mention];, according to the context either #emph[quanto magis]; or #emph[quanto minus];.]; (#he[וְ];) serves to connect two or more sentences, or single words (on its various vocalization, cf. #xref(<p104d>)[§ 104#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];–#emph[g];). Its use, however, is by no means restricted merely to joining sentences which #origpage("485") <orig-485>;are actually co\-ordinate. Frequently the language employs merely the simple connection by #emph[Wāw];, even to introduce an antithesis (#bref("Gen 17:21");, #bref("19:19");, #bref("Lev 2:12");, #bref("Job 6:25");, and very frequently in circumstantial noun\-clauses), or when one of the two clauses is not co\-ordinated, but subordinated to the other. On the use of #he[וְ]; to introduce circumstantial clauses, cf. especially #xref(<p141e>)[§ 141#h(0.18em)#emph[e];]; and #xref(<p142d>)[§ 142#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];; introducing causal clauses, #xref(<p158a>)[§ 158#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];; comparative clauses, #xref(<p161a>)[§ 161#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];; final clauses, #xref(<p165a>)[§ 165#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];; consecutive clauses, #xref(<p166a>)[§ 166#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];. On #emph[wāw apodosis];, cf. #xref(<p143d>)[§ 143#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];, and the sections there cited; on the use of #emph[Wāw]; in numerical sayings, cf. #xref(<p134s>)[§ 134#h(0.18em)#emph[s];];.

#ml("b") <p154b> Rem. Sometimes wāw copulativum joins a sentence apparently to what immediately precedes, but in reality to a sentence which is suppressed and which must, therefore, be supplied from the context. So especially #he[וְ]; with imperatives to express inferences, e.g. #bref("1 Kgs 2:22"); #he[וְשַֽׁאֲלִי]; #emph[ask now rather];; #bref("Ezek 18:32"); #emph[for I have no pleasure in the death of him that dieth];..., #he[וְהָשִׁיבוּ]; #emph[wherefore turn yourselves];. Also at the beginning of a speech in loose connection with an act or speech of another person, e.g. #bref("Exod 2:20");, #bref("2 Sam 18:11");, #bref("24:3");, #bref("2 Kgs 4:14");, 41, 7:13, #bref("2 Chr 25:9");; cf. also #bref("Josh 7:7"); (#he[וְלוּ];), #bref("Ps 2:10");, #bref("4:4");, #bref("Isa 8:7");. Sometimes the suppression of the protasis is due to passionate excitement or haste, which does not allow time for full expression; this is especially illustrated by #bref("Num 12:14");, #bref("20:3"); (#he[וְלוּ];), #bref("1 Sam 10:12");, #bref("15:14");, #bref("22:14");, #bref("28:16");, #bref("2 Sam 18:12");, #bref("24:3");, #bref("1 Kgs 2:22"); (#he[וָלָ֫מָּה];), #bref("2 Kgs 1:10");, #bref("7:19"); (cf. verse 2); #bref("Isa 3:14");, #bref("Zech 2:10");, #bref("Ps 2:6"); (at the same time a circumstantial clause #emph[whereas I\=and yet I have];, etc.); cf. also a new clause beginning with the formula of wishing #he[וּמִי]; #bref("Num 11:29");, #bref("Judg 9:29");; on the disconnected use of #he[וָלֹא]; and #he[וָיֵשׁ]; cf. § 159 #emph[dd];.

#sec("155", [Relative Clauses], note: []) <s155>

#align(center)[See V. Baumann, #emph[Hebräische Relativsätze];, Leipzig, 1894 (cf. the heading of § 138 above); G. Bergsträsser, ‘Das hebr. Präfix #he[שׁ];,’ #emph[ZATW.]; 1909, p. 40 ff.#footnote[In this exhaustive article the author shows that between #he[שׁ]; (on the pronunciation see § 36) and #he[אֲשֶׁר]; there is syntactically no primary difference, but only a secondary distinction which arose in the course of the development of the language, namely that #he[אֲשֶׁר]; is preferred in combinations which are customary in the old literary language, and #he[שׁ]; in those which are derived from the popular language or from Aramaic.];];

#ml("a") <p155a> #strong[1.]; By #xref(<p138a>)[§ 138#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];, #emph[e];, relative clauses are divided into two classes: those which are used for the nearer definition of a noun (substantive or pronoun), and those which are not dependent on a noun. The former may be called #emph[incomplete];, the latter #emph[complete]; relative clauses.

#ml("b") <p155b> Complete relative clauses, as a rule (see the exceptions under #emph[n];), are introduced by the originally demonstrative pronoun #he[אֲשֶׁר];; see further in #xref(<p138e>)[§ 138#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];. Similarly, incomplete relative clauses may also be introduced by #he[אֲשֶׁר];, or by some other demonstrative pronoun; see further in #xref(<p138a>)[§ 138#h(0.18em)#emph[a];]; and #emph[g];–#emph[k];. Very frequently, however, especially #origpage("486") <orig-486>;in poetic style, the attributive relation is expressed by simple co\-ordination.#footnote[The old view that all these cases arise from the #emph[omission]; of #he[אֲשֶׁר]; is incorrect. These co\-ordinated attributive clauses are rather a mere subdivision of the various kinds of circumstantial clauses (see § 156) which may be attached to a #emph[nomen regens];. Cf. in English #emph[this is the letter]; (which) #emph[he wrote to me];.];

#ml("c") <p155c> The governing substantive or pronoun is frequently (in certain cases #emph[always];) resumed by a pronominal suffix or an adverb. The resumption may, however, be omitted, just as in relative clauses introduced by #he[אֲשֶׁר];, etc.; see #xref(<p138f>)[§ 138#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];.

#ml("d") <p155d> In Arabic a distinction is made between relative clauses used for the nearer definition of a #emph[determinate]; substantive (#emph[ṣila];), and those which are attached to an #emph[indeterminate]; substantive (#emph[ṣifa];). The former must be introduced by the demonstrative pronoun #emph[allaḏî];, the latter are always simply co\-ordinated. The same distinction was no doubt originally observed in Hebrew, since simply co\-ordinated relative clauses are most commonly found after #emph[indeterminate]; substantives (see the examples below), and in cases like #bref("Deut 28:49"); (#he[גּוֹי אֲשֶׁר לֹֽא־תִשְׁמַע לְשֹׁנוֹ]; #emph[a nation whose tongue thou shalt not understand];; cf. #bref("Isa 66:13");, and especially #bref("1 Sam 3:11");), the addition of #he[אֲשֶׁר]; is explained from the special stress laid on the indeterminate substantive,#footnote[So Baumann, op. cit., p. 14 f., following Böttcher, #emph[Lehrbuch];, ii. 80.]; #emph[a nation of such a kind, thou understandest not their tongue];. On the other hand, in poetic style at least, #he[אֲשֶׁר]; is somewhat frequently omitted even after a determinate noun, but only rarely in prose (except by the Chronicler; cf. #bref("1 Chr 9:22");, #bref("12:23");, #bref("29:1"); (read prob. #he[אֲשֶׁר]; for #he[אֶחַד];), #bref("2 Chr 15:11");; after #he[כָּל־]; #bref("1 Chr 29:3");, #bref("2 Chr 18:23");, #bref("30:17");, #bref("31:19");, #bref("Ezra 1:5");, but also #bref("Gen 39:4");; for further instances, see Driver, #emph[Introd.];#super[8];, p. 537, no. 30); so #bref("Exod 18:20");, #bref("Judg 8:1");, #bref("20:15");, #bref("1 Kgs 13:12"); (\=which way), so #bref("2 Kgs 3:8");, #bref("2 Chr 18:23");; #bref("Neh 13:23");; after a pronominal subject, #bref("1 Sam 6:9");. In #bref("Jer 52:12"); for #he[עָמַד]; read #he[עֹמֵד]; with the LXX.

#ml("e") <p155e> #strong[2.]; If the nearer definition of a substantive or pronoun is effected by simple co\-ordination of the relative clause, it may take the form—

(#emph[a];) Of a noun\-clause, e.g. #bref("2 Sam 20:21"); #emph[a man of the hill country of Ephraim]; #he[שֶׁ֫בַע שְׁמוֹ]; #emph[whose name was Sheba];; #bref("Zech 6:12");, #bref("Job 1:1");, #bref("3:15"); #emph[with princes]; #he[זָהָב לָהֶם]; #emph[that had gold];; #bref("Ps 11:4");, #bref("Prov 22:11");; when referring to a noun\-suffix, e.g. #bref("Ps 49:14"); #he[זֶה דַרְכָּם כֵּ֫סֶל לָ֫סוֹ]; #emph[this is the way of them who have]; (self\-)#emph[confidence];.—On periphrases of this kind to express negative attributes, as in #bref("Job 38:26"); #he[עַל־אֶ֫רֶץ לֹא־אִישׁ]; #emph[on a land]; where #emph[no man]; is, see #xref(<p152u>)[§ 152#h(0.18em)#emph[u];];, and cf. for this very short form of the relative clause, #bref("Gen 15:13"); #he[בְּאֶ֫רֶץ לֹא לָהֶם]; #emph[in a land]; that belongs #emph[not to them];; #bref("Deut 32:17"); (#he[לַשֵּׁדִים לֹא אֱלֹהַּ];); #bref("Hab 1:6");, #bref("Prov 26:17"); (#he[לֹא־לוֹ];).

#ml("f") <p155f> (#emph[b];) Of a verbal clause.

Here we must distinguish the cases in which the retrospective pronoun—

(1) Is the subject of the relative clause, and is contained in the #origpage("487") <orig-487>;verb; so after a determinate substantive, #bref("Ps 34:9"); #emph[happy is the man]; #he[יֶֽחֱסֶה־בּוֹ]; #emph[that trusteth in him];; #bref("Job 3:3");#super[#emph[b];]; #he[הַלַּ֫יְלָה אָמַ֫ר]; #emph[the night which said];; after #he[כָּל־]; #bref("Ps 71:18");; referring to a vocative, which is determinate in itself even without the article, #bref("Isa 54:1");, or to a noun\-suffix (see under #emph[e];), #bref("Ps 16:4");; after an indeterminate substantive, e.g. #bref("Job 31:12"); #emph[it is a fire]; (that) #emph[devoureth unto Abaddon];; #bref("Deut 32:17");#super[#emph[b];];, #bref("1 Sam 6:9");, #bref("Isa 55:13");, #bref("56:2");, #bref("Ps 68:31");, #bref("78:6");, #bref("Prov 30:17");, #bref("Lam 1:10");, #bref("2 Chr 28:9");; referring to the suffix in #he[הִנְנִי]; #bref("Isa 28:16");, prop. #emph[behold me, who have laid];, etc., but perhaps the participle #he[יֹסֵד]; is to be read; 29:14, 38:5 (but probably again the participle #he[יוֹסֵף]; should be read instead of the imperfect); #bref("Ezek 25:7");. The relative clause is used in this way especially to supply the place of an adjective, e.g. #bref("Gen 49:27"); #he[זְאֵב יִטְרָ֑ף]; #emph[a wolf that ravineth];, i.e. #emph[a ravining wolf];; #bref("Isa 51:12");; to express a negative quality, e.g. #bref("Isa 40:20");, #bref("Hos 4:14"); #he[עָם לֹֽא־יָבִין]; #emph[an undiscerning people];.

#ml("g") <p155g> Rem. Very frequently such relative sentences are attached to substantives which have the particle of comparison #he[כְּ‍];, e.g. #bref("Job 7:2"); #he[כְּעֶ֫בֶד יִשְׁאַף־צֵל]; #emph[as a servant]; that #emph[earnestly desireth the shadow];, etc.; #bref("Deut 32:11");, #bref("Isa 62:1");, #bref("Jer 23:29");, #bref("Hos 6:3");, #bref("Ps 42:2");, #bref("83:15");, #bref("Job 9:26");, #bref("11:16");; so also after #he[כְּמוֹ]; #bref("Ps 58:5");; after a determinate substantive, e.g. #bref("Isa 53:7"); (but the better reading is #he[כְּשֶׂה]; without the article), 61:10 f., #bref("Hab 2:14");, #bref("Ps 49:13");, 21, 125:1; see also the examples under #emph[h];. Sometimes it seems simpler in such cases, to take the verb directly as predicate to the preceding substantive, and to explain #he[כְּ‍]; (for #he[כַּֽאֲשֶׁר];; see #emph[Comparative Clauses];, #xref(<p161b>)[§ 161#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];) as a conjunction—a view which even Hupfeld was ready to accept, at least as regards #bref("Ps 90:5");, #bref("125:1");, #bref("Isa 53:7");, #bref("61:11");, but it can hardly be right.

#ml("h") <p155h> (2) The cases in which the retrospective pronoun represents an accusative of the object, or would do so if not suppressed, as it usually is in such cases in relative clauses with #he[אֲשֶׁר];, cf. #xref(<p138b>)[§ 138#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];. Examples with the retrospective pronoun are, #bref("Deut 32:17"); #he[אֱלֹהִים לֹא יְדָעוּם]; #emph[gods whom they knew not]; (see also the end of the verse); after a substantive with #he[בְּ]; (see above, #emph[g];), #bref("Jer 23:9");, #bref("Job 13:28");. Without a retrospective pronoun, after a determinate substantive, #bref("Judg 8:1");, #bref("Ps 33:12"); (preceded by a relative clause with #he[אֲשֶׁר];); #bref("Job 28:1");. Other examples of this kind, though the article is omitted according to poetic usage, are #bref("Isa 15:7"); (#he[יִתְרָה עָשָׂה];, for which #bref("Jer 48:36"); #he[יִתְרָת עָשָׂה]; with the substantive in the construct state governing the relative clause, see #xref(<p130d>)[§ 130#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];), #bref("Ps 7:16");, #bref("51:10");, #bref("Lam 1:21");.—Without the retrospective pronoun, after an indeterminate substantive, e.g. #bref("Isa 6:6"); #he[רִצְפָּה בְּמֶלְקָחַ֫יִם לָקַח מֵעַל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ]; #emph[a live coal which he had taken with the tongs from off the altar];; #bref("Exod 15:17");, #bref("Isa 42:16"); (48:17, #bref("Ps 25:12");, all after #he[בְּדֶ֫רֶךְ];; but #bref("Ps 32:8"); #he[בְּדֶ֫רֶךְ־זוּ תֵלֵךְ];); #bref("Isa 64:2");; #bref("Eccl 10:5"); (in 6:1 the same clause with #he[אֲשֶׁר];); moreover, in #bref("Jer 14:18"); read with the LXX #he[אֶל־אֶ֫רֶץ לֹא יָדָֽעוּ]; #emph[into a land]; (that) #emph[they know not];.

#ml("i") <p155i> (3) The cases in which the retrospective pronoun is dependent on a preposition, or its place is taken by the adverb #he[שָׁם];, as in #bref("Jer 2:6"); end. #origpage("488") <orig-488>;Thus after a determinate substantive, #bref("Ps 18:3"); #he[צוּרִי אֶֽחֱסֶה־בּוֹ]; #emph[my rock in which I take refuge];; #bref("Exod 18:20");, #bref("Isa 42:1");; in #bref("Job 3:3");#super[#emph[a];]; also, the omission of the article with #he[יוֹם]; is only a poetic licence. After an indeterminate substantive, #bref("Jer 2:6");, last clause but one; #bref("Ps 32:2");.

#ml("k") <p155k> In this case also the retrospective word is not infrequently suppressed, giving rise to extremely short, bold expressions, such as #bref("Isa 51:1"); #emph[look unto the rock]; #he[חֻצַּבְתֶּם]; (whence) #emph[ye were hewn, and to the hole of the pit]; #he[נֻקַּרְתֶּם]; (whence) #emph[ye were digged];; #bref("Job 21:27"); #emph[the devices]; (where\-with) #emph[ye act violently against me];.—A retrospective adverb is suppressed in #bref("Job 38:19"); #emph[where is the way]; (to the place where) #emph[the light dwelleth?]; cf. 38:24.

#ml("l") <p155l> Rem. 1. The omission of the retrospective word occurs most frequently in relative clauses which are governed by the construct state of a preceding substantive (especially an expression of time) and hence are virtually in the genitive. In addition to the instances already given in #xref(<p130d>)[§ 130#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];, cf. the following: after #he[בְּיוֹם]; #bref("Lev 7:35");, #bref("Ps 56:10");; after #he[מִיּוֹם]; #bref("Jer 36:2");; after simple #he[יוֹם]; #bref("Ps 56:4"); (#he[יוֹם אִירָא]; #emph[on the day]; when #emph[I am afraid];); after #he[בְּעֵת]; #bref("2 Chr 29:27"); (#he[בְּעֵת הֵחֵל הָֽעוֹלָה]; #emph[at the time]; when #emph[the burnt offering began];); 20:22, 24:11, #bref("Job 6:17");; after #he[לְעֵת]; #bref("Deut 32:35");; after #he[עַד־עֵת]; #bref("Mic 5:2");; after #he[מֵעֵת]; #bref("Ps 4:8"); #emph[thou hast put gladness in my heart more than]; (their gladness) #emph[at the time]; (when) #emph[their corn and their wine are increased];.

#ml("m") <p155m> 2. The agreement (#xref(<p138d>)[§ 138#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];) of the retrospective pronoun with a pronominal regens in the 1st or 2nd person also takes place in a simple co\-ordinated relative clause in #bref("1 Sam 26:14"); #emph[who art thou]; (that) #emph[criest?]; Cf., however, #bref("Isa 63:19"); #emph[we are become]; as they #emph[over whom]; (#he[בָּם]; not #he[בָּ֫נוּ];) #emph[thou no longer bearest rule];.

#ml("n") <p155n> #strong[3.]; Occasionally—chiefly in poetic or otherwise elevated style—even #emph[independent]; relative clauses are simply co\-ordinated with a regens, whereas we should expect them always to be preceded by a demonstrative pronoun, on the analogy of the examples in #xref(<p138e>)[§ 138#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];. The suppressed pronoun would stand—

(#emph[a];) As subject, #bref("Isa 41:24"); #emph[an abomination]; (is he) #emph[that chooseth you]; (but read perhaps #he[לִבְחֹר];); #bref("Job 30:13");, cf. #xref(<p152u>)[§ 152#h(0.18em)#emph[u];];.

(#emph[b];) As object, #bref("Isa 41:2");, with a retrospective pronoun; #bref("Mal 2:16"); #he[וְכִסָּה]; #emph[and]; him #emph[that covereth]; (or read #he[וְכֹסֶה];?); #bref("Job 29:12"); #emph[I delivered... the fatherless also, and]; him #emph[that had none to help him];.

(#emph[c];) In the genitive governed by a substantive (cf. #xref(<p130d>)[§ 130#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];), #bref("Exod 4:13"); #he[שְׁלַח־נָא בְּיַד־תִּשְׁלָֽח]; #emph[send, I pray thee, by the hand of]; him #emph[whom thou wilt send];, i.e. by the hand of some one else; #bref("Ps 65:5"); and #bref("Prov 8:32");, verbal\-clauses after #he[אַשְׁרֵי]; #emph[O the happiness of]; the man, etc.; #bref("Ps 81:6");, #bref("141:9");, #bref("Job 29:16");, #bref("Lam 1:14");; after #he[כָּל־]; #bref("Gen 39:4");, but we must certainly read here, with the Samaritan and LXX, #he[כָּל־אֲשֶׁר יֶשׁ־לוֹ]; as in verses 5 and 8; #bref("Exod 9:4");; verbal\-clauses after #he[כָּל־]; #bref("1 Chr 29:3");, #bref("2 Chr 30:19");, #bref("31:19");, #bref("Ezra 1:5");.

(#emph[d];) Governed by a preposition; so verbal\-clauses after #he[אַֽחֲרֵי]; #bref("Jer 2:8");; after #he[אֶל־]; (\=#emph[to the place where];), #bref("1 Chr 15:12");, but #bref("Exod 23:20"); before the same #origpage("489") <orig-489>;verb #he[אֶל־הַמָּקוֹם אֲשֶׁר];; after #he[בְּ]; #bref("Jer 2:11");, #bref("2 Chr 1:4"); (#he[בַּֽהֵכִין];\=#he[בְּהַֽה׳];\=#emph[to the place where];); after #he[לְ]; #bref("Isa 65:1"); #he[לְלוֹא שָׁאָ֫לוּ]; by them #emph[that asked not]; for me... #he[לְלֹא בִקְשֻׁ֫נִי]; them #emph[that sought me not];; #bref("Ezek 13:3"); that which #emph[they have not seen];, but the text is hardly correct; after #he[עַל]; #bref("Ps 119:136");, cf. #xref(<p158b>)[§ 158#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];; after #he[עִם]; #bref("2 Chr 16:9");.—A noun\-clause follows #he[לְ]; in #bref("Neh 8:10");. An analogous instance in Aramaic is #bref("Ezra 5:14"); #emph[to]; one #emph[whose name was Sheshbazzar]; \[so in the papyri, see the Lexicon, p. 1116#super[a];\].

#sec("156", [Circumstantial Clauses], note: []) <s156>

#ml("a") <p156a> #strong[1.]; The statement of the particular circumstances under which a subject appears as performing some action, or under which an action (or an occurrence) is accomplished, is made especially (apart from relative clauses, see § 155) by means of noun\-clauses connected by #emph[Wāw]; with a following subject (see further on this kind of #emph[circumstantial clause]; in #xref(<p141e>)[§ 141#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];), and by verbal\-clauses (see #xref(<p142d>)[§ 142#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];). Very frequently, however, such statements of the particular circumstances are subordinated to the main clause by being simply attached, without #emph[Wāw];, either as noun\-clauses, sometimes extremely short (see #emph[c];), or as verbal\-clauses (see #emph[d];–#emph[g];).

#ml("b") <p156b> Rem. Among relative clauses of this kind the commonest are the various noun\-clauses, which are most closely subordinated to a preceding substantive without #he[אֲשֶׁר];, e.g. #bref("Gen 16:12");; also statements of weight, #bref("Gen 24:22");; of name, #bref("Job 1:1"); (also introduced by #he[וּשְׁמוֹ]; #bref("Gen 24:29");, #bref("1 Sam 1:1");, etc., or #he[וּשְׁמָהּ]; #bref("Gen 16:1");, #bref("22:24");, etc.); of a condition of body, #bref("Judg 1:7");, and others.—Noun\-clauses which begin with #emph[wāw]; and the #emph[predicate]; have a somewhat more independent character than those introduced by #emph[wāw]; and the #emph[subject];#footnote[In #bref("Deut 32:31"); this form of sequence appears to be selected for another purpose, #emph[and indeed our enemies are judges]; thereof, with #emph[wāw emphatic];; to take it as a circumstantial clause is too artificial.]; (#bref("Gen 19:1");, etc.). The former, however, are also to be regarded as circumstantial clauses, in so far as they describe a #emph[state]; which is simultaneous with the principal action; thus #bref("Isa 3:7"); #emph[I will not be an healer];, #he[וּבְבֵיתִי אֵין לֶ֫חֶם]; #emph[while in my house is neither bread nor clothing];; #bref("Isa 6:6"); (#bref("Amos 7:7");); #bref("2 Sam 13:18");, #bref("16:1");. Cf. also the instances in #xref(<p152l>)[§ 152#h(0.18em)#emph[l];]; of #he[וְאֵין]; followed by a participle, as #he[וְאֵין מַצִּיל];, etc.

#ml("c") <p156c> #strong[2.]; Characteristic examples of circumstantial #emph[noun];\-clauses are #bref("Gen 12:8"); #emph[and pitched his tent]; #he[בֵּֽית־אֵל מִיָּם וְהָעַי מִקֶּ֫דֶם]; with #emph[Bethel on the west and Ai on the east];; #bref("Num 22:24");, #bref("2 Sam 18:14"); #emph[through the heart of Absalom];, #he[עוֹדֶ֫נּוּ חַי]; while #emph[he]; was #emph[yet alive];; #bref("Jer 30:6");, #bref("Ezek 9:2"); (cf. #bref("Song 3:8");), #bref("Nah 3:8");, #bref("Zech 14:5");, #bref("2 Chr 23:10");; with the predicate preceding, e.g. #bref("1 Sam 26:13");, #bref("Ps 32:8");.—In #bref("Gen 41:29"); a noun\-clause serves to announce a state in the future.—We may also include here certain set phrases, as #he[פָּנִים אֶל־פָּנִים]; #emph[face to face]; (prop. while #emph[face]; was turned #emph[towards face];), #bref("Gen 32:31");, #bref("Exod 33:11");, #bref("Deut 34:10");, #origpage("490") <orig-490>;etc.;#footnote[The expression #he[הִתְרָאָה פָנִים]; #emph[to look one another in the face]; (i.e. to contend in combat) #bref("2 Kgs 14:8");, 11, #bref("2 Chr 25:17");, 21, is probably only a shortened form for #he[הִתְרָאָה פָנִים אֶל־פָּנִים];.]; so also #emph[to cast oneself down];, #he[אַפַּ֫יִם אָֽרְצָה]; #emph[the face]; being turned #emph[to the earth];, #bref("Gen 19:1");, etc. (for #he[אָֽרְצָה]; we find #he[אֶ֫רֶץ]; in #bref("1 Kgs 1:31");, #bref("Isa 49:23");).#footnote[That #he[אָֽרְצָה (אֶ֫רֶץ)]; is really to be regarded as a virtual predicate to #he[אַפַּ֫יִם];, and not #he[אַפַּ֫יִם]; as a #emph[casus instrumenti];, is seen from #bref("Isa 49:23");, where #he[אַפַּ֫יִם אֶ֫רֶץ]; precedes the verb.];—Cf. finally the formula #he[אֵם עַל־בָּנִים]; #emph[mother with children];, #bref("Gen 32:12");; cf. #bref("Hos 10:14"); and #xref(<p119aa>)[§ 119#h(0.18em)#emph[aa];]; note 2.

Rem. On circumlocutions of this kind to express negative attributes by means of short noun\-clauses (complete or incomplete), cf. #xref(<p152u>)[§ 152#h(0.18em)#emph[u];];.

#ml("d") <p156d> #strong[3.]; As circumstantial #emph[verbal];\-clauses,#footnote[Some examples of these have been already discussed in another connection above, #xref(<p120a>)[§ 120#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];–#emph[c];.]; we find (1) sometimes affirmative clauses (see below), but far more frequently (2) negative clauses (see #emph[f];), and among these (3) a certain number of expressions which may be regarded simply as equivalent to negative adverbial ideas (see #emph[g];).

Examples of (1) #bref("Isa 5:11");#super[ #emph[b];]; woe unto them, that tarry late in the evening, #he[יַ֫יִן יַדְלִיקֵם]; #emph[while wine inflames them];; #bref("Isa 1:5");, #bref("10:24");, #bref("30:31");, #bref("Jer 7:26");, #bref("20:15");, #bref("Ps 4:3");, #bref("5:12");, #bref("21:13");, #bref("62:5");. The circumstantial verbal\-clause is used to particularize an action which has before been expressed generally, in #bref("Gen 44:12");, #bref("48:14");\=#emph[crossing his hands];; #bref("Deut 2:27");, #bref("Judg 6:19");; antithetically, #bref("1 Kgs 13:18"); #he[כִּחֵשׁ לוֹ]; #emph[wherewith however he lied unto him];. The verbal\-clause seems to assign a reason in #bref("Ps 7:7"); #he[מִשְׁפָּט צִוִּ֫יתָ]; #emph[since thou hast commanded judgement];; a consequence in #bref("Ps 103:5");.#footnote[In #bref("Gen 21:14"); the circumstantial verbal\-clause #he[שָׂם עַל־שִׁכְמָהּ]; is only due to a harmonizing transposition; read #he[וְאֶת־הַיֶּ֫לֶד שׂ׳ ע׳ שׁ׳];. According to the source used in cap. 21 Ishmael was still a young child; according to 17:25 he was about 16 or 17 years old.];

#ml("e") <p156e> Rem. On the cases in which an imperfect in the sense of a final clause is subordinated to a verb of motion (generally #he[קוּם];), see #xref(<p120c>)[§ 120#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];.

#ml("f") <p156f> Of (2), subordinate verbal\-clauses with #he[לֹא]; (in English usually rendered by #emph[without]; and the gerund, if the subject be the same as in the principal clause), e.g. #bref("Lev 1:17"); #he[לֹא יַבְדִּיל]; #emph[without dividing]; it #emph[asunder];; #bref("Job 31:34");; #he[לֹא]; with the perfect is so used in #bref("Gen 44:4");, #bref("Exod 34:28");, #bref("1 Sam 30:2");, #bref("Job 20:26"); (#emph[without its being blown upon it];). With a different subject, equivalent to a consecutive clause in English, #bref("Isa 27:9"); #he[לֹֽא־יָקֻ֫מוּ]; #emph[so that they shall rise up no more];.—Moreover, verbal\-clauses in the same sense (#emph[without]; doing, etc.) are frequently connected by #he[וְלֹא];; cf. #bref("1 Sam 20:2");, #bref("Job 24:22");, #bref("42:3");; in a concessive sense, #bref("Isa 33:1");, #bref("Ps 44:18");.

#ml("g") <p156g> Of (3), cf. #he[לֹא יֵדַע]; (prop. #emph[he knows it not];) #emph[unawares];, #bref("Ps 35:8");, #bref("Prov 5:6"); #he[לֹא יַחְמֹל]; #emph[unsparingly];, #bref("Isa 30:14"); (after an infinitive absolute); #bref("Hab 1:17");, #bref("Job 6:10"); (but #he[וְלֹא יַחְמֹל]; #bref("Job 16:13");, #bref("27:22");; see #emph[f]; at the end); #he[לֹא כִחֵ֑דוּ]; (prop. #emph[they hide not];) #emph[openly];, #bref("Isa 3:9"); (but #origpage("491") <orig-491>;#bref("Job 15:18"); #he[וְלֹא כִחֲדוּ];); #he[בְּלִי חָשָׂ֑ךְ]; (prop. #emph[he restrains not];) #emph[unceasingly];, #bref("Isa 14:6");; #he[בַּל־יִמּוֹט]; #bref("Job 41:15"); (#bref("Ps 93:1"); #he[בַּל־תִּמּוֹט];) and #he[לֹא יִמּוֹט]; #bref("Isa 40:20"); (#emph[without tottering];) #emph[immovably];; cf. also #he[לֹא אֶמְעָ֑ד]; #emph[without wavering];, #bref("Ps 26:1");.

#sec("157", [Object\-Clauses (Oratio Obliqua)], note: []) <s157>

#ml("a") <p157a> Clauses which depend on a transitive verb, especially on what are called #emph[verba cordis];, i.e. verbs denoting any mental act, such as #emph[to see, to hear, to know, to perceive, to believe, to remember, to forget, to say, to think];, etc., may be subordinated to the governing verb without the help of a conjunction by simple juxtaposition (#xref(<p120a>)[§ 120#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];), or they may be co\-ordinated with it either with or without #emph[wāw copulative]; (#xref(<p120d>)[§ 120#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];–#emph[h];). As a rule, however, the objective clause is introduced by the conjunction #he[כִּי]; #emph[that];, less frequently by #he[אֲשֶׁר]; #emph[that];.#footnote[On these clauses with #he[כִּי]; and #he[אֲשֶׁר]; and generally on clauses which we should render as subordinate, cf. P. Dörwald ‘Zur hebr. Syntax’ in #emph[Neue Jahrbb. für Philol. und Pädag.]; 1890, p. 115 ff.];

Examples:—

(#emph[a];) Object\-clauses without a conjunction. Besides the passages mentioned in § 120 (especially under #emph[e];) there are a number of examples, in which a clause depending on a #emph[verbum dicendi]; or #emph[sentiendi]; (the #emph[oratio obliqua]; of the Latin and English Grammar) is added in the form of an independent noun\-clause or verbal\-clause; e.g. #bref("Gen 12:13"); #he[אִמְרִי־נָא אֲחֹ֫תִי אָ֑תְּ]; #emph[say, I pray thee, thou]; art #emph[my sister];; #bref("Ps 10:13");, #bref("Job 25:3");#super[#emph[a];.14];, #bref("Neh 6:6");; #bref("Zech 8:23"); (after #he[שָׁמַע];); #bref("Ps 9:21"); (after #he[יָדַע];); verbal\-clauses, e.g. #bref("Ps 50:21"); #emph[thou thoughtest]; #he[הֱיֽוֹת־אֶהְיֶה כָמ֫וֹךָ]; #emph[I was surely like thyself]; \[but read #he[הָיוֹ]; for #he[הֱיוֹת];\]; #bref("Gen 41:15");, #bref("Judg 9:48"); #emph[what ye have seen me do];; #bref("Isa 48:8");, #bref("Hos 7:2");.

#ml("b") <p157b> (#emph[b];) Object\-clauses introduced by #he[כִּי];, e.g. #bref("Gen 6:5"); #he[וַיַּרְא יְהֹוָה כִּי רַבָּה רָעַת הָֽאָדָם]; #emph[and the Lord saw that the wickedness of man was great];, etc.—Direct narration also is very frequently introduced by #he[כִּי]; (analogous to the ὅτι #emph[recitativum];; frequently, indeed, with the secondary idea of a particle of asseveration, as in #bref("Gen 26:9");, #bref("27:20");), e.g. #bref("Gen 21:30");, #bref("22:16"); f., 26:22, 29:32, 37:35, #bref("Josh 2:24");, etc., even when the direct narration is not expressly indicated, #bref("Gen 4:25");, #bref("32:31");, #bref("41:51"); f., #bref("Exod 18:4");.—On the expression of a second object by means of a clause introduced by #he[כִּי];, see #xref(<p117h>)[§ 117#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];.#footnote[Instead of a complete objective clause we sometimes find a kind of accusative and infinitive construction, especially after #he[נָתַן]; (prop. #emph[to give up];) in the sense of #emph[to allow];, e.g. #bref("Num 21:23"); #he[וְלֹֽא־נָתַן סִיחֹן אֶת־יִשְׂרָאֵל עֲבֹר בִּגְבֻלוֹ]; #emph[and Sihon did not suffer Israel to pass through his border];; 20:21; followed by an infinitive with #he[לְ];, e.g. #bref("Gen 20:6");, #bref("31:7");, #bref("Exod 3:19");.—Cf. also the analogous examples in #bref("Deut 28:56"); (after #he[נִסָּה]; #emph[to venture];; see #xref(<p113d>)[§ 113#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];); #bref("Judg 11:20"); (after #he[הָֽאֱמִין]; #emph[to trust];); #bref("1 Kgs 19:4"); (after #he[שָׁאַל]; #emph[to request];).];

#ml("c") <p157c> (#emph[c];) Object\-clauses introduced by #he[אֲשֶׁר];, e.g. #bref("Esth 3:4"); #he[כִּֽי־הִגִּיד לָהֶם אֲשֶׁר־הוּא יְהוּדִי]; #emph[for he had told them that he was a Jew];; #bref("1 Sam 18:15");, #bref("Ezek 20:26");, #bref("Eccl 8:12");,#footnote[In #bref("Jer 28:9"); a #emph[subject];\-clause is thus introduced by #he[אֲשֶׁר]; instead of the usual #he[כִּי];.]; even before direct narration, #bref("1 Sam 15:20");, #bref("2 Sam 1:4");. Somewhat frequently #he[אֲשֶׁר]; is preceded by #origpage("492") <orig-492>;the #emph[nota accusativi]; #he[אֶת־]; (equivalent to #emph[the circumstance, the fact, that];), e.g. #bref("Josh 2:10");, #bref("1 Sam 24:11");, 19, #bref("2 Sam 11:20");, #bref("Isa 38:3");, but in #bref("Gen 30:29");, #bref("Deut 29:15"); equivalent to #emph[the way in which];.

#sec("158", [Causal Clauses], note: []) <s158>

#ml("a") <p158a> A complete clause, assigning the reason for statements, demands, threats, etc., sometimes follows with the simple #emph[wāw copulative];, e.g. #bref("Ps 60:13"); #emph[give us help against the adversary, and]; (for) #emph[vain is the help of man];; #bref("Gen 6:17"); (#he[וַֽאֲנִי];), 22:12, #bref("Exod 23:9");, #bref("Job 22:12");, perhaps also #bref("Ps 7:10");; or even without #emph[Wāw];, e.g. #bref("Gen 17:14");. As a rule, however, special conjunctions in various combinations are used to introduce causal clauses.

#ml("b") <p158b> The most common causal conjunctions are #he[יַ֫עַן כִּי]; #bref("Isa 3:16");, etc., and #he[יַ֫עַן אֲשֶׁר]; #emph[because];, prop. #emph[on account of the fact that];; both, however, may also be shortened to the simple #he[יַ֫עַן]; #bref("Num 20:12");, etc., or to #he[כִּי]; #emph[because];, #bref("Gen 3:14");, 17, etc., or to #he[אֲשֶׁר]; #bref("Gen 30:18");, #bref("31:49");, #bref("34:13");, 27, #bref("1 Sam 15:15");, #bref("20:42");, #bref("26:16");, 23, #bref("1 Kgs 3:19");, #bref("8:33");, #bref("Hos 14:4");, #bref("Zech 1:15");; also #he[בַּֽאֲשֶׁר]; #bref("Gen 39:9");, 23. On the other hand, the simple #he[יַ֫עַן]; is sometimes repeated for emphasis, #he[יַ֫עַן וּבְיַ֫עַן]; (something like the German #emph[sintemal und alldieweil];) #bref("Lev 26:43");, #bref("Ezek 13:10"); (without #he[וּ]; 36:3); also #he[עַל־אֲשֶׁר]; #bref("2 Sam 3:30");, and #he[עַל־כִּי];#footnote[Also #he[כִּֽי־עַל־כֵּן]; prop. #emph[for therefore];, #bref("Gen 18:5");, #bref("19:8");, #bref("33:10");, #bref("38:26");, #bref("Num 10:31");, #bref("14:43");, #bref("2 Sam 18:20"); #emph[Q#super[e];rê];, and #he[אֲשֶׁר עַל־כֵּן]; #bref("Job 34:27");, always mean #emph[forasmuch as];.]; #bref("Deut 31:17");, #bref("Judg 3:12");, #bref("Mal 2:14"); #emph[on the ground that];; #he[עַל־דְּבַר אֲשֶׁר]; #emph[because of the circumstance that];, #bref("Deut 23:5");; #he[עַל־כָּל־אֹדוֹת אֲשֶׁר]; #emph[for this very cause that];, #bref("Jer 3:8");. But just as the simple #he[יַ֫עַן]; is used for #he[יַ֫עַן אֲשֶׁר];, so also the simple #he[עַל־]; with the perfect stands for #he[עַל־אֲשֶׁר]; #bref("Ps 119:136");, #bref("Ezra 3:11");; cf. #he[עַל־בְּלִי]; #bref("Gen 31:20"); and #he[מִבְּלִי]; #bref("Deut 28:55"); both with the perfect, equivalent to #emph[because... not];.—Cf. further #he[עֵ֫קֶב אֲשֶׁר]; #bref("Gen 22:18");, #bref("26:5");, #bref("2 Sam 12:6");, all with the perfect, and #he[עֵ֫קֶב כִּי]; (#bref("2 Sam 12:10"); with the perfect; #bref("Amos 4:12"); with the imperfect) prop. #emph[in return]; for the fact #emph[that];; similarly again the simple #he[עֵ֫קֶב]; #bref("Num 14:24"); with the perfect, and #bref("Deut 7:12");, #bref("8:20"); with the imperfect; finally, #he[מֵֽאֲשֶׁר]; #bref("Isa 43:4"); #emph[arising from]; the fact #emph[that,\= because];; #he[תַּ֫חַת אֲשֶׁר]; #bref("1 Sam 26:21");, etc., and #he[תַּ֫חַת כִּי]; #bref("Deut 4:37");, #bref("Prov 1:29"); #emph[for the reason that];.

#ml("c") <p158c> Rem. 1. The preposition #he[עַל־]; (#emph[because of, on account of];) with the infinitive (#xref(<p114e>)[§ 114#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];) is frequently used as the equivalent of a full causal clause; cf. e.g. #bref("Amos 1:3");, 6, 13, 2:1, 6. Such a construction with the infinitive may, however, according to #xref(<p114r>)[§ 114#h(0.18em)#emph[r];];, be continued by means of a finite verb, in which case #he[עַל־]; governs the verb as a conjunction; e.g. #bref("Amos 1:9"); #he[עַל־הַסְגִּירָם... וְלֹא זָֽכְרוּ]; #emph[because they delivered up... and remembered not];, etc.; 1:11, 2:4; without #emph[Wāw];, #bref("Isa 30:14");.

#ml("d") <p158d> 2. The choice of tense is regulated by the general principles stated in § 106 ff., namely the perfect (cf. especially #xref(<p106f>)[§ 106#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];) refers to causes already brought fully into effect, the imperfect to those which may contingently arise; cf. e.g. #bref("Deut 7:12");, #bref("8:20");, #bref("1 Kgs 8:33");, where the imperfect leaves the possibility still open that the persons addressed will perhaps escape the threatened punishments by avoiding disobedience.—Cf. further, #xref(<p111h>)[§ 111#h(0.18em)#emph[h];]; on the imperfect consecutive, and #xref(<p112nn>)[§ 112#h(0.18em)#emph[nn];]; on the perfect consecutive in the apodosis to causal clauses. #origpage("493") <orig-493>;

#sec("159", [Conditional Sentences], note: []) <s159>

Cf. H. Ferguson, ‘The Use of the Tenses in Conditional Sentences in Hebrew’ (#emph[Journal of the Society of Bibl. Lit. and Exeg.];, Middletown, Conn., June and September, 1882).—P. Friedrich, #emph[Die hebr. Conditionalsätze];, Königsberg, 1884 (Inaug.\-Diss.).—Driver, #emph[Use of the Tenses];, 3rd ed., p. 174 ff.

#ml("a") <p159a> #strong[1.]; The great variety of construction in conditional sentences is owing to the fact that it frequently depends on the subjective judgement of the speaker, whether he wishes a condition to be regarded as #emph[capable of fulfilment]; (absolutely, or at least possibly), thus including those already fulfilled, or as #emph[incapable of fulfilment];. On this distinction depends the choice both of the conditional particle to be used (see below), and especially (as also in Greek and Latin) of the tense. The use of the latter is naturally determined according to the general principles laid down in § 106 ff.#footnote[It may, moreover, happen that a different idea is introduced in the apodosis, from that with which the protasis started—a source of many further variations.]; In the following sketch, for the sake of clearness, conditional sentences #emph[without]; conditional particles will be first discussed (under #emph[b];), and afterwards sentences #emph[with]; these particles (under #emph[l];).

#ml("b") <p159b> #strong[2.]; The relation between condition and consequence may be expressed, as in English, by the simple juxtaposition of two clauses. At the same time, it is to be observed in general as a fundamental rule (in accordance with the original character of the two tenses), that the #emph[imperfect];, with its equivalents (the jussive, cohortative, imperative, perfect consecutive, and participle), is used to express a condition and consequence which are regarded as being #emph[capable of fulfilment]; in present or future time, while the #emph[perfect]; represents a condition already fulfilled in the past, and its consequence as an accomplished fact. The other use of the perfect—to represent conditions regarded as #emph[impossible];—occurs only in connection with particles.

Examples:—

#ml("c") <p159c> (#emph[a];) #emph[Imperfect]; (cf. #xref(<p107x>)[§ 107#h(0.18em)#emph[x];];) in protasis and apodosis, #bref("Josh 22:18");, #bref("Ps 104:28"); ff. #he[תִּתֵּן לָהֶם יִלְקֹט֑וּן];#footnote[On the termination #he[\-וּן]; cf. #xref(<p47m>)[§ 47#h(0.18em)#emph[m];];. In verse 28 #emph[b]; also #he[יִשְׂבְּעוּן]; is probably to be explained from its immediately preceding the greater #emph[pause];. These terminations in verses 28–30 and #bref("Ps 139:18"); can scarcely have any connection with the conditional sentence, although it is strange that #he[\-וּן]; in #bref("Num 32:23"); appears after #he[אִם־לֹא]; in the protasis. In #bref("Num 16:29");, #bref("32:20"); #he[\-וּן]; as before #he[א]; (as in #bref("Job 31:10"); in the apodosis) is to be explained from the dislike of hiatus.]; (if) #emph[thou givest unto them, they gather];, etc.; #bref("Ps 139:18");, #bref("Prov 12:17");, #bref("Job 20:24");, #bref("Eccl 1:18");, #bref("Neh 1:8");; with an interrogative imperfect in the apodosis, #bref("Judg 13:12");; with the jussive, #bref("Job 10:16");; with the cohortative, Pr. 1:23; with the perfect, #bref("Isa 26:10"); (#emph[yet will he not learn righteousness];; the apodosis forcibly denies #origpage("494") <orig-494>;what the imperfect in the protasis had represented as still conceivable; cf. #bref("Hos 8:12");); with the perfect consecutive, #bref("Gen 47:25");, #bref("Exod 33:5");; with the protasis suppressed, #bref("Job 5:8"); (see #xref(<p107x>)[§ 107#h(0.18em)#emph[x];];).

#ml("d") <p159d> (#emph[b];) #emph[Jussive]; in protasis (cf. #xref(<p109h>)[§ 109#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];, #emph[i];) and apodosis, #bref("Ps 104:10"); #he[תָּֽשֶׁת־חֹ֫שֶׁךְ וִיהִי לָ֑יְלָה]; (if) #emph[thou makest darkness, it is night];; imperfect in the apodosis, #bref("Ps 104:29");#super[ #emph[b];];; cohortative #bref("Prov 1:23");. Also in #bref("Exod 7:9"); #he[יְהִי לְתַנִּין]; #emph[it shall become a serpent];, is the apodosis to a suppressed protasis #emph[if thou cast it down];; so in #bref("2 Kgs 5:10"); #he[וְיָשֹׁב]; is the apodosis to a protasis #emph[if thou wash];, contained in what precedes.

#ml("e") <p159e> (#emph[c];) #emph[Cohortative]; (see #xref(<p108e>)[§ 108#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];) in the protasis; perfect in the apodosis, #bref("Ps 40:6");; imperfect consecutive, #bref("Job 19:18"); #he[אָק֫וּמָה וַיְדַבְּרוּ־בִי]; (if) #emph[I arise, they speak against me];; on the cohortative in the apodosis, cf. #xref(<p108f>)[§ 108#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];.

#ml("f") <p159f> (#emph[d];) #emph[Imperfect consecutive]; in the protasis (#xref(<p111x>)[§ 111#h(0.18em)#emph[x];];), #bref("Ps 139:11"); #he[וָאֹֽמַד]; #emph[if I say];, etc. (with a noun\-clause as the apodosis); with a frequentative perfect consecutive in the apodosis, #bref("1 Sam 2:16");.

#ml("g") <p159g> (#emph[e];) #emph[Perfect consecutive]; in the protasis and apodosis (see the examples, #xref(<p112kk>)[§ 112#h(0.18em)#emph[kk];]; and #emph[ll];), #bref("Gen 44:22"); #he[וְעָזַב אָבִיו וָמֵת]; #emph[and should he leave his father, his father would die];; 9:15, 44:29, #bref("Exod 4:14");, #bref("12:13");, #bref("1 Sam 16:2");, #bref("19:3");, #bref("2 Sam 13:28");, #bref("1 Kgs 8:30");; with frequentative perfects, #bref("Exod 16:21"); (referring to the past, #bref("Jer 20:9");); with imperfect in the apodosis (being separated from the #emph[Wāw]; by #he[לֹא];), #bref("Num 23:20");, #bref("Job 5:24");; introduced by an infinitive absolute, #bref("1 Kgs 2:37");; an interrogative clause in the apodosis, #bref("Lev 10:19");; a noun\-clause, #bref("Ps 37:10");, #bref("Job 7:21");.

#ml("h") <p159h> (#emph[f];) #emph[A simple perfect]; (to represent actions which are to be regarded as completed) in the protasis and apodosis, #bref("Prov 18:22"); #he[מָצָא אִשָּׁה מָצָא טוֹב]; #emph[has one found a wife, he has found a good thing];; an imperfect in the apodosis, #bref("Job 19:4");, #bref("23:10");; an imperfect consecutive, #bref("Exod 20:25");, #bref("Prov 11:2");, #bref("Job 3:25");, #bref("23:13");#super[ #emph[b];];, 29:11; an interrogative clause, #bref("Num 12:14");, #bref("Job 7:20"); #emph[if I have sinned]; (prop., well, now I have sinned!) #emph[what can I do unto thee?]; 21:31, 35:6, #bref("Amos 3:8");; a noun\-clause, #bref("Job 27:19");.

#ml("i") <p159i> (#emph[g];) #emph[A participle as casus pendens]; (cf. #xref(<p143d>)[§ 143#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];, and the sections of the Grammar there cited, esp. #xref(<p116w>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[w];];) or a complete noun\-clause in the protasis; the apodosis mostly introduced by #emph[wāw apodosis];, e.g. #bref("Prov 23:24"); #emph[K#super[e];th.]; #he[יוֹלֵד חָכָם וְיִשְׂמַח בּוֹ]; #emph[if one begetteth a wise child, he shall have joy of him];; with perfect frequentative in the apodosis, #bref("1 Sam 2:13");, etc.; but also with a simple imperfect, e.g. #bref("Exod 21:12"); (cf. #xref(<p112n>)[§ 112#h(0.18em)#emph[n];];); with an interrogative imperfect, #bref("2 Kgs 7:2");, 19; with an interrogative perfect, #bref("Judg 6:13");.

#ml("k") <p159k> (#emph[h];) #emph[Infinitive with preposition]; (also as the equivalent of a conditional clause) in the protasis, and a perfect consecutive in the apodosis (cf. #xref(<p112mm>)[§ 112#h(0.18em)#emph[mm];];), e.g. #bref("2 Sam 7:14"); ff. #he[בְּהַֽעֲוֹתוֹ וְהֹֽכַחְתִּיו וג׳]; #emph[if he commit iniquity, I will correct him];; #bref("Exod 34:34"); f. (with imperfect, followed by perfects frequentative in the apodosis).

Rem. On the expression of condition and consequence by means of two co\-ordinate #emph[imperatives];, see #xref(<p110f>)[§ 110#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];.

#ml("l") <p159l> #strong[3.]; Particles used to introduce conditional sentences are #he[אִם]; (for which in the later and latest Books sometimes #he[הֵן];, see below, under #emph[w];) and #he[לוּ];#footnote[On #he[לוּ]; cf. Kohler in Geiger’s #emph[Zeitschr. für Wiss. und Leben];, vi (1868), p. 21 ff.]; (#bref("1 Sam 14:30");, #bref("Isa 63:19"); #he[לוּא];; #bref("Eccl 6:6");, #bref("Esth 7:4"); #he[אִלּוּ];, from #he[אִם לוּ];) #emph[if];, negative #he[אִם לֹא]; and #he[לוּלֵא]; (#he[לוּלֵי];) #emph[unless];; #he[כִּי]; #emph[supposing that]; (Lat. #emph[ut];), #emph[in case that];, sometimes used almost in the same sense as #he[אִם];. With regard to the difference between #he[אִם]; (#he[אִם לֹא];) and #he[לוּ]; (#he[לוּלֵא];), the fundamental rule is that #he[אִם]; is used if the condition be regarded either as already fulfilled, or if it, together with its consequence, be thought of as possibly (or #origpage("495") <orig-495>;probably) occurring in the present or future. In the former case, #he[אִם]; is followed by the perfect, in the latter (corresponding to the Greek ἐὰν with the present subjunctive) by the imperfect or its equivalent (frequently in the apodosis also). On the other hand, #he[לוּ]; (#he[לוּלֵא];) is used when the condition is to be represented as #emph[not fulfilled]; in the past, or as #emph[not capable of fulfilment]; in the present or future, and the consequence accordingly as not having occurred or never occurring. In the former case, #he[לוּ]; and #he[לוּלֵא]; are necessarily followed by the perfect (mostly also in the apodosis) corresponding to the Greek εἰ with the indicative of an historic tense, and the Latin imperfect or pluperfect subjunctive. In the latter case (which is extremely rare) the perfect, or the participle, or even the imperfect, may be used.

#ml("m") <p159m> Rem. Since it again frequently depends on the subjective judgement of the speaker (see under #emph[a];), whether a condition is to be regarded as possible or impossible, we cannot wonder that the distinction between #he[אִם]; and #he[לוּ]; is not always consistently observed. Although naturally #he[לוּ]; and #he[לוּלֵא]; cannot take the place of #he[אִם]; and #he[אִם לֹא]; (on the strange use of #he[לוּ]; in #bref("Gen 50:15"); see below), yet conversely #he[אִם]; is sometimes used where #he[לוּ]; would certainly be expected; cf. e.g. #bref("Ps 50:12");, #bref("137:5");, #bref("139:8");, #bref("Hos 9:12"); (cf. verse 11). These examples, indeed (#he[אִם]; with the imperfect), may without difficulty be explained from the fact that the connection of #he[לוּ]; with the imperfect was evidently avoided, because the imperfect by its nature indicates a still unfinished action, and consequently (as opposed to #he[לוּ];) a still open possibility. But #he[אִם]; is also used for #he[לוּ]; in connection with the perfect, especially when an imprecation is attached by the apodosis to the condition introduced by #he[אִם];, e.g. #bref("Ps 7:4"); ff. #he[אִם־עָשִׂ֫יתִי זֹאת... יִֽרַדֹּף וג׳]; #emph[if I have done this..., let the enemy pursue my soul];, etc., cf. #bref("Job 31:9"); ff. The speaker assumes for a moment as possible and even actual, that which he really rejects as inconceivable, in order to invoke the most severe punishment on himself, if it should prove to be the case.

On the frequent addition of an infinitive absolute to the verb in clauses with #he[אם]; see #xref(<p113o>)[§ 113#h(0.18em)#emph[o];]; above.

Examples:—

#ml("n") <p159n> #strong[A.]; #he[אִם]; 1. with #emph[perfect]; in the protasis to express conditions, etc., which have been completely fulfilled in the past or which will be completely fulfilled in the future (the perfect is here equivalent to the #emph[futurum exactum];, #xref(<p106o>)[§ 106#h(0.18em)#emph[o];];). The apodosis#footnote[We are not here concerned with the fact that the logical apodosis (the consequence of the condition) is sometimes mentioned before the condition; as in #bref("Gen 18:28");, 30, #bref("Judg 11:10");, #bref("Ps 63:6"); f., 137:6, and according to Dillmann #bref("Isa 4:4");.]; takes—

(#emph[a];) A #emph[perfect]; also, e.g. #bref("Prov 9:12"); #he[אִם־חָכַ֫מְתָּ חָכַ֫מְתָּ לָּ֑ךְ]; #emph[if thou art wise, thou art wise for thyself];; #bref("Ps 73:15"); (see below on #he[לוּ];).

(#emph[b];) #emph[Imperfect];, e.g. #bref("Deut 32:41"); #he[אִם־שַׁנּוֹתִי]; #emph[if I whet my glittering sword];... #he[אָשִׁיב]; #emph[I will render vengeance];, etc.; #bref("Job 9:15"); f. 30 (in both cases we should expect #he[לוּ]; rather than #he[אִם־];; so also in #bref("Ps 44:21"); f., with an interrogative imperfect in the apodosis); #bref("Job 11:13"); (the apodosis is in verse 15).

(#emph[c];) #emph[Jussive]; (or #emph[optative];), e.g. #bref("Job 31:9"); ff. (see #emph[m]; above); #bref("Gen 18:3");. #origpage("496") <orig-496>;

#ml("o") <p159o> (#emph[d];) #emph[Perfect consecutive]; (see the examples in #xref(<p112gg>)[§ 112#h(0.18em)#emph[gg];];), e.g. #bref("Gen 43:9"); #he[אִם־לֹא הֲבִֽיאֹתִיו וג׳]; #emph[if I bring him not... then I shall have sinned];, etc.; #bref("Judg 16:17");, #bref("2 Sam 15:33");, #bref("2 Kgs 7:4");. On the other hand, e.g. #bref("Gen 47:6");, #bref("Mic 5:7");, #bref("Job 7:4"); refer to actions already completed; in #bref("Gen 38:9"); and #bref("Num 21:9"); the perfect with #he[וְ]; is a perfect frequentative and refers to past time.

(#emph[e];) #emph[Imperfect consecutive]; (see #xref(<p111q>)[§ 111#h(0.18em)#emph[q];];), e.g. #bref("Job 8:4"); #emph[if thy children have sinned]; (#he[חָֽטְאוּ];)..., #he[וַיְשַׁלְּחֵם]; #emph[he has delivered them];, etc.

(#emph[f];) #emph[Imperative];, e.g. #bref("Gen 50:4"); #he[אִם־נָא מָצָ֫אתִי חֵן בְּעֵֽינֵיכֶם דַּבְּרוּ־נָא לג׳]; #emph[if now I have found grace in your eyes, speak, I pray you];, etc.; the imperative precedes in #bref("Gen 47:16"); and #bref("Job 38:4");, 18.

#ml("p") <p159p> (#emph[g];) A (complete or incomplete) noun\-clause, e.g. #bref("Jer 14:18"); (a vivid realization of the future) #emph[if I have gone forth into the field]; (\= #emph[if I go];, etc.), #emph[then, behold, the slain with the sword!]; etc.; #bref("Prov 24:14"); (apodosis with #emph[wāw apodosis];).

#ml("q") <p159q> 2. #he[אִם]; with #emph[imperfect]; in the protasis, to express what is possible in the present or future, as well as (according to #xref(<p107b>)[§ 107#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];) what has continued or been repeated in the past. The apodosis takes—

(#emph[a];) The #emph[perfect];, e.g. #bref("Num 32:23"); #he[וְאִם־לֹא תַֽעֲשׂוּן כֵּן הִנֵּה חֲטָאתֶם]; #emph[but if ye will not do so, behold, ye have sinned];; here the apodosis represents the time when the consequence has already taken place; so also #bref("Job 20:12");–14. On the other hand, #bref("Num 16:29"); (as also #bref("1 Sam 6:9"); and #bref("1 Kgs 22:28");) is a case of a pregnant construction, #emph[if these men die as all men die, then]; (it will follow from this) #emph[the Lord hath not sent me];.

#ml("r") <p159r> (#emph[b];) The #emph[imperfect];, e.g. #bref("2 Kgs 7:4"); #he[אִם־יֶחַיֻּ֫נוּ נִֽחְיֶה]; #emph[if they save us alive, we shall live];, etc.; #bref("Gen 13:16");, #bref("18:28");, 30, 28:20 ff., #bref("Exod 20:25"); (the second imperfect is equivalent to a #emph[jussive];); #bref("Isa 1:18");, #bref("10:22");, #bref("Amos 9:2");–4, #bref("Ps 50:12"); (where #he[אִם]; ironically represents an impossibility as possible); #bref("Job 8:5"); f. (with the insertion of a second condition in the form of a noun\-clause); 9:3, 20, 14:7; a frequentative imperfect referring to the past, #bref("Gen 31:8"); #he[אִם־כֹּה יֹאמַר]; #emph[if]; (ever) #emph[he said thus];..., #he[וְיָֽלְדוּ]; #emph[then they bare];...; #bref("Exod 40:37");. In #bref("Gen 42:37"); the consequence (on #he[תָּמִית]; cf. #xref(<p107s>)[§ 107#h(0.18em)#emph[s];];) precedes the condition.

(#emph[c];) The #emph[jussive]; (or #emph[optative];), e.g. #bref("Ps 137:5");; cf. #xref(<p109h>)[§ 109#h(0.18em)#emph[h];];.

(#emph[d];) The #emph[cohortative];, e.g. #bref("Gen 13:9");, #bref("Job 31:7");; cf. #xref(<p108f>)[§ 108#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];.

#ml("s") <p159s> (#emph[e];) The #emph[perfect consecutive]; (see the examples in #xref(<p112ff>)[§ 112#h(0.18em)#emph[ff];]; and #emph[gg];), e.g. #bref("1 Sam 20:6"); #he[אִם־פָּקֹד יִפְקְדֵ֫נִי אָבִ֫יךָ וְאָֽמַרְתָּ]; #emph[if thy father miss me at all, then shalt thou say];, etc.; #bref("Gen 24:41");, #bref("Judg 4:20");; with a frequentative perfect consecutive, #bref("Gen 31:8"); #emph[if he said]; (as often happened)..., #emph[then];, etc.

(#emph[f];) The #emph[imperfect consecutive];; so perhaps #bref("Ps 59:16");, if #he[וַיָּלִ֫ינוּ]; is to be explained according to #xref(<p111t>)[§ 111#h(0.18em)#emph[t];];.

(#emph[g];) The #emph[imperative];, e.g. #bref("Gen 31:50");, #bref("1 Sam 20:21"); (with #emph[wāw apodosis];, but in verse 22 simply #he[לֵךְ];), 21:10, #bref("Job 33:5");.

#ml("t") <p159t> (#emph[h];) A noun\-clause, e.g. #bref("Gen 4:7");, #bref("Ps 139:8");, #bref("Job 8:6");, #bref("31:26"); f.

3\. #he[אִם]; with #emph[cohortative];, e.g. #bref("Gen 30:31");; cf. the passages in #xref(<p108e>)[§ 108#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];.

#ml("u") <p159u> 4. #he[אִם]; with #emph[infinitive];, #bref("Job 9:27"); #he[אִם־אָמְרִי]; prop. #emph[if my saying is]; (but probably we should read #he[אָמַ֫רְתִּי];).

#ml("v") <p159v> 5. #he[אִם]; with a noun\-clause, e.g. #bref("Deut 5:22"); (in the apodosis a perfect with #emph[wāw apodosis];), #bref("Gen 27:46");, #bref("Judg 9:15"); (imperative in the apodosis); 11:9 (imperfect in the apodosis); #bref("2 Sam 12:8"); (cohortative in the apodosis); #bref("Hos 12:12");; especially if the subject of the conditional clause be a personal pronoun. In an affirmative sentence this pronoun is often joined to #he[יֵשׁ];, in a negative sentence to #he[אֵין]; (cf. on both, #xref(<p100o>)[§ 100#h(0.18em)#emph[o];];), while the predicate (cf. #xref(<p116q>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[q];];) is represented by a participle, usually expressing the future, e.g. #bref("Judg 6:36"); f. #he[אִם־יֶשְׁךָ מוֹשִׁיעַ]; #origpage("497") <orig-497>;#emph[if thou will save];, etc.; #bref("Gen 24:49"); #he[אִם־יֶשְׁבֶם עֹשִׂים]; #emph[if ye will deal];, etc.; #bref("1 Sam 23:23");. In #bref("Gen 24:42"); f. the condition is expressed in a more humble form by the addition of #he[נָא];. With #he[אֵין]; #bref("Gen 43:5"); #he[וְאִם־אֵֽינְךָ מְשַׁלֵּחַ]; #emph[but if thou wilt not send];, etc.; 20:7 (with imperative in the apodosis); #bref("Exod 8:17");, #bref("9:2"); f., #bref("1 Sam 19:11"); (all with a participle also in the apodosis). But #he[יֵשׁ]; and #he[אַ֫יִן]; may also be used after #he[אִם]; without a suffix; thus #he[יֵשׁ]; #bref("Gen 23:8");, #bref("1 Sam 20:8");, #bref("2 Kgs 9:15");, etc., #he[אִם־אַ֫יִן]; (#emph[if it be not the case];) #bref("Gen 30:1");, #bref("Exod 32:32");, #bref("Judg 9:15");, #bref("2 Kgs 2:10");; cf. also #he[אִם־כֵּן]; #emph[if it be so];, #bref("Gen 25:22");.

#ml("w") <p159w> #strong[B.]; #he[הֵן]; #emph[if];, generally supposed to be originally identical with #he[הֵן]; #emph[behold!];#footnote[There could be no doubt of their identity if #he[וְהִנֵּה]; in #bref("1 Sam 9:7");, #bref("2 Sam 18:11");, simply meant #emph[if];. We must, however, keep to the meaning #emph[but behold];.]; Probably, however, #he[הֵן]; #emph[if];, is a pure Aramaism, and since the Aramaic word never has the meaning #emph[behold];, it is at least improbable that it had originally any connection with #he[הֵן]; or #he[הִנֵּה];. Cf. #bref("Exod 8:22");, #bref("Lev 25:20");, #bref("Isa 54:15");, #bref("Jer 3:1");, #bref("Hag 2:12");, #bref("2 Chr 7:13");, and frequently in Job, as #bref("9:11");, 12, 12:14, 15, 19:7, 23:8, 40:23, always with wāw apodosis following, except in 13:15, where consequently the meaning #emph[see]; is no doubt preferable.

#ml("x") <p159x> #strong[C.]; #he[לוּ]; #emph[if];, #he[לוּלֵא]; (#he[לוּלֵי];) #emph[if not];.

1\. With #emph[perfect]; in the protasis and apodosis (cf. #xref(<p106p>)[§ 106#h(0.18em)#emph[p];];), e.g. #bref("Judg 8:19");; #he[אִלּוּ]; is used in the same sense as #he[לוּ]; in #bref("Esth 7:4");, cf. #bref("Eccl 6:6"); (with a question in the apodosis).—With the perfect in protasis and apodosis after #he[לוּלֵא]; #bref("Gen 31:42");, #bref("43:10");, #bref("Judg 14:18");, #bref("1 Sam 25:34");, #bref("2 Sam 2:27");, #bref("Isa 1:9");. On the other hand, in #bref("Deut 32:29"); #he[לוּ]; with a perfect is followed by an imperfect in the apodosis, #emph[if they were wise, they would understand this];; in #bref("Mic 2:11"); by a perfect consecutive.

#ml("y") <p159y> 2. With #emph[imperfect]; after #he[לוּלֵא]; #bref("Deut 32:27");, #he[אָגוּר]; probably as the #emph[modus rei repetitae, were it not that I ever and again feared];, etc.; so also the imperfect after #he[לוּ]; with the apodosis suppressed, #bref("Gen 50:15"); #emph[supposing that Joseph should hate us];; since, according to the context, the danger was real, the use of #he[לוּ]; here is strange; conversely in other cases, e.g. #bref("Ps 73:15");, #bref("Job 9:15"); f. 30, #he[לוּ]; would be more natural than #he[אִם];.

#ml("z") <p159z> 3. A noun\-clause occurs after #he[לוּ]; #bref("2 Sam 18:12");, #bref("2 Kgs 3:14");, #bref("Ps 81:14");, all with imperfect in the apodosis; #bref("Job 16:4"); #he[לוּ יֵשׁ];, with cohortative in the apodosis.

#strong[D.]; #he[כִּי]; #emph[supposing that, if];:—

#ml("aa") <p159aa> 1. #he[כִּי]; with #emph[perfect]; in the protasis, e.g. #bref("Num 5:20"); #he[וְאַתְּ כִּי שָׂטִית]; #emph[but thou, if thou hast gone astray];, etc.; with a frequentative perfect consecutive in the apodosis, #bref("Job 7:13"); f.; with an imperfect consecutive, #bref("Job 22:29");.

#ml("bb") <p159bb> 2. #he[כִּי]; with #emph[imperfect]; in the protasis, e.g. #bref("Ps 23:4"); #he[גַּם כִּֽי־אֵלֵךְ]; #emph[yea, though I walk]; (have to walk)..., #emph[I will fear no]; (#he[לֹֽא־אִירָא];) #emph[evil];; 37:24; #bref("Exod 21:2"); #he[כִּֽי־תִקְנֶה עֶ֫בֶד עִבְרִי וג׳]; #emph[if thou buy an Hebrew servant, six years shall he serve]; (but in verses 3–5 a series of definite conditions with definite consequences is introduced by #he[אִם];; so also the #he[כִּי]; in verse 7 is followed in verses 8–11 by the special cases with #he[אִם];; cf. also verse 17 ff.); cf. #bref("Gen 4:24");, #bref("24:41");, #bref("Job 38:5");; with a perfect consecutive in the apodosis, #bref("Gen 32:18"); f., #bref("Exod 18:16");; with a noun\-clause, #bref("Isa 1:15");.

3\. #he[כִּי]; with a #emph[noun\-clause]; (and imperfect in the apodosis), #bref("2 Sam 19:8");.

#align(center)[#sc[Remarks.];];

#ml("cc") <p159cc> 1. In #bref("2 Kgs 5:13"); the particle #he[אבי]; (Masora #he[אָבִי];, probably in the sense of #emph[my father];) appears exceptionally for #he[לוּ];; its meaning here is unquestionable, but #origpage("498") <orig-498>;its origin is obscure. Cf. the exhaustive discussion of Delitzsch and Wetzstein on #bref("Job 34:36");, where this #he[אבי]; appears to be used as a desiderative particle.—Sometimes when one case has been already discussed, another of the same character is added by means of #he[אוֹ]; or, e.g. #bref("Exod 21:36"); #he[אוֹ נוֹדַע וג׳]; #emph[or]; (another possible case) #emph[it is known that];, etc., i.e. #emph[but if it be known];, etc., LXX ἐὰν δέ, Vulg. #emph[sin autem];; cf. #bref("Lev 4:23");, 28, 5:1, 25:49, #bref("2 Sam 18:13");; with a following imperfect, #bref("Ezek 14:17"); f.—On the hypothetical use of #he[אֲשֶׁר]; (which is interchangeable with #he[כִּי]; in other senses also) #bref("Lev 4:22"); (in verses 3 and 27 #he[אִם];), #bref("Deut 11:27"); (verse 28 #he[אִם];), #bref("Josh 4:21");, see the Lexicon.

#ml("dd") <p159dd> 2. The conditional sentence is frequently found in an abridged form, where the suppressed clauses can be easily supplied from the context; cf. #bref("Gen 13:9");, #bref("24:49");, #bref("1 Sam 2:16"); #he[וְאִם־לֹא]; #emph[and if not];, i.e. #emph[and if thou wilt not give it to me, then I take]; it (perfect according to #xref(<p106n>)[§ 106#h(0.18em)#emph[n];];) #emph[by force];; cf. #bref("1 Sam 6:9");. The use of #he[וְיֵשׁ]; alone in #bref("Judg 6:13"); is peculiar, as also #he[וָיֵשׁ]; in #bref("2 Kgs 10:15"); (where read with the LXX #he[וַיֹּא֫מֶר יֵהוּא וָיֵשׁ];) in the sense of #emph[if it be so];.—In #bref("2 Sam 13:26");, #bref("2 Kgs 5:17"); #he[וָלֹא]; alone appears to be used in the sense of #emph[if really ... not];, in each case with a following jussive equivalent to #emph[may there at least];, etc. (cf. #xref(<p143d>)[§ 143#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];); but perhaps with Matthes, #emph[ZAW.]; 1903, p. 122 ff., following Kuipers, we should read #he[וְלוּ]; #emph[would that!];—In #bref("1 Sam 13:13");, #bref("Job 3:13"); the condition must be supplied from the preceding clause to complete the sentence introduced by #he[כִּי עַתָּה];, in #bref("Job 31:28"); by #he[כִּי];, in #bref("2 Kgs 13:19"); by #he[אָז];.—The apodosis also appears sometimes in an abridged form (e.g. #bref("Gen 4:24");, #bref("Isa 43:2");) or is entirely suppressed, e.g. #bref("Gen 30:27");, #bref("38:17");, #bref("50:15"); (see #emph[y]; above), #bref("Exod 32:32");, #bref("Ps 27:13");, #bref("Job 38:5");, where properly #he[הַגֵּד]; must be supplied with #he[כִּי תֵדָ֑ע]; as in verses 4 and 18; cf. #xref(<p167a>)[§ 167#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];.—In #bref("Ps 8:4");, instead of the apodosis #emph[I exclaim]; which we should expect, the exclamation itself follows.

#ml("ee") <p159ee> 3. The absolute certainty with which a result is to be expected is frequently emphasized by the insertion of #he[כִּי]; #bref("Isa 7:9");; #he[כִּי אָז]; #bref("2 Sam 2:27");, #bref("19:7");, #bref("Job 11:15");; or #he[כִּי עַתָּה]; #emph[now verily];, #bref("Num 22:29");, #bref("1 Sam 14:30"); after #he[לוּ];, #bref("Gen 31:42");, #bref("43:10"); after #he[לוּלֵי];, #bref("Job 8:6"); after #he[אִם];. On this #emph[corroborative]; #he[כִּי]; cf. such passages as #bref("Gen 18:20");, etc., and #xref(<p148d>)[§ 148#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];. On #he[כִּי אִם]; after an oath cf. 163 #emph[d];.

#ml("ff") <p159ff> 4. Sometimes the force of a hypothetical particle extends beyond the apodosis to a second conditional clause, as in the case of #he[אִם]; #bref("Prov 9:12");, #bref("Job 10:15");, #bref("16:6");, #bref("22:23");, and #he[כִּי]; #bref("Isa 43:2");.

#ml("gg") <p159gg> 5. In #bref("Exod 33:20"); a negative statement takes the place of a condition with a negative consequence, #emph[for a man doth not see me and live];, instead of #emph[for if a man sees me, he does not live];; cf. the similar passages, #bref("Deut 22:1");, 4 #emph[thou shalt not see ... and hide thyself];, instead of #emph[if thou seest ... thou shalt not hide thyself];.

#sec("160", [Concessive Clauses], note: []) <s160>

#ml("a") <p160a> Besides the use of the imperative in the sense of a concession, meant either seriously (#xref(<p110a>)[§ 110#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];) or mockingly (#xref(<p110f>)[§ 110#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];), and of concessive circumstantial clauses (#xref(<p141e>)[§ 141#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];, #xref(<p142d>)[§ 142#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];, and #xref(<p156f>)[§ 156#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];), concessive clauses may be introduced—

(#emph[a];) By a simple #he[אִם]; #emph[if];: thus #bref("Job 9:15"); with perfect, #emph[if]; (\=#emph[though];) #emph[I had been in the right];; #bref("Isa 1:18"); and 10:22 with imperfect in reference to a contingent event.

#ml("b") <p160b> (#emph[b];) By #he[גַּם כִּי]; #emph[yea though];, #bref("Isa 1:15"); with imperfect; for which we find simply #he[גַּם]; in #bref("Isa 49:15"); with imperfect, #emph[yea, though these may forget, yet]; ...; on the other hand, with perfect, #bref("Jer 36:25");, #bref("Ps 95:9");, #bref("Neh 6:1");; finally #he[כִּי גַם]; #emph[even if, though];, #bref("Eccl 4:14");. #origpage("499") <orig-499>;

#ml("c") <p160c> (#emph[c];) By the preposition #he[עַל־]; governing a complete noun\-clause, as #bref("Job 16:17"); #he[עַל לֹֽא־חָמָס בְּכַפָּ֑י]; #emph[notwithstanding]; that #emph[no violence is in mine hands];, or a verbal\-clause, #bref("Isa 53:9");. On #he[עַל־]; with the infinitive in a similar sense (equivalent to #emph[in addition to the fact]; that \= #emph[notwithstanding]; that), cf. #xref(<p119aa>)[§ 119#h(0.18em)#emph[aa];];, note 2.

#sec("161", [Comparative Clauses], note: []) <s161>

#ml("a") <p161a> #strong[1.]; A comparison between two facts is sometimes established by simply uniting them with #emph[wāw copulative];, especially in gnomic poetry, when facts of a moral nature are compared with those of the physical world, e.g. #bref("Job 5:7"); #emph[man is born unto trouble, and the sons of flame fly upward];, i.e. as the sparks by nature fly upward, so man, etc.; #bref("Job 12:11"); (in an interrogative form; in 34:3 the same comparison as a statement); 14:11 f., #bref("Prov 17:3");, #bref("25:3");, #bref("26:3");, 9, 14, 27:21, etc.#footnote[On this #emph[wāw adaequationis];, and in general on these proverbial comparisons, see Delitzsch, #emph[Das Salomonische Spruchbuch];, p. 9 f. Moreover, instead of entire clauses, the nouns alone (without predicates) are frequently grouped together, e.g. #bref("Prov 25:25");, #bref("26:21");, #bref("27:21"); (called by Delitzsch, the ‘emblematic Mashal’). The expressions #he[נֶחְשַׁב עִם]; prop. #emph[to be counted with some one];, #bref("Ps 88:5");, and #he[נִמְשַׁל עִם]; #emph[to be likened with some one];, #bref("Ps 28:1");, #bref("143:7");, also arise from the idea of comparison implied in grouping things together. On this use of #he[עִם]; cf. #bref("Job 9:26");, where #emph[with]; is equivalent to #emph[like];.]; Even without the connecting #he[וְ]; #bref("Job 24:19"); #emph[drought and heat consume the snow waters];, #he[שְׁאוֹל חָטָ֫אוּ]; so doth #emph[Sheol]; those who #emph[have sinned]; (cf. #xref(<p155n>)[§ 155#h(0.18em)#emph[n];];); cf. #bref("Jer 17:11");.

#ml("b") <p161b> #strong[2.]; The conjunction #he[כַּֽאֲשֶׁר]; (cf. #xref(<p155g>)[§ 155#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];; the simple #he[אֲשֶׁר]; occurs in the same sense in #bref("Exod 10:6");, #bref("14:13");, #bref("34:18");) #emph[as];, #emph[quemadmodum];, is used as a comparative conjunction (#bref("Obad 15");), frequently with #he[כֵּן]; #emph[so];, corresponding to it in the apodosis, #bref("Isa 31:4");, #bref("52:14"); f. Sometimes, however, #he[כֵּן]; (#emph[so also];) occurs even after independent statements, #bref("Isa 55:9");, #bref("Jer 3:20");.—Exact coincidence of two facts is expressed in #bref("Eccl 5:15"); by #he[כָּל־עֻמַּת שָׁ·];#footnote[In spite of its form this particle has originally nothing to do with #he[כֹּל];, #he[כָּל־]; #emph[all];. The expression is compounded of #he[כְּ‍]; and #he[לְעֻמַּת];, like the Aramaic #he[כָּל־קֳבֵל]; for #he[כְּלָֽקֳבֵל];; cf. M. Lambert, #emph[REJ.]; xxx. 47.]; #emph[in all points as];.

#ml("c") <p161c> Rem. On the use of #he[כְּ‍]; #emph[as];, with single nouns or pronouns to introduce comparisons, cf. 118 #emph[s];; on the alleged use of #he[כְּ‍]; as a conjunction (equivalent to #he[כַּֽאֲשֶׁר];), cf. #xref(<p155g>)[§ 155#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];.—It is to be further remarked that #he[כְּ‍];—#he[כְּ‍]; when used in correspondence with one another, #emph[as—so]; (e.g. #bref("Lev 7:7");, #bref("Judg 8:18");, #bref("Isa 24:2");, #bref("Hos 4:9");; also #emph[so—as];, #bref("Gen 18:25");, #bref("44:18");, #bref("Deut 1:17");, #bref("1 Kgs 22:4");; in #bref("Josh 14:11");, #bref("1 Sam 30:24"); #he[כְּ];—#he[וּכְ];; #bref("Ps 127:4"); and often, #he[כְּ‍];—#he[כֵּן];, cf. #bref("Joel 2:4");), are not to be regarded as conjunctions, but as virtual substantives with a following genitive; #he[כָּכֶם כַּגֵּר יִֽהְיֶה]; #bref("Num 15:15"); properly means #emph[the like of you shall be the like of the stranger];, i.e. #emph[your duty shall be]; (also) #emph[the stranger’s duty];; cf. #bref("Lev 24:22");. #origpage("500") <orig-500>;

#sec("162", [Disjunctive Sentences], note: []) <s162>

#ml("a") <p162a> The introduction of another possible case, excluding that which preceded, is effected by #he[אוֹ]; or, e.g. #bref("Exod 21:36");, equivalent to the Latin #emph[vel];; but also equivalent to #emph[aut]; with an exclusive antithesis, #bref("2 Kgs 2:16");; so #bref("Isa 27:5"); #he[אוֹ];\= #emph[it would then happen that];, for which elsewhere #he[אוֹ כִי];.

#ml("b") <p162b> In the sense of #emph[sive—sive]; we find #he[אוֹ];—#he[אוֹ];, or #he[אִם];—#he[אִם];, or #he[אִם];—#he[וְאִם]; (see the examples in the Lexicon), also #he[וְ];—#he[וְ]; #bref("Lev 5:3");, #bref("Num 9:14");, #bref("Deut 24:7");, #bref("Isa 2:13"); ff., #bref("Jer 32:20");, #bref("Ps 76:7");, #bref("Job 34:29");, perhaps also #bref("Exod 21:16"); (but not #bref("Prov 29:9");; cf. Delitzsch on the passage), and #he[לְ];—#he[לְ]; (see #xref(<p143e>)[§ 143#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];); cf. also #he[גַּם];—#he[גַּם]; (in #bref("Gen 24:44"); #he[גַּם];—#he[וְגַם];) #emph[both—and];; but #he[גַּם לֹא];—#he[גַּם לֹא]; (in #bref("Gen 21:26"); #he[וְגַם לֹא];—#he[וְגַם לֹא];; #bref("Zeph 1:18"); #he[גַּם];—#he[גַּם ... לֹא];) #emph[neither—nor];. On disjunctive questions, see #xref(<p150g>)[§ 150#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];.

#sec("163", [Adversative and Exceptive Clauses], note: []) <s163>

#ml("a") <p163a> #strong[1.]; After negative sentences (especially after prohibitions) the antithesis (#emph[but];) is introduced by #he[כִּי אִם];, e.g. #bref("1 Sam 8:19"); #emph[and they said, Nay, but we will have a king over us];; #bref("Ps 1:2");, etc.; frequently also by #he[כִּי]; alone, e.g. #bref("Gen 18:15");, #bref("19:2");, or even simply connected with #he[וְ];, #bref("Gen 17:5");, #he[וְהָיָה]; as perfect consecutive; 42:10; cf. #bref("Exod 5:18");.

#ml("b") <p163b> Rem. Sometimes the negation is only virtually contained in the preceding sentence, e.g. in the form of a rhetorical question (#bref("Mic 6:3"); f.) or of conditions which are to be regarded as not having been fulfilled (#bref("Job 31:18");); #he[כִּי]; or #he[כִּי אִם]; in such cases becomes equivalent to #emph[nay, rather];.

#ml("c") <p163c> #strong[2.]; #emph[Exceptive]; clauses, depending on another sentence, are introduced by #he[אֶ֫פֶס כִּי]; #emph[except that];, and (again after negative sentences, see #emph[a]; above) #he[כִּי אִם];#footnote[Very probably this use of #he[כִּי אִם]; arises from the original meaning #emph[for if, surely if]; (#he[כִּי]; in an affirmative sense); so evidently in #bref("Exod 22:22"); as a forcible resumption of the preceding #he[אִם];. Thus, e.g. #bref("Judg 15:7"); is simply #emph[surely when I have been avenged of you, after that I will cease];, equivalent to, I will not cease, until I have, etc. When the exception follows, an ellipse must be assumed, e.g. #bref("Ruth 3:18"); #emph[surely]; (or #emph[for];) #emph[when he has finished it]; (then the man will rest). It is far less natural to assume such an ellipse with #he[כִּי אִם]; #emph[but]; (before entire clauses as before single nouns); see #emph[a]; above.]; #emph[unless];; especially #he[כִּי אִם]; with the perfect (equivalent to #emph[unless previously];) after imperfects which contain a declaration, e.g. #bref("Gen 32:27"); #emph[I will not let thee go, except thou hast]; previously #emph[blessed me];; #bref("Lev 22:6");, #bref("Isa 55:10");, #bref("65:6");, #bref("Amos 3:7");, #bref("Ruth 3:18");. Finally, #he[בִּלְתִּי אִם]; #emph[unless];, #bref("Amos 3:4"); (with perfect after a rhetorical question), or simply #he[בִּלְתִּי]; #bref("Gen 43:3"); with a noun\-clause, #emph[except your brother be with you];; #bref("Isa 10:4"); after a rhetorical question, with a verbal\-clause. #origpage("501") <orig-501>;

#ml("d") <p163d> Rem. The principal statement, to which #he[כִּי אִם]; appends an exception, must sometimes be supplied from the context; thus, #bref("Gen 40:14"); (I desire nothing else) #emph[except that thou remember me];, equivalent to #emph[only do thou remember];, etc. (cf. § 106 #emph[n];, note 2; but it is probably better to read #he[אַךְ]; for #he[כִּי];). Cf. #bref("Mic 6:8");, where #he[כִּי אִם];, equivalent to #emph[nothing but];, is used before an infinitive, and #bref("Job 42:8");, equivalent to #emph[only];, before a noun. Similarly when #he[כִּי אִם]; after an oath introduces an emphatic assurance, e.g. in #bref("2 Kgs 5:20"); #emph[as the Lord liveth]; (I can do nothing else) #emph[except I run after him];, etc.; cf. #bref("2 Sam 15:21"); #emph[K#super[e];th.];, #bref("Jer 51:14");, #bref("Ruth 3:12"); #emph[K#super[e];th.];, and even without the oath, #bref("Judg 15:7");; cf. the Rem. on #emph[c];.

#sec("164", [Temporal Clauses], note: []) <s164>

#ml("a") <p164a> #strong[1.]; The relations of time existing between two different actions or events are frequently expressed without the aid of a conjunction simply by juxtaposition:—

(#emph[a];) Actions or events are represented as wholly or in part #emph[simultaneous]; by connecting a noun\-clause with another noun\-clause or verbal\-clause introduced by #he[וְ]; (or #he[וְהִנֵּה];), e.g. #bref("Gen 7:6"); #emph[and Noah]; was #emph[six hundred years old]; (prop. #emph[a son of six hundred years];), #he[וְהַמַּבּוּל הָיָה]; and (i.e. #emph[when];) #emph[the flood was];. This is especially the case when the predicate of the noun\-clause (frequently introduced by #he[עוֹד]; #emph[still];) is expressed by an active participle, e.g. #bref("Job 1:16"); f. #he[עוֹד זֶה מְדַבֵּר וְזֶה בָא וג׳]; #emph[he]; was #emph[yet speaking, and there came another];, etc.; see the numerous examples in #xref(<p111g>)[§ 111#h(0.18em)#emph[g];]; and #xref(<p116u>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[u];];. Instead of a complete noun\-clause there often occurs a simple #emph[casus pendens]; after #he[כָּל־]; with a participial attribute in the sense of #emph[whenever any one];..., e.g. #bref("1 Sam 2:13"); #he[כָּל־אִישׁ זֹבֵחַ זֶ֫בַח וּבָא וג׳]; #emph[whenever any man offered sacrifice, then came];, etc.; #bref("2 Sam 2:23");, etc.; see the examples (in which the second member is generally introduced by #emph[wāw apodosis];) in #xref(<p116w>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[w];];.

#ml("b") <p164b> (#emph[b];) Sequence is expressed by the juxtaposition

(1) of two imperfects consecutive, e.g. #bref("Gen 24:19"); #he[וַתְּכַל לְהַשְׁקֹתוֹ וַתֹּאמֶר]; #emph[and when she had done giving him drink, she said];, etc.; 28:8 f., 29:31, 30:9, 32:26, etc.; cf. #xref(<p111d>)[§ 111#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];;

(2) of a noun\-clause with a passive participle as predicate, and a verbal\-clause attached by #he[וְ];, e.g. #bref("Gen 38:25");; cf. #xref(<p116v>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[v];];; in #bref("Gen 49:29"); an imperative follows without #he[וְ];;

(3) of two perfects (frequently with the secondary idea of rapid succession#footnote[This secondary idea is implied here by the mere co\-ordination of two independent #emph[verbal];\-clauses, just as the idea of simultaneous occurrence (according to #xref(<p116u>)[§ 116#h(0.18em)#emph[u];];, note 1) is implied in the co\-ordination of a #emph[noun];\-clause with another clause. In #bref("Gen 27:30"); the immediate succession is especially emphasized by #he[אַךְ]; and the infinitive absolute, #emph[Jacob was yet scarce gone out... then Esau his brother came];; in #bref("1 Kgs 9:24"); by #he[אַךְ]; only; in #bref("Ps 48:6"); by #he[כֵּן]; and the addition of two more perfects without #he[וְ];.]; of the two actions or events in past time), e.g. #bref("Gen 19:23"); #he[הַשֶּׁ֫מָשׁ יָצָא... וְלוֹט בָּא וג׳]; #emph[the sun was just risen..., and]; (\=when) #emph[Lot came];, etc., cf. #bref("1 Sam 9:5");, #bref("2 Sam 2:24");; #bref("Gen 44:3"); f., #bref("Judg 3:24");, #bref("15:14");, #bref("20:39"); f.—In all these examples the subject follows immediately after the connective #emph[Wāw];, and then the (simple) perfect. On the other hand,

(4) a perfect consecutive follows another perfect consecutive to express the contingent succession of future actions, e.g. #bref("Gen 44:4"); #he[וְהִשַּׂגְתָּם וְאָֽמַרְתָּ אֲלֵהֶם]; #origpage("502") <orig-502>;#emph[and when thou dost overtake them]; (as soon as thou shalt have overtaken), #emph[thou shalt say unto them];. Naturally, examples of this kind are very closely related to conditional sentences; see, therefore, the examples in #xref(<p112kk>)[§ 112#h(0.18em)#emph[kk];]; and #xref(<p159g>)[§ 159#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];. On the connection of an imperfect consecutive or a perfect with detached expressions of time (as equivalent to complete clauses), cf. #xref(<p111b>)[§ 111#h(0.18em)#emph[b];];; on the imperfect consecutive after #he[וַיְהִי]; and a statement of time, cf. #xref(<p111g>)[§ 111#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];; on the perfect consecutive following a detached statement of time, as in #bref("Exod 16:6");, cf. #xref(<p112oo>)[§ 112#h(0.18em)#emph[oo];];.—In #bref("1 Sam 29:10"); an imperative with #he[וְ]; follows the perfect consecutive.

#ml("c") <p164c> (5) The fact that one action or event has not yet taken place on the occurrence of another, is expressed by #he[טֶ֫רֶם]; (an adverb, not a conjunction) with the imperfect (according to #xref(<p107c>)[§ 107#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];). The apodosis, which may consist of a subject and perfect or even of a noun\-clause (#bref("Gen 24:15");),#footnote[On the perfect in the protasis, which is critically doubtful, cf. #xref(<p107c>)[§ 107#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];.]; is then connected by #he[וְ]; (or #he[וְהִנֵּה];) as in the examples above, under no. 3, e.g. #bref("Gen 19:4"); (cf. #bref("Josh 2:8");) #he[טֶ֫רֶם יִשְׁכָּ֫בוּ וְאַנְשֵׁי הָעִיר ... נָסַ֫בּוּ וג׳]; #emph[they had not yet lain down, and]; (\=when) #emph[the men of the city]; ... #emph[compassed];, etc.; #bref("Gen 24:25");.

#ml("d") <p164d> #strong[2.]; Conjunctions used to introduce temporal clauses are #he[כִּי]; (with perfect, e.g. #bref("Gen 6:1");, #bref("Judg 1:28");, #bref("16:16");, #bref("1 Sam 1:12");; with imperfect, #bref("Gen 4:12");, #bref("12:12");, #bref("24:41");, #bref("Exod 3:21");, #bref("Lev 21:9");, #bref("Deut 31:21");, #bref("Isa 1:12");, #bref("8:19");) and #he[אֲשֶׁר];#footnote[On #he[אֲשֶׁר]; as an original demonstrative, cf. #xref(<p138a>)[§ 138#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];; hence #he[עַד־אֲשֶׁר נָשׁוּב]; is properly #emph[up to that]; (moment)—#emph[we shall return];.]; #emph[when]; (#he[כִּי]; with the imperfect also\=#emph[as often as];, #bref("Ps 8:4");; with perfect #bref("Job 1:5");); less frequently #he[אִם];#footnote[Cf. the frequent use of #emph[wenn]; \[prop. #emph[if];\] for #emph[wann]; \[\=#emph[when];\] in German.]; (joined with a perfect), e.g. #bref("Gen 38:9");, #bref("Num 21:9");, #bref("Judg 6:3");, #bref("Ps 41:7");, #bref("94:18");, cf. also #bref("Isa 24:13");\=#emph[quotiescunque];; also in the same sense with an imperfect, #bref("Num 36:4");; with a perfect, equivalent to the #emph[futurum exactum];, #bref("Isa 4:4");. Other conjunctions of time are the compounds #he[כְּמוֹ]; #emph[when];, #bref("Gen 19:15");; #he[כַּֽאֲשֶׁר]; #emph[when, after that];; #he[עַד־אֲשֶׁר];, #he[עַד־כִּי]; #emph[until]; (also the simple #he[עַד־];, e.g. #bref("Gen 38:11");, #bref("Josh 2:22");, #bref("1 Sam 1:22"); \[with the imperfect\=#emph[only when];, as in #bref("2 Sam 10:5");\]); 2:5, etc.; especially in the formula #he[עַד־בִּלְתִּי הִשְׁאִיר לוֹ]; #emph[until there was none left remaining to him]; (where indeed it would be very natural to read #he[הַשְׁאִיר]; the infin. constr., as elsewhere after #he[בִּלְתִּי];, #xref(<p114s>)[§ 114#h(0.18em)#emph[s];];) #bref("Num 21:35");, #bref("Deut 3:3");, #bref("Josh 8:22");, #bref("11:8"); (but #bref("1 Sam 14:19"); #emph[while, as long as];); #he[עַד אֲשֶׁר לֹא]; #emph[before that];, #bref("Eccl 12:1");, 2, 6 with an imperfect, as in #bref("Prov 8:26"); #he[עַד]; with a perfect; #he[עַד־אִם];, #he[עַד־אֲשֶׁר אִם]; #emph[until the time when];; #he[אַֽחֲרֵֽי־אֲשֶׁר]; (for which in #bref("Ezek 40:1"); #he[אַחַר־אֲשֶׁר];; #bref("Lev 25:48");, #bref("1 Sam 5:9"); simply #he[אַֽחֲרֵי];; #bref("Lev 14:43");, #bref("Jer 41:16");, #bref("Job 42:7"); simply #he[אַחַר];) #emph[after that];; #he[מֵאָז]; (prop. #emph[since that time];; the dependent clause is attached to it in the same way as the attributive clause to the demonstrative #he[אֲשֶׁר]; #xref(<p138e>)[§ 138#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];) #emph[since];, #bref("Gen 39:5");; #he[בְּטֶ֫רֶם]; (and simply #he[טֶ֫רֶם]; #xref(<p107c>)[§ 107#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];) #emph[before];; #he[קַדְמַת]; (for #he[קַדְמַת אֲשֶׁר];) #emph[before];, #bref("Ps 129:6");.

#ml("e") <p164e> Rem. 1. With regard to the tenses used with the above conjunctions, the rules are practically the same as those given in #xref(<p158d>)[§ 158#h(0.18em)#emph[d];]; for causal clauses. The perfect indicates actions completed in the past or future (in the former case corresponding to the Latin pluperfect, #xref(<p106f>)[§ 106#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];, and in the latter to the #origpage("503") <orig-503>;Latin #emph[futurum exactum];, #xref(<p106o>)[§ 106#h(0.18em)#emph[o];];), the imperfect denotes actions occurring contingently in the future. On #he[טֶ֫רֶם];, #he[בְּטֶ֫רֶם];, and #he[עַד]; with the imperfect as a #emph[tempus historicum];, cf. #xref(<p107c>)[§ 107#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];.

#ml("f") <p164f> 2. Clauses introduced by #he[עַד];, #he[עַד־כִּי]; or #he[עַד־אֲשֶׁר];, sometimes express a limit which is not absolute (terminating the preceding action), but only relative, beyond which the action or state described in the principal clause still continues; thus, #he[עַד]; with the imperfect, #bref("Ps 110:1");; #he[עַד־כִּי]; with the perfect, #bref("Gen 26:13");, with impf. 49:10; #he[עַד־אֲשֶׁר]; with the perfect, #bref("Gen 28:15");; with the imperfect, #bref("Ps 112:8");.—Like the Arab. #arabic[حَتَّى];, #he[עַד]; may even introduce a main clause; e.g. #bref("Exod 15:16"); #he[עַד־יַֽעֲבֹר]; prop. no doubt\=thus it came to this—#emph[they passed through];, i.e. so #emph[they passed through];.

#ml("g") <p164g> 3. The infinitive construct governed by a preposition (#xref(<p114d>)[§ 114#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];, #xref(<p114e>)[#emph[e];];) is very frequently used as the equivalent of a temporal clause; the infinitive with #he[בְּ]; may usually be rendered by #emph[when, as];, or #emph[while];; the infinitive with #he[כְּ‍]; by #emph[when, as soon as]; (in #bref("Prov 10:25"); followed by a noun\-clause introduced by #emph[wāw apodosis];), or, when referring to the future, by #emph[if];; the infinitive after #he[מִן]; by #emph[since];. According to #xref(<p111g>)[§ 111#h(0.18em)#emph[g];]; such statements of time are generally preceded by #he[וַיְהִי]; and the apodosis follows in the imperfect consecutive; hence in #bref("1 Sam 17:55"); (cf. Driver on the passage) #he[וְכִרְאוֹת]; with a simple perfect following, is unusual. On the continuation of these infinitival constructions by means of the perfect consecutive, cf. #xref(<p112v>)[§ 112#h(0.18em)#emph[v];];, and in general, #xref(<p114r>)[§ 114#h(0.18em)#emph[r];];.—With the participle, #he[כְּ‍]; appears to be used as the equivalent of a conjunction in #he[כְּמֵשִׁיב]; #emph[as he drew back];, #bref("Gen 38:29"); (unless we should read #he[כְּהָשִׁיב]; \[or #he[כְּמוֹ הֵשִׁיב];, cf. #bref("Gen 19:15");\]), and in #he[כְפֹרַ֫חַת]; #emph[when it budded];, #bref("40:10");.

#sec("165", [Final Clauses], note: [#footnote[Cf. H. G. T. Mitchell, #emph[Final Constructions of Biblical Hebrew];, Leipzig, 1879.];]) <s165>

#ml("a") <p165a> #strong[1.]; Like most of the dependent clauses hitherto treated, the final clause may also be joined by a simple #emph[wāw copulative]; to the main clause, unless the final clause is directly subordinated to the governing verb.

Examples of the connection: (α) of a final imperfect (or jussive?) with a perfect by means of #he[וְ];, #bref("Lam 1:19");, see #xref(<p107q>)[§ 107#h(0.18em)#emph[q];];; with an interrogative sentence, #bref("2 Sam 9:1");, 3, #bref("Job 38:24");; with an optative, #bref("Ps 51:9");; with an imperative, #bref("1 Kgs 11:21");; (β) of a cohortative with an imperative by #he[וְ];, #bref("Gen 29:21");, #bref("1 Sam 15:16");, or a jussive, #bref("Neh 2:5"); (#xref(<p108d>)[§ 108#h(0.18em)#emph[d];];); (γ) of a jussive with an imperative by #he[וְ];, #bref("Exod 9:1");, #bref("2 Sam 16:11");, #bref("1 Kgs 5:20");, #bref("Ps 59:14");, #bref("86:17");; with a jussive, #bref("Job 21:19");, or cohortative, #xref(<p109f>)[§ 109#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];, g (cf. also #bref("2 Sam 24:21"); the infinitive with #he[לְ];, #bref("Jonah 1:11"); #he[מָה]; with the 1st plur. imperf., and #bref("2 Chr 29:10"); #he[עִם־לְבָבִי];, which are equivalent to cohortatives); (δ) of an imperative with a jussive, cohortative, or interrogative sentence by #he[וְ];, #xref(<p110i>)[§ 110#h(0.18em)#emph[i];];; (ε) of a perfect consecutive after another perfect consecutive, #bref("Lev 14:36");; after an imperfect, #xref(<p112m>)[§ 112#h(0.18em)#emph[m];]; and #xref(<p112p>)[#emph[p];];; similarly after a jussive, #xref(<p112q>)[§ 112#h(0.18em)#emph[q];];; after an imperative, #xref(<p112r>)[§ 112#h(0.18em)#emph[r];];.—On negative final clauses joined by #he[וְלֹא]; to the imperfect (so #bref("Exod 28:43");, #bref("30:20");; and #bref("2 Sam 13:25"); after #he[אַל־נָא]; with a jussive in the main clause) see the Rem. on #xref(<p109g>)[§ 109#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];. In #bref("Exod 28:32");, #bref("39:23"); the negative final clause is simply connected by #he[לֹא];.—On the use of an historical statement after verbs of command\- #origpage("504") <orig-504>;ing, where we should expect a final clause (e.g. #bref("Neh 13:9"); #emph[then I commanded, and they cleansed];, equivalent to #emph[that they should cleanse, and they cleansed];; in #bref("Job 9:7"); a negative final clause is connected in this way by #he[וְלֹא];), cf. #xref(<p120f>)[§ 120#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];.

For examples of the direct subordination of the final imperfect (without #he[וְ];) see #xref(<p120c>)[§ 120#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];.

#ml("b") <p165b> #strong[2.]; Final conjunctions are #he[לְמַ֫עַן אֲשֶׁר]; #emph[to the end that];; also simply #he[לְמַ֫עַן]; #bref("Gen 12:13");, #bref("27:25");, #bref("Exod 4:5");, #bref("Ps 51:6");, etc.; #he[בַּֽעֲבוּר אֲשֶׁר]; prop. #emph[for the purpose that];, #bref("Gen 27:10");, and simply #he[בַּֽעֲבוּר]; #bref("Gen 27:4");, #bref("Exod 9:14");, #bref("20:20");; also the simple #he[אֲשֶׁר];#footnote[In #bref("Ezek 36:27"); a final clause is introduced by #he[אֵת אֲשֶׁר];, thus at the same time taking the form of an object\-clause.]; #bref("Deut 4:10");, 40, 6:3, 32:46, #bref("Josh 3:7");, #bref("Neh 8:14"); f.; negatively, #he[אֲשֶׁר לֹא]; #bref("Gen 11:7");, #bref("24:3");, #bref("1 Kgs 22:16");; or #he[שֶׁ·]; #bref("Eccl 3:14");; also negatively, #he[עַל־דִּבְרַת שֶׁלֹּא]; #emph[for the matter (purpose) that]; ... #emph[not];, #bref("Eccl 7:14");; #he[לְבִלְתִּי]; with imperfect, #bref("Exod 20:20");, #bref("2 Sam 14:14"); #emph[that]; ... #emph[not];.—Quite exceptional is the use of #he[מִן־]; (if the text be right) in #bref("Deut 33:11"); #he[מִן־יְקוּמוּן];, with the imperfect, equivalent to #emph[that]; ... #emph[not]; \[in prose, #he[מִקּוּם];\].

#ml("c") <p165c> Rem. All the conjunctions here mentioned are naturally always used with the imperfect, see #xref(<p107q>)[§ 107#h(0.18em)#emph[q];]; (on the apparent exception in #bref("Josh 4:24");, see #xref(<p74g>)[§ 74#h(0.18em)#emph[g];];).—On the negative conjunctions #he[אַל]; and #he[פֶּן]; #emph[that not, lest];, see #xref(<p152f>)[§ 152#h(0.18em)#emph[f];]; and #emph[w];. On the infinitive with #he[לְ];#footnote[On #he[לְ]; as a supposed conjunction (equivalent to the Arabic #emph[li];) #bref("1 Kgs 6:19");, see #xref(<p66i>)[§ 66#h(0.18em)#emph[i];];.]; (also #he[לְמַ֫עַן]; #bref("Gen 18:19");, #bref("37:22");, etc.) as the equivalent of a final clause (#bref("Gen 11:5");, #bref("28:4");, etc.), see #xref(<p114f>)[§ 114#h(0.18em)#emph[f];];, #emph[h];, #emph[p];. On the continuation of such infinitival constructions by means of the finite verb, see #xref(<p114r>)[§ 114#h(0.18em)#emph[r];];. On the negation of the final infinitive by #he[לְבִלְתִּי];, #xref(<p114s>)[§ 114#h(0.18em)#emph[s];];. On the preposition #he[מִן]; with a substantive or infinitive as the equivalent of a negative final clause (#bref("Gen 31:29");, #bref("1 Sam 15:23");, etc.), see #xref(<p119x>)[§ 119#h(0.18em)#emph[x];]; and #emph[y];.

#sec("166", [Consecutive Clauses], note: []) <s166>

#ml("a") <p166a> #strong[1.]; Consecutive clauses are added by means of simple #emph[wāw copulative]; with the jussive,#footnote[That such examples as #he[וִיֽכַזֵּב]; are to be regarded as jussive is probable from the analogy of #bref("Hos 14:10"); and #bref("Job 9:33");.]; especially after negative and interrogative sentences, e.g. #bref("Num 23:19"); #he[לֹא אִישׁ אֵל וִיֽכַזֵּב וּבֶן־אָדָם וְיִתְנֶחָ֑ם]; #emph[God is not a man, that he should lie, and]; (i.e. neither) #emph[the son of man, that he should repent];; #bref("Isa 53:2"); #he[וְנֶחְמְדֵ֫הוּ];; #bref("Hos 14:10"); #he[מִי חָכָם וְיָבֵן אֵ֫לֶּה נָבוֹן וְיֵֽדָעֵם]; #emph[who is wise, that he may understand these things? prudent, that he may know them?]; #bref("Job 5:12"); #he[וְלֹא];\=#emph[so that]; ... #emph[not];; in #bref("Prov 30:3"); #he[וְ]; is separated from the predicate by the object. In #bref("Gen 16:10"); a negative consecutive clause comes after a cohortative, and in #bref("Exod 10:5"); after a perfect consecutive.—On the other hand, in #bref("Job 9:32");, 33 the jussive in the sense of a consecutive clause is attached without #emph[Wāw]; to the preceding negative sentence (in #origpage("505") <orig-505>;verse 32 a second jussive follows, likewise without #emph[Wāw];, #emph[for he is not a man, as I am, that I should answer him];, that #emph[we should come together in judgement];). On the imperfect consecutive as expressing a logical consequence, see #xref(<p111l>)[§ 111#h(0.18em)#emph[l];];; on the perfect consecutive as a consecutive clause after a participle, see #xref(<p112n>)[§ 112#h(0.18em)#emph[n];];.

#ml("b") <p166b> 2. Conjunctions introducing consecutive clauses are again (see #xref(<p157c>)[§ 157#h(0.18em)#emph[c];];, note 3) #he[כִּי]; and #he[אֲשֶׁר];\=#emph[so that];; especially again after interrogative sentences, according to #xref(<p107u>)[§ 107#h(0.18em)#emph[u];];; cf. #bref("Num 16:11");, #he[כִּי]; with the imperfect, #emph[that ye murmur];; but in #bref("Gen 20:10"); with the perfect, in reference to an action already completed. On #he[אֲשֶׁר]; with the imperfect (or jussive) equivalent to #emph[so that];, cf. further #bref("Gen 13:16");, #bref("22:14");; with perfect and imperfect, #bref("1 Kgs 3:12f.");, with the demonstrative force clearly discernible, depending on #he[לֵב];; on #he[אֲשֶׁר לֹא];\=#emph[ut non];, cf. #bref("Deut 28:35");, #bref("1 Kgs 3:8");, #bref("2 Kgs 9:37");.

On #he[מִן]; with a substantive or infinitive as the equivalent of a consecutive clause, see #xref(<p119y>)[§ 119#h(0.18em)#emph[y];];.

#sec("167", [Aposiopesis, Anacoluthon, Involved Series of Sentences], note: []) <s167>

#ml("a") <p167a> 1. #emph[Aposiopesis]; is the concealment or suppression of entire sentences or clauses, which are of themselves necessary to complete the sense,#footnote[But those cases are not to be regarded as examples of aposiopesis, in which the answer, being closely connected with the question, is given simply in the infinitive with #he[לְ];; cf. #xref(<p147a>)[§ 147#h(0.18em)#emph[a];];, note 1.]; and therefore must be supplied from the context. This is especially frequent after conditional clauses; besides the examples already given in #xref(<p159dd>)[§ 159#h(0.18em)#emph[dd];];, cf. also #bref("Exod 32:32"); (the LXX and Samaritan supply #he[שָׂא];); #bref("Num 5:20");, #bref("Judg 9:16"); (in verse 19, after a long parenthesis, an imperative follows as the apodosis to this conditional clause); #bref("1 Sam 12:14"); f., #bref("2 Sam 5:8"); (where indeed the text is probably very corrupt; cf. the addition in #bref("1 Chr 11:6");); #bref("2 Sam 23:17");, #bref("Ps 27:13");, #bref("1 Chr 4:10");. For other examples of various kinds, see #xref(<p117l>)[§ 117#h(0.18em)#emph[l];];, and especially #xref(<s147>)[§ 147];; in Aramaic, #bref("Dan 3:15");.—On #bref("Gen 3:22");, cf. #xref(<p152w>)[§ 152#h(0.18em)#emph[w];]; at the end.

#ml("b") <p167b> 2. #emph[Anacoluthon]; is the change from a construction which has been already begun to one of a different kind. It is found especially after long parentheses, because the speaker has either lost sight of the beginning of his sentence, or for the sake of clearness purposely makes a new beginning; thus #bref("Gen 20:13");, #bref("31:52"); and #bref("Ezek 34:10"); (cf. #xref(<s149>)[§ 149]; at the end); #bref("Num 14:21ff.");, #bref("32:20ff.");, #bref("Deut 17:2ff.");, #bref("24:1ff.");, #bref("29:21ff.");, #bref("Judg 10:11"); (where, after a series of intermediate sentences, the predicate #emph[I saved you]; is pressed #origpage("506") <orig-506>;; but the text can hardly be correct); perhaps also #bref("Isa 66:18"); (cf., however, Delitzsch on the passage, which is certainly corrupt).#footnote[On the other hand, from the Semitic point of view the various kinds of compound sentences are #emph[not]; to be regarded as instances of anacoluthon, e.g. #bref("Gen 17:14, 17");, nor even #bref("Gen 31:40"); (cf. #xref(<s143>)[§ 143];).]; On #bref("Gen 23:13"); (#he[לוּ]; with the imperative), see #xref(<p110e>)[§ 110#h(0.18em)#emph[e];];.

#ml("c") <p167c> #strong[3.]; We may mention as instructive examples of #emph[involved series]; of sentences #bref("Gen 24:14"); and #bref("v. 42ff.");, and #bref("Gen 28:6ff.");

#bookhead(1, [The Paradigms]);
#origpage("507") <orig-507>;

In the paradigms of the verbs, those forms which are to be especially noticed by the beginner are marked throughout by an asterisk as model forms. Thus e.g. in the strong verb the 3rd #emph[sing. fem];. #he[קָֽטְלָה]; is the model for #he[קָֽטְלוּ];, which likewise has only a vocalic afformative, and #he[קָטַ֫לְתָּ]; is the model for #he[קָטַלְתְּ];, #he[קָטַ֫לְתִּי]; and #he[קָטַ֫לְנוּ];, which in the same way have a toneless afformative beginning with a consonant. On the other hand, the forms #he[קְטַלְתֶּם]; and #he[קְטַלְתֶּן];, where the affix beginning with a consonant has the tone, stand by themselves.—In the table of the pronouns the asterisk has a different meaning; see the footnote there.—The bracketed forms (for Paradigm G onwards) are merely analogous formations not occurring in the Old Testament.

The newly added paradigm (Q) consists of forms actually found, belonging to various verbs.

